\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 How The Debate Over Abortion Access Is Affecting Voters In Our Series America Decides\b0\par\par\uc2 John Dickerson, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 701 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And now to the midterm elections. They`re just over a month away. North Carolina is an outlier in the South when it comes to abortion rights. One clinic there is seeing a flood of patients from states that have banned or restricted the procedure.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns reports on how the debate over abortion access is affecting voters in our series America Decides.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN (Runs Woman`s Choice Clinic): A lot of patients are coming here, especially from Louisiana and Texas.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voice-over): Kelly Flynn runs a women`s choice clinic in Raleigh, North Carolina. She says, since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the majority of her patients are now coming from out of state.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: We just helped a patient from Alabama last week. They drove a 10-and-a-half-hour trip.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Ten-and-a-half hours?\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: Mm-hmm, each way.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: It`s an issue resonating with Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You just pray that it doesn`t happen here, but it`s definitely a real possibility if people don`t come out and vote.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The fate of abortion access here in North Carolina all depends on what happens in the legislature. Republicans have a majority there, but they don`t yet have enough seats to override a veto from the Democratic governor. That could all change after November`s elections.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D-North Carolina Senatorial Candidate): Fight hard for the constitutionally protected right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: It`s also tightening the campaign for an open \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seat long held by Republicans. Democratic nominee Cheri Beasley hopes the stakes will drive voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY: Even in the smallest rural communities, regardless of party affiliation, people want to talk about abortion access.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TED BUDD (R-North Carolina): I`m going to work, and I`m going to vote to stop inflation.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Beasley`s opponent, Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , supports federal abortion restriction, but believes the election will hinge on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Republican pollster Paul Shumaker says there`s little upside for GOP candidates to engage on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHUMAKER (Republican Pollster): Keep the discussion about the economy. Don`t have a discussion about the abortion issue, because, quite frankly, he runs the risk of stepping on his base enthusiasm for turning out.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And turnout will be key in a tight race with the balance of power on the line.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, CBS News, Raleigh, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The search for victims continues in the aftermath of Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 We will have an update on storm-ravaged Florida when we return.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : More than a week after Hurricane Ian made landfall on Florida`s southwest coast, rescue crews are still going door to door in search of victims.\par \par \uc2 At least 127 people have been killed, while hundreds of thousands have had their lives changed forever by the storm. More than 100,000 customers are still without electricity, and, for some, it could be at least a month before power is fully restored.\par \par \uc2 In a rebuke to Vladimir Putin`s war on Ukraine, this year`s Nobel Peace Prize is being shared among three championships of human rights, democracy, and peaceful coexistence. We learned today the Peace Prize will be awarded to a human rights organization called Memorial. Also receiving the honor is the Ukrainian Center for Civil Liberties and an activist jailed in Russian- allied Belarus.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next with an act of kindness straight from the heart.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221011eia7000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortions Increase In North Carolina\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Jacobson, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 2,782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In the last few months, North Carolina has become a haven for abortion access in the South. It is one of the few States in that region that hasn`t restricted it since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June. Abortion is legal in North Carolina up to 20 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In neighboring South Carolina, a ban starting at six weeks has been temporarily blocked. CBS News got exclusive access inside a clinic in Raleigh where a flood of patients is coming from out-of-state.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns shows us how this trend could affect key political contests in November.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Kelly Flynn runs a Woman`s Choice Clinic in Raleigh, which has been flooded with calls.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN (A Woman`s Choice Clinic CEO): We were at like 1,500 calls a week and now, we`re up to like 2,500 calls a week from patients coming from out-of-state.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Before the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, roughly 35 percent of patients treated at this clinic were from out-of- state. Now, with most neighboring states restricting or banning abortion, the Clinic says that number stands at 56 percent.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: Now, we just helped a patient from Alabama last week. They drove a 10-and-a-half hour trip to make sure they were at the clinic at nine o`clock that morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Ten-and-a-half hours.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: Each way.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: AND that is typical now.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: Yes. And so, we have patients here today that came from Florida and one came from Georgia and they have to draw it back today.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: It is an issue resonating with Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 MARY ADAMS (North Carolina voter): I have friends who are opening their doors to people in border states who can`t have a safe and legal procedure and we just pray that it doesn`t happen here, but it is definitely a real possibility if people don`t come out and vote.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The fate of abortion access here in North Carolina all depends on what happens in the legislature. Republicans have a majority there, but they don`t yet have enough seats to override a veto from the Democratic Governor. That could all change after November`s elections.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina`s tight \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race, Democratic nominee Cheri Beasley hopes the stakes will drive voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D) (North Carolina Senate candidate): You know, it certainly is very important, Caitlin. I can certainly tell you that that even in the smallest rural communities, regardless of party affiliation, people want to talk about abortion access.\par \par \uc2 REP. TED BUDD (R-NC): And I`m going to work and I`m going to vote to stop inflation.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But Beasley`s opponent, Congressman Ted Budd, who supports Federal abortion restrictions believes the election will hinge on the economy and his supporters agree.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We need our gas prices down. We need our grocery prices down.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: North Carolina Republican pollster, Paul Shumaker says there is little upside for GOP candidates like Budd to engage on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHUMAKER (North Carolina Republican pollster): Keep the discussion about the economy, don`t have the discussion about the abortion issue because quite frankly, he runs a risk of stepping on his base enthusiasm for turning out.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Caitlin Huey-Burns, Raleigh, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you will be talking about today, but first it is 7:41, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 There is Vlad.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hut-hut. Time for "What to Watch," but we are calling an audible.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Man, are we calling an audible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nice. I see what you did there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that audible is Mr. Duthiers got a positive COVID test like one second ago.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And so he has been spirited away and just three of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we are nibble on our feet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And I literally pushed -- I basically gave Nate the Heisman.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I was like, "No, I got this."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said, "Coach, put me in the game."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I got this. I wanted -- I haven`t done this in a while.\par \par \uc2 All right, shotgun wedding time. Ready for this?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s see it.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Shotgun wedding between \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , and you know this, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Is it ever going to happen?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Or are their trust issues going to get in the way?\par \par \uc2 Well, a Judge yesterday postponed a trial between the two set to start later this month after you may remember \uc2 Twitter\uc2  sued Musk for going back on his offer to buy the company. The Judge gave them a three-week deadline to complete the buyout. Musk says he expects to have the financing in place by October 28th. That is just a short time away.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, he wants \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to kill the suit. The company says Musk just wants further mischief and delay. Several media outlets report that a protester has filed -- oh sorry, I`m moving on. See this is what happens when you audible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The audible was --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I was already moving on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is like a true, like on with a center audible, last second.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No time at all.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So, the Musk thing is still crazy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That it is still --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because he was out, he`s in. He was out and now he is back in.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It reminds me of a lot of ex-boyfriends.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That may get lined up.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But \uc2 Twitter\uc2  didn`t want him and has basically said that, you know -- but now they do and I got a note here from Vlad actually, which kind of explains it. Apparently, it all comes down to money.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When he made his offer --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Shocking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Share prices went up. Then they went down --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When he pulled it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, they are back at 49.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is going to buy it at $54.00 a share, which means everybody who has got \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stock is going to make some bang.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Which does make a --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Land everything else Musk has touched has turned to gold.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock is up 10X.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, that`s next story, I think I just wanted to get to this because I`m dying -- you know this next story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`m dying to hear what you think of this.\par \par \uc2 Media outlets reporting that a protester has filed a police report against \uc2 LA Rams\uc2  linebacker, Bobby Wagner after this happened on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 A man ran onto the field carrying a device spewing pink smoke. Security guards couldn`t catch him. So Wagner and defensive lineman, Takk McKinley took him down instead, pretty firmly. This was an animal rights group known for public stunts, said the man was trying to raise awareness of a trial involving the alleged theft of pigs from a factory farm, says the protester was cited and released by police.\par \par \uc2 A representative told TMZ he filed his own report after a "blatant assault."\par \par \uc2 Wagner says the incident is behind him and he is more concerned about the security guard who was hurt chasing the protester. Rams coach Sean McVeigh says he supports Wagner.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, I was curious about what you think because here is the thing, you`re out there playing football, you`re on the field, anybody can come on the field. That`s not okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s not okay. It`s your workplace, right?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The cause is fine, if somebody is protesting something like that, but if I`m at work, and somebody runs onto this set, I don`t know what that person is doing. I don`t know if they`re armed with anything.\par \par \uc2 Now, Bobby Wagner is sitting there getting ready to play the next snap, and he sees somebody is running on the field. There have been fans that have ran onto courts and injured players. I think there was a stabbing on a tennis court.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Monica Seles.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You remember that. So, I don`t blame Bobby for doing that. He could have let the guy run, but at the same time, you never know what the person is up to.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It kind of reminds me of those cases where somebody trespasses and then is hurt after committing the trespass.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then they sue the person who owns the property and you`re like, "Come on."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Maybe you should have thought about where you`re going. You`re going on to an NFL field.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right. A new restaurant in San Francisco, a luxury restaurant in San Francisco, tasty pastries, unique creations, sounds really good. Look at this flower. It`s made out of wild venison heart and beet root.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, sounds delicious.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Steak tartar topped with quail egg.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`d try that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: A three-course tasting 