hat`s the most important thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Clark Dokoupil. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jericka talks with Patti LaBelle. You don`t want to miss this.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (08:00AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, the legendary Patti LaBelle for today`s "Morning`s Mixtape." She tells Jericka Duncan why she is not done climbing.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`m Dana Jacobson. Oliver Jeffers creates visual worlds that kids love to dive into. He`ll have perspective on his latest book about a family road trip through space.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. If you can go anywhere in the world right now, where would it be? We`ll look at the new "Conde Nast" Traveler poll filled with great recommendations from your fellow adventurer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, you`ll take the kids, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Russia unleashes more missile strikes as Ukraine gains ground, and President Biden warns that Putin is not joking about using nukes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: According to a British Intelligence report today, Ukrainians have captured so many Russian tanks at least 440.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sources tell CBS News, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  believes it has enough evidence for criminal charges against Hunter Biden.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The lawyer for the President`s son slammed the allegations as inaccurate and claimed Hunter Biden is a target simply because of his family name.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: President Biden says he`ll pardon all federal offenses for simple possession of marijuana.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: I`m calling on all Governors to do the same for State marijuana possession offenses. Too many lives have been upended because of our failed approach to marijuana.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gas prices in California surge $1.15 in a single month.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DE HAAN: We`ve never seen the gap between California and the rest of the country this high.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He said he wanted to serve in the military again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And in a touching ceremony, a World War Two vet is sworn in as an honorary member of the Michigan National Guard for his 100th birthday.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He served his nation not once, but twice.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP singing "Happy Birthday.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Looking pretty good for a hundred.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, when you serve, you leave, but you still serve, right? And they brought him back. You never really go.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It stays with you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It stays with you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221008eia70005n\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden Warns of Nuclear Armageddon; New Russian Attacks on Civilians; Nobel Peace Prize Announced; Hunter Biden Investigation; Biden`s Blunt Marijuana Move; Concern Over Rising Gas Prices; Deadly Las Vegas Strip Stabbings; Mourning in Thailand; Uvalde Officer Fired After Outrage; Abortions Increase in North Carolina; Cosmic Perspective; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Dana Jacobson, Charlie D`Agata,  Catherine Herridge, Jonathan Vigliotti, Jericka Duncan, Nikki Battiste,  Jonathan Head, Lilia Luciano, Caitlin Huey-Burns, \par \uc2 13,462 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday, October 7th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`m Dana Jacobson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off on a very fun assignment, but we`re all here. Here is today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A warning from President Biden. He says nuclear Armageddon is closer than it has been in 60 years after comments from President Putin.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER (Pentagon Press Secretary): This nuclear saber rattling is reckless and irresponsible. Again, we are taking it very seriously.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sources tell CBS News there is believed to be enough evidence to charge President Biden`s son with multiple crimes.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN (Former US attorney and Deputy Assistant Attorney General): It carries a significant penalty under Federal law.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Two people are dead and six others wounded after a man went on a stabbing spree on the Las Vegas Strip.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Gas prices are on the rise again after OPEC`s decision to cut oil production. What you need to know and why it`s worse in some States.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That`s way too expensive.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: American officials say two separate US military operations in Northern Syria have killed three senior ISIS leaders.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thursday Night Football, a big boring horse fight.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Colts had the last laugh.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Broken up in the endzone.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I am announcing a pardon for all prior Federal offenses for the simple possession of marijuana.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): It is a huge week for the characters on Scooby Doo. Think about it. Yes, first Velma comes out as a lesbian and now Shaggy is getting a Federal pardon. What?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): He is pardoning Federal marijuana simple possession offenses, all of them from the dankest nugs to the harshest ditch weed.\par \par \uc2 I`m talking hot, grass, Mary Jane, reefer, the sweet sticky icky, ganja, chum-chum, lime pillows, sticks and stems, herb, chronic, game, and the devil`s lettuce, wacky-tobacky, Acapulco gold, jazz cigarettes, and the Right Honorable Reverend Al Green.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. How about that for human ingenuity? It`s a pretty good list.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Did he leave any out?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I can name a few, believe it or not.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh really?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, believe it or not.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, we will talk about that in the show.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That would be for the podcast.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Or the pot-cast.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the pot-cast.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Nice. Nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, listen. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We are spending time with artists today, including the legendary Patti LaBelle. Her voice has been moving us for decades. Now, as she tells Jericka Duncan, she is not slowing down.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE (Singer): I am always on the run, I`m booked and busy and blessed.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Not bad and bougie.\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE: No.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Booked, busy and blessed.\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE: Right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, they don`t call her the Godmother of Soul for nothing. She is the subject of today`s "Morning`s Mixtape." All about the music we love.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And if you are a parent, you`ve probably stepped into the world of Oliver Jeffers, creator of books like "Here We Are" and "How To Catch Star." He spoke to Nikki Battiste, about helping kids to understand big ideas using a five-mile long model of the solar system.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay. He is very big in our household. So, I`m looking forward to that piece, but we are going to begin with the threat of nuclear war. A comment from President Biden reacting to threats from Russia`s President on the use of so-called tactical nuclear weapons in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden said he does not think that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is joking. Comments came last night at a fundraiser in New York where Mr. Biden said, "We have not faced the prospect of Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban Missile Crisis." He added, "I don`t think there is any such thing as the ability to use a tactical nuclear weapon and not end up with Armageddon."\par \par \uc2 This comes at a time when Ukraine`s Army appears to be weakening Russia`s military position and that is generating fears about what exactly the Russian leader will do to win the war that he started in February.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in Ukraine for us once again.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been to the frontlines in liberated cities, but you don`t have to go that far to see the destruction that Russia is capable of as we witnessed for ourselves in Zaporizhzhia.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Emergency teams scrambled in a desperate search for those who survived the missile strike, at times finding those who did not.\par \par \uc2 We arrived to find firefighters dousing a mountain of twisted metal and debris. War crimes investigators looking through the rubble. Local residents in disbelief.\par \par \uc2 Several hours after the rocket attack and the building is still smoldering. This is no longer a rescue operation. This is a building smack dab in the middle of Zaporizhzhia.\par \par \uc2 And while the city is firmly under Ukrainian control, it is the regional capital of one of the areas President Putin illegally annexed just days ago.\par \par \uc2 Olena (sp?) got a 5:00 AM phone call from her 70-year-old mother saying she was trapped beneath the rubble.\par \par \uc2 Is she going to be okay? Was she injured?\par \par \uc2 (OLENA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "She has broken ribs, a lung contusion, and a broken leg," she said, "But she is alive."\par \par \uc2 ARE you okay? You seem very composed. Were you not terrified? Are you not angry?\par \par \uc2 OLENA (Resident): Very angry. It is not possible in our time. It`s not possible. And our country, help -- we need help.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Lying on the pavement, three of at least 11 people killed here.\par \par \uc2 More innocent victims of President Putin`s senseless war that had nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 It`s the second major missile attack targeting residents in Zaporizhzhia within a week. Last Friday, we saw the aftermath of a strike on a civilian convoy that killed at least 30 people.\par \par \uc2 Battlefield Commanders tell us Russian forces are in complete disarray. Their retaliation launching long-range weapons into residential areas, spreading terror. What Russia can`t capture, it seeks to destroy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: One reason for the recent success according to a British Intelligence report today, Ukrainians have captured so many Russian tanks, at least 440. It is estimated that roughly half of the tank fleet now consists of Russian tanks -- Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, Charlie D`Agata in Ukraine. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Human rights groups in Ukraine and Russia and a jailed pro-democracy activist in neighboring Belarus will share the 2022 Nobel Peace Prize. The recipients were announced in Norway this morning.\par \par \uc2 Memorial is Russia`s oldest human rights organization. It was banned in December 2021 by President Putin. The Center for Civil Liberties in Ukraine has been documenting Russian war crimes; and in Belarus, political prisoners Ales Bialiatski who was honored for his work opposing dictator \uc2 Alexander Lukashenko\uc2 , a Putin ally. The Nobel Prize Committee said this morning, "Together, they demonstrate the significance of civil society for peace and democracy."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here in this country, we are learning more about the Federal investigation of the President`s son, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sources tell CBS News, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and \uc2 IRS\uc2  believe there is enough evidence to charge the younger Biden with crimes, although not in connection with his business dealings overseas.\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge is in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Good morning. Nate.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for the President`s son slammed the allegations as inaccurate and claimed \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is a target simply because of his family name. Hunter Biden`s lawyer also called on the Justice Department to investigate how information apparently leaked from within the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Multiple sources tell CBS News, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has believed for months, there is enough evidence to prosecute the President`s son with tax crimes and making false statements on a gun purchase. The sources said the \uc2 FBI\uc2  sent its findings earlier this year to the US Attorney in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 David Weiss was appointed by President Trump and is yet to bring charges.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN: He has got to make that final call under the principles of Federal prosecution, not the initial call that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has made in gathering evidence.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: A lawyer for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  called the apparently inherently biased, one-sided, and inaccurate, adding their team believes prosecutors should not be pressured, rushed, or criticized for doing their job.\par \par \uc2 The years` long Federal investigation into Hunter Biden`s business practices began before \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was President. Mr. Biden has consistently denied knowledge of his son`s work, as he did here on the presidential campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (Then candidate for US President): I`ve never spoke to my son about his overseas business dealings.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The Federal criminal probe explored whether the younger Biden, his Uncle James, and other business associates violated tax, money laundering, and foreign lobbying laws.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the probe narrowed focusing on delinquent tax payments, including income \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  earned as a Board member for Ukrainian energy company. A source close to the President`s son said, he repaid more than a million dollars in past due taxes.\par \par \uc2 In an interview last year with CBS News` Anthony Mason, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  downplayed potential charges.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There is a Federal tax investigation into you. Where does that stand now?\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN (President Joe Biden`s son): They are investigating. I can`t really talk about it other than to say is that I`m fully cooperative and I`m fully confident that at the end of the day, it`s all going to be fine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The timing coming four weeks before the midterm elections seems to raise the political stakes even though President Biden is not on the ballot. Senior Republicans say they will investigate the Biden family businesses if they take control of key committees in January - - Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is often said, no one is above the law. Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Laws do sometimes change; however, and we turn now to President Biden`s big step, at least symbolically toward decriminalizing marijuana under Federal law.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, he announced a mass pardon for everyone convicted of a crime for simple possession and urged State governments to follow him. So, what does that mean for people behind bars for pot-related crimes right now?\par \par \uc2 Well, in the short term, probably not a whole lot. No one in fact is serving Federal prison time solely for marijuana possession, the White House pointed out, and the vast majority of convictions happen at the State level, so do not expect anyone to be released from prison because of this Executive Order.\par \par \uc2 However, roughly 6,500 people do have Federal convictions for possession, convictions that could have blocked them from Federal housing, Federal student loans, and also some jobs, those obstacles would now be gone.\par \par \uc2 Simple possession by the way means only enough for personal use and no intent to sell or share, which means that convicted dealers are not included. And the President still does believe, he says that strict laws are required against trafficking and underage sales.\par \par \uc2 In another potentially historic move, Mr. Biden has directed the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  and the Attorney General to review marijuana`s classification at the Federal level. It is currently listed as Schedule One, that means no medical value and a strong potential for abuse.\par \par \uc2 And Dana, before we go from grass to gas in our next segment here, I would point out that to do anything more, the President is going to need Congress.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, and you and I were talking about it though, that classification could be the difference between people being able to do research with marijuana and so many groups are out there trying to see that done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a very big deal if it changes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, we are going to go from grass to gas now because gasoline prices are going up again. The national average for a gallon of regular rose to almost $3.90 yesterday. The average is much higher in California, up to $6.39 a gallon after the worldwide oil cartel, OPEC+ decided to tighten its supply.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is one of the millions paying those high gas prices in California. Sorry, Jonathan. He joins us from North Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Good morning to you, Dana.\par \par \uc2 I prefer to drive around on empty until my husband fills up the tank. It is a pretty good trick.\par \par \uc2 Here in LA County, it is normal to pay seven to even eight dollars a gallon for gas that easily adds up to around $100.00 for your typical midsize car.\par \par \uc2 To help ease the pain, the State now issuing gas rebates and for millions, it can`t come soon enough.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That`s a lot of money and it`s way too expensive.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Gas prices are on the rise again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s tough to live in LA right now.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But in California, it is on a whole other level, where prices have soared $1.15 in the past month.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DE HAAN (GasBuddy): We`ve never seen the gap between California and the rest of the country this high.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: This price spike comes after several California oil refineries shut down for maintenance, limiting supplies of gasoline.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just California, a total of six Western States are averaging more than $5.00 a gallon. And some Midwest States are also seeing an increase as they face refinery issues as well.\par \par \uc2 But now, prices may rise in all parts of the US after OPEC+ led by Saudi Arabia decided to cut oil production.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What`s your reaction to the OPEC+ decision, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Disappointment.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Leading President Biden having to defend his visit to Saudi Arabia this summer, which was in part intended to convince Saudi leaders to support a boost in oil production.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Are there any regrets about the trip now?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : No. The trip was not essentially for oil. The trip was about the Middle East and about Israel.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DE HAAN: With gas prices going up as Americans enter the voting booths, it could be a stark reminder that gas prices are very high priority for your average American.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And starting this morning here in California, some 23 million residents will get those rebates. They are linked to income and the size of family. Some households could receive more than $1,000.00 -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Las Vegas, there is new information this morning about the suspect accused of stabbing eight people on the strip yesterday. Two of those victims died and police call it an unprovoked attacked.\par \par \uc2 Video of the scene is disturbing. The suspect is identified as 32-year-old Yoni Barrios. Police say he used a large kitchen knife in the stabbings. Police say this security footage shows the suspect fleeing from the scene before he was quickly captured with the help of security from a nearby casino.\par \par \uc2 The two victims who died and attack lived in Las Vegas. Witnesses say some of the injured were show girls who were posing for pictures with the tourists.\par \par \uc2 Police are still looking for a motive for the stabbings, which appear to be random. The suspect is scheduled to appear in Court later this morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just an awful story. I mean, it includes tourists, it includes locals and shocking scenes there from Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is sad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We do have more on a story we told you about yesterday, also a very troubling story. It is the latest from Thailand, a country in mourning after at least 34 people were murdered in an attack on a daycare center. Most of those victims were children.\par \par \uc2 The suspect is a former police sergeant and Jonathan Head from our partners at the BBC filed this report from the scene.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HEAD (BBC News South East Asia correspondent): What you are seeing here, with all of these officials filling out forms and paperwork is a community coming to terms with an almost unthinkable horror.\par \par \uc2 Over here are people from this village, a small farming village; some people have come from even further away just in solidarity to offer a thick cushion of human support for the families of those 23 children and all the adults who lost their lives in this tragedy.\par \par \uc2 So, they filled out this community hall. People have been here since this morning and there is no end of volunteers here giving them counseling, helping them fill out the forms they need to do to recover the bodies of their young children preparing for funerals.\par \par \uc2 But also what is happening is, it is a great coming together. Everyone here is sharing this terrible burden. Of course, what nobody has up to now is any answers as to why this dreadful event happened.\par \par \uc2 This is a very quiet place with a little nursery school where nobody could have imagined anything like this happening, even the teachers who confronted the killer with his gun and then ran away from him say they never thought he would target the children, and it is the shock of that, that people here are still absorbing. And of course, those endless questions about what drove this man to do something so extraordinary and so dreadful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was Jonathan Head reporting.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Uvalde Texas hires a former State Trooper to protect its schools, then fires her. We`ll show you what she said at the scene of this year`s school massacre that led to her dismissal.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17, time to check a local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up: New limits on abortion in the south bring a rush of out-of-state patients to a clinic in North Carolina. We`ll hear from its CEO and show you how Democrats are We`re using it as a campaign issue.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up in our "Mornings Mixtape," Jericka Duncan sits down with the living legend, Patti LaBelle, and even gets a singing lesson.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: If you sing something, I`ll try to mimic it. Can we do that real quick?\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE: I`ve got you.\par \par \uc2 (PATTI LABELLE AND JERICKA DUNCAN vocalizing.)\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE: See you did it. I know, I hear it in your voice.\par \par \uc2 (PATTI LABELLE AND JERICKA DUNCAN vocalizing.)\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE: Girl, you better shut up. You can sing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wait a minute wait. Wait a minute.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s really good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wait, wait. You guys want to try it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am not.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: No, but that was really good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t have that. What I love is Patti did it effortlessly and Jericka had to (vocalizing). She had to scrunch her face up and bring it from the diaphragm.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Even that was good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can tell.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is not here so we can talk about her. I`m glad Jericka did that piece and not Gayle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, maybe Gayle could use the singing lessons from Patti LaBelle.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that would be great actually.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, we love you. We are not talking about it. We just miss you, okay.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jericka finds out what keeps LaBelle going strong after more than 60 years of performing.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Here at 7:30.\par \par \uc2 A police officer just hired by the Uvalde Texas School District has now been fired over comments she made on the day of the school shooting there. Body camera footage shows Crimson Elizondo arriving at Robb Elementary minutes after a gunman opened fire there. She was a Texas State Trooper at the time of the shooting.\par \par \uc2 When the District hired her, she was already the subject of a State Police internal investigation. Lilia Luciano has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Elizondo was among the first Texas Troopers to arrive at Robb Elementary on May 24th. Body camera video shows her enter the building and wait for instruction while the gunman terrorize students and teachers.\par \par \uc2 DISPATCH: He is going to be armed with a rifle --\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In new footage released by CNN, you can reportedly hear her talking with other officers and saying this --\par \par \uc2 CRIMSON ELIZONDO (Former Texas State Trooper): I know. If my son had been in there, I would not have been outside, I promise you that.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In his statement Thursday, the Uvalde School District cited the CNN audio in its decision to fire Elizondo, writing that which he said is not consistent with the District`s expectations.\par \par \uc2 But documents obtained by CBS News revealed that the District hired her even after the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  informed them that she was under investigation for actions inconsistent with training and department requirements.\par \par \uc2 What was your reaction to those comments that you heard her make on video?\par \par \uc2 BRETT CROSS (Parent): It`s sickening. As a parent, how do you say that? You know, how do you not just go in?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Brett Cross is the uncle of 10-year-old shooting victim, Uziyah Garcia. He has been camped outside of the district`s administrative building for more than a week demanding that all school resource officers connected to the shooting response be fired or suspended.\par \par \uc2 Are you satisfied that this officer was fired?\par \par \uc2 BRETT CROSS: I mean, I wouldn`t call it satisfied as much as why the hell did they do it in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And that is the big sentiment for many of the parents. In their statement, the Uvalde School District apologized for the pain the revelations around Elizondo`s comments may have caused the victims` families, but the District did not explain or address their decision for bringing her onboard after they knew she had been investigated.\par \par \uc2 We tried to contact Elizondo for comment and haven`t heard back.\par \par \uc2 But look, here`s the big thing. She and other officers are being investigated for not following their training, which says you go in, you stop the killing. And now in this video, we hear her saying, if it had been my son and there, I would have gone in basically. That`s what should have informed the training.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She was on the scene but wasn`t in a position of authority?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In a position of authority, was of course Pete Arredondo, who is already terminated. She is a second officer to lose her job after this of hundreds who showed up. Of 91 officers from DPS who showed up, only seven are being investigated, two have been suspended.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You see, you keep going back to though the idea that the District would hire her knowing this and the pain that it causes the parents again -- yet again.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And the parents feel like they have the homework. They have to be checking those videos, telling the District listen to us. That`s why Brett Cross is camping today on his 10th day. We want every one of those officers at least suspended until you finish the investigation. They shouldn`t be watching our kids.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The very least held accountable, right?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Lilia, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Okay, coming up, a rare look at an abortion clinic in the south where the debate over cutting off access may determine how people vote in next month`s election.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on the go podcast. You can hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. Everything you need in 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In the last few months, North Carolina has become a haven for abortion access in the South. It is one of the few States in that region that hasn`t restricted it since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June. Abortion is legal in North Carolina up to 20 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In neighboring South Carolina, a ban starting at six weeks has been temporarily blocked. CBS News got exclusive access inside a clinic in Raleigh where a flood of patients is coming from out-of-state.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns shows us how this trend could affect key political contests in November.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Kelly Flynn runs a Woman`s Choice Clinic in Raleigh, which has been flooded with calls.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN (A Woman`s Choice Clinic CEO): We were at like 1,500 calls a week and now, we`re up to like 2,500 calls a week from patients coming from out-of-state.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Before the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, roughly 35 percent of patients treated at this clinic were from out-of- state. Now, with most neighboring states restricting or banning abortion, the Clinic says that number stands at 56 percent.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: Now, we just helped a patient from Alabama last week. They drove a 10-and-a-half hour trip to make sure they were at the clinic at nine o`clock that morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Ten-and-a-half hours.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: Each way.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: AND that is typical now.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: Yes. And so, we have patients here today that came from Florida and one came from Georgia and they have to draw it back today.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: It is an issue resonating with Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 MARY ADAMS (North Carolina voter): I have friends who are opening their doors to people in border states who can`t have a safe and legal procedure and we just pray that it doesn`t happen here, but it is definitely a real possibility if people don`t come out and vote.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The fate of abortion access here in North Carolina all depends on what happens in the legislature. Republicans have a majority there, but they don`t yet have enough seats to override a veto from the Democratic Governor. That could all change after November`s elections.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina`s tight \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race, Democratic nominee Cheri Beasley hopes the stakes will drive voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D) (North Carolina Senate candidate): You know, it certainly is very important, Caitlin. I can certainly tell you that that even in the smallest rural communities, regardless of party affiliation, people want to talk about abortion access.\par \par \uc2 REP. TED BUDD (R-NC): And I`m going to work and I`m going to vote to stop inflation.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But Beasley`s opponent, Congressman Ted Budd, who supports Federal abortion restrictions believes the election will hinge on the economy and his supporters agree.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We need our gas prices down. We need our grocery prices down.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: North Carolina Republican pollster, Paul Shumaker says there is little upside for GOP candidates like Budd to engage on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHUMAKER (North Carolina Republican pollster): Keep the discussion about the economy, don`t have the discussion about the abortion issue because quite frankly, he runs a risk of stepping on his base enthusiasm for turning out.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, I`m Caitlin Huey-Burns, Raleigh, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you will be talking about today, but first it is 7:41, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 There is Vlad.\par