\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then they sue the person who owns the property and you`re like, "Come on."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Maybe you should have thought about where you`re going. You`re going on to an NFL field.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right. A new restaurant in San Francisco, a luxury restaurant in San Francisco, tasty pastries, unique creations, sounds really good. Look at this flower. It`s made out of wild venison heart and beet root.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, sounds delicious.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Steak tartar topped with quail egg.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`d try that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: A three-course tasting menu for $75.00.\par \par \uc2 One thing though, the food is for dogs.\par \par \uc2 Barclay out there? Dogs. The restaurant -- the new trendy spot is called Dogue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at how it is spelled.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: D-O-G-U-E. Douge. It is Mastiff in French.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Douge. So, the chef worked with a veterinarian to get these creations and develop a wonderful menu for our four-legged friends.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I will tell you that I`ve been to a lot of places where they have things by the register that look freakin` delicious.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And they are for dogs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then the guy would be like, "Sir, that`s for dogs."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Except, if you think about it --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you`re over there like Shaggy, like, "Are you sure?"\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You should try it, because usually they are organic. The ingredients are ones that you would want. There is probably no sugar in them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Fill