d out to me was a moment with a random stranger on a plane that changed everything.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE MADDON (Former professional baseball manager and coach): This lady wanted to talk. So, I`m just answering questions politely. Then all of a sudden, she said, "Remember one thing. Whatever you put out there comes back to you." I went like, "What? Could you say that again, please?" And she repeated it. I said, "Damn," I mean, that was like -- that smacked me in the face.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know. I mean, that sentence uttered to me at that moment changed me. I got off the plane and I was completely myself, again. Not worried about being passed over. I`ll get my opportunity. If I do the right things, it is going to come to me again.\par \par \uc2 Everything came back to what it had been just based on that one phrase.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Maddon is just an incredible guy. He`s a baseball lifer. He worked for decades in the minors before even getting an opportunity to coach in the majors. Fifty-one before he got a managerial job in the majors. It is just something that doesn`t happen now.\par \par \uc2 And when I tell you, it`s as much about life as it is baseball. He is incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`ll give you this tease for the interview that you`ll see on Saturday tomorrow. He is all about the three G`s right now, because he`s not managing. I`m not going to tell you what those three G`s are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, what? I was about to write them down.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It`s a tease.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, I`ll tune in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll tune in like I always do.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You will find out the three G`s tomorrow on "CBS Saturday Morning."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Although as a dad, I do have to say, I`m not sure