 isn`t that bad, kids, so just -- you know if you feel like you should be sitting at home, trust your gut. Sit it out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes. All right, FOMO no mo`. That`s terrible.\par \par \uc2 In case you missed it on week`s CBS Deals, we showed you products that might help you save some time, save some money as well and they include the Clean Light Air Pro. It is a portable, rechargeable air purifier designed to help clean the air in your home or office, and you can get it today for 58 percent off.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping for this and for other items we showed you that may become essentials in your everyday life, scan the QR code in the upper left corner of your screen right now. You can also visit CBSdeals.com.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up: Best-selling children`s book artist and writer, Oliver Jeffers.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Here We Are.")\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You probably know that voice, but maybe not the person who wrote the words.\par \par \uc2 Meryl Streep narrates the \uc2 Apple\uc2  TV adaptation of Oliver Jeffers` book "Here We Are: Notes For Living On Planet Earth." It`s just one offering by the artist from Northern Ireland who sold more than 14 million books worldwide.\par \par \uc2 In his new book, "Meanwhile, Back On Earth," Jeffers takes readers on a ride through space and explains some complicated topics for young audiences.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste met Jeffers in his Brooklyn New York studio to talk about his unique style, which has made him a favorite among kids and kids at heart.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: I think a lot of people may not know your name, but they know this handwriting.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS (Author): Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Oliver Jeffers is a name you might not recognize.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: When I was doing book tours, and I would stand up there and kids didn`t care who I was, until I started joining them like, "Oh, that guy."\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: But that guy`s work is sitting on kids` bookshelves and in bookstores around the world, including Brooklyn`s Books Are Magic where Jeffers bumped into a young fan.\par \par \uc2 Were you a big reader when you were growing up?\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: No, I was not.\par \par \uc2 But I was much more interested in playing football on the street or digging holes or drawing pictures.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The author`s childhood love of illustration drew him to the art world. He exhibited works in Northern Ireland and Australia when his career took a chance turn.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: The art that I was making was paintings. They were all, you know, single images, very narrative based. Rather than telling the beginning or the middle or the end of the story, each one of these paintings was one of those three, and then I have this one idea that rather than have it as a series of paintings, I recognized, this is going to be better as a book, and maybe a picture book and that became "Hard To Catch A Star."\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: From "How To Catch A Star" came 20 other picture books, including "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestseller, "Here We Are." It was his first written after the birth of his first child, Harland.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: until I had kids, the books were really just me kind of entertaining myself, just telling good stories that I would have wanted to hear as a child and thankfully, it just so happened that my sense of humor resonates with a lot of five-year-olds walking around the face of Earth.\par \par \uc2 But when we had a kid, this filter shift happened where it`s like, "Wait a minute, I have to explain everything to you."\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: His new book, "Meanwhile, Back On Earth" was inspired by Jeffers` own experience growing up in Northern Ireland. The story is told through the lens of a family road trip through space. It was a way for Jeffers to explain topics like climate change and global conflict.\par \par \uc2 The idea was born out of his art installation in the United Kingdom, "Our Place In Space." It`s an over five-mile long scale model of the solar system.\par \par \uc2 It feels like, "Meanwhile, Back On Earth" is fun for kids looking at space, but really a history lesson for adults.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: Yes. It`s a history lesson for adults, but it`s also a comment on the environment in which we live.\par \par \uc2 You can throw a dart back through time in history, and wherever it lands, pretty much you`re going to find some all-consuming battle that took place and humans fighting each other over things that with the distance of time and space ultimately haven`t really mattered that much.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: I think most people wouldn`t expect that a children`s book would address the climate crisis or global conflict.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: I would personally say to that, "Why not?" We make a mistake as a society if we just try to protect and idolize our children, because we`re not preparing them for the world at large.\par \par \uc2 A lot of times parents are not willing to have conversations that they themselves are not comfortable with, and I think if it is done in a healthy enough way, children a lot more intuitive, adept, and observant and they`re often given credit for.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: What did your son Harland think when you first read the book to him?\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: He just keeps looking to see if it`s him in the book.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Jeffers work goes beyond the page and include some unexpected collaborations..\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ordinary Love.")\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: Yes, I`ve worked with U2 a fair bit. Bono and I met in a bar and we kind of liked each other and --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: That`s a sentence everyone dreams of saying, "Bono, I met in a bar and we kind of liked each other."\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: Yes, yes, we got along and we got talking and he invited me to be a part of a workshop for his charity, ONE, and at that point, they were thinking about making a music video for "Ordinary Love."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ordinary Love.")\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: And so he got me to do that, too, and then that led on to other projects. I did all the art for the last tour on the big screen. I became like a collaborator with him rather than a gun for hire, which always felt like a nice, a very healthy relationship.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: But his magical mind seems to always find its way back to picture books.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: When you get in the flow, it quite one of the most joyous moments. I`ll crack myself up sometimes, just on my own. Then you forget that it goes out and has a life on its own and it`s not up to me what people think about it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And that is in millions of children`s bedrooms.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER JEFFERS: That`s, that`s a wild thought. I`m not going to think about that. I am just going to keep making the next one.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Jeffers` current book, "Meanwhile, Back On Earth" is on sale now. "Time" Magazine describes it as a dose of reality, a dash of whimsy.\par \par \uc2 And we have another very important two-year-old critic, my son, Beau, loves the book -- is it ready for view? Yes, we`ve read it quite a few times. I think this is one of the first times we read it, but he said, "I`m going to space. I`m going to space."\par \par \uc2 Yes, it is a great book --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Igniting the mind of a child.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s sweet. That`s sweet.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Yes. Tony, I think your kids are fans, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, they are. I was going to say shout out the Books Are Magic where you shot that. It is a fantastic bookstore in Brooklyn, which has a lot of fantastic bookstores, but that`s a particularly special one.\par \par \uc2 My kiddos, all four of them, have been read Oliver Jeffers, and I`ve got to say, the winner is not the big ideas one, but actually "Stuck," which is a story about a boy whose kite gets stuck in a tree and then the boy throws up everything in the neighborhood up to an including like cars, trucks, a whale, and everything gets stuck in the tree.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: A whale?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then he falls asleep and leaves it all there. It`s a great story.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: He`s such a talented guy and his mind was just spinning. I said, does your mind ever stop? He said, "No. My wife says we have to go on three-week vacations because it takes two weeks for my mind to just pause."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It doesn`t stop.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: He is really talented.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Great guy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nikki, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nice piece.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up next, a look at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up this weekend on "CBS Sunday Morning," Jane Pauley sits down with Julia Roberts. The Academy Award winner talks about her two loves -- acting and family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that. That`ll do it for us.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a look back now at all that mattered this week. Have a great weekend. We`ll see you Monday.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: See you tomorrow on "CBS Saturday Morning."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The devastation is unreal. You know, it`s just it`s kind of sad to see.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The terrible aftermath of Hurricane Ian as the crews sift through miles and miles of devastation. There are awful discoveries.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More than a hundred people were killed by the that storm.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Debris everywhere. The power is out.\par \par \uc2 These are families that were struggling to make ends meet before the storm happened.\par \par \uc2 MIRIAM ORTIZ (Gladiolus Food Pantry founder): And now with a storm, they don`t have nothing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Brittney Griner`s wife tells us she is terrified since Brittney Griner was sentenced to nine years in a Russian prison.\par \par \uc2 You`ve had two phone conversations.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER (Brittney Griner`s wife): I am sitting on a phone just literally in tears, just crying. I`m like "God."\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: When an older kid told them they couldn`t be twins. She said, "We`re twins because we share the same soul."\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Why didn`t let my dad have ice cream? Obviously, he doesn`t need it, but I`m just wondering.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "The Love Boat."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Love Boat."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "The Love Boat."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Soon you will be making another run."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real Love Boat.")\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROMIJN ("The Real Love Boat" host): Jerry surprises me with surprise dates.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Surprise dates.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROMIJN: I love a surprise date.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You do surprise dates.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROMIJN: They are very thought -- he puts so much thought.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what do you do, Rebecca?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROMIJN: I just enjoy the surprise.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great energy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I have been super, super patient here, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROMIJN: No, we have a good time.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bonfire at Tina`s.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have a special surprise for you.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY MCBRYDE (Singer/songwriter): Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe somebody that you know. Hey, it`s Garth Brooks.\par \par \uc2 GARTH BROOKS (Country singer): I am going to try not to cry. We would love for you to consider becoming the newest member of the \uc2 Grand Ole Opry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I am not ready.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: I feel like AARP ads follow me now everywhere.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Three --\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: In the mail, on TV.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ (Lifestyle expert): The deal on this? Okay --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk to me.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ (Lifestyle expert): It is so good, $100.00 off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: Making it $79.99.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what I am talking about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: I think you can do it better than I can.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One hundred.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: One hundred dollars off. There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Enzo dance right there.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Labelle`s performance.)\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE: A lot of people when they meet me they say, "Are you still working? Are you still touring?" I say, "\uc2 Google\uc2  me, Boo."\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: If you sing something, I`ll try to mimic it. Can we do that real quick?\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE: I`ve got you.\par \par \uc2 (PATTI LABELLE AND JERICKA DUNCAN vocalizing.)\par \par \uc2 PATTI LABELLE: Girl, you better shut up. You can sing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You better tune in because if you don`t that`s just crazy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I love "Ghost." For me, it is the show Casper, then Patrick Swayze.\par \par \uc2 UTKARSH AMBUDKAR ("Ghosts" Actor): Hey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What have you got? What have you got?\par \par \uc2 UTKARSH AMBUDKAR: Well, you know what --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 UTKARSH AMBUDKAR: I would just say?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 UTKARSH AMBUDKAR: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On TV.\par \par \uc2 UTKARSH AMBUDKAR: Okay. Thursdays, 8:30 after "Young Sheldon." What you`re smelling.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are not worthy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are not worthy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He has done it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If Aaron Judge wanted, it I would just give it to him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s not how it works, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. But I would. I would just give it to him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Gayle, you would not have caught that ball.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221007eia7000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  On Defense Days After Denying That He Paid For An Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 564 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, there is a surprising new development in the closely watched Georgia Senate race. Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has repeatedly denied knowing the woman who accused him of paying for her abortion in 2009. Well, now that woman claims she is the mother of one of his children.\par \par \uc2 Here \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-Georgia Senatorial Candidate): Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): In his first public campaign event since back- to-back bombshell reports by The Daily Beast, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  played defense.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : This here, the abortion thing, is false. It`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Heisman Trophy winner and staunchly pro-life candidate doubled down after the outlet revealed a woman who alleged Walker paid for her abortion in 2009 also had a child with him.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : If I knew about it, I would be honest and talk about it. But I know nothing about that.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: According to the latest report, the woman said she came forward after Walker`s repeated denials.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Have you figured out who it is?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Not at all.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: She told The Daily Beast: "I have been very civil thus far. I keep my mouth shut. But I`m also not going to get run over time and time again," adding: "He didn`t accept responsibility for the kid we did have together, and now he isn`t accepting responsibility for the one that we didn`t have."\par \par \uc2 The woman previously provided The Daily Beast with a receipt from an abortion clinic, a "Get well" card and a copy of a $700 check signed by Walker.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER (Son of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ): Everything has been a lie.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: After his son called him out on social media, Walker told reporters he`s not concerned.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : But I will always love him, no matter what my son says.\par \par \uc2 I`m \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , saved my grace.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Senate hopeful has raised at least a half-a-million dollars since the scandal broke, with some voters unswayed.\par \par \uc2 LINDA LARISCY (Republican Voter): I believe Herschel has made mistakes, but I truly believe that he`s changed as a person.\par \par \uc2 RACE LARISCY (Republican Voter): And if he`s proven to be wrong, and he lied about it, then I will be greatly disappointed. I don`t know that it will be disappointing enough to sway me to vote in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: National GOP leaders have also rallied behind Walker, who remains locked in a tight battle with Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock.\par \par \uc2 Warnock continued to steer clear of the controversy when asked about it today and said the election is about who is ready to represent Georgia in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A lot of layers to this story.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221007eia6000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Republicans Stand Behind Walker\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Robert Costa, Gayle King \par \uc2 457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Republicans, including former President Trump are throwing their full support behind Georgia Senate candidate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  despite a report in "The Daily Beast" claiming he paid for girlfriend`s abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Walker denies all of that and he prayed yesterday with a supportive crowd at a surprise campaign event, as he remains locked in a very close race with Democratic Senator, Raphael Warnock.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is following this in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning. What`s the reaction been in Georgia to all of this?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, Republicans are standing with Walker. That shows how important this race is to winning the Senate majority here in Georgia. It is mostly the same conservative activist, Erick Erickson told us. The next 48 hours will be critical for Walker`s campaign in terms of how he answers tough questions. Democrats expect the race against incumbent Democratic senator Raphael Warnock to tighten.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bob, Walker`s son Christian, who is known as a conservative influencer, posted this video on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  after his dad denied the abortion allegations. I want you to take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WARNOCK: He has four kids, four different women, wasn`t in the house raising one of them. He was out having sex with other women. Do you care about family values? I`ve recited lie after lie after lie. The abortion part dropped yesterday. It`s literally his handwriting in the card. They say they have receipts, whatever. He gets on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he lies about it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: These are strong words by his own son, Bob. Has the campaign responded to Christian`s remarks.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has said he loves his son, a 23-year-old who at times has been a controversial social media figure, but this entire episode and these clips really shows how in politics today, family dynamics can erupt at times on social media and it has become not just a political reckoning for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , but a personal one because these videos have generated over a million views online -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Eyes will be on this race for more reasons than one.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can certainly feel the son`s passion in that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221007eia50005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Bombshell Allegation Rocks Georgia`s Senate Race\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Robert Costa \par \uc2 607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, with just over a month before the midterm elections, a new accusation has rocked the pivotal Senate race in Georgia. Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is denying a report that he paid for a girlfriend`s abortion in 2009. Walker supports banning all abortions, without exceptions in cases of rape, incest or to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Robert Costa has more from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @ChristianWalk1r)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER: He gets on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he lies about it. OK, I`m done. Done! Everything has been a lie.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Christian Walker, the son of Georgia Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , blasted his father after The Daily Beast reported the Senate candidate, who opposes abortion rights, allegedly paid for a girlfriend`s abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 The unidentified woman supported her claim with a $575 receipt from an abortion clinic and a signed $700 personal check from Walker to cover expenses.\par \par \uc2 She told The Daily Beast she came forward because of Walker`s stance on abortion, saying: "I just can`t with the hypocrisy anymore. We all deserve better."\par \par \uc2 Walker, who prayed with supporters this morning at an Atlanta event, has denied the allegation.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Fox News)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-Georgia Senatorial Candidate): I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never paid for an abortion. And it`s a lie. I send money to a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Walker`s son, a conservative TikTok influencer, posted several times since the story broke, denouncing his father`s campaign.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @ChristianWalk1r)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER: He has four kids, four different women, wasn`t in the house raising one of them. He was out having sex with other women.\par \par \uc2 Do you care about family values?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Conservative analysts say the coming days could be critical for the embattled Republican, who trails incumbent Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock by just two points.\par \par \uc2 ERICK ERICKSON (Conservative Radio Host): I think the campaign is definitely in turmoil. They were caught off guard. What they do in the next 48 hours will make or break it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But voters we spoke to today said the bombshell allegation might not have much of an impact.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: He has told untruths enough that, what is one more untruth?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Do you believe Georgia Republicans will stand with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  regardless of these allegations?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I think so.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Republican leaders stood by Walker today, underscoring how important this race is to winning the Senate majority. But it all comes as several Senate Republican candidates are facing headwinds in key battleground states -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa in Atlanta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And there`s breaking news tonight. Lawyers for former President Trump today asked the Supreme Court to intervene in their legal battle over top secret documents seized from Trump`s Mar-a-Lago resort. Team Trump wants to block the Justice Department from holding onto and using classified documents as part of a criminal investigation during an independent review.\par \par \uc2 This follows a recent appeals court ruling saying the documents belong to the government, not the former president.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221006eia4000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Abortion Revelation\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Nikole Killion, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,063 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning to the midterms, where there is a new twist in the pivotal Senate race in Georgia. The website, "The Daily Beast" is reporting Republican candidate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid for a woman he was dating to get an abortion in 2009. He strongly denies it.\par \par \uc2 In his campaign for Senate, the former football star says he is staunchly antiabortion. This comes as polls show Walker in a close race against Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock with 13 percent undecided.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is following all of this.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker`s son posted a scathing reaction after this story broke. Meantime, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  says he plans to sue the media outlet that wrote the story and called it a defamatory lie.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) (Georgia Senate candidate): Right now, I`m for life and I`m no not going to make an excuse for it.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Georgia Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  says he is pro-life and supports a nationwide abortion ban, with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I believe in life. There is no excuses about that.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Does that include rape, incest?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I believe in life. You know, I don`t think I want to go into whether it includes, because who knows.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But new reporting from "The Daily Beast," which has not been independently confirmed by CBS News claims he encouraged a woman he was dating in 2009 to get an abortion and helped pay for it.\par \par \uc2 The woman whose identity was not published provided "The Daily Beast" with a receipt for the procedure, a get well card, and an image of a $700.00 check allegedly signed by Walker.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : It is a flat out lie.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Walker denied the allegations on FOX News Monday night.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : But I can tell you right now, I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never paid for an abortion. They think they can threaten mem, they think they can scare me. Right now, all of that has does is energize me more that I`m going to fight and win a seat for the great people of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Trump-backed nominee and former football star is taking fire from his own family. Walker`s son, Christian, a right-wing influencer posted, "I know my mom and I would really appreciate if my father, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  stopped lying and making a mockery of us."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Walker`s opponent, Senator Raphael Warnock hasn`t weighed in on this controversy yet, but in a sign of how competitive this race is, new fundraising numbers show that he raised more than $26 million in the last three months. Walker`s camp has yet to report its latest numbers -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, the big legal challenge about race and voting rights that the Supreme Court will have to settle.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a major settlement that forces Kim Kardashian to pay more than a million dollars. We`ll show you what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:16. To check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Still ahead, a CBS News investigation reveals a crisis in the military for thousands of parents who need care for their young children.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TOBIN (Married to Special Forces Commander): When we moved here in May of last year, she was on 17 waitlists when we moved into our house.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Seventeen waitlists?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TOBIN: Seventeen waitlists. I received an e-mail saying we have an alternative care option for you and it is an hour and 10 minutes from my house.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wow, how one Special Forces Commander says difficulties finding childcare are taking a toll on his soldiers` readiness.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Britney Griner`s appeal of her nine-year prison sentence in Russia is scheduled for October 25th. The \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star and an Olympic gold medalist was convicted of smuggling cannabis oil into the country earlier this year. She had been carrying less than one gram of the oil.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has called her sentence unacceptable and told her family his administration is committed to bringing her home. The administration has offered a prisoner swap for Griner and Paul Whelan, another American who is in Russian custody. Moscow has not responded to the offer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Less than one gram is a preposterously small something. I mean, that`s a fraction of an ounce, which is a fraction of a pound.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, we`re talking teeny, teeny, tiny amounts and her whole life is on the line here. She could go to a labor camp.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, I think that the administration has made it clear that they are doing everything that they can and I think the more pressure, the more media attention it gets, the more we won`t forget.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, you`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 And coming up on Thursday on CBS MORNINGS, Gayle will share an intimate conversation with Britney Griner`s wife, Cherelle. It is our first interview since the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star was given that prison sentence in Russia.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Ahead and first on CBS MORNINGS, we will talk with the costar of the beloved 90`s show, "Sister, Sister" Tamera Mowry-Housley. She will tell us about her new memoir and some of the important life lessons she learned along the way.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS podcasts on the go. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221006eia4000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Island Cut Off by Hurricane Ian; Desperation in Florida; President Biden Visit Puerto Rico; North Korea Launches Missile over Japan; On the Frontlines in Ukraine; Women Take to Streets in Iran; Opening Arguments in Oath Keepers Trial; \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Abortion Revelation; Military Daycare Crisis; Britney Griner Appeal; SCOTUS Takes on Voting Rights Case; Natalie Morales Joins CBS News; Our Missing Hearts; Call Him Overjoyed; Tamera Mowry-Housley Live; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Jericka Duncan, Manuel Bojorquez,  Elizabeth Palmer, Charlie D`Agata, Roxana Saberi, Nikole Killion, Catherine  Herridge, Jan Crawford, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 14,006 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday, October 4th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I`m Jericka Duncan in for Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is on assignment, but we`re in good hands and here is today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The search for survivors continues in Florida where hundreds of thousands are still without power, and the death toll keeps rising.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The devastation is unreal. You know, it`s just -- it`s kind of sad to see. There are people that lost their homes and entirely lost their lives.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The President visited Puerto Rico, where he pledged his administration will provide $60 million to help the island recover.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new report details systemic abuse in women`s soccer, including youth leagues in children as young as 10.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Verbal, sexual, emotional abuse.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Child care crisis in the US military, what a CBS News investigation uncovered.\par \par \uc2 COL. KEVIN TRUJILLO (US Army`s Seventh Special Forces Group Commander): The number one issue when I talk to service members and their family is childcare.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Kim Kardashian fined over a million dollars after she failed to disclose she was paid to promote a cryptocurrency company.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Albert Pujols has done it again, slammed his 703rd homer.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He is past Babe Ruth. Second all-time in history and runs batted in.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Monday Night Football and an overzealous fan hungry for some airtime gets clubbed by the Rams`, Bobby Wagner.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes. Oh yes. That`s what we`re talking about. Wagner, veteran, right, get him down.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): At a fishing tournament in Cleveland on Friday, a duo that had been declared winners were caught cheating.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He needs to be arrested. He needs to be prosecuted. Both counts. [Bleep].\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): Call the cops. Call them. Tell them. Tell them the fish. Call the cops right now.\par \par \uc2 I haven`t seen White dudes this man about fish since the \uc2 Disney\uc2  announced "The Little Mermaid" thing.\par \par \uc2 She has gone Black. Call the cops right now.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. Trevor has always got a way of putting it in context.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is incredible video though, Jericka. IF you didn`t see it yesterday, you`ve got to check it out.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes. Those people in Cleveland, they don`t play.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They don`t play.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They don`t mess around. We`ve got a great show for you today.\par \par \uc2 A lot of news to share in fact. Some good news off the top for us.\par \par \uc2 Award-winning journalist, Natalie Morales is joining us here in the CBS News family. \uc2 CBS\uc2  viewers already know her from "The Talk" and her two- decade career at another network that shall not be named, but we`re going to find out what she is looking forward to most in this next chapter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, first on CBS MORNINGS, Tamera Mowry-Housley has been getting it done ever since, "Sister, Sister" -- now, she has a new book out and it`s a great title. You should sit down for this, a memoir about life, wine and, cookie dough. We all love that. She sits down with us right here in studio. It`s going to be a good show.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s going to be wine and cookies, and she is back on streaming. I don`t know. We will see. If she hears this, I hope she does.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Will she bring wine and cookies?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we do need to get to the news off the top. There is a grim situation unfolding in Florida nearly a week now after Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has confirmed more than a hundred people were killed by that storm and as things stand currently, more than 400,000 people in Florida are still without power. And there is one community in particular, Sanibel Island, totally cut off from the Mainland because the storm destroyed part of a causeway, the only road in or out.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez traveled to that once thriving tourist destination, one of the prettiest spots in the world, in fact. It is now in ruins and we find Manny in Fort Myers Beach, another hard-hit area. Manny, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 More than half of the total deaths from Hurricane Ian occurred right here in Lee County and you can see this devastation around us. Also, hard hit as you mentioned Sanibel Island, which is virtually uninhabitable. We were able to reach it by boat yesterday to see what`s left.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: You can see they`re having to use barges to bring in heavy equipment and trucks. That is because the causeway here, with entire chunks of it that are missing, disconnecting this island from the Mainland.\par \par \uc2 In the aftermath of Hurricane Ian, Sanibel Island has not just been damaged, it has been left isolated, too. From above, the extent of the destruction is staggering. Trees flattened, homes shredded, roads ripped apart by sand and mud.\par \par \uc2 But from the ground, a clearer picture emerges of just how life changing Ian has been and will be.\par \par \uc2 The scenes here on Sanibel Island are almost apocalyptic. You don`t see a soul on the streets. There is not barely a sound here except for a few birds and some of the helicopters that are still doing rescues and bringing supplies in and out.\par \par \uc2 More than 2,000 residents have been rescued and evacuated in Florida since Ian made landfall including, Lacy McClary, a local artist who was airlifted to safety from Sanibel Island.\par \par \uc2 LACY MCCLARY (Artist): Yes, I can`t believe that they are still here.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: We traveled with her across the bay over the weekend at what used to be her studio on San Carlos Island.\par \par \uc2 McClary recorded the storm as it tore through her friend`s house where they rode it out.\par \par \uc2 When we were on Sanibel Island, she asked us to check on her house.\par \par \uc2 So, unless things have moved around, I think this is her house based on all the paintings. It definitely looks like it is destroyed.\par \par \uc2 As local officials sweep Sanibel Island to find survivors, some evacuees are returning to see the damage firsthand. Like the Jawkawka`s (sp?) brothers who took a boat here.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The devastation is unreal. You know, it`s just -- it is kind of sad to see.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s horrible. I mean, you walk down the streets, you can`t even recognize what streets they are.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is a tragedy and that`s all you can pretty much say.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Florida officials have defended the timing of evacuation orders here in Lee County, which went into effect less than 24 hours before Ian turned ashore. They`ve said that they issued those. They went into effect as soon as the storm`s track put the county in the bullseye.\par \par \uc2 But take a look. Forecast from the days leading up to the landfall show that at least parts of the county were always within Ian`s projected cone, even when a direct hit looked unlikely.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is expected to visit the region tomorrow -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Manny, as a former Floridian, I will say even when people have those warnings, a lot of folks just don`t want to get out. They trust riding it out in their home and you can see the damage there behind you.\par \par \uc2 Manny, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Manny mentioned President Biden. He visited Puerto Rico yesterday to survey the storm damage from another deadly storm, Hurricane Fiona, and when he was there, he promised $60 million to help the island prepare for future extreme weather events and told Puerto Ricans he will make sure they get every dollar promised.\par \par \uc2 At least 25 people were killed when Hurricane Fiona hit Puerto Rico as a Category 1 Storm that was two weeks ago and FEMA says more than 90 percent of the island has power once again, but that still means more than 100,000 customers are in the dark.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We can`t forget about Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 Well this morning, North Korea has sharply escalated its confrontation with the US and its allies with an aggressive new missile test. The country fired a suspected intermediate range missile over Japan for the first time in five years.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Coastal communities in Northern Japan woke up to the sound of sirens and warnings to prepare to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 North Korea fired the missile from near its border with China right over Japan. It took 22 minutes to fly 2,800 miles, a record distance for a North Korean missile and crash into the sea.\par \par \uc2 (FUMIO KISHIDA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Japan`s Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  condemned the launch. It was he said, "a reckless act."\par \par \uc2 The news was big and ominous enough for a Special Edition of the "Yomiuri" newspaper in Tokyo spelling out the details to startled commuters.\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe the missile was likely the huge Hwasong-12 on view in a military parade in January. Today`s is the latest and most hostile of what has been a bumper year of 23 separate launches and the first since 2017 to be aimed over Japan. That was at the last low point in North Korean relations just before President Trump`s attempt to negotiate with the leader Kim Jong-un.\par \par \uc2 So far, this time, Kim hasn`t said a thing, but his message is clear.\par \par \uc2 He is both angered and threatened by America`s growing military alliances with two of his neighbors in this region, South Korea and Japan.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Elizabeth Palmer in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Russia`s military is stepping up its so-called partial mobilization for its war on Ukraine. The DEFENSE MINISTER says half a million reservists will be recalled. Ukraine`s Army is pushing farther into Russian-controlled areas and recapturing more territory.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata reports from one area where Ukrainian troops are on the march and the Russian Army is falling back.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: This morning, we crossed over into the Donbas region in Eastern Ukraine, as far as President Putin is concerned, we are now walking on Russian soil, but you can see the Ukrainian flag flying behind me.\par \par \uc2 There is a heavy Ukrainian military presence here. On the way here, a couple of Ukrainian fighter jets flew overhead. Now, this town is full of residents. It is a fully functioning town. It`s on the way to Lyman. That is the strategic town in the east, that the Ukrainian forces liberated a few days ago. We understand they are making even more advances in that region.\par \par \uc2 Russian troops on the retreat, not just there, but south of here in Kherson, Ukrainian forces are pushing back Russia there, too. So Russians have been losing territory ever since President Putin`s announcement.\par \par \uc2 What makes it concerning is when President Putin annexed these areas, he said that he would use everything at his disposal to defend what he called now, Russian territory. He hinted at the use of nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 Now today, it`s the turn for the Russian Parliament, the Duma to ratify that annexation, of course, it is expected to sail through.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Charlie D`Agata, Pokrovsk, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Now, to Iran where the government`s violent crackdown on peaceful protests is now in its 18th day. One human rights group says at least 133 people have been killed so far. Hundreds have been arrested, mostly young people since the death of a 22-year-old woman in the custody of the country`s Morality Police.\par \par \uc2 As Roxana Saberi shows us, it is Iranian women who are front and center of the protests.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN chanting in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Unprecedented scenes in the Islamic Republic of Iran, schoolgirls across the country flouting the law, some uncovering their hair, many even chanting "Death to the Dictator."\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN chanting in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Calls for freedom are also spreading across universities.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: "Everyone is out on the streets." This college students said in a video message on condition of anonymity. "We have to keep going. They can`t arrest all of us."\par \par \uc2 Young people under the age of 25 appear to be fueling the protests despite the regime`s harsh crackdown.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, Security Forces besieged Tehran`s elite Sharif University where students have been protesting peacefully. The student union said, armed plainclothes agents chased students down in a parking lot and brutally arrested several of them. Eyewitnesses said the ground was covered in blood.\par \par \uc2 MAZIAR BAHARI (Iran Wire): They think that by using force -- brutal force, they can be in power forever, but of course they`re wrong.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Maziar Bahari is the London-based editor of Iran Wire News and a former political prisoner in Iran.\par \par \uc2 So how is this young generation different from previous young generations in Iran?\par \par \uc2 MAZIAR BAHARI: My generation and the generation after me, we gave the government the chance to reform itself.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP chanting in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MAZIAR BAHARI: But this generation can see that this government, the Islamic Republic cannot be reformed, so this government has to be ended.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The Islamic Republic has been around for 43 years, and many young Iranians are fed up with a global isolation and severe sanctions imposed on their country.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: "Our problem is not with Islam or religion. We just want people to live as they like in Iran and not be jailed for it."\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: "Please hear our voices," she adds. "Help us in any way you can."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Tony, as you know, I am half Iranian and I lived and reported to Iran for several years. These days, many Iranians are reaching out saying it is very important for the international news media to keep the protests and the regime`s response in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we`ll keep following it and I know you will as well, Roxana Saberi in London for us. Roxana, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in this country and to Washington where the January 6 trial is underway for high profile members of the extremist group, the Oath Keepers.\par \par \uc2 Founder, Stewart Rhodes and four other members are accused of seditious conspiracy, a serious charge reserved for people who plot to take on the \uc2 US government\uc2  by use of force.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors said in their opening statement, "That the group wanted to stop by whatever means necessary, the lawful transfer of presidential power." Planning a "armed rebellion" in the months before the attack.\par \par \uc2 A defense lawyer responded that, "Rhodes had no violent intent that day and did nothing illegal."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning to the midterms, where there is a new twist in the pivotal Senate race in Georgia. The website, "The Daily Beast" is reporting Republican candidate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid for a woman he was dating to get an abortion in 2009. He strongly denies it.\par \par \uc2 In his campaign for Senate, the former football star says he is staunchly antiabortion. This comes as polls show Walker in a close race against Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock with 13 percent undecided.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is following all of this.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker`s son posted a scathing reaction after this story broke. Meantime, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  says he plans to sue the media outlet that wrote the story and called it a defamatory lie.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) (Georgia Senate candidate): Right now, I`m for life and I`m no not going to make an excuse for it.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Georgia Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  says he is pro-life and supports a nationwide abortion ban, with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I believe in life. There is no excuses about that.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Does that include rape, incest?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I believe in life. You know, I don`t think I want to go into whether it includes, because who knows.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But new reporting from "The Daily Beast," which has not been independently confirmed by CBS News claims he encouraged a woman he was dating in 2009 to get an abortion and helped pay for it.\par \par \uc2 The woman whose identity was not published provided "The Daily Beast" with a receipt for the procedure, a get well card, and an image of a $700.00 check allegedly signed by Walker.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : It is a flat out lie.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Walker denied the allegations on FOX News Monday night.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : But I can tell you right now, I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never paid for an abortion. They think they can threaten mem, they think they can scare me. Right now, all of that has does is energize me more that I`m going to fight and win a seat for the great people of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Trump-backed nominee and former football star is taking fire from his own family. Walker`s son, Christian, a right-wing influencer posted, "I know my mom and I would really appreciate if my father, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  stopped lying and making a mockery of us."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Walker`s opponent, Senator Raphael Warnock hasn`t weighed in on this controversy yet, but in a sign of how competitive this race is, new fundraising numbers show that he raised more than $26 million in the last three months. Walker`s camp has yet to report its latest numbers -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, the big legal challenge about race and voting rights that the Supreme Court will have to settle.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a major settlement that forces Kim Kardashian to pay more than a million dollars. We`ll show you what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:16. To check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Still ahead, a CBS News investigation reveals a crisis in the military for thousands of parents who need care for their young children.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TOBIN (Married to Special Forces Commander): When we moved here in May of last year, she was on 17 waitlists when we moved into our house.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Seventeen waitlists?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TOBIN: Seventeen waitlists. I received an e-mail saying we have an alternative care option for you and it is an hour and 10 minutes from my house.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wow, how one Special Forces Commander says difficulties finding childcare are taking a toll on his soldiers` readiness.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Britney Griner`s appeal of her nine-year prison sentence in Russia is scheduled for October 25th. The \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star and an Olympic gold medalist was convicted of smuggling cannabis oil into the country earlier this year. She had been carrying less than one gram of the oil.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has called her sentence unacceptable and told her family his administration is committed to bringing her home. The administration has offered a prisoner swap for Griner and Paul Whelan, another American who is in Russian custody. Moscow has not responded to the offer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Less than one gram is a preposterously small something. I mean, that`s a fraction of an ounce, which is a fraction of a pound.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, we`re talking teeny, teeny, tiny amounts and her whole life is on the line here. She could go to a labor camp.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, I think that the administration has made it clear that they are doing everything that they can and I think the more pressure, the more media attention it gets, the more we won`t forget.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, you`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 And coming up on Thursday on CBS MORNINGS, Gayle will share an intimate conversation with Britney Griner`s wife, Cherelle. It is our first interview since the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star was given that prison sentence in Russia.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Ahead and first on CBS MORNINGS, we will talk with the costar of the beloved 90`s show, "Sister, Sister" Tamera Mowry-Housley. She will tell us about her new memoir and some of the important life lessons she learned along the way.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS podcasts on the go. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are back now at 7:30 here on CBS MORNINGS with a CBS News investigation into a childcare crisis in the Armed Services, a crisis that could affect the military`s readiness to fight.\par \par \uc2 According to our reporting 11,000 children whose parents are in uniform as we speak or work in the Department of Defense, are in need of childcare spots urgently, right now. In some cases, their parents actually had been forced to quit the service entirely. And among those affected are members of the Army`s elite Green Berets, including those who live and train in the State of Florida.\par \par \uc2 For them, their designated childcare site can take hours of commuting each way every single day.\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent Catherine Herridge joins us now from the Pentagon with more on this.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, it is a common problem for parents. It`s a big issue when you`re talking about our Armed Forces. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been investigating the childcare issue since June and found the Army`s Seventh Special Forces Group takes on some of the most challenging missions overseas, but for more than a decade, these families say they have faced a major challenge close to home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 COL. KEVIN TRUJILLO: The number one issue when I talk to service members and their family is child care.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Colonel Kevin Trujillo leads the Army`s Seventh Special Forces Group, the elite Green Berets take on missions across the globe.\par \par \uc2 But at home in Florida, 60 percent of the Colonel`s soldiers live in Crestview, where real estate and rents are cheaper. They train at Camp Bull Simon`s, 20 miles to the south.\par \par \uc2 Unlike most Army bases, which have child care on site, the Seventh Group`s designated Child Development Center is another 20 miles away within the sprawling Eglin Air Force Base.\par \par \uc2 COL. KEVIN TRUJILLO: It`s just not a viable option to drive over an hour a day for child care and then to repeat that process going home.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Chelsea, who asked us not to use her last name for security reasons showed us around Crestview.\par \par \uc2 Both in Special Forces at the time, Chelsea and her husband traveled up to three hours of commuting each day for childcare.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA (Former squad leader): Wasted time, minutes in the day that you could be spent actually living life.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: With heavy traffic on Route 85 and bottlenecks at the base gates, Chelsea was always running late.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA: You feel like, am I failing my guys? At that point I was a squad leader? Am I causing them hardship while trying to take care of my family as well? Because they`re both your family. They`re just different families.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TOBIN (Married to Special Forces Commander): Let`s go play.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Molly Tobin faced other childcare challenges with her daughter Olivia, as a civilian married to a Special Forces Commander.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TOBIN When we moved here in May of last year, she was on 17 waitlists when we moved into our house.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Seventeen waitlists.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TOBIN: Seventeen waitlists. I received an e-mail saying we have an alternative care option for you and it is an hour and 10 minutes from my house.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Now as a family advocate, Tobin shares the community`s concerns with Base Commanders.\par \par \uc2 How many Chelsea`s are out there?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TOBIN: There`s countless Chelsea`s out there.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: According to Defense Department data, more than 11,000 children are waiting for military provided childcare. The Seventh Special Forces Group told CBS News it has approximately 400.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. TRACY BEEGEN (Army`s Seventh Special Forces Clinical Psychologist): We see that children that don`t have access to quality childcare and educational opportunities. They have a lifetime of difficulties to include depression, anxiety learning disabilities, attachment issues.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Captain Tracy Beegen is the unit`s clinical psychologist.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. TRACY BEEGEN: I`ve seen this probably 20 to 30 percent of my patients have had spouses that have gone back home specifically because of childcare issues, and difficulty with finances related to not being able to work and have that second income.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The Air Force provides for the Seventh Groups to childcare because Camp Bull Simon`s is within Eglin`s boundaries.\par \par \uc2 According to the Air Force, it has not built a facility on the Army base for safety reasons, because it falls within a testing range.\par \par \uc2 But internal military records reviewed by CBS News suggest the Air Force has already made changes to remove all risk from the area.\par \par \uc2 How can it be a safety issue when you`ve got you know, a shop at a gas station? You`ve got a subway right here, and a church?\par \par \uc2 COL. KEVIN TRUJILLO: Those are the tough questions that family members ask me.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: What is the impact on the mission when you`ve got someone downrange and they are getting these concerning phone calls from family members about something as basic as childcare?\par \par \uc2 COL. KEVIN TRUJILLO: Yes, so their mind is not focused on the mission and they are not operating at an optimal level if they`re worried about concerns whether it`s family or finances.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA: Good job.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: In 2015, Chelsea hit her breaking point and left Special Forces to care for their two sons.\par \par \uc2 Was it hard to step away from your military career?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA: Very. I miss it a lot.\par \par \uc2 The group motto is the family business. They have La Familia on everything. And yet, the family is being left behind. There is a disconnect in what we are putting out and portraying and what`s happening in as far as childcare is concerned.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Late last night in a statement to CBS News, the Air Force and Army Secretaries confirmed they have been working together over the summer to identify short-term options and a long-term fix. They have agreed on a final location for a new Child Development Center.\par \par \uc2 The families told us, it should be on their base like the other services. Specifics on location and how long it will take are expected later this month -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A lot of families left behind and a lot of women forced to make sacrifices.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot. And the military can figure this out. They can build a bridge in six hours, an airstrip in 12. I mean --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They can get a child care center up there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They have the resource.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It sounds like they`re working on it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You would think, Catherine --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a really powerful piece.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Thank you so much, Catherine.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, why Kim Kardashian has to pay big bucks for what she didn`t tell her 330 million followers on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Supreme Court hears arguments this morning on a significant case focusing on race and voting rights in Alabama. The Court opened its new term yesterday with two Black Justices for the first time ever, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  joining \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court where the Justices are poised to make more historic rulings in the coming year.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 You know after that blockbuster term last year, they are back taking on more contentious cases this year on issues like race, gay rights discrimination, and they are jumping right in with a major case from Alabama later this morning on the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In 1965 during the struggle for voting rights, this was the epicenter, Selma, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Today, Selma is again at the center of a battle over voting.\par \par \uc2 SAM WALKER (Head of the National Voting Rights Museum): This one is called the Foot Soldiers Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Sam Walker is head of the National Voting Rights Museum at the foot of the Edmund Pettus Bridge.\par \par \uc2 SAM WALKER: This is the actual voting machine that Black people voted on when they voted back in 1966 after the signing of the Voting Rights Act in August of 1965.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But now, with Black voter registration at historic highs, Black leaders say Alabama is using redistricting to dilute the power of their vote and their ability to elect officials of their choice in violation of the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 SAM WALKER: Here in Alabama, we feel that, you know, the White community has never really represented the Black interest.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The state is 27 percent Black, but only one of Alabama`s seven congressional districts, which includes Selma is majority Black, now represented by Alabama`s only Democratic Representative, Congresswoman \uc2 Terri Sewell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EVAN MILLIGAN (Alabama Forward Executive Director): Having a congressional representation that is responsive to the needs of those communities is very important.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Evan Milligan who grew up in Alabama and now leads the group, Alabama Forward sued, saying the State should have two Black districts and that his organization has shown how it can be done.\par \par \uc2 EVAN MILLIGAN: If I know that I`m voting in a district where no matter how many times I come to vote for certain seats, my vote doesn`t matter because of how the district has been drawn, then there is little incentive for me to participate.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MERRILL\uc2  (Alabama Secretary of State): That is the district in question.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But Secretary of State, \uc2 John Merrill\uc2  says the only way to possibly create two Black districts is to focus solely on race, which Alabama argues shouldn`t even be a consideration.\par \par \uc2 It says the Voting Rights Act requires States draw districts neutrally.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MERRILL\uc2 : What the legislature attempted to do is to draw impartial districts that were unbiased, not according to race.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And he says creating two geographically compact districts would risk decreasing Black representation by taking some Black voters from the one safe district, which is now about 57 percent Black.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MERRILL\uc2 : The likelihood of there being seven White Members of Congress instead of six White and one African-American increases dramatically.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, Alabama lost in the Lower Court, but the Supreme Court put that decision on hold so Alabama can use its map for the elections next month. This case will affect future elections not only in Alabama, but in States across the country -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you will be talking about today. But first, it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER