\uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s how you do it.\par \par \uc2 As much as I like the Stephen Colbert show, that`s the name, right?\par \par \uc2 I want to get to some exciting news off the top. It is a very exciting news for us here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We are thrilled, thrilled, thrilled to welcome Natalie Morales to our CBS News family as a new national correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Now, you`re thinking, "I know that name." You`re going to see her covering stories across our network, including right here on CBS MORNINGS, and the reason she is so familiar is because she is an award-winning journalist with more than two decades of experience covering everything from natural disasters to inaugurations to the Royal family, to five, six, seven Olympics, I`ve lost track.\par \par \uc2 She has also recently been the cohost of "The Talk," and she is going to continue to do that right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and she joins us now from Los Angeles, California, her home for years now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Natalie. Welcome back to news. You couldn`t stay away, could you?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: You know, there`s just too much going on in the world, Tony, or as I`ve been told to call you, Katy Tur`s husband. The control put me up to that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I am proud to be.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Now, I am very excited to be joining you and the family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, being a journalist, Natalie, I did a little reporting about my new colleague, and I said, "Who is this is Natalie Morales?" I love her work, but what is she like to be like to be with?\par \par \uc2 Apparently, this is what I heard. This is true. She`s genuine. She does the work.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And this was a little bit intimidating for me. She`s not afraid to dance at the Christmas party.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ah.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I know where you got that from?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Oh, yes. You know it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You got that from the makeup artist.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we were chatting about you.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Makeup artists in there, you know, they like to talk.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What are you looking forward to most, Natalie?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Mom, the makeup artist. You know, I am looking forward to getting back to my roots. You know, of course, I love my job at "The Talk." We get to always talk about the important things going on in the world. But this is a chance for me to bring that perspective more, having been out in the field having covered stories. So, it`s an opportunity for me to get back to that.\par \par \uc2 I am also looking forward to getting back into true crime. I`ve already gotten started on a fascinating "48 hours." You know, true crime is really -- I feel like something that so many of us are so passionate about, about bringing justice to the families of victims.\par \par \uc2 So this one is 40 years in the making. You`ll see it next month in November, so I can`t wait to bring that to you.\par \par \uc2 But I`m really looking forward to just being part of this incredible family of yours. I mean, \uc2 CBS\uc2  has an incredible team. It is an incredible organization and I`m just so looking forward to being able to work with all of you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and you`re an incredible addition.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to continue to hosting "The Talk" and your interview with Camille Vasquez who represented Johnny Depp and in his trial between his ex-wife and him. Let`s take a look at that clip.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Did you ever hesitate as a woman taking on a case with such a high profile celebrity, especially in this post #MeToo movement?\par \par \uc2 CAMILLE VASQUEZ (Attorney): No. Because I know Johnny as person and I`ve worked with him for five years now, nearly, and I believed him. I never hesitated because I believe Johnny and I was an advocate for him and I wanted the opportunity to give him his life back. He deserved that.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Did Johnny ever give you advice?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With everything going on with that trial, I think we all thought collectively when we saw Camille Vasquez, "a star is born." What was your takeaway from that sit down?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes. That`s exactly it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, she became you know, famous pretty much overnight. She talks about you know, walking into a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , putting in her coffee order and the girl`s jaw just dropped like, "Ah, you`re Camille Vasquez, Johnny Depp`s attorney."\par \par \uc2 And, you know, if you look her up on TikTok, I believe she has over eight billion views if you look up the name, #CamilleVasquez. Camille Vasquez for President was trending. And you think about that, and I asked her that question about how did Johnny Depp tell you how to handle fame?\par \par \uc2 So, it was really interesting hearing her perspective on the trial and how she learned how to deal with the instant celebrity fame. But also, we got real personal. This was a piece that we did for Hispanic Heritage Month. It`ll air later today on "The Talk" and getting to her roots as she was raised -- she is Cuban and Colombian, but raised here in the Los Angeles area.\par \par \uc2 And she talks so much about her family, the love that she has for them, her traditions, her culture, and what it means to her to be able to represent and be such an important role model for Hispanic women.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And speaking of your roots, Natalie, I know that your family -- your mother is Brazilian. Your father is Puerto Rican. Talk about how that inspired your decision as a journalist and the influence they had. And again, welcome to the family.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Thank you, Jericka. I`m so glad to be a part of this family.\par \par \uc2 Yes, family is everything you know. I`m Puerto Rican and Brazilian. And my dad was Air Force through and through 24 almost 25 years. And so I grew up as an Air Force brat living around the world. I was born in Taiwan. I lived in Panama, Brazil, Spain, lots of different places learned my culture is Spanish and Portuguese. I speak both languages.\par \par \uc2 And for me being, a journalist, it came from that passion of seeing the world at such a young age and having experienced and seeing other cultures and history play out before my eyes in some cases.\par \par \uc2 So, I knew whatever I did in life, it would have to be to be able to tell stories of people who I was meeting and seeing who often you may not see in the American news media. So for me, it was important and it opened my eyes at a very young age to be able to tell stories that people want to see and hear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Natalie, your news roots are amazing. You started at News 12, the Bronx actual tagline "as local as local news gets."\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Then you competed against Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: That`s right. You did your homework.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As the local -- as the morning anchor in Connecticut. So, we`re so glad you`ve gone from competing with Gayle to being part of the big Gayle King family here at CBS MORNINGS and the wider CBS News family.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are so thrilled to have you.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, welcome, welcome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome. And we all echo that.\par \par \uc2 Natalie, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Thank you, guys. Nate, Tony, Jericka, so great. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, have a good one. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 From one incredible woman to another, now, check out who was in our progressive greenroom. The author of "Little Fires Everywhere," Celeste Ng is here to tell us about her new dark novel with an inspiring message of hope.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:08. Time to check in local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Coming up: "Sister, Sister" star, Tamera Mowry-Housley joins us. Find out if a reboot is in the works and get a first look at her new memoir.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "CBS MORNINGS Deals" has something for families with little ones. We`ll show you a bib that may help with messy eaters at an exclusive low price.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS. Look at those cute faces.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Little Fires Everywhere.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that is so good. That is Kerry Washington and Reese Witherspoon in the hit TV series "Little Fires Everywhere." You might have heard about it. It is about two mothers from very different backgrounds. It`s adapted from the best-selling novel by author Celeste Ng.\par \par \uc2 Now critics are calling her new novel, her most powerful work to date. "Our Missing Hearts" tells the story of a Chinese-American family in a dystopian near future.\par \par \uc2 In the book, a boy named Bird searches for his mother, a poet who was on the run.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, Celeste Ng joins us right now. Good morning. How you doing?\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG ("Our Missing Hearts" author): Good morning. Thank you so much for having me on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. So "Our Missing Hearts" is a bit of a departure for you. Was it difficult to write a darker book?\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: Well, it did feel like I was doing something new. As you say, the book looks at some dark subjects, but I realized that at the heart of the story, it is really a family story and there are the things that I always seem to write about, mothers and children. How do we raise children in a world that feels like it is hard and how do we hold on to hope through all of that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The difference is that it`s not the world we live in right now this second, but it`s also not our world, as you write in the back of the book. Explain the difference?\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: Yes, it is a world that I think is really governed by fear. There is a lot of distrust of other people and it is a world in which if you are acting un-American, your children can be taken away. And so, it is a world in which I think the characters have to make some really hard choices. And again, figure out how they`re going to raise their children to still survive in this world.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You write about anti-Asian violence in this book. Talk about, you know how difficult that was? You started writing this book in 2016. It was sort of a different direction, but you`re watching the world, you`re watching politics, and all of these events that are informing the story in the end.\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: Exactly. It always started with a mother-son story. For me, I am a mother, myself, and I always think a lot about family and parents. But as the world outside my office started to get really dark, it started to feel strange to not put that on the page and it crept into the book.\par \par \uc2 And so part of this book was me working through the questions I was asking myself as a parent, you know, how do I tell my kid like, okay, things seem hard, but we`re going to get through this, and we can still try and make the world better.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How important was it, though, to do it, through your perspective?\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: That became a really big part of it, especially after the outbreak of COVID started, and we started seeing a huge rise in anti-Asian hate. Growing up as a Chinese-American, I`m from Cleveland, but I think I`ve known my whole life that I am seen as other by other people and the way other people see me is not the way I know myself to be inside.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: And seeing that play out on the news with Asian people just being attacked on the street in the middle of the day, I think really made it important for me to try and talk about some of that in the book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That along with coming out of the pandemic, you know, did you ever struggle with hope? Because I`m always curious, when an author writes about something and you take away as a reader, something as powerful as hope is their struggles within the author`s own life?\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: There absolutely was. I mean, during the pandemic, I was feeling honestly really useless and helpless. I was -- I was thinking to myself, you know, if I had gone to Med School and I had become a doctor, maybe I could be in the hospital helping people, right? Like there is something tangible you could do.\par \par \uc2 And as a writer, what am I doing? Am I helping the world at all?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are though.\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: But I realized one of the things that was getting me through was reading my favorite poems, listening to music that I love, watching movies that I love that reminded me of sort of what the world could be, that the world wasn`t always like this. It`s not always going to be like this, like we can make a difference.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: And I think that`s one of the things art can do. It can give us hope for the future.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. It can be a source of solace, a source of good; discomfort that makes you think about what you don`t want to happen. You got a big old fat thank you in the back of the book to your mom and your sister, for putting up for what you call this whole creative writing thing. It`s turning out pretty well, right?\par \par \uc2 CELESTE NG: I think so. I come from a family of scientists. My mom is a chemist, my dad was a physicist. My sister is an engineer and I turned out to be a writer and I think they have been really supportive in this even though I think they don`t always understand what I`m doing. I`m so grateful for that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, we understand.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is writing great books.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am told I`ve got to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. Celeste Ng, thank you so much. "Our Missing Hearts" is on sale right now.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead or "Uplift at Eight." A little boy`s emotional reaction to a big birthday surprise. It`s going to make smile We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is now time, you can tell from the music, for the "Uplift at Eight." Stories that will bring you some joy, some new smile lines to your face, some happiness to your day and Vlad, walking streak of happiness is here to tell us more.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jericka said I could just bounce a ball. That`s the "Uplift."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bouncing a ball.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, they will. They know Vlad is coming in at 8:20 and 7:30, at 7:15.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you see Vlad bounce a basketball, I don`t know how that would work.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony has seen me at Nate`s house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turn the channel.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I tried to bounce a basketball, he was like, "Come on, Vlad."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a little bit sad, I would say.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was very sad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You were like, "I get it now."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so let me bring you the story of one child and opening that one present that you really, really wanted. Ever had that feeling when you`re a kid you really, really want something. So, check out eight-year-old, Titan`s (sp?) reaction when he unwrapped this present at his birthday party in front of all his friends.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CHILDREN chanting "It`s a phone. It`s a phone. It`s a phone.")\par \par \uc2 TITAN (Eight years old): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Try it.\par \par \uc2 TITAN : Thank you. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Wait, what kind?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, buddy.\par \par \uc2 TITAN: Who bought this for me?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Who else?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that. "Who bought this for me?" "Who else?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Who else? Who brought you in this earth?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Who else?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There you go. Titan racing to hug his father after opening his very own iPhone at his birthday party in Washington State. His dad told us, Titan had been asking for a phone for a while, but he didn`t expect that kind of reaction.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: You just want the best for your children and for him to show that emotion, I was not expecting it. So, it just -- it did me good. I had to hold back the little tears myself, you know.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So he said Titan has been loving his new iPhone and learning new responsibilities -- very important -- and that come with owning an iPhone. But I love that, all of those kids are like, "Yes, it is okay, buddy. It`s a phone."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a phone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s a phone.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It`s a phone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a phone. But there is a pressure for kids to have the new technology.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So it runs a little deeper than just wanting a phone.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I just wanted a Rock `Em Sock `Em Robot and a GI