It is a personal Portable Air Purifier. So, think about those big air purifier systems in your house. You can`t pack that up and take it with you, can you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: Now you can with this I traveled here from LA yesterday. I threw this in my purse. Second thing after I packed my phone, set it up on my little airline tray table and it filters the air around you, so it is filtering out things like smoke, odor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what?\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: Yes, that is right and it is fully chargeable. It runs for about four hours on a single charge or you can plug it in. You can leave it in your car. I love it for hotel rooms.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How large of the room does it --\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: So, it`s about a one bedroom size, right?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That`s amazing. One bedroom size.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: So, a small one bedroom size. It`s super quiet. Listen, all four of these are running right now. Do you hear anything?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: Whisper quiet. GREAT for high traffic areas like your cubicle, your desk, your car. The deal on this. Okay, it is so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk to me. Give me the bounce.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: $189.99. But you all, $100.00 off today. Okay? One hundred dollars off. You`re taking it $79.99.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what I am talking about.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: I think you can do it better than I can.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One -- on it.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: One hundred dollars off. There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can see Enzo dance right there. From back in the day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a great deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a great deal.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: It is an incredible deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a necessity.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: It is absolutely. And it is lightweight, super portable.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How exactly does it clean the air?\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: So what it does is uses UVC light and --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I am asking questions that we know about.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: And I know, I love this question.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 LILLIANA VAZQUEZ: So it uses UV light to clean those pollutants, it also uses negative ion technology.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Negative ion technology.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lilliana Vazquez, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We do partner with all of these companies to get you these incredible deals.\par \par \uc2 To purchase anything you saw today, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSdeals.com.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, we forgot to mention this actually breaks window.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In case of an emergency.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be hitting Florida right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That does it for us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Lilliana.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221004eia4000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 January 6th Revelations\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Scott MacFarlane \par \uc2 1,911 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now on to Washington, some major revelations on the January 6th attack. Former Republican Congressman, that`s Denver Riggleman, a former adviser to the January 6 Committee told "60 Minutes" Bill Whitaker last night about possible evidence and a connection to the Trump White House that we have never seen before.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with more on that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning. It was a very interesting conversation last night.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It sure was, Gayle. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The January 6 Select Committee is promising new revelations at its hearing Wednesday, but this comes as its former investigator makes strong allegations of an attempted coup on the 6th. The Committee members have been pushed back against any specific allegation, but they say he has been gone half a year and doesn`t know the current status of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Just two days out from the next January 6th Committee hearing, former Committee investigator, Denver Riggleman has revealed this.\par \par \uc2 DENVER RIGGLEMAN: You get a real "aha" moment when you see that the White House switchboard had connected to a rioter`s phone while it`s happening. That`s a big -- a pretty big "aha" moment. You get an aha --\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Wait a minute. Someone in the White House was calling one of the rioters while the riot was going on?\par \par \uc2 DENVER RIGGLEMAN: On January 6th. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: And you know who both ends of that call --\par \par \uc2 DENVER RIGGLEMAN: I only know one end of that call. I don`t know the White House end.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Riggleman says his team collected a tidal wave of thousands of texts and calls from Trump supporters on and ahead of January 6th, but only got a fraction of the funding he requested to look into the data.\par \par \uc2 Members of the panel pushed back.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): That`s one of thousands of details that obviously the Committee is aware of.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Arguing Riggleman, a one-time Republican Congressman, departed the Committee staff in April and has limited knowledge of the panel`s investigation.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): And without the advantage of the additional information we`ve gathered since he left the Committee, you know, it, I think, poses real risks to be suggesting things.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This weekend, members of the Committee also leveled criticism at the Department of Justice for the pace of its investigation and for asking the Committee for its records.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): My first reaction when we got the request "turn over all your files to us" was "Why don`t you have your own damn files?" You know, why haven`t you been conducting your own investigation?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Committee Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  says the panel has received hundreds of thousands of pages of Secret Service communications during the period around January 6th. That agency`s role during key events could be a key part of Wednesday`s Committee hearing -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His book is very interesting. Thank you very much, Scott.\par \par \uc2 In our next hour, former Congressman Riggleman will join us here at the table live to talk about the evidence that he saw while working on the January 6th investigation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Meantime, there are now fewer than 45 days into the midterm elections in November and that will decide which party controls Congress. According to the latest CBS News/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  Battleground Tracker, the estimates still hold that Republicans are going to win a majority, but their margin continues to shrink.\par \par \uc2 The newest estimate shows them taking 223 seats. That is down from August projection, but it still puts them over the 218 needed to win control.\par \par \uc2 CBS News elections and surveys director, Anthony Salvanto is here.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is a big shift if you go back even to July. Why is the margin for Republicans shrinking?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: It is shrinking largely because of the abortion issue and that speaks to what is this campaign about.\par \par \uc2 Let me set this up. Among women, to get your vote, a candidate has to agree with you on abortion. It is the top thing. It`s even higher than the economy. That`s number one. And then you look at well, who is winning the votes of people that think abortion is important, and they`re breaking Democratic and increasingly so, Tony, over the last month.\par \par \uc2 Now, set that up against the people who say the economy is important. They`re still breaking towards the Republicans, and that`s really big, and that`s why the Republicans still have the lead, but the abortion issue is why it has gotten tighter.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Abortion is a rights issue for Democrats, but there are rights issues on the Republican side as well, are there not?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: There are indeed and in fact, let me show you this. When we ask people, what do you think is at stake here? It`s your rights and freedoms that goes to the top even more so than your financial wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 So, for both sides, you see this like escalation of what the stakes are in this camp.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rights and freedoms for Americans. There is also this question of who gets to be an American. We`ve got all these encounters on the Southern border, and Republicans want to make an issue out of immigration. Is that having an effect on the race?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, it is. Start with this, among Republicans and in their base, this is important because this is a turnout election. Right? You want to motivate your base. Well, Republicans really liked the idea of Republican Governors sending immigrants to Democratic areas, that just 87 percent. And then they think it`s good because it puts the issue on the radar, it highlights the problem, right?\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s doing that in some respects, because you look at the people who think immigration is important in their vote, and that`s gone up. It`s gone up to 86 percent among Republicans, it`s gone up to 57 percent, up from 50 among Independents, so putting it on the radar a little bit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Anthony Salvanto, really great information. Appreciate it -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As always.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Italy have likely elected the first female Prime Minister in the country`s history. \uc2 Giorgia Meloni\uc2  is her name. She leads a political party, Brothers of Italy, which got more votes than any other in yesterday`s election. Meloni is expected to be asked to form a coalition government that would be the country`s most conservative leadership since World War Two.\par \par \uc2 All eyes on Giorgia in Italy.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a closer look at the likely new Prime Minister and the concern over electing a leader whose party is directly descended from the fascists who took power a hundred years ago.\par \par \uc2 But right now, it is 7:16, that`s how you say that, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up: Playing defense in deep space. NASA`s mission to knock an asteroid off course and why it could help future scientists save the Earth. I know, something out of a movie.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Rihanna puts a new gig on her calendar and it is huge. Her decision to play a show that she turned down a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Pressure. Steps away, throwing that. Incomplete.\par \par \uc2 Fumble recovery earlier.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tom Brady in the books, couldn`t get it done against Aaron Rodgers and the Packers in what could be the last time the legendary quarterbacks play each other. Rodgers came out on top, 14 to 12 yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Now in Miami, this play is now being called the butt punt. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Dolphins` punter hit a teammate in the backside and the Bills got a two- point safety. The play drew comparisons to an infamous play from the Jets in 2012. You guys remember this? Known as the butt fumble when quarterback Mark Sanchez ran straight into the backside of his teammate. Sanchez, now a FOX Sports analyst, he had a little fun with this and tweeted out: "Whoa. Stay out of my lane, bro." But I`m pretty sure he`s happy that he`s not the butt of all jokes. No pun intended.\par \par \uc2 Okay, now the Bills, they had a chance to win, but they ran out of time. I want you guys to check out this Bills coordinator after they ran out of time at the very end of the game. I`m pretty sure it was something that they worked on and the offensive coordinator is in the booth.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s funny.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, they lost the game right here, because the clock was tick-tick-tick ticking and then he lost his cool. That`s Ken Dorsey former, former quarterback.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at the other people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at the guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know. He is like -- that one guy backs away, but look at -- and takes off his headset and goes, "I`m out of here."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on, now. We`ve all been there before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, protective glasses. Yes. There you go, looking at the end there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve been there where you`ve been that mad and you started tearing up stuff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whooza. Yes, but I -- I wouldn`t do that on camera. He forgot that there are cameras in their booth.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was right there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right on top of him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It would have been fine, if it were down the other end.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But shout out to the \uc2 Miami Dolphins\uc2 . They`re now three and oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like that we had about 16 different angles on the butt punt. I see that thing go up there. The best play of the day for nine-year- olds.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, they love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Nate, the big question everybody wants to know: Was Gisele at the game with Tom Brady?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I didn`t see her, Gayle. I didn`t see her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t see her there. She was turning off the TV at home and throwing the remote on to the counter. Off her play.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see about that.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead in our series "Kindness 101," three students who changed their school with a surprise project share their secret for never giving up.\par \par \uc2 Local news though coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220929ei9q0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The End Of The Roe Era In America Has Put A New Spotlight On A Former Group Of Activists Who Wants What By The Code Name, Jane\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Charlie De Mar \par \uc2 482 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This week, Indiana and West Virginia joined 13 other states imposing a near total ban on abortion. The end of the Roe era in America has put a new spotlight on a former group of activists who once went by the code name, Jane.\par \par \uc2 Here is Charlie De Mar.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Eileen Smith is a proud member of the most unlikely group of outlaws.\par \par \uc2 EILEEN SMITH (Abortion Activist): Well, from 1968 until 1973, we provided illegal abortions in the city of Chicago. And it was an underground group. No one really knew that, you know, we were doing it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Janes, HBO)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We were ordinary women trying to save women`s lives, but we were criminals.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: A recently released HBO documentary shares the story of the Jane Collective, a group of Chicago women who provided over 11,000 illegal abortions before Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 EILEEN SMITH: I had an abortion through them and it was the best medical experience I ever had.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: In 1972, police arrested seven women in connection to the secret operation. The charges were later dropped.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Janes, HBO)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: There were 11 counts of conspiracy to commit abortion, 110 years for each us, so that`s a long time.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Dorie Barron also called on Jane for an abortion and later volunteered.\par \par \uc2 DORIE BARRON (Activist): It was an incredible lesson in sisterhood. Nobody wants to break the law when they`re trying to help people, but we had no choice.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: With the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade in June, the former Janes say their work is still relevant today.\par \par \uc2 DORIE BARRON: We have got especially men in Washington, passing laws that know nothing about the human body.\par \par \uc2 EILEEN SMITH: Anger, sorrow that we`re back having to deal with these same issues.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Janes, HBO)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Decades after the Janes outlaw ways, the fight over access to abortions goes on.\par \par \uc2 Charlie De Mar, CBS News, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, finding a perfect blend between grape growing and science to help fight the extremes of climate change.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220920ei9h0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Near Total Ban On Abortion Takes Effect In Indiana\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 826 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A sweeping new abortion ban went into effect in Indiana today, outlawing the procedure, with limited exceptions. The state was the first in the nation to pass legislation after the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adriana Diaz is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. KATIE MCHUGH (Physicians For Reproductive Health): This is an area that would normally be full of patients.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voice-over): Most of the lights are off at Women`s Med, an Indianapolis abortion provider.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATIE MCHUGH: As a physician, I get mad because insurance companies deny a claim. I`m not normally mad because my patients are being stripped of human rights.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The clinic is among two Indiana providers slated to close. The state`s five remaining clinics will still offer limited health care.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Katie McHugh says patient volume doubled here as more restrictive abortion laws were passed in neighboring states.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATIE MCHUGH: And we were able to support the states around us and take their patients and provide the care that they needed. That all happened until yesterday.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Indiana is now one of 13 states with a near total ban or that have outlawed abortions after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 SUE LIEBEL (Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America): Well, it`s an exciting day for Indiana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Sue Liebel is with Susan B. Anthony Pro-life America.\par \par \uc2 SUE LIEBEL: Now it`s back in state hands. It`s going to be messy. It`ll be different in every state. But it`s closer to the people, closer to the will of the voters.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Women`s Med will now send Indiana patients to its sister clinic in Dayton, Ohio, more than 100 miles away, where, just yesterday, a county judge temporarily restored Ohio`s abortion access.\par \par \uc2 When you look to the future, what do you see?\par \par \uc2 DR. KATIE MCHUGH: I see so much harm on these populations that will not be able to access abortion care. And I think about the generational damage that will be done to these patients and their families.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: A judge declined a request to temporarily block the ban today while lawsuits play out, but that could change at a hearing on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Women`s Med plans to keep its doors open to be available to answer patients questions at least for the next few weeks -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Adriana Diaz, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead: A boil water alert is finally lifted in Jackson, Mississippi, but concerns remain.\par \par \uc2 And a caught-on-camera moment. A fast-food worker suddenly becomes a crime fighter.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: For the first time since late July, people in Jackson, Mississippi, don`t have to boil their tap water before drinking it.\par \par \uc2 The alert was lifted today, but concerns remain about copper and lead levels in the city`s troubled water system. Families are urged to keep using bottled water to make baby formula.\par \par \uc2 Well, we learned today that President Biden will meet with the families of \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner and fellow American Paul Whelan at the White House tomorrow. Both remain jailed in Russia. It will be the president`s first face-to-face meetings with the families. His administration has been trying to negotiate a prisoner swap to get them released.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the former Ambassador to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Bill Richardson held meetings this week with Russian leaders in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next: Roger and out. Why tennis legend Roger Federer is calling it quits.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A fast-food worker in Florida is being called a hero after saving a woman from a carjacking.\par \par \uc2 A man snatched a woman`s keys as she was getting into her car with her baby at a \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  in Fort Walton Beach. A worker then wrestled the suspect to the ground and held him until police arrived.\par \par \uc2 Forty-one-year-old tennis great Roger Federer announced today that he`s hanging up his racket. Federer says: "Tennis has treated me more generously than I ever would have dreamt. And now I must recognize when it`s time to end my competitive career."\par \par \uc2 The 20-time Grand Slam winner has been sidelined by injuries. His last match will be at the Laver Cup next week in London.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back with the story of the queen and the cowboy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220917ei9f000jj\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Battleground Pennsylvania\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Robert Costa \par \uc2 2,449 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A new CBS News battleground tracker poll could signal good news for Democrats in the State of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 It shows Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is leading his opponent, Dr. Mehmet Oz by five points, outside the margin of error, in the race for Senate.\par \par \uc2 Over in the Governor`s contest, Democrat, \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  has opened an 11- point lead over a Trump-endorsed Republican, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of course, all of this could change. It`s all up to the voters. And on that subject, Robert Costa went the President Biden`s hometown of Scranton, PA to see what voters are talking about, what voters want.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania is certainly a hotspot on the political map. Many top races up and down the ballot, but the political winds here can sometimes be hard to read. That`s why we wanted to come to Pennsylvania, talk to voters directly about the concerns in their lives and about the candidates.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Let`s bring it back. We can do this.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: With multiple visits by the President and the former President.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Hello, Pennsylvania. Hello.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Pennsylvania is once again becoming the battleground in American politics.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` new survey shows the economy and inflation remain top concerns. Eight in 10 Pennsylvania voters say higher prices have been difficult or worse.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE HREBIN (Scranton, PA resident and registered Republican): I worry all the time about the economy and healthcare.\par \par \uc2 VERA WILSON (Scranton, PA resident, registered Democrat): Everybody up here, they struggling with food, it is expensive; the gas.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It is also clear voters here are paying close attention to national debates from the fight over abortion rights to the ongoing probes of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WILLIE SMITH (Scranton, PA resident and registered Democrat): Every time you look around this commercial zone about this person or that person.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Pennsylvania`s hotly contested campaigns will be a test of some of the big questions in American politics. Like is a Trump endorsement enough to win a tough race?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : He is going to work and fight for Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Dr. Mehmet Oz backed by Trump has been cast as an outsider by Democrats for living for years in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FONTINELL (Scranton, PA resident and registered Democrat): So, anybody can just decide they want to go and buy a house someplace to run for office.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Oz`s rival, Democratic Lieutenant Governor, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has long been the frontrunner.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): Who wants me to be the 51st vote?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He says, he is getting better every day since having a stroke earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Still, despite pledging to debate Oz once, Fetterman continues to face scrutiny about how the stroke has affected him.\par \par \uc2 HELEN LANCIA (Scranton, PA resident and registered Republican): I don`t think he is physically able to do the job. I just think they have nobody else.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Since returning to the trail, Fetterman has been busy rallying Democrats especially on abortion rights. And according to the CBS News poll, most Democrats say the overturn of Roe has made them more likely to vote this year.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : Women are the reason we win.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In the race for Governor, Democrat, \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  has also been rallying his party`s voters on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO (D) (Pennsylvania Governor candidate): You know how high the stakes of this election are.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And warning them about Republican, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , an Army veteran who has been backed by Trump.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting in Capitol.)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He was near the Capitol on January 6th, and has repeatedly echoed the former President`s false claims about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 DOUG MASTRIANO\uc2  (R) (Pennsylvania Governor candidate): I know there`s a lot of problems in our in our election system in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Amid the political firefight, both Fetterman and Oz have forcefully defended themselves. Oz has said that Fetterman`s talk about Oz`s residency is a distraction from policy and a personal attack. Fetterman has said that Oz`s remarks about his health are out of bounds.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, voters here tell us they won`t be making their vote based on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  battles -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Bob Costa out there with the people of Pennsylvania hearing what they have to say.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us because in a moment, we`ve got Vlad Duthiers bringing you the stories you`re going to be talking about today, but first, it is 7:44. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey, hey, hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey. Emmy-award winner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad is back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The big husband of the year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Accompanying Marian.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was an incredible moment to be there with her. And I`m glad that Nancy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But tell us what Marian won for?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Marian won her third along with the show -- her third Emmy Award for her producing work at "Last Week Tonight with John Oliver."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And she wore an incredible dress and just this morning --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She did wore and incredible dress.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She sent me a note and we were like on brides.com like the cutest couples on the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Damn right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Vlad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are a very supportive husband.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are on brides.com, that`s the cutest -- one of the cutest couples. We agree, Vlad. We agree.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No joke.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are all in on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should find that picture and show it before Vlad leaves. That`s really good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are a very supportive husband. And speaking of supporting partners.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s get into it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s talk about that. Gisele Bundchen is breaking her silence about Tom Brady`s decision to keep playing football and its effect on their family.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian supermodel`s husband announced his retirement from the \uc2 NFL\uc2  in February and then he changed his mind just six weeks later, because men can do that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In next month`s cover story for "Elle" Magazine, Bundchen says she has her concern saying football is a violent sport and she would like her husband to be more present with her and their three children.\par \par \uc2 She also acknowledged Brady`s love of the game saying he needs to follow his joy, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should say that article, whenever you see a magazine, they`ve normally done it at least three months out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that said, so that article was written before the beginning of the football season when Tom Brady was absent for 11 days.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Eleven days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it still talks about how she feels about it. But you know, there`s been some developments.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I mean --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those are fair concerns.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And as a husband, let me translate some of these quotes for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When she says, "I`ve done my part." That means, your turn, honey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come home. When she says, "I`ve had these conversations over and over again." That means, I`m tired of it. The moment has come.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Real talk.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, it is really --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If she says, I want you to be more present.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We have a very good translator.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re a very good husband.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You need to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It sounds like you`re relating to this, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When she says --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I concur.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When she says, I want you to be more present for the kids, that means they think your name is Tom.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is either translation or you`re speaking from experience.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re translating very well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am better at marriage. I am on season two of family life. I know from where I speak.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I also think there is something else here, which is just from my perspective, you know, it`s not about you need to be at home with the kids. They are privileged. They have you know, people that can help them.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes though, you`re like, "Hey, I just want you around. I just want to experience life with you on a weekend when you`re not working," because you know what, you did everything you had to do in the game and more.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he still enjoys playing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s a famous --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She also said, he should also have the right to pursue his joy. Clearly, they need to work some stuff out, it would seem in the public thing, but --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. All right. We`ll be watching. We will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Let`s give you a follow up to story on \uc2 Sanna Marin\uc2 , the Prime Minister of Finland who was criticized over a video showing her dancing at a party.\par \par \uc2 It sparked this massive discussion about the treatment of women in politics and led to the trending hashtag, #SolidarityWithSanna.\par \par \uc2 Now, the leader is calling for more women to join her field. In 2019, Marin became Finland`s youngest ever elected Prime Minister, just 34 years old.\par \par \uc2 Now, at a news conference yesterday, she was asked what advice she would give women looking to get into politics who might be afraid of being attacked by critics.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what she said.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SANNA MARIN\uc2  (Finland Prime Minister): What would I say? I would say you`re capable, you`re strong. You can change the world. Just do it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mic drop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mic drop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that. I love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was at a private party with friends. It was bad that somebody even leaked that tape.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She didn`t do that all the time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just do it. It should be a slogan. I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that. Nice to see you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sell that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up at this hour, \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2  will be here in our studio. She is out with her ninth children`s book and a documentary series with her mom, that would be Hillary, about gutsy women.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Children`s author, Mo Willems has an important message to share. Sometimes the ridiculous can be sublime.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Wax spent some time in Willems` unique universe where he says "The pigeon will ride the roller coaster."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, we`re sharing "Bon Appetit`s" Best New Restaurants of 2022. We hope you woke up hungry this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did, Nate and I am.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 A potential railway strike could wreak havoc on US supply chains if a deal is not reached this week.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK: So things that you`re used to getting, all of a sudden, sort of aren`t there anymore on a grocery shelf.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Queen Elizabeth`s coffin is being moved to Westminster Hall in London today as thousands of mourners line up to pay respects.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Queen is all we`ve ever known, so there has never been a any difference at all.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  proposes a bill that would effectively ban abortion nationwide after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Most of the members of my conference prefer that this be dealt with at the State level.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A new CBS News poll has Democrats leading in Pennsylvania`s crucial midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And do you want to feel a little old? Well, look at the reactions when these two kids see a landline phone for the first time.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yo, it is actually working.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, it is hooked -- it is a phone.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Can I do your number?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It will ring. It will call me.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Really?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, it`s a real phone.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Hello? Oh, my gosh. That`s a really awesome voice.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Like get off of it right now.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: What? It did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember those old fangled gadgets we used to use. I think it`s funny when they go, "It actually works."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Kids will never know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know what the bottom line is, it actually works better than your cell phone. Like the quality of the connection is solid. But if you really want to feel old --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not giving up my landline yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You remember having to meet somebody, get their number, write it on a piece of paper, put in your pocket, take it home and then and then dial in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and if you make a plan, stick to the plan. Like you stand there as Cinnabon for two hours.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two hours.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because that`s where you`re going to meet, because you have no way to change it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or Nate, you sit by the phone hoping it rains, picking up, "Is it working? Is it working?" Yes, it`s working.\par \par \uc2 That was funny.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220915ei9e0018h\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Stock Market Slide\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Ed O`Keefe \par \uc2 1,837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day to most of us, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street plunges on worse than expected news on prices, even as President Biden celebrates his Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The American people won. This is what it looks like when the American government works for the people.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Boston, Federal investigators are on the scene after a package exploded at \uc2 Northeastern University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAN: One person has been injured.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  proposes a nationwide abortion ban after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): We take back the House and the Senate, I can assure you we`ll have a vote on our bill.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In Southern California, relentless rainfall triggering mudslides.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thousands of people are under evacuation or stay-at-home orders.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is startling testimony from the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  whistleblower accusing the company of putting its users at risk.\par \par \uc2 PEITER ZATKO (Former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Head of Security): Over a decade behind industry security standards.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was an emotional night in London as the body of Queen Elizabeth the Second returned to Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 NBA\uc2  suspended the owner of the \uc2 Phoenix Suns\uc2  and fined him $10 million accused of misconduct including racist and sexist remarks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Emmy ratings are in and it is not looking good.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Five point nine million people tuned in to the award show. That`s a record low for the Emmys.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL\uc2  ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): It was also on Peacock, so when you add in the people who streamed it, there`s still 5.92 million people.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): President Biden is currently hard at work trying to prevent a railroad strike that could take place as soon as this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Biden has been working directly with the unions and the railroads to try to work something out.\par \par \uc2 Unions and railroads, if they somehow incorporate ice cream, and a 12- minute story about a guy named swizzle stick. This is a full Biden bingo. That`s what it is.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS and we`re going to share history with you today.\par \par \uc2 You`re looking right now at live pictures from London at the Duke of York steps on The Mall where thousands are lining up to honor Queen Elizabeth and watching the ancient rituals of mourning for a monarch and take you there for a very emotional day of remembrance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. We`re also going to have \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2  here in studio. You, of course first met her as the first daughter of this country, but she is now a mother herself, and she has got a new book for kids, parents, too, really about what happens when you bring a new baby into the family.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, are you hungry? Trust me, you will be very soon when we reveal "Bon Appetit`s" Best New Restaurants of 2022 first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that, but we`re going to begin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it`s always fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure. The appetite has arrived, I got it. Don`t worry.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with potential new trouble for the economy ahead following the worst day for stocks since June of 2020.\par \par \uc2 The major markets all plunged yesterday with the NASDAQ down the most, more than five percent, all because a key government report showed less improvement in the fight against inflation than expected.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, there is a looming threat of a railway strike, which could have a big impact on supply chains. Ed O`Keefe is following at all for us at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, the President cannot be happy about any of it. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: No, he cannot. Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 That inflation report dashes hopes of a quicker economic rebound, and while the stock market goes up and down all the time, this one-day drop for most indexes was the biggest since the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Even though the average price of gas is down 26 cents in the last month, other prices aren`t falling.\par \par \uc2 Government figures show groceries climbing more than 13 percent on average, electricity spiking nearly 16 percent, and rent up nearly seven percent over the last year.\par \par \uc2 Both high prices, plus the likelihood the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise interest rates again sent the markets tumbling. Just as the President and Democrats were celebrating passage of their Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The single most important legislation passed in the Congress to combat inflation and one of the most significant laws in our nation`s history in my view.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The new law provides funding to fight climate change, ease healthcare costs, and hike taxes on big corporations, but it doesn`t nothing to address short term price hikes.\par \par \uc2 The President said last night, he isn`t worried.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The stock market doesn`t necessarily reflect the state of the economy. I think we`re going to be fine.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Republicans disagree.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): These numbers are staggering and the White House seems to be just in absolute denial.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And now Washington is preparing for another development that could slam the brakes on an economic rebound.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 30 percent of all US freight shipments could be halted by Friday if the nation`s railroads can`t cut a deal with unions at odds over sick time and attendance policies.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JERRY MORAN\uc2  (R-KS): One of the last things we need is more difficulties in getting agricultural commodities and food.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  is preemptively canceling long-haul service on affected tracks, and some commuter rail lines are warning customers, make alternative plans beginning Friday.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK (\uc2 KPMG\uc2  chief economist): The effects of it compound very quickly over the course of even a few days. So, things that you`re used to getting all of a sudden, sort of aren`t there anymore on a grocery shelf.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now, while the economy and inflation remain the top issues of concern for voters, we`ve noticed abortion rights has also become a big concern, and that`s what was so curious about what Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina proposed yesterday.\par \par \uc2 He has unveiled the proposed national ban on abortion rights. His bill would ban that procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy with rare exceptions, and has no chance of becoming law as long as Democrats control Congress, and when asked, even Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wouldn`t commit to holding a vote on the proposal if the GOP retakes Senate control.\par \par \uc2 He noted most Republican senators believe each State, not the Federal government should determine what to do about abortion. And of course, he has seen the polls that show a majority of Americans do not support a national ban on abortion rights -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Abortion, big issue on the campaign trail this year. But of course, the economy is as well.\par \par \uc2 Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning back to the economy and those worse than expected inflation numbers, we are joined now by CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESSINGER: Doing well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so gas prices are down over the month, but higher than a year ago. Other consumer prices are still up. What`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESSINGER: So this report was really important because that headline number did go down, it didn`t go down by as much as expected, but I think the other areas of the report were really somewhat worrisome.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s look at food costs, which were up by more than 11 percent from a year ago. That`s the fastest annual acceleration since 1979. So, that`s a big number.\par \par \uc2 And then we look at shelter. The number isn`t that big. It`s up 6.2 percent from a year ago, but think about how much money of your household goes towards rent or paying for your house. So, that core inflation rate, all the other stuff besides food and energy, rising pretty substantially. That`s the worrying aspect of the report.\par \par \uc2 Great that news that gas prices are down, but everything else is still expensive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But inflation has been here for months. So, why the market punch?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESSINGER: So, I think it`s all about these expectations, because there was some hope that inflation would come down by more at the headline, and then that core prices would sort of stagnate, and now, we have a bunch of investors who say, "Oh, my God. Now, we really are going to see the Fed act next week. There`s going to be three quarters of a percentage point increase, maybe the Fed is going to take us into a recession."\par \par \uc2 And there`s great fear that the Fed is going to have to raise rates by even more than expected and that just raising those interest rates is going to slow things down too much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Jill, we`ve got holiday shopping around the corner. Will these prices come down?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESSINGER: Well, here`s the issue. I think there is another aspect to the selloff in stocks. I think that there is a worry that consumers, if you`re paying so much for food, for gas, for energy, for everything else, that you are not going to have as much money to spend on the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 And I`m unfortunately here to report that I don`t think prices are going to come down substantially in the next few months, and that means that you may have to spend a little bit less this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: During the commercial break, I`m going to ask you about the R-word, "recession" because seems like it keeps coming up.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESSINGER: We`ll do that next week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not