nd I`m unfortunately here to report that I don`t think prices are going to come down substantially in the next few months, and that means that you may have to spend a little bit less this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: During the commercial break, I`m going to ask you about the R-word, "recession" because seems like it keeps coming up.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESSINGER: We`ll do that next week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In London this morning, huge crowds have gathered to see Queen Elizabeth`s coffin carried in a grand procession through the city this morning.\par \par \uc2 Last night, a motorcade brought the Queen for the last time to Buckingham Palace, where she has lived for most of her 96 years.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is outside the Palace.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning to you. What are we going to see later today?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, just over two hours from now, the Queen`s coffin will pass from her family, that`s the Royal family to the nation.\par \par \uc2 It will pass down this road just behind me, drawn on a gun carriage through Central London to the Palace of Westminster, where she will lie in state until her funeral on Monday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The Queen was born in London and last night she came home. Flown by a military transporter, accompanied by her only daughter, Princess Anne.\par \par \uc2 The princess said in a statement that she was fortunate to share the last 24 hours of her dearest mother`s life.\par \par \uc2 "To my mother, the Queen. Thank you."\par \par \uc2 The cortege drove slowly in the twilight through the streets of the British capital in the English rain to Buckingham Palace for one final night.\par \par \uc2 Inside, a glimpse of family members, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  as well as Harry and Meghan.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is moving. It was so touching, you know, and I`m glad I was here to see things for myself, to see history being done.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Queen is all we`ve ever known since we were little, so it`s never been any different. It`s always been the Queen.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: It`s hard to believe that just over three months ago, the Queen appeared on the balcony of Buckingham Palace to meet her adoring public.\par \par \uc2 The Platinum Jubilee marked her record 70-year reign.\par \par \uc2 The concert at the Palace was star-studded, a whimsical celebration of her service and her trademarks.\par \par \uc2 Her son and successor \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  was in Northern Ireland yesterday. He has been traveling across the country in recent days, meeting what are now his people.\par \par \uc2 The last time this country buried a monarch was 1952, the late Queen`s Father George VI.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A guard watches over the sleeping King.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Then the public lined up to pay their final respects.\par \par \uc2 This time, around 750,000 people are expected in a line nearly five miles long.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Behind the Queen`s coffin today will walk \uc2 King Charles\uc2 , Princes William and Harry, along with other members of the Royal family. Kate, the Queen Consort, Camilla, and Meghan are all expected to travel by car -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly Williams for us. Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And we do want to linger for a moment on the size of those crowds just how long a line of people were talking about. To give you an idea, let`s go to the map.\par \par \uc2 This line could stretch along the ancient River Thames, past Central London, and far beyond the famous Tower Bridge.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi spoke with some of the people already waiting to view the Queen`s coffin as it passes. She is at the Lambeth Bridge across the river from Westminster Hall where the Queen will lie in state.\par \par \uc2 