eart of it, you will see the family and they will have to grieve their mother again in front of hundreds of thousands of people. So, there will be a very astonishing procession, which will leave in shortly under two hours, and at the head of the procession, will be Her Majesty, the Royal Standard on the coffin and on a gun carriage and on the coffin will be the Imperial State Crown, which she wore the day she was crowned, her orb, and her scepter, the symbols of Her Majesty.\par \par \uc2 And there will be -- I mean, it will be a very impressive, but very solemn procession, which will last precisely 38 minutes. It will go along the ceremonial route of the Mall. Of course, Horse Guards parade where Trooping of the Colour takes place.\par \par \uc2 Then down Whitehall, the heart of \uc2 British government\uc2  past Downing Street to this astonishing 11th Century Hall -- Westminster Hall.\par \par \uc2 Her Majesty, was I think, the 41st monarch since William the Conqueror, Westminster Hall was built by William the Conqueror`s son, and I think was for many years the biggest room in England.\par \par \uc2 So, it is an astonishing place where the Charles I trial was, where Winston Churchill lay in state, were both of the Queens lay in state, and there hasn`t been a State Funeral since Churchill`s funeral in 1965, and there hasn`t been a ceremonial procession, which will be seen today, since the one that I witnessed in 2002 for Her Majesty`s late mother.\par \par \uc2 And of course, behind the coffin will be the King, Her Majesty`s other children, grandchildren, most of the Royal family and it will be an incredibly powerful moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Wesley, this is what struck me. The fact that people -- men, women, children, say, "If I have to wait over 20 years, or 20 hours, rather, that`s okay." I had a friend of mine, I was talking to him last night. He said, "Listen, I love my mother very much. But I wouldn`t wait 20 hours to see -- to stand in line to see my mother," not making light of it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I`m curious about what is it about her that the people are willing to do this?\par \par \uc2 WESLEY KERR: I mean, they did it for her coronation as well. So, there is something about the monarchy as an institution that holds the country together, but this is the end of a remarkable era, and its farewell to a remarkable lady.\par \par \uc2 I absolutely made a point in downtime yesterday evening of going to see her coffin. It was driven past my birthplace, it came through my neighborhood, and there were several hundreds of us just in that location.\par \par \uc2 This is a chance personally to say goodbye either in that way or of course, people are going to wait for a day or so to see and it is actually amazing camaraderie. We had camaraderie for two hours waiting yesterday, but there is a powerful sense of togetherness.\par \par \uc2 There are people from all over the world, people have flown in from New Zealand, from Angola, from Canada just this week for this occasion. And they reminisce. Often, they are ex-service people, but Elizabeth II was an astonishingly popular and successful monarch and we all feel a sense of personal connection.\par \par \uc2 On the way here, I went to her birthplace and just said a prayer. Just a very powerful moment today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I can only imagine. I`m really very touched by all the pomp and circumstance of the moment.\par \par \uc2 Wesley Kerr, I thank you.\par \par \uc2 I`ll be flying to London this weekend. So, I look forward to meeting you in person. We will have lots to discuss.\par \par \uc2 We will show you the precession of the Queen`s coffin from Buckingham Palace to Westminster Hall in a CBS News Special Report. It starts at nine o`clock Eastern.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, we take you to Pennsylvania, where big personalities are running in crucial midterm races, and we`ll reveal a new CBS News poll that shows strong feelings for the candidates there.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve got something for you. Have you ever wanted today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes? Whoa. You`ve got to go to CBS MORNINGS on the go podcast. You`ll get exactly that. Sounds like a deal to me.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A new CBS News battleground tracker poll could signal good news for Democrats in the State of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 It shows Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is leading his opponent, Dr. Mehmet Oz by five points, outside the margin of error, in the race for Senate.\par \par \uc2 Over in the Governor`s contest, Democrat, Josh Shapiro has opened an 11- point lead over a Trump-endorsed Republican, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of course, all of this could change. It`s all up to the voters. And on that subject, Robert Costa went the President Biden`s hometown of Scranton, PA to see what voters are talking about, what voters want.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania is certainly a hotspot on the political map. Many top races up and down the ballot, but the political winds here can sometimes be hard to read. That`s why we wanted to come to Pennsylvania, talk to voters directly about the concerns in their lives and about the candidates.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Let`s bring it back. We can do this.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: With multiple visits by the President and the former President.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Hello, Pennsylvania. Hello.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Pennsylvania is once again becoming the battleground in American politics.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` new survey shows the economy and inflation remain top concerns. Eight in 10 Pennsylvania voters say higher prices have been difficult or worse.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE H