ts. And according to the CBS News poll, most Democrats say the overturn of Roe has made them more likely to vote this year.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : Women are the reason we win.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In the race for Governor, Democrat, Josh Shapiro has also been rallying his party`s voters on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO (D) (Pennsylvania Governor candidate): You know how high the stakes of this election are.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And warning them about Republican, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , an Army veteran who has been backed by Trump.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting in Capitol.)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He was near the Capitol on January 6th, and has repeatedly echoed the former President`s false claims about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 DOUG MASTRIANO\uc2  (R) (Pennsylvania Governor candidate): I know there`s a lot of problems in our in our election system in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Amid the political firefight, both Fetterman and Oz have forcefully defended themselves. Oz has said that Fetterman`s talk about Oz`s residency is a distraction from policy and a personal attack. Fetterman has said that Oz`s remarks about his health are out of bounds.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, voters here tell us they won`t be making their vote based on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  battles -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Bob Costa out there with the people of Pennsylvania hearing what they have to say.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us because in a moment, we`ve got Vlad Duthiers bringing you the stories you`re going to be talking about today, but first, it is 7:44. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey, hey, hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey. Emmy-award winner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad is back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The big husband of the year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Accompanying Marian.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was an incredible moment to be there with her. And I`m glad that Nancy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But tell us what Marian won for?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Marian won her third along with the show -- her third Emmy Award for her producing work at "Last Week Tonight with John Oliver."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And she wore an incredible dress and just this morning --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She did wore and incredible dress.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She sent me a note and we were like on brides.com like the cutest couples on the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Damn right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Vlad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are a very supportive husband.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are on brides.com, that`s the cutest -- one of the cutest couples. We agree, Vlad. We agree.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No joke.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are all in on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should find that picture and show it before Vlad leaves. That`s really good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are a very supportive husband. And speaking of supporting partners.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s get into it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s talk about that. Gisele Bundchen is breaking her silence about Tom Brady`s decision to keep playing football and its effect on their family.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian supermodel`s husband announced his retirement from the \uc2 NFL\uc2  in February and then he changed his mind just six weeks later, because men can do that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In next month`s cover story for "Elle" Magazine, Bundchen says she has her concern saying football is a violent sport and she would like her husband to be more present with her and their three children.\par \par \uc2 She also acknowledged Brady`s love of the game saying he needs to follow his joy, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should say that article, whenever you see a magazine, they`ve normally done it at least three months out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that said, so that article was written before the beginning of the football season when Tom Brady was absent for 11 days.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Eleven days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it still talks about how she feels about it. But you know, there`s been some developments.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I mean --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those are fair concerns.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And as a husband, let me translate some of these quotes for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When she says, "I`ve done my part." That means, your turn, honey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come home. When she says, "I`ve had these conversations over and over again." That means, I`m tired of it. The moment has come.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Real talk.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, it is really --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If she says, I want you to be more present.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We have a very good translator.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re a very good husband.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You need to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It sounds like you`re relating to this, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When she says --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I concur.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When she says, I want you to be more present for the kids, that means they think your name is Tom.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is either translation or you`re speaking from experience.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re translating very well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am better at marriage. I am on season two of family life. I know from where I speak.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I also think there is something else here, which is just from my perspective, you know, it`s not about you need to be at home with the kids. They are privileged. They have you know, people that can help them.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes though, you`re like, "Hey, I just want you around. I just want to experience life with you on a weekend when you`re not working," because you know what, you did everything you had to do in the game and more.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he still enjoys playing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s a famous --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She also said, he should also have the right to pursue his joy. Clearly, they need to work some stuff out, it would seem in the public thing, but --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. All right. We`ll be watching. We will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Let`s give you a follow up to story on \uc2 Sanna Marin\uc2 , the Prime Minister of Finland who was criticized over a video showing her dancing at a party.\par \par \uc2 It sparked this massive discussion about the treatment of women in politics and led to the trending hashtag, #SolidarityWithSanna.\par \par \uc2 Now, the leader is calling for more women to join her field. In 2019, Marin became Finland`s youngest ever elected Prime Minister, just 34 years old.\par \par \uc2 Now, at a news conference yesterday, she was asked what advice she would give women looking to get into politics who might be afraid of being attacked by critics.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what she said.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SANNA MARIN\uc2  (Finland Prime Minister): What would I say? I would say you`re capable, you`re strong. You can change the world. Just do it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mic drop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mic drop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that. I love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was at a private party with friends. It was bad that somebody even leaked that tape.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She didn`t do that all the time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just do it. It should be a slogan. I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that. Nice to see you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sell that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up at this hour, \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2  will be here in our studio. She is out with her ninth children`s book and a documentary series with her mom, that would be Hillary, about gutsy women.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Children`s author, Mo Willems has an important message to share. Sometimes the ridiculous can be sublime.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Wax spent some time in Willems` unique universe where he says "The pigeon will ride the roller coaster."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, we`re sharing "Bon Appetit`s" Best New Restaurants of 2022. We hope you woke up hungry this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did, Nate and I am.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 A potential railway strike could wreak havoc on US supply chains if a deal is not reached this week.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK: So things that you`re used to getting, all of a sudden, sort of aren`t there anymore on a grocery shelf.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Queen Elizabeth`s coffin is being moved to Westminster Hall in London today as thousands of mourners line up to pay respects.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Queen is all we`ve ever known, so there has never been a any difference at all.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  proposes a bill that would effectively ban abortion nationwide after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Most of the members of my conference prefer that this be dealt with at the State level.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A new CBS News poll has Democrats leading in Pennsylvania`s crucial midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And do you want to feel a little old? Well, look at the reactions when these two kids see a landline phone for the first time.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yo, it is actually working.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, it is hooked -- it is a phone.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Can I do your number?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It will ring. It will call me.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Really?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, it`s a real phone.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Hello? Oh, my gosh. That`s a really awesome voice.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Like get off of it right now.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: What? It did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember those old fangled gadgets we used to use. I think it`s funny when they go, "It actually works."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Kids will never know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know what the bottom line is, it actually works better than your cell phone. Like the quality of the connection is solid. But if you really want to feel old --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not giving up my landline yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You remember having to meet somebody, get their number, write it on a piece of paper, put in your pocket, take it home and then and then dial in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and if you make a plan, stick to the plan. Like you stand there as Cinnabon for two hours.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two hours.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because that`s where you`re going to meet, because you have no way to change it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or Nate, you sit by the phone hoping it rains, picking up, "Is it working? Is it working?" Yes, it`s working.\par \par \uc2 That was funny.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with an author and an illustrator who is a favorite among children and the young at heart. That`s a lot of us.\par \par \uc2 You may not recognize the name, Mo Willems, but there is a very good chance you`re going to recognize this guy`s work.\par \par \uc2 Willems, who began his career as a writer and animator for "Sesame Street," that`s a good way to start -- has created a series of memorable characters like Elephant and Piggy, the Naked Mole Rat and who cannot forget can Knuffle Bunny.\par \par \uc2 These days, Willems is pouring most of his creative energy into his very first picture book character the Pigeon.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Wax recently caught up with him in New York`s Coney Island to talk about this new book and why he is touring that always passionate bird the Pigeon for a bit of a loop.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: So what do you think? Should we ride it? Are you going to come with me?\par \par \uc2 As we learned recently in Coney Island --\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS (Author): Let me think about this. You`ve got to give me a moment.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Author, Mo Willems isn`t a fan of roller coasters.\par \par \uc2 But one of his characters is.\par \par \uc2 A character so beloved, the Brooklyn Cyclones recently dedicated a game night to the Pigeon.\par \par \uc2 No, not that pigeon, this one.\par \par \uc2 It was a celebration of Mo Willems latest book, "The pigeon Will Ride the Roller Coaster."\par \par \uc2 Every single type of animal or character has been covered in children`s storytelling.\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: How did you land on a pigeon?\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: Well, the Pigeon is the only character that came fully formed. I moved to Oxford, England for a month or so to write the great American picture book. I sat there and I wrote and I wrote and I wrote important meaningful stories.\par \par \uc2 And this Pigeon popped up in my notebook saying, don`t write about this. It`s terrible. Write about me. I`m awesome.\par \par \uc2 So I wrote this idea about a kid trying this stop the pigeon from driving the bus and the pigeon said, what do you need this kid for? I don`t need a co-star. Let me star.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: And star, he has. Since its debut in "Don`t Let The Pigeon Drive The Bus" nearly two decades ago, the excitable bird has found his way into the pages of Willems` 67 children`s books, alongside much loved characters like can Knuffle Bunny, the Elephant, Gerald and his friend, Piggy.\par \par \uc2 All your books feel deeply personal and deeply in the moment you create them, but I wonder how much the Pigeon is really you?\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: I aspire to piggy-ness, I am approaching Gerald-ness and I hope I have left the Pigeon behind.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: I hope.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: That might be my favorite Mo Willems` quote ever.\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: I can see it now, in all of its roller coaster-y glory.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: What I really loved is in the evolution of the Pigeon books, they became less about telling all the rest of us what to do with the Pigeon to telling the Pigeon, you have to do something you don`t want to do like Pigeon has to go to school.\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Pigeon will ride that roller coaster right behind you.\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: Yes. Every Pigeon book is that -- wants, needs, has to, will, can`t. It`s all of these sort of imposed verbs on his life. Like everything becomes a theme on them.\par \par \uc2 I will need a ticket, but it will have tickets.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: And from the first time I discovered you as a children`s author, it feels as though you`re getting more and more direct and economical in your storytelling.\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: My job, I feel very strongly is to do 49 percent of the work. I`m creating the scaffolding for the kids and the adults to put in the performance, to put in the meaning, to put in the ideas.\par \par \uc2 My job is to write incomprehensible books for illiterates.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: You`re speaking my language.\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: So, because then you bring something in, I`m in the dialogue. What I want for the grownups is to have a shame-ectomy. I actually want you to be silly because you`re my orchestra particularly with the littles.\par \par \uc2 You are the wall between the story and them because they can`t read yet. Having children, one of the blessings is, it remind you that you can be ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Kennedy Center.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: And while Willems` blend of ridiculous yet profound storytelling now extends well beyond the page, the message remains the same.\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: True story.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Why do you think you`ve been so successful at translating your work to visual mediums?\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: I respect the audience and I gravitate to projects that terrify me because that`s the excitement of it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Kennedy Center.")\par \par \uc2 MO WILLEMS: I think some people want their books to be read. I want my books to be sparks. Does it make you perform? Does it make you sing? Does it make you draw?\par \par \uc2 A book is not meant to be read. It`s meant to be read a billion times and it`s meant to create a new environment for kids and grownups to create.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: What a fun day at Coney Island with that guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There was something about him, Jamie. I like, gravitate to things that terrify him, incomprehensible books for illiterates, and I aspire to Piggy-ness. I love his personality, and the way that he thinks about his work.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: You know, he creates experiences. I mean, he -- really when I met him, I felt like I was meeting someone my children knew and that I knew because his books are as important in the spaces between the drawings and the words as they are in the drawings and the words. They are experiences.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is so right though. Parents, as you perform for your little ones when you read that good night story, you are half author of the book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When they get complicated, there are a lot of words on the page, you`re skipping through and condensing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dump in the head, and like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Skipping a page.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, gosh.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a major milestone.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Look, we are going to books --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And your kids notice when you skip a page.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: We`re going to have books for Luke and Eloise and little Jamie Duthiers who is on the way. That`s just my suggestion. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jamie Duthiers, Gayle Duthiers. Yes, she is coming. Yes, I love that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it is terrific.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a wonderful profile.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good stuff, Jamie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you Jamie. "The Pigeon Will Ride The Roller Coaster." I love the title, too. It`s on sale now.\par \par \uc2 And you can hear more of Jamie`s conversation with Mo Willems on today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2  joins us to talk about her new children`s book, how to raise gutsy girl. Separate conversation there, and her childhood in the spotlight. What it taught her about being a mom herself.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:09, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, a new documentary looks at the US and the Holocaust, including a new part of Anne Frank`s story that many people may not know.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk with the two filmmakers, Ken Burns and Lynn Novick about how US policies influenced the Nazis.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning, we are getting a look at a new documentary that examines American policies and opinions before, during, and after the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 "The US and the Holocaust" is co-directed by Ken Burns, Lynn Novick and Sarah Botstein. And in the clip you`re about to see, you can tell easily a society can change and the vital lesson we can all learn from the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL MENDELSOHN (Writer): The fragility of civilized behavior is the one thing you really learn, because these people who we now see in these photographs, these sepia photographs and they are receding into time, they`re no different -- no different from us.\par \par \uc2 You look at your neighbors, the people at the dry cleaners, the waiters in the restaurant, that`s who these people were. Don`t kid yourself.\par \par \uc2 GUY STERN: We have seen as an ideal of human behavior, and we have no guarantee that it won`t recur. If we can make that clear and graphic, and understandable, not as something to imitate, but as a warning of what can happen to human beings. Then perhaps, we have one shield against its recurrence.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Ken Burns and Lynn Novick join us now.\par \par \uc2 That is such a well-chosen clip, because it is so true. We want to imagine that we, if we were alive then, would be the heroes, the ones that stood up, the ones who shouted.\par \par \uc2 And yet the documentaries about how we as a country had that opportunity, and clearly did not shout loud enough.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS ("The US and the Holocaust" co-director): Let`s say the important thing. We`re not responsible for the Holocaust. We let in more people than any other sovereign nation, but we didn`t do anywhere near enough what we could -- even if we fulfilled the pernicious and tiny quotas, we could have had five times as many people rescued from the Holocaust, we could have shouted louder.\par \par \uc2 Our public leaders could have done that. The Congress could have revoked some of these terrible immigration -- anti-immigration laws. The American public could have woken up to the evidence that they were seeing from the very beginning of Hitler`s regime and said, "You know what? Who we are as a nation of immigrants, we`ve been built on the strength that immigrants add and we should try to get as many people in." We did none of those things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then Lynn when the US tried to shout and criticize, they would say, "Well, wait a second, what are you doing in your own country?" They would point to Mississippi, for instance, in Jim Crow, could you talk about that?\par \par \uc2 LYNN NOVICK ("The US and the Holocaust" co-director): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`d never thought about that before until looking at your documentary.\par \par \uc2 LYNN NOVICK: So, Hitler and the Nazis instituted something called the Nuremberg Laws and early in the regime to basically take away Jewish people`s rights as citizens of their country, and they used our Jim Crow laws as a model.\par \par \uc2 So they came here, and they studied how we had created a whole legal structure, Jim Crow, to make sure that African-Americans did not have the full rights of citizenship, and they were very aware of everything we were doing, and they used it as a model.\par \par \uc2 And so when we would criticize them and say, "Wait, you`re persecuting the Jews." They would turn around and say --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Back, back America. And it shows some very graphic images of that, too.\par \par \uc2 LYNN NOVICK: Yes, they are hard to look at, but it is important for us as Americans to see some of the dark parts of our own story as well.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: And we were swept up by a thing called eugenics, which was the pseudo-science that was an attempt to sort of make a hierarchy of races and ethnicities.\par \par \uc2 There is only one race and that`s the human race, and the fact is, is that this was -- became very popular. It started in Britain, very popular in United States. People subscribed to it across the board.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said, feeble minded.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: For feeble minded.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And criminals.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: Criminals -- and the idea was that you would force the sterilization of people among many aspects of eugenics. Eugenics is that mostly -- the most insidious thing is the characterization of superior races, which Hitler would borrow in this Aryan idea.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also the idea that you would force the sterilization of disabled people and of people who had mental differences and all of that sort of stuff, those laws were still in the books in 2016.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that`s now the laws, this is not a fringe idea.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: No. No. No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is taught in schools. This was passed by elected bodies. It was preached from pulpits.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: We just have to own what our part is. Obviously, we played an important part in ending the Second World War, but we also have to understand the roles in which we stood by when we could have helped at every level.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is true.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: And both are true and that`s the thing, the exceptionalism that we have has to come with, if you`re going to be exceptional, you have to be self-critical as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have to look in the mirror and see that reflection.\par \par \uc2 LYNN NOVICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Identify with that duality.\par \par \uc2 This documentary was originally supposed to end in 1965 with the shift in US immigration policy. Why did you feel like it was important to bring it all the way up until present day?\par \par \uc2 LYNN NOVICK: As we were working on the film, we`ve been working on it for the last seven years, events around us, we could see the echoes and the parallels between what was happening during the 1930s in Europe and here and what is happening all around us, and we saw things that we felt we needed to sort of pull the thread all the way through to the present.\par \par \uc2 And so yes, we did rectify some of the pernicious immigration laws in 1965, but the sort of stream of racism and antisemitism and bigotry is still part of our society, as we know, and the threat of authoritarianism and the danger of our democracy being under siege that, you know, free and fair elections and we take that for granted.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: We want to join the conversation right now. There was an urgency. This film was supposed to come out next year, but we accelerated the process. The great Holocaust scholar, Deborah Lipstadt in the film says, the time to stop a genocide is before it happens. The time to save a democracy is before it is lost.\par \par \uc2 And that, it is incumbent upon us to go and say, look, if you wanted to be in the hippest place, you know, most vibrant in arts in 1930 and 1931 and 1932, it would be Berlin. And then the next year, not so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We thank you both because they`re saying less than 30 seconds, we have to go. But Ken, I just want to say something about your look, you have a new look. We all remember you from The Beatles days. Is that look you`re trying you`re doing.\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: Well you know Tom Brokaw from another network told me that I needed to get a big boy haircut and so, I finally, after about eight or nine years of temporizing and kicking the can.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All grown up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s working for you, Ken.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, it is a little touch and --\par \par \uc2 KEN BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Some people would not accept that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will be back with "Talk of the Table" -- we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: First on CBS MORNINGS, we will reveal "Bon Appetit`s" new list of Best New Restaurants offering things like miso mac and cheese and Mexican shrimp. Oh, that sounds delicious.\par \par \uc2 You`re local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" and Tony, tell you something good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got something really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Isn`t that a good song for this?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a great song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good song for this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got something really good. Parents would want to hear this. I wish I knew this not so long ago.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so one of the hardest things for a parent to do is to soothe your baby. You`ve got the baby there, you`re holding the baby, and then you`ve got to do that slow bend into the crib, which is way down here without waking the child.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So researchers in Japan have done some work to figure out how to give yourself the best chance of success. They say do five minutes of walking, followed by eight minutes of sitting, and that will put your baby into the deepest sleep according to this small study, 21 moms and two- thirds of the babies put into the crib after this routine did not wake up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One-third still did. That`s not good news. Very frustrating. You`ve got to start again. However, a two-thirds success rate, pretty good, I`ve got to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even though the study is small, it does appear to work.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I like the idea of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you ever tried that? No?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, this is new to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`ve never heard of it either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve definitely lost my mind. You know, you`re so --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As soon as your baby hits the cover, eyes wide open, "Like what you doing?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are like, "Where are you going?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Where are you going?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know we are starting that movie right now. Katy is in the other room eating popcorn, "Come on down."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they say sometimes you`re supposed to let them cry. That`s very hard to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a bigger conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very hard to let them cry. My "Talk of the Table" is Serena Williams. Boy, did she wow the crowds the other night. This time she was at New York City`s Fashion Week.\par \par \uc2 So, she opened the "Vogue" World Runway Show with a mix of music and an interview that she did in 2000 about playing at Wimbledon when she was still a teenager.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS (Former professional tennis player): My goal is definitely not destined to be not only number one, but I want to be remembered -- I want to be remembered as someone in tennis. I don`t want to do a sport and be good and just be number one and that`s it. I want to do something that I can be remembered at. I want people to remember me as a girl who changed tennis. I just was able to bring something new to the game.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Serena, you did bring something new to the game and changed tennis. It was really awesome. The tennis great sashayed down this catwalk and a silver Balenciaga tank dress with a matching floral cape.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ain`t that great, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s fire.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was flanked by four young ball girls. They were dressed in tennis whites, they were all carrying tennis rackets.\par \par \uc2 And it was fascinating because all the supermodels were there. Carly Klaus, Kylie Jenner -- you name it. They were there sashaying down the thing. And I wonder how they were able to walk on those cobblestones in high heels. Serena did it very well.\par \par \uc2 And she was a big surprise. Nobody knew she was there. Anna Wintour knew, of course, because she planned the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when Serena came out, the crowd went nuts.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I could imagine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nuts. Nuts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beautiful outfit. I loved the little girl holding the tennis racket.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In a vogue like way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`re going to go nuts when Serena comes on this show. Okay?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If we get through the distance. Serena, come on our show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so for my "Talk of the Table" I have something that you`re seeing first on CBS MORNINGS. We`re revealing "Bon Appetit`s" new list of Best New Restaurants of 2022.\par \par \uc2 It will be published in their October issue. That list is a decade`s long tradition for Bon Appetit.\par \par \uc2 Staff members travel the country sampling foods at new eateries -- talk about a dream job. Get a place on this lineup, it can make a big difference for the restaurants.\par \par \uc2 Now, before we reveal this year`s picks, let`s take a look at what goes into making the list and what it means to be on it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Every year, there`s an enormous responsibility. You want to get it right.\par \par \uc2 You want to make sure that the restaurants you choose really represent the best and the most exciting right now.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And I think now more than ever, we`re thinking about not just, "Oh, is this food delicious?" But, what is the vibe of this place? How are the people that are working here feeling?\par \par \uc2 MAN: You really, really get a sense that for a lot of new restaurants being recognized in this way and feeling seen in this way by a publication like "Bon Appetit" is such a gratifying moment.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Being in the Hot 10 has forever changed "Kindred."\par \par \uc2 MAN: It does bring in a lot of customers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It went from like $20,000.00 a week to like $60,000.00 a week like that.\par \par \uc2 MAN: But it also is this moment where a chef or a sommelier or a restaurant owner gets to step back and realize what an amazing thing they`ve done.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It pushed me as a chef. It made me want to be better. There`s a lot of power in that list.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You start with the chef. Is the chef doing something that feels true to them?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re trying to introduce people to Laosian food, understanding that this is what we grew up with. My mom and grandma cooked this type of food for us.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: All food is culture and all culture is history.\par \par \uc2 MAN: True essence of Southeast Asian Laos cuisine is, is making things from scratch.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And so I think there`s just so much more that goes into a restaurant than you might expect from first walking in, and I think all of that goes into choosing a restaurant and knowing it`s one of the best new restaurants in the country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Bon Appetit`s" list of best new restaurants this year includes in no particular order.\par \par \uc2 Supperland in Charlotte, North Carolina. It`s described as Southern steakhouse meets church potluck. Sign me up. Cafe Mutton in Hudson, New York, which uses meats and vegetables from local farmers and foragers, and Republica in Portland, Oregon, which honors Mexican heritage.\par \par \uc2 Dawn Davis is editor-in-chief of "Bon Appetit." Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS ("Bon Appetit" editor-in-chief): Good morning. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so you`re saying that Supperland is worth a trip to Charlotte, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Absolutely. As you mentioned, it`s a steakhouse meets a potluck -- church potluck. It`s in a mid-century old church. It`s got a speakeasy and a champagne bar.\par \par \uc2 I have made the miso mac and cheese. My mother happens to be visiting and this is a woman who never changes the Thanksgiving menu, and she is putting it on the Thanksgiving menu.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And mac and cheese is one of those things that people don`t adjust.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: People don`t adjust, but modern comfort food that you can share with big parties and it is super great atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Cafe Mutton in Hudson, New York. I`d actually been there on a recommendation of a friend before this came out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What did you think, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Eloise broke a glass, so I don`t think I can go back, but they are changing the game you say. It was very good.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: They`re changing the game. The chef had been working in New York City, got tired of being told what to put on the menu, grain bowl, and avocado toast and just followed her own instincts.\par \par \uc2 She works with foragers and farmers, often using parts that other people don`t want and turns it into magic. Stews, makes her own pates. She also makes crepes by day. We have a recipe for the crepes in our issue. She also makes delicious muffins.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s really kind of an all-day place where you`re going to trust the chef`s instincts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said there`s a lot of restaurants on the list, Dawn, that you described as what-if restaurants. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Kind of, I think after the pandemic, the chefs had an opportunity to rethink what they want to be post-pandemic. So what if they could make modern comfort food that`s Korean and American and one chef, her adoptive father is Jewish, so she also has Korean and American comfort food with a nod to Jewish food as well.\par \par \uc2 So really, really interesting combination. They are bold.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Republica. Did I say that right? Republica.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Republica.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In Portland, Oregon.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: In Portland, Oregon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did they make your list?\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Five tastes, five-course tasting menu under $100.00 paying homage to the food -- the indigenous food of Mexico. So, think one course is with maize. They import it from century old farms all over Mexico and then kind of transform it here in the States.\par \par \uc2 They have a Mezcal tasting menu and wines imported from Mexico and I think wines from Mexico are really underrated. So, a really special experience there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because so you said you made the miso mac and cheese. On this list, there are recipes of places that you may not be able to make it to, but you can actually make it yourself at home.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Exactly and we`ve adapted them for the home cook. We spent a lot of time in making sure that these recipes are obtainable and something that the home cook can actually do.\par \par \uc2 If I can make the miso mac and cheese, anyone can.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is that what this pie is right here guys?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We have a home cook.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have a home cook.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that is not me by the way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shout out to you, Chris Stover.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chris Stover. Supervising producer, hope we didn`t wrong you in any way recently. This is going to be a good pie. Anything in it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, what is this, Dawn?\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: This is the extremadura custard pie. It`s from Bocadillo Market in Chicago, one of our Best 10. What I love about Bocadillo market is he fell in love -- James Martin fell in love with the food of Spain, but he also wanted to pay homage to the food from the American South.\par \par \uc2 So by day, again, you can get sandwiches, cafes, pies --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is a custard pie.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: This is a custard pie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There goes my abs.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: And he puts a little gin or vodka in the crust --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Popping buttons already.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: So, I think that gives it a little kind of bounce. Really good. And almond flour and eggs make this delicious custard really great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s really good.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: I see some smiles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is really good.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: They really did a really good job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Get it over. Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good, Dawn.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love the variety of the restaurants on your list. It`s really amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You used to call it Top 10 though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kansas City, Missouri. They`ve got a Palestinian restaurant named Baba`s -- Baba`s Pantry.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Baba`s Pantry.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is terrific.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Baba had been working for other people for over 20 years in the food industry. But his dream was to have his own place and to bring the food of Palestine to the people in Kansas City and he did that.\par \par \uc2 Some of the best hummus with shredded beef or shredded lamb if you like, it`s fantastic. And already you know we have a Top 50 list and since it made that top 50 list, he has had to close for a couple days to restock because people are loving it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I like, you went from Top 10 to just Best Restaurants.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. We --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it`s the best way --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are all foodies and we appreciate you coming, Dawn Davis. You are the best.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just want to hear her finish her sentence about why they changed from Top --\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: We changed it from Hot to Best because after the pandemic, we thought restaurants had been through so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: And it`s not just about the hot environment, it is about the story. It`s about the ambiance. And ultimately, it`s about the food.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is very good.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: And we thought, this is the best interpretation of that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chris Stover, you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it makes it sound like it is hip. This is more than just hip. This is good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s really good.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: This is hip, heart, and delicious.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And for you at home, you can head to CBS MORNINGS on your favorite social media platform to get the recipe for this pie and many others. "Bon Appetit`s "October issue hits newsstands next week.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DAVIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Dawn.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re going to check out our progressive green room. Doing all the things your mother told you not to do, talk with your mouth full, but \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2  is here. Hey, Chelsea -- to tell us about her latest book. She is raising three kids and how her relationship with her mom has changed since she became a mother. I want to hear about that.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:40. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I am very excited about this.\par \par \uc2 Former First Daughter and author, \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2 , she has written nine books for young readers. They help teach kids about everything from endangered animals to activism, and gutsy women in history.\par \par \uc2 Her new book is called -- I love this -- "Welcome to the Big Kids Club." It answers questions children ask when a new baby joins their family, like can my baby see me? Why does my baby sleep all the time? I love that they call it "my baby."\par \par \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2  as a mother of three kids, Vice Chair of \uc2 the Clinton Foundation\uc2 , and executive producer of the new docuseries "Gutsy" with her mother. Her mother, by the way is \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Chelsea. And full disclosure, I just bored Chelsea with pictures of my favorite and only grandson, Luca.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON (Author): Not a bore. Not a bore.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So this topic I think is really great. First, because I know eventually they would like to have another baby. But I remember when favorite daughter, Kirby was born, 11 months older -- 11 months later, my favorite son, Will came.\par \par \uc2 She took a look at him in one of those little baby things and then just flipped the thing over.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: Oh no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the attitude was, "How long is he staying?"\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: And when can I return him?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When is going back?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, even at 11 months, there is something that they know when a new baby comes. It changes the dynamic in the family.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so you wrote this book, why? To address all of those things?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: Thank you, Gayle. Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, I wrote this book kind of in my head when I was pregnant with Jasper, our third; because Charlotte and Aiden, our first two were four and three, and they had so many questions about the new baby and as soon as Jasper was born, Charlotte, who was four, would say, "Well, my baby." I`m like, well, my baby. I said, well, actually, it is our baby. It`s your baby, like I want you to feel this connection .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like when little kids do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: . to your baby brother. And still the conversation just continued after, you know, Jasper was home and I finally thought, we can`t be the only family who are having these conversations.\par \par \uc2 And whenever I have that thought that rattles around persistently, I think "Oh, well, I have to write a book."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Chelsea -- to me, Chelsea because you`re an only child.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: I am.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And yet you have three children of your own. Did you go through life thinking, "I wish I had a sibling?" Was that your story?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: I did. I really wanted -- well, I first wanted an older brother.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Me too.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: And then I realized, you know, maybe in kindergarten, I was like, oh, that can`t really happen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It doesn`t work that way.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: And then I really wanted just a younger brother or a younger sister and my mom had spoken about how they did try to have another child and were not able to. And I do have a memory, Gayle, of maybe being eight or nine and thinking, "oh, I should stop asking, like this is painful for my parents."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Only child, only child, only child.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I grew up in a household with four boys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I`m the oldest of four girls.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: Four boys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Four boys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I`m the oldest of four girls, and I always wanted an older brother, so I can relate to that. He is four boys. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I want to go back to the advice for parents. So advice first is, you know, buy the book. It will help.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: Tony --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It will help ease the path. What other advice do you have for families who are going to welcome a new addition into the family?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: You know, I think really to listen to our kids, so that it doesn`t feel like this is being imposed on them, although of course it sort of is, but you know, listening to them, listening to their questions, their concerns. So, they do feel like this is a family decision. This is a family kind of journey of our family being expanded. And being honest, when we don`t know the answers.\par \par \uc2 You know, when I was talking to other kids, not just my kids about kind of what they would want to see in the book, you know, I got asked questions like, well, you know, when can the baby get its own pet?\par \par \uc2 Like, you know, I don`t know, and you should definitely talk to your parents about whether you`re going to get that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: Don`t blame \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2  and they`ll be like, "Well, she said I could get a dog."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: So just being candid about what we know and what we don`t know and what we are going to discover together about our new baby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of the challenges for parents is there`s the new baby in the family, but then that baby grows up and at a certain point, there`s a new adult in the family and as a parent, you`ve got to deal with your children as full-fledged people on their own, potty-trained, as you say and employed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s your relationship like with your mom these days as you`ve become an independent person --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Potty-trained and employed.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: I mean --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am assuming, I don`t know. It is not on the clip her.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: You know, feel free to assume that, I think, thankfully, Tony. I have such a good relationship with my mom. I am so proud of what we`ve done with "Gutsy" and I also just had so much fun working with her as an adult, and it just candidly felt like such a gift to be able to do that during COVID, to spend this really special time together when I know so many families were separated and we got to be able to do this work together.\par \par \uc2 It was just a joy producing every day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is Hillary Clinton`s grandma name?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: My children call my mom, Grandma and my dad, Pop-Pop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what kind of grandmother is she?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON\uc2 : Oh, very hands on, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does she give advice?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: That won`t surprise you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you like it?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON\uc2 : Well, I don`t know if she gives advice directly to my children, but she gives me a lot of advice. And sometimes I`m like, thank you so much. Sometimes I`m like that was like circa 1980. We don`t do that any longer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I get that, too. I get that too.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Chelsea. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CLINTON: Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The name of the book is "Welcome to the Big Kids Club." It`s on sale wherever you like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. You know time flies when you`re having fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wouldn`t you agree?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s true, and when you have pie or that cake.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pie is very good. We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Delicious.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220914ei9e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Battle For The Midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Ed O`Keefe \par \uc2 985 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turning to politics and President Biden who is hitting the road ahead of the coming midterms with a stark new message. Among the events, a primetime address on Thursday outside Philadelphia`s Independence Hall on what the White House is calling "The Continued Battle for the Soul of the Nation."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden is expected to argue that democracy itself is on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the White House for us once again.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Tony, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 You know, we haven`t seen much of the President in recent weeks between his bout with COVID-19, his own two-week summer vacation, and the fact that the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s search of former President Donald Trump`s Florida estate has been dominating the headlines.\par \par \uc2 But the current President is back, hitting the road today and headed to a State that he believes is key to Democratic chances of winning in November.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President Biden is gearing up for a busy full travel schedule designed to tout his party`s accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN(President of the United States): And to state the obvious, there is a lot at stake.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Mr. Biden`s travels begin today with the first of three stops he will make in Pennsylvania in the next week. Democrats believe the Keystone State`s open races for Senate and Governor, plus several competitive House races are key to their chances this November.\par \par \uc2 In Wilkes-Barre today, the President is scheduled to speak on crime and gun violence.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I`m determined to ban assault weapons in his country.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Then Thursday, he is set to deliver a primetime address outside Philadelphia`s Independence Hall on "The Continued Battle for the Soul of the Nation."\par \par \uc2 And on Labor Day, the President will visit Pittsburgh. That day, he will also stop in Wisconsin; and later next week, Ohio, two more big Senate and gubernatorial battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "What do we want? Abortion Rights.")\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: As the fall campaign begins, the future of abortion rights is emerging as a big motivator. Likely voters say their vote for Congress this year will be to support abortion rights rather than oppose them.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  has made his dangerous ideas on abortion easy to understand.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Democrats are now filling the airwaves with ads targeting Republicans on the issue.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Supreme Court supported by Republicans just overturned Roe vs. Wade endangering women`s health care choice across America.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And that has Republicans like Washington State candidate, Tiffany Smiley, trying to preempt Democratic attacks.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY SMILEY (R) (Washington State Senate candidate): I`m pro-life, but I oppose the Federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now, it is important to point out, our poll also finds that the top issue of concern remains the economy and inflation, that`s why you`re going to hear Republican candidates predominantly talking about issues of dollars and cents; Democrats will do it too, but they`ll point out that inflation is starting to ease, the unemployment rate is in good shape.\par \par \uc2 And they`ll also, of course, Tony, continue talking about those issues of social concern, including abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s talk about the overall issue of the President`s approval rating. In the State of Pennsylvania, where he was born and partially raised, it is not only below 50 percent, it is below 40 percent according to a recent poll. So yes, two critical races there for Senate and Governor. But I guess the calculation is he`s going to make a difference in the positive way.\par \par \uc2 Why is he going there now?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, he has been to Pennsylvania more than a dozen times as President. It is a place he goes often to discuss politics.\par \par \uc2 Part of what they`re trying to do here at the White House is boost his standing among Democrats overall. Part of the reason his numbers have slipped so much is because his numbers slipped with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And so, if he can bring those back up, our Battleground Tracker shows us as well, then his numbers overall improve. That`s why you`re seeing him talk in the next week about three issues important to Democratic voters -- gun control, a ban on assault weapons, the fate of democracy and then workers issues when he is in Pittsburgh on Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 You bring up the numbers among Democrats, you boost his numbers, you hopefully boost Democratic turnout. White House hopes that`s the key to victory in November.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we`ll be following it. Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s see if that strategy works.\par \par \uc2 One of the two people killed in a supermarket shooting in Bend, Oregon on Sunday is being hailed as a hero. Authorities say that 66-year-old, Donald Ray Surrett, Jr. who worked in the supermarket tried to disarm the gunman.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @IAmDaveFelton.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Terrified shoppers and employees ran for cover when the suspect entered the store and just began shooting an assault rifle.\par \par \uc2 Police say that Surrett, who was an Army veteran may have prevented more deaths from happening. An 84-year-old customer was also shot and killed in the store.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A hero indeed.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220831ei8u0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For August 28, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Strassmann, Debora Patta, Robert Costa, Michael Morell,  Anthony Salvanto, Major Garrett \par \uc2 9,232 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I`m Major Garrett in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Alarming information surfaces from the affidavit justifying the search of former President Trump`s Florida home.\par \par \uc2 The 38-page document was heavily redacted, but its release at this early stage of the investigation very unusual. Already, we`re starting to see the impact of this historic \uc2 FBI\uc2  search on the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 One key question, how damaging was it that highly classified material, according to the government, was improperly stored at Mar-a-Lago. Another question, is Trump`s legal jeopardy increasing? We will have the latest.\par \par \uc2 Our CBS News Battleground Tracker shows Trump`s true believers are still with him, but for how long? What about voters troubled by these recent revelations?\par \par \uc2 Both sides are gearing up for a blistering fall campaign, as can be heard on the campaign trail for Florida governor.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida): We will never, ever surrender to the woke agenda. Florida is the state where woke goes to die.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2  (D-Florida): This governor couldn`t care less about your freedom. He`s abusive. He is a bully. He is a bully. And he`s dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Nationally, the political landscape appears to have shifted in the last month. Democrats overperformed in some races last Tuesday, as midterm primaries near the end.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): Republicans should be very, very, very scared this morning about their prospects.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: One factor helping Democrats, abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): MAGA Republicans don`t have a clue about the power of women.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Let me tell you something. They are about to find out.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Will that momentum last to November? We will talk to the head of the Democratic Party, DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , and Maryland Republican Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, we will have the latest from Ukraine, as the threat of a disaster grows as Russian and Ukrainian forces trade fire near the continent`s largest nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 Finally, nearly 50 years since NASA`s last trip to the moon, we will preview Monday`s launch of America`s next moonshot, the first rocket of the Artemis mission. We will talk with astronaut and Artemis team member Kate Rubins.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, everyone. Welcome to Face the Nation. Margaret is out and we hope in the final stages of recovery from COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 August has been -- there`s really no other way to say it -- an extraordinary month here in Washington. And one of our tasks today is to work to understand the legal and national security implications of Friday`s release of a redacted affidavit outlining the justification behind the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s retrieval of classified documents stored at former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago resort.\par \par \uc2 To help us, CBS News chief election and campaign correspondent Robert Costa is back from West Palm Beach, Florida. And we have also brought in Michael Morell, former acting director of the CIA, now a CBS News national security contributor.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Bob, I want to start with you.\par \par \uc2 Outline for the audience what you see as the potential legal peril for the former president.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The affidavit from the government spells it out.\par \par \uc2 There have been tensions between the Trump legal team and federal investigators for months about his handling of classified material. The affidavit mentions possible obstruction. It also mentions how Trump had in possession, in their view, documents that were highly sensitive, that could even deal with information derived from human intelligence sources.\par \par \uc2 And this led to them to have an \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of the property Mar-a-Lago just weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And there is a political dimension to this, of course.\par \par \uc2 How do you assess that? And you were down in West Palm Beach for the entire week. What is the atmosphere there?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It injects uncertainty into the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, this is the standard-bearer of their party. Even though he`s former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he is still someone who`s eying 2024 presidential bid. So many candidates in the party are echoing his version of politics. Now to have him facing legal challenges across the board, not just in Florida, adds that uncertainty to the discussion.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And one thing that happened recently, a judge reviewing a request from the former president for a special master, put in a legal document that this judge is possibly inclined to do so. Does that change anything, from your vantage point?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: To be determined at this point. We will watch in the coming days whether a so-called special master or neutral party is appointed by a Florida federal judge to review and return the evidence. They have asked the government to provide a list of information. It could move in that direction.\par \par \uc2 But let`s remember the government already has a filter team in place at the Justice Department reviewing what they collected weeks ago. So this is a late entry into the legal discussion.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: \uc2 Mike Morell\uc2 , evaluate the affidavit for our audience, please.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Major, I had two reactions when I read it.\par \par \uc2 The first was the fact that these documents were mixed in with unclassified documents. You had classified documents in the vast majority of the boxes. That suggested to me a sloppiness in the handling of classified documents at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The two White Houses that I know best, the Bush White House and the Obama White House, there were very rigorous and strict protocols with regard to the handling of classified information, where it was. Records were kept. Retrievals were made. That`s what normally happens. That didn`t happen in this case, it sounds to me.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that jumped out at me were the markings HCS, HUMINT control system, and S.I., special intelligence. HUMINT control system means information from CIA spies. And special intelligence means information from technical operations of the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2 . This is the -- this is the most sensitive material of the United States intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, the next natural question, it seems to me, is, how vulnerable to compromise were the documents you were just talking about and were outlined in this affidavit?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: So, I think they were vulnerable, even at the White House, since they seem to have been mishandled at the White House as well, right? We have to look at that, as well as Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 And, as the damage assessment goes forward, I think they need to look at both of those places. Not everyone at the White House has a top secret clearance. So you have to worry about who had access to those documents who didn`t have a clearance to do so.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the vulnerability from foreign intelligence services, a little context. If you look back at the history of espionage in the United States, you will see a number of Americans who were charged and convicted of espionage.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at how long they spied before they were caught, and you do all of that math, what you what you learn is that, at any given moment in time, they`re on average four Americans spying for foreign intelligence services without us knowing it at the time. And those are the ones we ultimately caught.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a lot of spying going on in Washington, right? And if you`re a foreign intelligence service, and you want to target the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , what`s the number one place you want to target? The White House.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You mentioned your experience with the Bush and \uc2 Obama\uc2  White Houses.\par \par \uc2 There`s a procedure inside the building, obviously, for classified and secure documents. Is there a similar process off-site for any president, meaning at a place like Mar-a-Lago or for President Biden right now when he goes back to Delaware?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: So, there are things called SCIFs, sensitive compartmented information locations, that are actually approved for holding classified information. I had one in my attic when I was the deputy director.\par \par \uc2 And you`re allowed to hold classified there. But these are places that are approved by security officers, right?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And, if they`re not, then they don`t follow procedures, and they may not follow federal law?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Correct. And you may be at risk in those cases of mishandling classified information.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And, Mike, this is a question that circles around this relentlessly. Is there a formalized process for a president to declassify classified information?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Unfortunately not. There are statutes that allow the president to declassify information. The Supreme Court has upheld those statutes a number of times.\par \par \uc2 But those statutes do not outline a step-by-step process for the president to do so. So it`s murky. I actually know a case from the Bush White House where President Bush declassified part of the 2002 Iraq WMD national intelligence estimate, so Scooter Libby could use that information at his grand jury testimony. And President Bush did that without ever telling the intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 So, presidents can do this, right? But there`s an appropriate way to do it. And the appropriate way to do it is to paper it over, right, to have the president sign a document that says: I hereby declassify this information.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: With important reporting and context, Bob Costa, \uc2 Mike Morell\uc2 , thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Our new CBS Battleground Tracker finds Republicans are still leading the race for control of the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2 , but Democrats are gaining momentum. In July, our estimate was that Republicans stood to win 230 seats, 12 more than the 218 needed for a majority. Today, Republicans would stand to win 226, still a majority, but a slimmer one, after Democrats narrowed the gap last month.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to CBS News elections and surveys director Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 So, this is not a prediction. It`s an estimate, but it appears that the prospects Republicans once thought of a big red wave election appeared to have dimmed. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, there are some breakwaters in here, to continue your metaphor, against that wave.\par \par \uc2 And we learn, when we talk to people across all the districts -- because, remember, this is a contest for 435 seats -- is that they are abortion. It is a little bit of improvement in the economy and prospects, so a little bit of improvement for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and then, three, the Trump factor.\par \par \uc2 Let me take those in order. Let me start with abortion, because that really stands out. Democrats now say that is very important to their vote, even more so than the economy. And then we see gains for the Democrats among this key voting bloc, college-educated women. They live in swing districts. Democrats have rebounded with them.\par \par \uc2 They have always counted in the last two cycles on that group. So that`s important. And then related, there`s this view among women, among independents that should the Republicans gained Congress, that the Republicans would prioritize abortion restrictions. So that`s how that all ties together on the abortion front for Democrats, Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, one of the problems Democrats have had structurally all year is President Biden`s low approval ratings. Is there anything in this data that suggests those have begun to turn?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: And some of those approval ratings have come from lower approval among his own base, among Democrats. So that`s the story coming in.\par \par \uc2 But now we see a little bit of improvement there for him. So, his overall numbers are up. That`s bolstered by a sort of return, the sort of coming home from a lot of Democrats, and that`s tied to the economy, so his handling of gas prices, of inflation a little bit better, certainly from Democrats saying that.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things we have seen is that his numbers have been tied to those gas prices. It`s often how people think about the economy, right, what they`re paying, right, right up there at the cash register or the pump.\par \par \uc2 And then the other part of this is the Democratic base shoring up with young people, maybe some of that related to support for the debt relief. But you do see a little bit of that bounce-back among Democrats, and that`s helping him.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Anthony, history tells us midterm elections, new presidency, almost always a referendum on the current occupant of the White House. That would be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , obviously.\par \par \uc2 But there`s data, I understand, that suggests that former President Trump still looms large in this midterm conversation.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, this midterm is different. And I will say that a lot over the next few months. But one way it is, is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , former president, is still a factor, voters tell us, for most people in their vote choice, one way or the other, positive or negative.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s look right now at the effect of Mar-a-Lago and the search, first of all, big difference in partisan views. For Republicans, this is a political attempt to damage the former president. For Democrats, for independence, it`s looking after national security, OK? So that`s number one.\par \par \uc2 But then what happens politically? Republicans want their leaders to defend \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on this and stay with him. That`s seven in 10. It`s a big number. But, for everybody else, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ends up being a net negative in that sense, for Democrats, for independents, in the vote.\par \par \uc2 The other part of this, Major, is, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was endorsing candidates throughout the primary process. We pick up now from voters a feeling among key groups that the Republicans have nominated candidates who they feel are more extreme than the Democrats have nominated in general.\par \par \uc2 And that`s part of that effect beyond the Republican base that you see from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Historically, midterm or general election, economy front and center. There has been some, as you have outlined, encouraging signs for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And, look, they`re desperate to find encouraging signs. They haven`t had any for months. But yet the economy still appears to be difficult for them. True?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, people still say it`s not good, even if there`s some improvement. And Republicans are winning the voters for whom the economy is most important.\par \par \uc2 And that is underpinning that Republican gain, however large we estimate it is at this point, right? So that`s number one. Number two is, Republicans are still seen as more likely to prioritize inflation if they gain Congress. And that`s a gap that the Democrats still have not filled.\par \par \uc2 And maybe they`re not going to erase that because there`s a year of voter frustration behind it, right, behind that for the Democrats, all of which is to say, Major, again, what is this election about? That`s often the most important question. If it`s about the economy, the Republicans have an advantage, if it`s about all these other things we have been talking about, maybe some hope for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And very quickly, Anthony, energy on both sides.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Energy, enthusiasm is up. Enthusiasm is high.\par \par \uc2 At this point, very strong turnout would be what we`d be looking at here.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Anthony Salvanto, always a pleasure to be with you. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Please stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We go now to the head of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 . He joins us from Columbia, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chairman, great to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2  (\uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  Chairman): Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, you heard in our battleground tracker that there`s momentum Democrats have.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that momentum is real? And is it so real, Mr. Chairman, you`re prepared to predict this morning, Democrats will retain their slender majority in the House of Representatives?\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: That momentum is real.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve been predicting since I became chairman that Democrats are going to keep their majorities in the House, that they`re going to grow the majorities in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , and we`re going to pick up some governor`s mansions along the way.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, our battleground tracker shows that 33 percent of the country believes Democrats have a plan to battle inflation, meaning the vast majority of Americans don`t believe Democrats have a plan to deal with inflation, the number one economic issue in the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know you`ll probably refer to the Inflation Reduction Act, Mr. Chairman. I invite you to do that. But also polling tells us that, after the American Rescue Plan and the infrastructure plan, either you didn`t sell it or the American people didn`t give Democrats credit for it.\par \par \uc2 What`s going to be different?\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: Well, listen, in the end of the day, this is about the accomplishments of this administration.\par \par \uc2 And they`re vast. When you take a look in a historical perspective of what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been able to do in two years, there`s some presidents who`ve served for eight years can`t add up to what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been able to do, the Inflation Reduction Act, the American rescue plan, the bipartisan infrastructure law, the PACT Act helping our veterans, Violence Against Women Act reauthorization, the CHIPS and Science Act.\par \par \uc2 I can go on and on and on. And we`re seeing record job growth. We`re seeing the lowest unemployment that we`ve seen. And this is -- this is the package that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done, because his focus has been on improving the standing of all of America`s people.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we`ve done. That`s part of our plan. And we`re seeing gas prices right now go down. We`re seeing now that seniors are so excited that prescription drugs costs are going to go down, capped at $2,000. For the first time ever, Medicare`s going to get an opportunity to negotiate lower prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 We would have gotten insulin prices down for all Americans, but the Republicans voted to take that out of the legislation. So, in the end of the day, Major, what we`re seeing is that the American people are waking up to how Democrats under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have been delivering for the American people, while the Republicans definitely don`t have a plan.\par \par \uc2 They -- the only plan they do have is how to gut Social Security and Medicare. You can ask \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  about that. But Democrats are fighting for the American people each and every day.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mr. Chairman, the President took pains to highlight abortion as an issue in the midterm elections this week.\par \par \uc2 When you think about the history of these midterm elections, when that history is written, will abortion or the economy be more important?\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: I think abortion is going to be extremely important, and because the reason why so many people come to America is because we are the land of freedom. It`s liberty and justice for all.\par \par \uc2 And abortion is about privacy and freedom, freedom of women to control their own bodies. For the first time in 50 years, these extreme MAGA Republicans have chipped away at a freedom that we`ve had as American people. And women are upset about it. Men are upset about it. And you`re seeing it in special election after special election.\par \par \uc2 You saw it in Kansas, that people are rising up, because they know it`s a slippery slope. You take away privacy and freedom rights. You are chipping away at voting rights. You`re going after people`s freedom of speech. I mean, that is not who America is.\par \par \uc2 But that`s who the MAGA extremists in the Republican Party want America to be. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is standing in that gap and saying, you will not do this. You will not do -- President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is there: You will not do this on my watch.\par \par \uc2 It`s about protecting freedom. And that`s what the Democratic Party is doing each and every day.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mr. Chairman, earlier this week, the president referred to the MAGA, Make America Great, agenda as almost like -- quote -- "semi- fascism.\par \par \uc2 You heard the President`s inaugural address, the same way I did. In that inaugural address, President Biden said we should not view each other as adversaries in this country, but as neighbors, and we should treat each other with dignity and respect.\par \par \uc2 How does semi-fascism as a label for the Republican Party fit with that inaugural address?\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: Well, the one thing that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been is always been consistent, and he has always been somebody who does what my grandfather used to do, which is speak it plain, say it plain to the American people.\par \par \uc2 And what we see right now is a full frontal attack by these extreme MAGA Republicans in this country. And that extreme attack in our freedoms...\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And so you -- you -- Mr. Chairman, you embrace...\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: ... freedoms as a people.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mr. Chairman, you embrace the rhetoric semi-fascism to describe the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: Well, it`s not about the embracing. It`s calling what it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 In the end of the day, we are a country built on freedom. And when you chip away at that, when you see the bullying that takes place in place like Florida with DeSantis, when you see them chip away at privacy rights, when they try to demonize the other, the attacks on transgender kids and their families, the attacks on marriage equality that we`re hearing from the Supreme Court, this is not who America is.\par \par \uc2 We`re about freedom and rights for all of America`s people, not just a select few. But the Republicans are turning a blind eye, this extreme agenda. And this is what this election is all about. It is the great contrast between a party, the Democratic Party, that is standing up for the hopes and aspirations of the American people and protecting our rights as Americans and our freedoms.\par \par \uc2 And a Republican party that is focused on fear, that`s focused on fraud, that is focused on just getting power.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mr. Chairman...\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: I mean, what party do you ever see...\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mr. Chairman...\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: Did you ever think that the party of Reagan would actually celebrate when they stalled the PACT Act in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 But that`s what we have in this -- in today`s Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mr. Chairman, will President Biden run for reelection and should he?\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: The president has consistently said that his intention is to run for president in the United States. And I can tell you, the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  will be fully behind him and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This president has a record of achievement, and America needs President Biden to continue that effort.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Can you tell us what the status of the Iowa caucuses is? The \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  has continued to delay a decision about that. It appears that Iowa will lose its first-in-the nation caucus status. True?\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: No, that is not true.\par \par \uc2 Listen, in the end of the day, the DNC is focused on these midterm elections, and we`re going to allow the presidential cycle schedule to be determined after we`re done. And the end of the day, for us at the DNC...\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mr. Chairman, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JAIME HARRISON: ... it`s about money -- we`ve raised record amounts of it -- message and mobilization.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Thank you for that. Thank you. Mr. Chairman, thank you for that. I know Democrats in Iowa will listen to you very, very carefully.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chairman, thanks so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 We are joined now by Maryland Republican Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN (R-Maryland): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, you heard my conversation with the DNC Chair Harrison about semi-fascism as a label that President Biden has applied to Republicans. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: You know, I think it`s that kind of divisive rhetoric on both sides that`s really bad for America.\par \par \uc2 And I have been talking about the toxic politics, and when -- if Republicans are calling Democrats socialists and communists and we have the president of the United States calling Republicans fascist, I don`t think it adds to the -- the overall discussion. We ought to just talk about the differences we have on the issues and focus on the problems that most people in America want us to focus on.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Real quickly, do you see any strains of authoritarianism in the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Well, there`s no question we see some -- some signs of that. And I`m one of the ones speaking out.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Very good.\par \par \uc2 Governor Hogan, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Please stay with us. We will continue our conversation after a quick break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Here`s a promise. We will be right back with Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 . And we will also have a preview of the Artemis launch.\par \par \uc2 Please stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION. I`m Major Garrett, in for Margaret.\par \par \uc2 We continue our conversation with Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, we started the show talking about not only the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search at Mar- a-Lago but the affidavit that was released late this week. Many Republicans believe this is a political effort to harm and tarnish the reputation of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with them? And do you think the government has been transparent enough in this extraordinary set of events?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, it is an extraordinary set of events. It`s never happened before with a former president. And so, the very first day, I called for more transparency. And I -- I thought it was a good idea for the attorney general to finally come out and make comments. I thought it was a good idea for them to release some of the information on the affidavit.\par \par \uc2 But we still don`t know a whole lot. I mean most of it was redacted. It didn`t give us a lot of color. And so I think some Republicans are saying, without you showing us more to it, we don`t - we think it may be political.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Are you satisfied?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : No, I mean, I`d like to see more transparency because --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: What would you like to see that you haven`t seen yet?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, it`s hard to tell because in a - in a federal investigation they`ve got to keep some things confidential.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you want to know more about what the documents were?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : We want to know whether or not it was justified or not. And so, on the one hand, it could be, as some Republicans think, just a - you know, just a political witch hunt. On the other hand, it could be really serious, you know, federal felonies that we don`t know about yet.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Where do you come down on that question, witch hunt or legitimate exercise of the federal government`s legitimate, not only curiosity, but prosecutorial interest in the handling of sensitive documents.\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, it`s hard for me to imagine that -\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You haven`t made up your mind on that?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, I think -- I don`t know that we have enough information yet. I think - it`s hard to believe that the Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  would take steps unless they had something pretty serious that they were investigating. We just don`t know the facts yet.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you think there`s any Republican hypocrisy remembering the "lock her up" chants from 2016 about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and her email server?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, I think so, but I think there`s also, you know, some of the argument the Republicans are making is that they didn`t really take these kind of actions - these steps when --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Then and they`re taking them now?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Yes, then, but they are now. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, you - so you side with Republicans who are still very deeply skeptical about this?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : I understand why it`s dividing the country and why Democrats and Republicans view it differently.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: As I understand your travel schedule, Governor, you`re heading to New Hampshire again. You`ve been there before. You`ve been to Iowa. When are you announcing your 2024 bid?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, maybe this morning. No, I`m - I`m just teasing. I -\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Go right ahead, Governor.\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : I`m just going to finish my term as - as governor. In January -\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Well, why are you going to Iowa and New Hampshire?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, I`m going to New Hampshire to help the Republican caucus, the House Republican caucus up there. I`m helping candidates across the country. But, you know, it`s great to get out and see --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You are looking at it very seriously, true?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : I think it`s -- that`s probably an exaggeration. I think we`re going to finish the term as governor -\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Lukewarm looking at it?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Lukewarm maybe, lukewarm.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Very good.\par \par \uc2 In that context, as you think about what you may or may not do on the national stage, how either alarming or maybe giving you moment for pause was it to see your preferred Republican gubernatorial candidate, Kelly Schultz, lose in a Republican primary for governor in your own state of Maryland? What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : What was really -- it was really sad. And it`s what I`ve been talking about for two years, that, you know, this should be a really huge year for Republicans just because of the failures of the Democrats and -- who are in control of everything and Biden`s low approval ratings. But we could blow it by nominating unelectable people. And that`s exactly what`s happening across the country and why the wave is going to be more of a - of a ripple rather than a tidal wave.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You called the winner in that Republican gubernatorial primary, Dan Cox, a QAnon whack job and a nut. I take it you`re endorsing the Democratic nominee, Wes Moore?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : No, I`m not endorsing anybody in the race.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Why not?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : But what I`m doing is helping --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: But you`re bashing all the Republican nominees. Isn`t that the same thing?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, I`ve just told the truth. I - you know, when people ask me a question, I usually give them a direct answer. And I, you know, made it very clear that this guy should not be the nominee. He shouldn`t be governor. But I`m not getting involved in endorsing in the race.\par \par \uc2 But this is just -- not just Maryland, this is happening across the country. This is something that it`s why \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is saying they`re not -- we may not win the Senate. It`s why we were hoping to pick up seats in governors` races and now we`re not.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: It`s curious to me --\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : It`s why we`re -- the margin in the House is so much smaller.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: It`s curious to me, though, Governor, I mean you won election and then you won re-election with a good number of Democrats in a blue state supporting you. They look to you in this question. Are you going to sit out and not tell them who you think the next governor of Maryland should be?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, I think they already have their mind - I think they already have their mind made up. But, yes, Maryland`s only got 23 percent Republican. You know, and I had to win, you know, a big -- almost all the Republicans and independents and 25 percent of the Democrats to win. But that -- this candidate is not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, is, for you, Governor Hogan, for the remainder of your active political life, election denialism a litmus test?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, it`s certainly something that we shouldn`t be focused on. Look, I think the voters are going to decide on issues, like inflation, like the out of control crime --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I mean would you endorse someone who denied that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the 2020 election?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : No. No, I would not.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Would you actively campaign against that Republican?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, it depends on who they`re running against, whether it was a primary or not. But I`ve been supporting folks all across the country in Republican primaries that were running against candidates like that because I think if the Republicans are to get any power back, we`re going to have to start talking about the issues people care about and not relitigating what happened in 2020 or, you know, denying things that are fact.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, I want to talk about something that`s somewhat topical in Pennsylvania, in the governor`s race there. GOP nominee \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  posed for a picture in 2017 wearing a confederate uniform. Now, this was part of a faculty photo at the Army War College.\par \par \uc2 Now, Mastriano has a Ph.D. in history. The district he represents in the legislature includes Gettysburg. Is this disqualifying in your estimation?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, it`s the first I`ve heard the story and I don`t know the circumstances around it, but these are the kinds of problems that I think we`re having. We have a similar situation in Maryland with an attorney general candidate whose a (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Not disqualifying, but problematic?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : It`s very problematic and could be disqualifying.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Could be disqualifying. Why? Tell -- explain to voters who are like, what`s the big deal? What would you say (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, again, I said, I don`t know the facts regarding that, but this is a - you know, just not the kind of way that Republicans are going to win races, that`s for sure. And that`s my big concern and what I`ve been talking about for two years.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, what is your assessment as governor of Maryland of the president`s decision on student loan debt relief? Did he have the authority? Is it inflationary? Support/against?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : I`m not sure whether he had the authority or not, but I think it was the wrong solution to what is a real problem, but the wrong solution at the wrong time because, I agree, it`s like throwing gasoline on a fire. Inflation is out of control. And, you know, it`s an issue we`ve tried to solve in Maryland by holding a line on tuition, by giving, you know, scholarships to community colleges, by removing the requirement for four- year degrees, by trying to make interest deductible on your student loans off of your taxes. But just handing out money to people and being unfair to the people who worked hard to pay off their debt and pouring that money into the inflationary economy is really bad.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Back to politics. If Republicans underperform in these midterms, how much of that will be credibly attributed and blamed on former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Well, I think the focus away from the issues and onto President Trump and away from the future and back to the past is going to hurt Republicans. It`s a matter of how bad it`s going to be.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: What are your fears?\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : My fear is that we don`t - we don`t win the Senate, that we don`t pick up gubernatorial seats and potentially lose them, and that -- I think we`ll still take the House, but it`s going to be closer than people think.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Republican Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , a pleasure, sir.\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 We are joined again by directors of elections and surveys, Anthony Salvanto, and chief election and campaign correspondent Robert Costa.\par \par \uc2 Bob, I want to start with you. In your conversations with Republicans quite separate from the drama at Mar-a-Lago, what is the Republican sentiment? Do they feel they are losing momentum?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: There`s alarm inside of the Republican Party. Just weeks after the vote in Kansas on abortion rights, you now see the Democrats passing the climate and spending bill and starting to gain traction. They`re also starting to frame the Republican Party in a sharper way on the issue of democracy, running hard against election deniers and even President Biden, taking a new step in terms of his rhetoric, referring to the Republican Party, as you said, as semi-fascist.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Anthony, does our data tell us anything about the possible strength of that kind of rhetoric or any warning signs possibly for the president to go not maybe that far.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We went through the primaries, in one after another, and voters for the Republican side were saying they wanted the party not only to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but they wanted Donald Trump`s endorsement behind a candidate and they also wanted Republican candidates to be talking about things like the 2020 election. All of that, other voters tell us, is out of sync with what the larger electorate wants to talk about, the economy, that`s the number one issue, inflation, the number one issue. So, it`s not just that kind of rhetoric, but it`s the subject matter too.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And you heard the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  Chairman \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  say the president has got this tremendous set of accomplishments, he should run for re-election, the DNC will be fully behind him, and yet our data and other data continues to show Democrats aren`t yet sold on that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, no, because they`ve got a lot of ground to make up, number one. Number two, on the House -\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: But they`re not even sold on Biden as a re-election candidate.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, that -\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Specifically.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, that may be -- look, a president is always a factor, as we`ve discussed, and Biden is certainly a factor. The question is, can they motivate Democrats to actually go out and support him? And what we have seen, this is changing, but what we have seen so far is Democrats a little bit more reluctant to do that than Republicans who are sort of chomping at the bit to go and vote against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTAS: There`s no one chomping at the bit based on my reporting to run against President Biden in 2024 in a Democratic primary. One of his rivals from 2020, Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , has been in lock-step politically most of the time with President Biden when it comes to the big picture on policy.\par \par \uc2 And look at the comments just this past week from California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  comparing President Biden to FDR in terms of his governing accomplishments. Newsom`s widely seen as a future presidential candidate, but he`s not making any move to challenge Biden. In fact, he`s praising Biden.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And looking to maybe dial back the sense that some Democrats had a month or a month and a half ago that he was trying to position himself just in case. Looked like he was dialing that - dialing that back a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, what to watch in the next couple of weeks?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Oh, start with the Senate. We`ve got to - we`ve got to start looking at the Senate. We`ll be polling in a lot of these races. And whether folks have -- the Senate is different from the House in that it is going to be more candidate focused. And it comes back to what I was just saying about, have Republicans nominated candidates that can appeal to a wider electorate? I think we`re going to see tests of that in Pennsylvania. We`re going to see tests of that in Georgia. We`re going to watch Arizona, among others. These will become household state names, if you will, as we go through this campaign.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Bob, in that regard, Nevada, Colorado, Arizona, Pennsylvania, Ohio, all test cases for the Trump effect and this extremism label.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTAS: And there`s a tension inside of the GOP I`m picking up in my reporting between what I would call the McConnell world of the GOP, the long-time Republican business friendly establishment in power in the Senate, versus Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who runs the national Republicans campaign on the NRSC.\par \par \uc2 There`s a strategic sense that Scott is aligned with Trump and Trumpism inside the Republican Party and that he`s embraced candidates who might not have the best shot to win. So now you see McConnell and his super PAC arm, the Senate Leadership Fund, trying to do their own thing, not necessarily everything that Rick Scott`s doing at the NRSC.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Intraparty maneuvering, it`s not uncommon.\par \par \uc2 Bob Costa, Anthony Salvanto, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We turn now to the war in Ukraine, where fears are growing over a potential nuclear disaster at Zaporizhzhia, the largest nuclear power plant in Europe.\par \par \uc2 CBS News foreign correspondent Debora Patta has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: There are fresh warnings of the risk of radioactive leaks at the Russian occupied Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant. Ukraine and Russia have traded accusations of renewed shelling there this weekend, and, on Thursday, the plant was down to a precarious last resort when it was disconnected from the grid due to fire damage. For more than 24 hours, the plant was operating on backup diesel generators.\par \par \uc2 HERMAN HALUSHCHENKO (Ukraine Energy Minister): Frankly, that is one step before the catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Energy Minister Herman Halushchenko explained a constant electricity supply is critical for cooling down spent nuclear fuel and avoiding a disastrous meltdown. If the backup generators fail, it sets in motion a chain reaction.\par \par \uc2 HERMAN HALUSHCHENKO: We have probably an hour and a half, two hours before the reaction started inside the - the unit.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (on camera): So we narrowly escaped nuclear disaster?\par \par \uc2 HERMAN HALUSHCHENKO: I must say that we were not far. Not far.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Ukrainians living in the shadow of the reactors prepare for a worst-case scenario, and authorities are handing out iodine tablets to help protect against radiation.\par \par \uc2 The minister is hopeful the United Nations nuclear watchdog will inspect the site early this week.\par \par \uc2 HERMAN HALUSHCHENKO: From our point of view, it`s very important to create something to like permanent mission. Not just to come there to check and to leave.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: It`s been just over half a year since \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  did the unthinkable and invaded Ukraine. In the east, war-weary residents still wake up every day to a living hell. They count the dead, clear the debris.\par \par \uc2 The Russians are torturing us, said Katiana Veahintza (ph). We`re exhausted by the endless shelling. But we are still alive.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: As the war stretches beyond the halfway mark, Ukrainians are digging in their heels, prepared for the long haul. Just today, a rash of explosions in the south and the east, which Ukrainian authorities claim destroyed Russian military bases.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The other big story we`re watching is the Artemis mission. Tomorrow, \uc2 NASA\uc2  hopes to launch its first flight as our Mark Strassmann reports from the Kennedy Space Center in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): NASA`s now in its countdown cadence for tomorrow`s launch, with its mammoth SLS (ph) rocket on Pad 39-b.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE BLACKWELL-THOMPSON (Artemis 1 Launch Director): I think it`s ready. By on launch day I`ll know.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Charlie Blackwell-Thompson has final say on readiness, the Artemis 1 launch director.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE BLACKWELL-THOMPSON: It is that first step, or that next step in getting humans into deep space again. And then, after that, it`s boots on the moon.\par \par \uc2 MAN: OK, Neil, we can see you coming down the ladder now.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In the \uc2 Apollo\uc2  era, America`s space dream, get boots on the moon first.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A man on the moon, a walk on the moon, and yet to say the words and to stop just a moment to think about them still sends a shiver up and down the old spine.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But with \uc2 Apollo\uc2  17 three years later, moon fatigue closed the program. No one has moon walked since 1972.\par \par \uc2 RICK LABRODE (Artemis 1 Lead Flight Director): The technology is so different than what we use today.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: When Artemis 1 lifts off, Rick LaBrode takes charge of this test flight. It`s Orion`s space capsule, without a crew, will go to the moon, orbit it for three weeks, and return to earth. NASA`s deep space hopes will ride along.\par \par \uc2 RICK LABRODE: We`ve got to have a successful flight to prove the capabilities before we put the astronauts on the next mission. Otherwise, you`re not putting astronauts on the next - next mission.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Two Artemis missions from now, \uc2 NASA\uc2  intends on a moon landing. Artemis 3`s crew, yet to be announced, will include an astronaut of color and a woman.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Apollo\uc2 , the moon was the dream. With Artemis, it`s a way station to deeper space.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA BARRON (NASA Astronaut): We want to travel to Mars. And so we need to learn how to put humans on another planetary body to live there for a long duration. And the perfect place to practice that is the lunar surface.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This one round trip will turn this Orion capsule into an instant million miler flyer. Then again, \uc2 NASA\uc2  engineers also have a lot of ground to cover in these 42 days of flight.\par \par \uc2 Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mark Strassmann, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are joined now by Astronaut Kate Rubins, a candidate for a future crewed Artemis mission. She joins us from the Kennedy Space Center.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rubins, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This is a test flight. What are you looking for in terms of safety as you evaluate what we`re about to see in the coming days?\par \par \uc2 KATE RUBINS (NASA Astronaut, Artemis Team): Good morning. It`s great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 As you said, this is a test flight. And so one of the reasons that we`re testing before we put humans on top of this incredible machine is to really push the edge of the envelope. So, from an engineering perspective, one of our main objectives is to look at the heat shield. In order to get the heat shield at this 5,000-degree heating and check out all of our objectives around the moon, we need to do this test launch.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re looking for things like the loading to go out, the launch, and then we`re -- our eyes are really on re-entry for the heat shield and the capsule recovery.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And for those who might remember, as I do, \uc2 Apollo\uc2 , what is different between the heat shield then and the heat shield stresses now?\par \par \uc2 KATE RUBINS: Yes. So, we`ve got similar profiles and similar re-entry speeds, but the materials are completely different. So, you know, we`ve had 50 years in the intervening time to adopt a lot of these modern advances in material science. I work on the space suits, and we`re actually adopting a lot of that in our new space suit design as well.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I mentioned that I remember \uc2 Apollo\uc2 . I don`t remember \uc2 Mercury\uc2 . But I remember all of the excitement nationally about the space exploration projects then. They were all almost entirely led by white men. There is a greater diversity for women and for people of color at \uc2 NASA\uc2  now.\par \par \uc2 Talk about the component involving women, such as yourself, in Artemis and everything else that NASA`s undertaking right now.\par \par \uc2 KATE RUBINS: Yes. I think one of the great things about the astronaut corps these days is, we`re not really looking at it in terms of categories anymore. We have such a great diverse and talented workforce. And you see this in the whole of \uc2 NASA\uc2 . If you look at all the centers across the U.S. So, our astronaut class, we`ve got a diversity of backgrounds. You know, we`ve got scientists, engineers, fighter pilots. We`ve got military and civilian. We, of course, have thrown open those doors for women and people of color. And it`s pretty cool to get to hang out with these people from a variety of backgrounds and see what they all bring to the program.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And, Dr. Rubins, for those who might say, yes, it`s been 50 years since we`ve been to the moon, do we need to go back? And is that the only thing we`re trying to accomplish? And doesn`t that feel somewhat repetitive? What would you tell them?\par \par \uc2 KATE RUBINS: That is a really good question. And we do need to go back. We`re going to go back in a completely different way. So, the first part of this program is really to establish a sustainable lunar presence on the - on the lunar surface and then both in orbit around the moon. This is helping us get ready for Mars. We really need to learn how to operate long term in deep space in order to be able to explore.\par \par \uc2 And the places that we`re going are incredibly different. So, \uc2 Apollo\uc2  was focused on one kind of pretty easy to get to equatorial area. We`re taking the challenge on to go to the polar regions, these permanently shadowed regions. They are always in darkness. That`s where we found water ice. And this is -- water ice is so crucial for things like building fuel for a Mars mission and a lot of the scientific discoveries. We`ve got volatile compounds in that water ice that could unlock a lot of things about how the earth and our solar system formed.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And for a layperson like me, should we think of the moon as a potential launching platform for this eventual exploration of Mars?\par \par \uc2 KATE RUBINS: It absolutely could be. You know, it`s also a place that we`re probably going to take vehicles and do some long-term deep space checkouts before we really commit ourselves to a Mars voyage. And it`s also where we`re going to be learning about how to do extensive surface operations.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re building new planetary suits. We`re learning how we can have humans live in rovers, how they can do a human/robotic partnership to uncover a lot of terrain and explore a lot more. And what`s it - what`s it like to really have that sustained presence on another planetary body.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So you are a candidate to be one of these Artemis astronauts. So, just personally, what`s it going to be like for you tomorrow, your level of personal, scientific anticipation and maybe apprehension?\par \par \uc2 KATE RUBINS: Yes, we were talking about it with the other astronauts that are here. And everybody said, you know, when it`s your launch, you get calmer and calmer as the launch approaches because you`ve trained for this, you know your procedures, you`ve been in the sim for thousands of hours. So, I mean, you`re just - you`re just absolutely calm right up until the moment of liftoff.\par \par \uc2 With this, we`re getting more and more nervous as we go. I think we are all so excited about this. It is a test flight, so, you know, we`re tempering our expectations. We`ve got a lot of great Florida weather and those kinds of things, but - but -- we are -- we`re very excited and we can feel the excitement mounting.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And very quickly, Dr. Rubins, for America, do you think this is a turning point in terms of the next phase of space exploration?\par \par \uc2 KATE RUBINS: Absolutely. I really see that when I go talk to kids in classrooms all across the U.S. and you tell them, you know, we`re going to the moon. And it`s something that we haven`t had for several decades in terms of something to inspire kids and provide this kind of exploration activity that the whole world can look to.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Dr. Kate Rubins, we thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 CBS News will be carrying a special report tomorrow around 8:30 a.m. when the rocket is expected to launch.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That is it for us today. We thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 For FACE THE NATION, I`m Major Garrett in for Margaret. Good day.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020220828ei8s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Battle Over Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 448 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: More than two months after the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade the fight over abortion rights, dwindling in some states, continues. Eleven states now have laws in effect that ban almost all abortions, with few or no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns spoke to a Louisiana woman who is now part of the national conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voice-over): Nancy Davis has three children and planned for one more.\par \par \uc2 NANCY DAVIS: We were elated that we were expecting another child.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But happiness turned to heartbreak.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the fetus was diagnosed with acrania, a rare condition in which the skull doesn`t develop. The prognosis is grim. Most are either stillborn or die soon afterwards. Doctors advised Davis to terminate her pregnancy, but Woman`s Hospital in Baton Rouge later denied her from getting the procedure.\par \par \uc2 NANCY DAVIS: I was very heartbroken. And the only thing that could replay in my head was, I was carrying my baby to bury my baby.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Today, attorney Ben Crump pointed to Davis` case as an example of Louisiana`s vague and confusing laws regarding which abortions are now allowed.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP (Davis Family Attorney): Ms. Davis was among the first women to be caught in the crosshairs of confusion due to Louisiana`s rush to restrict abortion, but she will hardly be the last.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Louisiana is one of 11 states with the nation`s toughest abortion laws. And other surrounding states have similar restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The choice is to travel far or carry a fetus that`s likely not viable to term.\par \par \uc2 NANCY DAVIS: These last few weeks has been very traumatizing. It`s been very emotional.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: This is a hard decision for you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY DAVIS: Very difficult decision for me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The \uc2 Louisiana Department of Health\uc2  says its guidelines are evolving, and they`re going to add acrania to its list of conditions that would allow for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 But, for Davis, that may come a little too late. She plans to travel next week to seek the procedure in another state -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220827ei8q0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Takes Aim At MAGA Republicans, Defends Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nancy Cordes \par \uc2 739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Meantime, we`re going to go north to Washington where President Biden is going on the offensive as the crucial midterm elections approach. During a Democratic rally in Maryland last night, he put abortion rights front and center and he criticized Trump supporters calling "MAGA Republicans a threat to our very democracy."\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House for us. Nancy, this is a decision by the Biden team. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It sure is, Tony.\par \par \uc2 This was a speech designed to fire up the Democratic base in advance of the November midterm elections and President Biden went aggressively after the Trump movement laying out what he says is on the line in these elections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We will codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Testing out some campaign themes, President Biden in Maryland Thursday night argued women`s reproductive rights are at stake this November.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : There is a race to pass the most restrictive abortion limitations imaginable, even without exception for rape or incest. If the MAGA Republicans win control the Congress, it won`t matter where you live, women won`t have the right to choose anywhere.\par \par \uc2 MAGA Republicans don`t have a clue about the power of women. Let me tell you something, they are about to find out.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In ramped up political rhetoric, the President took aim at Trump followers, especially those in office.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: In this moment, those of you who love this country -- Democrats, Independents, mainstream Republicans -- we must be stronger, more determined, and more committed to saving America than the MAGA Republicans that are destroying America.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: With just over 10 weeks to go before midterm elections. President Biden and his party are looking to capitalize on a series of recent policy wins, including the healthcare and climate change law, gun reform, and the new student loan forgiveness plan.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): It`s completely unfair.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Which Republican lawmakers slammed Thursday.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): For our government just to say, you know, okay, well your debt is completely forgiven. Obviously, they have an agenda for that, they need votes in November.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The White House Twitter account went on the offensive, calling out Republicans like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  by name for criticizing student loan forgiveness, even after their own pandemic era business loans known as PPP were forgiven by the government.\par \par \uc2 Democrats feeling new optimism after their candidates beat Mr. Biden`s 2020 margins in four recent special congressional elections, including in New York where Pat Ryan beat a tough Republican opponent by focusing on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT RYAN (D-NY): When the Supreme Court ripped away reproductive freedoms, access to abortion rights, we said, this is not what America stands for.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Candidate Biden may have run on civility and bringing Americans together, but now with control of the House and the Senate hanging in the balance, he is clearly ratcheting up the rhetoric -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Nancy, in regards to abortion access, there are a number of new laws taking effect all around the country. What can you tell us about those?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Absolutely. A bunch of trigger laws taking effect this week. Take a look: Near total bans went into effect just yesterday in Tennessee and Idaho. Penalties were tightened in Texas.\par \par \uc2 And this weekend, Oklahoma is going to enact a law increasing penalties on doctors who perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 Now, the White House thinks that all of these restrictions are going to galvanize Democrats across the country the way that they did in Kansas a couple of weeks ago when an antiabortion measure was defeated at the polls, but whether this issue has as much potency come November as it does right now remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, time will tell. Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220827ei8q0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 DOJ to Release Mar-a-Lago Affidavit; Biden Launches Midterm Attacks; College Monkeypox Preparations; Inside Putin`s War; Severe Storms and Travel Impact; The Cost of Artemis; Prince Harry`s Charity Efforts; Muse`s Inspiration; Best New Fall Books; Discovering the Joy Of Donkeys; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Jamie Yuccas, Robert Costa, Nancy  Cordes, Meg Oliver, Debora Patta, David Begnaud, Mark Strassmann, Manuel  Bojorquez, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 13,271 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday, August 26th. You made it. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Just barely. And I`m Jamie Yuccas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off, but we`re all here. Let`s go straight to today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A Judge ordered the Justice Department to reveal information today about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s search of Mar-a-Lago. How it could affect the case.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): Trump did later say that the redactions are concealing exonerating material or something along that nature.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Biden goes on the attack before the midterms accusing Trump supporters of embracing political violence.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The very survival of our planet is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Torrential rain flaming parts of the southeast leading to evacuation. Fast-moving waters forcing dozens of rescues.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Colleges still reeling from COVID also prepare for monkeypox as the fall semester begins.\par \par \uc2 ROB STAHL (Executive Director of Student Health Services): Our team has practiced this, we`re ready for this, and this is what we do every day.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Novak Djokovic, withdrawing from the US Open.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Djokovic can`t enter the US because he is not vaccinated against COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Travelers saw a bald eagle go through a TSA checkpoint with the Charlotte Douglas Airport. California\par \par \uc2 MAN: America.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Regulators voted to ban the sale of new gas-powered cars by the year 2035.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): No one here follows through with plans they made a week ago for sure. Yes. This will take effect in 2035, of course it will.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Teen pop star, Olivia Rodrigo surprised Billy Joel fans during his concert at the Garden.\par \par \uc2 MAN: "Uptown Girl" was 20 years old when she was born.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Olivia Rodrigo and Billy Joel performance.)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got three Billy Joel fans here, also Olivia Rodrigo fans, I`ve got to say.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes. She is 19, he is 73. They`re rockin` out. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are rockin` out, you guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Joel looking good in his 70s.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Speaking of rockin` out, we are a little bit of rock `n` roll today.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I like it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anthony Mason spent time on the dark side with Muse frontman, Matt Bellamy. Their new album has a unique take on society. Plus, we`ll show you how the band got their whole families involved.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And David Begnaud`s final installment of "How Are You Now?" takes us back to a donkey sanctuary. That`s where a former media executive found joy and purpose rescuing donkeys.\par \par \uc2 This is a great story. We`ll show you what he is doing to help other people as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: David is helping all of us with these pieces. I find little lessons in kindness.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Progress for yourself and growth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To overcome obstacles. It`s wonderful.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 But first, we`re going to begin today with some major revelations expected on why the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched former President Trump`s home for hundreds of classified documents allegedly taken from the White House.\par \par \uc2 A Judge has ordered the Bureau to explain their reasoning for the Mar-a- Lago search with appropriate redactions. That deadline to unseal the document is now just hours away and it follows a request by CBS News and others to make it public.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is not far from the Federal Courthouse in West Palm Beach, Florida for us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Bob, many people waiting to get another piece of this puzzle.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has until noon today to release a redacted version of the affidavit to the public. Now, there could be heavy redactions. This is an ongoing probe, but it could still offer new insight into why \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents arrived at former President Trump`s home search warrant in hand.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Almost three weeks after the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s search of former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home, a Federal Judge in Florida ordered Thursday that the Justice Department release the redacted affidavit behind the search.\par \par \uc2 Judge Bruce Reinhart wrote that he approved the DOJ suggested redactions to the affidavit to protect the identities of witnesses, law enforcement agents, the investigation strategy, direction, scope, sources and methods and grand jury information.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WEINSTEIN (Former Assistant US attorney): If what the public is going to learn is, again, just how much effort went into this affidavit, the fact that there are pages and pages and pages of probable cause.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Justice Department officials had opposed releasing the full affidavit saying earlier this week they are very concerned about the safety of the witnesses involved in their investigation into Trump`s alleged mishandling of classified information, some of which went up to the level of top-secret.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts say even though the public will only likely see a heavily redacted version, it could still be revealing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WEINSTEIN: It may also give some details about how long this investigation has been going on, whether it has been going on for months or whether it`s been going on for a year.\par \par \uc2 The benefit of transparency is going to be to let everybody know exactly what took place and to prevent people from trying to guess what`s going on here.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President Trump has called for the affidavit to be released. He was conferring yesterday with his lawyers in Bedminster, New Jersey and keeps insisting and social media posts that he has done nothing wrong. His legal team has a deadline today to find out more information about why it has requested a so-called Special Master or neutral party to review the evidence.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Bob, thank you very much. We know you`ll stay on it for us.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, we`re going to go north to Washington where President Biden is going on the offensive as the crucial midterm elections approach. During a Democratic rally in Maryland last night, he put abortion rights front and center and he criticized Trump supporters calling "MAGA Republicans a threat to our very democracy."\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House for us. Nancy, this is a decision by the Biden team. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It sure is, Tony.\par \par \uc2 This was a speech designed to fire up the Democratic base in advance of the November midterm elections and President Biden went aggressively after the Trump movement laying out what he says is on the line in these elections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We will codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Testing out some campaign themes, President Biden in Maryland Thursday night argued women`s reproductive rights are at stake this November.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : There is a race to pass the most restrictive abortion limitations imaginable, even without exception for rape or incest. If the MAGA Republicans win control the Congress, it won`t matter where you live, women won`t have the right to choose anywhere.\par \par \uc2 MAGA Republicans don`t have a clue about the power of women. Let me tell you something, they are about to find out.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In ramped up political rhetoric, the President took aim at Trump followers, especially those in office.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: In this moment, those of you who love this country -- Democrats, Independents, mainstream Republicans -- we must be stronger, more determined, and more committed to saving America than the MAGA Republicans that are destroying America.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: With just over 10 weeks to go before midterm elections. President Biden and his party are looking to capitalize on a series of recent policy wins, including the healthcare and climate change law, gun reform, and the new student loan forgiveness plan.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): It`s completely unfair.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Which Republican lawmakers slammed Thursday.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): For our government just to say, you know, okay, well your debt is completely forgiven. Obviously, they have an agenda for that, they need votes in November.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The White House Twitter account went on the offensive, calling out Republicans like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  by name for criticizing student loan forgiveness, even after their own pandemic era business loans known as PPP were forgiven by the government.\par \par \uc2 Democrats feeling new optimism after their candidates beat Mr. Biden`s 2020 margins in four recent special congressional elections, including in New York where Pat Ryan beat a tough Republican opponent by focusing on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT RYAN (D-NY): When the Supreme Court ripped away reproductive freedoms, access to abortion rights, we said, this is not what America stands for.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Candidate Biden may have run on civility and bringing Americans together, but now with control of the House and the Senate hanging in the balance, he is clearly ratcheting up the rhetoric -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Nancy, in regards to abortion access, there are a number of new laws taking effect all around the country. What can you tell us about those?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Absolutely. A bunch of trigger laws taking effect this week. Take a look: Near total bans went into effect just yesterday in Tennessee and Idaho. Penalties were tightened in Texas.\par \par \uc2 And this weekend, Oklahoma is going to enact a law increasing penalties on doctors who perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 Now, the White House thinks that all of these restrictions are going to galvanize Democrats across the country the way that they did in Kansas a couple of weeks ago when an antiabortion measure was defeated at the polls, but whether this issue has as much potency come November as it does right now remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, time will tell. Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: College students headed back to campus this fall will have familiar guidance about COVID and new warnings about monkeypox.\par \par \uc2 The CDC has recorded nearly 17,000 monkeypox cases nationwide. White House officials spoke with university leaders yesterday about this outbreak.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver looks at the ways colleges are preparing for yet another public health emergency.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: \uc2 Hofstra University\uc2  in Hempstead, New York is prepared for monkeypox if it arrives here in the fall.\par \par \uc2 ROB STAHL: Our team has practiced this. We`re ready for this and this is what we do every day.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Executive Director of Student Health Services, Rob Stahl says they ramped up cleaning and disinfecting protocols to help stop the spread.\par \par \uc2 ROB STAHL: The good news is that monkeypox is not like COVID and that it is not as easily spread. It does not spread through small droplets; it really requires much more prolonged contact.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Of the some 17,000 cases nationwide, New York has the most of any other state followed by California, Florida, and Texas.\par \par \uc2 How are you educating students?\par \par \uc2 ROB STAHL: So we have posters up in all the bathrooms on campus as well as common spaces on campus that students are in with access, and also providing them information about health and infectious disease in general and how they can best prevent themselves from getting sick while they`re here at college.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: That also includes having testing in place for students who might show symptoms like fever and a characteristic rash.\par \par \uc2 AMESH ADALJA (Center for Health Security \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2 ): Monkeypox is primarily spread by close contact with individuals. That`s skin-to-skin, skin-to-mouth, mouth-to-mouth type of context.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Amesh Adalja is an infectious disease physician and senior scholar at \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2 . He says schools like Hofstra are doing the right thing about making sure infected students have somewhere to stay for a while.\par \par \uc2 AMESH ADALJA: They need to make sure that they have ways for students to isolate in a comfortable manner for a couple of weeks if necessary.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Is that feasible for most universities to isolate students for up to two weeks?\par \par \uc2 AMESH ADALJA: What we know is that universities really had to reconfigure their operations because of COVID-19 and they always had isolation dorms and isolation rooms.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: But overall, Adalja says the risk for universities is low.\par \par \uc2 AMESH ADALJA: I don`t necessarily think this is going to be a disruptive force on college campuses, but it is something that colleges need to proactively plan for, so it isn`t disruptive.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: We reached out to more than 30 schools for this story, and while many were making some kind of preparation, mostly sending informational e-mails to students, none expressed that monkeypox was as dire a concern as the COVID-19 pandemic -- Jamie.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s good. I hope they have plans in place.\par \par \uc2 Meg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Six months into the war on Ukraine, Russia`s President is calling for a bigger army. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has ordered an increase of 137,000 troops starting in January.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we have new information on what it is like to be part of the Russian invasion force. Debora Patta spoke with a Russian paratrooper who was so disgusted with the war, he defected and is now telling his story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: As missiles rained down on Ukraine in the early hours of 24 February, it became impossible to ignore that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  had planned something truly awful.\par \par \uc2 Astoundingly, many Russian soldiers on the frontlines had no idea they had just invaded Ukraine. They hadn`t even been told what to do.\par \par \uc2 (PAVO FILATIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: We just started moving forward. When the shelling began, we thought it was \uc2 NATO\uc2  approaching us, not Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Paratrooper, Pavo Filatiev (sp?) says he served in Russia`s 56 Air Assault Regiment sent to capture Kherson early in the conflict.\par \par \uc2 (PAVO FILATIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: It was only 10 days later when I realized there is no \uc2 NATO\uc2  here, it is only Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Do you feel tricked?\par \par \uc2 (PAVO FILATIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: I know that we`ve been tricked for many years, and everything shown on state TV has nothing to do with reality. Unfortunately, now the same thing is happening in the war.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: He provided us with documents to support his claim, which we could not independently verify. He says he was eventually evacuated because of an injury, but until then, felt trapped.\par \par \uc2 (PAVO FILATIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: The Russian Paratroop Brigade has a similar attitude to the Navy SEALs of the US. It is cowardly and shameful to lay down your weapons and abandon your post even though we started to realize this war was wrong, we didn`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: With outdated weapons and no food, water, or blankets in the freezing cold, they were forced to steal supplies, but he claims many went too far.\par \par \uc2 (PAVO FILATIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: There was a lot of looting of laptops and cell phones. I understand why. Not only are they paid very little, but when they think they might die tomorrow, human greed takes over.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And as for the war crimes Russia is accused of in places like Bucha, it was only when he finally had access to the internet that he read about this. He insists he did not witness any atrocities.\par \par \uc2 (PAVO FILATIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: At first, I didn`t want to believe that it was true. It is horrible for me to realize that in my Army, in our country, there are people who did this.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: As the ugly truth began to sink in, Filatiev said many of his comrades went to extreme lengths to avoid fighting against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (PAVO FILATIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: They would deliberately shoot themselves in the legs, pretend it was an accident so that they wouldn`t be sent home and given a huge compensation.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Why have you decided to come forward and tell your story.\par \par \uc2 (PAVO FILATIEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: Because if nothing is done, there will be nuclear war or our government will turn Russia into North Korea.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Filatiev fled Russia after publishing his story, but although he escaped, he is not revealing his location as he is in hiding fearing for his safety -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Debora Patta in Ukraine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It could be another messy travel day in this country as severe thunderstorms target some of the nation`s busiest airports. Rivers are still rising in Mississippi from the big storms that caused flooding on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking all of the stormy weather.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 What`s the biggest problem right now?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 It is the fact that we`re going to see some big-time storms in the northeast. We actually could see damaging winds and also a very heavy rainfall. Anywhere you see the red shading on the map is where we have the best chance of seeing the storms. They really fire up into the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, Boston needs this rain. We`re over 10 inches below average. The better news is all of this moves out for the weekend and it is going to be gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about that rain in the south. Dallas, Texas; Jackson, Mississippi the wettest August on record, that obviously is bad news for all the flooding that it caused. The good news is it did take away the exceptional drought into Dallas.\par \par \uc2 $We still have rain to come though, in waterlogged areas like Louisiana and also in to Mississippi. And two areas to watch here in the Atlantic, it has been 54 days since we`ve had a named storm, but the environmental conditions for this one could improve as it heads towards the Yucatan.\par \par \uc2 For live coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or your favorite TV streaming devices and Nate, we are just a few weeks away from the peak of the season, September 10th.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that, and you had me at the weekend will be gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, with the fast-rising price of a trip to the moon, why NASA`s cost overruns make it crucial for Monday`s scheduled a blast off of a lunar mission to go absolutely smoothly, but first, it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, David Begnaud has the final installment of our series, "How are you now?" where one man traded in the rat race for a donkey sanctuary -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: My man, have you ever hugged a donkey before?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nope.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes, right. I know it is a bizarre question, but I did and it is really sweet.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, meet that former media executive who started a donkey sanctuary in Northern California after losing his high-flying job in New York City and finding his new purpose: Rescuing those donkeys.\par \par \uc2 Hear how he is using the donkeys to help other people.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Come on back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: US Open Tennis begins on Monday with Serena Williams playing in what is likely to be her final tournament. Williams will take on 27- year-old, Danka Kovinic of Montenegro in Monday`s first round match.\par \par \uc2 Williams who has won 23 grand slam singles titles announced her retirement plans earlier this month in "Vogue" Magazine.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Novak Djokovic confirms that he will not play in this year`s Open. The 21-time grand slam winner has not been vaccinated for COVID and CDC guidelines saying non-US citizens must be fully vaccinated in order to enter the country.\par \par \uc2 Djokovic tweeted, "I`ll keep in good shape and positive spirit and wait for an opportunity to compete again."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s wild. He is sticking to his convictions on this one, but, man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is wild. He is sticking to it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Such a good player, prime of his career.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, the prime of his career.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t get these opportunities back.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, a new safety concern for \uc2 Instagram\uc2  users. We will show you how turning off a particular setting could make you less vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Check it out. You`re looking at the Kennedy Space Center where \uc2 NASA\uc2  is counting down to its most ambitious moon mission in half a century.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Blast off for the Artemis 1 mission is scheduled for Monday morning. A successful test flight would put the US one step closer to returning astronauts to the lunar surface.\par \par \uc2 The revived Moon Program has had to deal with massive delays and giant cost overruns that threatened to ground it.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann is at the Kennedy Space Center.\par \par \uc2 Mark, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Everybody waiting for this to happen.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Good morning to you, Jamie.\par \par \uc2 Yes, returning astronauts to the moon has been America`s on again, off again goal for the last 20 years, and \uc2 NASA\uc2  has had to keep moving its target date for a moon landing. Now, that becomes expensive. But that`s also only one reason behind Artemis` soaring costs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GENE CERNAN (\uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut): It`s got to be one of the most proud moments of my life, I guarantee you.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Our interest in exploring the moon has waxed and waned over the half century since \uc2 Apollo\uc2  and these images.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 NASA\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The big question: When will we see moonwalkers again?\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  Administrator, Bill Nelson.\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON (\uc2 NASA\uc2  Administrator): On this schedule, we think we will land at the end of `25.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Twenty-five.\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON: That`s our plan.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Is a 2025 moon landing realistic?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN (\uc2 NASA\uc2  Inspector General): No.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Paul Martin is NASA`s Inspector General, the in-house watchdog for our can-do space agency.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN: That culture of optimism results in unrealistic cost in scheduled projections.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And why is that?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN: I think for two main reasons. The first is the technical complexity of the mission.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Understandable.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN: This is difficult stuff. And the second they do wonderful things, but they have less than stellar project management practices.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The Artemis program is years behind schedule, and billions over budget; total spent so far $40 billion.\par \par \uc2 The first four launches will cost $4 billion a piece. The worry here, that level of spending is not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN: At $4.1 billion per launch, you`re going to be limited to one launch perhaps every other year, and that cadence of launches is not going to achieve NASA`s mission of having a permanent presence on the lunar surface.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The dollars just aren`t endless.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN: The dollars aren`t going to be there.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Last November, Martin`s office published this reality check. Artemis` total costs, something \uc2 NASA\uc2  had never revealed.\par \par \uc2 What is the price tag?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN: From fiscal 2012 through fiscal 2025, $93 billion.\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON: Yes. \uc2 Spaceflight\uc2  is expensive, and when you are doing new things for the first time, it is expensive. Over time, that cost is going to come down dramatically.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: One cost cutting move, reusing the Orion crew capsule on later flights, but the massive Space Launch System rocket is single use.\par \par \uc2 Mandated by Congress, built by NASA`s space shuttle-era contractors and at least $2 billion over budget.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NASA Animation.)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It is currently the only rocket powerful enough to lift Orion to lunar orbit.\par \par \uc2 If there were an equally capable, but cheaper commercial alternative, would you consider it?\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON: Of course, but there isn`t and there won`t be for a while. This is the only rocket and spacecraft that are here and we`re ready to go.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But Nelson agrees, challenges remain for a 2025 moon landing, a lunar lander to take astronauts down to the surface and spacesuits for moonwalking, both still in development, both behind schedule.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN: By spending much more perhaps than it should have cost, \uc2 NASA\uc2  can do less science and do less exploration and it is going to take \uc2 NASA\uc2  longer to accomplish those missions.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Have you projected what is a realistic date for American boots to be back on the moon?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MARTIN: The earliest possible we see at some point, in late 2026, but even I think that`s optimistic.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Long term, \uc2 NASA\uc2  wants to take what it learns on the moon and put astronauts on Mars, but here is what NASA`s Inspector General told us: Either the space agency learns to spend its money more effectively and 