onths. But generally speaking, we`ve always been outsiders, you know.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Muse performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Muse won their audience on the road with their extravagant live shows.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: That`s just really how we connected with our fan base, you know, so I don`t think a lot of the fans that we have will say the same thing. It`s like until you`ve seen the band live, you can`t really sort of know what they`re about, you know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I mean, it was not like it was really hit singles.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: No, no. We`re still waiting to have a hit, if we ever have one.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Muse performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But when the new Wembley Stadium opened in London in 2007, they filled its 75,000 seats twice.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Muse performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You were the first to sell it out.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: We were actually the first to play it as well and then George Michael came in and just jumped in. He jumped in right in front of us.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He bragged about it in the artist`s guestbook at Wembley.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: George Michael wrote: "I was the first to play here. Na-na- na-na-na." And then we wrote, "Okay, we were the first to sell it out twice. Na-na-na-na-na."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Muse performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Even bigger for Bellamy was playing the Glastonbury Festival in England, which he`d snuck into as a teenager.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: You give like five pounds to a local farmer, and he put you under the bales of hay in the back of his tractor and just drive you in, you know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I imagine, it is actually pretty spooky under a bale of hay.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: Oh, it`s exciting, though. You know, going to Glastonbury like that a number of times and then headlining it like 10 or so years later, that was just -- that`s always going to stand out as a very special moment.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Teignmouth Community College.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Bellamy grew up in 90 minutes south of Glastonbury in Devon, England.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe the music scene in Devon?\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: The music scene? I mean, there really -- we were the music scene in in Devon.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Matt, Dominic and Chris met in high school and formed Muse in 1994.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: And we were just left going like, "Okay, someone is going to have to sing," like "Who`s it going to be?" And I just like -- I gave it a shot. I mean, like, obviously, I couldn`t sing at all the first -- you know, the first two years of Muse. I was just kind of mumbling.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jeff Buckley Live in Chicago.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Then at a music festival, he heard a voice similar to his own.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: And I straightaway -- I was like, "What is that?"\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It was Jeff Buckley.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: Discovering Jeff Buckley really sort of changed me and put me on a kind of path of feeling more confident to actually give singing a shot, because I realized that kind of tenor, high pitch especially falsetto male voice can actually work in a rock setting.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: I`ve got his guitar here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Do you really?\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: Yes. Yes. It is in the storage in the back there. Yes, I`ve got his guitar that he made the "Grace" album with, and I actually used it a little bit on this album.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Bellamy had another inspiration for this album.\par \par \uc2 Who is this?\par \par \uc2 His daughter, Lovella, born during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: And I really started to tuck into making the album, I needed that little face just to look at, the cutest face I`ve ever seen. Those chubby cheeks.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Muse performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And when he is writing, Bellamy is always imagining the next massive Muse tour.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: The best of, I think, is almost like subconscious. But it feels a bit like a visualization of something from the future. Like, I feel like I`m imagining myself playing that onstage in a year`s time. You know what I am saying?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is that where the thrill comes from?\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: I think so, yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Muse performance.)\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: I`m so lucky. The bands are lucky. We`ve got to the point where we can make those kinds of dreams or realize, you know, things become real, you know.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anthony, Matt Bellamy is incredibly talented. What impressed you the most?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, one of the things that`s really impressive, Nate is he is a self-taught musician. You saw a little clip of him there playing piano when he was 12 years old. He actually did take lessons, but he said he had an authority problem, he always has. Quit the lessons and went off and taught himself.\par \par \uc2 And one of the other things I love is that his dad -- this is a fun fact -- his dad was in a band called The Tornados, a British band that had a number one hit in 1962, an instrumental called "Telstar" and that`s kind of what inspired him ultimately to pick up the guitar.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that? "Telstar" and his son ended up being one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Anthony, I want to go back to them having one of the greatest live acts going right now.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know you did the interview. I know you listen to a lot of the music. Did you get a chance to see them perform live?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Big question.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`m going to in October. They`ve got actually what for 