 number one "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestseller.\par \par \uc2 And this new book, I think, really takes her out of her comfort zone as an author and it works. It is really, I would say, like one of my favorite books of the year, if not in multiple years.\par \par \uc2 It is about a son looking for his mother in a dystopian America, where children are taken away from their parents if the government deems them to be unfit or unpatriotic, and a lot of Asian-American parents.\par \par \uc2 I read this book right in the wake of the Salman Rushdie attack, and it was so poignant to think about the importance of freedom of speech, and just the power of art and expression.\par \par \uc2 One of my colleagues said this was fiction of revolution and I think that`s such a good description.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Oh, wow. That sounds powerful.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It is so good. Like chills good.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: All right. I`m in. You also have a book about a young immigrant`s memoir. What`s making this one so special for you?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Is it another chills book. It`s such -- it`s a great fall reading. I`m just going to say that. This is "Solito" by Javier Zamora. This is a memoir. So Javier Zamora is a young poet and he is writing about when he was nine years old, he migrated from El Salvador to the United States.\par \par \uc2 He was supposed to be on a two-week journey, and he was going to meet his parents who lived in the States already, but the journey ended up taking over two months and he was alone. He wasn`t with any family. He was with a group of strangers.\par \par \uc2 And you`re reading this and you just like, you feel the dust from the desert. You feel thirsty. It`s just so visceral.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I just -- I read it and I feel like as a mother, myself and an American, a human being, this is -- it is required reading.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We also have a debut thriller by an author who is in real life, was an autopsy technician.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Knows gore it sounds like.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes and also the co-host of the popular podcast, "Morbid." It is Alaina Urquhart, and this is "The Butcher and The Wren."\par \par \uc2 Now my mom has a sweet lady from Ohio and she loves the most morbid TV shows and books she can get her hands on. You know the type like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am like your mom. Shoutout to your mom. That`s neat.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Actually, most women are really in to crime.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It`s such a weird thing.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Anyway, this is a book for all of them, all of you people that like morbid things. It`s about a medical examiner that is helping to find a serial killer who performs medical experiments on his victims. It is scary.\par \par \uc2 It is a read in one sitting kind of book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, there is something for nonfiction lovers.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, nonfiction lovers. It`s called "Like, Comment, Subscribe." It`s about \uc2 YouTube\uc2 `s rise by Mark Bergen, and Bergen captures this cultural moment where social media was really ascendant.\par \par \uc2 And the way that we were all consuming TV was just blowing up.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: And he looks at the early days of \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and their rat- infested offices to like this tremendous growth that they`ve realized today.\par \par \uc2 I feel like -- I have my three-year-old who is asking me to watch unboxing videos on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and like, that`s how much they`ve infiltrated our culture and this is in this new wave of books that are kind of investigati