ut that is $1.34 billion, the point four in that sentence is worth 40 million,\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Think about how big that is.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Right. Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s how big the number is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. My "Talk of the Table" is my big mouth and sometimes small brain and to ease ourselves into that topic. I`ve got some footage of men in Canada wearing a period pain simulator.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Somedays/@getsomedays via Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Viewers of our show yesterday will recall we showed similar footage and we had a serious and important and overdue conversation about endometriosis.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of that conversation, there was some talk about sympathy and then I made a comment about how about some sympathy for guys who get a football to the groin, which I thought was a light-hearted note.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said it in jest.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I thought it was a lighthearted note at the end of a serious segment. It did not go over that way at all.\par \par \uc2 You had some side eye in the moment.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Views that I should have kept it in my pocket. The entire room here, everyone we work with had their opinion, it was negative.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was like a scene from "Naked Gun." Everybody in the back went --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, it got worse after that. And so by about like 9:30 AM, I was like all right, I officially feel bad. I feel bad. I`ve done bad. So, I`m saying I`m sorry. I did not intend to tarnish an important segment with my juvenile joke at the end.\par \par \uc2 Jamie, I`m sorry if it hurt your piece. And people, hear me, I was wrong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Look, Tony, you`re a great guy. Everybody who knows you knows the intention was not malicious.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Of course.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: So you know, we all make mistakes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Apology accepted, right, room? Everybody. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, room. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we all get it. I mean, guys getting hit down there, it does hurt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As a matter of fact, just to clear it out.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For all the women out there, stand up, I got you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Katy already took care of that last night. Katy, I am sorry to you as well.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The point is that women have to experience this every month.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: For several days in some cases. I mean, the woman and our piece, you know, it is debilitating. She goes to the hospital sometimes; she is in so much pain. So it`s just -- you know, a little awareness that is a little different.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure. No doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . was like you, you`ve got to say something.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done. Well done. You`re going to have a good weekend, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope so. I am feeling that already.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look who is back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, God.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: David Begnaud with his latest in the series, "How Are You Now?" The story finds a man who followed his bliss to an unlikely new calling.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had so much fun this week checking in with people that we`ve introduced you to over the past few years with the goal of answering this one pretty simple question: "How are you now?"\par \par \uc2 This morning we visit with the former media executive, Ron King. He was forced to say goodbye to his high-powered position covering high fashion, but in losing his job, he found himself in a really unexpected place surrounded by donkeys.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Among the coastal hills of Northern California, right in the middle of wine country.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: My favorite part of my day is my donkey hug.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Ron King has created a donkey sanctuary.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: It`s the weight of the head that lets you know he is like happy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This is so sweet.\par \par \uc2 This 75-acre ranch is a nonprofit dedicated to their rescue and rehabilitation.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: They are intelligent and they have very high emotional intelligence. I give them love and they give me love back. I`m a donkey guy now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It`s a far cry from his pre-pandemic life, attired in Gucci, shuttling between New York and Los Angeles as a senior VP for "\uc2 Time Inc\uc2 ." He managed sales and marketing for some well-known brands.\par \par \uc2 Was there a moment in your life where you thought: "Damn, I`ve made it."\par \par \uc2 RON KING: Yes, I think it was when you know, I was flying first class to Milan to sit front row at Versace. That was a big deal.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But in 2018, the sale of "Time" led to the elimination of King`s position, so he freelanced, but then COVID hit. The work dried up and the despair set in.\par \par \uc2 Then came a call from an old friend, Phil Selway, a prominent pop art dealer. He hired Ron to move to his ranch and sell it because it wasn`t being used.\par \par \uc2 PHIL SELWAY (Pop art dealer): I thought it would be a win-win. He could help me tremendously and it would help him, give him another project.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: King took the job, and while hiking around the property, he felt something new, this sense of calm.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: My head is always like a snow globe that`s being shook. It never stops. That sense of sort of chaos in my head was just normal for me.\par \par \uc2 When the snow globe stops after 20 years, you feel that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip form Oscar`s Place.)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But you were still enough to recognize.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: I was still enough to recognize it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And respectful enough to act on it.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: I had no thoughts in my head, except this beautiful surrounding and I realized this is the first time I felt serenity in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Well, serenity met serendipity.\par \par \uc2 King discovered a story about the plight of donkeys known historically as strong pack animals capable of hauling goods, but they`ve outlived their usefulness.\par \par \uc2 So, donkeys which could live around 30 years are sold at auction, slaughtered, and then skinned for their hides to be used for traditional Chinese medicine.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: They don`t have any advocates, they don`t win races, they don`t feed a food chain. And I thought, I need to help donkeys. So I said, Bill, I have an idea that I want to run by you. I want you to take it off the market, not sell it, and let me turn this into a donkey sanctuary.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That sounds bananas.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: And he -- he will tell you, he also said it sounded bananas.\par \par \uc2 PHIL SELWAY: The first thing I thought when Ron was giving this presentation, was that he was crazy and I would be crazy to go along with this. It`s absolutely blown me away and I could not be happier.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Donations support their cause. Every donkey here would have been killed.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: Patches was dying of starvation. The only way to help a donkey that`s been abused, emotionally recover is to love it back to health. And I had this moment where I realized that there is a difference between things that I enjoy, and things that bring me joy.\par \par \uc2 And I was 52 years old when I realized those are two different things. It`s a whole different way of living.\par \par \uc2 You will never be this sick again.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And in helping donkeys to heal, he may be healing his own invisible scars.\par \par \uc2 King`s struggles began at an early age when this son of a Southern Baptist preacher first realized he was gay.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: They were young and they were religious and they had a little sissy boy. So, that didn`t go well. All I really wanted all along was to matter.\par \par \uc2 Come on.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: By his 20s, he was homeless, addicted to drugs, and alcohol. He got sober only after surviving an overdose. Understanding the power of resilience.\par \par \uc2 You wanted to take the donkeys hiking?\par \par \uc2 RON KING: Yes, sir. Keep these guys fit.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: King rebuilds the donkey`s strength and trust so that they are ready for adoption.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: For a long time, proving that I could be someone or be something motivated me. That part has gone, but I want to matter to the world, to donkeys, to people.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You know, changing lives may be a heavy burden for a lot of people. But it is a load that these animals seem to haul with grace.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: I was pretty accomplished by all those standards and I found myself with the rug pulled out from under me and for that level of despair to be so close so recent is really an important lesson in resilience and turn around.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 There is light at the end of the tunnel and it`s not always a freight train. Sometimes, it is donkeys.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That was seven months ago.\par \par \uc2 Ron, how are you now?\par \par \uc2 RON KING: Yes, things are going great. We have been flooded with donations and volunteers and adoptions. So, it`s been super busy and super fun.\par \par \uc2 She`s brand new here. She`s just learning to trust us.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: With the surge in donations, they`ve gone ahead and built five new pens for quarantining animals when they arrive. That`s to protect the health of the herd.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: We`ve taken in five donkeys in the last three weeks.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Do you still feel like what you are doing matters?\par \par \uc2 RON KING: What I really realized is as long as we stay true to what we`re doing and why we`re doing it, that that mattering starts to amplify and then we now have 91 volunteers. All of our volunteers share how much they felt like they matter.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And his vulnerability struck a chord. He was invited to share his story with a group of medical professionals who help troubled teenagers.\par \par \uc2 RON KING: I really spoke about different ways in which teens can feel like they matter, why mattering is of such importance. As you know, I was a struggling teen and so that was really meaningful to me.\par \par \uc2 Hi, baby girl.\par \par \uc2 It`s never too late for a new start and chasing joy is amplified out to literally thousands of people. And once that happens, you really feel like you`re making a difference.\par \par \uc2 Oh, my baby.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Such important lessons there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Here`s the takeaway. There is a difference, he says between things that I enjoy and things that bring me joy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s good.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I think the most impactful part though is that -- and I think many people feel this way is that you think that you have it all, you want to be somebody, but it`s really when it comes down to it, you just want to matter.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s right. And Oprah says, we all want to know, "Do you hear me?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do you hear me?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And what I have to say matter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s what they say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I am sorry, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say, people clearly heard your story. So, he has got donations coming in, there are new partnerships, donations, volunteers coming in.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Yes, volunteers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Volunteers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love the pride he takes in wearing that shirt that says "Staff" on the back.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would wear one of those.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: David, thank you so much. All week, it has been awesome.\par \par \uc2 On today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast, I spoke to Will Graham, one of the c o- creators of the new \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime series, "A League of Their Own." It is a great conversation. Hopefully, you tune in.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Guys, before we go. Thank you so much for having me this week.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It has been our pleasure. I do agree with, Nate.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We`ve had so much fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That we`ve got no time to say anything more.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us. We will see you Monday on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, a look at all that mattered. Bye.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A monsoon storm system has hammered the drought stricken west.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The flash flooding prompted some water rescues as the water was rising quickly around them.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The former President has now taken his first legal action to challenge that \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s search at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He claims he is the front runner for the Republican nomination. The Justice Department`s response, this is not political.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: A historic announcement on student loans.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The measure could affect up to 43 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Progressives argue, at least for now, this move helps to level the playing field.\par \par \uc2 MAYA PAYNE SMART (author): Remember, you`re already spending a ton of time with your child and you can build these simple literacy practices into everyday life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`ve got another kid who is screaming for something, the kitchen still needs to be cleaned. It`s --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Are you speaking from experience?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am speaking from absolute experience.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Monkees performance.)\par \par \uc2 MICKY DOLENZ (The Monkees): I`ve got nothing for that record.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That Anthony Mason piece -- is exceptional.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll do it again. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have everything we don`t --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at.."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Damn. Yes -- that put those sorts of -- all right, one more time. I`ve got it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Because of a toddler demand of his crib.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I haven`t had a drink since Saturday. I don`t know where this is going.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank God, I asked. Okay.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I got him. I got him. Oh God. You guys are crazy.\par \par \uc2 EWEN (Producer): Whoa. Oh. [Bleep]. I am very sorry that you have to go through this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Put your hands up, guys. Show your support.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: David, I want to bring you in. You look like the jeweler down there.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL ROTHMAN (Chief Technologist and Executive Technical Director of the Good Housekeeping Institute): I`ve got something for you.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Who`s your doppelganger?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe Usher.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The guy from --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Let me see the dance moves.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know. My suit is too tight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: People always say I look like Bill Hader and I do not agree.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I love a good mullet.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You can`t be serious.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: I love a mullet. I am totally serious.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Come on, Jamie. Come on, Jamie.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MCENROE (Former professional tennis player): He could win the whole thing if he kept his head together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You often won the whole thing and you didn`t keep your head together.\par \par \uc2 They`re going to take us off. I want to tell people the title, it`s called "McEnroe." You cannot be serious we are out of time. Are you kidding me? You can see it on Showtime.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MCENROE: I`m out of here. Forget out it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m out of here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was raised on eggs and bacon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I mean, I don`t eat as much bacon as I used to. It is like an ex-girlfriend. We don`t see each other as much as we do, but we had some good times.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We had some good, good times.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Dogs can produce tears of joy when they are reunited with their humans.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s why I am trying to convince my wife to get a dog.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: To get a dog. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`s not going to let me. I did ask and I did not get a dog.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Maybe your old girlfriend, eggs and bacon would.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was good.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220826ei8q0012x\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 An Unexpected Battleground In The Fight Over Abortion Rights, Access To Medications Needed For Other Illnesses\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Turning now to an unexpected battleground in the fight over abortion rights, access to medications needed for other illnesses. One common drug used by more than a million patients for arthritis and even cancer has become harder to get.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian goes in depth to report on the impact for patients.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Noraa Wise is living with a rare autoimmune bone disease that causes pain in her ribs.\par \par \uc2 NORAA WISE (Suffers from Autoimmune Bone Disease): It felt like I didn`t want to breathe. It just felt like needles.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The 10-year-old Indiana girl struggled before her mom found treatment.\par \par \uc2 TYSE WISE (Mother): We started the methotrexate. It took about two weeks, and she said she wasn`t in pain anymore.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Methotrexate is often used to treat rheumatoid arthritis and Crohn`s disease, even cancer, but it can also be used to terminate nonviable pregnancies. Now pharmacies in some states have made methotrexate harder to get, regardless of what it`s being prescribed for.\par \par \uc2 TYSE WISE: As soon as I started hearing about women in other states having this issue, I started asking the question, is this going to be a problem?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: It was a problem for Annie England Noblin. She takes the drug for rheumatoid arthritis in Missouri, a state now banning almost all abortions. She says her pharmacist wouldn`t fill it until confirming it wasn`t for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 When you heard that, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 ANNIE ENGLAND NOBLIN (Suffers from Rheumatoid Arthritis): It was immediate anger.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In Austin, OB/GYN doctor John Thoppil says he`s prescribed methotrexate about 10 times over the last year for nonviable pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Some women, he says, are now reporting trouble getting it from pharmacists, who fear prosecution over aiding a Texas abortion.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOHN THOPPIL (OB/GYN): This is a wild world of, you know, of concern, because people are individually interpreting laws, and women are definitely going to get hurt.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Back in Indiana, Noraa`s mom says she can only hope there`s no interruption because methotrexate is a game-changer.\par \par \uc2 TYSE WISE: She`s felt so much better, and she`s being a kid.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Navigating a new landscape for medication amid the battle over abortion.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, CBS News, Austin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Schools across the country are struggling to find enough teachers. We will show you what some of them are doing to ease the crisis.\par \par \uc2 That story in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220816ei8f000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Tributes Pour In For Olivia Newton-John\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, we are remembering the life and the legacy of superstar, singer, and actress, you know that voice. That`s Olivia Newton- John.\par \par \uc2 We all heard the news yesterday, she died at the age of 73 after a decade`s long battle with breast cancer. Newton-John dominated the music charts and she stole our hearts on the big screen. Her "Grease" co-star, that of course would be \uc2 John Travolta\uc2  shared a tribute writing this: "My dearest Olivia, you made all of our lives so much better. Your impact was incredible. I love you so much. We will see you down the road and we`ll all be together again."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Grease.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Olivia Newton-John was a global superstar whose career spanned decades and continents. In the 70s and 80s, the English-born, Australian- raised singer was one of the world`s most popular entertainers with 14 Top 10 singles in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Olivia Newton-John performance.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the 1978 blockbuster "Grease," her flirtatious duet with \uc2 John Travolta\uc2 , "You`re the One that I Want" was a massive hit, from there the movies and hit songs just kept coming.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Xanadu.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With Newton-John playing opposite Gene Kelly in 1980s, "Xanadu."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Physical.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Physical" landed her the biggest hit of her career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Physical.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Named Billboard`s Song of the Year, "Physical" was actually banned by some radio stations for being too suggestive.\par \par \uc2 Newton-John was characteristically upbeat when we met for "CBS Sunday Morning" back in 2019.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: "I want to get physical, let`s get into physical. Let me hear your body talk."\par \par \uc2 Let`s get animal, I mean it was like I sang it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s get animal?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: Yes, let`s get animal, I want to get animal.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Physical.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The four-time Grammy Award winner would go on to sell more than 100 million records. In 1992, Newton John was diagnosed with breast cancer for the first time. That began a 30-year battle.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: I try not to think about it too much, but I try to meditate and be peaceful about it and know that everyone I love is there. So something to look forward to.\par \par \uc2 I have chosen that path to be grateful and feel good about things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In 2018, Newton-John reunited with Travolta and the rest of the original "Grease" cast for the film`s 40th Anniversary. A year after, Newton-John was diagnosed with cancer for a third time.\par \par \uc2 Auctioning off her wardrobe to benefit her Cancer Research Center. She humored us by trying on those iconic skin tight leather pants.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: Ta-dah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did it. Come out. Whoa. That`s amazing. That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: Well, they live again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: They live again.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Honestly Love You.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Newton-John will be remembered for her legacy of advocacy and optimism in the face of adversity.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: It`s a decision how you choose to feel about something. So I`ve chosen that path. I`m happy. I`m lucky. I`m grateful.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Honestly Love You.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll never forget that afternoon. It really was magical. We spent a lot of time with her. I would say that we became close, she would text me and send me pictures of things and say "Just thinking of you." She was really such a light and she never sugar coated what was happening with her body.\par \par \uc2 She said, I don`t want to dwell on it. I don`t want to -- you know, I want to help others. It`s not how I live my life. And when we went in 2019, we were all thinking maybe, you know, we`re not going to have a much longer as it turns out, we did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We had her for a few years, but you know, I saw the news yesterday, guys, a tear came to eyes. Olivia Newton-John was my first crush and I remember --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You aren`t the only one, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is actually a true story. I know, a lot of you boys out there that were like me. I remember waking my sister up in the middle of the night, telling her -- this is a true story -- I`m moving to Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 You know, kids run away like and they`re like, you know, for adventure. I`m like, no, I`m moving to Hollywood. And I`m going to find Olivia Newton- John. I`m going to tell her I love her. And she`s going to want to marry me. I`ll see you later. She rolled over and went back to bed.\par \par \uc2 But that`s how that`s -- the effect that she had on me. I watched "Grease" on VHS every day until the tape broke.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was a huge fan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, why didn`t I know this story before I interviewed her? Boy, would I have made a love connection.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. Oh my --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was happily married to her husband, John, for many years, but she would have loved to meet you, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was something else.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, absolutely amazing. "If You Love Me, Let Me Know," my favorite Olivia Newton-John song. She`ll be missed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And all of that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s your favorite --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All of that music --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you love me, let me know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mine is on --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, Aubrey Plaza is here to talk about her new movie, "Emily the Criminal." But right now, it`s 7:47, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, the latest on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home and reaction from Republican Senator, Tim Scott. There he is. He will be live in studio.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: Republican Senator Tim Scott will be here. He reacts to the search of former President Trump`s Florida home, and we`ll talk with him about his political future.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. A new movie explores how far one woman will go to pay a gigantic student loan bill. We`re going to talk to Aubrey Plaza and Theo Rossi, stars of "Emily the Criminal."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. It`s our debut of "\uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals." We`ll show you some cool products that could help you with the summer heat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like all things cool. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener," it is true "At Eight."\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents search Donald Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home looking for classified documents the former President may have taken from the White House.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN: They are treating him like any other criminal suspect and that`s high drama.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden tours communities in Kentucky devastated by deadly flooding.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We have all government resources. We`re not leaving. However long it takes, we`re going to be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Albuquerque Police boost security around mosques and schools over what appeared to be targeted killings of Muslim men.\par \par \uc2 AHMAD ASSED: They are missing their daily routine. They are missing their life. They are missing their sanity.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The US approves another billion dollars in military aid to Ukraine as Russian forces bombard towns and villages.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hopelessly Devoted to You.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And tributes pour in for Olivia Newton-John, the legendary pop singer and actress died after a very long battle with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Xanadu.")\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: I try not to think about it too much, but I try to meditate and be peaceful about it.\par \par \uc2 I`m happy. I`m lucky. I`m grateful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was such a light. I think today we`ll all be playing our favorite Olivia Newton-John songs. You`re going to hear her voice all day long. Deeply loved and deeply missed.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220811ei890008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 FBI Searches Trump`s Mar-a-Lago Home; Federal Sentences in Arbery Case; Search Intensifies in Albuquerque Murders; Ukraine`s War of Attrition; Sandy Hook Mother Speaks; Tributes Pour in For Olivia Newton- John; Interview with Sen. Tim Scott (R-SC); Castle Fit for Veterans; Emily the Criminal; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Catherine Herridge,  Major Garrett, Omar Villafranca, Charlie D`Agata, Vladimir Duthiers, Roxana  Saberi \par \uc2 14,147 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday, the 9th of August. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents search former President Trump`s Florida home, sources say the warrant was tied to missing White House records.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN (Former prosecutor): This is unexplored territory. This not something that happens every day or basically ever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Police in Albuquerque increase security at mosques and schools after the murders of four Muslim men.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: What is the reaction of this community?\par \par \uc2 AHMAD ASSED (Islamic Center of New Mexico President): It is one of astonishment.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  traveled to Eastern Kentucky to tour the area impacted by historic flooding.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): We never give up. We never stop. We never bow. We never bend, we just go forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we remember superstar, Olivia Newton-John. She died after a long battle with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN (Actress): I`m not a victim. I don`t want to be one. I feel that what has happened to me has had purpose.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The family of Gabby Petito announced a multimillion dollar wrongful death lawsuit against the Moab Police Department in Utah.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The belly flop for the ages and it happened at a waterpark in France.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Senate`s passage of Democrats` spending bill is another major victory for President Biden after other accomplishments including the signing of a gun reform bill and the death of al-Qaeda`s leader.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): You realize what this means, if he keeps this up, his approval rating is going to shoot all the way up to like 39 percent.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): One senator was maybe less than thrilled -- Bernie Sanders. Bernie tried reintroducing a lot of progressive ideas that Joe Manchin removed from the bill, but all of his efforts failed, which might be why he was later photographed sitting on the Capitol steps like this.\par \par \uc2 He looks like the depressed legislation from "Schoolhouse Rock."\par \par \uc2 I`m just a bill. I`m only a bill, and I`m sitting here waiting for soup.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS today. It really is a very tough morning for many of us. I know you`re feeling it, too.\par \par \uc2 We`re all thinking about the glorious career of Olivia Newton-John and she is no longer with us. You heard the news yesterday.\par \par \uc2 She had a string of top 10 hits from the 70s and the 80s. We all remember her with \uc2 John Travolta\uc2  in the movie version of "Grease." Look at her. This morning, we`re going to take a look back at her music and hear what she told us in a revealing "CBS Sunday Morning" interview this week. We did this interview back in 2019.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And first on CBS MORNINGS, we`ll talk with a Sandy Hook mom, who won a huge court victory against conspiracy theorist, Alex Jones, who claimed for years the massacre never happened.\par \par \uc2 Scarlet Lewis will be here in studio.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, there is so much news today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, there really is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But let`s get to the breaking news, it is coming from South Florida.\par \par \uc2 You heard about this, too, overnight. Something that has not happened before in American history. So just take that in for just a second.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  executed a search warrant at former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home. Agents spent much of yesterday at the club in Palm Beach, and CBS News has learned that the search was in regards to missing White House records.\par \par \uc2 After they left, dozens of demonstrators arrived showing their support for the former President. Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge is at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters in Washington, DC right now.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 We know that that word "unprecedented" gets used a lot, but searching a former president`s home really is an unprecedented move.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Well, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Yes, absolutely unprecedented. Former President Trump said his home was raided and he said \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents even broke into his safe. Neither the \uc2 FBI\uc2  nor the Justice Department is commenting on the search warrant. Though a source close to the former President told CBS News, he was not at his Florida home when Federal agents arrived.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: As he left New York`s Trump Tower, the former President had no comment about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s search warrant, but at a Monday tele-rally with US House candidate, \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 , he had this preview.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Another day in paradise. This was a strange day. You probably all read about it.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: According to a Trump adviser, \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents arrived at Mar-a-Lago Monday at around 10:00 AM. On social media, Trump said: "The raid on my home was not necessary or appropriate," claiming he was working and cooperating with the relevant government agencies.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN: This is unexplored territory.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Former prosecutor Harry Litman told CBS News securing a search warrant in a case like this would require a high standard of evidence.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gayle Martin.)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Have we ever been here before with a former President?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN: This kind of high stakes, high level service of a search warrant by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , I think that`s unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Multiple sources told CBS News the search warrant for Mar-a-Lago was connected to missing White House Records. Earlier this year, the National Archives asked the Justice Department to investigate Trump`s handling of official documents.\par \par \uc2 Under the Presidential Records Act, documents sent and received as President must be preserved. But in January, the National Archives said some records returned by Trump were ripped up or taped together.\par \par \uc2 In addition, sources saying classified material was among 15 boxes retrieved from his Florida estate. On Monday, a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporter posted these photos promoting an upcoming book claiming they showed Trump trying to flush documents while he was President.\par \par \uc2 Why does the \uc2 FBI\uc2  execute a search warrant versus request the records?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN: They were worried that he A. Wouldn`t give it up, and B. Might destroy the evidence.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: In his statement, Trump called the search warrant an attempt by radical left Democrats who desperately don`t want me to run for President in 2024.\par \par \uc2 ERIC TRUMP (Donald Trump`s son): They are threatened by \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and honestly, I hope and I`m saying this for the first time I hope he goes out and beats these guys.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: What does this tell you about the posture of the Justice Department in terms of their investigations of former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN: This is a big time move that in part is going to be stepping on a hornet`s nest, not the same as saying they`ll charge him, but man they are treating him like any other criminal suspect and that`s high drama.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Executing a search warrant on the private home of a former President would take several high level approvals, including the Courts, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and the Justice Department. We simply don`t know what was taken. But yesterday, a White House official insisted to CBS News, the White House had no advance notice of the raid -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on all of this, let`s go to Chief Washington correspondent, Major Garrett. He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Major, good morning.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s important to point out that the only reason we are talking about this today that we know about it at all is because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself issued a statement on it. That, as context, how significant is this \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of the former President`s home.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: It doesn`t get any more significant than this. Not only is former President Trump someone who looms large in America`s political conversation, he is actively talking about seeking the presidency again.\par \par \uc2 So this would be something the Justice Department would have to review at the highest levels, as was said in Catherine`s piece and a grand jury and a Judge would have to say there is probable cause to execute a search warrant because that search warrant is in pursuit of a possible crime having been committed.\par \par \uc2 All of that is true, but what we don`t have is very much transparency from the Justice Department or the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . We have one statement from the former President using very florid language, "My home is under siege, raided, occupied," essentially a search warrant was executed because the judicial authorities with all the relevant facts believe there is probable cause that a crime was committed.\par \par \uc2 But in the larger political context, this is a dangerous and unstable moment in American history. This is now a conversation about weaponizing the justice system at the Federal level and Trump supporters hear that and they may react in a very combustible way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, that`s where I was going to go with this follow up question here, Major. \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director, who presumably had to sign off on a raid of this significance is a Trump appointee after the current President famously fired the last \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director and also the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is supposed to be a nonpartisan agency of law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 It is doing its work on tenure terms at the director level. So, what -- talk a bit more about the implications of the former President using those explosive words to describe this important arm of the government?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Well, it`s not only the President`s words, so many other Republicans almost instantaneously as if former President Trump was a ringmaster directing them rhetorically into this battle describe this as a prime example of judicial corruption.\par \par \uc2 They described the Biden administration as a regime. They basically said that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  should be defunded. Some Republicans did, rushing to the defense of former President Trump, assuming that this has to be a nefarious and illegitimate and corrupt exercise of law enforcement prerogatives when it may be quite the opposite.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know what the underlying facts are. We haven`t seen the affidavit we haven`t seen the search warrant. And as I understand it, because of this sensitivity, there would be a conversation between former President Trump`s lawyers on the scene about what was taken and why and a receipt given to the former President describing what Federal authorities took and under what legal authorities.\par \par \uc2 The President -- the former President is in possession of that and could help explain to the public what happened he could release that if he wanted to, so could the Justice Department, so could the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What we lacked here is real insight into the underlying justification for this search warrant, what it sought, and what the applicable laws that possibly were violated were.\par \par \uc2 Until we know more about that this feels deeply politicized and the President is reveling in the atmosphere of victimhood as he has many times before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, Major, I hear the critics of the President saying, listen, there better be a real there, there, or this could further divide the country. His supporters say, look, once again, it`s a witch hunt against the President. You know, even using the word "unprecedented," depending on your point of view, unprecedented because this has never happened before, a witch hunt for President Trump, unprecedented if you`re against the President, because you think, well, surely they have something.\par \par \uc2 How do you square those two?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So we hear this said all the time, Gayle, the rule of law in our country matters, and no one is above the law. \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the Attorney General has said that repeatedly. He said it very emphatically to our colleague, Jeff Pegues, who asked him that question specifically referencing former President Trump about possible prosecution.\par \par \uc2 So the country has this moment where it has to evaluate underlying evidence, and what the evidence is and what the possible crimes are, and without that, you have a very political atmosphere, one in which the former President currently has the upper hand.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Major Garrett, so much to unpack here. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning to the Ahmaud Arbery case, three men convicted of killing him were sentenced again yesterday, this time on Federal hate crime charges.\par \par \uc2 A jury ruled they targeted Arbery because of his race. A Judge sentenced Greg and Travis McMichael to a second life sentence each. Both were already serving life for murder convictions in State Court, and she sentenced William "Roddie" Bryan to 35 years in addition to his previous life sentence.\par \par \uc2 Ahmaud Arbery`s mother responded to the news outside the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WANDA COOPER-JONES (Ahmaud Arbery`s mother): We finally have gotten justice for Ahmaud again. I know that Ahmaud is pleased with the work that we`ve done as a community. I know he`s in heaven, he is pleased, and with that being said, I`m pleased as well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All three men will serve their sentences in a Georgia State Prison.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Police in Albuquerque, New Mexico are increasing security at mosques and schools after the recent murders of four Muslim men there. Authorities believe the killings may be linked and they`re asking for the public`s health.\par \par \uc2 Local investigators and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  say they are looking for this vehicle of interest. That`s what they`re saying.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca has made his way to Albuquerque for us where he is talking to members of the Muslim community there.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good morning. We learned this is a small Muslim community, around 3,000 to 5,000 people and they are scared. The mosque President told us that some people have even moved away until a suspect is caught. And CBS News has learned after talking to Federal sources that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is looking to see if these are hate crimes, the work of a serial killer or both.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHARIEF HADI (Brother of Mohammad Zaher Ahmadi): They shot him and a cigarette was over there, right there.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Emotions are still raw for Sharief Hadi were the nine months after his younger brother, Mohammad Zaher Ahmadi a medic was killed behind their families Hello Market in what investigators say may be a targeted attack.\par \par \uc2 You put your heart into this.\par \par \uc2 SHARIEF HADI: Yes, his heart not my heart. It was his heart. I have to run his heart right now.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The brothers were born in Afghanistan, but fled during war in the 1980s. Now, Hadi tells us, he, like others in the Muslim community feel terrified.\par \par \uc2 Do you find yourself looking over your shoulder?\par \par \uc2 SHARIEF HADI: The honest with you, I don`t care about my life anymore. When I lost my younger brother, I didn`t care.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Authorities announce they are investigating a possible link between his brother`s murder and the murders of three other Muslim men.\par \par \uc2 The most recent victim, 25-year-old, Naeem Hussain was shot just hours after attending a funeral service for Muhammad Afzaal Hussain and Aftab Hussein. Both were also killed in the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Local police brought in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  after the second murder.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, investigators and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  released a photo of what appears to be a dark silver Volkswagen Jetta, which they say could be involved in all four murders.\par \par \uc2 Ahmad Assed is President of the Islamic Center of New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 What is the reaction of this community?\par \par \uc2 AHMAD ASSED: It is one of astonishment, one of dishearten. They are disheartened. They are concerned. They live in fear.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: People are so scared, they`re missing prayer service.\par \par \uc2 AHMAD ASSED: Well, they are. They`re missing their daily routine. They`re missing their life. They`re missing their sanity.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Muslim leaders are telling the faithful that they can pray it home if they don`t feel safe. Police have set up an anonymous tip line and the reward is up to $20,000.00 -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The US is sending another billion dollars` worth of ammunition and military equipment to Ukraine. It`s the largest single draw down from US military stockpile so far. This comes amid increased fighting in the south and east of the country.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in Mykolaiv, a frontline city on Ukraine`s south coast.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 That is the trauma ward of a hospital bombed just over a week ago. Amazingly, nobody was hurt.\par \par \uc2 Weapons, weapons, weapons and more weapons, that`s the message we got from the top General here ahead of a massive counteroffensive in a showdown for this port city.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: We spoke with Major General Dmytro Marchenko in the rubble of the regional government headquarters.\par \par \uc2 (MAJOR GENERAL DMYTRO MARCHENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: No one here needs reminding that Russia is still holds the upper hand in heavy weapons. The General told us that for every 100 shells the Russians fire at the city, his forces can respond with only 10.\par \par \uc2 But the Kremlin`s forces are running out of precision-guided missiles like the one that destroyed this building in the early days of the war. General Marchenko gave us a long list of weapons being unleashed on the city from long range missiles to Soviet-era rockets from the 60s, even firing anti- aircraft munitions at buildings.\par \par \uc2 And they`re using cluster bombs here to?\par \par \uc2 (MAJOR GENERAL DMYTRO MARCHENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The General credited American supplied HIMARS for striking weapons depots and severing supply lines far behind Russian frontlines.\par \par \uc2 What difference has the HIMARS weapon system made for the war effort?\par \par \uc2 (MAJOR GENERAL DMYTRO MARCHENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "It`s one of the main factors why we kept our ground," he said, "And started taking advantage on the field."\par \par \uc2 But the Russian onslaught has been relentless. The frontline town of Bakhmut has suffered ferocious bombardment. The shelling of the Eastern Donbas region`s main water supply has left residents in the city of Slovyansk without water for two months, adding to the suffering in cities under siege.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: This city comes under nightly bombardment. It was hit again last night and early this morning, explosions off in the distance. The General told us it happens around three times a day hitting random civilian targets, even hospitals -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Charlie D`Agata reporting from Ukraine, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to celebrate the extraordinary life -- and extraordinary is the word -- of Olivia Newton-John. The moving tribute from \uc2 John Travolta\uc2  and a touching moment she had with us showing off that iconic outfit. This was back in 2019. Go, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, the mom who lost her son in the Sandy Hook massacre, and just won a massive jury award against a conspiracy theorist who lied about it. Scarlet Lewis talks with us about confronting Alex Jones from the witness stand in court.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning, bestselling author 