jury awarded Scarlet Lewis -- that`s Jesse`s his mom -- and Neil Heslin -- that`s Jesse`s dad -- more than $49 million in damages in their lawsuit against Alex Jones.\par \par \uc2 The couple says that they were viciously harassed by his followers, who believed his claim that the killings were staged. Twenty children, we all remember this, 20 little children and six adults were killed in the 2012 mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 In her testimony, Scarlet Lewis confronted Jones directly.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS (Jesse Lewis` mother): Jesse was real. I am a real mom. I know you know that. That`s the problem. I know you know that and you keep saying it. You keep saying it. Why?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should note that Alex Jones later acknowledged that the Sandy Hook shooting was "100 percent real."\par \par \uc2 Scarlett Lewis joins us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Scarlet Lewis.\par \par \uc2 I watched that video over and over and I kept watching your face wondering how you could be so calm speaking to him, because that`s the first time or was it the first time you had seen him face-to-face?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Absolutely the first time live, yes, and I was literally looking into his eyes from the witness stand.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so what were you thinking, Scarlett, because at one point, he had accused you of being a crisis actor, you`re Jesse`s mom, he accused you of being a crisis actor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And by implication, arguing that Jesse never lived. You guys are actors and Jesse was never a real boy in the real world.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Never existed. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take us in the courtroom that day for you as you`re facing this man face-to-face.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who is so callous and cruel.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: I was so nervous. I remember taking a cup of water and my hand was shaking. But then I looked into Alex Jones` eyes who was sitting right in front of me and I really felt I could only feel compassion for him. And really, that`s how I maintained my composure.\par \par \uc2 I had already forgiven him, but that doesn`t mean that you don`t hold somebody accountable. And what I saw was just a man, a human being, in pain, lonely, with no one there to testify for him. He had not a person there, and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I find your compassion extraordinary under the circumstances, because we all know how much you loved your little boy. You had sued for $150 million in damages. You were awarded $49 million.\par \par \uc2 What were your thoughts about the verdict? Did you think that it was fair? Were you angry about the verdict?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: I thought that it was fair, and really my goal was to send a message about the importance of truth.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It wasn`t about the money for you?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Absolutely not. No, I have been -- I`ve been spreading a Choose Love Movement for the past nine-and-a-half years with the focus of keeping kids safe. And to me, everything that Alex Jones was saying about the tragedy, not even being real and 24 percent of the American public, either doubting the facts around Sandy Hook or thinking that it didn`t happen was going directly against my goal, which is to keep kids safe and safeguard their wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I never thought it could be about the money for you because nothing, no amount of money could replace your little boy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you made a decision consciously to return to this terrible topic and confront Alex Jones in Court.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That had to be a difficult choice for you. What is that message 