 With such poise and such grace, considering it had to be one of the worst moments of your life.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How are you able to do this?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: A message that my six-year-old son left on our kitchen chalkboard --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Is really the reason he wrote three words, "Nurturing, healing, love" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At six years old?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: At six years old, phonetically spelled because he was in first grade just learning to write, but I knew that that was a spiritual awareness that he had, that he wasn`t going to be on Earth for very much longer. It was a message of comfort for his family and friends, but also inspiration. It is where we needed to turn in order to survive and thrive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle was asking how you deal and how you heal, we are in terrible times, I`ll just call it what it is. Seems like we`re talking about a mass shooting on a weekly basis.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Over 350 since Sandy Hook, nine-and-a-half years ago.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It might hit us differently because it hasn`t impacted us. We`re just delivering the news to our viewers. But you, when you read another story, turn on the news, you see another story about a mass shooting, how does that affect you?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: It`s devastating. It`s devastating. And it makes me double down on my efforts because I am literally trying to change our direction in our country towards truth and love and away from fear and hate.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that we`re making progress, but we need everybody to join the movement in order to be successful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Alex Jones is still talking, isn`t he?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Any remorse?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is still talking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Any remorse from him? When you were looking in his eyes, from our camera angle, we can`t see his face.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is there remorse there?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: I believe that there was. I believe that he knows that Sandy Hook happened, and that he`s made bad choices and I believe that he could make different choices going forward.\par \par \uc2 I think that he wants to. I think sometimes greed gets in the way. But you know, we have one life and we are here really to love and support one another, and I hope that he makes that decision. I know that he knows now that it is a choice that he can make.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. All right.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: And I have hope.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Scarlett, let me say this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am in awe of it, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: it`s not a surprise that Jesse wrote the word "nurturing" on the chalkboard at your house. He didn`t get that way by himself.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He didn`t get that way by himself. Scarlett, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For coming to talk to us this morning. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Up next, remembering the global superstar, Olivia Newton- John and what she told Gayle about being happy, lucky, and grateful.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, we are remembering the life and the legacy of superstar, singer, and actress, you know that voice. That`s Olivia Newton- John.\par \par \uc2 We all heard the news yesterday, she died at the age of 73 after a decade`s long battle with breast cancer. Newton-John dominated the music charts and she stole our hearts on the big screen. Her "Grease" co-star, that of course would be \uc2 John Travolta\uc2  shared a tribute writing this: "My dearest Olivia, you made all of our lives so much better. Your impact was incredible. I love you so much. We will see you down the road and we`ll all be together again."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Grease.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Olivia Newton-John was a global superstar whose career spanned decades and continents. In the 70s and 80s, the English-born, Australian- raised singer was one of the world`s most popular entertainers with 14 Top 10 singles in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Olivia Newton-John performance.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the 1978 blockbuster "Grease," her flirtatious duet with \uc2 John Travolta\uc2 , "You`re the One that I Want" was a massive hit, from there the movies and hit songs just kept coming.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Xanadu.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With Newton-John playing opposite Gene Kelly in 1980s, "Xanadu."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Physical.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Physical" landed her the biggest hit of her career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Physical.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Named Billboard`s Song of the Year, "Physical" was actually banned by some radio stations for being too suggestive.\par \par \uc2 Newton-John was characteristically upbeat when we met for "CBS Sunday Morning" back in 2019.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: "I want to get physical, let`s get into physical. Let me hear your body talk."\par \par \uc2 Let`s get animal, I mean it was like I sang it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s get animal?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: Yes, let`s get animal, I want to get animal.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Physical.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The four-time Grammy Award winner would go on to sell more than 100 million records. In 1992, Newton John was diagnosed with breast cancer for the first time. That began a 30-year battle.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: I try not to think about it too much, but I try to meditate and be peaceful about it and know that everyone I love is there. So something to look forward to.\par \par \uc2 I have chosen that path to be grateful and feel good about things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In 2018, Newton-John reunited with Travolta and the rest of the original "Grease" cast for the film`s 40th Anniversary. A year after, Newton-John was diagnosed with cancer for a third time.\par \par \uc2 Auctioning off her wardrobe to benefit her Cancer Research Center. She humored us by trying on those iconic skin tight leather pants.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: Ta-dah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did it. Come out. Whoa. That`s amazing. That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: Well, they live again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: They live again.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Honestly Love You.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Newton-John will be remembered for her legacy of advocacy and optimism in the face of adversity.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: It`s a decision how you choose to feel about something. So I`ve chosen that path. I`m happy. I`m lucky. I`m grateful.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Honestly Love You.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll never forget that afternoon. It really was magical. We spent a lot of time with her. I would say that we became close, she would text me and send me pictures of things and say "Just thinking of you." She was really such a light and she never sugar coated what was happening with her body.\par \par \uc2 She said, I don`t want to dwell on it. I don`t want to -- you know, I want to help others. It`s not how I live my life. And when we went in 2019, we were all thinking maybe, you know, we`re not going to have a much longer as it turns out, we did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We had her for a few years, but you know, I saw the news yesterday, guys, a tear came to eyes. Olivia Newton-John was my first crush and I remember --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You aren`t the only one, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is actually a true story. I know, a lot of you boys out there that were like me. I remember waking my sister up in the middle of the night, telling her -- this is a true story -- I`m moving to Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 You know, kids run away like and they`re like, you know, for adventure. I`m like, no, I`m moving to Hollywood. And I`m going to find Olivia Newton- John. I`m going to tell her I love her. And she`s going to want to marry me. I`ll see you later. She rolled over and went back to bed.\par \par \uc2 But that`s how that`s -- the effect that she had on me. I watched "Grease" on VHS every day until the tape broke.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was a huge fan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, why didn`t I know this story before I interviewed her? Boy, would I have made a love connection.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. Oh my --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was happily married to her husband, John, for many years, but she would have loved to meet you, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was something else.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, absolutely amazing. "If You Love Me, Let Me Know," my favorite Olivia Newton-John song. She`ll be missed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And all of that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s your favorite --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All of that music --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you love me, let me know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mine is on --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, Aubrey Plaza is here to talk about her new movie, "Emily the Criminal." But right now, it`s 7:47, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, the latest on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home and reaction from Republican Senator, Tim Scott. There he is. He will be live in studio.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: Republican Senator Tim Scott will be here. He reacts to the search of former President Trump`s Florida home, and we`ll talk with him about his political future.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. A new movie explores how far one woman will go to pay a gigantic student loan bill. We`re going to talk to Aubrey Plaza and Theo Rossi, stars of "Emily the Criminal."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. It`s our debut of "\uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals." We`ll show you some cool products that could help you with the summer heat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like all things cool. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener," it is true "At Eight."\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents search Donald Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home looking for classified documents the former President may have taken from the White House.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN: They are treating him like any other criminal suspect and that`s high drama.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden tours communities in Kentucky devastated by deadly flooding.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We have all government resources. We`re not leaving. However long it takes, we`re going to be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Albuquerque Police boost security around mosques and schools over what appeared to be targeted killings of Muslim men.\par \par \uc2 AHMAD ASSED: They are missing their daily routine. They are missing their life. They are missing their sanity.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The US approves another billion dollars in military aid to Ukraine as Russian forces bombard towns and villages.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hopelessly Devoted to You.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And tributes pour in for Olivia Newton-John, the legendary pop singer and actress died after a very long battle with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Xanadu.")\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN: I try not to think about it too much, but I try to meditate and be peaceful about it.\par \par \uc2 I`m happy. I`m lucky. I`m grateful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was such a light. I think today we`ll all be playing our favorite Olivia Newton-John songs. You`re going to hear her voice all day long. Deeply loved and deeply missed.\par \par \uc2 Right now though, we`ve got the latest on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of former President Trump`s Florida home. Trump said last night that his Mar-a-Lago resort was "under siege." He was not there when the agents arrived around 10 o`clock yesterday morning with a legal search warrant.\par \par \uc2 They were there, we are told, for most of the day. Sources tell CBS News that the warrant is part of a Justice Department investigation into claims that Trump mishandled records that should have been preserved by law.\par \par \uc2 The National Archives said in January that some of the records returned by Trump were ripped up and then taped together. Our sources say the Archives found 15 boxes of records including classified material at Mar-a-Lago earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s Republican supporters have denounced this search saying the Biden administration is targeting him for political reasons. The Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have declined to comment on this matter.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are joined now by Republican Senator, Tim Scott of South Carolina. He is here because he is the author of a new book. It`s called "America: A Redemption Story." Senator, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We have the book right here. It`s a good read. I think it`s going to connect with a lot of people. It`s got two chapters on the former President, President Trump.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You clearly have a good relationship with him. He`s very nice to your mom.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re at this extraordinary moment in American history where that former President is a subject of multiple investigations and his home was searched by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  overnight.\par \par \uc2 So I have to put the question to you, do you still feel that he is the best representative of the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Certainly, as the largest voice in American politics, period, Republican or Democrat, and it feels like he`ll continue to be. The more focus you put on him, the more likely the supporters will rally around him.\par \par \uc2 There`s a huge divide in our country --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But wait a second, before you go, Senator, that wasn`t the question --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You said largest, which is --\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): The largest voice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Effectively true. Right. He won a straw polling.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s representation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you still think -- yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, I mean, the good news is I get to answer the question now. I see the big question.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is that he is without question, the most powerful voice in all of politics and that`s why so many people, left and right, continue to focus on President Trump.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, but why this is such a dangerous moment in our history, and a lot of people have pointed this out is because we are divided.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The wings of our government that are supposed to be nonpartisan, but now seem to be politicized, and one of those things is the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So do you think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- do you still believe and trust in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to do its job even with a former President in a non-partisan way or do you believe as the former President says that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is executing a witch hunt.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, we`ve had the \uc2 FBI\uc2  before the Judiciary Committee just in the last two weeks trying to figure out why they do what they do, and there has been lots of questions before this raid about whether or not the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is doing their job, apolitical. And we don`t know the answer to that question yet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): This is going to raise more questions in my opinion, we need to let this play out and see exactly what happens. But we should all have been stunned and surprised and shocked at what happened yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The real question is, A: What is the Federal Records Act? And B: What are we talking about? It has to be something of incredible magnitude for at least my side of the aisle to say that was warranted. Without that, I think we`re going to find ourselves in a very big mess as it relates to the credibility of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you don`t have any concern that they`re going to find anything at the Mar-a-Lago --\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, I think we should really, as opposed to rushing to judgment, the most important thing that we could do is let it play out, because I have no idea where they were looking for. I don`t think anybody knows what they were looking for.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On that point, I think it`s interesting that as you sit before us today, you are not saying the former President Trump is the best representative of the Republican Party and you`re also not saying don`t worry, America, you can trust the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, I think that I am saying that I love my new book and that is what our conversation is about, why I believe in second chances.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  was appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , we should point that out, too.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But in your book, you say when it comes to President Trump, rather than try to soften his heart edges, you found it best to learn to utilize them. And on January 6th, you said it was a tragic day, a culmination of individuals making bad choices.\par \par \uc2 You do not hold \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in the book anyway, accountable for what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Gayle, one of the reasons why your viewers would want to buy the book is to get that behind-the-scenes view of what actually happened at the Capitol, as opposed to just hearing the media`s account for it, which most were not inside the United States Capitol, I was.\par \par \uc2 We can walk through how it feels to be hunted. You walk through rolling your sleeves up, taking your tie off, and literally asking yourself, "What weapon can I find to defend myself until I die?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you talked about having a pen.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I had a pen in my hand. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You talked about actually calming everybody down and saying, "Look, everybody, let`s just take a second."\par \par \uc2 But I want to go back to Donald Trump`s role in it for just a second. He has taken no responsibility for it and January 6, you heard from rioters who said, look, we went because he asked us to. We were there because he called us there.\par \par \uc2 Doesn`t the President have -- don`t his words have power? And shouldn`t he be more careful with his words?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was encouraging people that he knew were armed.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, I don`t know that anyone knows that the crowd was armed when the call to come to the Capitol was put out there, number one. Number two, I will say this though, every person has the responsibility of taking fully the decisions that they make, and be responsible for those decisions.\par \par \uc2 I think giving that to anybody other than the individual is giving the individual a pass and I want the people --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But doesn`t the President have responsibility, too, Senator?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Of course, of course. Of course.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I would say the most responsibility.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I would say that we all have the most responsibility for our own words. This is easy for us to put the blame on someone else.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): But as the guy that felt hunted in the moment, and watching my -- watching my Senate colleagues, look for a route to escape, one of the things you`ll see in the book is how important it is in the middle of a chaotic situation to find peace, clarity, and then be decisive in going back out that same --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It sounded terrifying.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): -- decision.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I talk about Chapter Five in the book because the headline is "Tim Scott for President." I go, "Oh, oh. He is getting ready to tell us something."\par \par \uc2 Tim Scott, for President, the beginning of Chapter Five.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you go on and say it`s for President of your high school. Tim Scott, for President. Tim Scott for President.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then you go on to talk about it was a campaign that you ran, but it does raise the question. Many people think when you`re writing a book like this, it`s because you are thinking about running for President.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It certainly reads that way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And your publisher apparently thought, you know, this guy is running for President because they wrote it in the front of the book in a message.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So talk to us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Let me share --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Break it down for us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Let me break it out right here for you. So breaking news, please, listen carefully.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, we are listening.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): If you`re going to run for President, you don`t say in a book, according to my publishers in 2022.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s right. They did make a typo error there.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I failed Civics, but I got that part right, there`s no presidential election in 2022. There is, however, a re-election campaign in 2022 and I am a part of that. So I`m looking forward to representing the people of South Carolina for hopefully the next six years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know and you love South Carolina, blah-blah-blah.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I do love South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the people blah-blah-blah.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): But you love South Carolina, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all understand that, but, but what would it take for you to consider a run for President? Do you think you would bring something to the table that maybe other candidates don`t have at this point?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I certainly believe that my best objective today is to make sure that I`m as successful as possible in my re-election. And as a part of my re-election strategy, it is to make sure that the voters and the constituents of South Carolina, whether you vote for me or not, see me as your United States Senator.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, you should not worry about what`s next if you haven`t finished what`s now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 Now, this book is titled "America: A Redemption Story," sir, this is a great country, but we`ve had some dark times.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Where are we in our redemption journey as a country?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes, thank God, we`re on the right path and the last 50 years have been the best 50 years in this America -- in America`s history. Think about the fact that we`ve suffered through a Civil War. We`ve had the Jim Crow South, my grandfather was born in 1921, and I talk a lot about his journey in the book.\par \par \uc2 We also have the 1960s. We had the challenges of the 70s. We had Rodney King in the 90s.\par \par \uc2 Comparatively speaking, we are in a far better position than we have been. We are not finished yet with the journey. There is much to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And a lot more do go.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Without any question. But listen, we`re also celebrating the fact that three out of four, on this stage right now, of African-Americans. We are talking about the fact that we`ve had an African- American President, African-American Vice President. We`ve had a Chief of Staff who is an African-American.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had more success in the last 50 years than we had the first 190 combined. And so we should continue to focus on what needs to be done while celebrating --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What has been done.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): What we`ve already accomplished together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The American Dream is never realized overnight, not an event, but a way of life. One of the many good lines in this book.\par \par \uc2 Senator Scott, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The book is "America: A Redemption Story," and it is on sale right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Choosing hope, creating unity.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, there`s two subtitles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Senator Scott.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Presidential.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to talk with Aubrey Plaza and Theo Rossi about their new thriller. It`s called "Emily the Criminal." That`s the perfect title for that movie.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, how a former soldier suffering from PTSD rebuilt his life one stone at a time and created a sanctuary for other veterans who need support.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One in five American veterans who were deployed to Iraq and Afghanistan have suffered from post traumatic stress disorder.\par \par \uc2 One former British soldier has found a unique way to rebuild after the trauma of his service tore his life apart.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi visited him in Wales to see how he is now helping others.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Day after day, Mike Allen haul stone after heavy stone and log after log up a mountain in Wales.\par \par \uc2 So this is it.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN (Veteran): Yes. This is the castle I`ve been building now for last three years.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Allen didn`t plan to build a castle at first, but as the foundation took shape, he began rebuilding something else -- his spirit.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: The goal to create some form of hope has kept me alive to a certain degree.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Kept you alive.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: Yes. I think so.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Skill and luck kept him alive when he served with the British Army in Afghanistan over a decade ago, but death was never distant. One day on patrol, he saw an Afghan police officer step on a \uc2 Taliban\uc2  mine.\par \par \uc2 What happened then?\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: This big explosion. Me and my colleague went sideways getting across the floor. Best way to describe it, it looked like his legs had been stuck together with superglue, just all different directions. His torso was twisted sideways.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Back in Wales, Allen suffered from post traumatic stress disorder.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: You come back and you`re feeling different person.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: He left the Army and turned to drinking and drugs. His marriage broke down, and one day, so did he.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: And last time, I just kept eating painkillers and eating them and eating them with the hope not to wake up --\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: But you woke up.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: I woke up the next day. That`s when things went really bad though to be honest.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Homeless, Allen searched for solace and the solitude of these mountains. He made a wooden cabin, but within months, local authorities demolished it, sending him spiraling again.\par \par \uc2 Then a nearby farming family learned of Allen`s plight and gave him a chance to start over on their land.\par \par \uc2 As Allen pushed his body to its limits, carrying up to 130 pounds of stone at a time and chopping logs, barely resting, he found purpose and call.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: Putting the stone together, it is like mindfulness, like a giant jigsaw. So you can kind of just forget, be in the moment.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This one.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: And he found love again. Rachel Whiting lost her husband, who was also in the military to suicide in 2011.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL WHITING (In a relationship with Mike Allen): For me, that night, it was complete and utter hell. It`s a pain that will never go.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Has this place helped at all?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL WHITING: Being here and helping him and him helping me, we`ve grown so much. And even when I feel like I don`t want to do much and I am them having one of those days, he`ll say, you sure you don`t want to chop a tree time with me? Are you sure you don`t want to come? Just try one store. Come on. Oh, all right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: And three, two, one.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: As the castle has grown their community has, too.\par \par \uc2 Each week, around a hundred people visit. Local residents, other troubled veterans, strangers inspired by the 42-year-old whose charity called Endex, Allen offers fitness sessions, skills training, counseling, a sanctuary nestled in nature and hope.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: As bad as things are, there`s always a future. It is that belief, the belief is important.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Trauma therapist, Gareth Noble says like his friend Allen, we all face adversity. How we overcome it is up to us.\par \par \uc2 Do we need to build a castle?\par \par \uc2 GARETH NOBLE (Trauma therapist): A castle, not hide. A castle in here.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL WHITING: Nice.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Allen now lives in a nearby village, but his journey goes on.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: I think I`m still in the healing process to a certain degree.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: What will it take for you to feel that you`ve healed?\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN: There won`t be a need for me to keep doing this.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: He plans to finish the third and final floor in the coming months. More space for more healing.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Roxana Saberi in the Sirhowy Valley, Wales.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No matter what happens, when things fall apart, you can always rebuild.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, we`ll show you the products to help you keep cool for hours on these hot summer days. Plus, we`ll be talking with the actors, Aubrey Plaza and Theo Rossi about their new crime thriller.\par \par \uc2 But first, local news coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Get your thing, do what you want to do. All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is about the power of movement. Researchers working with a British health campaign recently found that 67 percent of active adults polled, that`s two out of three of us reported that their mood improved after physical activity.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think you needed a research to tell you that, because we know that`s true. One doctor with the campaign suggested very specific ways for tackling our biggest hurdles. She says walking. That`s right, walking is the best way to give your mind a boost if you`re feeling stuck or unmotivated. It brings more oxygenated blood to your brain.\par \par \uc2 If stress is bothering you, she says this, go for a swim. Swimming releases cortisol which helps your body deal with stress. You`ve got to change your clothes, get in the water -- that`s a lot to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Got to find the pool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve got to find a pool. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Goggles on and everything.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: First world problems.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t want your hair wet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a pool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And if you`re feeling anxious or worrying too much, this is the best of all, dance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get this. Dancing releases all kinds of hormones that bring on feelings of happiness. I think Tony --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What kinds of hormones?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re not talking -- I`m not saying this for you about anxiety or stress, but I do know you like a good dance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s see, dancing brings out anxiety at best. For me personally, and I think other people feel uncomfortable, too when they start dancing. So it`s not good anybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, you better change that, Mr. Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so I`ll grab it. Speaking of dancing, my "Talk of the Table" is about guys who actually dance when they score a touchdown.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Players from the \uc2 Green Bay Packers\uc2 . They`re joining one a little fan with an impromptu tea party. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Ashley Rubens via Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 GIRL: You want some tea?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes. Oh.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome right there.\par \par \uc2 Listen, training camp, practice, the monotony of waking up every day doing the same thing. It`ll really get to you. Your body will start to fatigue. Your mind will start to fatigue, but it`s fans like this that make it all worth it. The players walk by --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there is nothing in the cup.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing in the cup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love how they just go along with it. I love that, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And couple of players said "Oh, this is the best tea ever," and she just lit up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I think that`s so sweet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" real quick is about Red Lobster, which is delicious. Sells a lot of lobster --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cheddar cheese biscuits.\par \