ds of problems, right? People sleep in separate bedrooms because of snoring.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: It`s true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So this might be of help.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: That`s right. So Smart Nora, when you get this home, you`re going to place this inflation device into your own pillow on the bottom. Then next to it here, you`ll see, there it is. It looks like a mouse. That`s actually a listening device and it`s going to detect when you snore.\par \par \uc2 As soon as it detects it, this will slowly inflate and deflate and that actually moves the head. It stimulates the neck muscles.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Smart Nora.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: While you`re sleeping? While you`re sleeping --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, but it`s very slow and gentle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Snoring is caused by a flap in your throat that`s making noise.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Right. So then once you stimulate those neck muscles --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve done research on that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Once you stimulate those neck muscles --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How much is that? I do think that is cool.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Normally it`s $399.00. Today it`s $299.00. A hundred dollars off, it`s a 25 percent saving, and it will save your marriage, too. It did mine.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: I was going to say save the marriage.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A divorce lawyer is that per hour.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: It saved mine. It saved mine.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Elizabeth Werner, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, we partner with all of these companies. To get these incredible deals, to purchase anything that you just saw, scan the QR code on your screen. Go ahead. You can also visit CBSdeals.com.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This QR code.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jon Towers, did you know the Bread song? Did you know the Bread song?\par \par \uc2 No one knew the Bread song, but me?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Bread song?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Aubrey knew it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that the Aubrey song? Well, she was named after the song.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, she knew the song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You learn something new every day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Every day.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We had so much in the show today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We had a big --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jam-packed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wait till tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you know, we`re going to see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you should tune in anyway.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And here at the table. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220809ei8900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Doctors Share Abortion Law Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 2,704 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of Indiana`s largest employers is sharing its concerns after the State passed a near total ban on abortions. Now, Indiana is the first State to pass such a measure since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. The pharmaceutical giant, that`s \uc2 Eli Lilly and Company\uc2  warned this: "We will be forced to plan for more employment growth outside our home state," as state laws change across the country.\par \par \uc2 Now doctors are becoming increasingly concerned. They are very worried about what they can and cannot do for their patients. So, we spoke to four OB/GYNs from Arizona, Wisconsin, Ohio, and Georgia about how state abortion laws create a lot of confusion and all of them expressed concerns the new laws will put their patient`s health at risk.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY (Wisconsin OB/GYN): As soon as we heard the decision, we closed everything down, leaving over 70 women in Wisconsin with appointments that we weren`t able to do for them.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA (Georgia OB/GYN): We`ve been preparing, it didn`t make it any more devastating.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID HACKNEY (Ohio OB/GYN): I remember asking, when is the law going to change? How much time do we have to get ready for it? And they started to text back and said the law has actually changed right now.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: I`ve lived in the South for most of my life. This is my community, and I`m very concerned about the ability of my patients, my community to access the care that we need.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIA KWATRA (Arizona OB/GYN): When Medicine is criminalized, physicians are scared to do the jobs they`re trained to do and that absolutely impacts patient care.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: People are very quick to talk about how these abortion restrictions have medical emergency exceptions, but for us as clinicians actually practicing on the ground, those exceptions just don`t really make sense.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY: Now, many of those options have been taken away and that is just unbearable for all of us.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIA KWATRA: Some women will have what we call a spontaneous miscarriage where they start to miscarry on their own.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY: We have to counsel them that they may get very sick, that they may develop a life-threatening infection and it could potentially cost them their lives.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: You can`t make a one size fits all definition or law that can take all of these situations into account.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIA KWATRA: We`re seeing issues in labor and delivery, for example of women with pre viable pregnancies that come in with infection that need to be delivered and so there`s a lot of back and forth as to how sick does the patient need to be to get delivered?\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: Does she have to develop an infection? Can we intervene before she develops an infection?\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIA KWATRA: How sick does she have to be before the staff felt comfortable delivering her are because of this law hanging over our head?\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: If someone who`s pregnant comes in after getting in a car accident or comes in with an inflamed appendix, are doctors going to be afraid to get imaging to take them to surgery if they need surgery because with those treatments, there is a chance that you could inadvertently cause an abortion?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID HACKNEY: What if someone has cancer and they`re not going to die right then, but because they don`t receive abortion care, they don`t receive the usual cancer care that they would receive unless they die from the cancer five years later. Is that a life that we could have saved?\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: Doctors are having to figure out, does this fit under the law? Am I going to be criminalized for intervening?\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY: I think physicians are afraid that they will be accused of providing abortion care when really they`re providing miscarriage management.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: I think my greatest fear is how our patients are going to be able to get the care they need.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY: It means so much to me to be able to take care of my patients and they are suffering because of this, suffering economically, suffering -- their families are suffering. Their futures will suffer. But some of them are going to die and they could be saved. That`s a travesty.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another big concern is misinformation the doctors say. One of them we spoke to said she heard from a pregnant patient who is too afraid to get the medical care, why? Because she fears that her health will be sacrificed for her unborn child.\par \par \uc2 OB/GYNs are also concerned that doctors will now leave their states and create even more of a healthcare vacuum.\par \par \uc2 You know, the doctors on the ground, guys, who are doing the work have a very important voice in this conversation. They laid out different scenarios I`ve never thought about before that they deal with all the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. And we have to understand because there is no uniformity, there is confusion with these doctors, all the while there`s lives at risk -- the mother in the baby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it`s clearly a lot more complicated than you think if you just read the headlines and this is government getting in between the relationship of a patient and their doctor and doctors don`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, it should be sorted out. We`ll keep following it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we will be right back with more CBS MORNINGS. Thank you for watching. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are just one day away from the start of our new series and it`s something cool just for you. "\uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals."\par \par \uc2 Starting tomorrow, an expert will join us to share devices that will make your life a little bit easier including innovative gadgets that may help you and your family stay safe on the road.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have it all at exclusive discounted prices available only on CBS MORNINGS. To sign up for first access to the exclusive deals, visit CBSdeals.com or text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s that number again, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do it right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead we`re going to introduce you to a mom who is on a mission to help kids feel included at play and when reading.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And doesn`t it feel good? Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" and Gayle, are you walking on sunshine?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. You know, when people said what`s your song? What`s the song that best describes you? I always pick that one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a good song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I just feel it`s one of those songs that makes you feel happy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, you bring sunshine to this table.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I come back from vacation fatter and darker. More me to love, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You look good, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: More me to love.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is hacks to make the most of your summer vacation. Tony and I just came back from one. So this is the thing they say. They come from some very busy executives.\par \par \uc2 First the number one rule they say, do not read the e-mails, your work e- mails that you get on vacation. Do not read them and do not respond.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ooh, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very hard to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very hard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t -- I always respond. Do you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re carrying your phone around.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To take pictures and stuff when you`re on vacation and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And it pops up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then it pops up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can act like you didn`t see it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Swipe and delete it. You`re on vacation, they should know better than to send them e-mails.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, they also say when you post the "out of office," make sure you say you`re on vacation. Don`t just say I`m away say, I`m on vacation. Let`s see if that helps. The idea is that anyone who really needs to get in touch with you will do so once you return.\par \par \uc2 Number two, do not include your phone number in your out of office messages. A lot of people, their phone number is their automatically because once you do that, people will call you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s like, you can reach me still.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you can still reach me. My number is -- number three, have a pre and post vacation meeting before you leave. So you tell everybody I`m going on vacation, you`ve got anything you want to discuss. Let`s do it now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t call and don`t e-mail because I am not going to be checking it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll fly for two hours. Get it off your chest right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right now. Okay?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And finally, they say keep your return date to yourself. I don`t actually get that one. But don`t tell people when you`re returning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What are you talking about to yourself? You`ve got to tell somebody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But they`ll know when you touchdown. It`s like, all right, we`ve got to holler at her. We`ve got to holler Tony because he just got back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, but I mean, I had to inform you I want to come here. Somebody else could be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just saying, you`re not working until Monday. You come back on a Saturday. They`re going to harass you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All I am going to say --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: None of these hacks work for us. Do not respond to work e- mails.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: These don`t apply.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number two, don`t include your phone number and don`t let people know when you`re coming back. That doesn`t work here --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is 24/7.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re telling me to keep moving. But I mean, we have some pictures from my vacation, which is what`s at stake here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, let`s see. Go share. Go share.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We want to savor the moments. Can we throw up a couple of those moments.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, look at this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: While I was away from you all --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, those are nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Taking it in on Cape Cod -- Brewster, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, those are so nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Carrying my multiple children. That`s the one they linger on because that`s why I have back pain. That`s why there is a little slouch here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Those are nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t check your e-mail by keeping your hands full of children and then you can`t even reach for anything and taking pictures.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is a quick one. It`s a little update on the James Webb telescope. There was a picture posted on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A couple of weeks ago of the following. It looks to be a new close up of the star closest to Earth and that is in fact what a French scientists claimed it was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There it is. People shared it. They were amazed. Look at this level of detail. New galaxies have woken every day?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at the texture, the landing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it turns out on closer inspection, it`s been acknowledged to be actually a slice of chorizo, which is the kind of sausage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the scientist has come forward now and he said, "Guys, sorry, a little bit of a hoax. Little scientist joke."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He didn`t think it would take off the way it had.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He didn`t think it -- I think he skipped breakfast and so he sent that out, but he`s not --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A scientist joke.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He walked it back. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Initially, I thought it looked like pepperoni.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s what you thought.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Untrained eye, actually very trained.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. For sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a galaxy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so, all right, for my "Talk of the Table" I`m going to just give you an opportunity right now. Grab yourself some tissue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. I have a heartfelt moment on deck for my "Talk of the Table", and this is about nine-year-old Felix who plays baseball in Sioux Falls, South Dakota. Now, he often practices with his grandpa who has given him pointers here and there and also goes to all of his games.\par \par \uc2 So when Felix hit his first homerun, then got a grand slam in the same day, he found a way to thank his number one supporter. Please watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: What did you do?\par \par \uc2 BOY: So, you know, I got two homeruns, right?\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: You got two deck.\par \par \uc2 BOY: And then my grand slam, I signed the ball for you.\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: Okay. And the date is?\par \par \uc2 BOY: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: I`ll put the date down for you.\par \par \uc2 BOY: And it says "Papa, I love you."\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: Why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 BOY: Because you taught me everything about baseball.\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: Oh, honey. You`re so sweet. I`ll put it right on my -- that was so good. Wow. Honey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Nate, that`s so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m laying in bed with my wife. She shows me this, immediately. I text my sister and I said, I need this for "Talk of the Table." This is one of the best videos I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I know. Now that I have a grandchild myself, my grandson, Luca is only 10 months old. My deepest desire is that I can live long enough like he does, that his grandson really knows him and loves him and wants to be with him. Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have those moments.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that really. That`s a good one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You taught me everything about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, I`ve already taken dibs of. I get to take Luca to see "Lion King" when he`s five. I`ve already -- I`m already putting an order for that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re setting dates already.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But when I see stuff like that, you just think that`s really what you want.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s all it really matters.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s called success.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220810ei88000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Historic Spending Bill Passes Senate; Political Impact of Spending Bill; Flooding Emergency; Extreme Heat and Storms; Muslim Murders Investigation; Easing School COVID Guidelines; Ukraine`s Grain Fight; Ukraine`s Stolen Children; Anna Heche in Stable Condition; Further Supply Chain Backlogs; Hanson`s Harmonizing Hit; Doctors Share Abortion Law Concerns; Page Turning Inclusivity; Tips to Lower Electric Bills this Summer, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Ed O`Keefe, Nikole  Killion, Nancy Chen, Charlie D`Agata, Madison Conner, Chris Livesay, Carter  Evans, Vladimir Duthiers, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 13,629 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Every member of my caucus is elated about what happened.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): Joe Biden has pushed us and these plans are pushing us into a recession.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Biden and other Democrats celebrate a major victory after the Senate passes his spending bill.\par \par \uc2 MAN: President Biden has emerged from isolation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Biden is visiting Kentucky Monday to survey flood damage and meet families.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We need some fried eggs. It`s all we need.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Police in Albuquerque say the killing of four Muslim men could be connected.\par \par \uc2 AHMAD ASSED (Islamic Center for New Mexico President): The message of hope is always here, and that is to defeat hate. We will defeat hate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The supply chain could fail again over a shortage of rail workers.\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA (LA Port Director): There are about 35,000 containers that are designated for rail on our docks right now.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A ceasefire in Gaza went into effect after days of Israeli strikes and counter attacks from militants.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Seattle Sue Bird plays her last regular season home game.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is adorable. A young fan hands her a flower.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Final round of the Wyndham championship for the final round in the \uc2 CBS\uc2  booth for Nick Faldo.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Faldo retired after 16 years as a golf analyst. He and the entire panel got emotional.\par \par \uc2 NICK FALDO: On a single show and I found at 65, three brothers. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The dog days of summer are upon us literally. The 2022 World Dog Surfing Championship hit the water in San Francisco and crowds of people gathered to watch canines hang 10 off the beaches of California.\par \par \uc2 MAN: People just love it and we just like to keep it positive, make people happy, and we just really have a great time.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, Gayle, we should --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love those pictures.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Me too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Having a great time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We should have extended our vacation to see the dogs surfing. Could have made a group trip out of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. But weren`t you ready to come back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was. I miss --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The band is back together. It`s like that Peaches & Herb song "Reunited and it feels so good."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How was your time off?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to be back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was good. Got a haircut, little trim here. Not so trim here. But that`s good. That`s a sign of fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s vacation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you`re back and we have a big show for you, guys.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be another sweltering day in much of the country and if you`re worried about the cost of keeping the air conditioner on, we`ve got some timely advice for you. An expert from Consumer Reports will share some ideas and products that will help you cut down on your home energy bills all year round.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Plus we have Hanson`s in our Mornings Mixtape." It`s been 25 years since the three brothers in that band released a giant mega MMMbop. I know you know it.\par \par \uc2 The Hanson`s say the song and the band, however, have always been misunderstood, and I agree. All three of them now husbands and dads will talk about making it big when they were shaggy haired, adorable little kids and staying together as a band.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they`re still going strong.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are going strong. They have legions of fans to this day, even if you are not among them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to hear the tease. I liked them back in the day.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin in Washington with a landmark victory for President Biden on a bill to tackle climate change and raise taxes on big corporations.\par \par \uc2 In a strict party line vote just yesterday, Senate Democrats advanced those pivotal parts of Mr. Biden`s agenda. The Democrats say that bill will cut inflation, make unprecedented investments in clean energy and reduce the price of prescription drugs. Republicans see it differently. They say the bill will hurt the economy.\par \par \uc2 Our team is covering all the angles including the possible impact of the upcoming elections. I see you there, Ed O`Keefe, but first we`re going to go to Capitol Hill where Nikole Killion has details of the bill.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, we`re starting with you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hey, good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 This legislation has been more than a year in the making, and while it has been substantially scaled back, Democrats view it as a big win for the President and the party.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice President of the United States): The Senate being equally divided, the Vice President votes in the affirmative, and the bill as amended is passed.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Applause erupted on the Senate floor after Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  delivered the tie-breaking vote to pass Democrats` signature economic health and climate agenda.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): It`s been a long, tough and winding road. At last, we`ve arrived and we are elated.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Called the Inflation Reduction Act, it is the largest Federal investment to address climate change with $369 billion for clean energy programs including tax credits for individuals who make their homes more energy efficient or buy electric vehicles, as well as the manufacturers who build them. It allows Medicare to negotiate lower prescription drug prices and caps drug costs for seniors at $2,000.00 a year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  says the $430 billion package is expected to cut the deficit by $300 billion. It will be paid in part through a 15 percent minimum tax on corporations and increased IRS enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Republicans who oppose the measure had choice words.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN SASSE (R-NE): The bill`s title shows you what [bleep] it is. It`s an inflation increase act.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): This is going to continue to drive us into a further -- a bigger recession than we are.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): It is a terrible idea.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Republicans were successful in blocking a $35.00 cap on insulin prices for most Americans. That provision needed 60 votes and only seven Republicans joined the 50 Democrats in favor of it. It was one of dozens of amendments that were voted on during a marathon weekend session that stretched into the early morning hours Sunday.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2  (R-IA): It is 4:50 AM.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But the strict party line action finally passed.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is there objection?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And while Democrats acknowledged only some of the bill`s provisions would be felt immediately, they emphasized the significant long- term benefits.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): The world will be a better place for my grandchildren because of what we did today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Senate is now out on recess for the rest of this month. The House will come back from its break to vote on the bill Friday - - Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I understand the Senate would need a rest, very long weekend.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With the President`s approval ratings below 40 percent, Democrats are hoping that the bill can help improve their chances in the upcoming midterm elections. And we find Ed O`Keefe at the White House for us once again.\par \par \uc2 Ed, on paper, it would seem like a good week last week for the President and the start of another good one this week, but the numbers don`t suggest so much.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good morning, Tony. Welcome back from vacation, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 And while Democrats didn`t get everything they wanted, no matter how you slice it, this has been one of the most successful stretches of the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the health and climate bill expected to be passed later this week, consider all the other things that they`ve gotten done. There was a passage of major legislation boosting the nation`s high tech manufacturing sector and a plan to provide Federal aid to military veterans exposed to burn pits and other toxins, both of which the President plans to sign this week.\par \par \uc2 Those follow passage this summer of a major gun control and mental health bill; and internationally, there is the Senate approval of a plan to let Finland and Sweden join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , as well as the successful killing of al- Qaeda`s Ayman al-Zawahiri.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned that on the legislation, senior aides have had more direct interaction with lawmakers on key details of the bill than President Biden has. Climate change and health bill has been the focus of really intense work here at the White House, especially in the last 10 days or so. But the President had no direct interaction with Senator Joe Manchin, that key Senate broker until this past weekend when he also spoke to about a dozen other senators to ensure final passage.\par \par \uc2 Polls won`t give us a sense of whether this worked or not until about Labor Day and remember, the top concern for voters remains the economy and inflation. None of this legislation provides any immediate relief for that -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ed, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden travels to Eastern Kentucky this morning to visit survivors of last month`s catastrophic flooding that killed nearly 40 people. That region expects more rain this week, and that could cause more flooding.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado yesterday, at least 19 people had to be rescued after thunderstorms caused flash flooding in the Denver area. Another round of flash flooding hit California`s Death Valle, one of the hottest and driest areas in the country. Officials say hundreds of visitors were trapped for hours when nearly a year`s worth of rain around one and a half inches fell in just three hours at Death Valley National Park. They needed heavy machinery to open a path so visitors could drive out.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, Jordan Steele with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is watching all of this today, the heat advisories that is affecting 58 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 Jordan, good morning. What`s the biggest problem you see?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Yes, well, I mean, you just think about the weather dominating the headlines in the last several weeks. The flooding, the lightning, and of course the heat is going to be the big topic for today.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to break down what`s it going to look like in your neck of the woods, we`re talking the northeast. I know it was hot over the weekend, we`ll continue to cruise with the heat alerts up and down all the way down into the Jersey Shore and even into the interior where we do have the heat emergency that has been extended for today and for some of us even into tomorrow in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 So back in the 90s, Logan International tied records both Saturday and Sunday. In fact, yesterday`s tied record was almost a 100-year record. That`s how hot it is.\par \par \uc2 Now with the heat comes the rain chances and we`re also watching a storm system kind of drop down from the Great Lakes and we`re going to do a squeeze play here with that southerly flow. So more rain in areas that probably don`t need it. I am talking about folks in Kentucky, just looking at Nashville. You guys are trending above average rainfall as well. We`re going to get soaked here.\par \par \uc2 It`d be good to get it in your neck of the woods, Nate, and maybe up into the Boston area as well where we`ve had the drought move through, but not necessarily in some of those areas that have had the flooding like in to Kentucky, but we got a couple of days here of some rainfall for us throughout the week and this of course, through Wednesday, where it looks like we could likely pick up anywhere from one to two inches. That`s a general rule of thumb, it does look like that could maybe even be a little bit more in some isolated spots.\par \par \uc2 Remember, you guys can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  online, and of course your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Nate, there will be hit or miss storms today when the President does move through those hard Eastern Kentucky areas when he goes through, so he`ll be having to dodge some showers and storms from time to time as well.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I hear you on that. Jordan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In Albuquerque, New Mexico, a small Muslim communities living in fear today after the recent killings of four Muslim men. Now, police believe that these murders may be connected. The latest happened just Friday night when a man was shot dead after attending a funeral for two other victims.\par \par \uc2 Police say they are tracking down the leads and they`re looking for a car that may be linked to the killings.\par \par \uc2 Madison Conner of our affiliate, that`s KRQE has more on this story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MADISON CONNER: A community mourning a string of murders now feels targeted and under threat.\par \par \uc2 Pakistani immigrant, Nayeem Hussein was shot and killed on Friday, the third Muslim man to be killed in less than two weeks in Albuquerque. Hussein was killed while returning from the other victims` funeral.\par \par \uc2 The attacks follow a similar murder in November of last year when a fourth Muslim man Mohammed Ahmadi was gunned down.\par \par \uc2 In a tweet Sunday, President Biden said he was angered and saddened by the killings.\par \par \uc2 And what Governor Michelle Lujan Grisham has called "targeted killings," Albuquerque`s Muslim community is fearful of what may still come.\par \par \uc2 Local leaders are trying to calm nerves.\par \par \uc2 AHMAD ASSED: The message of hope is always here, and that is to defeat hate. We will defeat hate.\par \par \uc2 MADISON CONNER: Police on Sunday said they have identified this vehicle of interest and officials including Albuquerque`s Mayor are pledging increased patrols around mosques.\par \par \uc2 TIM KELLER (Albuquerque, New Mexico mayor): This is a frightening time for many. We have members of our Muslim community who are afraid to participate. This is where our city must demonstrate a commitment to try and make this a safe and inclusive place in their time of need.\par \par \uc2 MADISON CONNER: Among those living in fear of further attacks is Mohammad Imtiaz Hussain, the brother of victim Mohammad Afzal Hussein.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMAD IMTIAZ HUSSAIN (Brother of victim, Mohammad Afzal Hussein): It`s painful. We are in shock. We cannot go out of our house. My kids, whenever I step out of my room, they start crying. We didn`t have any fear. Now, it is super painful.\par \par \uc2 MADISON CONNER: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Madison Conner, Albuquerque, New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turning now to the COVID pandemic: As early as today, the CDC is expected to relax some of its COVID-19 restrictions, including those for kids going back to school in the fall. Nancy Chen is here with more.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good news for a lot of parents here. What are we learning?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has obtained a draft report on the guidelines that could be coming today. They are likely to involve two key areas for students: COVID testing and social distancing. And they`d becoming just as children across the country are getting ready to head back to school.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The CDC is trying to simplify and streamline a wide number of its COVID-19 recommendations starting in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 According to a draft document obtained by CBS News, the changes could allow schools to relax their strict social distancing measures, which have already been effectively phased out by the CDC. They would also de- emphasize so called test-to-stay strategies where students exposed to the virus take regular tests to stay in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT GOTTLIEB (Former \uc2 FDA\uc2  Deputy Commissioner for Medical and Scientific Affairs): So the test-to-stay strategies, we`ve turned over a lot of infections that were asymptomatic and forced kids out of the classroom. I think they can lift those things at this point and that seems to be where CDC is heading.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The school Superintendent Michael Cornell are fully supports the possible changes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL CORNELL (Hamburg Central School District Superintendent): We have to focus on in school, is making sure our kids experience joy and value and connection in school.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Another potential update, those exposed to COVID would no longer need to quarantine, vaccinated or not. Instead, the recommendation would be to mask for 10 days and test five days after exposure.\par \par \uc2 Still, the proposed changes are raising some concerns with only about 30 percent of younger children fully vaccinated and more than 80 percent of Americans living in areas with medium to high COVID rates.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb says the CDC would still ask schools to remain vigilant.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT GOTTLIEB: For example, notifying parents when it`s a closed contact so parents can keep an eye on their children and get them tested.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And part of the CDC`s reasoning in lifting the quarantine recommendation for anyone exposed to the virus is the increase in people who now have COVID-19 antibodies. They also want to limit the social and economic impact that quarantines are having on the nation.\par \par \uc2 And there`s also this aspect that with simpler and more streamlined rules, people will follow them a little bit better, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. We`ve got the antibodies, we`ve got vaccines, we`ve got other treatments. Kids need to be in school and as that one expert said, they need that love that connection, that learning and if this can help them get it, I`m all for it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`ve been talking about getting to this place for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Totally.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ve been in this fight. Seems like finally, we`re putting the gloves down.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but we`re still getting COVID.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but it`s just not as extreme. Hearing the words - emphasized is a good thing. The kids need to be in school.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the war in Ukraine, there is growing concern that the fight could seriously damage Europe`s largest nuclear power plant. The facility is in Russian occupied territory. Ukraine and Russia have accused each other of shelling the plant. This comes as more ships carrying grain leaves Ukraine`s Black Sea ports.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in a southern Ukrainian city of Odessa.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Four more cargo ships left these ports yesterday and other two vessels set sail today, but there is a backlog of 20 million tons of grain and farmers on the frontline are putting their lives on the line to gather this year`s harvest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The tug-of-war over the country`s grains may have shifted in Ukraine`s favor for now, but in addition to overflowing silos at the port, millions of tons of grain are piling up at farms like this north of Odessa.\par \par \uc2 Third generation farmer, Yuri Yelivchuk (sp?) tells us if the Green Deal collapses, so will his farm.\par \par \uc2 (YURI YELIVCHUK speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "We do not have much trust in Russians," he says. "They can strike the grain ships with a missile at any moment." He is banking on shipping out this year`s harvest first, it`s worth more fresh and he`s got nowhere to store it anyway.\par \par \uc2 This is last year`s harvest. Mountains of grain stuck in warehouses across Ukraine. Farmers are now harvesting this year`s crops while Russian forces attack fields, farms, and storage facilities.\par \par \uc2 Whole fields turned into infernos. Livelihoods wiped out starving the world of vital food supplies. Shelling warehouses and storage facilities, farmers under fire forced to wear flak jackets as they work.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian military reported Russian shelling in dozens of towns along frontlines in the southeast, hammering nearby Mykolaiv with sustained artillery and missile strikes.\par \par \uc2 While the Russian Emergency Services released images of damage around the Zaporizhzhia Region Nuclear Power Plant after both sides traded accusations of shelling the compound again.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy called the renewed shelling Russian nuclear terror. The nuclear watchdog, the IAEA expressed grave concern over the situation and the \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary-General called it nuclear suicide calling on inspectors to have access to the compound.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Charlie D`Agata in Odessa, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And coming up, our coverage of the war continues with a shocking story today. Ukraine is accusing Russia of kidnapping thousands of Ukrainian children and then taking them into Russian territory. Why? What we`re learning ahead.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, another supply chain slowdown on the West Coast that could complicate your Christmas shopping. Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 We will show you what the problem is this time.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a big standing ovation for a woman in basketball. See why thousands of Seattle fans showed up to honor a \uc2 WNBA\uc2  legend.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you were around in 1997, you could not have missed the phenomena that was MMMbop. But there`s a pretty good chance you did miss the entire meaning of the song.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re nine.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON (Hanson`s): Nine, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You were 11.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR HANSON (Hanson`s): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You were 13.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HANSON (Hanson`s): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you were singing about the ravages of time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in our "Mornings Mixtape," the Hanson brothers talk about why they and their song have been misunderstood.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They look good.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS now at 7:30.\par \par \uc2 And as the war in Ukraine grinds on, the government in Kyiv is accusing Russia of a truly heinous crime: Kidnapping thousands of children and taking them into Russian-controlled territory.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine says Russia is trying to force the children to become Russian.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay has the extraordinary story now of two young boys who after being rescued were brought back to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Their music is Ukrainian, their clothes are Ukrainian, but Maxim (sp?) and Ivan (sp?) were almost forced to become Russian.\par \par \uc2 (IVAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "Russian forces took us away," says 16-year-old Ivan. He and 15-year-old Maxim were living in an orphanage in Mariupol when Russia invaded.\par \par \uc2 The boys tried to flee, but say they were captured by Russian forces and held with 20 other children, some as young as eight.\par \par \uc2 Were you afraid that you would never come home?\par \par \uc2 (IVAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "I had such thoughts, yes."\par \par \uc2 Ukraine says Russia is systematically stealing their children and stripping them of their identities.\par \par \uc2 SERGIY KYSLYTSYA (Ukrainian Ambassador to the U.S.): Russia continues its kidnapping of Ukrainian children. After forcible transfer to Russia, they are illegally adopted by Russian citizens.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Russian TV says they are saving them instead.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Here is Russia`s Children`s Rights Commissioner telling \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that Russian citizens have big hearts and are lining up to take the children.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2  speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "Very good," he says, vowing to eliminate any delays.\par \par \uc2 It is cynical propaganda says Ukrainian Vice Prime Minister, Irina Vereshchuk.\par \par \uc2 We are talking about children whose parents have possibly been killed by Russians, and now, these children are in Russia. As a Ukrainian and as a mother, how does this make you feel?\par \par \uc2 (IRINA VERESHCHUK speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "It`s very hard," she says. "It`s my destiny to bring them home." And she is doing it, one by one helping relatives and guardians track down their children and negotiating their return, many with unfathomable trauma.\par \par \uc2 (IRINA VERESHCHUK speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: One 12-year-old girl had to dig a hole to bury her mother. "They tried to bury her brother, too, but his corpse was too heavy for them to drag. I could have seen small children whose hair has turned white."\par \par \uc2 Yet, such children like Ivan and Maxim are the lucky ones who have escaped, thanks to Anton Dalay (sp?), guardian from the orphanage in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 (ANTON DALAY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "I could not leave them behind," he says.\par \par \uc2 Saving them meant crossing into enemy territory, not through the deadly frontline, but through Poland, Lithuania, Latvia, and Russia. Seventy hours of travel by bus. He arrived just before dawn.\par \par \uc2 (MAXIM speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "I was still sleeping," says Maxim. "When I saw Anton, I hugged him. I was so happy. We had not seen each other for so long."\par \par \uc2 After more than two months in captivity, they`re now back in Ukraine. Ivan messages his friend, Philip (sp?), an abducted Ukrainian orphan like him, but unrescued. He`s been sent to an orphanage in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 "Do you still want to come home to Ukraine?" He texts. "It`s all the same," Philip responds.\par \par \uc2 Like so many Ukrainian children, he has no one left to come home to.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Chris Livesay, Khmelnytskyi, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, that`s just trauma on top of trauma. First, you`re without your parents and then you`re ripped away from people that you know and love that could possibly take care of you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then your identity stripped away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t need a UN Convention to say that`s wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it appears this is just the way Russia fights wars these days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wrong on so many levels, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Back in this country, a friend of Anne Heche says she is in stable condition. She`s in the hospital after the actress crashed her car into a home in a Los Angeles suburb over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Now, the footage from a nearby doorbell camera appears to show Heche`s vehicle speeding by on Friday. Police say she was doing twice the legal speed limit at the time.\par \par \uc2 Photos obtained by TMZ reportedly show Heche minutes earlier when witnesses say she crashed her car into a garage. Additional video allegedly shows her speeding away from that garage before ultimately slamming into a nearby home.\par \par \uc2 Heche`s car smashed a hole in the house, which then went up in flames as you see the aftermath there. The LA Fire Department says it took nearly 60 firefighters more than an hour to put out those flames.\par \par \uc2 A woman living in the house was trapped for a while, but she was uninjured. She was not hurt. She told our LA station, that`s KCBS that she lost everything in her home.\par \par \uc2 Heche has not been charged and police are still investigating exactly what happened, because right now nobody really knows. There`s all sorts of speculation, but nobody knows for sure. We`re just glad she`s okay, Anne Heche is okay, but it`s very scary story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Glad they are both okay, but the woman in the home I`m glad she`s uninjured.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. She is still in hospital.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sitting there on your couch, you hear that noise and that fire.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anne is in the hospital and the woman is recovering, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, a worsening bottleneck in one of our country`s busiest ports, how it could affect what you buy this upcoming holiday season.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder for you. You can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Get today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. It doesn`t get any better than that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One of the busiest cargo gateways in the country, the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2  is once again seeing bottlenecks that could disrupt the supply chain to businesses and consumers nationwide. This time, it`s because of a shortage of rail workers.\par \par \uc2 As Carter Evans shows us, the disruptions could eventually be even worse than what we saw at the height of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Tis the season for ships packed with holiday gifts to start flooding America`s ports, but the containers are already piling up, clogging the docks, waiting for trains to transport cargo across the country.\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA: There are about 35,000 containers that are designated for rail on our docks right now. On a normal day, it looks more like 9,000 units.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: LA Port director, \uc2 Gene Seroka\uc2  is sounding the alarm to prevent another scene like this.\par \par \uc2 How long before we see a backup at sea again?\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA: We`ve probably got another four to six weeks if we do nothing.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Over the last three years, railroads have lost 20 percent of their employees.\par \par \uc2 BEN NOLAN (Stifel Financial Rail Infrastructure Managing Director): A lot of that is because they cut their own workforce.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Analyst, Ben Nolan says it began when they tried to streamline operations with a practice called precision scheduled railroading.\par \par \uc2 BEN NOLAN: When you`re hyper efficient, you`re ill-prepared for unexpected things like pandemics.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So where are the bottlenecks right now?\par \par \uc2 ERIC GEHRINGER (Union Pacific Executive Vice President of Operations): The bottlenecks right now are on that last mile.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Union Pacific Operations VP Eric Gehringer says his railroad has already hired hundreds of new employees.\par \par \uc2 ERIC GEHRINGER: We`re handling that volume. Resources beyond the railroad, that`s where we need to see gains.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The rails move containers inland where merchandise is transferred and delivered, but with truck driver shortages and now a glut of goods arriving --\par \par \uc2 BEN NOLAN: The warehouses are full. There is nowhere to offload the goods.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And the containers keep coming.\par \par \uc2 Is it going to get any worse?\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA: That number continues to grow every day, every week.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Would more trains here help?\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA: Absolutely. But you have to have somewhere to put the trains when they get to their destination.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And none of it will happen without railroad workers who have been working without a contract for two years now with no raises.\par \par \uc2 An emergency team from the White House will offer its recommendations in the next two weeks in hopes of heading off a railroad strike in September.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS. I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that we`ll be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:42. Time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, everybody do the two steps. Yes. Peaches & Herb.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Step now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One perfect fit and sugar, this one is it.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so excited. I know. It does feel good. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It feels good?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Feels good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Feeling good as --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t ask me to quote Lizzo, all right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is that, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Lizzo? Feeling good?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Feeling good is hell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This part, you`ve probably heard that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad, you can`t be mixing genres. You`ve got errors, okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I got like soul and my --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like what is he doing? Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She wanted to turn to Nate and be like, "What`s Vlad talking about?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know if it was James Brown, "I Feel Good." I wasn`t sure what you were doing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She knows she can`t turn to me, because I`d have no idea.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony was like, "I`m just going to ease my way out of this conversation and let you guys take it."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 We are beginning this morning, though, looking back at the wonderful career of actor Roger E. Mosley who died sadly, tragically yesterday, after a car crash. The 83-year-old was best known for playing T.C. Calvin on the original "Magnum P.I." back in the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 (Clip form "Magnun P.I.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "I want nothing to do with it." He appeared in more than 150 episodes as a Marine turned helicopter pilot who worked with Tom Selleck in the \uc2 CBS\uc2  hit drama.\par \par \uc2 Mosley also played heavyweight champion, Sonny Liston in "The Greatest." That was the 1977 movie about Muhammad Ali`s rise to fame.\par \par \uc2 Mosley`s daughter announced his death on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  saying not to cry for her dad. Instead, she said: "It is time to celebrate the legacy he left us all."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s nice. You might not know his name, but the minute you see that clip and his face, you know instantly who that is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And he was getting fan mail up until the very end.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I believe it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People would send him letters and say they had such fond memories of his character on "Magnum."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Rest