guy, I have to say this, Sue Bird, you were absolutely amazing. And it wasn`t just about the male athletes in our city, it was about you and you fit right in and oftentimes, you led the way for them.\par \par \uc2 So thank you for representing us on a global scale and bringing championships. My daughter was born the same year the Seattle Storm won and I`ll always remember that you because of a special. I took pride in the team winning a title and also my daughter being born. So Sue Bird, you did it well.\par \par \uc2 I hope retirement is everything you want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and I`ve seen her play, too, and what`s so fascinating about her, I was lucky to be close enough. You could hear her talking to the refs. She`s a good trash talker, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was sort of surprised. I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her image is like.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s something about Seattle guards, you know, Gary Payton was also a trash talker. Another Seattle legend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good trash talker.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 All right. Let me leave you with a little bit of laughs this morning on a Monday. Check out this weather forecast. It is clear skies with a chance of laughter.\par \par \uc2 Watch as meteorologist -- I didn`t write that, that`s pretty good, Chai -- our writer. Watch as meteorologist, Greg Dutra from \uc2 WLS TV\uc2  in Chicago realizes that his monitor is actually a touchscreen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (\uc2 WLS TV\uc2  meteorologist): ... which right now kind of hung up just a little, oh, I moved the map. I didn`t know I could do that? No way.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Are you serious? Did you just discover that?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ve got to try it. Hold on, hold on.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA: Can you believe that? Just touch it, you can go anywhere you want.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That`s so cool.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA: I didn`t know that. Can I zoom? Oh man. It`s a great day. It`s a great day. Our temperatures are warming up nicely this afternoon, but we are still getting --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You did not know that?\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA: I didn`t -- I just -- I`ve never --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Like seriously?\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA: I`ve never touched it, but -- oh my gosh, you can tilt it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s just so excited over it. I don`t know if it`s like he is being pranked by a producer or what? Like but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But can you please tell Greg, it is 2022. That`s how it all works now, Greg. It is good to see Terrell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Terrell Brown\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: #WhenTheEdibleKicksIn.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to talk about Hanson, MMMbop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have that look.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: Are you struggling with a high price of staying cool in a heatwave? A lot of people are. "Consumer Reports" has some ideas to turn down the cost of your air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. We catch up with the three brothers who make up the band, Hanson, in our "Mornings Mixtape." It is still going strong 25 years after their biggest hit, you know it, MMMbop.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 We visit a special store for children in Chicago, where kids from all types of backgrounds get to see themselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the sound of that. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And the bill as amended is passed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A huge win for Democrats. The Senate passes a massive bill meant to lower healthcare cost and invest in clean energy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): This Senate Democratic majority has achieved what countless others have come to Washington promising to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden goes to Eastern Kentucky where people are fearful of more catastrophic flooding.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It was devastation and it was just a sea of rapid water.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The CDC is expected to relax COVID rules in schools allowing students exposed to the virus to remain in class.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL CORNELL: We have to focus on in school. This making sure our kids experience joy and value in connection.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bird. What a delivery on the money to Williams for two.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Great Sue bird takes a bow as she and her fans celebrate her final \uc2 WNBA\uc2  regular season game.\par \par \uc2 SUE BIRD: Thank you so much and I`ll see in the playoffs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the \uc2 New York Jets\uc2  recruit a young fan with cerebral palsy to join their scrimmage and he goes all the way.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Go, go, go. You`re good. Go, go, go.\par \par \uc2 GROUP: Touchdown.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I saw an interview this morning with that little boy`s mother who said he always dreamed of running on a big field. I thought that was such a great moment for him. He will never forget it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a great moment. Also, I got to do a little play by play there.\par \par \uc2 He could go all the way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pretty good, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that was great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going to begin this hour though with a bit of a flashback. It`s our "Morning`s Mixtape" series where we highlight nostalgic songs and musicians that brought those songs to us. It`s a moment in time and we`re going to visit it. Today, we are looking back at the band, Hanson.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MMMbop.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is their 1997 mega hit, MMMbop. It`s a song that climbed to number one -- get this -- in 27 different countries simultaneously.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And turned the kid musicians behind it into true global celebrities. And while you may know the chorus by heart, what the song actually means might surprise you, and you might also be surprised by the thoughtfulness of the three brothers behind it.\par \par \uc2 We caught up with Zac, Taylor, and Isaac who are now all grown up. They told us about their new album and the true meaning of MMMbop.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MMMbop.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All these 25 years later, no one is more aware of the success of MMMbop than the three brothers who created it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MMMbop Video.)\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HANSON: We all know how catchy the song is. It worked.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MMMbop Video.)\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: It was a phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HANSON: On its own.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: It was a cultural wave, tsunami of some kind.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The song of Hanson`s debut, "Middle of Nowhere," help sell more than 10 million albums and knocked the Notorious B.I.G, off the top of the charts.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Notorious B.I.G. performance.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you were around in 1997 --\par \par \uc2 JANE ROBELOT (Television host): Hanson? Heard of them? Of course, you have.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You simply could not miss it. MMMbop was everywhere.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The three brothers have taken the world by storm.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, they have.\par \par \uc2 BOY: It`s on the radio.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: We`ve got their autograph.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s been described as sounding like a song made in a lab especially designed to capture the attention of the entire world.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR HANSON: People mostly just feel that way and say those things just out of elation, "Oh you`re a teen idol, you`re a heartthrob," those kinds of things, but for you, you`re like okay that`s -- like that`s great, but we wrote this.\par \par \uc2 HANSON BROTHERS: We are the Hanson brothers and we like to sing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Growing up in Tulsa, Oklahoma, the brothers formed a band and bonded around this collection of rock `n` roll hits from 1958.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HANSON: It was one of these time life compilations. It was advertised on TV.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR HANSON: Very naturally from that, we started emulating. I mean, songwriting starts with emulating something, and now, all of a sudden, you`re singing along and you`re making your own.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The chorus for MMMbop popped into their heads while at work on another song entirely.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: We were trying to write a background part for a song called "Boomerang." So "Boomerang" was already syncopated, "Big bang, hit me like a boomerang, light turn green, let`s starts flowing," right. And for some reason, we thought, that`s not complicated enough, we should add like a complicated part underneath it.\par \par \uc2 And so, big bang hit me like a boomerang, light turns green, let`s start flowing. Big Bang you hit me boomerang, light turns green and my heart starts pumping.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HANSON: It`s just a lot of gumbo.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Boomerang.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They ended up separating the two songs.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Hanson`s performance.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And a year or two later, matched that irresistible tune to an idea, and a lesson you might have missed.\par \par \uc2 What is an MMMbop?\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HANSON: It is a friend of time and in MMMbop they`re gone. In an MMMbop they`re not there.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: It`s saying, look, like that you`re going to be at the end of your life, you`re going to be past this moment. Be conscious that every choice you choose not to take on is one you may not have the opportunity to make again.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Hanson performance.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re nine.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: I am nine. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You were 11-ish.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR HANSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You were 13.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HANSON: Thirteen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re singing about the ravages of time.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: We were already getting these messages from our parents, hey, you know, you`ve just got to be you and people won`t always understand you and it was -- it really connected.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Though the band recorded other hits, nothing could match with a commercial triumph of MMMbop. Yet the brothers did find a loyal base of fans and a new path to success through their own independent record label.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Hanson performance.")\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HANSON: At that moment in our life, you`re saying, "You know what? We`re going to decide to claim what success means." This for us is to own our own destiny, to talk to those fans and let`s see if we could have a number one billboard independent album, and we did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that`s what we`ve been doing ever since.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR HANSON: And that`s what we`ve been doing ever since.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: This theater is the legendary spot for Nashville.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We caught up with the band at Ryman Auditorium on a world tour for their latest album, "Red Green Blue," featuring five songs from each brother.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: I don`t think there`s a lot of bands like that where every member sings and plays and writes and kind of could be doing it completely on their own, but choose to do it together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s that bond --\par \par \uc2 HANSON BROTHERS: You can say, I can`t give it, our own way, another minute our way. This time around --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that sound --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Hanson performance.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That continues to attract fans who still pack into theaters to hear the band perform their latest --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Hanson performance.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And greatest.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Hanson performance.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Twenty-five years after they first harmonized about holding on to those who care, the Hanson brothers are still living those words.\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: We are a band that probably is not much more understood than we were misunderstood when we started out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why do you say that?\par \par \uc2 ZAC HANSON: Because I think just like we were influenced by music that wasn`t from our time, we are a band kind of happily outside of our genre because it`s really all just about what we`re saying.\par \par \uc2 The story is what matters, the message behind the song and if it takes 25 years, we`ll still be here.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MMMbop performance.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it`s great, they are still performing, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think they are delightful and incredibly talented. I love these guys. I`ve got to tell you. I brought my daughter along with me. She`s a budding songwriter. They chatted with her for a while.\par \par \uc2 And the thing about MMMbop is you know, people didn`t catch what it really was saying. It was a sped up pop song. In the original, it was slower, more methodical and so when everyone said "Oh you guys are adorable" you`re a boy band, they were like, "No, we are Musicians" with a, capital M and that`s what they`ve been living since then.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`ve been fighting to get outside that box, right, their entire career?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really ever since, since like it`s possible to have a song that is so catchy that it defines your career and you`re like, wait, wait, hold up. We`re not N`Sync. No shade N`Sync. We`re musicians in a different sort of way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and we write our own stuff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we write all of our stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They take that very seriously when they write their own thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Totally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I love the album too, that they each wrote five different songs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: RBG, right there, Red, Blue, Green.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Red, Blue, Green, I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. The different brothers. And like the childhood influences as well, that 1958 rock `n` roll tape that they were listening to, their parents moved them out of the country for dad`s job. So the three little brothers were in South America, had no other things around them that were American, except for that tape. It`s like one of those influences that changes you forever.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was fascinated that they are not really off the charts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That surprised me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Totally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember all of that.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`ve got some tips to save you money on your energy bill. Everybody wants to do that, including a few things that you can do today, as in right now.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:10. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of Indiana`s largest employers is sharing its concerns after the State passed a near total ban on abortions. Now, Indiana is the first State to pass such a measure since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. The pharmaceutical giant, that`s \uc2 Eli Lilly and Company\uc2  warned this: "We will be forced to plan for more employment growth outside our home state," as state laws change across the country.\par \par \uc2 Now doctors are becoming increasingly concerned. They are very worried about what they can and cannot do for their patients. So, we spoke to four OB/GYNs from Arizona, Wisconsin, Ohio, and Georgia about how state abortion laws create a lot of confusion and all of them expressed concerns the new laws will put their patient`s health at risk.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY (Wisconsin OB/GYN): As soon as we heard the decision, we closed everything down, leaving over 70 women in Wisconsin with appointments that we weren`t able to do for them.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA (Georgia OB/GYN): We`ve been preparing, it didn`t make it any more devastating.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID HACKNEY (Ohio OB/GYN): I remember asking, when is the law going to change? How much time do we have to get ready for it? And they started to text back and said the law has actually changed right now.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: I`ve lived in the South for most of my life. This is my community, and I`m very concerned about the ability of my patients, my community to access the care that we need.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIA KWATRA (Arizona OB/GYN): When Medicine is criminalized, physicians are scared to do the jobs they`re trained to do and that absolutely impacts patient care.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: People are very quick to talk about how these abortion restrictions have medical emergency exceptions, but for us as clinicians actually practicing on the ground, those exceptions just don`t really make sense.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY: Now, many of those options have been taken away and that is just unbearable for all of us.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIA KWATRA: Some women will have what we call a spontaneous miscarriage where they start to miscarry on their own.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY: We have to counsel them that they may get very sick, that they may develop a life-threatening infection and it could potentially cost them their lives.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: You can`t make a one size fits all definition or law that can take all of these situations into account.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIA KWATRA: We`re seeing issues in labor and delivery, for example of women with pre viable pregnancies that come in with infection that need to be delivered and so there`s a lot of back and forth as to how sick does the patient need to be to get delivered?\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: Does she have to develop an infection? Can we intervene before she develops an infection?\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIA KWATRA: How sick does she have to be before the staff felt comfortable delivering her are because of this law hanging over our head?\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: If someone who`s pregnant comes in after getting in a car accident or comes in with an inflamed appendix, are doctors going to be afraid to get imaging to take them to surgery if they need surgery because with those treatments, there is a chance that you could inadvertently cause an abortion?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID HACKNEY: What if someone has cancer and they`re not going to die right then, but because they don`t receive abortion care, they don`t receive the usual cancer care that they would receive unless they die from the cancer five years later. Is that a life that we could have saved?\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: Doctors are having to figure out, does this fit under the law? Am I going to be criminalized for intervening?\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY: I think physicians are afraid that they will be accused of providing abortion care when really they`re providing miscarriage management.\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA: I think my greatest fear is how our patients are going to be able to get the care they need.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY: It means so much to me to be able to take care of my patients and they are suffering because of this, suffering economically, suffering -- their families are suffering. Their futures will suffer. But some of them are going to die and they could be saved. That`s a travesty.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another big concern is misinformation the doctors say. One of them we spoke to said she heard from a pregnant patient who is too afraid to get the medical care, why? Because she fears that her health will be sacrificed for her unborn child.\par \par \uc2 OB/GYNs are also concerned that doctors will now leave their states and create even more of a healthcare vacuum.\par \par \uc2 You know, the doctors on the ground, guys, who are doing the work have a very important voice in this conversation. They laid out different scenarios I`ve never thought about before that they deal with all the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. And we have to understand because there is no uniformity, there is confusion with these doctors, all the while there`s lives at risk -- the mother in the baby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it`s clearly a lot more complicated than you think if you just read the headlines and this is government getting in between the relationship of a patient and their doctor and doctors don`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, it should be sorted out. We`ll keep following it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we will be right back with more CBS MORNINGS. Thank you for watching. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are just one day away from the start of our new series and it`s something cool just for you. "\uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals."\par \par \uc2 Starting tomorrow, an expert will join us to share devices that will make your life a little bit easier including innovative gadgets that may help you and your family stay safe on the road.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have it all at exclusive discounted prices available only on CBS MORNINGS. To sign up for first access to the exclusive deals, visit CBSdeals.com or text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s that number again, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do it right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead we`re going to introduce you to a mom who is on a mission to help kids feel included at play and when reading.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And doesn`t it feel good? Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" and Gayle, are you walking on sunshine?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. You know, when people said what`s your song? What`s the song that best describes you? I always pick that one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a good song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I just feel it`s one of those songs that makes you feel happy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, you bring sunshine to this table.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I come back from vacation fatter and darker. More me to love, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You look good, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: More me to love.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is hacks to make the most of your summer vacation. Tony and I just came back from one. So this is the thing they say. They come from some very busy executives.\par \par \uc2 First the number one rule they say, do not read the e-mails, your work e- mails that you get on vacation. Do not read them and do not respond.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ooh, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very hard to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very hard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t -- I always respond. Do you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re carrying your phone around.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To take pictures and stuff when you`re on vacation and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And it pops up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then it pops up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can act like you didn`t see it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Swipe and delete it. You`re on vacation, they should know better than to send them e-mails.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, they also say when you post the "out of office," make sure you say you`re on vacation. Don`t just say I`m away say, I`m on vacation. Let`s see if that helps. The idea is that anyone who really needs to get in touch with you will do so once you return.\par \par \uc2 Number two, do not include your phone number in your out of office messages. A lot of people, their phone number is their automatically because once you do that, people will call you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s like, you can reach me still.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you can still reach me. My number is -- number three, have a pre and post vacation meeting before you leave. So you tell everybody I`m going on vacation, you`ve got anything you want to discuss. Let`s do it now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t call and don`t e-mail because I am not going to be checking it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll fly for two hours. Get it off your chest right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right now. Okay?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And finally, they say keep your return date to yourself. I don`t actually get that one. But don`t tell people when you`re returning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What are you talking about to yourself? You`ve got to tell somebody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But they`ll know when you touchdown. It`s like, all right, we`ve got to holler at her. We`ve got to holler Tony because he just got back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, but I mean, I had to inform you I want to come here. Somebody else could be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just saying, you`re not working until Monday. You come back on a Saturday. They`re going to harass you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All I am going to say --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: None of these hacks work for us. Do not respond to work e- mails.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: These don`t apply.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number two, don`t include your phone number and don`t let people know when you`re coming back. That doesn`t work here --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is 24/7.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re telling me to keep moving. But I mean, we have some pictures from my vacation, which is what`s at stake here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, let`s see. Go share. Go share.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We want to savor the moments. Can we throw up a couple of those moments.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, look at this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: While I was away from you all --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, those are nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Taking it in on Cape Cod -- Brewster, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, those are so nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Carrying my multiple children. That`s the one they linger on because that`s why I have back pain. That`s why there is a little slouch here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Those are nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t check your e-mail by keeping your hands full of children and then you can`t even reach for anything and taking pictures.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is a quick one. It`s a little update on the James Webb telescope. There was a picture posted on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A couple of weeks ago of the following. It looks to be a new close up of the star closest to Earth and that is in fact what a French scientists claimed it was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There it is. People shared it. They were amazed. Look at this level of detail. New galaxies have woken every day?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at the texture, the landing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it turns out on closer inspection, it`s been acknowledged to be actually a slice of chorizo, which is the kind of sausage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the scientist has come forward now and he said, "Guys, sorry, a little bit of a hoax. Little scientist joke."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He didn`t think it would take off the way it had.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He didn`t think it -- I think he skipped breakfast and so he sent that out, but he`s not --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A scientist joke.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He walked it back. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Initially, I thought it looked like pepperoni.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s what you thought.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Untrained eye, actually very trained.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. For sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a galaxy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so, all right, for my "Talk of the Table" I`m going to just give you an opportunity right now. Grab yourself some tissue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. I have a heartfelt moment on deck for my "Talk of the Table", and this is about nine-year-old Felix who plays baseball in Sioux Falls, South Dakota. Now, he often practices with his grandpa who has given him pointers here and there and also goes to all of his games.\par \par \uc2 So when Felix hit his first homerun, then got a grand slam in the same day, he found a way to thank his number one supporter. Please watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: What did you do?\par \par \uc2 BOY: So, you know, I got two homeruns, right?\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: You got two deck.\par \par \uc2 BOY: And then my grand slam, I signed the ball for you.\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: Okay. And the date is?\par \par \uc2 BOY: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: I`ll put the date down for you.\par \par \uc2 BOY: And it says "Papa, I love you."\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: Why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 BOY: Because you taught me everything about baseball.\par \par \uc2 GRANDPA: Oh, honey. You`re so sweet. I`ll put it right on my -- that was so good. Wow. Honey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Nate, that`s so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m laying in bed with my wife. She shows me this, immediately. I text my sister and I said, I need this for "Talk of the Table." This is one of the best videos I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I know. Now that I have a grandchild myself, my grandson, Luca is only 10 months old. My deepest desire is that I can live long enough like he does, that his grandson really knows him and loves him and wants to be with him. Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have those moments.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that really. That`s a good one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You taught me everything about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, I`ve already taken dibs of. I get to take Luca to see "Lion King" when he`s five. I`ve already -- I`m already putting an order for that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re setting dates already.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But when I see stuff like that, you just think that`s really what you want.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s all it really matters.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s called success.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, turning now to a topic on the minds of many parents at home. The importance of inclusion in children`s books, how kids are represented can inspire children to place themselves in a story and that can enable them to see themselves as the hero of their own story.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz has the story now of one mom who made it her mission to make sure kids from all races, religions, and families could find books featuring characters like them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They say never judge a book by its cover.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH (Kido owner): As you can see, it`s a rainbow.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But it`s the covers at this Chicago children`s store that move people.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: We get a lot of gasps from children.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Really?\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: We do. We`ve had parents and adults cry.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That`s because at Kido, the faces on the books are Black and Brown and protagonist you don`t usually see, a girl in a wheelchair, a mom in a hijab. Those are deliberate choices says Kido owner, Keewa Nurullah.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: We want every kid to feel reflected, to feel seen, to feel included. Kids with disabilities, kids who are growing up in foster care, or have been adopted or whose parents are going through a divorce.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With titles like "Queer Heroes," "Hair Love" and "The ABCs of Black History." Books that celebrate Aztec heroes and Chicana ones.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of these books?\par \par \uc2 BOY: They`re beautiful and I really like them.\par \par \uc2 BOY: They all have different lessons to be learned in these books. So it is really nice.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: Beeswax crayons that show --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: The different skin shades.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh, I wish I had these growing up.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We talked about coming over.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: These regulars who Nurullah introduced us to come from the store`s inclusivity and community.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Rayan (sp?) has two beautiful brilliant mothers and we have an exceptional daughter, as we want her to be represented in the books that she sees.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: For people who say, you know, to play devil`s advocate that there is too much focus on race these days. What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: Easy for you to say. You know, it`s just like why Women`s History Month? Why not Men`s History Month? You know, the reason we need these things is because there is a deficit.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Nurullah got into this work as a new mom. She struggled to find clothes that reflected her family, so she started designing her own onesies like this one and an entrepreneur was born.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: I was really excited about that one.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Do you have a business background?\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: Not at all. Not at all.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Keewa Nurullah performance.)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Her background is on stage.\par \par \uc2 You`ve embodied the idea of diversity in characters that you played in the past. You know where I am getting.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: Well, I`m a retired Princess -- Disney Princess. If you ever wanted to know what happens to them when they grow old --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: When they grow up.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: Yes. When the "Princess in the Frog" came out, I was one of the princesses in the show at Disneyworld.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Keewa Nurullah performance as Disney Princess.)\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: I think even in my performance career, you`re thinking about your representation on stage and what that could mean for a little kid who wants to sing and dance, too.\par \par \uc2 Hi. Your total is --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But business is in her blood, too, stretching back to her great grandfather.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: Simeon Neal Sr. was a tailor and he had a family in Tulsa, Oklahoma. He had a tailor shop on Black Wall Street and his family was forced to flee during the massacre of 1921.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: According to oral history, a customer helped the family escape hiding them under Chris Hayes: on a wagon.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: And so they made it on their journey to Chicago and thankfully he was able to reopen his tailor shop on the south side.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How does that feel being this extension of this entrepreneurial spirit in your family?\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: When I was growing up, I knew this part of my history, but no one else did. We didn`t learn about it in school. The fact that my great grandfather had a business on Black Wall Street is special, you know, it`s unique. You know, that`s like, Black royalty almost. And I do try to uplift his legacy and you know, live up to that foundation.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That legacy lives on here, inspiring future generations to thrive, just as they are.\par \par \uc2 KEEWA NURULLAH: We don`t want any child to feel isolated in their experience. We want them to come into the Kido Store and say, "Oh, wait, like, I`m not the only one."\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And when books have heroes of all backgrounds, real world heroes are born.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Adriana Diaz, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Keewa, and thank you, that`s such an important thing you`re doing there.\par \par \uc2 Ahead how to keep your energy bills low, low, low despite the summer heat.\par \par \uc2 Tanya Christian, there she is, from "Consumer Reports" is in our progressive green room. Hey, Tanya.\par \par \uc2 She`s got some ideas. She`s going to show us gadgets that can help and some things you can do right now, right this second. Good news is you don`t have to spend a penny.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:41. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Late night in the middle of June get really hot and parts of the nation are dealing with intense heat waves this morning, forcing many Americans to crank up their air conditions to stay cool. This comes as families deal with the rising cost of electricity.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, the Federal government says electricity prices rose nearly 14 percent from last June to June this year, the largest annual increase in 16 years. "Consumer Reports" Tanya Christian joins us right now with her tips on how to help keep those power bills down. How are you doing? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN ("Consumer Reports" home and appliance writer): I am well. How are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Now one of the biggest things that can drive up those monthly expenses, is that AC bill.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. So what can we do to help?\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes, that accounts for nearly 50 percent, you know, the heating and the cooling. So what we like to say is, why don`t you invest in a programmable thermostat?\par \par \uc2 What`s nice about these is you can program it, weekdays or weekends. If you`re someone who is home, on the weekends, but you`re away during the week, program it so that it`s not kicking in while you`re not home.\par \par \uc2 Seven to 10 degrees can save you 10 percent dialing it back seven to 10 degrees.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the temperature that you think you get the most bang for your buck?\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: So anything over 72 in the winter and then 77 in the summer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Seventy-seven.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seventy-seven.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In the summer.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes, yes. I go by 78.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s not cool.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: But you`re not home, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Then when you get home, you could turn it down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Okay. I know. Because I like my kitchen to be comfortable.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you also say that when you have your devices, even if you`re not using them, they`re still using energy. So you`re saying you should unplug them?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t think people really know that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I didn`t. I didn`t.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Absolutely. So even when it`s in idle mode, thinking about your phone charger, your laptop charger, your kitchen appliances, they`re all taking up energy, even though they`re not being used.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you have to go around the house unplugging everything, and then plugging it back in. Do you do that, Tanya?\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: I actually do, do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You do.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Especially in my guest room, right? People are not in there most of the time. So unplug the TV. TVs can account for $200.00 -- almost $200.00 a year just keeping them plugged in at all times.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really? Now, you said there`s a device you can use to show you how much energy you`re taking -- you`re using?\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes. So this is Kilowatt and what you do is you plug your device in it like that, okay, there`s a kilowatt meter here, and it tells you exactly how much you`re using, even when it`s turned off. It`s in idle mode right now.\par \par \uc2 It might not seem like a lot now. But think about it cumulatively. If you have a toaster, an air fryer, a toaster-oven at all times for the year, you can say over $400.00.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Empire energy they call it.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, so when you`re sweating through your shirt and you`ve got a big old pile of laundry to do, what can you do to save money on that front?\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Run the cold cycle. A lot of people just use the hot water because they think oh, that`s going to get it cleaner. We have done the study. It gets it just as clean in cold water and then you don`t have to use the energy to heat up the washing machine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My grandmother told me hotter water makes it cleaner. You`re saying grandma was wrong.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Grandma was well intentioned.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s what they are saying.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Grandma was just making up a whole bunch of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, she`s no longer here. So that`s okay. And you also say that we should buy appliances that are energy efficient.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes. "Consumer Report" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they are designated, aren`t they?\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes. On CR.org, you can see dishwashers, washers. If you`re in the market, make sure you get one that is green choice designated. Because over the long run we`re talking about from creation to the grave. We have studied them. They`re more environmentally friendly, and more energy efficient.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so what about LED light bulbs? Is it really worth going out there and making the switch?\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Well, it is. If you have a regular light bulb, these LED light bulbs are super inexpensive. And over time, let`s say $7.00 a year, you know, you`ll get that money back. However, they last so much longer so you don`t have to be replacing them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s talk about ceiling fans. What`s the adjustment that you can make to help the ceiling fans do their work?\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes, so in the summer, it`s running counterclockwise. In the winter, flip the switch so that it`s running clockwise and that way, that`s going to bring the warm air down and that cool air that`s rising, it`ll get filtered out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know ceiling fans could go in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: Yes, hit the reverse button.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is reverse button.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You lost me at counterclockwise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tanya, where is the -- honest to God, I did not know that. I thought that you`ve turned it on, it just goes one way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Me either.\par \par \uc2 TANYA CHRISTIAN: You know, if you have a newer ceiling fan, it probably has a remote and you really just click the button. Other ones you go to the top and you click it up at the top.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Tanya.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tanya, thank you so much. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love "Consumer Reports."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, earlier we had my vacation photos, five of them, got them pre-approved from Miss Katy Tur. Where are the pictures that I need to see?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Katy is nicer than favorite daughter Kirby. She`s not giving me anything yet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everything has to be signed off by Kirby. I`m going to work on that today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We get to see them same during the commercial break.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are some cute ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going to work on it today.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220808ei8800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fallout From Kansas Abortion Rights Vote\b0\par\par\uc2 David Begnaud, Steven Portnoy, Michelle Miller \par \uc2 951 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The backlash to the Supreme Court`s decision to throw out Roe versus Wade has taken on new shapes.\par \par \uc2 One day after Kansas voters rejected a measure to remove abortion rights from the State`s Constitution, President Biden announced a new executive order to assist abortion seekers.\par \par \uc2 Steven Portnoy of CBS News Radio looks at the potential for major ripple effects in the midterms and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re going to get through this difficult period.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: The loss has left conservatives flatfooted.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER MARSHALL (R-KS): I was absolutely surprised and I have no explanation and again, it was just a gut punch.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: More than 900,000 Kansans cast ballots Tuesday, a stunning turnout for an early August primary.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hi, how are you?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: The defeat of the proposed constitutional amendment, which was aimed at undoing the effects of a State Supreme Court decision girding abortion rights shows not only how the issue has galvanized Democrats, but how it could also threaten Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at Leavenworth County. In 2020, fifty-nine percent of voters there chose former President Trump the same percentage voted no on Tuesday. And in Sedgwick County, the state second most populous, 54 percent voted for Donald Trump, 58 percent voted against the amendment.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MILLER (University of Kansas Associate Political Science professor): So those of us who watch politics that abortion is not as polarizing as really we think.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: Patrick Miller is a Political Science professor at the \uc2 University of Kansas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MILLER: On this one issue, when they were allowed the opportunity to vote on abortion, they did so, and they expressed their preference.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: The latest CBS News poll shows women under 50 see abortion as their top issue heading into the midterms and most Democrats say their party isn`t doing enough to protect abortion access.\par \par \uc2 This fall, voters in Vermont and California will be asked whether they support codifying abortion rights in their State`s Constitutions. Kentucky voters will weigh an amendment similar to the one just defeated in Kansas. And in Montana, Republicans have proposed an amendment requiring care for any infants that might survive attempted abortions.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, President Biden said the Kansas result proves abortion opponents have misjudged voters` anger.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): They don`t have a clue about the power of American women. Last night, the Congress in Kansas they found out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN PORTNOY: Now, President Biden`s comments yesterday came as he signed his second Executive Order aimed at protecting abortion rights. This order directs the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to see about using Medicaid funds to help low income women access abortions out of state -- Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Steven, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, a bill covering other big elements in the Biden agenda could be close to getting a vote after months of negotiations, but Democratic senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  has not promised her support and that could torpedo the whole plan.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning,\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Michelle, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 After Joe Manchin struck a surprise deal last week, all eyes are now on another moderate Senate Democrat, \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  of Arizona, who we`ve seen huddling through the week with her colleagues amid reports she is pushing back on one of the tax provisions in this bill, which would scale back an existing tax break for investment fund managers, some of whom have donated significantly to Sinema`s campaign. She also wants to scale up money for drought programs.\par \par \uc2 For the Biden administration and for congressional Democrats, this would be a big victory less than a hundred days until the midterm elections. The bill has potentially popular tax breaks for people who buy energy efficient appliances or fuel efficient cars.\par \par \uc2 Also billions of dollars to extend some healthcare subsidies for the Affordable Care Act in an effort to lower some prescription drug costs and an effort to try to lower the hulking national debt with new taxes on some corporations.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have been calling this the Inflation Reduction Act. Republicans criticized that name and have told CBS News raising taxes at a time of inflation and possible recession is potentially dangerous to the economy -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 You know, flags at the Capitol are flying at half-staff this morning for Indiana Congresswoman \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2 . She was killed along with two staff members in a car crash yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The 58-year-old from South Bend was the top Republican on the House Ethics Committee. Walorski and her husband once served as missionaries in Romania.\par \par \uc2 The Elkhart County Sheriff`s Office tell \uc2 CBS\uc2  news that her SUV was hit head on by another vehicle. Walorski was traveling with her district director, Zachery Potts and her communications director, Emma Thomson, they died along with the other car`s driver.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said Walorski lived a life of service and she was admired by her colleagues on both sides of the aisle for her personal kindness.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thoughts and prayers go out to her family and the others as well.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220805ei840002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Decisive Win For Abortion Right In Kansas\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 859 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Tonight: a decisive win for abortion right in Kansas. Voters turn out in record numbers. President Biden calls the result a powerful signal. What it means for November`s midterms.\par \par \uc2 Election results from around the country are in. Former President Trump keeps his hold on the GOP, as election deniers win in several states.\par \par \uc2 Deadly crash. A Republican congresswoman and two of her staff members killed in a head-on collision in Indiana. We will have the latest details.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky`s flooding disaster. Waters begin to recede, as we get a closer look at hard-to-reach areas and the devastation left behind.\par \par \uc2 Severe weather. Thunderstorms moving into the Midwest, while the Northeast braces for dangerous heat.\par \par \uc2 U.S.-China fallout. A defiant House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  leaves Taiwan, as Beijing responds on multiple fronts, military drills and economics sanctions.\par \par \uc2 Monkeypox outbreak. Cases in the U.S. are doubling every five to seven days, and now a major U.S. city will run out of vaccines by the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 LIV golfers teeing off on the PGA Tour. Phil Mickelson and 10 other players file an antitrust lawsuit after being banned for joining a Saudi-backed league.\par \par \uc2 Hail of a storm. A scary incident, as grapefruit-sized ice balls smash vehicles in Canada.\par \par \uc2 VIN SCULLY (Former Broadcaster, \uc2 Los Angeles Dodgers\uc2 ): High fly ball into right field. She is gone!\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And remembering Vin Scully, who called some of the greatest moments in sports history.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS with Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening, everyone. Thank you for joining us. I`m Major Garrett, in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with a resounding victory for abortion rights in Kansas, where voters there rejected a ballot measure that would have eliminated state constitutional protections for the procedure. Voters turned out in droves, with numbers on par with a general election.\par \par \uc2 At the White House, President Biden said Republicans -- quote -- "don`t have a clue about the power of American women. Last night in Kansas, they found out" -- unquote.\par \par \uc2 The president also signed an executive order aimed at making it easier for women seeking abortions to travel between states to obtain care. Election results from five states showed former President Trump remains highly influential in the Republican Party, with several 2020 election deniers winning.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe will have more on that part political story in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns will start us off from Kansas.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good evening, Major.\par \par \uc2 Well, those results surprised many on the ground here in Kansas, because it`s a conservative state. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won here by nearly 15 points in 2021, and Republicans control the state legislature with a supermajority.\par \par \uc2 But voter turnout broke records and sent a message heard around the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): In conservative Kansas, an unlikely victory for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I`m super proud to be from Kansas tonight.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Voters rejected a measure that would have removed abortion protections the state Constitution, which would have paved the way for the Republican legislature to enact further restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ALL (Kansans For Constitutional Freedom): And what was at stake was our constitutional rights and our freedom.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Anti-abortion rights groups say this is just a setback.\par \par \uc2 PETER NORTHCOTT (Executive Director, Kansas For Life): Our resolve has never been stronger than in this very moment.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The results were decisive, with 59 percent voting to keep current abortion rights in place, reflective of the national mood. The CBS News Battleground Tracker shows 56 percent of voters disapprove of the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 It`s a dynamic activists are hoping translates to other states.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ALL: We need to be able to have conversations with people who disagree with us, but share the common goal of protecting people`s personal autonomy, their constitutional rights to make these decisions for themselves.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In November, Kentucky will have a similar ballot question, and Montana will have a born alive measure. California and Vermont will ask voters if they want to codify abortion rights into the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Democrats hope the issue will galvanize their voters in the midterms. President Biden issued an executive order today that would expand access to reproductive health care, including helping women travel out of state to receive abortions.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The voters of Kansas sent a powerful signal that, this fall, the American people will vote to preserve and protect the right and refuse to let them be ripped away by politicians.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But, without federal protection, abortion access remains a patchwork, and Kansas has actually become a destination for patients seeking care from states where the procedure is banned.\par \par \uc2 And clinics, like the one behind me, are already seeing a rush of patients -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220804ei8300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Kansas Voters Preserve Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,069 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I`m Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle and Tony off, but we are in great hands.\par \par \uc2 Here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A big win in Kansas for abortion rights advocates, what it means for the midterms and America after Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You`re going to take care of me and my female friends and everyone that can get pregnant in the State of Kansas. We are protected tonight.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fresh threats from China against the United States as House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  visits leaders in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): We come in friendship to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Eastern Kentucky, dozens of people are still missing after the historic flood and thousands have no electricity or running water.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): We`ve lost 37 Kentuckians.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Senate Republicans reverse course on a veteran`s healthcare bill dealing with toxic burn pits.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: What was the reaction up top in the chamber?\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART (Comedian and activist): A lot of tears, man.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Legendary sports broadcaster Vin Scully has died, the voice of the Dodgers throughout his 67-year career.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 NFL\uc2  stripped the \uc2 Miami Dolphins\uc2  of two draft picks and fines the suspended owner \uc2 Stephen Ross\uc2  and its tampering investigation.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): A passenger on a flight from Bali to Australia was fined more than $1,800.00 for having to undeclared Egg McMuffins in their luggage.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if they`ll be able to pay for that, you know. No, because I mean, the type of person who saves Egg McMuffins for later --\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  touches down in Taiwan and the Chinese military goes on high alert.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Chinese have also conducted live fire drills and scrambled jets for an 82-year-old woman with bones made of peanut brittle.\par \par \uc2 Tensions are so bad the Defense Department has upgraded its readiness to DEF CON Mee-maw.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You should call more. You should call more.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happy Hump Day and welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get right to it. We have a big show for you today, including some unprecedented moves by the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . Just days after the Deshaun Watson suspension, the \uc2 Miami Dolphins\uc2  in their owner are now facing stiff penalties as the NFL`s response to allegations by the team`s former head coach, Brian Flores and we`ll break it all down.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, a new scam is putting restaurants at risk and it could affect where you eat. We speak to some victims of this online blackmail where scammers are demanding ransom from restaurant owners.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That is not good.\par \par \uc2 But we have a lot of news to get to. We begin with some important primary election results including a major victory for supporters of abortion rights. They celebrated in Kansas last night after voters rejected a move to remove abortion right protections from the State`s Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is the first referendum on the issue since the Supreme Court struck down Roe versus Wade. Voters rejected the measure put forth by Republican legislators nearly 60 to 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Olathe near Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 This result is obviously getting a lot of attention all across the country.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this is a result that could have national implications. President Biden says this makes clear that a majority of Americans support access to abortion. Still, the results were a surprise here in Kansas because this is a conservative state. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won it by about 14 points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But turnout here was huge, which suggests that both Republicans and Democrats support access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering.)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A resounding victory for abortion rights supporters in a conservative state.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I feel like my state just showed up and boldly told me that they are going to take care of me.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Kansas voters rejected a ballot measure that would have eliminated constitutional protections for abortion in the state, a move that would have allowed the Republican legislature to further restrict or enact a near ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Is this the end? Can the legislature do anything on this issue?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BAUMGARDNER (R) (Kansas State Senator): I think it might be a while before we get another bite at the apple.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: It was the first time voters had a direct say on the issue since Roe v. Wade was overturned and turnout was significantly higher than expected for a primary contest in the state at 47 percent.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ALL (Abortion rights supporter): What was at stake was our constitutional rights and our freedom, and so you know, a coalition of voters across the political spectrum came together today and voted no.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Abortion rights supporters hope the win reverberates beyond Kansas.\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS (Kansas City, Missouri mayor): And if you can see that the no votes win here, then that would mean that you could have that in any part of the country, no matter how red it is.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: So what happens here in Kansas could be a roadmap for other states.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR QUINTON LUCAS: It is a roadmap for how do we make sure that we`re asserting, defending and protecting the rights of women to have access to care in any part of the country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: As voters here in Kansas decided to preserve abortion rights, at the Federal level, President Biden is expected to sign an Executive Order to expand access to reproductive care -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Caitlin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220804ei8300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Is On The Ballot For The First Time Since Roe Was Overturned\b0\par\par\uc2 John Dickerson, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 722 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Tonight, the Justice Department is suing the state of Idaho over its abortion law that will ban nearly all of those procedures, including abortions considered necessary to protect the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 That`s something the government says conflicts with the state`s obligation under Medicare to provide emergency care.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Kansas tonight, where voters will be the first to weigh in on abortion rights since Roe was overturned in June.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): For Laurie Mosqueda, tonight`s vote on abortion access is personal.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE MOSQUEDA (Kansas Voter): When I was 15 years old, I accepted a ride home from work from a co-worker. And he took me down to the river and left me there. I screamed no. And I wasn`t heard then. He didn`t listen to me. But I`m going to vote no, and people are going to listen.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Walking through these suburban Wichita neighborhoods, it`s easy to find different points of view.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Vote yes! Vote yes!\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Ideally, of course, I think we would all love a total ban.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Right now, there are only four abortion clinics left in the state, where the procedure is legal up to 22 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Voters will decide whether to amend the state`s Constitution. Voting no will keep abortion rights in place. Voting yes will overturn them and allow the state legislature, which is controlled by Republicans, to act.\par \par \uc2 As a lawmaker, do you want to see a ban?\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MIKE THOMPSON (R-Kansas): Well, my personal opinion, I think we need more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Kansas is the first state in the nation to vote on reproductive rights following the decision to overturn Roe. But it will not be the last, and the decision will have an impact beyond its borders.\par \par \uc2 So, what`s at stake in this election?\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS (D-Mayor of Kansas City, Missouri): I think not just reproductive choice in Kansas, but in Missouri and every border state, and, frankly, access to vital family and women`s health care really in the Great Plains entirely. I think it reverberates nationally.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: No matter how the vote goes tonight, abortions will still be legal here in Kansas until the state legislature decides. And they come back into session in January -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Caitlin Huey-Burns in Kansas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next: The monkeypox outbreak worsens, as a federal emergency official is put in charge of the national response.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: The Biden administration has appointed a top official from FEMA to coordinate the administration`s response to the worsening monkeypox outbreak in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Robert Fenton helped lead FEMA`s mass COVID vaccination effort last year. More than 5,800 cases of monkeypox have now been confirmed in the U.S. in every state, except Montana and Wyoming. At least four children have tested positive. States of emergency have been declared in California, Illinois, and New York.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead: A gunman storms into a liquor store, and wait until you see what happens next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: A group of robbers apparently targeted the wrong liquor store in Southern California.\par \par \uc2 Surveillance video shows one of the masked suspects armed with an assault- style rifle bursting into the store on Sunday, when he was greeted by more than the pleasing jingle on the door. The 80-year-old owner blasted him with his shotgun. The gunman ran out yelling: "He shot my arm off."\par \par \uc2 Police later tracked down the suspects at a hospital. The owner later had a heart attack, but is recovering.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a lifesaving assist from a basketball champion.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220803ei82000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Kansas Abortion Rights Vote\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 924 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 This year`s first direct test of abortion as an election issue suggests that voters will go to the polls to have their say.\par \par \uc2 Kansas is the first State to have abortion rights on the ballot since the Supreme Court overturned the Roe v. Wade decision in June.\par \par \uc2 There`s been a huge increase in early voting for today`s primary and Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Olathe, a suburb in Kansas City with more.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Well, voters here in Kansas are voting on an amendment to the State`s Constitution that would remove protections for abortion, and if it passes, the Republican-led State Legislature could enact further restrictions or a near total ban on the procedure and voters here in Kansas have been telling us that this feels even bigger than a presidential level campaign.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hi, how are you?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Knocking on doors and rallying voters in 90 degree heat.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP protesting.)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Activists on both sides of one of the most divisive issues in the country are making sure voters know that access to abortion is literally on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Walking down the streets in these suburban neighborhoods, it is easy to find very different points of view.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI FISCHER (Kansas voter): Ideally, of course, I think we would all love a total ban. We do live in a realistic world, so even if we could get restrictions, we would be very thankful and happy for that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: For Laurie Mosqueda (sp?), the issue is personal.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE MOSQUEDA (Kansas voter): When I was 15 years old, I accepted a ride home from work from a co-worker and he took me down to the river and left me there. I screamed, "No" and I wasn`t heard then, he didn`t listen to me. But I`m going to vote no and people are going to listen.\par \par \uc2 I am a Christian woman, but I had that choice back then and I want every woman to have that choice.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Voting "No" will keep abortion rights in place; voting "Yes" will overturn them and allow the State Legislature which is controlled by Republicans to act.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it would empower the State Legislature to add restrictions?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE THOMPSON (R) (Kansas State Senator): Well, obviously the legislature can write new laws, but the trick is getting everyone on the same page. So, it`s going to be an uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But as a lawmaker, do you want to see a ban?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE THOMPSON: Well, my personal opinion, I think we need more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA TONG (Trust Women Foundation co-executive director): It`s creating a manufactured health care crisis. And now it`s like hundreds of phone calls.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Rebecca Tong is the co-executive director of a Wichita abortion clinic, which has seen an influx in calls from patients states away.\par \par \uc2 Several neighboring states have banned abortion already. They`re coming here to Kansas. What does that mean for them?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA TONG: It means that their state has left them behind that they are going to be forced to carry their pregnancy further than they wanted.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: If the measure passes tonight, abortions will still be legal here in Kansas, but only until the State Legislature goes to act and they come back into session in January -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The first January 6th defendant to receive a jury trial has been sentenced to more than seven years in prison, the longest of anyone so far.`\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors argued Guy Reffitt wasn`t just part of the mob that attacked the US Capitol, he helped lead the charge with tactical gear and a gun and a megaphone.\par \par \uc2 They say Reffitt was aligned with a militia group and threatened his own family not to turn him in. His daughter pleaded with the Judge for leniency, then outside, she slammed the sentence and former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PEYTON REFFITT (Guy Reffitt`s daughter): Trump deserves life in prison if my father`s imprisoned for this long.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Reffitt apologized in Court, but he did not criticize former President Trump. Other high-level defendants are still to be tried, including members of the far-right, Oath Keepers accused of seditious conspiracy, the most serious charges yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, the decision in the sexual assault investigation of star quarterback, Deshaun Watson. Will the \uc2 NFL\uc2  accept an arbitrator`s ruling or ask for a longer suspension?\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes -- less than 20 minutes, you get it all.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a deal.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220803ei82000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Doctor Speaks Out\b0\par\par\uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, Norah O`Donnell, Gayle King, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,664 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, to the latest battleground in the fight over abortion rights. This was the scene in Indiana Statehouse as lawmakers they`re considered a near total ban.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Evening News" anchor and Managing Editor, Norah O`Donnell sat down with someone who has become a symbol of this debate. That`s Dr. Caitlin Barnard who is an OB-GYN in Indiana who according to State records obtained by CBS News, provided a medication abortion to a 10-year-old rape victim from neighboring Ohio. That state already bans most abortions after six weeks. Because of privacy laws, Dr. Barnard can`t confirm this was her patient.\par \par \uc2 But in our first TV interview, she spoke about what the end of Roe v. Wade means for doctors.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD (Indiana OB-GYN): I think we`re at a time in our country where people are starting to realize the impact of these anti- abortion laws, and now, when it`s finally become impossible for some people, I think people realize that that is actually not what they intended. That is not what they want for children, for women to be put in these situations of life-threatening conditions of traumatic pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: When your name was publicly attached to this case, the Attorney General of Indiana said, he would investigate you. What was your reaction?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: It`s been really difficult.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Indiana`s Attorney General Todd Rokita described you as an abortion activist, acting as a doctor. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: I`m a physician. I`ve spent my entire life working to have this position to be able to take care of patients every single day.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Did you at any point violate privacy laws?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: No.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And have you failed to report any recent abortions?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: No.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How would you describe how things have changed since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: We`re hearing stories all across the country of people who are in dire circumstances, complications of their pregnancies or traumatic situations and are needing abortion care and not able to get it.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Is it worse than you imagined?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: It`s worse faster.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You`ve spent your life, your career taking care of, and providing care for women and babies. What has this Supreme Court decision done?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: You know, this will affect our ability to take care of miscarriages. This will affect our ability to take care of complications in early pregnancy that could kill someone.\par \par \uc2 This will affect our ability to provide infertility treatment, contraception, the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How would you address concerns by conservatives or those with deeply held religious beliefs that abortion is immoral and wrong?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: What I would say is, if you don`t believe that you would have an abortion, then don`t have one. You cannot stop other people from accessing medical care that they need based on your personal religious beliefs. You would never want somebody to do that to you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dr. Barnard told us the Indiana Attorney General has only just reached out to her and her lawyer for the first time, nearly two weeks after saying he would investigate her. Dr. Barnard is also pursuing a defamation case against him.\par \par \uc2 I think the point that she makes at the end there is key.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is very powerful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s critical.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Because if you believe it`s wrong, and it is against what you believe, then don`t have one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t have one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The simplicity in which she said, it makes you go, "Oh, okay."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It puts it in perspective.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought so.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. I agree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to turn now to Missouri where torrential downpours and historic flash flooding prompted a State of Emergency there. Record amounts of rainfall left at least one person dead and hundreds more -- look at this -- trapped in their homes and vehicles around St. Louis. You know how tall those signs are.\par \par \uc2 In some areas, First Responders needed boats to save people. Other people just gave up, they abandoned their vehicles and left.\par \par \uc2 This is just the latest in a series of extreme weather events in America. Meteorologist, Jim Cantore from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  has the why -- why this is happening.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Jim.\par \par \uc2 The pictures are pretty stunning, and it does seem -- extreme -- would be the word.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Yes. Extreme is the word. I mean, you had a rainfall event of historic proportions without even dealing with a tropical system, Gayle. So that`s what makes it incredible here.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for our virtual view technology here at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 . I`m standing in Kiener Plaza Park in downtown St. Louis, and what you see is what you get. More rainfall, unfortunately, expected today.\par \par \uc2 We are not going to dry out, which is exactly what we need to do and we won`t really dry -- we`ll get a dry day on Friday, and then we have to wait until next week when it heats up.\par \par \uc2 But it is raining again this morning in the St. Louis area, not to the extent of yesterday, 9.04 inches. We crushed the old record, which was set by a dying tropical system back in 1915 and it wasn`t just that one spot. Remember, you have a huge footprint here of anywhere from three to 13 inches of rain right over the Metro area.\par \par \uc2 Today, we focus more on Kentucky, Southern Virginia, and West Virginia. We may see a very similar setup to what we saw at St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 Is it going to flood like it did in St. Louis? We don`t know, but the potential is there for very, very heavy rainfall in a short period of time.\par \par \uc2 Now, we don`t have five to eight inches or eight to 12 on the forecast, but we could get that in a very short period of time.\par \par \uc2 Hey Gayle, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable and live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jim, got it. Did you buy a Mega Millions ticket?\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: You know, Gayle, I didn`t, but I probably should.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: That`s a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because if you buy one and you win, you could buy \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Byron Allen could work for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s going to be raining money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it`s going to be raining money.\par \par \uc2 Thank you Jim. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: Buy all The Weather Channels.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ve got big news about that Mega Millions jackpot. Have you heard? Nobody won last night. The huge sum you could win, let`s just say it starts with a B as in boy.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, new developments in the case of a South Florida police sergeant who was caught on camera lunging at a fellow officer. We will show you why.\par \par \uc2 And a major announcement about the International Space Station, what it could mean for the future of the project and US-Russia cooperation.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, it is a big day in Court in Russia for \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner. Today`s hearing could be significant.\par \par \uc2 And find out what brought a security guard to tears during a Kendrick Lamar show. You won`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner is back in a Russian courtroom this morning. You know she has been in jail in Russia since mid-February on drug charges. Griner pleaded guilty earlier this month, but she says she had no intent to break the law when she accidentally packed two vape cartridges just before a flight.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi is following the story from London.\par \par \uc2 Roxana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Griner is expected to testify today and possibly be cross-examined on why she brought vape cartridges containing cannabis oil to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, her defense team argued she had been prescribed medical marijuana to treat pain from injuries. Standing in a metal cage as its standard in Russian courtrooms, Griner held up photos of loved ones.\par \par \uc2 When asked if she had any complaints, she said no, just waiting patiently.\par \par \uc2 The two-time Olympic gold medalist has been detained since February after she flew to Moscow to play in a Russian team during the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  offseason. The US said she is wrongfully detained, an accusation Russia denies.\par \par \uc2 Griner faces up to 10 years in prison if convicted. It`s unclear how long the trial will last, but a Court has authorized her detention until mid- December -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we all just went Britney back home. Thank you very much, Roxana Saberi reporting from London.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220728ei7r0015p\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trumps Actions Under Investigation; Fed Expected to Raise Interest Rates; Indiana Considers Abortion Ban; Abortion Doctor Speaks Out; Deadly Missouri Flooding; Brittney Griner to Testify; Russia`s Surprise Space Announcement; Assault on Fellow Officer; The Future of COVID Vaccines; A Passion for Sharks; Highland Park Virtual Benefit Concert; Art Without Obstacles; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Jeff Pegues, Robert  Costa, Kris Van Cleave; Mark Strassmann, Roxana Saberi, Manuel Bojorquez,  Celine Gounder, Omar Villafranca \par \uc2 14,239 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is off today, but let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world and 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight: The Justice Department is looking at what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did after the last election as he considers running for President again.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Our country is now a cesspool of crime. It`s going to hell very fast, it is a very unsafe place.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Fed is set for another interest rate hike to curb inflation amid recession fears.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE (National Economic Council director): Two negative quarters of GDP growth is not the technical definition of a recession.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: At least four people are dead and dozens more injured after a powerful earthquake in the Northern Philippines.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Flooding in St. Louis turns deadly and forces multiple water rescues, part of a pattern of severe weather in America.\par \par \uc2 MAN: At this point, just glad to be in one piece.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Los Angeles, an armed man carjacked a construction truck.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh that innocent person right there.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Mega Millions is more like Mega Billion. Now, the jackpot raises to $1.02 billion. What?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show With Stephen Colbert" host): Are you ready for even more football? The \uc2 NFL\uc2  hopes so because they`re launching their own streaming service for the first time called NFL+, it`s a similar model to CNN+, but with viewers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Russia now saying, it is going to pull out of the International Space Station amid heightened tensions over the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): Good luck to them making a dramatic exit when there is zero gravity --\par \par \uc2 Come join with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , unless you want to feel the full force of Mother Russia. That`s right. You people don`t mess with us. Ha-ha.\par \par \uc2 Okay, can somebody push me towards door. Push me. Just -- just push me towards -- push me towards -- again, I want to make dramatic exit. This is okay. This is not exit I was hoping for.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the point Trevor is making there, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s basically that, you know, you`re so far up in space and you`re lobbying all of these threats, and everybody is like, "No problem. Got you covered."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Stay up there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Got it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got some stories about healing today. We`ll talk with Paul de Gelder, a former Navy diver who survived a horrific attack by a shark. Now, he`s made it his mission to educate people about the animals and how to deal with fear. You`ll want to hear this story even if you never go near the water.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, we are talking to the Smashing Pumpkins` front- man, Billy Corgan and his partner, Chloe Mendel. They live in Highland Park, Illinois, site of a terrible mass shooting at this year`s Fourth of July parade, and guess what? They`ve decided to help their community through music.\par \par \uc2 We are going to ask Billy and Chloe how a tragedy like this affects them as artists and as parents.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, we all are feeling that. We all remember that terrible day.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin though with some remarkable breaking news overnight: President Trump`s actions leading up to the January 6 riots are now under criminal investigation by the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes after weeks of hearings on Capitol Hill with evidence and testimony from many of his former allies about the effort to undermine the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the former President delivered his first speech in Washington, it was his first since leaving office, where he appeared to lay the groundwork that he is going to run again for President.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Pegues has more from the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Gayle, it is too early to tell whether the former President is a target of this criminal investigation here at DOJ, but one thing is for certain, people in his orbit are definitely in the crosshairs of prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 Investigators right now we`re told focused on seized phone records and that fake electoral scheme that if successful, would have potentially overturned the results or at least tried to overturn the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Then I won a second time, did much better the second time.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Did a lot better.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  repeated his false claims of election fraud on Tuesday, as the Justice Department deepened its investigation into the election aftermath.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : If I renounced my beliefs, and just took it easy, the persecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would stop immediately.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: CBS News has confirmed the DOJ prosecutors working on the January 6 investigation questioned witnesses before a grand jury including two top advisers to then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT (Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  Chief of Staff): I did receive a subpoena for the Federal grand jury and I complied with that subpoena.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Part of the investigation is focusing on potential fraud after the 2020 election and the alleged efforts by Trump allies to use fake electors supporting Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 The other focus is on the alleged conspiracy to stop a government proceeding and the certification of electoral votes as prosecutors looked into the pressure campaign on then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . He was also back in DC on Tuesday for a dueling speech, offering a vision for a GOP that didn`t embrace Trump loyalists.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former Vice President of the United States): I don`t know that the President and I differ on issues, but we may differ on focus.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: This DOJ investigation is different from the current congressional inquiry, which cannot result in a criminal prosecution, but Committee members have supported a criminal probe into former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): I sure as hell hope they have a criminal investigation at this point into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: In an interview with NBC News on Tuesday, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said that no one is above the law.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (US Attorney General): We intend to hold everyone, anyone who was criminally responsible for the events surrounding January 6th, for any attempt to interfere with the lawful transfer of power from one administration to another accountable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Almost daily now, the AG is peppered with questions about whether he could indict or charge a former President. It is a weighty decision and it would be unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 In the past, the former President has denied any wrongdoing -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jeff, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For what this all means, we`re joined in studio by CBS News chief election and campaign correspondent, that`s Robert Costa.\par \par \uc2 Bob, I`m so excited to see you in person. Thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 We just heard \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  say no one -- no one, Bob -- is above the law. Is he signaling something? Should we read anything into that? What does this investigation mean, ultimately for the former President?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Well, great to be here with you all.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned that questions are being asked about former President Trump`s conduct during the final days of the administration. What was he doing with conservative lawyer, John Eastman, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  to try to upend and overturn the 2020 election? We`re still not sure if Trump is going to be a criminal target of any Justice Department investigation, but the pressure is mounting.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  told me, she and others believe inside Congress that there was a conspiracy here to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 The challenge for Garland, is criminal intent provable at this point? But they are having a grand jury investigation to see where it all goes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, President Trump is making or at least he is leading us all to believe that he is going to run again. I think that remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 Should he still be considered the leader of the Republican Party despite all of the chatter and swirl around him, much of it negatives?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Great question. He certainly wants to be when you talk to his allies, but it is not clear he is going to remain the standard bearer for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 So many other rising stars in the GOP, Governor Glen Youngkin of Virginia, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , they are making moves toward possible 2024 runs. That`s in part why Trump is getting out there now, not announcing but giving these big speeches. He wants to potentially freeze the field, stop others from gaining traction.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is uncharted territory for the Justice Department. I spoke to former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  and asked him, if you were the Attorney General, would you have indicted? Would you indict former President Trump? He said yes, because no one is above the law. But it does set a dangerous precedent. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s a complicated and charged legal and political question. On one side, there are even some Democrats who privately worry about the way this could all tear at the civic fabric. But there is also a question of what would it mean, if he didn`t prosecute? Should he believe there was criminal conduct that took place inside the White House, that also could tear at this nation`s civic fabric if people felt Trump somehow got away with something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they keep calling it partisan and they keep saying witch hunt, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says, but many of the people who are testing find are Republicans, who were his allies, who were key people in his administration.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What sense are you getting about their perception of Trump? His political peers?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Well, you know that right now, former Pence aides like Marc Short and his lawyer, Greg Jacob, they are going before a grand jury. That`s serious business, and they`re telling the truth under oath to Justice Department prosecutors about what happened.\par \par \uc2 It`s significant that the Justice Department is moving closer to Trump. We`ve paid so much attention to the congressional side of this, the January 6 Committee, but at the end of the day, it`s the Justice Department that will likely have the biggest impediment on this whole story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bob, thank you so much. It`s good having you in studio.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good being with you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, today, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to raise interest rates for the fourth time this year to help curb inflation amid fears of a potential recession.\par \par \uc2 The markets finished lower yesterday on those rate hike concerns, in addition to some disappointing earnings reports.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave is in Beloit, Wisconsin with more on how this affects everyday expenses. Chris, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the world`s biggest retailer was one of those companies that sounded alarm bells about inflation hurting earnings, and here in the Midwest where prices are rising faster than much of the country, that is certainly being felt, the Fed is hoping its action today will ease some of that off without pushing the country into a recession.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: For the fourth time since March, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to hike interest rates later today. The potential three quarters of a point increase would push the Feds benchmark rate to its highest point in four years.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zandi\uc2  is the chief economist at Moody`s Analytics.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZANDI\uc2  (Moody`s Analytics chief economist): It is going to cost more to get a mortgage, it is going to cost more to get an auto loan. It means that if you owe money on your credit card, you`re going to have to start paying more on that because of the higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: None of that sounds very good.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZANDI\uc2 : Well, it`s not great, you know, but it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Necessary, the Fed says to counter skyrocketing inflation up 9.1 percent in June, but comes as the economy flirts with recession. AAA says 64 percent of adults have hit the brake, changing their driving habits or lifestyle since March, combining errands and cutting back on dining out because of high prices at the pump, which while falling again, a gallon of gas is still up nearly $1.20 in the last year.\par \par \uc2 Food prices are also surging, ground beef up 53 cents per pound, a gallon of milk 59 cents.\par \par \uc2 JASON TURNER (Pinnon Meats co-owner): When the electric bill goes up, when the cost of cash register tape goes up a little bit, everything goes up a little bit, we have to watch it very carefully or we will be like, out of business.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: At Pinnon Meats in Beloit, Wisconsin, owner, Jason Turner is seeing his costs rise on seemingly everything.\par \par \uc2 JASON TURNER: I see people that are there working and having to buy all this gas just to get back and forth to work, so we hate to raise the prices, but sometimes we have to.\par \par \uc2 All righty, ma`am. That`s going to be $89.90.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Katrea Ward (sp?) says as her grocery bill has jumped 150 bucks. She came here because she found a deal.\par \par \uc2 KATREA WARD (Grocer): I went to another store. One ribeye there is $15.00. I come here, I get them a lot cheaper.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: So you are comparison shopping.\par \par \uc2 KATREA WARD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Turner, the butcher, says cost used to be so stable, he didn`t actually even know how to change meat prices in his scales. Now, the Biden administration points to unemployment levels at 50-plus year lows as a sign of a healthy economy. Tomorrow, we get GDP numbers that are widely expected to show a second consecutive quarter of negative growth and that is a key indicator traditionally, that the US has slipped into recession -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We will be watching for that number. Kris Van Cleave, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the latest battleground in the fight over abortion rights. This was the scene in Indiana Statehouse as lawmakers they`re considered a near total ban.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Evening News" anchor and Managing Editor, Norah O`Donnell sat down with someone who has become a symbol of this debate. That`s Dr. Caitlin Barnard who is an OB-GYN in Indiana who according to State records obtained by CBS News, provided a medication abortion to a 10-year-old rape victim from neighboring Ohio. That state already bans most abortions after six weeks. Because of privacy laws, Dr. Barnard can`t confirm this was her patient.\par \par \uc2 But in our first TV interview, she spoke about what the end of Roe v. Wade means for doctors.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD (Indiana OB-GYN): I think we`re at a time in our country where people are starting to realize the impact of these anti- abortion laws, and now, when it`s finally become impossible for some people, I think people realize that that is actually not what they intended. That is not what they want for children, for women to be put in these situations of life-threatening conditions of traumatic pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: When your name was publicly attached to this case, the Attorney General of Indiana said, he would investigate you. What was your reaction?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: It`s been really difficult.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Indiana`s Attorney General Todd Rokita described you as an abortion activist, acting as a doctor. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: I`m a physician. I`ve spent my entire life working to have this position to be able to take care of patients every single day.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Did you at any point violate privacy laws?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: No.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And have you failed to report any recent abortions?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: No.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How would you describe how things have changed since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: We`re hearing stories all across the country of people who are in dire circumstances, complications of their pregnancies or traumatic situations and are needing abortion care and not able to get it.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Is it worse than you imagined?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: It`s worse faster.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You`ve spent your life, your career taking care of, and providing care for women and babies. What has this Supreme Court decision done?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: You know, this will affect our ability to take care of miscarriages. This will affect our ability to take care of complications in early pregnancy that could kill someone.\par \par \uc2 This will affect our ability to provide infertility treatment, contraception, the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How would you address concerns by conservatives or those with deeply held religious beliefs that abortion is immoral and wrong?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BARNARD: What I would say is, if you don`t believe that you would have an abortion, then don`t have one. You cannot stop other people from accessing medical care that they need based on your personal religious beliefs. You would never want somebody to do that to you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dr. Barnard told us the Indiana Attorney General has only just reached out to her and her lawyer for the first time, nearly two weeks after saying he would investigate her. Dr. Barnard is also pursuing a defamation case against him.\par \par \uc2 I think the point that she makes at the end there is key.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is very powerful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s critical.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Because if you believe it`s wrong, and it is against what you believe, then don`t have one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t have one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The simplicity in which she said, it makes you go, "Oh, okay."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It puts it in perspective.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought so.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. I agree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to turn now to Missouri where torrential downpours and historic flash flooding prompted a State of Emergency there. Record amounts of rainfall left at least one person dead and hundreds more -- look at this -- trapped in their homes and vehicles around St. Louis. You know how tall those signs are.\par \par \uc2 In some areas, First Responders needed boats to save people. Other people just gave up, they abandoned their vehicles and left.\par \par \uc2 This is just the latest in a series of extreme weather events in America. Meteorologist, Jim Cantore from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  has the why -- why this is happening.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Jim.\par \par \uc2 The pictures are pretty stunning, and it does seem -- extreme -- would be the word.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Yes. Extreme is the word. I mean, you had a rainfall event of historic proportions without even dealing with a tropical system, Gayle. So that`s what makes it incredible here.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for our virtual view technology here at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 . I`m standing in Kiener Plaza Park in downtown St. Louis, and what you see is what you get. More rainfall, unfortunately, expected today.\par \par \uc2 We are not going to dry out, which is exactly what we need to do and we won`t really dry -- we`ll get a dry day on Friday, and then we have to wait until next week when it heats up.\par \par \uc2 But it is raining again this morning in the St. Louis area, not to the extent of yesterday, 9.04 inches. We crushed the old record, which was set by a dying tropical system back in 1915 and it wasn`t just that one spot. Remember, you have a huge footprint here of anywhere from three to 13 inches of rain right over the Metro area.\par \par \uc2 Today, we focus more on Kentucky, Southern Virginia, and West Virginia. We may see a very similar setup to what we saw at St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 Is it going to flood like it did in St. Louis? We don`t know, but the potential is there for very, very heavy rainfall in a short period of time.\par \par \uc2 Now, we don`t have five to eight inches or eight to 12 on the forecast, but we could get that in a very short period of time.\par \par \uc2 Hey Gayle, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable and live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jim, got it. Did you buy a Mega Millions ticket?\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: You know, Gayle, I didn`t, but I probably should.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: That`s a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because if you buy one and you win, you could buy \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Byron Allen could work for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s going to be raining money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it`s going to be raining money.\par \par \uc2 Thank you Jim. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: Buy all The Weather Channels.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ve got big news about that Mega Millions jackpot. Have you heard? Nobody won last night. The huge sum you could win, let`s just say it starts with a B as in boy.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, new developments in the case of a South Florida police sergeant who was caught on camera lunging at a fellow officer. We will show you why.\par \par \uc2 And a major announcement about the International Space Station, what it could mean for the future of the project and US-Russia cooperation.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, it is a big day in Court in Russia for \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner. Today`s hearing could be significant.\par \par \uc2 And find out what brought a security guard to tears during a Kendrick Lamar show. You won`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner is back in a Russian courtroom this morning. You know she has been in jail in Russia since mid-February on drug charges. Griner pleaded guilty earlier this month, but she says she had no intent to break the law when she accidentally packed two vape cartridges just before a flight.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi is following the story from London.\par \par \uc2 Roxana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Griner is expected to testify today and possibly be cross-examined on why she brought vape cartridges containing cannabis oil to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, her defense team argued she had been prescribed medical marijuana to treat pain from injuries. Standing in a metal cage as its standard in Russian courtrooms, Griner held up photos of loved ones.\par \par \uc2 When asked if she had any complaints, she said no, just waiting patiently.\par \par \uc2 The two-time Olympic gold medalist has been detained since February after she flew to Moscow to play in a Russian team during the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  offseason. The US said she is wrongfully detained, an accusation Russia denies.\par \par \uc2 Griner faces up to 10 years in prison if convicted. It`s unclear how long the trial will last, but a Court has authorized her detention until mid- December -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we all just went Britney back home. Thank you very much, Roxana Saberi reporting from London.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The continuing tensions between Russia and the US are raising concerns about the future of the International Space Station. Moscow now says it intends to withdraw from the project after 2024.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  and other international partners, they want to keep the station in orbit until 2030, but without the Russians, that may not be possible.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann is outside the Kennedy Space Center.\par \par \uc2 Mark, where does \uc2 NASA\uc2  go from here?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Hey, Vlad. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 No question, the Russians caught \uc2 NASA\uc2  off guard with this announcement, which came without warning and still without official confirmation to the \uc2 US space agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s odd behavior given the fact that the Russians have been NASA`s major international space partner for the last quarter century.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Already orbiting on borrowed time, the ISS could have its longevity shortened further by this announcement from Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Yury Borisov, Russia`s new space director tells President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , "The decision to leave the station after 2024 has been made." So Russia can begin building its own space station. Putin`s response, "Good."\par \par \uc2 MAN: Do you want the relationship to end?\par \par \uc2 ROBYN GATENS (International Space Station Division deputy director): No, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Robyn Gatens, NASA`s ISS Director taken by surprise at a conference.\par \par \uc2 ROBYN GATENS: We haven`t received any official word from the partner, as to the news today, and so we`ll be talking more about their planning going forward.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Also caught off guard, the \uc2 US State Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE (\uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  Spokesperson): It`s an unfortunate development given the critical scientific work performed at the ISS.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Up in space, the ISS crew currently includes an Italian, three Russians, three Americans. The rotating commander, a Russian cosmonaut, Oleg Artemyev.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut, Kjell Lindgren`s reaction?\par \par \uc2 KJELL N. LINDGREN (\uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut): We haven`t heard anything officially. Of course, you know, we were trained to do a mission up here and that mission is one that requires the whole crew.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: On the ISS, the Russian segment provides all propulsion, controlling the station`s altitude. The US controls orientation and provides satellite communication and solar power.\par \par \uc2 Cosmonauts and astronauts work together, but operate their respective segments, even during difficult moments like the war in Ukraine, and this announcement from Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Here`s is what Lindgren told us back in March.\par \par \uc2 KJELL N. LINDGREN: The only way that we can succeed is if we all as a crew work together.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: How do you keep that level of professionalism when there are issues happening 250 miles below?\par \par \uc2 KJELL N. LINDGREN: Our lives depend on them, their lives depend on us.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Among the unanswered questions: What`s the exact timeline of the Russian pull out? Is Moscow serious about it? And could the US and its partners somehow keep the ISS operating without its critical Russian partners?\par \par \uc2 I asked \uc2 NASA\uc2  Administrator Bill Nelson about this possibility back in March.\par \par \uc2 What is the contingency plan?\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON (\uc2 NASA\uc2  Administrator): Well, we know that we can continue it for the short term, and we`d have to do other things and those contingency plans are already there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But the specifics of those contingency plans are unclear and there is absolutely no question that keeping the ISS in orbit without Russian help would become a whole lot more challenging -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As always, we invite you -- it`s more like a gift -- you can get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. You can hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That sounds like a deal to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And next, prosecutors take action after troubling video showing a police sergeant grabbing the neck of a female officer during an arrest.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have the details when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A Florida police sergeant has been charged with felony battery after being caught on bodycam video grabbing a female police officer by the neck during an arrest.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from police bodycam.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The footage shows Sergeant Christopher Pullease lunging for the officer after she pulled him away from a handcuffed suspect in a police cruiser. The female officer has not been publicly named and Pullease has been placed on administrative leave.\par \par \uc2 We asked him for comment and have not heard back.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez shows us how this confrontation unfolded.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT: All right, I am getting in.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: By the time Sunrise Police Sergeant Christopher Pullease arrives at the squad car, the suspect is handcuffed in in the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 SGT. CHRISTOPHER PULLEASE (Florida Police): Look at me [bleep]. You want to [bleep] games. You ever disrespectful with my [bleep] officers. I will remove the [bleep] from your [bleep] body.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: That is when another officer intervenes and tugs at the Sergeant`s belt, removing the superior officer from the situation.\par \par \uc2 But the Sergeant lunges for the female officer`s neck. His hands eventually falling toward her shoulders.\par \par \uc2 SGT. CHRISTOPHER PULLEASE: Never [bleep] touch me again.\par \par \uc2 FEMALE OFFICER: Sir.\par \par \uc2 SGT. CHRISTOPHER PULLEASE: Get the [bleep] off me.\par \par \uc2 FEMALE OFFICER: Sir.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The disturbing body camera footage led prosecutors in Florida to charge Pullease last week with assault and battery on an officer.\par \par \uc2 SUE RAHR (Former Sheriff of Kings County, Washington): That should not have happened.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Sue Rahr, a former Sheriff of Kings County, Washington is an expert in police tactics.\par \par \uc2 SUE RAHR: We need to train our police officers that no matter what you think you`re going to do, you have to override your brain and take steps.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Within days of George Floyd`s killing in Minneapolis, many law enforcement agencies reinforced active bystandership training. Three officers were convicted of Federal Civil Rights violations for failing to intervene.\par \par \uc2 The female officer who has been on the force for about three years is being praised for her actions.\par \par \uc2 FEMALE OFFICER: Sir.\par \par \uc2 SGT. CHRISTOPHER PULLEASE: Everybody turn their off [bleep] cameras.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Which may not have come to light had the officers complied with their Sergeant`s commands.\par \par \uc2 SUE RAHR: I was extremely proud of what that officer did and I think the more we celebrate her courage in that situation, the more we`re going to see that kind of behavior happening.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Pullease pleaded not guilty to all charges. If convicted, he could end up behind bars for up to 11 years.\par \par \uc2 He is currently on administrative leave.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS. I`m Manuel Bojorquez in Miami.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think she had it right when she said, "We should applaud her courage." And also in that moment, she is still calling him "Sir."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was actually trying to help the fellow officer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She was trying to help him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who she could see was clearly -- appeared to be a little out of control because the guy was in the car. She`s trying to help him and the other officers are standing by and she intervenes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll applaud that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The scary thing is, and Gayle, you might have said this. If he can do that to a fellow officer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What happens when he is arresting someone?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If that`s how he treats fellow officers on camera --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: On camera --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With other people standing around, imagine what he could do to other people? It`s very frightening.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it is disturbing he tells them to turn off --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Their bodycams at one point.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is very troubling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will continue to stay on top of that story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you`ll be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:42, Vlad will --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Bruno back in the day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bruno back in the day with Travis McCoy, "Millionaire."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, while we are singing that song, what we`re talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because we are talking Mega Millions. Mega Millions.\par \par \uc2 Who wants to be a billionaire?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everybody is like me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Mega Millions jackpot is up to $1.02 billion after no one hit all of last night`s winning numbers. The next drawing is Friday, and if there is a winner, they could take the cash option and that is still a whopping $602 million before taxes, not too shabby. Not too shabby.\par \par \uc2 So let`s point out that because the jackpot is so big, your chances of winning the grand prize don`t actually go up if you buy a lot of tickets. There have been a lot of stories of people buying thousands of dollars` worth of tickets.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve done that before.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Have you really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It didn`t work, because your odds don`t really increase it by all that much. It is still around one in 302 million. One in 302 million.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s not a bad risk to take. The experience --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like those odds. I like those odds.\par \par \uc2 For perspective, Gayle, that would buy you -- I think this is a funny joke -- that would buy you 255 million Popeyes chicken sandwiches.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A funny joke to who?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not me. Whoever wrote that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is not what I would do if I won?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What would you, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What would you do?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, it sounds very shallow. I will buy a private plane.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing wrong with that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is shallow. I would save the world. I would be very generous to the people I care about. I will definitely do that, but for myself, I would definitely buy a private plane. I would do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Makes travel easier.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What would you do?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think I would buy a sports team.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`d invest half immediately right off the rip and then after that, I would get an investment group together, buy a sports team and enjoy something that I love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like that idea.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What would you, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I would try to start a film studio and produce movies that I want. What Jordan Peele said yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The movies that I see in my mind, the movies that I like, would like to see on the screen. I would try to be like a film mogul, like a movie mogul.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vladimir Global.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Those are good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad+ Streaming Services.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is fun to have a dream.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Take care of our family and our loved ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Going to hook them -- all of those people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is fun to dream. I do like to play this game, and I agree. I think if you`re going to win you really only need one ticket.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Righ