ow you want everybody to perceive your music and you know, make them feel good. Make them feel like a moment they attach to that could live forever.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? Kendrick --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I want to know what he is going through, too, because he is clearly going through something.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it just shows you the power -- what music can do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. The power of music.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I like.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The music, and that`s what Kendrick says, I don`t know what he`s going through, but somehow, he is feeling my music, and it is making him -- taking him to a better place. That`s what music does for us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, we`ve got an update to a story we brought you on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Boaters in Cape Cod were fishing on Sunday when this juvenile humpback whale breached, slamming into their boat, right?\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re getting a look at the extraordinary scene from the boater`s perspective. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, I see big school there ahead of us. Oh.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know pretty frightening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am surprised you didn`t have to bleep anything. It was all, "Ah." Very frightening how close it was.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, it was pretty frightening like when it happens. Luckily, no one was hurt including the whale. Now, I just want to be clear about this.\par \par \uc2 Authorities are warning people to stay -- steer clear of whales that are feeding in the area whenever you`re out there, whenever you`re fishing. But, the people in the boat say that they were steering clear and they watched the whales about 300 yards off their back and then it just made its way over to where they were.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is amazing footage. Oh my goodness. Thank you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Glad and speaking of the water, we`re going to talk with a professional diver and "Shark Attack" survivor Paul de Gelder about a surprising passion for the animals and his new "Shark Week" show, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: A shark attack survivor tells his story. Why professional diver, Paul de Gelder says he is less afraid, and now he wants to save sharks.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers, America`s gun violence epidemic hit close to home for The Smashing Pumpkins` Billy Corgan and his partner, Chloe Mendel, we will talk to them about their benefits show tonight in Highland Park.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. We will introduce you to an artist with a remarkable talent for creation, defying all kinds of expectations. This young man will inspire you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is amazing.\par \par \uc2 But first, let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener at Eight o`clock."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : If I renounce my beliefs, if I agree to stay silent, the persecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would stop immediately.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Breaking overnight: The Department of Justice is conducting a criminal investigation into potential attempts by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The challenge for Garland is, is criminal intent provable at this point?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Fed is expected to raise interest rates again, to help stop surging inflation.\par \par \uc2 JASON TURNER: We have to watch it very carefully or we will be like out of business.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Brittney Griner will testify in Court in Russia today. She faces up to 10 years in prison on drug charges.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Russia says it is withdrawing from the International Space Station after 2024, raising concerns now about future missions.\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE: It`s an unfortunate development given the critical scientific work performed at the ISS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And if you like eating cheese and drinking martinis, well, Velveeta has got a product for you. Introducing the Veltini, made with cheese infused vodka.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: We were able to get our hands on Veltini. Let`s give it a whirl.\par \par \uc2 You know what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The whole time he was holding it up, I was like, he is not, he is not.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what I was thinking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That doesn`t even kind of sound good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That smile is one of those like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, my.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to pass on that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you want one of those, Celine?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We need you to help --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A Veltini.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A Veltini. Velveeta cheese and martinis.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin the hour with this, not with that. We`re going to be talking about the new ways to treat COVID in the future.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, a group of experts gathered at the White House to talk exactly that, about the state of this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder was there. She is a CBS News contributor and editor-at- large at KFS Kaiser Health News and joins us to fill us on what was discussed.\par \par \uc2 But before we think about the new ways, let`s talk about the old ways for just a second.\par \par \uc2 Because Celine, when we first got the vaccine, everybody thought it was a superpower and we don`t have to worry, because that`s normally what vaccines do.\par \par \uc2 Then we`re told you`ve got to get a second vaccine, then we`re told you`ve got to get a booster, then we`re told you got to get a second booster, and then you still get it.\par \par \uc2 So how do you explain how we`re supposed to process this? I think that`s part of the vaccine fatigue.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s part of the frustration.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I think on some level, we over promised what the vaccines could do. I think people thought they would prevent all infections, it would prevent transmission.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: In fact, most vaccines are not really designed to do that. Most vaccines are designed to prevent severe disease, hospitalization and death. So preventing you from getting really sick.\par \par \uc2 And so, what people have realized is, "oh, I`m still getting an infection." You know, why is this happening despite the vaccination? The key is that they are keeping you out of the hospital, but people didn`t really understand what they were supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I think if we had been told that, then our expectations would --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was the messaging, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was the messaging.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because honest to God, when I got it, I thought, I have a superpower, I`m good. All right, so, now as we sit here today, we keep hearing about a new vaccine that`s coming in the fall. Will that vaccine be the one that just solves all of the COVID problems for us? Or does it -- is it just targeted for one type of variant?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So what we`re getting in the fall is being updated for the Omicron BA.5 variant. So that`s the one that you`re seeing across the country right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That the President has.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Exactly, he probably has BA.5.\par \par \uc2 The issue though, is we know variants continue to emerge, the virus continues to mutate. So, we don`t know even by this fall, BA.5 may not be the variant. We may have something else, and we`re kind of chasing our tails right now trying to keep up with the virus.\par \par \uc2 By the time we figured out, there is a new variant, develop an updated vaccine, we might already be on to a new variant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So will it be possible to have one vaccine that covers all the variants no matter what form they take?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So this is one of the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What will it take to get that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes. So that`s one of the technologies we were discussing at the White House yesterday in terms of vaccine advancements. It`s what we would call say, a universal coronavirus vaccine.\par \par \uc2 So it`s trying to target a part of the virus that doesn`t mutate as much. Figuring out what that is, and still getting a vaccine that creates a good immune response, that protects you, is a little bit of a challenge. And it is going to require more resources just like we had Operation Warp Speed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As in money? Is that what you mean?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It is money. It is money. So just like Operation Warp Speed poured in a ton of money to try to expedite the development of the original coronavirus vaccines. If you want these faster, it`s going to cost more money.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But Doctor, going back to Gayle`s point, I mean, if we`ve received the vaccine, then the second one and then the two boosters, and we`re not going to the hospital and we`re not ending up in an ICU or worse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re not compromised.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re not compromised. If I get another vaccine, it is the same thing, right? In other words, I could still be infected, but I wouldn`t be sick enough to go to the hospital. So why not just -- most Americans are going to say, "Well, I`m good."\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because I`m only getting --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am healthy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am healthy. I`m getting infected and it was just a sniffle and it is doing what it said. But I don`t need another vaccine if the results are that I can still be infected.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Get it.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes. We don`t know that it`s going to continue to be that way, that they will continue to hold up and we`ve seen, especially with delta and then omicron, we are seeing especially the elderly right now ending up a bit more in the hospital, even if they`ve had, you know, two or three doses of vaccine, which is also why we`re telling them to get, you know, that fourth dose.\par \par \uc2 And whether we`ll continue to see that erosion in the immunity that you get from these vaccines, we don`t know yet. But we do anticipate they may have more and more trouble protecting us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Earlier you mentioned, vaccine advancement and technology. You have something sitting in front of you. What is that exactly?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I do. I do. So this is a skin patch. It has tiny microscopic needles, so you can`t even see them on here. It`s kind of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you feel them?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Can`t really even feel them, no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But that`s for a vaccine?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It is for vaccines. So you have dry vaccine embedded in these micro needles, and then you take it off kind of like you would have Band-Aid and you stick it on your arm just like that, and you leave it on for a couple of few days and it is delivering the vaccine into your skin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Self-application vaccine.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: You could even do that. I mean, theoretically, you could have your doctor mailing you one of these, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That would replace an injection.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes. This would replace an injection.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And when you take a bath, what happens?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or shower, you can do that.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I have not asked them that. I would suspect you probably need -- want to cover it, so it doesn`t fall off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: But --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m not sure I trust myself enough to do that or trust the technology to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m just not sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, me too.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes. I mean, it`s a pretty new technology. We haven`t used it really for other vaccines yet, but I think there`s a lot of promise, not just for COVID, but for other vaccines.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For other vaccines. Okay.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you so much. You`re the best.\par \par \uc2 All right, professional diver, Paul de Gelder survived a shark attack that made him more compassionate towards the animals. He`s in our progressive greenroom and he will tell us why he got back in the water with a new passion for saving sharks. Yes, that`s right. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:07, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Tonight, Tonight.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Smashing Pumpkins music video for "Tonight, Tonight."\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to talk with front-man Billy Corgan, along with his partner, Chloe Mendel, about hosting a benefit concert for those affected by the Highland Park shooting on July 4th.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shark sightings are increasing from San Diego to Cape Cod fueling people`s fascination with the creatures. This week on "Shark Week" on Discovery Channel, it is longest running cable television programming event in history.\par \par \uc2 Paul de Gelder, is a professional shark diver who appears in several shows this week. His love for the animals actually came after he survived the shark attack in 2009 during a professional Navy dive.\par \par \uc2 Paul was attacked by a nine-foot bull shark and lost his hand and leg.\par \par \uc2 In this clip from the "Shark Week" show this week hosted by Tracy Morgan, Paul gets on the paddleboard to look at Great White sharks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER (Shark attack survivor): It`s there. There`s a shark right there. About six foot Great White.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I see it. I knew you two would come through.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: It is six feet, Scott. I am not harassing it. I`m not following it. Just sort of moving a little bit into the general area, then it comes to check me out.\par \par \uc2 There`s Great White shark right there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Paul de Gelder together joins us now. How are you doing, Paul? Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: I am doing amazing. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re looking good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are amazing.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I want to go back to 2009. What do you remember about that moment when you were attacked by a shark?\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Everything.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Everything?\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: I do a lot of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you were in the military then, right?\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Yes, yes. I was Army Airborne and then I changed over to Navy Clearance Divers, which is like bomb disposal and a lot of other things, which is why it`s such a cool job because it`s very varied, very dangerous, very exciting.\par \par \uc2 And then you turn up to work one day and a nine-foot bull shark decides to eat for breakfast. Not what you`re trained for.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: But also, you are trained for steel under pressure, and so even though the shark grabbed me, took me underwater, ripped me apart, I am in extreme agony, like seconds away from death, I still have the training to get to the surface, swim back to my safety boat with one hand and one leg through a pool of my own blood and still somehow survive, which is incredible to me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In that moment when you swim up, did you know how bad it was?\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: No, I didn`t at all. When I got to the surface. The shark was on the surface as well. It thrashed its tail, splashed water in my face, sort of made me realize, "Oh, I`m not dead."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: I`ve got to get out of here before the shark comes back. So, I started to swim back to the boat and I took a stroke, and that`s when I saw that my hand was gone.\par \par \uc2 And so my training tells me I`ve got to keep that wound above my heart to stem the bleeding not knowing I had an arterial bleed from my whole hamstring being ripped out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: And I`m literally swimming through a pool of my own blood. So I didn`t know how bad my leg was until a week later in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You talk about this, Paul, so calmly. In that moment, did you cry?\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: I have cried myself to sleep more nights that I can even tell you in the early days, absolutely. Because I was terrified of it. It wasn`t about losing my limbs, it was about losing my career. It was everything I`d worked so hard to achieve.\par \par \uc2 I wasn`t a good kid. I wasn`t a good teenager. I was a terrible kid. I did a lot of bad things. But I turned my life around joining the military and doing amazing things, peacekeeping with the UN in war-torn countries, to doing selection for the Navy clearance divers and reaching the elite level of the Navy.\par \par \uc2 And so to put everything into that, and then lose it in eight seconds, that was terrifying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, I`ve watched the Tracy Morgan piece and he had some fun at your expense.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, it shows this buff picture of you coming out of the water, and he said "Paul is a badass shark finder, vegan environmentalist." Tracy said, I`m going to put that on my Tinder profile.\par \par \uc2 But you should also know this. He said, "Paul hasn`t learned a damn thing from his previous encounter." And I think that begs the question, because most people would think after you`ve been through something like that, you were spared. You would think okay, I`m not going to do that again. But that is not what you`re thinking.\par \par \uc2 Even your girlfriend who`s with you today -- Rihanna (sp?), very lovely -- says "I don`t know why he does it, but he is very good at it."\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Well, that`s the thing. I have learned so much. And that`s why I can do what I do. From the shark scientists I work with on "Shark Week" to the cinematographers that film sharks underwater, you just learn everything you can from these people, because it`s like the military, you`re going into really dangerous situations, and you have to rely on the training that you`re given.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Learning is good, Paul, but why are you not afraid? Clearly, you`re not afraid.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: What`s left to be afraid of. I`ve already been eaten alive. That`s part of the reason I went vegan, I am like, I know how much it hurts to be eaten. I don`t want to inflict that on you now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But let`s talk about that confronting your fears and what that means. For you, it means getting back into the water with 18-foot predators who have been evolved to the point where they are today for the last 400 million years.\par \par \uc2 I mean, a shark like this looked exactly the same way more or less hundreds of millions of years ago.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which means that it`s perfect. It`s a perfect, perfect, perfect, perfect predator.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Perfect predator. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How do you -- every time you jump to the water confront that fear? And what`s your message to people about fear?\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Well, think about this, okay? If you went to Africa and you ran into a lion, you couldn`t share that space with that lion. It`ll kill you. Hyenas, bears, wolves, any apex predator -- any predator like that at all in the wild will not let you share it environment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: A shark would let you share its environment. It is an apex predator. It could kill you in a second if it wanted to, but you can swim with it. It will let you share its environment. That`s what makes them so special. No other predator will allow you to do that. And it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said they don`t really want to kill us. What do they want?\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: No. They`re not interested.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said they are curious.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: As you said, they`ve evolved over millions of years. We know what pancakes smell like. They know what food smells like. They know what it acts like.\par \par \uc2 We are not food. We`re not on the menu.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was probably accidental.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: It was absolutely. I talked to the police the day before and they said they pulled a dead body from underneath one of the wharves the day before. So maybe it was attracted to that smell. The water in Sydney Harbor is murky a lot of the time, early morning, cloudy, low light levels, and I`m on the surface.\par \par \uc2 And when you think about looking up from underneath, what do you see? You see a silhouette, you don`t see what it is. So, I could have looked like an injured seal just kind of flapping around on the surface.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So he`s just curious?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is fascinating that it`s almost like you`re giving excuses for this animal that almost killed you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because generally those bites that they take would kill a human being.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It could be fatal.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But it`s actually probably a test bite to see if you are it`s natural prey.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Exactly. Except in my case where it did actually eat me. But you`re right, I am standing up for him and I see this as part of my transfer from military service where my job was to protect people that can`t protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 Now, I have to stand up for this animal and speak up for this animal that doesn`t have a voice, and yet, we slaughter them in the hundreds of millions every year.\par \par \uc2 And so by comparison, 10 people maybe a year in the whole planet die from shark attacks. So, who`s the real monster? Sharks or us? We kill a 100 million, 150 million. They kill 10.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s so powerful coming from you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a unique way of looking at -- yes, coming from you who has lost one arm and one leg. So what does scare you? I`m curious. Clearly, you know how to handle stuff. Clearly you do? What does scare you?\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Well, I just -- I just started snowboarding. Rihanna would know that, and then mum took me to Telluride for the first time. I`ve got a legit snowboarding leg, and there`s like a cliff there. Like right there and I`m snowboarding for the first time. I don`t know how to stop. I don`t know how to turn.\par \par \uc2 I could go off that cliff. There`s no fence to stop me. Snowboarding terrifies me, and that`s why I love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man. Well, listen, we love you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re an amazing individual.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for joining us, Paul de Gelder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So nice to meet you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re the best.\par \par \uc2 PAUL DE GELDER: Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Shark Week" is airing this week on Discovery and streaming on Discovery+.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Watch Paul`s episode.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, an artist with an incredible way of creating -- you thought Paul was incredible -- that he has been honing since birth. He has got an amazing story and guess what? You will only see it here.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Harry Styles. That Harry Styles album is so, so, so good. That`s Harry Styles right there.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some stories that we call "Talk of the Table" this morning because we`ve got a table and we`re talking.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we`re talking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Music for a Sushi Restaurant," right? Harry Styles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the points in that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, so old school.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is President Barack Obama`s annual summer playlist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that on it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s the playlist, Gayle. Yes.\par \par \uc2 The former President tweeted his picks which include some new summer hits, of course, like the one we just played, Harry Styles` "Music for a Sushi Restaurant." Also making the list, some old favorites. This is what I love about President Obama`s list that he puts out every year.\par \par \uc2 It`s got new things on there like Harry Styles, like the new Wyclef Jean with Lauryn Hill "Guantanamera."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? But it`s also got old school stuff like Otis Redding and Dave Brubeck`s "Take Five" which I love. Right?\par \par \uc2 So get this Wyclef Jean, my Haitian brother responded to the tweet saying: "I am happy." I like it that he also says, "Mister."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mister before --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s very Haitian, very polite. It`s the former President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He gets a "Mister." "I`m happy that I made to your summer playlist. I look forward to coming to a show so we can really dance to that."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, he has such an eclectic taste in music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it spans so many varieties. I love, you know, he and Bruce Springsteen are tight. But I love that his favorite Bruce song that made the list is "Dancing in the Dark."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, he`s got "Dancing in the Dark."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is my favorite Bruce Springsteen song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is it yours as well?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I love that song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, that`s so cool. He`s got Miles Davis "Blue and Green." He`s got "Let`s Go Crazy."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Prince. It`s just a really, really, really, really cool list. He even has "Die Hard" Kendrick Lamar on there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. It`s just like him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Go, Barack Obama.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He has got his music list and his book list all of us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it is awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The epitome of cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just like him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very much so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very much so.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What have you got, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve got something good. So my "Talk of the Table" is a small study that asked some college students about the phenomenon known as "ghosting."\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure if you know what this is, but it`s when someone cuts off communication.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I know what it`s like.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know what it is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We all --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They ignore you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad is like, girls ghosted me in high school. Vlad, we`re not talking about your high school experience, okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, damn, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 All right, almost half of the students said they ghosted someone to remove themselves from what they call a toxic, unpleasant, or unhealthy situation. Others admitted to ghosting someone to protect the other person`s feelings by not having them know exactly what`s going on, and just not telling them that they were interested in having a relationship overall.\par \par \uc2 The research found that ghosting can have a negative mental health effect on a person who is ghosted, leading some feeling to have low self-worth or low self-esteem.\par \par \uc2 Here is the fascinating thing about this. There is a lawmaker who has filed a bill seeking to declare the act of ghosting or any communication with someone without explanation a form of abuse.\par \par \uc2 Yes, a form of emotional abuse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know if it is a form of abuse. Most people don`t like unpleasant conversations. They don`t want to just say, "I don`t want to talk to you anymore." So you just ghost --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t want to go to the prom with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you just don`t answer the phone, yes. Don`t answer the phone or you`re busy at your house, February 31st for you to have lunch. These are people --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Girls back then, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was either just the phone or in person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s hard.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, how many men, you ghosted?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I refuse to answer on the grounds it may tend to incriminate. I don`t like -- I don`t like confrontation. I don`t. I don`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You should go, you know what? I wasn`t feeling that date. I`m not going to call him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A lot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t like confrontation.\par \par \uc2 My talk of the table is Lego. We`ve all -- we all know Legos. We play with Legos. We`re still -- everybody loves Lego. So they are launching their new mini-figure factory online. What does that mean? You can now go to the website, you can customize a Lego mini figure that looks like you.\par \par \uc2 Somebody -- I know, ain`t that cool?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can get one to your liking. You get to choose the head, the hair, the hat, the accessories, even the clothes. Does your little mini figure need a hairbrush, a sword, or a tennis racquet? Each custom mini figure -- and I like this -- runs -- it is $11.99, also known as $12.00.\par \par \uc2 But the catch is, there is only one per household because they suspect they`re going to be so popular everybody is going to want one. But you know you`ve seen those Lego figures. They can do great things with Legos I think it`s cool to get your own little mini Lego person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One per house hold?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What if you have multiple kids?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I don`t get that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then you get to choose.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You have to choose one. That`s great. Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know what I would do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe you give different names and different address. I am not encouraging anybody not to do that -- to break the law. But I do think this is a very cool thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very cool. We love Lego.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to switch gears here and now to an act of healing.\par \par \uc2 You know what heals?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What? Music.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "\uc2 Live Nation\uc2 " Smashing Pumpkins performance.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh man, that`s my favorite part of that song "Shake These Zipper Blues." That is the Smashing Pumpkins at their 2018 reunion for performing their hit song in 1979. Front-man Billy Corgan and his partner, fashion designer Chloe Mendel are using music to give back to their Highland Park community outside of Chicago.\par \par \uc2 The couple is hosting a virtual benefit concert to help those affected by the July Fourth Parade shooting that happened there. It is called "Together and Together Again," and tonight`s show includes performances by Billy, Perry Farrell, Jane`s Addiction, electronic duo, Bob Moses, and other very special guests.\par \par \uc2 The July 4 shooting left seven people dead and dozens of others injured at Highland Park`s Annual Independence Day Parade and we are so very happy to welcome Billy Corgan and Chloe Mendel who are joining us from their cafe, Madame ZuZu`s, the venue for tonight`s concert.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you both. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 BILL CORGAN (Smashing Pumpkins front-man): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE MENDEL (Fashion designer): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BILL CORGAN: Thank you so much for having us.\par \par \uc2 CHLOE MENDEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, definitely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Billy and Chloe, before you say anything, Vlad was supposed to go to his next job, but he said, I have to stay here because I`ve been to several Smashing Pumpkins concerts. I love these guys. So, I want to be here. So Vlad is here just for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`s been singing all morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve been singing all morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He has been singing all morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m a big fan since way back with Gish and Siamese Dream. It`s just an honor to meet both of you.\par \par \uc2 So Billy, let me ask both of you about -- you`ve lived in Highland Park for 20 years. What does tonight`s benefit mean to you?\par \par \uc2 BILLY CORGAN: Well, it`s tough because the shooting was literally one block away from where we`re at right now. Our business was closed for a week by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . So during that time, of course, we were so shocked by what had happened, and of course, the damage to our community, not only the carnage that happened, but the psychological effects, the mental health effects.\par \par \uc2 Twelve hundred people a day have been going to the local high school seeking mental healthcare. So we felt that void, and we wanted to step in that void. And so kind of reclaim the space here at Madame ZuZu`s being so close to the incident. As soon as we opened, we started talking about holding a virtual benefit.\par \par \uc2 Last night, we had a private concert for first responders and victims and that was super emotional. So tonight, we bring Highland Park to the world on the Smashing Pumpkins` \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel, and at 8:00 PM Central, and we just want to share love and unity.\par \par \uc2 We`re trying to just use music to kind of bring people together to get everybody moving back in the same direction, and I`ll tell you, this community really needs it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, you know, Chloe, you and Billy are raising your kids in Highland Park. What did you tell them about what happened there? And how do you hope tonight`s concert brings the community together?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE MENDEL: So our children do not know about the event, we still don`t know how to share with them what happened because so many people are hurt, so many children and families are affected by this.\par \par \uc2 So our goal is to be a part of the solution and help people heal. You know, the mental health everything. It`s so overwhelming. So our children will find out about it eventually and we will have a family talk about community and safety and how it`s important to help each other.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CORGAN: And our kids are really young. Our kids are six in three. And yes, I`m an old dad. So that`s a conversation -- that`s a conversation that`s really tough for us to have at this time.\par \par \uc2 Our kids are definitely asking questions, and the day will come, but we`re just not there yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I was wondering how old your kids were. That was my next question. So Billy, you`re reading my mind, and how do you even begin to explain it to kids? It`s hard to process as adults. When you`re watching it, the July 4 Parade, it`s still very hard to believe that this has happened in your community.\par \par \uc2 So how is the community healing? I know music is very healing. I so get that and so believe that. But how is the community healing right now?\par \par \uc2 BILLY CORGAN: Well, Highland Park is traditionally a very tight-knit community anyway. A lot of people moved up out of the city to deal with depression in the city, mostly Jewish community up here.\par \par \uc2 So this is a community that is very sensitive to gun violence. And so I think they`re turning to each other not only to heal, but to sort of organize and get focused on the future.\par \par \uc2 As far as young people go, you see them really at a loss for what to do. They`re animated, they want to do something, but they feel so powerless and you really see it with the young people in this community. They are really grappling with what this means for them.\par \par \uc2 They`re so terrified, and at the same time, they want you to turn that into some form of action. And so you see people out at the vigils just a half a block away from here, really trying to kind of grapple with how to animate their heart into something meaningful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To the outside world, we often identify these cities and small towns with the tragedies they experience.\par \par \uc2 Chloe, you say it`s important for Highland Park not to be remembered or identified with this tragedy that it is important to move on. Why so?\par \par \uc2 CHLOE MENDEL: Because we`re not defined by this tragedy and I hope everybody knows that. We`re defined by how the community has come together to recognize those who need help, and be there for each other. It`s incredible.\par \par \uc2 And to help not only the physical struggles people are going to deal with your being impacted by the gun violence, but also the mental health help that they`re going to need today, tomorrow, and for years. People know that and see that and they want to be there to help each other.\par \par \uc2 So the funds that we`re going to be raising will go towards all of that help. And we are honored to be able to use our platform to reach all of these people.\par \par \uc2 Our hearts hurt, our neighbors are hurting. We see it every day. We have customers coming in who say I picked up my three-year-old and just ran for my life, and it`s over and over and over again. So, our hearts really hurt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and we hurt with you.\par \par \uc2 Billy and Chloe, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The benefit will show live stream on Smashing Pumpkins` \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel where viewers can donate to the Highland Park Community Foundation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`ll introduce you to an artist whose creativity could not be contained even without tools most of us take for granted every single day.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning we`re introducing you to a talented artist who says he won`t let anything stop him from creating art.\par \par \uc2 These other works of Desmond Blair, some of which were featured in an exhibit at a North Texas gallery this summer. His work though is partly inspired by a unique circumstance, some would regard as a disadvantage, but which he used as a strength.\par \par \uc2 Although he was born with immense creativity, he was also born without hands.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca introduces us to Blair and his work.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Every stroke of Desmond Blair`s brush --\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR (Artist): I know where I`m blocking my lights, my shadows.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Brings a rich and beautiful subject to life on canvas.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: Just kind of contours where those happen at.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The way he paints eyes and the vivid details of light in his portraits hint at the years he has been practicing his craft, but his dedication and how the 35-year-old handles a brush is a work of art in itself.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: Part of it was because of the way I was born. So since I was born with our hands, it`s just like, people would tell me what I wouldn`t be able to do, but it`s like, if I had a sheet of paper, and like a Pilot Pen, like you`re not going to take that away from me. And if you do, I can just go get another one.\par \par \uc2 And so the same thing, once I started painting and doing other things, it`s just like, you can say what you want, but I`m still going to go buy a canvas. I`m still going to keep painting.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Blair was born without hands caused by a genetic mutation. Growing up in Dallas, his mother and grandmother encouraged and pushed him to find his own way to do things.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: Your journey is going to be different and we have to figure this out. And so it was always that mindset of, you know, still expect excellence, but we`ve got to figure out how to get there.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: At school, he had to get creative to learn how to write with a pencil. But when the art bug bit Blair at an early age, he taught himself how to handle crayons with his wrists, and explore his gift.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: We tried a bunch of different things like putting crayons in my mouth, holding crayons with my feet. So I figured out how to write.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: That`s the writing side. That`s for you to be this, Desmond the Student.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: What was it about crayons that triggered Desmond the Artist?\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: I watched cartoons. And so I wanted to learn how to draw the characters in the cartoons.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: As a kid, his mom enrolled him in his summer art program in Dallas. There, he met one of his mentors, Emmanuel Gillespie.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL GILLESPIE (Artist): I think everyone has that creative ability, but he had that passion. Anyone can do it, but it is the passion that drives it.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: From crayons, Blair turned to acrylics, then to oil. In college, his talent grew, as did his skill. And more than 25 years later.\par \par \uc2 This is a person you drew?\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: In the series yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: His first public exhibit at Dallas` Pencil on Paper Gallery, owned by his biggest supporter, Gillespie and his wife, Valerie.\par \par \uc2 I think when people would look at this, they focus on the art, and they see you and they`re like, okay, here`s the artist, but they`re always drawn to the fact that you don`t have hands.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: How do you balance that and say, no, no, look at that, not me, that.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: I look at it as an opportunity to educate people about limb differences.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: His collection is titled "In the Garden" and features Black women as Eve in the famous biblical story.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We have Desmond portraying us in this beautiful light. He captures our essence, our skin, our mannerisms, everything just stunningly. He sees us and he appreciates us and he translate that onto canvas so all of us can see.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: His next project will be difficult and personal. He plans to paint his muse and motivation, Joyce Blair, his mother. She passed away in 2020 after a battle with ALS.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND BLAIR: I wanted to make sure I was in the right space and place to be able to do it. It would be like a full circle moment because the things that she taught me growing up as she advanced with her disease, it was just like, "Okay, we`ve done this before. We`ve learned how to adapt."\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Art is meant to inspire others, but sometimes the artist is a masterpiece.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Omar Villafranca, Dallas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Omar, nice way to end it sometimes, Nate the art --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is the masterpiece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The artist is the masterpiece. I love that the influence of his mother and his grandmother who said despite everything that`s happened to him, they still expect excellence.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he`s still doing it -- the detail on his work.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The detail, and when he was younger, putting him in that art camp.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Saying regardless of what my son is doing --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at that detail.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not having hands, we want to put him in something that he is passionate for and look where he is at now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Desmond Blair.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We will be right\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your mom`s watching you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mom is watching you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope you were a little inspired today because we had two people. We had Paul who lost an arm and a leg, but he is still diving with the sharks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I still can`t get over Desmond the Artist.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes. Who was born without hands.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Born without hands.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, right. It is inspiring.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Their abilities are their superpowers.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220727ei7r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 An Exclusive Interview With Dr. Caitlin Bernard, The Indiana OB-GYN Who, According To State Documents, Provided A Nonsurgical Abortion To A 10- Year-Old Rape Victim\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 860 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, the state-by-state battle over abortion rights are far from over. In Indiana, thousands of protesters demonstrated at the statehouse, as lawmakers gathered to consider a Republican bill that would ban nearly all abortions and punish doctors who perform them.\par \par \uc2 One of the doctors is Caitlin Bernard. She`s an OB-GYN in Indiana. State health records obtained by CBS News show that she provided a nonsurgical abortion to a 10-year-old rape victim from Ohio. That state has a strict ban, without exceptions, for rape and incest. But, because of privacy laws, the doctor can`t confirm this was her patient.\par \par \uc2 And now Dr. Bernard is sitting down for her first television interview since the case garnered international attention.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: I think we`re at a time in our country where people are starting to realize the impact of these anti-abortion laws, and now, when it`s finally become impossible for some people, I think people realize that that is actually not what they intended, that is not what they want, for children, for women to be put in these situations of life-threatening conditions, of traumatic pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Generally speaking, how often do you receive calls from doctors in other states that say they have young, young women who have been raped and need an abortion?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: Unfortunately, sexual assault in children is not uncommon. I`m not the only provider who has taken care of young children needing abortion care.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: When your name was publicly attached to this case, the attorney general of Indiana said he would investigate you. What was your reaction?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: It`s been really difficult.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Have you felt threatened?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: Yes. Yes, I have.\par \par \uc2 And it -- you know, it shows how abortion, instead of being part of health care, which it is, a needed lifesaving procedure, which it is, has been used to create a wedge between people politically and personally.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Indiana`s attorney general, Todd Rokita, described you as an abortion activist acting as a doctor. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: I`m a physician. I spent my entire life working to have this position, to be able to take care of patients every single day.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Did you at any point violate privacy laws?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: No.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And have you failed to report any recent abortions?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: No.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How would you describe how things have changed since the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: We`re hearing stories all across the country of people who are in dire circumstances, complications of their pregnancies, or traumatic situations and are needing abortion care, and not able to get it?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Is it worse than you imagined?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: It`s worse faster.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You have spent your life, your career taking care of and providing care for women and babies. What has this Supreme Court decision done?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: You know, this will affect our ability to take care of miscarriages. This will affect our ability to take care of complications in early pregnancy that could kill someone. This will affect our ability to provide infertility treatments, contraception. The list goes on.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How would you address concerns by conservatives or those with deeply held religious beliefs that abortion is immoral and wrong?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD: What I would say is, if you don`t believe that you would have an abortion, then don`t have one.\par \par \uc2 You cannot stop other people from accessing medical care that they need based on your personal religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And this is new tonight. Dr. Bernard told us that the Indiana attorney general has reached out to her and her lawyer for the first time today, nearly two weeks after saying he would investigate her.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard is also pursuing a defamation case against him.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in Texas today released surveillance video from that shooting incident at Dallas Love Field Airport on Monday. Portia Odufuwa has been charged with aggravated assault against a public servant after allegedly opening fire into the ceiling and at an officer. She`s recovering after an officer returned fire.\par \par \uc2 Officials say the suspect is apparently obsessed with singer Chris Brown and has been found incompetent to stand trial more than once in recent years.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220728ei7q000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Maternal Health Aftershock\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 1,825 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour with a startling look at maternal health in this country.\par \par \uc2 Black women are about three times more likely to die from a pregnancy related cause than White women. The maternal mortality rate for Black women increased significantly from 2018 to 2020. The new documentary "Aftershock" follows the families of women who died from preventable causes related to pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We keep showing up. We`re not letting the pain stop us.\par \par \uc2 We are holding people who have the most power accountable for how they use it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Our maternal health community is really pushing for change on Capitol Hill, trying to bring about different legislation in order to make sure that women have the care that they are supposed to.\par \par \uc2 BRUCE MCINTYRE III (Amber Rose Isaac and her longtime partner): I can`t let Amber be another statistic. I`m making people aware of what`s going on in these systems.\par \par \uc2 I planned on spending a lifetime with Amber, I wanted to get her in my life. This way I`m still going to.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, the husband stories are heartbreaking. Tonya Lewis Lee is "Aftershock" co-director and co-producer; Dr. Neel Shah is Chief Medical Officer of Maven Clinic and Assistant Professor at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We welcome you both. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 I want to set the table guys with statistics from the CDC, so people have an understanding about what we`re going to talk about.\par \par \uc2 Black women, three to four times more likely to die than White women with the same symptoms. Over 60 percent of the deaths are preventable. And Black women today are more likely to die of childhood complications than their parents, which means our daughters, Tonya have a better chance of dying than we did.\par \par \uc2 You know, I knew that this was a crisis. I had sort of heard about it. But when I saw this documentary, it made me so angry and so sad. I think "Aftershock" is a very good title for it.\par \par \uc2 Why is this happening, Tonya?\par \par \uc2 TONYA LEWIS LEE ("Aftershock" co-director and co-producer): Well, it`s happening because unfortunately, the healthcare system is built on a system of racism. And people don`t want to listen to Black women when they have complications and symptoms.\par \par \uc2 Amber Rose Isaac, Shamony Gibson, two women whose families we follow in the film, they went looking for help and they were dismissed when they had -- when they were expressing their symptoms.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, they went to the hospital Neel and they were complaining about the symptoms -- shortness of breath, I can`t breathe, chest pains, and time after time in one case, the medical staff on three different occasions says "Is she on drugs? Does she do drugs?"\par \par \uc2 Why are Black women not believed, do you think?\par \par \uc2 DR. NEEL SHAH (\uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2  Assistant Professor): Tonya said that the healthcare system is built on racism and one of the things that I like to remind myself and remind others often is that a bad system will beat a good person every time and you know, bias is sort of built into the way doctors are trained where --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doctors are trained to be biased? What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 DR. NEEL SHAH: They`re not -- well, you know, there`s a really thin line between clinical intuition, which is celebrated, which is like looking at someone and knowing if they`re sick or not, and what`s effectively racism.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you think it has to do with training?\par \par \uc2 DR. NEEL SHAH: I think a hundred percent, it has to do with training.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, abortion isn`t mentioned in this film, but the overturning of Roe v. Wade is the backdrop in which people are watching this. So when you think about the connection between abortion access, safe births, and also the overall maternal mortality crisis, how do you see that all almost laying together?\par \par \uc2 TONYA LEWIS LEE: Yes, I`m deeply concerned. I`m deeply concerned for Black women. I`m deeply concerned about delay of care when Black women have complications that they may have delayed care. I`m deeply concerned about the criminalization of women, when there are miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 I`m deeply concerned that Black woman will be criminalized. I just think that we are in a system in a situation where the autonomy of women`s bodies is in conversation and we are already in a crisis around maternal health, and the Dobbs decision just is going to exacerbate it.\par \par \uc2 I believe -- there was a study that just came out that maternal death is going to go up 21 percent given this decision, 33 percent for Black women.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t think people realize this is a crisis, so guys, there`s a line in the doc that really made the hair stand up on my neck where it says, "Black women having a baby is like a Black man in a traffic stop." What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 TONYA LEWIS LEE: Well, I think you`ve got to pay attention every step of the way. Again, when we, as Black people are in certain systems, whether it`s the criminal justice system, or the healthcare system, as Neel just talked about, there is a bias against Brown and Black people.\par \par \uc2 And so you need to be paying attention to who is dealing with you and how they`re perceiving you and making sure that you advocate for yourself in the best way possible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In other words, one wrong move, and it could cost you your life.\par \par \uc2 TONYA LEWIS LEE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, Dr. Shah, one of the things that I found fascinating in the documentary is you talk about Cesarean sections, and how before 1970, less than five percent of women who were delivering had Cesarean section (C-sections) and it`s risen by 30 percent in 2019, but the outcomes have remained the same. We`re talking major surgery.\par \par \uc2 And yet hospitals make more money if they perform C-sections and spend less time in the operating room with a patient. Break that down for our audience.\par \par \uc2 DR. NEEL SHAH: That`s exactly right. So since the 1970s, C-section rates have gone up by 500 percent. It is designed to be a life-saving surgery, but the reason that we`re here is that maternal mortality is going in the wrong direction, it`s going up. And term infants are zero percent better off on average over that time period.\par \par \uc2 And there is a lot of conventional wisdom about what`s driving C-section rates --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But they`re only -- it`s higher for Black women.\par \par \uc2 DR. NEEL SHAH: Yes, it is even higher for Black women.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s startling.\par \par \uc2 DR. NEEL SHAH: Which I think is really revealing, right? It`s not necessarily that people are demanding more C-sections. It`s that people are investing less and supporting people in labor, which is why the film depicts an alternate way to support people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But yet, you all say that you want this film to be empowering, because I tell you, I choked up a couple of times when you see the women before they had their babies, so full of life and joy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, that was empowering.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and what their husbands are dealing with.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very excited to be mothers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very excited. Yes, they were so ready, Vlad. So I choked up. But then it made me so angry. But you say Tonya, you want the takeaway to be empowering? How?\par \par \uc2 TONYA LEWIS LEE: I do. I do. First of all, I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The more it is a must-see. It is a must see.\par \par \uc2 TONYA LEWIS LEE: Yes. Well, first of all, I think it`s really important that you feel for these families, and we needed to put a human face on the women who are dying.\par \par \uc2 We read the statistics, but they`re not numbers. They`re people. So you have to understand who they are.\par \par \uc2 But then on the other hand, you know, we do show an amazing birth in the film by a woman who really decided, I mean, she thought she was going to do the regular thing and then sort of made a decision to see what other options are out there for her.\par \par \uc2 So I would say, I want the film to be empowering because I want women to come away -- and families -- to understand there is choice in birthing. You can birth in many different ways. You don`t necessarily have to do it at a hospital, and that there are things that we all can do to improve birthing outcomes, and it is really incumbent upon us to figure out what that is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you gave us a lot to think about.\par \par \uc2 One woman in the film, Shamony Makeba Gibson, who is no longer with us, we will not forget you. I mean, it`s very powerful and very well done.\par \par \uc2 Bravo to you both.\par \par \uc2 TONYA LEWIS LEE: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Excellent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The name of the documentary is called "Aftershock." It is streaming now on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 , so you can actually see it today.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, bestselling author and actress, Danica McKellar shares the importance of getting kids excited about math and how her new book can help, but first, it is -- where is the clock -- 8:09.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Eight oh nine.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, we sit down with Jordan Peele, the filmmaker behind the number one movie in the country, "Nope." And you`ve got to say just like that -- why likes to combine his talent for comedy with a love of horror stories.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Speaking of horror, this guy was scary on the field. We talk with former "former" \uc2 NFL\uc2  star Rob Gronkowski about his recent retirement. Yes, right. And what`s ahead for him. He has a special surprise for one student athlete nominated for a top award.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to take bets. Do we think Gronk is retiring? Go Clark, go. Be careful.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220728ei7q000um\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Indiana`s Abortion Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nancy Chen \par \uc2 410 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The state by state battle over abortion rights continued in Indiana yesterday. Protesters demonstrated at the Statehouse as lawmakers gathered to consider a bill that would ban nearly all abortions.\par \par \uc2 As Nancy Chen shows us, the implications go far beyond state lines.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Thousands of demonstrators gathered both inside --\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And outside the Indiana Statehouse on Monday as members of the State`s General Assembly met in a special session to discuss a bill that would ban almost all abortions, except in cases of rape, incest, or to protect the mother`s life.\par \par \uc2 The protesters were often so loud, they drowned out the debates inside the chamber.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD shouting.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m just sorry, I didn`t get all of that, Senator.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GRIFFIS (Pro-abortion rights protester): It is not a man`s choice. It`s not the government`s choice. It`s not your church`s choice. It`s a woman`s choice.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was also in Indianapolis yesterday meeting with State and local leaders about protecting reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice President of the United States): We are seeing indeed many states since the Dobbs decision and attempting even before to criminalize health professionals, to punish women.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Indiana is in the spotlight as the first State since the overturning of Roe to hold a special legislative session aimed at further restricting abortion.\par \par \uc2 And just last month, a 10-year-old rape victim made headlines after crossing state lines from Ohio to have the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But without any new criminal penalties against doctors performing an abortion, that bill`s future could be in doubt, as some Republicans argue the law would be too weak.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG HESS (Anti-abortion rights protester): Indiana has this opportunity to end abortion and we need to take advantage of it and end abortion. Period.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Nancy Chen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220728ei7q000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Latest Court Battle Playing Out Across The Country Since The Supreme Court Overturned Roe Vs. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Manuel Bojorquez \par \uc2 635 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A West Virginia judge today blocked that state from enforcing its 150-year-old abortion ban. It`s the latest court battle playing out across the country since the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade, including in Florida, where a 15-week ban was allowed to take effect while on appeal.\par \par \uc2 Here \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Dr. Samantha Deans of Planned Parenthood says their facilities took in as many women seeking abortions as they could before Florida`s 15-week ban on the procedure went into effect this month.\par \par \uc2 DR. SAMANTHA DEANS (Associate Director, Planned Parenthood of South, East, and North Florida): We had to increase our staffing. We had to increase our appointments.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The law was briefly blocked by a judge, but reinstated when the state appealed, adding to the confusion.\par \par \uc2 DR. SAMANTHA DEANS: Our patients equally have been confused and devastated and struggling and panicked.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: So, you had to turn women away?\par \par \uc2 DR. SAMANTHA DEANS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: What are those conversations like?\par \par \uc2 DR. SAMANTHA DEANS: Those are some of the hardest conversations that I have to have with patients.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Still, Florida`s abortion law is less restrictive than those in neighboring states, meaning many women from out of state come here. Florida is on track to surpass last year`s total of out-of-state abortions, nearly 4,900.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW SHIRVELL (Founder and Executive Director, Florida Voice for the Unborn): Florida is turning into an abortion destination state here in the South.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Andrew Shirvell is founder of Florida Voice for the Unborn.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW SHIRVELL: We need a total ban on abortions here.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Shirvell is calling on Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to convene a special legislative session to further restrict access.\par \par \uc2 But after signing the 15-week ban into law in April, the governor has yet to provide specifics on what additional restrictions he`d support. It`s fueling speculation that it`s a political tight spot as he seeks reelection this year and eyes a potential run for president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW SHIRVELL: I`m 100 percent confident that Florida will enact further abortion restrictions or bans. How far we go, though, is really up to the governor.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The governor has said he is confident the new law will survive the legal challenges. As it stands now, Florida has the third highest abortion rate among the states -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, still ahead on tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS: A New York City firefighter is hailed as a hero for two acts of bravery, including -- look at this one that sent him to a hospital.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, a New York City firefighter is recovering after getting pinned underneath an SUV that drove on top of another vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Warnock was trying to rescue passengers, when the driver hit the gas, causing this SUV to fall on top of him. And this happened on Sunday, just days after Warnock took part in another heroic rescue effort after that boat capsized in the Hudson River.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next: how this North Carolina man is sowing seeds of success for the next generation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220720ei7i0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Why An Indiana Doctor Is Under Investigation After Performing The Procedure On A 10-Year-Old Rape Victim\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Elaine Quijano \par \uc2 458 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Turning now to the abortion case that has gained international attention after a 10-year-old rape victim from Ohio was taken to Indiana for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Quijano.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voice-over): Tonight, Indiana`s Republican attorney general, Todd Rokita, told CBS News that his office is investigating whether Dr. Caitlin Bernard, a local OB-GYN physician, followed state law, which requires her to report that she performed an abortion on a 10-year- old girl who was a victim of rape.\par \par \uc2 The girl traveled from Ohio, where most abortions are illegal once there is cardiac activity, usually at six weeks. Yesterday, Dr. Bernard tweeted: "My heart breaks for all survivors of sexual assault and abuse. I am so sad that our country is failing them."\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2  works for an abortion rights group.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH NASH (\uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 ): That the best thing for a sexual assault survivor who needs an abortion is not an abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, Ohio is one of at least nine states which make no exceptions for rape or incest. In Indiana, abortions are still legal up to 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are physicians now about potentially being in legal jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH NASH: Well, they`re incredibly concerned, because, more than anything, providers want to provide care.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: The girl`s case sparked international attention after President Biden made reference to it as he signed an executive order to protect access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Ten years old, raped, six weeks pregnant, already traumatized, was forced to travel to another state.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Yesterday, 27-year-old Gerson Fuentes was arraigned in Ohio for allegedly raping the girl.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Tonight, the Indiana doctor`s lawyer released a statement, saying -- quote -- "My client, Dr. Caitlin Bernard, took every appropriate and proper action in accordance with the law and both her medical and ethical training as a physician. She followed all relevant policies, procedures and regulations in this case."\par \par \uc2 The attorney says they are considering legal action to clear her name -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elaine Quijano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220716ei7e0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Ohio`s Abortion Law Controversy\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Elaine Quijano, Vladimir Duthiers, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 914 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turning now to a case that`s getting national attention after the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. This week, a 27-year-old man named Gerson Fuentes was charged with raping a 10-year-old girl in the state of Ohio, and now, that little girl then traveled to Indiana for an abortion because under a new law in the State of Ohio, it would have required her to give birth unless it was determined her life was in danger.\par \par \uc2 As Elaine Quijano reports, the case is showing the results of America`s new abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY HUHN (Columbus, Ohio detective): She indicated that her 10-year- old daughter was pregnant.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Columbus detective, Jeffrey Huhn testified in an arraignment, Wednesday, that 27-year-old, Gerson Fuentes confessed to raping a 10-year-old girl at least twice leading to her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 PROSECUTOR: Is that daughter still pregnant today?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY HUHN: No.\par \par \uc2 PROSECUTOR: Why not?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY HUHN: The victim went to -- went out of state to have a medical -- a medically terminated, abortion.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Huhn said the victim underwent the abortion in neighboring Indiana at the end of June. The Indiana doctor who said she performed the abortion said the child was six weeks and three days pregnant. That`s three days longer than Ohio allows an abortion to take place.\par \par \uc2 Ohio was one of 11 states with bans that have no exceptions for rape or incest. That`s despite national polling that says an overwhelming majority of people support abortions in those conditions.\par \par \uc2 TODD ROKITA (R) (Indiana Attorney General): We are gathering the evidence as we speak.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Indiana`s Attorney General is promising to investigate the doctor who performed the procedure and if she followed proper reporting protocols.\par \par \uc2 TODD ROKITA: We`re going to fight this to the end, including looking at her licensure. If she failed to report in Indiana, it`s a crime to not report -- intentionally not report.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Imagine being a little girl.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: The case sparked outrage from President Biden last week while he`s signed an Executive Order to protect access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Ten years old, raped, six weeks pregnant. Already traumatized, was forced to travel to another state.\par \par \uc2 CARY FRANKLIN (UCLA Center on Reproductive Health Law and Policy director): We`re going to start to see a lot more tragic and difficult cases.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Cary Franklin, Director of UCLA`s Center on Reproductive Health Law and Policy said since the nationwide right to abortion was eliminated by the Supreme Court, these types of cases will happen more often.\par \par \uc2 CARY FRANKLIN: In a lot of anti-abortion states, people will find themselves unable to terminate pregnancies that are the product of rape and incest and they will find themselves in difficult, traumatic situations.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Detective Huhn said DNA tests are being conducted to confirm the paternity. Fuentes is scheduled to be in Court again next week. There has been no comment from his defense attorney.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is a tough story. "The Columbus Dispatch" in Ohio, which has been covering the stories you know, Elaine says that since May 9th, they found -- this is quoting them -- up to 50 incidences, police reports filed of people, young women under the age of 15 that have been raped.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: This story really illustrates just how complex this issue is. How deeply personal this issue is. And you know, this is a case that has a lot of Americans reflecting and we`re going to hear I`m sure more about it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And 10 years old, it`s just like "ugh." It`s a punch in the gut.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is an awful story.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Really horrific.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s an extreme case, but it shows the extremity of the law in about 11 states. No exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Elaine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Carter Evans goes out on the water to take a look at great white sharks that live close to shore. The animals are having an impact on tourism that you might not expect.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, a star-studded roadblock to Southern California`s water conservation efforts. We`ll show you how some of Hollywood`s elite are using up to three times as much water as they are supposed to, and yes, we`re naming names.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: "Are We Out of the Woods?" That is apropos because coming up we will take you on a culinary adventure where all the food is free.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Yuccas introduces us to a popular forger who is showing millions on social media how to find food and turn it into a delicious meal.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220715ei7e000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Vasectomies On The Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Manuel Bojorquez, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 1,444 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got some big news to tell you about the battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration now says that Federal law will take precedent over State law when it comes to providing mothers with abortion services if their lives are at risk.\par \par \uc2 Now yesterday, the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  told hospitals they must provide such services for emergency conditions like ectopic pregnancies and complications of pregnancy losses even in states where abortion is banned with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: At the same time, more men are taking action themselves in the aftermath of Roe v. Wade turning to vasectomies. The number of \uc2 Google\uc2  searches for the word "vasectomy" peaked just one day after the Supreme Court`s ruling last month.\par \par \uc2 And now, doctors are saying that it`s not just searches, more patients are asking for the procedure, IRL -- in real life.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is in Miami Shores with more.\par \par \uc2 Manny, I`m going to be watching this very closely. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: That`s right. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 You know, we also spoke with a doctor who said the way he found out about the Supreme Court`s decision that day is by the sheer number of phone calls coming in to his clinic. He said they tripled their normal volume and the number of men signing up for the procedure tripled, too.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN (Urologist): I just want to get a feel for why you feel so confident that you`re going to be happy with your vasectomy decision indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: From his office near Tampa, urologist Doug Stein calls patients who are under 30 or do not have children to discuss whether they are 100 percent sure, they want a vasectomy, a call he is now making more often.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: We generally see about 12 percent in our practice, men who are childfree, and then we generally say about 12 percent of men under the age of 30, but the number of both young and childfree nearly doubled.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The two-doctor practice perform six vasectomies an hour most days.\par \par \uc2 Some decisions not just made by men, but by families.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: Fifty percent of our calls are from women.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I just need to sign and date it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Twenty-seven-year-old Thomas Figueroa says he and his girlfriend do not want children. So getting a vasectomy was always on his mind.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS FIGUEROA (Vasectomy patient): I`ve always thought about this decision. The Supreme Court did push me to finally do it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The procedure which block sperm from entering semen is outpatient, and takes about 10 minutes with about a two-day recovery, as opposed to a female tubal ligation, which is riskier and much more invasive.\par \par \uc2 For Figueroa, this is also about easing the burden on women who have had their constitutional right to abortion taken away, and for those who feel restrictions on birth control could come next.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS FIGUEROA: I feel for a lot of men, especially for myself that this is a way where they`re trying to protect their girlfriends or try and protect their partners, they`re trying to protect their future partners.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: In Houston, Jordan Castro`s post about getting a vasectomy went viral. He did it last fall after Texas passed a law banning abortion after about six weeks of fertilization.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN CASTRO (Vasectomy patient): Really try it put yourself in a woman`s shoes. I really think I should take that into account and just take a little bit more responsibility.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And while vasectomies are reversible, not all reversals are successful.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: All right, very good. Do you have any pain?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: That`s something Dr. Stein stresses to his growing number of patients.\par \par \uc2 And do you see this as a trend that will continue or just kind of a spike?\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: I think that may be where the spike is over, and we`re tapering down a little bit, but I think we haven`t tapered back down to pre Roe overturned numbers.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: In fact, he says they are booked through August and that`s as far in advance as they`ve ever been booked before -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Manny, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of cringing in the studio.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We all had a reaction to that shot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I didn`t realize that you would be awake while the procedure is actually happening. That`s -- I thought you`d go under the knife even though it`s only under the -- you know, to sleep even though it`s only 10 minutes. But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, Nate has talked about having a vasectomy, so I`m not telling tales out of school. He compared it to cutting off his sprinkler system, which I thought was a really good way to say it and he said that when he went in for the procedure for a consultation, he ended up getting it that same day because it was so simple.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the laws must be different in New Jersey because I`ve been -- I talked to Nate about it. I talked to other friends about it and there is a waiting period in New York. You have to wait 30 days and then have to get it within a six-month window. It`s easier to buy a gun than it is to get a vasectomy in New York State.\par \par \uc2 But my two questions were, going into it, is the fun stuff still fun? And the doctor was like, yes. I can confirm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the fun stuff?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s tender hour.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they said yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they said yes. The fun stuff is still fun.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The rock `n roll.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then, like your inner caveman, does he still feel like he`s there? Like do you still feel a little sense of virility? This is a real thing for guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Nate says, yes. And other friends I`ve talked to have said, yes. So I`m all in. If not were for the waiting period, I would do it today also.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You would?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. If they had like a mobile van outside right now, why don`t we have that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mobile van.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You would? You would do it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Totally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was interesting to see all the men in the studio cringe. Maxi goes, how come he`s not knocked out? I`m surprised that he`s just looking at it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. That was the weird thing because the shot of the camera was like -- he`s like this. He`s like, "Okay, what`s going on down there?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it is not painful -- I guess, it`s not that painful, either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know. I have no idea. I`m in the waiting period.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People are looking at Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ll let you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Klaus? Yes, Klaus.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let me grab this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is interesting that more men are choosing to do this now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a small thing compared to what women do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In the wake of this decision. Yes, indeed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, coming up, four-time Olympic gold medalist, Mo Farah tells an amazing story about his origins in Africa, and how he was brought to London to do housework.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. You will hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220713ei7c000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 After The Supreme Court Struck Down Abortion Rights, The Medical Procedure More Men Are Asking For\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Manuel Bojorquez \par \uc2 547 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And breaking tonight, Louisiana`s law banning abortions has once again been blocked by a court. This opens the door for clinics to resume services, at least temporarily.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty about abortion rights in the aftermath of the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe vs. Wade has more American men now looking into and requesting vasectomies.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN (Tampa, Florida): You have so much of your reproductive future ahead of you.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): In the days following the Supreme Court`s decision, Dr. Doug Stein says the number of calls to his clinic near Tampa tripled, as did the number of men signing up for vasectomies.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: With rights being taken away from women, and we know what they have been through with them being primarily responsible for contraception, it`s just time for men to step up to the plate.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The number of patients under 30 without children nearly doubled. He takes extra steps to confirm they are 100 percent sure they want the procedure.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS FIGUEROA (Patient): Let`s go for it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Twenty-seven-year-old Thomas Figueroa is one of them. He says he and his girlfriend do not want children, so a vasectomy had been on his mind.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS FIGUEROA: I have always thought about this decision. The Supreme Court did push me to finally do it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: He let us in on the procedure, which blocks sperm from entering semen. It`s outpatient, takes about 10 minutes and has about a two-day recovery. A female tubal ligation is much more invasive and risky.\par \par \uc2 Some men are concerned lawmakers may restrict birth control, too.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS FIGUEROA: I feel, for a lot of men, especially myself, that this is a way where they`re trying to protect their girlfriends, they`re trying to protect their partners.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: These are some decisions not just made by men, but by families.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: Fifty percent of our calls are from women.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: This two-doctor practice can do six vasectomies an hour, and although they are reversible, Dr. Stein stresses not all reversals are successful.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: A lot of the young men say, Doc, I know that vasectomies aren`t to be considered reversible. But right now, an unintended pregnancy would be worse for me than a failed reversal years down the road.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Dr. Stein says the spike in patients is starting to taper off a bit, but they`re still well above the numbers they had before Roe v. Wade was overturned. In fact, they are booked through August -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Just fascinating.\par \par \uc2 Manny Bojorquez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220713ei7c000ma\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Role of Extremists During Capitol Attack; Yosemite Fire Threatens Sequoias; Billion Dollar Disaster; Parkland Father Interrupts Gun Speech; Uvalde Families Demand Action; Controversial Trip to Saudi Arabia; First Lady Criticized for Comments; Japan Holds Funeral for Abe; Vasectomies on the Rise; Olympian Reveals Shocking Past; Sheryl Crow`s Story; Preparing for a Recession; Where the Crawdads Sing; An Immigration Story, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle king, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Nikole Killion,  Anthony Mason, Lilia Luciano, Christina Ruffini, Elizabeth Palmer, Jonathan  Vigliotti, Manuel Bojorquez, Charlie D`Agata, Jill Schlesinger \par \uc2 13,927 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, but we`re still here.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A public hearing today of the January 6 Committee will look at whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  helped mobilize extremist groups at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2  (D-FL): How the President`s tweet activated groups and how the Members of Congress amplified that tweet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are in Yosemite where firefighters are in a battle to save the Giant Sequoias from raging flames.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You`ll look up and you`ll see 200-foot flame lengths.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And there are growing calls for surveillance video from the Uvalde school shooting to be made public.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): The public has a right to know, the people of the Uvalde have a need to know.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: First Lady Jill Biden is facing backlash this morning after a comment she made about the Latinos.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN (First Lady of the United States): As unique as the breakfast tacos here in San Antonio --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: One of Britain`s best known Olympic champions, Mo Farah has revealed he was trafficked to the United Kingdom as a young boy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 NASA\uc2  is unveiling the first image from the James Webb Space Telescope.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The deepest picture of the universe ever taken.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Mustard off to the races, but all of a sudden loses his pants.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The famous Royals Hot Dog Derby took place at Kauffman Stadium in Kansas City, Missouri.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Sometimes its agony of defeat.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Size matters. Kelsey Plum won the MVP award at the WNBA All-Star Game, and now the tiny trophy she received is getting a lot of attention online. Even Plum`s teammates are in on the joke.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The recipient of this MVP Award goes to Kelsey Plum.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP cheering.)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very tiny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does seem a little tiny. It`s supposed to be tiny on purpose?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I guess so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What am I missing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Patience trophy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, they don`t get paid enough money and now their trophy isn`t even like regulation size.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If it`s not one thing, it is another.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And speaking of another thing, we`ve got a lot to share with you today including this -- stick with me -- it`s a look at how one -- some men are responding to the Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. I`m kind of sweating as I even discuss it.\par \par \uc2 A doctor is telling us that men are getting vasectomies in much larger numbers and at younger ages than before, considering one myself, believe it or not, we`re going to talk to some men who have already made the big decisions.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s breaking news.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, your voice seemed to be going up a couple of octaves there, Tony. You okay?\par \par \uc2 And we all know Sheryl Crow`s music, but do you know Sheryl Crow`s story? She`s kept a lot private over the course of her career, but now, she is opening up in a very revealing new documentary and she`s talking to Anthony Mason.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Do you feel underestimated?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW (Singer/songwriter): There have been moments where I felt like I`ve really been not taken seriously, that I`ve been overlooked, that I`ve been an outsider into the cool club, and maybe, that`s why I`m still going.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is certainly a member of the cool club in my book. She`s got a very powerful story to tell. You don`t want to miss what Sheryl has got to say today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love Sheryl Crow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going to begin with the news on Capitol Hill, another public hearing of the January 6 Committee today. This time focused on possible connections between former President Trump and his allies and radical groups that were part of the assault on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned the panel will hear from a rioter who pled guilty to illegally entering the Capitol and the former spokesperson for a self- appointed militia.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is following it all for us once again on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hey, good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 And the Committee hasn`t officially named the witnesses due to security concerns, but it will examine the role of extremist groups like the Proud Boys and Oath Keepers and whether they had any connections to the Trump White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2  (D-FL): They came with explosives. They came armed. They came with maps of the Capitol and with plans that extended beyond just a peaceful protest.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Two sources with knowledge of the hearing tell CBS News, former Oath Keeper spokesman Jason Van Tatenhove will address the House Select Committee investigating the January 6th attack.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: He met with him in March and is expected to discuss his relationship with leader, Stewart Rhodes, who is jailed awaiting trial on seditious conspiracy charges. He has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 JASON VAN TATENHOVE (Former Oath Keepers spokesperson): We`ve got -- we`ve got put all over the place on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Committee will also hear from Stephen Ayres, an Ohio man who attended the Stop the Steal rally and pleaded guilty to disorderly conduct after converging on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 According to Court documents, he suggested "Civil War will ensue" if former President Trump was denied the presidency and cited this December 19th tweet from the former President urging supporters to come to Washington, "Be there. It will be wild."\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2  (D-FL): You`ll see the evidence that we have of how the President`s tweet in the wee hours of December 19th activated the groups and how the Members of Congress amplified that tweet, and the tragic end result was January 6th.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The tweet followed a meeting Trump had with outside advisers to discuss seizing voting machines and appointing a Special Counsel to investigate election fraud.\par \par \uc2 The House panel plans to highlight possible links between some of Trump`s associates like former National Security Adviser, \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  and Roger Stone, and the violent extremists.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from January 6 Committee Exhibit.)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GERMAN (Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent): But to the extent there was any other direct involvement with these groups, it`s really important because the purpose of these hearings we have to remember is for Congress to write laws to prevent this kind of violence from occurring in the future.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Committee members say it is also possible we`ll see new video testimony from former White House Counsel, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , who met with a panel for about eight hours last week. They say, he corroborated accounts of other witnesses, including former White House aide, Cassidy Hutchinson -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And we will of course bring you live coverage of today`s January 6 Committee hearing in a CBS News Special Report that begins at 1:00 PM Eastern.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This morning, firefighters are making some progress in their battle to contain the fire threats of California`s Giant Sequoias.\par \par \uc2 The Washburn Fire in Yosemite National Park is now 22 percent contained, as firefighters work to protect some of the largest and oldest trees in the world. So far, it has burned more than four square miles of historic parkland.\par \par \uc2 Yosemite is partly closed because of the fire and hundreds of people have been evacuated.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The firefight in Yosemite rages on. Time lapse video shows the thick plumes of dark smoke that triggered air quality alerts over the entire region, reaching the Bay Area and as far north as Oregon. Despite flames being attacked from all angles, the Washburn Fire continues to expand.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from AIO Filmz.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are getting as many resources as we possibly can. We have tankers, we have helicopters, we have dozers.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: But the safest way to fight the fire is also the most difficult, on foot. In some cases, firefighters have had to hike more than a mile and ash like this to reach the flames.\par \par \uc2 It`s all part of a multifaceted effort to save these national treasures. The Giant Sequoias are known for being fire resistant, but even they have a limit.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You`ll look up and you`ll see 200-foot flame lengths and that kind of fire is why they are less resistant to survival.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: In the past six years, over 85 percent of the Giant Sequoia habitat in the Sierra Nevada has been hit by wildfire compared to just 25 percent the previous century.\par \par \uc2 While the worst of the Washburn Fire is moving away from the Sequoias, it is now burning in a remote area of the park that`s hard to reach. Fire crews are now re-strategizing.\par \par \uc2 They`re starting small fires known as back burns to try to coax the flames down the hillside.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The crews will go up to the actual uncontrolled fires edge and they`ll start working off of that uncontrolled edge bringing fire down the slope.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And around 500 Giant Sequoias are still threatened this morning. Some are 3,000 years old. Fire crews are now working to establish a perimeter around them. They also put down a water sprinkler system. We reported on this yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Park rangers told me this is the first time they`ve had to use this technique in the entire park`s history -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jonathan, thank you. We all want the trees to survive.\par \par \uc2 The Yosemite region has been in a terrible drought, one of $9 billion weather disasters so far this year. Yikes.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  joins us now with more on that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, always good to see you. But these numbers are scary. What more do we need to know about this?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Well, Gayle, those nine disasters cost nearly $10 billion in damage so far and caused eight deaths. The severe drought conditions in the West are tightening their grasp, prompting harsh water restrictions that actually could become mandatory if California usage isn`t cut enough.\par \par \uc2 Now climate change is fueling the driest period to hit the region in at least 1,200 years. Now Lake Mead, the country`s largest reservoir currently sits at 1,042 feet, the lowest level since the lake was filled in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the levels of Lake Powell dropped another 169 feet, and it will no longer be able to use water generated power for the Glen Canyon Dam.\par \par \uc2 Now, when you add the drought to the eight other severe weather events across the country, you get the fifth highest total on record for disasters between January and June behind the last two years, and those went on to become some of the most destructive in history.\par \par \uc2 Now, the strongest storm to date was a monster 1,300 yard wide EF4 tornado in April that barreled through Pembroke, Georgia with 185 mile an hour winds; just one week later, the most expensive disaster so far this year, a three-day event unleashed destructive winds, damaging hail and tornadoes on more than half a dozen states across the south in mid-April resulting in $2.2 billion in damage.\par \par \uc2 And billion dollar disasters are on the rise. The number of events and the overall costs have both increased by more than four times since the 1980s when \uc2 NOAA\uc2  began keeping track and Gayle, we haven`t even hit the peak of hurricane season yet, which is forecast to be above average.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, something to look forward to, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thanks. See you next time.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now back to Washington and to an event hosted by President Biden to celebrate the passage of new gun reform legislation, an event interrupted by a protester demanding that the President actually do more to stop the epidemic of violence.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): Sit down, you`ll hear what I have to say.\par \par \uc2 Let him talk. Let him talk. Okay.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The man trying to talk there is Manuel Oliver. His son was killed in the 2018 high school shooting in Parkland, Florida. He was escorted out of the event, but the sentiment there is clear.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the families of victims in Uvalde, Texas are still looking for answers as well after the deadly shooting in their community.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano has more now from Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VINCENT SALAZAR (Lost granddaughter, Layla, in Uvalde School shooting): Changed the laws, so our teachers and children go to school safe.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Vincent Salazar`s 11-year-old granddaughter, Layla was among the 19 children and two teachers massacred on May 24th.\par \par \uc2 VINCENT SALAZAR: And I cry every night because she is not here and she is not here because our system from Texas failed us.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Activists including the Chicano Movement Brown Berets, families and friends of the victims walked from Robb Elementary School to the Town Square for the Unheard Voices of Uvalde March and Rally organized by the family of nine-year-old victim, Jacklyn Cazares.\par \par \uc2 FAITH MATA (Tess Mata`s sister): We will not be silent until we know our kids and teachers did not die in vain.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: State newspapers have published screengrabs of surveillance video from inside the school showing officers waiting in the hallways for 77 minutes as the shooter attacked, despite being armed with at least 13 rifles and ballistic shields.\par \par \uc2 The Uvalde Mayor, State lawmakers, and the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  have all insisted the public should have access to that video. But the Uvalde District Attorney is stopping its release citing the ongoing investigation.\par \par \uc2 FAITH MATA: I challenge all of you cowards that were in that class in that hallway to step down.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: That Parkland father who spoke out during President Biden`s bill signing yesterday said, "You cannot be polite with gun violence because gun violence is not polite with you." The President did say that he supports further action including reinstating a Federal Assault Weapons Ban.\par \par \uc2 But I`ll tell you that is a measure that`s far from popular here in Texas, even in this community -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still a lot of people want to see that happen. Thank you very much, Lilia.\par \par \uc2 The President will leave Washington tonight for a long awaited and politically delicate trip you could say to the Middle East. Mr. Biden will meet with Saudi Arabia`s Crown Prince who he has bluntly criticized over the murder of a journalist.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini is at the White House with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Christina, as you know, the President taking a lot of heat for this trip. So, why is he going?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good morning, Gayle. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Now first, the President will leave this evening and start his trip in Israel where he is going to assure leaders there of America`s steadfast support.\par \par \uc2 Then as you mentioned, he is headed to Saudi, a country candidate Biden once said should be treated as a pariah after the 2018 killing and dismembering of "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " journalist, Jamal Khashoggi at the hands of Saudi mercenaries.\par \par \uc2 Now, with oil prices still high due in part due to the war in Ukraine, President Biden will meet with Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , the man US Intelligence says approved that murder.\par \par \uc2 Now in a briefing yesterday, \uc2 CBS\uc2  asked National Security Adviser, Jake Sullivan if this trip means that strategic interests like energy and security are more important than human rights. Sullivan said human rights are also a strategic interest and America can do multiple things at once.\par \par \uc2 Now, as the President heads abroad, there is a new "New York Times" and Siena College poll that shows that nearly two-thirds of Democratic voters surveyed think the party should nominate another candidate in 2024. That includes 94 percent of voters under 30.\par \par \uc2 The poll did show however, Mr. Biden beating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a hypothetical matchup, but it is clear Democratic voters are looking for new leadership.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Christina, let me switch gears here. First Lady Jill Biden is taking some heat after people took offense at comments she made it a Latino Rights event in Texas yesterday. Let`s listen to what she said.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: With the understanding that the diversity of this community as distinct as the Bogota`s of the Bronx, as beautiful as the blossoms of Miami, and as unique as the breakfast tacos here in San Antonio.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, obviously, equating tacos with the Latino community is the issue that many find incredibly offensive. Christina, what is the White House saying about this?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Yes, no comment yet from the White House this morning and those comments come a day before Mexican President \uc2 Lopez Obrador\uc2  is here today to meet with Mr. Biden. So the timing is especially uneasy.\par \par \uc2 You know, look, those remarks from the First Lady came as part of a prepared speech praising the work of a well-known activist, the National \uc2 Association of Hispanic Journalists\uc2  condemned the comments saying in part that "Latinos should not be reduced to a stereotype" -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is exactly right. Christina, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all agree with that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The First Lady`s team probably huddling today.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You think?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because when you know Jill Biden, that`s not who she is, but it didn`t come out correctly.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo this morning, thousands of people lined the streets to say farewell to Japan`s longest serving Prime Minister, that`s \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . The retired leader was assassinated on Friday while making a campaign speech for another candidate.\par \par \uc2 The killing with a homemade gun shocked Japan because firearms are highly controlled and gun violence there is really very rare. Elizabeth Palmer reports from Tokyo on Abe`s funeral.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The hearse carrying Shinzo Abe`s remains took him one last time past the Parliament he had served as a lawmaker, and then his old headquarters, the Prime Minister`s residence were colleagues including Japan`s current Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  waited to say a last goodbye.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Japan`s political elite, family members, and close friends had gathered for a Buddhist service.\par \par \uc2 The funeral held at Tokyo`s Zojoji Temple was a private affair, but that did not stop thousands of people from coming down to get as close as they could to pay their respects.\par \par \uc2 Patiently, they waited to pray and pay tribute to their country`s longest serving Prime Minister.\par \par \uc2 Keiko (sp?) had come with her daughter, Riyoka (sp?).\par \par \uc2 KEIKO (Attended Shinzo Abe`s funeral): He did a lot of things for Japanese people.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Did you want your daughter to be with you because this is an historic moment?\par \par \uc2 KEIKO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: A proud nationalist and conservative with a playful side, \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  was confident on the world stage and a political giant at home.\par \par \uc2 Japan is still reeling from his loss.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: As for the man who killed him, he has told police he did have a grudge against Abe for supposed connections to the Unification Church, better known as the Moonies, who he blames for bankrupting his family -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Liz Palmer for us in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Liz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, an unexpected side effect of the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn abortion rights. Why a growing number of men are choosing to have vasectomies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He winced. He winced.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, okay, pass the tissue. But first it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Sheryl Crow performance.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In a new Showtime documentary, multi-Grammy winner, Sheryl Crow talks about writing that breakout hit song, "All I want to do is have some fun," I love that song.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, she tells Anthony about what keeps her going nearly three decades later.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, long distance runner Mo Farah received the knighthood as one of Britain`s greatest Olympic champions. Now, he is revealing his true identity and how he came to the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 It`s a story you won`t want to miss.\par \par \uc2 Your local news, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got some big news to tell you about the battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration now says that Federal law will take precedent over State law when it comes to providing mothers with abortion services if their lives are at risk.\par \par \uc2 Now yesterday, the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  told hospitals they must provide such services for emergency conditions like ectopic pregnancies and complications of pregnancy losses even in states where abortion is banned with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: At the same time, more men are taking action themselves in the aftermath of Roe v. Wade turning to vasectomies. The number of \uc2 Google\uc2  searches for the word "vasectomy" peaked just one day after the Supreme Court`s ruling last month.\par \par \uc2 And now, doctors are saying that it`s not just searches, more patients are asking for the procedure, IRL -- in real life.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is in Miami Shores with more.\par \par \uc2 Manny, I`m going to be watching this very closely. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: That`s right. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 You know, we also spoke with a doctor who said the way he found out about the Supreme Court`s decision that day is by the sheer number of phone calls coming in to his clinic. He said they tripled their normal volume and the number of men signing up for the procedure tripled, too.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN (Urologist): I just want to get a feel for why you feel so confident that you`re going to be happy with your vasectomy decision indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: From his office near Tampa, urologist Doug Stein calls patients who are under 30 or do not have children to discuss whether they are 100 percent sure, they want a vasectomy, a call he is now making more often.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: We generally see about 12 percent in our practice, men who are childfree, and then we generally say about 12 percent of men under the age of 30, but the number of both young and childfree nearly doubled.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The two-doctor practice perform six vasectomies an hour most days.\par \par \uc2 Some decisions not just made by men, but by families.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: Fifty percent of our calls are from women.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I just need to sign and date it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Twenty-seven-year-old Thomas Figueroa says he and his girlfriend do not want children. So getting a vasectomy was always on his mind.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS FIGUEROA (Vasectomy patient): I`ve always thought about this decision. The Supreme Court did push me to finally do it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The procedure which block sperm from entering semen is outpatient, and takes about 10 minutes with about a two-day recovery, as opposed to a female tubal ligation, which is riskier and much more invasive.\par \par \uc2 For Figueroa, this is also about easing the burden on women who have had their constitutional right to abortion taken away, and for those who feel restrictions on birth control could come next.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS FIGUEROA: I feel for a lot of men, especially for myself that this is a way where they`re trying to protect their girlfriends or try and protect their partners, they`re trying to protect their future partners.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: In Houston, Jordan Castro`s post about getting a vasectomy went viral. He did it last fall after Texas passed a law banning abortion after about six weeks of fertilization.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN CASTRO (Vasectomy patient): Really try it put yourself in a woman`s shoes. I really think I should take that into account and just take a little bit more responsibility.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And while vasectomies are reversible, not all reversals are successful.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: All right, very good. Do you have any pain?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: That`s something Dr. Stein stresses to his growing number of patients.\par \par \uc2 And do you see this as a trend that will continue or just kind of a spike?\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: I think that may be where the spike is over, and we`re tapering down a little bit, but I think we haven`t tapered back down to pre Roe overturned numbers.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: In fact, he says they are booked through August and that`s as far in advance as they`ve ever been booked before -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Manny, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of cringing in the studio.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We all had a reaction to that shot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I didn`t realize that you would be awake while the procedure is actually happening. That`s -- I thought you`d go under the knife even though it`s only under the -- you know, to sleep even though it`s only 10 minutes. But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, Nate has talked about having a vasectomy, so I`m not telling tales out of school. He compared it to cutting off his sprinkler system, which I thought was a really good way to say it and he said that when he went in for the procedure for a consultation, he ended up getting it that same day because it was so simple.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the laws must be different in New Jersey because I`ve been -- I talked to Nate about it. I talked to other friends about it and there is a waiting period in New York. You have to wait 30 days and then have to get it within a six-month window. It`s easier to buy a gun than it is to get a vasectomy in New York State.\par \par \uc2 But my two questions were, going into it, is the fun stuff still fun? And the doctor was like, yes. I can confirm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the fun stuff?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s tender hour.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they said yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they said yes. The fun stuff is still fun.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The rock `n roll.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then, like your inner caveman, does he still feel like he`s there? Like do you still feel a little sense of virility? This is a real thing for guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Nate says, yes. And other friends I`ve talked to have said, yes. So I`m all in. If not were for the waiting period, I would do it today also.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You would?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. If they had like a mobile van outside right now, why don`t we have that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mobile van.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You would? You would do it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Totally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was interesting to see all the men in the studio cringe. Maxi goes, how come he`s not knocked out? I`m surprised that he`s just looking at it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. That was the weird thing because the shot of the camera was like -- he`s like this. He`s like, "Okay, what`s going on down there?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it is not painful -- I guess, it`s not that painful, either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know. I have no idea. I`m in the waiting period.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People are looking at Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ll let you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Klaus? Yes, Klaus.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let me grab this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is interesting that more men are choosing to do this now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a small thing compared to what women do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In the wake of this decision. Yes, indeed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, coming up, four-time Olympic gold medalist, Mo Farah tells an amazing story about his origins in Africa, and how he was brought to London to do housework.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. You will hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of Britain`s greatest Olympic champions is finally telling the truth about how he came to that country. It`s an amazing and heartbreaking story.\par \par \uc2 Mo Farah won four gold medals in distance running at the 2012 and 2016 Games. Queen Elizabeth rewarded him with a knighthood.\par \par \uc2 Now, a documentary from our partners at the \uc2 BBC\uc2  airing tomorrow reveals that Farah was a victim of human trafficking as a child.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in London to tell us more.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 The backstory was already compelling enough. A Somali refugee who became an Olympic hero, but the adversity runs much deeper than that, forced into child labor in the very country he would one day represent on the world stage, carrying that burden until now.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: And last bit of glory strike for Mo Farah striking for home.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It is a secret, Sir Mo Farah has been running from his entire life.\par \par \uc2 MO FARAH (Olympic gold medalist): Most people know me as Mo Farah, but it`s not my name or it`s not the reality.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: In reality, his name is Hussein Abdi Kahin, renamed by a woman who brought him illegally to the UK and forced him to work as a domestic servant at the age of nine.\par \par \uc2 MO FARAH: As a family, we were torn apart. I was separated from my mother and I was bought into the UK illegally under the name of another child, called Mohamed Farah.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Before the bombshell revelation dropped in the \uc2 BBC\uc2  and Red Bull Documentary, Farah said he had moved to the UK with his parents from Somalia. Instead, he had been flown over from the East African country of Djibouti.\par \par \uc2 His real father was killed in the Somali Civil War when Farah was just four years old. He found himself in a London flat forced to look after other children while pretending to be part of the family.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mo Farah documentary.)\par \par \uc2 MO FARAH: I wasn`t treated as part of the family. I was always the kid who did everything. "If you say anything, they will take you away."\par \par \uc2 After a while I`ll just learned not to have that emotion.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Tragically, a familiar and heartbreaking story says anti- child trafficking advocate, Patricia Durr.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA DURR (Anti-child trafficking advocate): You know, it`s a horrible, horrible crime and for children, they are reliant on adults and systems to protect and care for them.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And all they can do is trust.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA DURR: Absolutely, and this crime is based on you know, kind of betrayal.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Exploiting that trust.\par \par \uc2 Farah found somebody he could trust when he was eventually enrolled in school at age 12. He later shared his secret with a PE teacher who found him a Somali foster family, and it was at school that the four-time Olympic gold medalist found sanctuary on the track.\par \par \uc2 Even after earning a knighthood, he says he always feared he would be deported for entering Britain illegally.\par \par \uc2 MO FARAH: That is my photo, but it`s not my name.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And left to wonder what might have been.\par \par \uc2 MO FARAH: I know I have taken someone else`s place, and I do wonder, what is Mohamed doing now?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The documentary "See Sir Mo" reunited with his mother and two brothers in Somaliland. He says he came forward now because he can no longer block out what happened to him. He wanted to come clean to his kids, in his words, "Just wanted to feel normal" -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow, you never know what a person is going through. Charlie D`Agata for us in London.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has got the stories you`ll be talking about today. But first, it is 7:44, exactly. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Vlad, we`ve changed chairs. What have you got?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Dancing in the Moonlight." A little dancing in the moonlight because we`re getting our very first colored look at distant worlds from the James Webb Space Telescope. \uc2 NASA\uc2  called it the deepest image of the universe we have ever seen. Look how stunningly beautiful this is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pretty, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So what you`re looking at is a cluster of stars and galaxies from just a slice of the vast universe. Webb doesn`t just explore galaxy far, far away, it actually looks back in time. Why? Because the image shows light from more than 13 billion years ago.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It takes that long for the light to travel to our eyes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To our eyes where we are here on Planet Earth. So keep in mind, if you`re looking up at the night sky, what you`re seeing in this image represents a spot about the size of a grain of sand.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  plans to release the rest of the telescopes` never before seen images later today. Isn`t this wonderful?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I stare at this and I`m just thinking, this is the vast universe that which we are a part of.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think the best way to understand it is, it is just like baby pictures of the universe, right?\par \par \uc2 So the Big Bang happens and then the universe is a toddler. This is a toddler shot of the universe. That`s how astronomers see this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, very much so. Very, very cool.\par \par \uc2 All right, beachgoers beware. Video from La Jolla, California shows two sea lions -- have you seen this?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Favortown/TikTok.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, apparently chasing people on a crowd -- look at everybody running for their lives. It`s like the blob.\par \par \uc2 An onlooker says a woman tried to get a close up picture of one of the sea lions while it was sleeping.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t wake her up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t wake her up. When she got within four feet or so of the animal, it did wake up and it went after her and everybody started running. No one was hurt. The video has been viewed more than 10 million times.\par \par \uc2 People, you`re supposed to stay at least 50 feet away from sea lions. Let me remind you, they are protected by Federal law.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But if they catch you, Vlad, what are they going to do? They have teeth? Their things seem wobbly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are wild animals. They`ve got paws.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nobody wanted to find out what they were going to do if they - -\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you mean they have paws?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think they have like little like nails at the end of their flippers, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They don`t?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. I mean, I am not a great biologist, but --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They have teeth.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They have teeth, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They have whiskers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I just think that they are wild and maybe they will just like ch-ch-ch-ch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It did look very menacing and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was frightening. They are big animals.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. All right. So, let me explain what`s going on in my last story, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I`m confused.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: McDonald`s fans are saying, "Wait a minute. Wait a minute."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Get that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I get it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: After a chef returned a Happy Meal into so gourmet dish. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANNY KIM (TikToker): Hey chef, can you turn my Happy Meal gourmet?\par \par \uc2 AMY BRANDWEIN (Chef): Yes. I would love to. French fries, chicken McNuggets, sweet and sour sauce, two apple pies. So the first step we`re going to pate a choux milk. Water, salt in there.\par \par \uc2 More butter.\par \par \uc2 All purpose flour. Off the heat. Let just sit for a second.\par \par \uc2 Let`s work with these French fries.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So here`s what happened, TikToker, Danny Kim challenge Chef Amy Brandwein to transform chicken McNuggets, French fries and apple pie into a fancy meal. She made pasta from scratch using the McNuggets and the apple from the apple pie as tortellini filling.\par \par \uc2 She turned the fries into French potato puffs, which she says is her version of pommes dauphine. One person on social media said it was a lot of work for very little food. Others said, my inner Italian is raging and screaming because this is not really Italian food.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I was wondering how she did it? But she did that flour. See, I thought when you said she turned chicken nuggets into its pasta, but she added the flour.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She added flour, cracked some eggs. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She added a lot of other stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Right. Right, right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s pretty cool, I guess.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But people want -- they want their Happy Meal. They don`t want like a pasta dish.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it is creative.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look, if you`re in the mood for it, a Happy Meal itself is gourmet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is delicious.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead, singer/songwriter, Sheryl Crow talks with Anthony about her legendary career and how music got her through tough times, and us, as well.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this hour, "If it makes you happy, it can`t be that bad," or could it? Those are the immortal words of Sheryl Crow who is telling Anthony Mason about her rocky road to happiness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil. With prices at 40-year highs, a recession fear is growing, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  has some advice for you on how to handle all this uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers, "Where the Crawdads Sing" went from bestselling novel to highly anticipated film. We will talk to the movie star Daisy Edgar-Jones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The movie is good, so is she.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 The House Select Committee will hear from a January 6th rioter later today to understand how the tweets from then President Trump incited the violence.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GERMAN: The purpose of these hearings is for Congress to write laws to prevent this kind of violence from occurring in the future.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Firefighters are battling to preserve Yosemite`s Giant Sequoias while a dangerous wildfire continues to burn.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Firefighters have had to hike more than a mile in ash like this to reach the flames.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Sit down, you`ll hear what I have to say.\par \par \uc2 Let him talk. Let him talk.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As President Biden hailed the passing of new gun legislation, protesters demanded more action.\par \par \uc2 FAITH MATA: We will not be silent until we know our kids and teachers did not die in vain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Federal government says all States must perform abortions in emergencies. In the meantime, more men are getting vasectomies after Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 DR. DOUG STEIN: The number of both young and childfree nearly doubled.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A brand new model. I bought it from `72.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And after having lunch with his family, a father spotted a car he once owned, but sold to help raise his kids. That`s when his daughter handed him the keys.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What is this?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh boy. I didn`t expect that to like jump up and bite me, but I`m -- quite moving and beautiful right there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a beautiful moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always say, there`s something about boys and their cars\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know, but to see something that you used to own and wonder, "Wait. I used to have one of those."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is that, and it is yours.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is yours.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re nicely done, children of the dad.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with an iconic singer/songwriter who is now the subject of a very revealing new documentary.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Soak up the Sun.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t that just make you feel good as you`re sitting in your bed or in your kitchen or in your car? Yes, you go Sheryl.\par \par \uc2 That is Sheryl Crow`s hit, "Soak up the Sun" which turned 20 this year. Yowzer.\par \par \uc2 In the documentary on Showtime, which like \uc2 CBS\uc2  is part of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 , she talks about the highs and the lows of her career struggles with mental health and making a home in Nashville and that is where Anthony caught up with her.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: It`s always hard to look back and talk about who you were.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Sheryl Crow was reluctant to tell her story.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: It shows a little bit like I`ve taken off all my clothes and run around town for a couple hours, but --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is that a good feeling?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Not really. I`m sort of like my mom. I think most people look better in their clothes than out of their clothes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "Sheryl," the Showtime documentary directed by Amy Scott is the story of what "\uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 " called Crow`s incredibly underrated career.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel underestimated?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: That`s hard. There have been moments where I`ve felt like I`ve really been not taken seriously, that I`ve been overlooked, that I`ve been an outsider into the cool club, and maybe that`s why I`m still going.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Michael Jackson performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Her first break came in the late 80s performing with Michael Jackson on his Bad tour.\par \par \uc2 Jackson`s manager, the late Frank DiLeo offered to make her a star, but only recently Crow revealed he also sexually harassed her.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: I think it took the #MeToo Movement for me to be able to say "This happened to me."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You went to a very high profile entertainment lawyer.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: I did, who I would love to name, but I won`t.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Who told you what?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: You know, some people will consider themselves lucky to find themselves in this position.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Crow went home to Los Angeles and plunged into a serious depression.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: And then I got myself up.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Which you seem to do again and again.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: I think, yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What is that quality, do you think?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: I don`t know. I mean, I think my way out -- my way through, it was music and expression, and the only way my soul had air to breathe was to find the chords.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In 1993, a poem called "Fun" would inspire her breakout hit.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: And I picked up the poetry book and started just kind of freeform. "All I want to do is have a little fun before I die. There`s a man next to me, out of nowhere." And I just kind of acted out the poem.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All I Wanna Do.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Her debut album, "Tuesday Night Music Club" won her three Grammys. But some of Crow`s co-writers claimed she took too much credit.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: And then to get blamed for things along the way. That was hard.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So she self-produced her second album, and won two more Grammys.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: I was different after the first record. I mean, I look at the artwork on the first one I`m wearing like, no makeup, jean shirt. Nice Southern girl. Second one, I`m like Goth, black on makeup, don`t talk to me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "If it Makes You Happy.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But the pressures of celebrity and sustaining a career would weigh on her mental health. She worried about checking into a hospital.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: I just didn`t want it publicized.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But you ended up going to Mount Sinai.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. But nobody found out. That`s pretty nice actually.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: I have a great manager.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Sheryl`s biggest challenge came in 2006, when six days after breaking off a highly publicized engagement with cyclist, \uc2 Lance Armstrong\uc2 , she was diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 It changed your life in a lot of ways, including the way you looked at your old life.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Oh, yes. I mean, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 When I got diagnosed, I wouldn`t let myself play or write anything over a year.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because why?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Because I think my whole life I`ve made that the go to, you know. It was always that thing that put a Band-Aid on it. Being okay with the feeling of uncertainty was the challenge, but also wound up being the gift.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What made you move to Nashville?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Well, once I got diagnosed, I decided I`ve been in LA for 20 something years. I don`t feel like I have roots.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The year she was diagnosed, she settled in Nashville, where she has built a studio.\par \par \uc2 Got a few guitars.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Got a few guitars. Yes, I`m a little bit, but I guarantee you, we use every single one of them.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s housed above her stable.\par \par \uc2 Who is this in here?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: This is Big Boy. He is a Tennessee Walker.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I mean, I still can`t believe you have a studio upstairs, but --\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Yes. Yes, it`s idyllic, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is here she has raised the two boys she adopted after her cancer was caught in the early stages.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Sheryl Crow family videos.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: With Wyatt now 15, and Levi, who is 12 have not seen the documentary yet?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: I don`t know if they`re ready or not, but I`m not ready for their reaction.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Why aren`t you ready do you think?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: We have a beautiful little cocoon we live in, and I don`t know if I want to bring her into it. Do you know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 You make a point of calling yourself a legacy artist.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Actually Scooter, my manager is the one that said you know, `You`re a legacy artists now." And like, does that just mean like, I just haven`t gone away? You know, I`m just still around.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I mean, I think endurance is a pretty big achievement.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: Actually. No, it is awesome.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Sheryl Crow performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Especially when Sheryl Crow looks out on an audience of thousands all singing her song.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Sheryl Crow performance.)\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW: That`s when you go, "Yes, I am a legacy artists," you know.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Sheryl Crow performance.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, what a legacy it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Sheryl.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, I think, there have been some folks who have tended to discount her importance because of hit songs like "All I Want to Do is Have Some Fun," which sounds a little lightweight, but I can tell you among the musicians, the female musicians I talked to who are in their 30s or 40s, even in their 20s, she is enormously influential.\par \par \uc2 And the deeper you get into her catalogue, the better she gets. You see her in the documentary duet with Michael Jackson, Mick Jagger, Prince, she more than holds her own with all of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love her story though, all that she has been through and yet, she is still standing. I love your line, Anthony, where you said "Endurance is a big achievement."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I think it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think it`s one of the principal achievements.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And she has got hit songs and endurance.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She has got it all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she is still so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And she`s not only just so good, when she talks about, "The only way my soul had air to breathe was to find the chords." Well, we, the listeners, we find that same air to breathe by listening to her music.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Exactly. And she has for so many women particularly, she has meant an enormous amount.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wonder why she decided to tell her story this way at this time because she`s very candid.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She was persuaded.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She went through a lot.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She did -- she said "I don`t -- I don`t like watching it because I don`t like watching myself age." She said, "But I was persuaded."\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s interesting. The Bee Gees documentary got a lot of people to look at the Bee Gees in a new way. I think this will do the same for her. I think people will really respect her even more watching this doc.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a funny way to say about watching yourself age, because she looks great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She looks amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was thinking that too, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everybody see themselves --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where is she? Where?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, she looks -- I mean, in a way, she looks better now than she did with that teased hair singing with Michael, you know?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I think a lot of that was a wig.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: With all the spandex.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She looks great.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She does. She sure does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She looks great. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anthony, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  will share some tips on how you can weather the economic uncertainty right now from high inflation to a possible economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11. Hey, Jill.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Taylor Swift song, "Carolina." She wrote it for the new movie, "Where the Crawdads Sing." It`s coming out on Friday. Ahead, we`re going to ask film star Daisy Edgar-Jones. She`s here about Taylor Swift`s involvement in the movie and why Daisy says this film was really a story of female empowerment.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In today`s "Money Watch," we`re talking about how to prepare for a potential recession with inflation at a 40-year high, many experts warn of a possible economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 In a new poll, 42 percent of Americans say they are struggling to remain where they are financially. That is the highest percentage we`ve seen in the past five years. Only nine percent say their financial situation is improving.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Jill, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So interesting. The Fed has raised interest rates, jobs -- people are -- companies are adding jobs. And yet, we say that we`re heading towards a recession. Are we headed towards a recession?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think the likelihood is really increasing, and it is the combination of those four-decade highs in inflation because then all of a sudden businesses and consumers start shifting their behavior some. They`re going to pull back, and also the higher interest rates chasing inflation by the Fed can often lead to the economy slowing down too much.\par \par \uc2 We`ve talked about this, where we say the Fed goes too fast, they slow things down too much and boom, we`re in a recession. Here`s what is clear, even if we don`t go into this classic definition of a recession, things are going to slow down and we want people to be prepared for that slowdown.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how should people be prepared?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I think that while things are still pretty good and while the job market remains solid, it`s a great time to go back to the basics and you know I have these boring three concepts, Gayle King, I`m looking at you right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t use the word "boring."\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I know. Well, they are. Let`s just say they`re classics and the classics are, we want people to have an emergency reserve fund, and this was vitally important during the pandemic we learned this lesson and people built their savings up enormously during the pandemic. There was not a lot to spend money on, six to 12 months of your living expenses in a safe, boring bank, CD savings, money market account.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, if you`re retired, maybe it`s one to two years of those expenses. So you don`t have to dip in. We want people to pay down credit card debt. In a rising interest rate environment, servicing debt costs more. So, that`s really important.\par \par \uc2 If you`ve got those two things covered, then great. Put some more money into your retirement account. It`s a really good time to get into that habit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I always say, how do you do this when you`re living paycheck to paycheck? I remember back in the day, I would use that to pay the cable bill, to the phone company, pay the phone company, electricity -- because I figured that would buy me some time. They would think, "Well, she`s trying to pay it." And by the time the check comes back to you, then you think okay, now I can do it.\par \par \uc2 But how do you do this Jill, save six to 12 months?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I think it is really a lot easier today, because we can automate our savings. We can actually say to a bank or a financial institution, take five or 10 bucks out, and it`s really easy to do.\par \par \uc2 You start small, you go slowly. And you know what? There are also some other big decisions you can make. You could say, "Hey, I was going to buy something big, and maybe I won`t do that right now."\par \par \uc2 "Hey, I was going to move out of my parents` house, maybe I won`t do that until I get my reserves built up a little bit." I think it`s important for us to try to make these changes while we can.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where are you on the question of whether people should switch jobs or leave their job if they`re not happy at this particular moment?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I am really at this point where I think we have to understand the labor market is shifting. So, we now know the first three months of the year, half a million jobs a month were created, the next three months, it was down to 380,000. That means workers still have an advantage. There`s 11.3 million open jobs.\par \par \uc2 But before you just go for an extra buck an hour, I would be very careful. You may have a great boss, you may have flexibility, you may have an ability to actually upskill yourself with some classes that your boss is going to pay for. I think before you just jump to the next job. Remember when the economy rolls over, what often happens is the last person in is the first one to go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really quickly, for the sophisticated investor, does this represent a buying opportunity in the stock market?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I would say this, it`s a 40-year high and inflation. Prices are up overall. What is the one thing that`s down? Stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 So if you can afford it, then I think it`s a great time to up that contribution into your retirement account.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Afford it is the key thing. Sophisticated, you mean rich.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Or if you can bear the risk.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is what we would say in my old profession.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you can bear the risk.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jill, thank you very much. Always a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, "Talk of the Table." We`ve got a new trend in hygiene we`re going to be talking about. Plus, we`ll show you what some people are using to wash their hair, it is not shampoo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now look who is in our progressive greenroom, actress Daisy Edgar-Jones is here to talk about -- say "hi" Daisy to the viewers.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES (Actress): Hi there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Talking about starring in the new movie "Where The Crawdads Sing" based on the hugely popular novel. You did anything interesting last night? Hold that.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stores that we call "Talk of the Table." I`m just laughing at Patty because I`m distracted which I am. In fact, he is like, "Over here, Gayle. Over here." Okay, I got it. Patty. Thank you because I was very distracted.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, your first "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so my "Talk of the table."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just to tease it a little bit, about the man who composed the signature theme song for Hollywood`s most debonair spy? It`s not me. You know how it goes?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from James Bond.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? The James Bond theme was created by British composer, Monty Norman. Monty Norman died yesterday at the age of 94. He was hired to write songs for "Doctor No."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Doctor No.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was the first James Bond movie released back in 1962. Norman came up with the 007 theme song, it`s the one that goes dun- dun-dun- dun-dun-dun- dun-dun-dun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that one also.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He did all 25. All of them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The music has been used -- the music has been used in all 25 James Bond films.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bond movies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He had a very successful career writing stage musicals for Broadway and the West End. But what I find really remarkable is he -- this song, this guitar line that he composed for the James Bond theme was actually a retread of a song that he had written earlier for V.S. Naipaul`s "A House for Mr. Biswas."\par \par \uc2 And he changed that iconic guitar riff from a sitar to a guitar and he said this, I love this why he composed it this way. He said, "James Bond`s sexiness, his mystery, his ruthlessness, it is all there in those few notes."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So we might not know his name, but we certainly knew his work.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Monty Norman, we do know his work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Thank you, Monty Norman.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So my "Talk of the Table" is an article I read in "The Wall Street Journal" this morning about a hygiene movement called the "No Poo Movement," no shampoo.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wait. I was like.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No shampoo.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How do you do that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They did shorten it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No Poo, but as I said it, I was like going to give you the full name and then I`ll shorten it for you.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, there are thousands, maybe even millions of people who are foregoing traditional shampoo from the store thinking it strips their hair of natural oils, that`s the claim, and instead using things like according to the article, egg yolk to wash their hair.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Apple\uc2  cider vinegar, rice water, chickpea flour.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There are starving people in the world.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they say they get better benefits. You know, some of -- the newspaper did some good work here in tracking down the significant others of some of the people who`ve tried this, and they used words to describe it as "horrendous." The results were "horrific." One boyfriend said "I gagged."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I say just go to your drugstore, get Tracee Ellis Ross. She has great shampoo products. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What have you got, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" it`s about a cowboy who kept his cool you could say when a huge, huge fan approached him right in the middle of his concert. Funny video. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mr. Estes Park.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just think this is so sweet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is like a \uc2 Disney\uc2  movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. That`s Brad Fitch, better known as Cowboy Brad. You see him stayed calm after an elk came up for a closer look at him.\par \par \uc2 He was performing last week in Estes Park, Colorado. Fun fact, for the past 14 -- I just love how this guy is walking -- Cowboy Brad has worked as a Wilderness Ranger in Rocky Mountain National Park. So he is no stranger to elks.\par \par \uc2 He said he knew the elk was just being curious. So, he kept right on singing. The elk eventually just walked away. But Brad said the purpose of my music is to have fun and share it with others. I intend for songs to express the wonder of being alive on this beautiful planet, and that`s how I feel.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way, Estes Park is a wonderland in and of itself. It`s like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve never gone there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s incredible. I had an encounter with an elk there. I was in a vehicle, didn`t hit the vehicle. I was parked at a light and the elk crossed and looked at me and then kept walking.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so they`re part of them. I mean, I love the elk just approached --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very calmly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To hear the music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s like he likes the music. So Cowboy Brad, you did your job.\par \par \uc2 We are very excited about our next guest. It is actress Daisy Edgar-Jones. She is best known for her role as Marianne in the wildly popular TV show "Normal People." Did you see that?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Where the Crawdads Sing.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, she is starring in a highly anticipated highly anticipated new movie called "Where the Crawdads Sing." It is based on the bestselling novel of the same name. This book is huge.\par \par \uc2 Daisy plays the main character, Kya who raised herself in the Marshlands of North Carolina. Kya is charged with murder after a man she had a romantic relationship is found dead.\par \par \uc2 This is all in the trailer, so we`re not giving anything away.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, Kya`s lawyer tries to convince her to testify so the jury sees what a kind and good person she is. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Where the Crawdads Sing.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whoa. Daisy Edgar-Jones -- let`s try that again, Gayle -- joins us in the studio. Good morning to you, movie star, and I say that because last night was the movie premiere. It was your first what was that like for you, Daisy?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: It was crazy. It was crazy. It was a year ago to the day kind of that we finished filming, so to actually be there finding all together as well because most of the crew I only saw half their face because they were wearing masks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: But it was really lovely just to be there. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know what I think is so interesting is that you have -- you`re British, so you had to learn a Southern accent. How difficult was that for you? Did you have certain phrases that -- you worked with the dialect coach. I didn`t know you were British really?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Oh really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I didn`t.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Yes, I worked -- because it would have been a bit strange if Kya had a North London accent. It is really important to try and get that right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were there certain phrases for you?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Yes. I loved "Get out of my lagoon, you low down dirty creep" was one that I really liked because I had to scream that line.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how do you say it with your American accent?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Gosh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s been a while.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: It`s been a while, "Like get out of my lagoon."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, there you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Yes. Sometimes it would go a bit Australian but yes, that one was helpful and North Carolina same way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Kya, your character in the movie based on the book is a kind of genius naturalist in the marsh. Did you have to learn a lot about the marsh yourself, in order to embody her?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Yes. I tried to, you know, in the book that it`s based on, there are a lot of books that are referenced within that. So, I tried to read all of the books that Kya was reading and actually just filming on location was incredible.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`d never been in an environment like it and for Kya, she has such a love and respect to that environment. So, to actually be able to be on location, not be, you know, on a soundstage having to imagine it all was so, so helpful.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Where the Crawdads Sing.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How quickly did you walk back to your trailer after a scene? Because it`s swampy, right?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was wondering about conditions too there?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean this is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The backyard behind.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: It is funny because when you watch the film, it looks so beautiful. But you can also feel the like intense humidity and mosquito bites that were also a part of the experience, so yes, it wasn`t always very comfortable, but it looked really good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So one of the things that`s really fascinating is Taylor Swift surprised everyone when she wrote a song for the film and we were playing it a little bit earlier.\par \par \uc2 It`s called "Carolina" and she posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  as soon as she heard that you were starring in this film and that it was being produced by Reese Witherspoon, she knew that she had to write something. She wanted to be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 What was your reaction to that? Taylor Swift is saying, yes, Daisy Edgar- Jones, you`re in this. I`ve got to compose a song.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Yes, I mean, it`s just -- it is bonkers really is the only way I could describe it. I am -- I didn`t know Taylor had written a song for it. I knew somebody had, but they wouldn`t tell me who, so I found out when the trailer came out as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you found out when we found out.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Yes. I think I saw it like --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wait, you found out when the trailer --\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s remarkable.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: I think I saw the trailer like the day before, so I like fell off my chair and was like, "What?" The whole song is so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is a movie where it`s hard to overstate how big the book that it`s based on really is. Millions and millions and millions of copies. So for people who know the book, and I don`t know the book, I know the movie.\par \par \uc2 What do you -- how faithful is it? What do you say to readers who want to see the film?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: I mean, I think it`s incredibly faithful to the book, you know. I think you`re really left with the same feeling when the film ends that you`re left with when you put the book down, you know, and I think you know, it`s quite -- there is a large amount in this story to cover.\par \par \uc2 You know, there`s a murder mystery. There`s a love story. There`s a survival story. And I think --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Where the Crawdads Sing.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a love story to nature, too.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: It is love letter to nature. And I think -- I think the filmmakers have done just such an excellent job of capturing all of those elements and the heart of Kya, so ...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I got the book when it first came out because there was such buzz and I thought I`m going to read, I`m going to read it. Now I`ve seen the movie. Now, I definitely want to read the book.\par \par \uc2 But this was what Reese said about you: "Daisy Edgar-Jones is a once in a lifetime talent. She can morph herself into many different characters." What do you think when you see that, Daisy? Because most people knew you were British. They knew "Normal People." That was huge.\par \par \uc2 And now you`re going to a next level. What does it feel like for you at this time?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: You know, it is so surreal, like you know, I used to take the tube all the time to my auditions and things like that. Now, there is a poster in my local tube station and my friends keep sending it to me - -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With your face on it?\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: With my face in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s cool.\par \par \uc2 DAISY EDGAR-JONES: It is really special and I just feel very lucky, you know, I love acting and I love -- I love the people I get to work with and meet and I just feel very lucky to be doing it really.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I can`t wait for people to see the movie. You were so good in it and you nailed every scene that you were in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And this is one where it`s worth going to the theater.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a big immersive story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so true, because I had to watch it on my iPad. It is not the same.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I cried at the end, I have to say -- weren`t you crying at the end?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Giving too much away.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, all right. They were happy, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. They were happy, too, just because I like it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Too much away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, I`m done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Terrible ending. Daisy Edgar-Jones. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you and Bravo. Cheering you on.\par \par \uc2 You can see "Where the Crawdads --" what is a the crawdad, guys?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a little shrimp.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Crayfish. A little crayfish.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Where the Crawdads Sing," I thought it was a big bug -- in theater starting on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Right now it is 8:40. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our next guest grew up with a unique perspective on what it means to be an American and he has written a book about it. His name is Rafael Agustin. He moved here from Ecuador to Southern California when he was seven.\par \par \uc2 What he did not know is that his family was carrying a secret, and it wasn`t until he was in high school that Agustin learned the truth that he and his parents were undocumented.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, he was able to get US citizenship and went on to write for the hit show "Jane the \uc2 Virgin\uc2 ," and in his memoir, "Illegally Yours," you didn`t really sign e-mails that way, did you? Agustin documents his American journey and Rafael joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN ("Illegally Yours" author): Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It would be your sense of humor and completely hilarious to sign e-mails that way.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: Don`t think I didn`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The book opens with, I think, an anecdote that illustrates the absurdity, I think of just sort of lines in the sand and rules about who can cross and who can`t. But I actually want to start with the moment you found out your family secret. You were trying to get a driver`s license.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: I was.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pick it up.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: So I mean, essentially the big secret is I grew up undocumented and I didn`t know it. Like my mom tried to shelter me from this harsh reality. So, I grew up watching "Friends" and you know, watching "Saved by the Bell" like everyone else, and I discovered I`m undocumented. I don`t know how to react to it because there`s no episode of "Saved by the Bell" where Zack gets deported.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That doesn`t resolve that one, yes.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: So my all-American life came crashing down on me as I discovered the truth of our legality. My mom sheltered me from that, and I thank her for it because it allowed me to grew up an oblivious all-American kid.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say, why did you then eventually decide to get to the point where you know what? I`m going to write it down.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: Well, honestly, I wanted to tell my parents` journey to this country. I mean, we don`t get to hear these stories in our media and our film and our TV and our books.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because your parents are doctors, both of them.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: My dad is a pediatric surgeon who came to this country to work on a carwash. My mother is an anesthesiologist who came to work at a \uc2 Kmart\uc2  and they struggled really hard.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they thought they were going to come here to become doctors again, right? It was their American Dream. But then they had the harsh reality that the American Dream was not going to be for them, but for their children, and it is a harsh reality that a lot of immigrants in the United States have to come to face with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but I think of you as this little boy, Rafael. You`re watching \uc2 Disney\uc2  cartoons, DC Comics, you wore your Batman costume all around town all the time.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: Everywhere. Everywhere.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At nine years old, there`s a little girl, Erin is your first girlfriend. Tony, there`s that funny part in the book where she leans in, you think, "Is she trying to smell my shirt?" No, she`s trying to give you a kiss.\par \par \uc2 It was funny the way you wrote it. But I mean --\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: But also at that at that age, I`m like, "What does it mean to be someone`s boyfriend? Does that mean I have to have extra chores?" I didn`t know, it`s a little scary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re watching "Saved by the Bell" and you think I want to be Zack.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then later on you see Ricky Martin and you go, "Aha, I want to be that guy." Talk about the difference between the two for you.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: So as a first generation American, I didn`t have a how-to guide, right? I didn`t know how to be American. So, I would watch and was like, okay, "Saved by the Bell," I want to stop living in this ghetto Hispanic barrio and I want West Covina High School where I grew up to be like Bayside High.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: Bayside High, so, then everything Zack did and every episode, I tried it on for size. I mean, he`s going to run for Class President. I`m going to run for Class President. He`s going to join a band. I`m going to join a band.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: But then late 90s come around and the land explosion hit. Ricky Martin takes the stage at the Grammys and it truly changed my life because he redefined sexuality and what it was to be a Latin man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: Unfortunately, that sexiness didn`t trickle down to me. Like I kept walking into like parties with my tight shirts, like, "Hey, how is it going." No one cared.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think a lot of people watching disagree with you in that not particularly loose shirt you`re wearing at the moment. But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You apply -- you apply for the test -- and then the questions that you`re asked. I knew all the questions except for one.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: For the citizenship test?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: So, I go take the citizenship finally. And they asked me all the questions like the 13 colonies, the branches of government, and I`m like answering them as quickly as I can.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: And then it turns out that the guy asking me the questions, the Federal worker, starts trying to stop me. He starts asking me harder questions and harder questions. He brings out another set of questions. And I`m like, "What`s happening?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Less than in 30 seconds. What was the question?\par \par \uc2 He said what?\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: Who was George Washington`s right-hand man? And that really did stop me. I see the painting. I was like, "Who is George Washington`s right hand man?" I finally say, "I`m sorry, I don`t know." And he said, "Oh, my God. I don`t know either. But if you knew that that would have been amazing."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great. Rafael, you say these stories are not being told, but you are now in a position to actually tell these stories and you`ve told it very well.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for being here. The book is "Illegally Yours." It is out right now wherever you buy your books.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you can hear more from Rafael Agustin, our guest today on today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast. I always forget the podcast, but it is there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: It is such an amazing podcast.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, was it? Good conversation?\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: Yes, I loved it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was a good experience.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL AGUSTIN: It was. Fantastic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is an endorsement.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, a story you are not going to want to miss tomorrow. We`ll tell you how a social media post by our David Begnaud led a family to unlock a long buried and very patriotic chapter in its history.\par \par \uc2 David sits down with a 102-year-old World War Two veteran and her granddaughter to get a remarkable story of service to this country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh it is interesting, when you said "her granddaughter" because I just assumed that it was a man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did as well, until I got a little more in the background.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never assume because you don`t mix this out of that out of that.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220712ei7c000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For July 10, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Costa \par \uc2 8,390 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: I`m Robert Costa in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Supporters of abortion rights push for more action from the Biden administration and Congress. And the midterm campaign ramps up.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: My body!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My choice!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Outside the White House, frustration over abortion rights intensifies, amid growing confusion over where and how women can safely get abortions.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s executive order seeking to expand and strengthen access can only go so far. His solution, this November`s elections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): What we`re witnessing wasn`t a constitutional judgment. It was an exercise in raw political power.\par \par \uc2 We need two additional pro-choice senators and a pro-choice House to codify Roe as federal law. Your vote can make that a reality.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: We sat down with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She will outline the administration`s strategy on abortion rights and more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Finish this sentence for me: Americans need to vote for Democrats in November because?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): Our democracy is on the ballot, truly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Do Republicans see it differently? We will talk with a rising star in the GOP, Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin, a businessman who won in an upset last November.\par \par \uc2 We will preview this week`s January 6 hearings with Maryland Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finally, a look at the documentary shot behind the scenes during the final weeks of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): It`s not even a contest, but you still need a judge that has courage. And, so far, we haven`t found that judge.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation. Margaret is out today.\par \par \uc2 Last week, we witnessed a series of shocking events around the world. After refusing to resign for months, British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  finally succumbed to political pressure and quit. In Japan, a country with some of the toughest gun laws in the world, former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  was shot and killed during a campaign event.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, seven people were shot and killed and dozens injured while watching a Fourth of July parade in Highland Park, a suburb of Chicago. So far this year, in the U.S., there have been 327 mass shootings, 12 of them since that shooting a week ago.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  visited Highland Park just after the shooting. We sat down with her in her ceremonial office on Friday. And that`s where our conversation began.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: When you meet with first responders, when you meet with families of these victims, you cannot avoid the reality of what the impact of this gun violence is on a community, on families, on people who -- who love their community, who love their family member, and assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 So let`s just go right to that, assault weapons. Assault weapons were specifically and intentionally designed to kill a lot of human beings quickly. It is a weapon of war. If you`ve ever looked at, if I may be so blunt, an autopsy photograph to see what it does to the human body, and the fact that we can`t get Congress to renew.\par \par \uc2 It`s not like we`re pulling something out of our hat -- we`ve done it before as a nation -- to renew the assault weapons ban is outrageous. And you can support the Second Amendment. I support the Second Amendment. But we should agree we should not have weapons of war on the streets of America.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Why do so many mass shooters, often young men radicalized online, seem to still have access legally to weapons and to slip past red flag laws, like those in Illinois?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Because those weapons are available. And we have to stop allowing those weapons to be available to civilians living in communities of people who have a right to believe that they are not in a war zone.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: President Biden issued an executive order...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: ... on abortion seeking to expand and bolster access.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But so many Democrats in this country remain very angry about the recent Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  said this in May:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GAVIN NEWSOM (D-California): Where is the Democratic Party? Where`s the party? Why aren`t we calling this out? This is a concerted, coordinated effort. And, yes, they`re winning.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Is he right?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I think all of us share a deep sense of outrage that the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  took a constitutional right that was recognized, took it from the women of America.\par \par \uc2 I mean, sit back and think about that for a moment. The highest court in our land just took a constitutional right that was recognized for women to make decisions about their own bodies. And so now we are looking at a situation where the government can tell a person in our country what they can and cannot do with their own body.\par \par \uc2 You don`t have to agree that you want to or would advocate that you or a loved one would have an abortion to agree that the government should not be making that decision for any individual woman.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Did Democrats fail, past Democratic presidents, congressional leaders, to not codify Roe v. Wade over the past five decades?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I do believe that we should have rightly believed, but we certainly believed that certain issues are just settled. Certain issues are just settled.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Clearly were not.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: No, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I do believe that we are living, sadly, in real unsettled times. There`s another example of this point, voting rights. The fundamental principle of our democracy is, everyone should have access, who are entitled, to have access to vote.\par \par \uc2 And now you see states across the country that are intentionally making it more difficult for people to vote. We thought that was settled. We thought that was settled at least by law in the `60s.\par \par \uc2 We look now at the issue of choice. We thought that was settled.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: What will this administration do to try to codify Roe, to try to through Congress put into law some of these priorities?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: What we will do through the executive branch to ensure that women have the ability to travel freely, unencumbered, that women will have access to the medication, but we also need Congress to act, because that branch of government is where we actually codify, which means put into law the rights that, again, we took for granted, but clearly have now been taken from the women of America.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen this in the past, if you think about the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Congress acted. Civil Rights Act, Congress acted, because where there was any question, especially through the courts or any other system, about the sanctity of these rights, we decided as a nation we would put it into law. That`s what we need to do with Roe and the principles behind Roe.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Some senators have suggested that Justice Gorsuch, Justice Kavanaugh misled them during the confirmation hearings on Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats have even called for those justices to be impeached. Do you believe they should be impeached?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I start from the point of experience of having served in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 I never believed them. I didn`t believe them. It`s why I voted against. And...\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So, what now, though?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We need to understand that states are passing laws because of what the Supreme Court has now allowed to happen.\par \par \uc2 So what does this mean? It means that we`re looking at elections coming up in 120-something days.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Right.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: They`re going to be about who serves in Congress, and we need a pro-choice Congress.\par \par \uc2 Again, you don`t have to advocate or believe that this is right for you or your family, but don`t let the government make the decision for her family, whoever she may be.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Right.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: It means state offices, governors, secretaries of state, attorneys general. It means local races. Who`s going to be your DA, who`s going to be your sheriff enforcing laws that are being passed to criminalize medical health providers, and maybe even the women who seek the service?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Basketball star Brittney Griner...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: ... she remains imprisoned in Russia. You and President Biden spoke with Cherelle Griner, her wife.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You reassured her. But what kind of reassurance is possible now? She has pled guilty. Is a prisoner swap on the table?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We take very seriously -- and we`ve been very clear -- Brittany Griner. And there are sadly other Americans who are being unlawfully detained around the world.\par \par \uc2 And it is on our highest priority list to bring those folks home. I can`t talk to you about the details what`s happening behind the scenes, but I can tell you that it is one of our highest priorities to bring these Americans home.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: We`re just months away from the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Finish this sentence for me. Americans need to vote for Democrats in November because?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Our democracy is on the ballot, truly, truly.\par \par \uc2 If you look at an issue like choice, it`s on the ballot, a woman`s right to make decisions about her own body and potentially what can happen in the not-too-distant future around issues like access to and making decisions about contraception or same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Elections matter. The people who are elected, especially to the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , will decide the outcome of all of these issues, voting rights. The ability of us to pass voting rights legislation, the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act, the Freedom to Vote Act, will be a function of who is in Congress.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: When I go around the country as a reporter and I talk to Black Americans, they bring up voting rights. They also bring up police reform.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And they say, two years after George Floyd was killed, you have Jayland Walker in Ohio...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: ... 60 wounds from police officers. And...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Sixty gunshots.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Sixty gunshots.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Gun violence still killing Black Americans from police officers.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Polls, though, show that support for this administration among Black Americans has softened. How will you turn that around?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Black Americans, Americans in general should be demanding, and rightly, that we pass legislation that addresses these issues that you have mentioned.\par \par \uc2 I was an author of the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act. I believe very strongly that we should have accountability in all systems, including in policing.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But they`re frustrated police reform has stalled.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, but, again, there is a connection between what we are -- what we want to have happen and the power of Congress to actually make it happen, when it comes to changing the laws, when it comes to writing and then putting, in effect, laws that create accountability and greater fairness in our system, be it our criminal justice system, be it our health care delivery system, all of that.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this. What we have also seen is that Black Americans and all Americans said -- look at the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . There has never in the history of that court been a Black woman to serve. There now is, because people stood in line in 2020 for hours demanding that, of the things that they would stand in line to see happen, there would be a \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  on the United States -- Justice -- excuse me -- \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, there are demands that were made by voters, saying these are the things we want out of this administration. And a lot of those demands have been met in the first year-and-a-half. There`s still more work to do, without any question.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Up on Capitol Hill, there is speculation among some Democrats and some Republicans that President Biden won`t run for reelection in 2024.\par \par \uc2 What`s your message to those who say that?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Listen to President Biden. He intends to run. And, if he does, I intend to run with him.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: So there you go.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Another run by former President Trump is possible. He is signaling that. Would that make it more likely that the president runs?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: He did it before.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Another issue is immigration.\par \par \uc2 Texas Governor Greg Abbott this week authorized state officials there to arrest migrants and take them to federal border crossing facilities. And he`s blamed the administration for the immigration issue in his state. He`s called it a -- quote -- "invasion."\par \par \uc2 What will the administration do about Governor Abbott`s decision in the migrant issue?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I would suggest that so-called leaders focus on solutions, instead of attacks, if they really are concerned about a problem.\par \par \uc2 And that includes on the issue of immigration, passing a pathway for citizenship, fixing what, in particular under the prior administration, was a badly broken system, so that we can have a humane and appropriate approach to this issue, including, of course, what we will continue to do in terms of prioritizing border security, but understanding we also need to create a pathway.\par \par \uc2 And that`s where I think everyone should be focused, if they`re actually concerned about solutions instead of flamethrowing.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Vice President Harris, thank you very much for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: You`re welcome. Thank you. It`s good to be with you, Bob.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Our full interview with Vice President Harris can be seen on our Web site and on our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: We turn now to the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin is a newcomer to politics who won in an upset last November.\par \par \uc2 We want to welcome Governor Youngkin to the broadcast.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Governor.\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin with the Supreme Court and abortion. You just heard from Vice President Harris. She said, you don`t have to abandon your faith to believe that a woman should have the ability to decide whether she gets an abortion or not.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-Virginia): Well, first of all, good morning, and thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 And the debate on abortion has been raging for a long time. And I believe, as a pro-life governor, that life begins at conception. And my job as a pro-life governor in a state in Virginia, where, 18 months ago, the debate in our General Assembly was around whether abortion should be allowed all the way up through and including birth, funded by taxpayer money.\par \par \uc2 And so this in Virginia is a debate that we are going to run to. The Supreme Court`s decision, I agree with, that this is a decision for states to make by elected officials by the citizens of Virginia\par \par \uc2 And that`s why, right out of the box, I called for a 15-week pain threshold bill to be formed and crafted by a bipartisan group of legislators. I think this is what it`s all about is, this is a moment for our country to have a discussion around this. And each state will decide something different. And I think that`s the -- that`s the real value of the Supreme Court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You`ve said you`ve proposed a 15-week ban. Will you ever pursue a full ban on abortion in Virginia?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, the reality is that, as a pro-life governor in a state like Virginia, where I have a Senate that`s controlled by Democrats and a House that`s controlled by Republicans, we have to find a way to get things done.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that`s what we`ve been able to do, is get things done at a time where you have to bring people together in order to make progress. As I said, I believe life begins at conception. In Virginia, we`ve got to work with a Senate and a House. This is what we`ve been doing.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But will you ever pursue a full ban?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, I believe that what my job is, is to get something done.\par \par \uc2 And I believe we can get a 15-week pain threshold bill done in Virginia for the first time. Think about it. This was a state, again, that just 18 months ago was talking about enabling abortion all the way up through and including birth. And now we`re able to talk about a 15-week pain threshold bill, where a baby feels pain.\par \par \uc2 This is a remarkable moment for us. And it`s an opportunity I`m not going to let go.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You support exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother. Some other Republican governors do not, Republicans. Are they wrong?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Again, I think this is a moment where we have to reflect on our personal beliefs.\par \par \uc2 And as a pro-life governor, I have really reflected on my own faith and my beliefs. And I do believe that there should be exceptions that are made in the case of rape and incest and when the life of the mother is truly at risk.\par \par \uc2 Again, this is a moment, though, where people have to come together. The one thing that`s very much agreed upon in Virginia today is that we want fewer abortions, not more. And I think this is a chance over the next five months for a bipartisan group of legislators to craft a path there and give me a bill that I can sign.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: On education, one of your first actions as governor was to propose the end of teaching of -- quote -- "inherently divisive concepts."\par \par \uc2 What`s an example?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, in Virginia schools -- and we saw Loudoun County literally be ground zero.\par \par \uc2 We saw students being taught that they should view everything through a lens of race and judge one another. And, again, we`re not talking about forgetting our history. And, in fact, one of the things we have to do is teach all of our history, the good and the bad.\par \par \uc2 But we shouldn`t -- we shouldn`t play -- we shouldn`t play privilege bingo with children. We shouldn`t teach children that they should judge one another, and one group is privileged and another group is going to find it hard in life necessarily. And we shouldn`t blame someone and have them form a view that they`re inherently racist because of their race or their sex or their religion.\par \par \uc2 We shouldn`t judge one another. We can do this. And so, right out of the box, we worked in order to remove inherently divisive concepts from curriculum. We absolutely -- we absolutely are pushing to teach all history, the good and the bad. And, again, we can bring people together around this, as opposed to divide them.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s a challenge, though. You say Virginia is willing to teach all history. Suppose your Board of Education comes to you, Governor, and says to you, Governor, the Civil War, we recommend it`s too divisive. We want to call it the war between the states.\par \par \uc2 What would you do?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, I don`t think they`re going to do that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So they could.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: But I don`t believe they will.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we all recognize is that slavery was an absolute -- was an absolute black spot in our history. I mean, it is so, so abhorrent. We have to teach it. And this is not a moment where we`re running away from these topics. And this is why I`m so excited about our education agenda in Virginia. I mean, we stepped into our day-one game plan with a comprehensive education plan to have the highest education budget in the history of Virginia, to fund special ed programs, to fund into laboratory schools to provide choice, to give teachers raises.\par \par \uc2 And we got it all done, all of it. That`s what`s so exciting is that we brought people together around an education agenda which is common-sense- focused. And, again, I believe that there`s certain groups that want to drive people apart. And my job is to pull them together around a vision for education that can deliver for our states.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Security is also an issue in your state. We`ve been talking about the Supreme Court. Some of those justices live in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Do you have confidence the justices who live in Virginia are adequately protected?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Our judges in -- our judges in Virginia are adequately protected. I wish we could do more.\par \par \uc2 And this is why I have called on Attorney General Garland to enforce the federal law, which is so clear. You can`t parade and picket in front of the judge`s home and try to influence them. And I have been -- and I have been consistent in calling on him to please enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 We have substantial state resources positioned right around from the justices` homes. I speak to them frequently to make sure that they feel adequately protected. And I`m encouraged by those conversations. This is not a moment where we can allow anything to happen to our justices. Broadly speaking, this is about democracy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: On the court, the court is moving right now on several different fronts. It could move on same-sex marriage in the coming years. Will you take any steps to codify same-sex marriage in Virginia?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, I believe that what the Supreme Court has done most recently is so consistent with what we know the Constitution stands for, which is returning the rights to states to make these decisions like Roe v. Wade, protecting, in fact, the right of lawmakers to make laws, not an executive branch to pass rules and regulations that overstep boundaries.\par \par \uc2 This is what our Supreme Court has -- has been so focused on.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Yes or no, though, protect same-sex marriage or not?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: In Virginia -- we actually do protect same-sex marriage in Virginia. That`s the law in Virginia And, therefore, as Governor of Virginia, we protect same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 I think what`s happening in -- right now is that there are so many -- there`s so many elements that are trying to pull people apart. And we have a chance to hold them together.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Governor, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 We need to take a break for a moment, but we`ve got a lot more to ask Governor Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: If you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR. We`re available on demand.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ app. And we`re replayed on our CBS News Streaming Network at noon Eastern.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: We will be right back with more from Governor Youngkin, plus January 6 Committee member \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  and more.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We want to continue our conversation with Virginia Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 Governor Youngkin, you were just in Nebraska on Saturday speaking to Republicans across the country. You have also said President Biden was legitimately elected. Former President Trump continues to falsely claim the 2020 election was stolen. Should he stop saying that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, let me begin with my trip to Nebraska, which was really great fun. Governor Rickets had really helped me in my race, and it was a great chance for me to show up and help in the next race to elect a Republican in Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that I`m really focused on is helping Republican governors get elected. You know, I`ve got a big job in Virginia, and it takes the - it takes the vast majority of my time. But I do think there`s spots that I can be helpful. And so I plan on doing that this year.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the other thing we plan on doing this year is helping congressional wins in Virginia. And I think there`s a great opportunity there.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Well, what about former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, I think this is a moment for the Republican Party to recognize exactly what I recognized last year, was this is not a or moment but an and moment. I mean we brought together Virginians last near, forever Trumpers, never Trumpers, independents, Democrats. We won the - we won the Latino vote, the Asian vote, the largest voter turn-out for black Virginians that anybody can remember to vote for Republican. I mean I think this is the future of the Republican Party, which is to recognize that we have to come together in these common sense, kitchen table issues that are on the forefront of everybody`s mind, inflation, schools, safe communities, an economy that isn`t stealing everybody`s hard working money through inflation but gives them a job. This is what the Republicans --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So, stop talking about 2020.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: This is the Republicans who - that we can win.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So, former President Trump should stop talking about it?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: I think -- what I did last year was I focused on 2021. And we had 5,000 people come and volunteer at elections, and that gave everybody confidence in our election process. They showed up. They voted in record numbers for the governor`s race, and we won.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The January 6th committee continues its work this week. Some members of the committee believe the former president has criminal wrongdoing. There have been many revolutions made by this committee. What do you believe you should do in the wake of all of this new evidence, can you continue to support someone like President Trump?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, let me begin with, what happened on January 6th with the - with the invasion of our Capitol was awful. And it`s a blight on our democracy.\par \par \uc2 I have to be honest, though, I talk to thousands of Virginians and the topics they want to talk about are not January 6th. What they want to talk about is run-away inflation, gas prices. They want to talk about crime in their communities. They want to talk about education and the rights of parents to be engaged in their kids` schools.\par \par \uc2 I just fundamentally believe that the media`s attention on this, while - while I think it`s important to the media, is really not what voters are focused on.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Well, but look - but - it`s not just - it`s not just the media. Some Republican governors, like Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  of Maryland, have said President Trump shouldn`t run again. Is that your view?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: President Trump`s going to do what President Trump wants to do. And my job is to have the best Virginia we can have and to demonstrate that Republican agenda, on low taxes and great schools and safe communities and, by the way, a thriving economy that`s working with companies like \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and \uc2 Raytheon\uc2  moving to Virginia, this is the Republican agenda of the future. And this is the one that I think we`re delivering in Virginia and I think this is the one that`s going to really carry a red wave across the nation this year.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So that - so that`s -- that`s your agenda. That`s your focus. You also have political capital. You are a rising star in the Republican Party. Every reporter wants to know how you use that political capital.\par \par \uc2 You have a political action committee that`s busy right now. Will you support and spend money through your PAC to help candidates like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , the election denier who`s running for governor in Pennsylvania on the Republican side?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, I believe what I can do with - with our supporters is, first, get congressional representatives elected in Virginia. First and foremost. I think the road to the majority in our House of Representatives comes through Virginia. And I`m going to spend - I`m going to spend real time there making sure that we win those elections.\par \par \uc2 And then I believe there are governors` elections around the country where I think I can make a difference.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Is Mastriano`s one of them?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Well, I have a huge job in Virginia, and so I`ve got to be real careful in how much I spend time out of Virginia. We`re still working on my agenda. But I think there are states, like Virginia, where people recognize that a Democratic governor has not done a good job and a Republican governor, like the Republican governors all over America, can do a good job. And those are the ones that I`m going to go support. We haven`t figured out where I`m going yet. But, at the end of the day, I`m going to help some Republican governors get elected.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: When you look at a map, Nebraska right next to Iowa, the early voting state in the 2024 presidential election. Are you open, and your family, to considering a run for president in 2024?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: First, I am hugely humbled by this discussion that`s going on.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So donors have come to you.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: And the reality, of course, is, I think it`s based on the fact that I won in a state that was blue and we turned it red. I ran on a platform that we`re delivering. And I often find it shocking that people are surprised that a person runs for office and then goes to work to deliver on what they promised they were going to do. And so I think my job is to be a great governor in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But are you open to considering, along with your family, the idea of a presidential run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: That`s not a decision that we have even begun to undertake. The reality, of course, is I`ve been in - I`ve been governor of Virginia for six months. We`ve got an extraordinary amount done. $4 billion tax cuts. A record budget for education. $400 million in to law enforcement to raise salaries and keep our communities safe. This is where I`m focused.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: I didn`t - I didn`t hear you rule it out. And I hope if you make a decision you come back here and break the news with \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN: Great.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Governor Youngkin, thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President Trump`s top White House lawyer, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , talked with the January 6th committee for eight hours Friday. The committee tells us this morning that they received critical system on nearly every major topic in its investigation, reinforcing key points regarding Donald Trump`s misconduct and providing highly relevant, new information that will play a central role in its upcoming hearings.\par \par \uc2 We`re back with Maryland Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 . He will be a lead questioner in Tuesday`s January 6th committee hearing.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Congressman. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Delighted to be with you, Robert.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: A rare statement from the committee characterizing Cipollone`s testimony from Friday. What will we hear Tuesday? And will it include some of Cipollone?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: Well, we`re going to continue the story of Donald Trump`s attempt to overthrow the 2020 presidential election. And at the last hearings we showed how lots of doors were closing on him, if not all the way at least part of the way when the state legislatures, that didn`t work for him. The Department of Justice mini coup didn`t really work for him. The attempt to get the DOJ to say that the election was corrupt had not come through. The effort to intimidate election officials like Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  in Georgia had not succeeded. But now he was turning his attention to January the 6th. And we are going to get to use a lot of Mr. Cipollone`s testimony to corroborate other things we`ve learned along the way. He was the White House council at the time. He was aware of every major move, I think, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was making to try to overthrow the 2020 election and essentially seize the presidency. And so I considered his testimony valuable (ph).\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So, we`ll see him perhaps on video or part of his testimony excerpted. But who will we have in the hearing room as live witnesses?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: Well, one of the things that people are going to learn is the fundamental importance of a meeting that took place in the White House on December the 18th. And on that day, the group of lawyers, of outside lawyers who have been denominated team crazy by people in and around the White House, came in to try to urge several new courses of action, including the seizure of voting machines around the country.\par \par \uc2 And, so some of the people involved in that were Sydney Powell, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was around for part of that discussion. \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  was around for that. But against this team crazy were an inside group of lawyers who essentially wanted the president at that point to acknowledge that he had lost the election and were far more willing to accept the reality of his defeat at that point.\par \par \uc2 So -- so there will be -- there will be other witnesses coming.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Witnesses who were at the December 18, 2020 meeting? First- hand witnesses?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: No, no. There will be testimony about that, that -- there will be other kinds of evidence submitted about that. But there will be other witnesses and I`m afraid I`m not authorized to disclose who those witnesses are at this point.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Oh, (INAUDIBLE). If anyone`s authorized, it`s you.\par \par \uc2 What about \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , the former White House chief strategist, seems to be signaling he`s willing to come in. Do you buy it? Is it a genuine offer and will you accept it?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: Look, we want everyone`s testimony. We`ve talked to more than a thousand people and, to me, it vindicates the way our system of justice works and the way that our democracy works, and legislative democracy works.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: What about Bannon?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: Well, I understand from reports today he`s had a change of heart. And after watching presumably all of these people come forward, you know, including Cassidy Hutchinson, you know, he`s decided that he wants to come in. And if he wants to come in, I`m certain that the committee would be very interested in hearing from him.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Would it -- would it be closed door with Bannon or do you want to -- because he could go out there and pontificate if it`s a public, live hearing.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: The way that we have treated every single witness is the same, that they come in, they talk to the committee. There is -- if they`re going to take a deposition, they`re sworn under oath. It`s videotaped. It`s recorded. And then we take it from there.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Bannon was at the Willard Hotel in Washington, D.C., on the night of January 5, 2021, the eve of the insurrection. What are we going to learn from your hearing on Tuesday about the Willard Hotel, \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , also present at the Willard that night, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . Is there a real connection between those so-called war rooms, the violence outside, the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers gathering, and what was happening inside the West Wing?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: Well, I think your question practically answers itself. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was, of course, the central figure who set everything into motion. He was the person, Rob, who identified January 6th as the date for the big protest. And he announced that in his tweet in the middle of the night on December 19th after a crazy meeting, one that has been described as a crazy meeting in the entire Trump presidency ended December 18th and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  escorted \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  out the door. It sort of ended at that point.\par \par \uc2 And then just an hour or two later, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sent out the tweet that would be heard around the world. The first time in American history when a president of the United States called a protest against his own government, in fact, to try to stop the counting of Electoral College votes in a presidential election he had lost.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: Absolutely unprecedented. Nothing like that had ever happened before. So, people are going to hear the story of that tweet and then the explosive effects it had in Trump world and specifically among the domestic violence extremist groups, the most dangerous political extremists in the country at that point.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: What about Ginni Thomas, the spouse of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . Her lawyer now says she doesn`t want to appear. Will she ever appear before your committee?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: I mean, again, that`s like with \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , I mean, the vast majority of people, young, middle-aged, old, Cassidy Hutchinson`s a great example, have done their civic duty, have done their legal duty, have done their patriotic duty and have said, I`m going to come forward and tell you everything I know and nothing but the truth and the entire truth.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: What`s your message to her if she`s watching?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: So -- to whom?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Ginni Thomas.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: My message to her is my message to all Americans, which is, if you have relevant information, if you are a material witness in any respect to these events, come forward and tell us what you know.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Can you force her hand?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: Again, you know, I don`t want to enter into questions and negotiations about specific witnesses.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: I mean it`s kind of a violation of our responsibility to the public. But we are telling everybody in the public, come forward and tell us what you know. And, you know, if you get a subpoena, I mean, a subpoena is not an invitation to a valentine`s day party. A subpoena is telling you -- the world subpoena means under penalty of law. You get a subpoena. You come in.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: When you look at your previous comments, you said these hearings would, quote, blow the roof off the house. Major hearing this week on Tuesday led by you in part. Another major hearing on Thursday. Will your statement still stand by Friday, that these hearings will blow the roof off the house?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: Well, not literally, certainly. But I think what I meant is that when you add all of this up together, it is the greatest political offense against the union by a president of the United States in our history. Nothing comes close to it. It -- you know, the --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: A criminal offense.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN: The attempt -- the attempt to overthrow the result of a presidential election through a political coup and the mobilization of an armed, violent mob cannot really be compared to anything else a president has done. It makes the Watergate break-in look like the work of Cub Scouts.\par \par \uc2 So I -- I just hope that we`re telling everything we know in a competent and effective way to the American people because, you know, Madison said that in a democracy, the people have the right to the most awful truth, which is the truth about the nature of government and their rulers. And we need Americans to look very carefully at what happened.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Congressman Raskin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The next January 6th hearing will be on Tuesday, July 12th. And you can watch it right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  on our broadcast and our streaming network.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: During the final weeks of the Trump presidency, including the day the Capitol was attack, British film maker Alex Holder had behind the scenes access to former President Trump and his inner circle as part of a documentary project. The footage his team gathered was subpoenaed by the committee probing the attack and investigators have interviewed Holder as they seek to piece together what happened.\par \par \uc2 Alex Holder`s three-part documentary series "Unprecedented" premiers today on Discovery Plus\par \par \uc2 And he is here with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER, ("Unprecedented" Director): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Your documentary, which I`ve watched, powerful footage of the brutal violence on January 6th. Your documentary also focuses on the Trump family in an intense way. Do they feel any culpability for what happened?\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER: I mean, in my - in my interactions with them, no, not at all. I mean I think President Trump, when I interviewed him in Mar-a-Lago, he doubled down on the position. In fact, he even says that the reason why the protesters went into the Capitol on January 6th was because they had -- believe that the election was stolen. Well, who told them that the election was stolen other than him?\par \par \uc2 And then with respect to his children, they declined to discuss the subject of January 6th. So, I think that their silence will be for others to determine what that really means.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You and your crew captured this amazing moment in January 2021 with Vice President Pence as he has to confront a decision on something most vice presidents never have to make a decision about, whether to invoke the 25th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a look at that moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 ON SCREEN TEXT: Six days after certification.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I join the Senate Democrat leader on calling on the vice president to remove this president by immediately invoking the 25th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 ON SCREEN TEXT: The 25th Amendment gives the vice president, along with a majority of cabinet secretaries, the power to declare the president unfit for office.\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : If the vice president or cabinet do not act, the Congress may be prepared to move forward with impeachment.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Vice President of the United States): Huh, it`s 7:48.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s when I received it. But the House members got it a while back.\par \par \uc2 ON SCREEN TEXT: During our interview, the vice president received an email with the congressional draft resolution demanding that he invoke the 25th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Yes. Excellent. Tell Zach (ph) to print me off a hard copy for the trip home.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Sure. Sure.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Great.\par \par \uc2 I`m always hopeful about America. I always believe that America`s best days are yet to come. And I still believe that.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: What did you witness behind the scenes that day that you may have shared with the January 6th committee that we didn`t see?\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER: Well, I mean, that day was pretty extraordinary. And this is about six days or so after the events of January the 6th. We`re now in the White House in the OEOB (ph). Soi this is the ceremonial room for the vice president. And we saw things in the corridors of desks that was sort of, you know, upended and chairs outside because they were obviously moving out for the new administration. There was this sort of really dower, quite, sort of depressed mood in the building at that time. And some of the aids around the vice president as well were also, you know, talking quietly, whispering. Things were, you know, just -- it was a very uncomfortable situation. And then the vice president walks in, he sits down and this is, I mean, on that day he was probably the most famous man in the world because this was all about the 25th Amendment and whether or not he was actually going to invoke this procedure.\par \par \uc2 So, it was an extraordinary day. And he sits down in the chair, and his aid hands him over his phone. And myself and a lot our director of photography saw that it was from the speaker`s office. And we captured this extraordinary moment. And also the aftermath of it as well.\par \par \uc2 So, it was -- we were walking in history all the way through this entire process of making this documentary. And I hope people, you know, tune in and watch it and see these incredible moments unfold.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You were walking through history. You were with the Trump family again and again. And you cast them as a family that`s also a brand and it`s a brand that cannot accept defeat. Do you believe the former president will run again?\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER: Oh, I don`t know whether or not he`ll run again. I mean I --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But you`ve been up close with him.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER: I mean I think, you know, he tends to not do the same thing twice when he fails. And I think the reason for that, and this is what the series shows, is that it`s all about the brand. It`s all about the word "Trump." And the association of the word "Trump" to failure is something that he can`t accept and his kids can`t accept. So, the idea that it may happen again, that he might fail again is something that would be something that he could not accept. So, maybe he might take the risk, maybe he won`t take the risk, we`ll see. I mean he says we`ll see, so.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You spoke to former President Trump about democracy. What did that conversation reveal to you?\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER: Well, I don`t think he really understands what that really means. I mean when I was sitting in front of him in the White House on, you know, sort of four or five days after his own attorney general has said there`s no evidence to support his claims of election malfeasance and he`s giving me all these different reasons as to why there actually is election issues and how we need to intervene and we need to sort of get brave and courageous judges. I mean, this isn`t a man who really sort of fully understands what it means to be -- what democracy actually really means.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You`ve probably spent more time with \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  than almost anyone else outside of that family. Do you believe they are angling to continue the Trump brand politically and run for office, if not in 2024, at some point in the future? You know them.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER: I mean I wouldn`t be surprised if they do. I mean, Don Jr. certainly came across, at least in my interactions with him, and I think in the series people will see that he does have an interest in politics for sure, and he goes into sort of the background of where his political awakening comes from, which is pretty interesting.\par \par \uc2 I think Eric maybe not as much. And Ivanka we`ll see as well. But I think that they`re - but, ultimately, with these -- with this family and what the series shows is this sort of -- the dynamic, the sort of succession and vibe, the dynamic between the three children and the relationship they have with their father and vice versa.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Alex Holder, thank you so much for coming by. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER: A pleasure. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: That`s it for us today. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget, if you can`t watch us live, be sure to set your DVRs and watch us later.\par \par \uc2 And Margaret will be back next week.\par \par \uc2 For FACE THE NATION, I`m Robert Costa.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020220710ei7a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Signs An Executive Order Aiming To Protect Abortion Access\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And back here in this country, the Biden administration is celebrating a better-than-expected jobs report. The Labor Department says employers added 372,000 new jobs in June. That`s well above a projected 275,000. But the president is battling back critics tonight in his own party who say he hasn`t done enough to protect women`s reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns reports from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : What we`re witnessing wasn`t a constitutional judgment. It was an exercise in raw political power.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voice-over): Under pressure from abortion rights activists and members of his own party to do more in the wake of the Supreme Court ruling, President Biden issued an executive order aiming to expand access to \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved abortion medication, protect online privacy and access to contraception, and provide legal help for patients and providers and the right to travel out of state.\par \par \uc2 But the president suggested his power is limited and urged Americans to head to the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : There is no constitutional right to choose. Only the way -- the only way to fulfill and restore that right for women in this country is by voting. The court now, now practically dares the women of America to go to the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The move comes as eight states have already implemented abortion bans, including Mississippi on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Keep the clinics open!\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Louisiana`s ban went into effect today, despite protests.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Like, there are certain pregnancies that are not viable. Abortion needs to be able to be accessed by everybody who`s able to give birth, regardless of just, like, health care options.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Democratic lawmakers, including Senators \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  and Elizabeth Warren, insisted the president must go further.\par \par \uc2 MINI TIMMARAJU (President, \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 ): We want to see them exhaust every opportunity.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Mini Timmaraju is president of \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What else would you like to see the president do?\par \par \uc2 MINI TIMMARAJU: As more and more states pass bans that become more cruel and more problematic, declaring a national public health emergency would allow us, much like COVID, to have a really coordinated whole-of-government response that this executive order serves to do, but could really be more robust.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, part of this executive order includes protections or aims to include protections for privacy for people searching for reproductive information online.\par \par \uc2 And the administration also says that those living in states with abortion bans should be very careful about using apps that track women`s health -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Caitlin Huey-Burns for us at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220712ei780002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden To Address Nation On Reproduction Rights And Sign Executive Orders\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Jacobson, Christina Ruffini \par \uc2 403 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In Washington this morning, President Biden will deliver a new response to the recent Supreme Court ruling allowing states to outlaw abortion. Many Democrats have criticized the President for not acting more strongly after the Justices overturned the Roe versus Wade decision, which made abortion a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini is at the White House this morning. Christina, what is the President expected to say?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good morning, Dana.\par \par \uc2 In just a few hours, we`re expecting the President to take his first concrete steps towards protecting abortion access. And as you mentioned, this comes amid growing pressure from his own party to do more.\par \par \uc2 Now, the President is expected to announce that he is going to direct \uc2 HHS\uc2  to protect access to medication abortion, and take steps to ensure emergency medical care for pregnant women and those experiencing pregnancy loss.\par \par \uc2 The order will direct agencies to educate medical providers and insurers about how and when they are required to share privileged patient information with authorities.\par \par \uc2 Information, abortion rights advocates fear could be used to prosecute women who`ve had the procedure. The President will also ask the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  to take steps to protect the privacy of those seeking reproductive care online.\par \par \uc2 Now, since the Court`s ruling, at least 10 states have enacted a near total ban on abortion. Within months, the procedure could be illegal or severely restricted in almost half the states.\par \par \uc2 But Dana, President Biden has stressed there is only so much he can do without Congress and has urged lawmakers to codify the rights of Roe into Federal law.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And a programming note, CBS News` Robert Costa will be speaking with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  about abortion rights, gun violence, and the economy. You can see some of that interview on tonight`s "CBS Evening News" and more Sunday on "Face the Nation."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220712ei780002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Former Japan Prime Minister Assassinated; Action on Abortion Rights; Former White House Counsel to Testify; Boris Johnson`s Messy Exit; Remembering James Caan; Push to Vaccinate Kids; Supply Excess Forces Discounts; The Changing Face of America; Singer-Songwriter Chelsea Cutler; The Mysteries of the Great White Shark; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Dana Jacobson, Elizabeth Palmer,  Christina Ruffini, Scott MacFarlane, Vladimir Duthiers, Charlie D`Agata,  Carter Evans, Jamie Yuccas \par \uc2 13,479 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday, July 8th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And I`m Dana Jacobson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off, but we`re in good hands and here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news in Japan: Former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  is assassinated. The suspect under arrest. What we know so far.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): Our thoughts, our prayers are with him, his family, with the people of Japan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Under intense pressure from his own party, President Biden is set to take Executive Action on abortion rights today.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): He`s going to do everything that he has the legal authority to do.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The head of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is calling for a government watchdog to review the tax audits of former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Deputy Director \uc2 Andrew McCabe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Both were frequent targets of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: He was with former President Trump on January 6th, former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  testifies today.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): He represents the presidency, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the man.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Iconic Hollywood actor, James Caan has died. Tributes are pouring in from fans and his fellow actors.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Big news out of Wimbledon, Rafael Nadal withdraws ahead of the semifinal match because of a tear in his abdominal muscle.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: For the first time since taking office, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  presented the Presidential Medal of Freedom to 17 exceptional Americans.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The award is the nation`s highest civilian honor.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): We, the People do what we can to ensure the idea of America.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Padres\uc2  against the \uc2 San Francisco Giants\uc2  with another exciting extra innings finish.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bases loaded for Jorge Alfaro. He has done it before and he does it again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Alfaro hits one that gets to centerfield. Slater going back. That`s the ballgame.\par \par \uc2 Alfaro walks it off again. \uc2 Padres\uc2  win two to one.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Got that long playoff haircut already.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Oh, he`s got some time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the total package, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The rally there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The swing, the hit, the hair.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it. Big Padres fan as well.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got a lot to share with you today including my visit to a city in flux. Allentown, Pennsylvania, it is a lot more diverse than it used to be, and not everyone is comfortable with that. But this is not an Allentown story, this is an America story. Changing demographics across this country are stoking fear and anger and also widening our political divisions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, we remember actor, James Caan, who has died at the age of 82. How collaborators from his most iconic projects, including "The Godfather" are paying tribute to his incredible career.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re going to begin with the breaking news in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  assassinated during a campaign event near Osaka. The incident was caught on camera and as you might imagine, the footage is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 Japan`s public broadcaster, NHK share this video of what happened and in the clip, you get at least two loud bangs, you can hear them, and then the alleged gunman is pinned down by security with Abe mortally wounded on the ground nearby.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has made her way to Tokyo for us.\par \par \uc2 Liz, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 It would be hard to overstate just how much of a shock this is in normally nonviolent Japan. People are really feeling traumatized.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SHINZO ABE speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  was mid speech when suddenly two shots rang out. His security tackled the gunman immediately, but it was too late.\par \par \uc2 The former Prime Minister lay on the ground bleeding with a chest wound. Police say the suspect is a 41-year-old local man Tetsuya Yamagami, who formerly served in Japan`s Armed Forces. Local media report his weapon appear to be a homemade gun.\par \par \uc2 The critically wounded Abe shielded by a blue tarp was medivac`d by helicopter to a local hospital, but officials already knew that he wasn`t breathing and his heart had stopped.\par \par \uc2 In Indonesia, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  expressed support.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 : Our thoughts, our prayers are with him, with his family, with the people of Japan. This is a very, very sad moment.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Japan`s current Prime Minister, \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  visibly shaken told the nation, "We cannot accept that this violent act took place during an election."\par \par \uc2 (FUMIO KISHIDA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: "The foundation of democracy in the strongest terms, I condemn it."\par \par \uc2 Although former Prime Minister Abe was giving a stump speech in a wide open area, security around him wasn`t particularly tight. After all, this is a country where yearly gun deaths typically number in the single digits.\par \par \uc2 Abe was Japan`s longest serving Prime Minister when he left office in 2020 due to ill health. A polarizing figure, he was a right-wing nationalist and conservative and a fierce supporter of Japan`s military.\par \par \uc2 (SHINZO ABE speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: He fought to amend the country`s pacifist constitution in the face of the rising threat from China.\par \par \uc2 While in office, Abe met former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  several times to reaffirm Japan`s military and trade alliances with the United States. His political opinions were controversial, but the country is united in shock and sympathy at the news of his death.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Now as for motive, the police say they don`t really understand it yet. But apparently the gunman has said he did have a grudge against Abe. But surprisingly, it wasn`t political, it was personal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Interesting, Liz, let`s go back to why this happened in the first place. You mentioned it is shocking. It`s so stunning because the country has so little gun violence. I think in your piece, you said single digit deaths by guns every year.\par \par \uc2 Given that fact what`s the response been when it comes to gun policy?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Well, people take huge comfort in the fact that their gun laws are so strict, and there`s a big mental health test. There hasn`t been an attack on a sitting national politician for more than 60 years, and that was by a sword.\par \par \uc2 So this has just come out of the blue, and the fact that the gunman appears to have made the gun and maybe guns at home has people feeling vulnerable and really shaken.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. There is a very new dimension to all this. Elizabeth Palmer for us in Tokyo. Liz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In Washington this morning, President Biden will deliver a new response to the recent Supreme Court ruling allowing states to outlaw abortion. Many Democrats have criticized the President for not acting more strongly after the Justices overturned the Roe versus Wade decision, which made abortion a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini is at the White House this morning. Christina, what is the President expected to say?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good morning, Dana.\par \par \uc2 In just a few hours, we`re expecting the President to take his first concrete steps towards protecting abortion access. And as you mentioned, this comes amid growing pressure from his own party to do more.\par \par \uc2 Now, the President is expected to announce that he is going to direct \uc2 HHS\uc2  to protect access to medication abortion, and take steps to ensure emergency medical care for pregnant women and those experiencing pregnancy loss.\par \par \uc2 The order will direct agencies to educate medical providers and insurers about how and when they are required to share privileged patient information with authorities.\par \par \uc2 Information, abortion rights advocates fear could be used to prosecute women who`ve had the procedure. The President will also ask the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  to take steps to protect the privacy of those seeking reproductive care online.\par \par \uc2 Now, since the Court`s ruling, at least 10 states have enacted a near total ban on abortion. Within months, the procedure could be illegal or severely restricted in almost half the states.\par \par \uc2 But Dana, President Biden has stressed there is only so much he can do without Congress and has urged lawmakers to codify the rights of Roe into Federal law.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And a programming note, CBS News` Robert Costa will be speaking with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  about abortion rights, gun violence, and the economy. You can see some of that interview on tonight`s "CBS Evening News" and more Sunday on "Face the Nation."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The House Select Committee investigating the assault on the Capitol will question a key witness today. \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  who was former President Trump`s White House counsel on January 6th will deliver videotaped testimony behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 Officials say he could tell the Committee more about Trump`s inner circle and what they were doing before, during, and after the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The January 6 Select Committee believes former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  has exclusive insights into what then President Trump did or didn`t do as his supporters attacked the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 How important an interview is this for the Committee?\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): I think it`s important for the Committee. I mean, Mr. Cipollone was in the room. He -- we have testimony from others about what he said and did.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Cipollone appeared informally before the Committee in April, but for weeks there has been pressure for him to testify on the record.\par \par \uc2 Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  used social media to do so and called them out by name.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): Our Committee is certain that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not want Mr. Cipollone to testify here.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Committee then took it to the next level and issued a subpoena just 24 hours after former White House aide, Cassidy Hutchinson`s blockbuster testimony in which she said Cipollone warned of criminal exposure if Trump went to the Capitol January 6th.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON (Former White House aide): Mr. Cipollone said something to the effect of, please make sure we don`t go up to the Capitol, Cassidy. Keep in touch with me. We`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Committee will likely press Cipollone on whether he gave the same advice directly to Trump and whether the then President pushed to go to the Capitol anyway.\par \par \uc2 The panel may have to navigate certain privilege claims, but says there are many questions Cipollone should be able to answer.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): There can be an assertion of attorney-client privilege, but remember he represents the presidency, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the man.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Today`s interview comes four days ahead of the Committee`s next public hearing, which CBS News has learned will focus on the mobilization of the mob, including by far-right groups and domestic violent extremists -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Staying in Washington and turning to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , which is asking for two unusual audits of former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and ex-Deputy Director \uc2 Andrew McCabe\uc2  to be reviewed by the Treasury`s Inspector General.\par \par \uc2 Both officials, frequent targets of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who you will recall fired Comey back in 2017 and it was Trump who appointed the current \uc2 IRS\uc2  Commissioner who was in charge when the two audits were requested.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports that Comey`s audit started in 2019 and McCabe`s in 2021. The newspaper says -- get this -- fewer than 0.01 percent of taxpayers -- that would be, one in 10,000 are randomly selected for intensive audits like Comey and McCabe`s. It`s not clear how the two officials` tax returns were selected to be audited.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IRS\uc2 , for its part says that the Commissioner is not personally involved in those choices, and that the agency does have safeguards to protect against politically motivated audits.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In the UK, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is generating controversy even after agreeing to step down as Prime Minister. Some are calling for him to leave office immediately instead of waiting for a successor to be picked. That race is already well underway with many possible contenders in the Conservative Party.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is following this outside the Prime Minister`s official residence.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Dana.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you could call it the long goodbye, like maybe months long and as you said, there are many that are saying that the Prime Minister should leave immediately.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the race for his replacement is underway.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2  (Outgoing British Prime Minister): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has not left the building. His exit has been just as messy as the end days deciding to hang on and hold out until a new Conservative Party leader has been found has caused chaos.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : And I, today appointed a Cabinet to serve as I will until a new leader is in place.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But as this sun set on the day he stepped down, that decision has driven a wedge between his party and the public alike.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s why we`re shocked really. It was Boris. It`s going to be like it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It is going to be a big old tear up with, you know, all of them trying to get leader become leader. So offering these, I think he should stay for the time being until they sort themselves out.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Are you going to make a bid for the leadership, sir?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Sorting themselves out could take months. Around a dozen potential contenders will be fighting for the crown, campaigning and betting is already underway.\par \par \uc2 British media has even speculated that one reason he won`t let go is a belated big wedding bash at the end of the month with wife, Carrie planned at Chequers, the country estate that comes with the top job.\par \par \uc2 JON SOPEL (Political writer and commentator): If you have a Prime Minister who says, "No, I`m not going to go. I`m staying exactly where I am." We`re in unprecedented territory here. We don`t know, because our politics relies on the fact that the Prime Minister, more broadly speaking played by the rules.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is not playing by any rulebook.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: We may learn more about that rulebook when Johnson shares his plans next week. Many say he should just hand over to the Deputy Prime Minister. Meanwhile, he`s not going anywhere -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Charlie D`Agata in London, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to Hollywood, and the loss of an icon. Actor, James Caan has died at the age of 82. His "Godfather" costar Al Pacino called him daring and brilliant. His friend, director Michael Mann said he had a vitality in the core of his being.\par \par \uc2 Vlad Duthiers has more on Caan`s enormous impact in cinema.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Godfather.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A moment of restraint for the-hot tempered Sonny Corleone, a perfect encapsulation of the impeccable range of movie star James Caan.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Godfather.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: His starring role as the oldest Corleone son made him a made-man in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Godfather.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And while a little too much for morning TV, his brutal death scene remains one of the most shocking in Hollywood history.\par \par \uc2 It was a pinnacle of a career filled with unforgettable performances.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Brian`s Song.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Including his heartbreaking turn as Brian Piccolo a football player dying of cancer in "Brian`s Song." The role earned him his first Emmy nomination in 1971.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Brian`s Song.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And from a jewel thief, to an author taken captive by his number one fan in "Misery."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Misery.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To a curmudgeonly father to Buddy, the Elf.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elf.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Caan never lost his hunger to perform.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CAAN (Actor): I enjoy working. I love to work with good people. I have more fun when I`m working. I have a lot of laughs.\par \par \uc2 And I get respect, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tributes for the movie star poured in following the news of his death. His costar in "Brian`s Song," Billy Dee Williams wrote: "Teammates and friends till the end." And Godfather director, Francis Ford Coppola called him " ... one of the funniest people I`ve ever known who will never be forgotten."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Gambler.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Caan leaves behind five children including his son Scott, an actor, of course, you may know from "Ocean`s 11" and "Hawaii 5- 0." Caan`s family did not release the cause of death and asked for privacy as they mourn his passing.\par \par \uc2 You know, we had that line where he gets up to Michael and says, you know, "Bada-bing." He ad-libbed that line. That was not in the script, when it was first presented to him and of course, it became an iconic line.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Oh, everything from "The Godfather" and all "The Godfather" --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: His statement is iconic.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It`s amazing because those movies, "The Godfather" movies, "Elf," all these films are on so regularly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That you almost forget that he has aged, that he was 82.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: What a legacy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He wrote that you only pray -- that you start in this business, you get to the point where people recognize you and when you saw the outpouring of grief from Rob Reiner, Michael Mann, who you mentioned, Robert De Niro --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Generations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Barbra Streisand -- I mean, it`s incredible. He will be missed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, we throw around the words "icon" and "legend" in sports and entertainment just frivolously, but when you look at his career, he was an icon.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He really was.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He really was a legend. You know, he did everything.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did everything.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Did the full spectrum. I remember him as a coach in the movie, "The Program."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "The Program." Great film.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Man, I want to play for a coach like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right, right, right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes. He was a legend in May and may he rest in peace. I`m going to check out these "Godfather" films.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony, come on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead -- thank you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: ... somewhere is not happy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look, I`ve got a lot of shorter days and all the kid movies.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a new COVID sub variant, may be the most contagious yet, I am sorry to tell you, why the numbers are surging including among children.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up, after months of inflation, shoppers get a welcome surprise. Many retailers are slashing prices because they suddenly have too much to sell. We`re going to show you why.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Rafael Nadal`s decision that shakes up the men`s singles final at Wimbledon this weekend.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And a quick reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on the go podcast. We call that an invitation as well. You`ll get the day`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, though, a look at the future of our changing country and we do it with a visit to Allentown, Pennsylvania where we found that not everyone there is ready for change.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So just to be clear, there are White people in Allentown who think that the problem with this street is all the non-White people who`ve moved in. That`s why the pride has declined. That`s why the condition of the street has declined. Do I have that right?\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK (Raised in Allentown): Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Those people who are out there.\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: Yes. Definitely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Visiting a community there were White Americans are quite suddenly no longer the majority, and find out what it means there and all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Local news next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Friday, July 8th. Time for a check of your 7:30 headlines and we`re going to start with a new Federal prison sentence for Derek Chauvin, the former Minneapolis police officer already behind bars serving more than 22 years on State charges of murder in the case of George Floyd. Well now, after pleading guilty to also violating Floyd`s Civil Rights, a Federal Court has given him 21 years.\par \par \uc2 The two sentences will run at the same time, but Chauvin will now move to a Federal prison, and he`ll likely spend more total time behind bars.\par \par \uc2 In another high profile Court case, \uc2 Sunny Balwani\uc2 , the former President of \uc2 Theranos\uc2  and the ex-boyfriend of founder, \uc2 Elizabeth Holmes\uc2  has been found guilty on a dozen counts of fraud, charges tied to defrauding investors in that company, which had promised to revolutionize blood testing.\par \par \uc2 His lawyers had blamed Holmes for the crimes while lawyers for Holmes had blamed Balwani. In the end, the juries found them both guilty.\par \par \uc2 And finally, Texas Governor Greg Abbott is escalating his feud with the White House over border security. The Republican there has authorized the Texas National Guard and the State`s Department of Public Safety to arrest and return migrants who enter the state from Mexico between ports of entry.\par \par \uc2 Now normally, that`s a Federal job, so it is not clear if this is legal. Also not clear, Dana, when it will take effect as I send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are going to turn our attention now to the pandemic. The omicron variant of the coronavirus is not going away. The BA.5 sub variant now circulating in the US may be the most contagious yet. It accounts for more than half of the cases with infections surging across all age groups.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder is editor-at-large for KFS Kaiser Health News and an infectious disease specialist.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 What do we know as far as the severity of illness, the seriousness of the BA.5 that seems to be more contagious?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER (Infectious Disease Specialist and Epidemiologist): There are early hints that the BA.5 sub variant of omicron may be more virulent. So in other words may cause more severe disease. It`s a little early to say. This is based on animal data.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, if this is spreading widely, if we`re seeing a lot of people infected, even a small percentage of many people means a lot of people in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, that`s what I was going to ask: Hospitalizations? Deaths? Is there more concern now that we`ll see some of those numbers go up as well?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: We are seeing hospitalizations go up. The good news is most of those people are not ending up in the ICU. The percentage ending up in the ICU is a lot lower than in previous waves. But I think it`s really important to hammer home, vaccines work.\par \par \uc2 So the parts of the country, the counties where you have that biggest increase in hospitalizations, these are counties that have lower vaccination rates, somewhere between the 30s and 60 percent fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: About 300,000 children under the age of five have received a vaccine dose for COVID-19. Another 18 million have not. Are you concerned about that pace?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: My colleagues at the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  have done polling on this. About one in five parents of kids under five plan to get their kid vaccinated right away. That includes my three-year-old niece who has an appointment next week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: We`re very excited about that, but then you have another 40 percent or so who want to wait and see it. I think the question is going to be, how long? How long do they want to wait and see? And then you have another 40 percent who are like, no way, not unless there`s a requirement.\par \par \uc2 So I do think there`s work to be done to encourage parents and pediatricians are going to be really central to that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On the timing question, and forgive me if you mentioned this, but for grownups who are thinking about an omicron booster that might be available in the fall, should they get a booster now that`s not omicron specific or should they wait?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Tony, I`ve been getting this question a lot over and over again. I think people are really confused. Do not try to game this out. If you are eligible for a vaccine, so if you are over 50 -- fifty and up -- and anybody who has an immunocompromised condition is eligible for that second booster, so that fourth dose of vaccine. Don`t wait. Getting a booster now will not prevent you from getting the updated vaccine in the fall.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I was going to say, so then there might be another -- I was going to say a fifth, but it depends how many shots you have. You may be looking for one more because as you said, this still is, the vaccines are working the way that they are supposed to.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: They are working the way they are supposed to, but the virus continues to mutate. BA.5, you can think of it as omicron number five. It is really different from the original omicron, the OG. There is a big difference between the two, and so even if you had your vaccinations, even if you`ve had an infection with omicron, your protection against BA.5, omicron number five is just not as good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking down the line, is this going to become the norm? I have friends and family that are asking me, "Nate, are we just going to take this many shots every single?"\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Once like the flu.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When do you -- I have a family member who wants to go to a funeral. It`s actually my mom actually, guys, COVID positive. Her brother died. Now, she can`t go and participate fully in the funeral on Saturday, because -- when are we going to get to a point where people are like, "You know what? It`s like the flu. You can still do big important things in your life that really -- that matter."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I think this is going to take a couple of years. You`re probably looking at, at least another year or two of, you know, more variants emerging trying to match the vaccine to the variant. More boosters especially for older people, immunocompromised people. Eventually this will plateau and settle out. We`re not quite there yet.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Wow. That feels like we`ve been saying a couple of years for a couple of years. But Dr. Gounder, thank you. We appreciate it. It`s not on you. We know that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right, Dana. You`re right.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up: Prices in stores suddenly falling? Yes. That`s thanks to retailers who have too much to sell. Think about that. See how consumers like you can take advantage.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One of the reasons inflation surged this year is that stores didn`t have enough goods to sell because of shipping delays. All of a sudden, those products have arrived and in many areas, they`re being sold at deep discounts.\par \par \uc2 But as Carter Evans shows us, that change may be too late to head off a recession in retail business.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s just racks on racks on racks of clearance.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: It`s a big surprise for shoppers burdened by rising prices.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Is this really sick $6.64, was $21.00?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Deep discounts across the country.\par \par \uc2 BURT FLICKINGER (Retail consultant): It`s a retail Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And retail consultant, Burt Flickinger says that`s good news for shoppers.\par \par \uc2 BURT FLICKINGER: Biggest discounts, consumer, electronics, sporting goods, apparel, clothes, accessories.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Its cargo that was stuck in a traffic jam at sea during the pandemic. Well now, those ships have come in at a time when inflation is forcing consumers to cut back.\par \par \uc2 BURT FLICKINGER: Too many goods in too many stores chasing too few shoppers with too few dollars.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: He estimates some stores are overstocked by more than 30 percent, and there is just no place to put everything. Target recently admitted it needs to right size its inventory and the retailer`s plans include additional markdowns.\par \par \uc2 NORM RANKIN (Merchandising and Wholesale of Bargain Hunt SVP): We`ve got 25,000 of these Adirondack chairs from a national chain.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But the extra goods often end up with liquidators like Bargain Hunt which discounts up to 70 percent. But this year, merchandising VP Norm Rankin is seeing something different.\par \par \uc2 NORM RANKIN: The condition of the product, it`s never left the case, it didn`t make it to the stores. It`s not dog eared or wrinkled or ruffled having been on a shelf.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And goods from last year are still coming.\par \par \uc2 NORM RANKIN: We have been in negotiation with some large retailers on Christmas goods, almost $30 million with a new Christmas product that didn`t make it in time.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: When do you think we`ll start seeing the deepest discounts?\par \par \uc2 BURT FLICKINGER: Deepest discounts will be between Labor Day and Columbus Day and then very big discounts right after Halloween.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And when it comes to returns, especially larger items.\par \par \uc2 BURT FLICKINGER: The stores can`t take it.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Is that a hint for consumers here, if you take something back, you`re likely going to get the money back and get to keep it.\par \par \uc2 BURT FLICKINGER: A very good chance.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For CBS MORNINGS, Carter Evans, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Bargain shoppers out there, take note.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories you will be talking about today, including Paul Rudd`s good deed for a boy who needed a friend.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:42, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." And we welcome our viewers in to a plenty of time "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s scary. That`s dangerous.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. It is.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Big mistake.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Run with it, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I always tell our senior broadcast producer, Jon Tower. Oh, I`m going to use this time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re using it right now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am using it right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I see you`re massaging it right now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. He is like, all right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Foreplay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, we`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s get into it.\par \par \uc2 It was a very special day for an athlete with an Olympic sized impact on her country.\par \par \uc2 Simone Biles --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Medal of Freedom ceremony.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is of course, Simone Biles receiving the Medal of Freedom yesterday from President Biden. The most decorated gymnast of all time added a new record to her resume, at 25, she is the youngest ever to receive the nation`s highest civ