.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, the man with the most Grand Slam tennis titles will not add to his collection at Wimbledon this weekend. Rafael Nadal bowed out of today`s semifinals because of a torn abdominal muscle. That sounds really painful.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, it does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is it painful?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is painful.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: He is playing with it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was going to say -- yes, how do you -- okay, abdominal muscle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve never tore yours in the mini sports that you have played?\par \par \uc2 Whoa. Why did everybody laugh so loud?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was touching the --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yee.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Abdominals.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. Oh man, all those sit ups, I guess, they`re not shown. Never tore a muscle doing those.\par \par \uc2 The withdrawal though came a -- Nate is curious -- an automatic trip to Sunday`s final. Nadal is not happy about injury.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you thinking about your --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am just thinking about your thinking about everybody laughing at me.\par \par \uc2 Injury forcing him out of a Grand Slam for the second time in his career, but he says he had no choice.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL NADAL (Professional tennis player): If I keep going, the injury is going to I`m going to be worse and worse and that`s the thing that I can say now and I feel very sad to say that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, Nadal is out and people are upset.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He was playing very well, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, I was -- there is a historic first, Ons Jabeur is the first African -- the first Arab Muslim to reach a Grand Slam final.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I saw that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s kind of history in the making. I know we talked about the big stars, and she is a star. She`s from Tunisia, but I just wanted to give a little shout out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I saw that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is the first time that`s ever happened.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am pulling for her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes. It`s going to be good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the matchup with Djokovic, against who? Who is the newcomer?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: He is not a newcomer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve heard it described as --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: He`s been around.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Both are controversial figures.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did hear the fans.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The last time they played each other, they were like smacking balls at each other and they got, you know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got real intense.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Got very intense.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know, so if they make them it --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Djokovic has never beaten him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going to love this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It should be good if they both are the ones facing each other.\par \par \uc2 All right, so let`s talk about kindness. You never know where it`s going to come from. Not the studio.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Wow. Ouch.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You should be ashamed of yourselves.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: By the way this is what happens when you`re told you have plenty of time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. Exactly. All right, so let me get -- this is a really sweet story. I really love this story.\par \par \uc2 There is one Colorado family. Kindness did come in the form of a Marvel superhero.\par \par \uc2 You may remember this heartbreaking photo from earlier this year. Cassandra Ridder posted it on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  saying that her son, Brody, was in tears because no one had signed his yearbook. On the mostly blank page, Brody had written to himself. "Hope you make some more friends."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is an absolute worst. I mean, like they don`t know how to be people. They need to be able to be taught everything.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the photo made the rounds on social media and a special someone took notice. That special someone is Paul Rudd.\par \par \uc2 The Ant-Man actor apparently saw Ridder`s post and decided to reach out. Brody`s mom says she received the letter from Rudd and posted it on social media along with a text exchange between Paul Rudd and Brody. "You`re my favorite superhero," Brody wrote. His mom says Rudd responded, "Your mine."\par \par \uc2 The star also sent a signed Ant-Man hat.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, a signed Ant-Man helmet for his good friend, Brody for when he takes on the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Paul Rudd is the man.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We really like that. Be more like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Be more like Paul Rudd.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Paul Rudd is the man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And he actually even did a little video with him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a hug out.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`ll get out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Television hug.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. White shark coming up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re going to miss your tease.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS) **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: How fear of change is dividing our country. We`re going to take you to a community where not everyone welcomes the newcomers.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`m Dana Jacobson. Singer-songwriter Chelsea Cutler is making her voice heard on songs like "Crazier Things." Jamie Yuccas got a backstage pass to hear her remarkable story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Solving the mysteries of the great white shark. We`ll talk to the leader of an upcoming expedition that CBS News will be joining.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not me. I`ll be staying on the boat.\par \par \uc2 First, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 (SHINZO ABE speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Former Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  is assassinated by a gunman while speaking at a campaign event.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Apparently the gunman has said he did have a grudge against Abe, but surprisingly it wasn`t political. It was personal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  faces growing calls to step down immediately. He says he is waiting until a successor is named.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He is just prolonging the agony. Really, at the thought of that. He has to go and he has to go now.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: COVID infections are rising again due to a highly contagious omicron some variant called BA.5.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: The parts of the country, the counties where you have that biggest increase in hospitalizations, these are counties that have lower vaccination rates.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tributes pouring in for actor, James Caan, known for iconic roles in films like "Misery" and "The Godfather."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Godfather.")\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A nice touch right smack goes with the right foot. What a beauty of a girl. Banding it with the outside of her right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And US Women Soccer beats Jamaica in a blowout to qualify for their ninth consecutive World Cup.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Smith, now Hatch. Actually Hatch makes it three.\par \par \uc2 For Team US slams it home.\par \par \uc2 Trinity Rodman. Trinity Rodman slams home, the fifth goal. A massive step for the United States.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How many times can we say "wow" watching that?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I know. We are a little spoiled because of that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: A little bit spoiled.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m very excited about the World Cup.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s going to be great.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It is always great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You and me, both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we`ve got a tough turn to make here off the top.\par \par \uc2 While the House Select Committee investigating the January 6 riot wants to know who organized the attack with particular focus on former President Trump. There are others who are focused on these still simmering question of who participated in it and why they participated?\par \par \uc2 Were they motivated only by the false idea of a stolen election? Or was there something deeper, something that won`t pass as quickly as a single campaign cycle?\par \par \uc2 Well, researchers at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  have been looking into these questions and they find evidence of a conflict here in America, a conflict smoldering underneath all the other conflicts and it begins with a single fact.\par \par \uc2 More than 90 percent of the people arrested for the attack on the Capitol were White. And the key characteristic, according to the researchers, uniting these attackers was not ideology or geography, but in fact, race: "They come from counties where the White share of the population is declining fastest."\par \par \uc2 Well, earlier this year, you`ll notice my winter coat in these pictures, we visited a place that has already experienced a sudden demographic change. It`s a city in Pennsylvania, where White Americans accustomed to being an overwhelming majority are quite suddenly not.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We arrive in Allentown, Pennsylvania one day last winter, and to us, absolutely nothing seems out of the ordinary.\par \par \uc2 But talk to Ed Frack and he`ll tell you that`s because we haven`t lived through the changes here.\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: My mother grew up a couple of blocks away Six and Liberty area and it just -- back then, you could walk around. You could leave your doors unlocked.\par \par \uc2 GARY IACOCCA (Allentown resident): As a kid, I mean Allentown was amazing because we --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ed`s friend, Gary Iacocca also remembers that older Allentown and the nonstop crowds that used to shuffle through the hotdog shop his grandfather opened after coming here from Italy.\par \par \uc2 GARY IACOCCA: I will tell you the original Yocco`s was open at 6:00 AM in the morning, and we closed at 1:30 at night and we were busy all day. Well, this was a blue-collar town. Third shifts, they would get off, they`d come down to Yocco`s. They`d get a couple of hot dogs, drink a beer, and go home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And while that family business continues in other locations, the original Yocco`s is gone, closed after 82 years.\par \par \uc2 GARY IACOCCA: The problem is the neighborhood began to change. As other little restaurants opened up, you know Hispanic restaurants, soul food restaurants, Asian restaurants that became a little more competitive.\par \par \uc2 I remember clearing that out. It was heartbreaking because you know, I grew up. Sad, very sad.\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: I`m kind of upset on how some of the stuff has gone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As for Ed Frack, by the time his mother passed away in 2020 at 90 years old, he says it had been at least a decade since she felt at home.\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: It was just a different kind of culture. People talked, people said "\uc2 Hello\uc2 ," shook your hand. And now it`s you know, a little bit different.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Frack says he often doesn`t see the same level of pride or consideration.\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: That`s Allentown.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s changed is not only the culture or economy, but the people.\par \par \uc2 In the 1970s, Allentown was 97 percent White. By 2020 though, the White share of the population had plunged to 31 percent as Hispanic residents became what the local paper calls "a strong majority."\par \par \uc2 MAN: Men and women and children who come --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And just as it took years for previous waves of immigrants to be accepted into their communities, there is a sense of tension here.\par \par \uc2 So just to be clear, there are White people in Allentown who think that the problem with this street is all of non-White people who`ve moved in? That`s why the pride has declined. That`s why the condition of the street has declined. Do I have that right?\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Those people are out there.\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: Yes. Definitely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of the more interesting theories about the riot in the Capitol on January 6th, is that the people who went, the biggest predictor of whether they would go was whether their county had the loss of the White population relative to minorities. Does that surprise you?\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: No, not really.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why not?\par \par \uc2 ED FRACK: Well, we had some folks right from here go. After our conversation right now, you know, yes, it`s -- I could see that more so than what I would have thought before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Most of America, not just Allentown is changing, at least demographically according to Census data. The number of White Americans dropped last decade across 35 states, and in three out of four counties.\par \par \uc2 In headlines you may have seen America will no longer have a white majority by 2044.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE NORRIS (The Race Card Project founding director): We`re in an explosive moment in America right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Michele Norris has been covering this long moment in America for more than a decade, reporting in "National Geographic" back in 2018 that some White Americans react anxiously and angrily to a sense that their way of life is under threat.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE NORRIS: People feel like something is being snatched from them sometimes, and even though it`s not something that is tangible, community pride, a sense of belonging, a sense that this is my town and we built it. And now, it doesn`t feel like it`s mine anymore.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s not the one that they grew up in.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE NORRIS: And the irony is, for many of the immigrant families that came there, people were saying the same thing about them when they arrived.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So that idea of something being snatched from them, it makes me think of former President Donald Trump`s campaign slogan, "Make America great again." It`s nostalgic.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE NORRIS: Well, nostalgia can be sweet, and sometimes nostalgia can be toxic.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "You will not replace us.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The concern is that toxicity can sometimes tip into outright violence.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Something another Allentown family, the Minaya`s worries about inside their corner grocery store.\par \par \uc2 EMELY MINAYA (Allentown resident): Thankfully nothing has happened, but you can never put it past anyone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Emely Minaya came from the Dominican Republic as a child and she says she`s got a theory about what`s driving all this tension.\par \par \uc2 EMELY MINAYA: White has always been equal to superior, on top. And so when you have such a long time coming with that lifestyle with that mentality, all of that like that notion to just see it, like slip out of your fingers in like just a few years or whatever. It`s like, wait a minute.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you think Allentown is an example of the future of America?\par \par \uc2 EMELY MINAYA: One hundred percent. Even though States where there`s probably no Hispanics at all, no Black people, they will be seeing a lot of that in the next few years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So for someone who hears that sentence, and thinks, "Uh- oh."\par \par \uc2 EMELY MINAYA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Even a little bit. You say what?\par \par \uc2 EMELY MINAYA: I don`t know. It wouldn`t be very nice to say it on TV or whatever. Suck it up. And it`s going to happen. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So while things may feel different for those living in Allentown, our first impression was the right one. Nothing about this is out of the ordinary.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE NORRIS: The country is changing and in some ways, people are afraid of change that has already arrived. It`s already here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How bumpy of a ride are we in for?\par \par \uc2 MICHELE NORRIS: It is very bumpy. I mean, put your seatbelt on. It`s going to be a very, very, very bumpy ride.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Michele Norris is one of the best in the country when it comes to talking about these issues and explaining them on the subject of putting your seatbelt on.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the reason we went to Pennsylvania is because it sent the second highest number of rioters, they hailed from there, on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh wow. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And also almost every county has seen a decline in the White population there. But again, this is really a national story. Most Americans, 80 percent about live in a county where the White population has declined. And for most people, that`s fine, or it`s welcome. It`s even something to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a portion that the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  has identified for whom it`s very dangerous. And one more note, I just want to shout out Ed Frack and Gary Iacocca, I appreciate them talking with me. They welcome the new residents, but they`re in touch with this part of town and part of their State that maybe is less welcoming and not comfortable with the change.\par \par \uc2 They say these new folks are no different from the Germans and the Italians who weren`t exactly welcome when they came.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not so long ago.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and since the "discovery of America," this country has been built from change. So regardless of race, religion, socioeconomic class, that change around you looks like it`s up to us to adjust.\par \par \uc2 We all are in pursuit of this American Dream, right? A roof over our head, clothes on our back, food on the table, and we want our kids to be raised in a harmonious society. We have to accept what change is.\par \par \uc2 I once heard something, I`ll leave everybody with this: Change is inevitable. Growth is optional. And it`s up to us to grow with change. You don`t have to like love, but coexisting, that`s something we all can do.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Blame somebody else for your problems, and that`s where the trouble begins.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s not going to be easy.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It is not somebody else`s fault.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great piece, Tony.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, guys. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right, you can hear more of my conversation with journalist, Michele Norris on today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast. You`ll learn what led her to create something very interesting. It is called "The Race Card Project."\par \par \uc2 It invites people to distill their thoughts about race their experiences, their observations into a sentence of just six words. In most years, most of the people responding are White people, interestingly enough.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, OCEARCH has been tagging and tracking sharks for years. We`ll talk with its founder about why he calls the next expedition the final part of the white shark puzzle.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:12 time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Ahead, our candid conversation with singer-songwriter, Chelsea Cutler. She tells us about the personal messages behind her music.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re learning about a new expedition to study great white sharks, the ocean research group, OCEARCH say there`ll be going to a new region to study sharks. And we can reveal that CBS MORNINGS will be joining them later this month off the coast of Nova Scotia.\par \par \uc2 Dana, Tony, I`m signing you up.\par \par \uc2 Their goal is to find and tag 100 great white sharks. They`ve already tagged 84. This helps researchers understand shark behavior and health. OCEARCH founder and expedition leader, Chris Fischer joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Chris. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FISCHER (OCEARCH founder and expedition leader): Good morning. How are you? Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. So why do you call this expedition the last part of the white shark puzzle?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FISCHER: Well, there are eight white shark puzzles around the world and we are solving all those puzzles because as the white shark goes, the ocean goes.\par \par \uc2 And so we are in the middle of wrapping up solving the puzzle of the white sharks that live off the East Coast of the United States, as well as Atlantic Canada and we are just about to wrap that project up identifying for migration, birthing, and mating, and so forth so we can manage the white shark.\par \par \uc2 So we`re wrapping up off the East Coast in the western North Atlantic, and the next puzzle to solve is over in the Mediterranean starting next year.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So what are you learning about them right now as you`re tracking about where they are, that is going to help us in some way.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FISCHER: Well, what we`re learning about what`s going on this time of year is the sharks have just endured a long winter. They are skinny. They`ve been off the Southeastern United States in the Gulf of Mexico and they`re migrating north to the Northeastern United States and Atlantic Canada to forage on seals, to bulk back up like many animals do, so they can endure the oncoming winter.\par \par \uc2 So everything is moving north right now. We have animals strung out from around the Carolinas all the way through and up to Newfoundland, far north of the Northeastern United States. And in the next few weeks or so, they`ll all be up north focusing on putting pressure on those seals, so the seals don`t wipe out our fish stocks and bulking up for the oncoming winter.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And OCEARCH was part of a study that found that sharks are -- they`re staying closer to shore when it comes to being near big cities. Do we know why and what does that mean for people that are getting in the water potentially?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FISCHER: Well, it`s not just the big cities. You know, there was a big misconception about white sharks. For many years that they were these open ocean wandering animals. When we started our work about 15 years ago and began putting more modern tagging technology on those animals, we began to see that the white sharks are actually very coastal.\par \par \uc2 They`re really moving from event to event, which means if there`s a big bait run in front of a river, an estuary, they will be up and in that. If there`s a fish run, they`ll be on it. They`re obviously on the seals when they`re up north.\par \par \uc2 So before, it was thought they were more of a pelagic animal, an offshore animal, but that`s just not the case. They`re super coastal. We`ve all been swimming with them all our lives. Nothing`s changed. We just know now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, one of the realities of going into the ocean is that you`re stepping from the habitat that we all inhabit, the land.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk about it Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To like what is the habitat of the sharks. We`re on their turf at that point, and shark attacks were very rare. They do happen. They happen where I go in the summer in Cape Cod. They happened off the coast of Long Island recently in California and Hawaii.\par \par \uc2 What can people do who want to swim, maybe want to learn to surf and still feel safe in the water?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FISCHER: I think the biggest thing is, you know, when you head down to the beach and you`re thinking about safety in regard to sharks, because there`s a lot of other safety issues with drowning and rip currents and everything else.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re focused specifically on sharks, you know, you want to approach the ocean the same way you`d approach going on a hike in the forest. You want to look at it first. You want to understand the environment that you`re going to be playing in with your family, so that you can be personally accountable for your safety and your family`s safety.\par \par \uc2 If you see things like birds crashing on the water, bait moving around, game fish crashing on that bait, then the sharks are going to be on that, too, and you don`t want to swim out into the middle of the food chain. You want to go find a quieter section of beach.\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest thing that`s really going on right now is that we`ve had this incredible return to abundance of both our East and West Coasts. We are winning.\par \par \uc2 Our fish are coming back, our whales, our turtles, our seals, and our sharks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FISCHER: There`s more life in the ocean now than has been seen since the 40s or 50s.\par \par \uc2 All of us grew up with a compromised ocean. We grew up with an ocean that was heavily overfished.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chris, we`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got to get going here. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FISCHER: Oh, okay. Be safe. Enjoy the ocean.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate the notes and nuggets. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll be right back. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hands up and touch the sky.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that are at the "Talk of the Table" this morning. And Dana, we`re starting off with you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`m starting with animals again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I have an animal theme going.\par \par \uc2 This is about a dog reunited with his family more than eight years after he vanished. This is a crazy story.\par \par \uc2 Harley disappeared into the woods by his home near Fort Myers, Florida in 2014. The family searched frantically. There were posters everywhere, but they couldn`t find him.\par \par \uc2 Fast forward to now. Two women found the dog wandering around a park, they took him to Animal Services. They found his microchip. Get your microchip, and they were able to contact Harley`s family.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, very, very skinny. Now, the family had moved to Missouri, but his owner, this woman drove 1,100 miles -- I get this -- to be reunited with her dog, healthy as we`ve said, little skinny and that reaction was everything when you watch that video.\par \par \uc2 If you have a dog, you know, he knew exactly who she was. This was so beautiful. And I really mean it.\par \par \uc2 If you have a pet, get your dog -- if you have a dog, get the microchip, because that`s how people they know where to look. There`s certain spots for it. It may have taken eight years. We don`t know where Harley was, but man, what an amazing reunion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. It always blows me away about the connection between human and animal.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: After so much time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Eight years. After so much time.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Eight years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The distance.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes. It was crazy. All right, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was emotional. I like that. That was good.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You`re up. Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My "Talk of the Table" is an apparent proof that music can be a pain reliever. Researchers studied mice by measuring their tolerance for pain when music or sounds were playing. The effect was remarkable.\par \par \uc2 All the sounds led to significantly lower pain levels for the mice. The mice reacted equally to classical music, dissonant music, and white noise when each was played five decibels louder than ambient noise.\par \par \uc2 Scientists hope that this will lead to alternative low cost methods for treating pain and I love this is because we all love music here. But music is a part of everyday for me. You know, I wake up to it. I drive to it. Even at night, it is playing in the background and it does just make me feel good.\par \par \uc2 And now, it`s more than just making people feel good. I think research shows it can lower pain levels when you have issues.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`ll take it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Okay. It`s an alternative remedy. I don`t know if my doctor would prescribe music if I feel like, I`m going to get a second opinion.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`ll still pop an Advil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My legs broken. I need a cast.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am going to need more.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It helps. It helps in it. Right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. It`s an assist.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" is kind of a cautionary tale about handing over any contest at all to people on the internet. This one was a sticker contest in beautiful Ulster County, New York, just north of New York City. People there are choosing the design for the "I voted" stickers that voters get after they cast the ballot.\par \par \uc2 All the artists are local school children and here are the finalists. Five hundred eighty people voted for 18-year-old Karen Bredberg`s eagle design, very nice; 14-year-old -- yes, there it is.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s cute.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "I voted" 14-year-old Wendy Stewart went with the Capitol Building and a patriotic look, more than 5,000 voted for hers. But there is a clear leader on this board. I don`t know if you`ve spotted it yet. That is 14-year-old, Hudson Rowan said his style is not really stars and stripes and eagles.\par \par \uc2 And so he went with the colorful head on aqua colored a spider legs. It`s got 62,000 votes run away when -- more people have voted for the "I vote" sticker than voted in the county of Ulster in the last election.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Oh my God. Sense of humor. That`s what they have. Sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or a taste for great art.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Honestly, it works for that county and it`s the home county of Woodstock. Remember Woodstock? I mean, there are people still hitchhiking for the Woodstock right now. So I think it`s great. Rowan, well done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done. I like that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Spider head.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, up next, singer-songwriter, Chelsea Cutler talks to us about her music, how fans connect with her very personal songs and what she wants to accomplish in her young career, but first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chelsea Cutler performance.)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That is singer-songwriter and producer, Chelsea Cutler performing her hit song with Noah Kahan, "Crazier Things."\par \par \uc2 The 25-year-old just announced that she was going back on tour this fall for her sophomore album, "When I Close My Eyes."\par \par \uc2 Since breaking into the music scene in 2017, Cutler has reached more than two billion streams on \uc2 Spotify\uc2 . This year, she was listed on "Forbes" 30 under 30 in music.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Yuccas recently caught up with Cutler in New York City to talk about her rise to fame, mental health, and identity.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Lifeboat`s Empty.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Chelsea Cutler is a performer for the modern age.\par \par \uc2 Hey, are you cool to hug.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER (Singer-songwriter): I am. Oh, I`d love a hug. Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Good.\par \par \uc2 She`s approachable and warm, but also undeniably cool.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chelsea Cutler performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We caught up with the 25-year-old backstage at the Governor`s Ball Music Festival in Queens, New York.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chelsea Cutler performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Not far from her Connecticut hometown, she was with her girlfriend Tilly and her mom.\par \par \uc2 Your mom told us a little secret that at one point when you were about nine or 10 years old, you were playing at the piano and she said to you, "I think you could do this for a living." And you looked at her and apparently said, "No. I`m going to be a soccer star."\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: I wanted to play soccer my whole life. For sure.\par \par \uc2 I played in college, but like I wasn`t very good. So that dream like ended very early on.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That dream may have ended, but another one started.\par \par \uc2 In high school and college, Cutler was casually posting music online to \uc2 SoundCloud\uc2 , mostly for friends when the record labels started the call.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chelsea Cutler video.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: In 2017, her song "Your Shirt" went viral.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chelsea Cutler video.)\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: My parents kind of sat me down and they were like, "You need to leave college."\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: With her parents` support, Cutler dropped out and began touring the country with her friend, Quinn XCII. In 2019, she signed with Republic Records.\par \par \uc2 A year later, her album "How to be Human" peaked at number 23 on the Billboard 200 Music Chart.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chelsea Cutler performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But even as her star was rising, Cutler was struggling with panic attacks, anxiety, and depression,\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Devil on my Shoulder.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The song, "Devil on my Shoulder" on her most recent album is about depression.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Devil on my Shoulder.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What are some of the lyrics in that song that are meaningful to you?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: There`s a line that talks about like I need to be reminded, like just to breathe, because I think existing is exhausting when you have depression and anxiety.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: How do you manage it?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: I`m really grateful for medication. I`m really grateful. You know, I`ve been therapy consistently for four years now, and I`m like the hugest proponent of therapy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sad Tonight.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It hasn`t always been this easy for Cutler to be this open about her personal life.\par \par \uc2 When her love song "Lucky" came out, Cutler told people it was about her dog, Cooper.\par \par \uc2 And it was really about your girlfriend?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: So how did you end up being able to go from this is about my dog to this is about my girlfriend?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: Well, I think like once people knew I was kind of like, "Oh, ha-ha," that, you know, that was actually about my relationship.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Was it nerve wracking the first time you put your relationship out into the world?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: Yes, yes. Very nerve wracking.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But it meant a lot to her fans who say they see parts of themselves in her.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: She has a girlfriend and she`s like, so open about it. It is so awesome.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Very supportive. Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Why do you guys care about that so much?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are gay, so --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: So you`re supporting it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, very, very, very --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Is it important to see someone that represents that community on such a big stage?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: They told us they appreciate both Cutler`s vulnerability and accessibility. Something we saw firsthand in the middle of our interview.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my gosh, she`s right there. Chelsea. Chelsea.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: When people come up to you and say, "You really helped me. I was struggling. I appreciate your music." What does that do for you?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: It`s so awesome that something that I do so exclusively for my own healing can be so helpful to other people.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: What is your pre-concert routine?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: Pre-concert routine? I like to -- I love -- I feel like performing just requires like so much social energy for me that like, I kind of want to be left alone.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: So what`s next for Cutler? She`s going back on tour this fall. After that --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chelsea Cutler performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: She plans to hunker down and write her next album right here in the studio she recently built in her New York City apartment.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chelsea Cutler performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Do you ever pinch yourself sometimes that you have made it?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: In a lot of ways I would tell you right now, I don`t feel like I`ve made it. And there are a lot of things that I want to accomplish still.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Like what?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: I`d love to be able to sell arena someday.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It`s more about the crowds and the stadiums for you than it is like winning a Grammy.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: Like winning a Grammy would be really cool. I wouldn`t say no. I should get a new one. What I do think matters though, is real people whose lives genuinely are affected by my music. Like that`s more important to me, for sure. But that being said --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: If you`re listening.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA CUTLER: If you`re listening.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Jamie Yuccas, Queens, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I mean, welcome to New York, Chelsea Cutler.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am glad she is moving here. It sounds great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par 