he competition and also a protester.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m going to eat these hot dogs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I`m going to choke you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: America, stars, stripes.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Oh, look at that. It`s America`s favorite fast food restaurant is, for the eighth year in a row, \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2 . What is it about Chick-Fil-A`s. It`s just a chicken sandwich, right?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It`s the pickles.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: It`s the pickles.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The Polynesian sauce. And the fact that their fries are waffle. Frozen lemonade and you get the diet lemonade.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: I feel like Ed filled out this survey many, many, many times.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A new attitude.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new attitude. You`ve got a new attitude?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not today. Not today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got this --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Anytime soon. Always something around here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hard to groove though while I sit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say, is it hard for you to dance in your chair?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve still got a little you know, the grind, but like - -\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That little bounce with the shoulder. Take it, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s hard. It`s hard.\par \par \uc2 To take a look at this the giant African land snail which grows to be eight inches long and can breed like you wouldn`t believe.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Is that what it`s doing right there?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: Because that looks -- very tender.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t think it is breeding.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It looks like a tender moment.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: I think we need to move on. It`s a morning show.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Will be difficult to do a prisoner --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Cleanup on aisle seven.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mine is pealing up here. That ever happen to you? Peeling on your head? Because you don`t have enough sunscreen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, I have hair on my head.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Have you seen "Elvis," you guys? Have you seen "Elvis?"\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Not yet, but I will.\par \par \uc2 ANNE-MARIE GREEN: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Have you seen "Elvis?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Can I see that Elvis shimmy. There it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a cut-cut.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Shawna Thomas.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS (CBS MORNINGS executive producer): Hi.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let them eat cake.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We let them eat pie.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: I`d like to say thank you to all of you. I have one of the best jobs in the world and that`s because I get to work with you all.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Make a wish.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220708ei78000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Ohio Protesters Arrested\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Elise Preston, Dana Jacobson \par \uc2 1,028 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Protests have taken a surprising turn in Akron, Ohio where officers shot Jayland Walker more than 60 times last week after a traffic stop and foot chase.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bianca Austin.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Demonstrators scuffled with officers last night outside Akron Police headquarters and some people were arrested there. CBS News has been told the detainees include family members of people shot by police in other high-profile cases.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston is in Akron for us.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good morning. What do you know?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Among the relatives at the protest were the father of Jacob Blake who was shot and paralyzed in 2020 by officers in Kenosha, Wisconsin, and the aunt of Breonna Taylor, who was shot and killed in her Kentucky home two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Late last night, relatives of Blake told us the father came to Akron to stand in solidarity with protesters when he ended up getting arrested and then hospitalized in custody. That arrest was caught on camera by Taylor`s aunt, who during our interview said she was being detained.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: This was the moment when Bianca Austin, aunt of Breonna Taylor and an unidentified companion say they were being detained by Akron Police.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What is going on?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: You`re under arrest.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What did I do?\par \par \uc2 BIANCA AUSTIN (Breonna Taylor`s aunt): Nothing. You`re alive.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Austin had been in Akron to protest the police shooting death of Jayland Walker and was speaking to us from a parking lot. She had just finished following an ambulance that was transporting Jacob Blake, Sr. He was hospitalized after being arrested by police as he was protesting.\par \par \uc2 Austin recorded the incident outside the city`s police department as it unfolded on her cell phone.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @FamilySoupTV.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Multiple protesters appeared to refuse orders by police to disperse.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @FamilySoupTV.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: What are you doing? What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: At the same time, Blake, Sr. seen here in the black hat also appears to be resisting officers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bianca Austin.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The video then shows officers appearing to struggle to handcuff Blake, Sr. --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bianca Austin.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Who pleads with officers claiming that he is handicapped as they forced him across the side walk.\par \par \uc2 After several minutes on the ground, several officers appear to lift Blake, Sr. onto a stretcher and push him toward an ambulance. Minutes later, police allow Austin to leave in her vehicle.\par \par \uc2 She catches up with the ambulance carrying Blake, Sr.\par \par \uc2 BIANCA AUSTIN: Saying he`s in a lot of pain.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Austin had just spoken with Blake, Sr. when we spoke to her around one in the morning. Our interview ended when police arrived and Austin said she was being detained.\par \par \uc2 BIANCA AUSTIN: We`re out of here.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: No, he is detained.\par \par \uc2 BIANCA AUSTIN: He`s detained for what? What is he detained for?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: He`s not moving right now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: So as you saw, a very chaotic scene with still a lot of unanswered questions this morning. We still don`t know why Blake was hospitalized while in custody, but we do know that he faces a handful of charges this morning including failure to disperse and resisting arrest.\par \par \uc2 Austin faces similar charges and will both will be arraigned in Court this morning. We have not heard back from police -- Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Police as you said, a lot of unanswered questions there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi abortion clinic at the center of a Supreme Court ruling that overturned Roe versus Wade is about to shut down. The state`s trigger law banning almost all abortions takes effect today.\par \par \uc2 Protesters on both sides of the debate surrounded Jackson Women`s Health Organization yesterday. The clinic known locally as the Pink House has already stopped performing abortions. It was the only provider in the state, and last week, a Mississippi Judge denied the clinic`s request to block the state`s trigger law.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is now illegal there except, in the case of a rape reported to police or where the pregnancy threatens a life.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi is one of a growing number of states where most abortion care is banned or severely restricted after the historic Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, new developments in the case of \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner as supporters rally to demand her release from a Russian prison.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are still waiting on Boris Johnson to come out of Downing Street to make what is expected to be his resignation statement.\par \par \uc2 Coming up ahead, we`ll take it back to London to hear what Johnson says if and when he says it and also talk about what it means for Britain and the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, some good news for the fans of "Stranger Things." The \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series has a big announcement. It is breaking records, by the way, so if you haven`t seen it, you better check it out.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Comes the washing machine. I guess, because Gayle is not here, I`ve got to do the follow around.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There it is.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220708ei770002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  to Resign; Shooting Suspect Considered Second Attack; Uvalde Report Reveals Missed Chances; Ohio Protesters Arrested; Mississippi Abortion Clinic to Close; Fight to Free Brittney Griner; Dermatology`s Diversity Problem; Burning Love for Young Elvis; A Passion for Fashion; Homeward Bound; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Dana Jacobson, Charlie D`Agata,  Adriana Diaz, Elise Preston, Janet Shamlian, Ed O`Keefe, Vladimir Duthiers,  Jamie Yuccas, Christina Ruffini \par \uc2 13,669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil here with Dana Jacobson and Nate Burleson. Gayle is off today. We`re doing two things at once watching 10 Downing Street where \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  may resign as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, but first we`re going to go to today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2  (British Prime Minister): The job of a Prime Minister in difficult circumstances when he`s been handed a colossal mandate is to keep going and that`s what I`m going to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Breaking News: After defying calls to step down, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  agrees to resign.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A haunting revelation about the Fourth of July shooting suspect. Police say he considered carrying out a second attack.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER COVELLI (Lake County Major Crime Task Force Deputy Chief): Using the firearm he had in his vehicle to commit another shooting.\par \par \uc2 MAN: According to a new report, a Uvalde police officer had the gunman in his sights before he entered Robb Elementary School.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And supporters rally for \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner as she heads back to Court in Russia.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER (Brittney Griner`s wife): We are not going to ever be quiet until she is home safely.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Donald Trump`s former White House Counsel, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  has agreed to testify before the House Committee investigating January 6.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A shocking moment for a couple in Tampa, a family`s truck hit by lightning.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Rafael Nadal who was able to outlast American, Taylor Fritz in an epic four-hour, 21-minute, five set thriller --\par \par \uc2 MAN: And yet again, Rafael Nadal finds a way. Epic.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: How to ruin a wedding and seconds. This guy set the wedding dance floor on fire and not with his moves.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Fire. Fire.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Did he tried to put out the flames with his sleeve and that was a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Look at him.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Stomps it out and gets back to the good vibes, and that is the most important lesson. Number one: Do not dance with sparklers in your hand. And also, don`t stop dancing.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Don`t stop dancing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t stop dancing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, don`t stop dancing. You know, all that ends well. He didn`t catch on fire in the end.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The barn, the barn. The barn is on fire.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a jam-packed show for you. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re taking you behind the scenes of an industry built for size zero. Fashion Designer Christian Siriano has become the go-to designer for stars who have had trouble finding the right fit.\par \par \uc2 He shows us the studio where he makes clothes for every body so everyone can feel good.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Jamie Yuccas tours Hollywood with an actor getting such a star and see his dreams come true. Chaydon Jay plays the young Elvis in the new hit movie about the King he talks about embodying the icon in his early years and meeting his heroes along the way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s no "Top Gun: Maverick," but "Elvis" is a very good movie. I will.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Pretty good role.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I will say.\par \par \uc2 All right, but, we`re going to get to that breaking news I mentioned off the top.\par \par \uc2 It`s in the UK where any moment now, British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is expected to emerge from his official residence in Downing Street and step down as Conservative Party leader. There is the front door.\par \par \uc2 It comes after a series of scandals and it means his time as Britain`s leader, a position he`s held since 2019, will soon come to an end.\par \par \uc2 One British tabloid summed it all up with the headline, "Squatter holed up in No. 10."\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is outside Number 10 following the latest developments there in Downing Street.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning. "The Economist" newspaper also had a headline of note, it said "Clown fall." How did we get to here?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we are expecting the Prime Minister to emerge from Number 10 Downing Street sometime within the next hour. His office won`t confirm whether that is to announce his resignation, but it follows a tumultuous 48 hours in politics in a fight of his political life.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : This is where we had meetings with ministerial colleagues and others.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD jeering.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Despite desperately clinging to power, it was a mass revolt, not even \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  could survive.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD booing.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Some of the loudest boos in yesterday`s session of Parliament came from his own party. In characteristic style, he chose to laugh it off.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : Thank you, Mr. Speaker. Today is a big day as we implement the biggest tax cut -- as we implement the biggest tax cut for a decade.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Opposition leader \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  was having none of it.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER (Member of Parliament): Anyone quitting now after defending all that hasn`t got a shred of integrity.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD "Hear, hear.")\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER: Mr. Speaker, isn`t this the first recorded case of the sinking ship`s fleeing the rat.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: A reference to dozens of Johnson`s Ministers resigning, including senior members of Cabinet leaving the PM scrambling to fill positions.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER: Doesn`t the country better than ...\par \par \uc2 (CROWD answering "Yes.")\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER: ... a Z-list cast of nodding dogs.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD answers "Hear.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Following a series of scandals, what finally brought Johnson down is a crisis that erupted after lawmaker Chris Pincher was accused of groping men at a private member`s club. After first denying he knew of previous sexual misconduct complaints, Johnson had to apologize that he had been briefed before.\par \par \uc2 Senior Cabinet members said "Enough is enough," and fellow Ministers began to fall like dominoes. Yet, Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  remained defiant to the bitter end.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : Frankly, Mr. Speaker, the job of a Prime Minister in difficult circumstances, when he`s been handed a colossal mandate is to keep going and that`s what I`m going to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Now, we`re hearing the Prime Minister may want to hold on to leadership until the fall when a new leader is chosen. However, several members within his party says he needs to go now -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Charlie D`Agata for us in London.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on all this, let`s go to Jon Sopel, who spent years at the \uc2 BBC\uc2  explaining American politics to the UK. Now, he is here to explain British politics to all of us.\par \par \uc2 Jon, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JON SOPEL (Political writer and commentator): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If in fact \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  walks through that front door and announces his resignation. What happens then?\par \par \uc2 JON SOPEL: We don`t know. We have no idea what \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is going to say or what he`s going to try and do. I cannot begin to describe how surreal British politics has become in the past 48 hours. It has kind of gone slightly "Monty Python," it is a bit of scruffy "Downton Abbey," and a bit of "Succession" thrown in as well because it has gone mad.\par \par \uc2 We have had a new Treasury Secretary appointed on Tuesday night, the next morning, he goes on around to the TV and radio studios to defend \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . This morning, he issues a letter saying \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has got to go.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a new Education Secretary appointed who has resigned within 24 hours. So, what Boris does or says next is anyone`s guess, and I think that what he will try to do is he will try to hold on until the autumn and he is apparently reappointing Ministers as we speak, which seems utterly bizarre, but whether he will be allowed to remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 And the desperate thing that the Conservative Party is worried about is embroiling the Queen in a row over the constitutional succession of Prime Minister.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Boris -- there are many \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  quotes that come to mind at a moment like this one, in particular, his philosophy on resignation. He was asked about it back in 2015-2016, and he said he preferred to Sellotape everything together and kind of shamble through.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ll see if that is in fact going to happen. Dana, I`ll hand it over to you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I think that the next question even though we may not know when it seems to be the fate is that resignation, so who might be Prime Minister next?\par \par \uc2 JON SOPEL: You`re going to wish you hadn`t invited me on the television this morning because I would say "I don`t know." There are so many candidates. I can -- I mean, there are so many Cabinet Ministers who are looking at themselves in the mirror and saying, "I could be Prime Minister." They all think they`ve got a shout.\par \par \uc2 So, I think \uc2 Nadhim Zahawi\uc2  who is this -- the Treasury Secretary, Chancellor of the Exchequer who has been appointed, he`ll fancy himself. The Foreign Secretary \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 . She`s cutting short a trip to Indonesia at the G20 to come back early.\par \par \uc2 I can go on with a list of runners and riders, but there is no standout charismatic figure in the way that \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  was charismatic.\par \par \uc2 David Cameron\uc2 , the former Prime Minister described \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  as slippery to get hold of as a greased piglet.\par \par \uc2 Today, he`s been turned into sausage meat.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jon, in one word, what is Boris Johnson`s legacy?\par \par \uc2 JON SOPEL: Brexit, chaos, and a bit of untrustworthiness.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jon Sopel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll go back to London as soon as Johnson comes out of Downing Street to make his statement.\par \par \uc2 In this country, police say the man accused of killing seven people at the Fourth of July parade thought about carrying out a second attack. Monday shooting in Highland Park, Illinois north of Chicago also injured more than 30 people. Authorities claimed the suspect confessed after his arrest.\par \par \uc2 Robert Crimo, III made a virtual Court appearance yesterday. He was ordered held without bail, charged with seven counts of first degree murder.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has obtained this photo of the gun that police say was found in the alleged gunman`s car. It was just one of five -- one of five -- that was legally obtained despite a series of incidents three years ago that led police to call him a clear and present danger.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz is in Highland Park with more on the attack and the victims.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the suspects allegedly planned this shooting for weeks. More charges are expected to be filed including for attempted murder. Federal charges could also be filed.\par \par \uc2 But we`re also learning more about the gut-wrenching decisions parents had to make to protect their children.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA RING (Shooting survivor): It was almost as though a light switch flipped and everyone just started running for cover.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Dana and Gregory Ring grabbed their three children and ran for their lives.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chicago Critter.)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: As gunfire erupted at the Highland Park Fourth of July Parade.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY RING (Shooting survivor): The energy just really changed quickly from smiles to just screaming and then again pop, pop, pop, pop, pop, pop.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Josefrivera2/TikTok.)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: In the chaos, the Ring`s say they found themselves caring for a toddler.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY RING: He was saying, "Mommy, Daddy," so I went back up to look for his mom and dad and then I saw the scene -- the carnage.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The little boy turned out to be a two-year-old whose parents, Irina and Kevin McCarthy were among the seven people killed in the shooting. Witnesses say the boy`s father died shielding him.\par \par \uc2 According to investigators, the gunman picked July 4th because he had an affinity for the numbers four and seven, but they wouldn`t get into his possible motive.\par \par \uc2 Despite previous encounters with police, he was able to legally purchase five firearms, including this one, authorities say was found in his car.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say after the gunman unleashed at least 83 bullets on the parade, he had roughly 60 rounds left and seriously contemplated carrying out a second attack at a celebration in Madison, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER COVELLI: Indications are that he hadn`t put enough thought and research into it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The suspect was later apprehended after returning to Illinois.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He admitted to what he had done.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Josefrivera2/TikTok.)\par \par \uc2 GREGORY RING: Children should not be running from Fourth of July Parades in the Midwest or anywhere.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The Ring`s are frustrated and furious this was able to happen in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from leonarcos11/\uc2 Twitter\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 GREGORY RING: The only issue that every single community in America has in common is gun violence.\par \par \uc2 DANA RING: We just know something different has to happen and it has to happen like now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: A detective later arrived at the Ring`s home to pick up that little two-year-old boy. They had to put a car seat into the squad car. The boy was later reunited with his grandparents.\par \par \uc2 As for the suspect, he next appears in Court, July 28th -- Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Adriana, people across the country thinking of that little boy, there is no doubt, and thankful that the Ring`s were there for him. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 A new report on the school massacre in Uvalde, Texas confirms police could have stopped it before 19 children and two teachers were killed. An active shooter training center run by \uc2 Texas State University\uc2  points to multiple occasions when officers had a chance to target the gunman at Robb Elementary School.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian has reaction to the new report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Stunning new information details missed opportunities Uvalde Police had in preventing the Robb Elementary massacre.\par \par \uc2 An 