f people shot by police in other high-profile cases.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston is in Akron for us.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good morning. What do you know?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Among the relatives at the protest were the father of Jacob Blake who was shot and paralyzed in 2020 by officers in Kenosha, Wisconsin, and the aunt of Breonna Taylor, who was shot and killed in her Kentucky home two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Late last night, relatives of Blake told us the father came to Akron to stand in solidarity with protesters when he ended up getting arrested and then hospitalized in custody. That arrest was caught on camera by Taylor`s aunt, who during our interview said she was being detained.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: This was the moment when Bianca Austin, aunt of Breonna Taylor and an unidentified companion say they were being detained by Akron Police.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What is going on?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: You`re under arrest.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What did I do?\par \par \uc2 BIANCA AUSTIN (Breonna Taylor`s aunt): Nothing. You`re alive.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Austin had been in Akron to protest the police shooting death of Jayland Walker and was speaking to us from a parking lot. She had just finished following an ambulance that was transporting Jacob Blake, Sr. He was hospitalized after being arrested by police as he was protesting.\par \par \uc2 Austin recorded the incident outside the city`s police department as it unfolded on her cell phone.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @FamilySoupTV.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Multiple protesters appeared to refuse orders by police to disperse.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @FamilySoupTV.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: What are you doing? What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: At the same time, Blake, Sr. seen here in the black hat also appears to be resisting officers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bianca Austin.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The video then shows officers appearing to struggle to handcuff Blake, Sr. --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bianca Austin.)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Who pleads with officers claiming that he is handicapped as they forced him across the side walk.\par \par \uc2 After several minutes on the ground, several officers appear to lift Blake, Sr. onto a stretcher and push him toward an ambulance. Minutes later, police allow Austin to leave in her vehicle.\par \par \uc2 She catches up with the ambulance carrying Blake, Sr.\par \par \uc2 BIANCA AUSTIN: Saying he`s in a lot of pain.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Austin had just spoken with Blake, Sr. when we spoke to her around one in the morning. Our interview ended when police arrived and Austin said she was being detained.\par \par \uc2 BIANCA AUSTIN: We`re out of here.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: No, he is detained.\par \par \uc2 BIANCA AUSTIN: He`s detained for what? What is he detained for?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: He`s not moving right now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: So as you saw, a very chaotic scene with still a lot of unanswered questions this morning. We still don`t know why Blake was hospitalized while in custody, but we do know that he faces a handful of charges this morning including failure to disperse and resisting arrest.\par \par \uc2 Austin faces similar charges and will both will be arraigned in Court this morning. We have not heard back from police -- Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Police as you said, a lot of unanswered questions there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi abortion clinic at the center of a Supreme Court ruling that overturned Roe versus Wade is about to shut down. The state`s trigger law banning almost all abortions takes effect today.\par \par \uc2 Protesters on both sides of the debate surrounded Jackson Women`s Health Organization yesterday. The clinic known locally as the Pink House has already stopped performing abortions. It was the only provider in the state, and last week, a Mississippi Judge denied the clinic`s request to block the state`s trigger law.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is now illegal there except, in the case of a rape reported to police or where the pregnancy threatens a life.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi is one of a growing number of states where most abortion care is banned or severely restricted after the historic Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, new developments in the case of \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner as supporters rally to demand her release from a Russian prison.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are still waiting on \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  to come out of Downing Street to make what is expected to be his resignation statement.\par \par \uc2 Coming up ahead, we`ll take it back to London to hear what Johnson says if and when he says it and also talk about what it means for Britain and the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, some good news for the fans of "Stranger Things." The \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series has a big announcement. It is breaking records, by the way, so if you haven`t seen it, you better check it out.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Comes the washing machine. I guess, because Gayle is not here, I`ve got to do the follow around.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There it is.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. You are looking at a live picture of the door to Number 10 Downing Street, official residence of the British Prime Minister, and as of this moment, 7:30 Eastern Time, that Prime Minister is \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He of the rumpled suit and messy hair, he is expected to walk through that door, and resign as Conservative Party leader and leader of his nation any moment now, not known for punctuality, not known for a firm grasp of names and details. An entertaining politician, much like some we`ve had on this side of the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 For more on the atmosphere of anticipation there in London, let`s go to Charlie D`Agata who is in Downing Street. In fact, nope. \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , the wind blowing through his hair is about to speak. Let`s listen in.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : Good afternoon, everybody. Good afternoon. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 It is clearly now the will of the Parliamentary Conservative Party, that there should be a new leader of that party, and therefore a new Prime Minister, and I`ve agreed with Sir \uc2 Graham Brady\uc2 , the chairman of our backbench MPs that the process of choosing that new leader should begin now and the timetable will be announced next week.\par \par \uc2 And I have today appointed a Cabinet to serve as I will, until a new leader is in place.\par \par \uc2 So I want to say to the millions of people who voted for us in 2019, many of them voting conservative for the first time, thank you for that incredible mandate, the biggest conservative majority since 1987, the biggest share of the vote since 1979.\par \par \uc2 And the reason I have fought so hard in the last few days to continue to deliver that mandate in person was not just because I wanted to do so, but because I felt it was my job, my duty, my obligation to you to continue to do what we promised in 2019.\par \par \uc2 And of course, I`m immensely proud of the achievements of this government from getting Brexit done to settling our relations with the continent for over half a century, reclaiming the power for this country to make its own laws in Parliament, getting us all through the pandemic, delivering the fastest vaccine rollout in Europe, the fastest exit from lockdown, and in the last few months, leading the West in standing up to Putin`s aggression in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And let me say now, to the people of Ukraine, that I know that we in the UK will continue to back your fight for freedom for as long as it takes. And at the same time in this country, we`ve been pushing forward a vast program of investment in infrastructure and skills and technology, the biggest in a century because if I have one insight into human beings, it is the genius and talent and enthusiasm and imagination are evenly distributed throughout the population, but opportunity is not.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why we must keep leveling up, keep unleashing the potential of every part of the United Kingdom. And if we can do that in this country, we will be the most prosperous in Europe.\par \par \uc2 And in the last few days, I`ve tried to persuade my colleagues that it would be eccentric to change governments, when we`re delivering so much and when we have such a vast mandate, and when we`re actually only a handful of points behind in the polls, even in midterm after quite a few months of pretty relentless sledging and when the economic scene is so difficult domestically and internationally.\par \par \uc2 And I regret I have not been successful in those arguments, and of course, it`s painful, not to be able to see through so many ideas and projects myself. But as we`ve seen at Westminster, the herd instinct is powerful. When the herd moves, it moves. And my friends, in politics, no one is remotely indispensable.\par \par \uc2 And are brilliant and Darwinian system will produce another leader, equally committed to taking this country forward through tough times, not just helping families to get through it, but changing and improving the way we do things, cutting burdens on businesses and families and yes, cutting taxes, because that is the way to generate the growth and the income we need to pay for great public services.\par \par \uc2 And to that new leader, I say, whoever he or she may be, I say I will give you as much support as I can; and to you, the British public, I know that there will be many people who are relieved, and perhaps quite a few who will also be disappointed, and I want you to know how sad I am to be giving up the best job in the world, but them`s the brakes.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank Carrie and our children, to all members of my family who have had to put up with so much for so long. I want to thank the fearless \uc2 British Civil Service\uc2  for all the help and support that you have given our police, our emergency services, and of course, our fantastic \uc2 NHS\uc2 , who at critical moment, helped to extend my own period in office, as well as our Armed Services. And our --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There has been a major change this morning on the world stage. You`ve been listening to British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , and now he said, he is leaving what he calls the greatest job in the world.\par \par \uc2 For more on that speech, and we heard the protesters there underneath the Prime Minister`s remarks, let`s bring in British journalist Jon Sopel, \uc2 BBC\uc2  correspondent, expert on all things British and can translate it all for us.\par \par \uc2 Jon, what stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 JON SOPEL: There were two things that stood out for me from that, he very pointedly said it was the Conservative Party at Westminster i.e., the Members of Parliament who wanted him gone. The implication of that clearly being that he believes he still has the support in the country.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing he then went on to say was he said it was their herd instinct i.e., they were just sheep that were following the lead, and that they didn`t really know what they were doing do and do you really know what you`ve done to bring down this great man? I thought it was remarkable that there was little self-analysis of maybe that he has contributed enormously to the problems that he has faced.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jon, Boris mentioned this Darwinian system. So the question is, how does the \uc2 British government\uc2  evolve from here?\par \par \uc2 JON SOPEL: And that we haven`t heard yet from \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . There are a huge number of Conservative Members of Parliament who think it is simply untenable for him to carry on for months as Prime Minister, while you are waiting for a new one to be appointed, what they are saying is there needs to be a caretaker, and \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  clearly wants to carry on for as long as possible.\par \par \uc2 And that is why today, I mean, I thought yesterday was the maddest day I`ve ever seen in British politics. I am now thinking that today is even more nuts, because \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , without any authority, having announced that he is resigning is trying to appoint Ministers to take up positions. Who would want to take up a position given these circumstances?\par \par \uc2 So, there is still a lot in this Darwinian system that we just don`t know how it`s going to play out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Jon Sopel for us with his fearless analysis. Jon, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For folks who is tuning in, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  swept into power with the largest Conservative majority in the UK since \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  in the 80s announces the end of his reign as Prime Minister. Details still to be worked out about who will succeed him.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We continue to follow what the State Department is calling the wrongful detainment of Brittney Griner. That`s the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star who has been in Russian custody since February.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we are learning the \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  is saying it is "difficult" at the moment to engage in any kind of prisoner exchange with the US.\par \par \uc2 In Phoenix last night, Griner`s fans rallied to demand her freedom to bring her home.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is following developments from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Dana, good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 As you said, supporters sat at her home court last night ahead of her second scheduled Court appearance today in a Moscow area Courtroom.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER: Truthfully, I can`t rest as her humanity is being stripped from her.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Supporters of the 31-year-old basketball star gathered at Footprint Center, home of her \uc2 WNBA\uc2  team, the Phoenix Mercury to rally for Brittney Griner`s return home. Griner`s wife, Cherelle addressed the crowd.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER: I`m frustrated that 140 days have passed since my wife has been able to speak to me, to our family and our friends. I`m frustrated that my wife is not going to get justice.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The rally came after the star Olympian penned an Independence Day letter to the President who read it and responded with a phone call to Cherelle. The White House says the President told her he is working to ensure the release of Brittney as well as the release of Paul Whelan and other US nationals who were wrongfully detained or held hostage in Russia and around the world.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WHELAN (Paul Whelan`s sister): You know why the outreach to some families and not others?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That statement sparked criticism from Whelan`s sister who says the President hasn`t called her family directly about securing her brother`s release.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WHELAN: Whoever thought this was a good idea has to remember that other families with far less resources have been waiting for years and years to see some action to bring their loved ones home.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Paul Whelan is serving a 16-year sentence on an espionage charge ever since being detained in Russia in 2018. As for the President, he is said to have written a response to Brittney Griner in response to that letter, she sent him on Independence Day, but the White House has not yet revealed the contents of that message -- Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, Ed, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Vlad will have the stories you will be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:44. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 Vlad feeling good vibes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Chill vibes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You look amazing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pop quiz for you. A guy named Richard Starkey turned 80 today. Who am I talking about?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ringo Starr.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I knew you would know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Peace and love. Peace and love as Ringo always loves to say.\par \par \uc2 All right, so I`ve got a great story for you to kick off "What to Watch." If you`re afraid of heights though, brace yourself. We`re going to begin with a very scary situation at a construction site in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 Before we show you what happened, I want to assure everybody, women and children. Hide the kids. The man in the video is very, very safe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh my God. How the [bleep] just happened.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes, yes, yes. I love that. My hosts are looking at this video and Nate is like, "Wait, what`s going on?"\par \par \uc2 So let me tell you what happened here, how -- the Ontario Labor Ministry is investigating how this worker was left dangling from a crane more than a dozen stories in the air this happened on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The construction company in charge of the project says the worker`s hand got caught in a rope. You see him dangling, dangling.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Frantic bystanders are yelling for the man to "Hang on, hang on" before he was safely lowered to the ground without any serious injuries.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: How long did you say he was up there?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was up there for quite a while. That`s the entirety of the video there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Twelve storeys?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Twelve storeys and he is like dangling and kicking his legs.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Until he makes it to the bottom.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Oof.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those grippers were working.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Holding on for dear life.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Literally.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is okay, folks. He is okay.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" yesterday, Tony, you weren`t here; Dana, you weren`t here either, but I talked about "Stranger Things" and how "Master of Puppets" from Metallica and Kate Bush is trending again.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you thought things couldn`t get any stranger, there is some big news, we`re getting a spin off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. \uc2 Netflix\uc2  announced that the show`s creators Matt and Ross Duffer will expand the franchise. They`re being tight-lipped about the new project, but we do know the brothers will develop it under the umbrella of their new production company, Upside Down Pictures.\par \par \uc2 The finale of "Stranger Things" Season 