 back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Coming up this hour: A lack of diversity can be dangerous when it comes to medical care. How a lot of patients seeking skincare get misdiagnosed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. We visit fashion designer, Christian Siriano, one of the most innovative in the business. Why he is fiercely determined to make clothes for everyone, not just runway models.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`m Dana Jacobson. Elvis Presley wasn`t always the King. Jamie Yuccas hangs out in Hollywood with the actor playing young Elvis in the popular new movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Can I see that Elvis shimmy? There it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Cut-cut.\par \par \uc2 British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is stepping down following a series of scandals.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : I want you to know how sad I am to be giving up the best job in the world. But them`s the brakes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Police say the gunman on the deadly Fourth of July parade shooting considered carrying out a second attack.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY RING: Children should not be running from Fourth of July parades in the Midwest or anywhere.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: A new report says an armed Uvalde police officer had the gunman in his sights before the school massacre, but did not take a shot.\par \par \uc2 PETE BLAIR: And use of force decisions always rests with the officer who is going to use the force.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A Russian official says it will be difficult to do a prisoner exchange with the US as Brittney Griner supporters rally for her return.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER: I am frustrated that my wife is not going to get justice.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What a pathetic expectedly.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER: The dying act of his political career as to parrot that nonsense. Have some self-respect.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Prime Minister Boris Johnson`s announcement comes just a day after he insisted he stay on during an appearance in Parliament.\par \par \uc2 GARY SAMBROOK (Member of Parliament): If the Prime Minister constantly tries to deflect from the issue, always tries to blame other people for mistakes.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : You`d expect the government to continue with its work, not to walk away.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Does the Prime Minister think there are any circumstances in which he should resign?\par \par \uc2 (GROUP cheering.)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We`ve answered that question now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Apparently, the answer is yes. And he summarized the situation as them`s the breaks. That will be trending on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  later today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the new catchphrase.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re going to begin the hour with that breaking news out of the UK this morning.\par \par \uc2 British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  announces that he is stepping down. Here is just part of what he said.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : It is clearly not of the will of the Parliamentary Conservative Party that there should be a new leader of that party and therefore a new Prime Minister, and I`ve agreed with Sir \uc2 Graham Brady\uc2 , the chairman of our backbench MPs that the process of choosing that new leader should begin now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie D`Agata is outside the Prime Minister`s official residence in London.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning to you. What was the atmosphere there like this morning as the Prime Minister spoke for one of the last times?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Well, aside from the world`s press gathered here and hundreds of protesters outside, this sounded more like a victory speech even in defeat. He outlined his successes during his almost three years in office, which included the vaccine rollouts, and support for the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 He said he was saddened that he couldn`t finish his full term. However, he said that he would remain in office until a new leader is chosen. If that`s the case, that may take all the way to autumn to the fall.\par \par \uc2 Several influential leaders in the Conservative Party say that isn`t going to work, he needs to go now.\par \par \uc2 Now this resignation, the step down all follows a series of scandals including being caught what appeared to be a lie earlier this week. After that, dozens of Ministers including Cabinet Ministers resigned in protest until he could not form a manageable government in his words. He said -- these are his words -- he said, "The herd instinct was powerful."\par \par \uc2 Now, during the speech, the protesters I told you about before, they were booing throughout. They were singing "Bye-bye Baby," changing the words to Bye-bye Boris" even as the Prime Minister was speaking as we said.\par \par \uc2 The headline we`re going to see everywhere as he steps down, in his own words, "Them`s the brakes." -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Them`s the brakes.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata in London. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 In this country, we`re learning more about the damaging consequences of inadequate medical care offered to minority communities, and today, we`re focusing on skincare. Why African-Americans make up 13 percent of the US population, only three percent of dermatologists nationwide are Black, and nearly half of all dermatologists report feeling their medical training did not properly prepare them to treat darker skin, which can lead to increased chances of misdiagnosis or lack of treatment.\par \par \uc2 And that`s exactly what happened to one California man who was misdiagnosed for years.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Despite seven years of treatment, Shane Bloomfield`s skin discoloration remained a mystery to multiple dermatologists.\par \par \uc2 SHANE BLOOMFIELD (Skin and hair condition misdiagnosed): So I looked like crispy critter, a burn victim. I was embarrassed to walk down the hall.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: His last doctor simply gave up.\par \par \uc2 SHANE BLOOMFIELD: He stood right in front of me and said, "I can`t help you anymore." There was, you know, hopelessness and then a little glimmer of anger.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shane was finally referred to Dr. Nada Elbuluk, the Director of USC Skin of Color and Pigmentary Disorders Program at Keck Medicine.\par \par \uc2 DR. NADA ELBULUK (Director of USC Skin of Color and Pigmentary Disorders Program at Keck Medicine): Hi, good morning, Mr. Bloomfield. How are you? So nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right away, Dr. Elbuluk recognized Shane had something more commonly found in melanin-rich skin, lichen planus pigmentosus, which causes darkening, as well as related scarring type of hair loss called lichen planopilaris.\par \par \uc2 DR. NADA ELBULUK: I think there was just immediate relief, just in the -- even just have a name to put towards it. Like what it actually is that he has, the fact that the two things were connected, which he never realized was possible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Elbuluk says at least once a week she meets with patients who have been misdiagnosed or under treated.\par \par \uc2 DR. NADA ELBULUK: The more pigment that somebody has, the more it can change the way that a condition can look. What became defined as classic for any condition became what we see in lighter skin, right? But that`s not necessarily classic in darker skin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She now offers a fellowship at her clinic to provide much needed perspective for dermatologists in training.\par \par \uc2 DR. NADA ELBULUK: Many of these conditions even if they don`t kill you, they have profound effects on quality of life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because Shane went many years before being properly diagnosed, he may not make a full recovery. However, he remains optimistic.\par \par \uc2 SHANE BLOOMFIELD: I`m confident that we are on the right track for treatments and that this will be reversed. I`m still hopeful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re now joined by Dr. Jenna Lester, Assistant Professor at \uc2 UC San Francisco\uc2  and Founder and Director of its Skin of Color Program. How`re you doing?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNA LESTER (\uc2 UC San Francisco\uc2  Assistant Professor): I`m doing well. Thanks for having me today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 So nearly half of all dermatologists felt like they weren`t prepared to treat darker skin. How can we fix this?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNA LESTER: In my opinion, one of the main avenues for addressing this is addressing the gaps that exist in our dermatology education.\par \par \uc2 In a paper that I published in "The British Journal of Dermatology," we found that there`s an under representation of patients with dark skin in our common dermatology teaching textbooks. So that means, the photos we`re using to learn what different skin conditions look like are mostly in light skin, and this can lead to misdiagnosis.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, only three percent of the 11,000 dermatologists are Black. So what are some of the barriers that these dermatologists are facing?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNA LESTER: The barriers are numerous. One that I want to highlight is the cost of applying to become a dermatologist. So some analyses suggest that medical students pay $10,000.00 to even take that first step to become a dermatologist, and this is on top of what we know to be the median debt that a graduating medical student has, which is about $170,000.00.\par \par \uc2 And we know that Black and Brown students disproportionately shoulder a graduate medical education debt, and you can imagine how this might be more challenging for some than others.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It has to be frustrating thinking even if I go in to see somebody I may be misdiagnosed, but your advice to somebody who is seeking care would be, "Still go."\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNA LESTER: Definitely still go. Ask around. I think word of mouth is really helpful in finding who is a great doctor and generally can ask friends, neighbors, your primary care physician; have questions before you go in, because you know that white coat anxiety can make you forget everything you came in to talk about. Don`t be afraid to ask them, and don`t be afraid to get a second opinion as well.\par \par \uc2 That`s a totally normal thing to do if you want to have more information about the diagnosis or understand what all your treatment options are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because asking around and then also maybe finding another doctor is time and money. Is there kind of rules of thumb a person should ask themselves about whether they should trust whatever their initial diagnosis is?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNA LESTER: I think really trusting your gut. Your experience in healthcare is really important, and the comfort that you feel with a person, I think is something that should guide how you proceed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve dealt with everything from discoloration, even losing my hair and one of the questions that I ask over the phone is: Do you treat darker skin? And if I get the response that I need then I know, right out the gate that they have seen people like me with my skin color, which helps me to have a little bit more comfort when I walked through that door.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNA LESTER: Yes. I think that`s great advice.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In every aspect, diversity is so important.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and representation.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jenna Lester, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNA LESTER: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, an inside look at the fashion world. Famed designer, Christian Siriano talks about overcoming major challenges creating affordable clothing and why he designs for diverse bodies. I know that`s right.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:09. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up: Actor, Chaydon Jay shows Jamie Yuccas what it`s like to move like Elvis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY (Actor): Okay, so listen, I`ve got the music. You`ve got flat arms. You`d go wide and you put the feet together, like that, and then you put your weight on one leg and then you can switch legs and put it on the other leg.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Elvis Presley performance.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Information that could have been brought to my attention earlier when I attempted to do the Elvis move.\par \par \uc2 Chaydon tells Jamie about leading the role of a lifetime -- landing the role of a lifetime as young Elvis Presley and shares his big ambitions for his acting career.\par \par \uc2 A bright future.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: This year marks the 45th anniversary of Elvis Presley`s death, but the King of Rock n` Roll is still going strong.\par \par \uc2 Baz Luhrmann`s biopic, "Elvis" introduces us to Australian newcomer, Chaydon Jay who plays Elvis as a boy. It`s the teen`s first film, but he already has his sights set high.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Yuccas joined the actor for a walk through Hollywood where he shared what it was like to feel all shook up in the role.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elvis.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: There`s a whole lot of shaking going on in the new musical extravaganza, "Elvis."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elvis.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But before he became a rock n` roll showman --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Elvis Presley performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Elvis was a lonely kid who grew up in a poor predominantly Black Mississippi neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elvis.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: The inspiration young Elvis found in gospel music is brought to life on screen by Chaydon Jay.\par \par \uc2 You had to be really dialed in to the feelings of Elvis.\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: It was very important to be able to get Elvis within my own bones.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elvis.")\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: Not just moving like Elvis, but understanding where that movement comes from. His passion, his energy. Walking into that tent, everything is so real, which was so exciting and so electric.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elvis.)\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: It was so alive. There was real praising going on, and it was one of the most fun experiences that I`ve -- that I`ve had within my life.\par \par \uc2 BAZ LUHRMANN ("Elvis" director): Action.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Australian director, Baz Luhrmann, shot the entire production in Queensland, even meticulously recreating Graceland.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elvis.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: In the film, it is Austin Butler who takes the audience on Elvis`s career journey. And with Luhrmann`s guidance, young Chaydon discovers the roots of the King`s soulful beginnings.\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: Working with Baz was such a once in a lifetime experience. He made every single moment on set just feel electrical.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elvis.")\par \par \uc2 BAZ LUHRMANN: It was funny, someone could actually embody the interpretation that Austin Butler was doing. So, I thought the chances of doing that were very, very slim.\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: He really just laid out the path for me in getting a good understanding and feeling of the role. So, that was -- there wasn`t really any pressure for me. There was just excitement because he really is amazing.\par \par \uc2 BAZ LUHRMANN: And Chaydon was just like, finding a needle in a haystack to be honest. He is wonderful in it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Now, 15 years old, the actor experienced his first red carpet event in Queensland, alongside Oscar-winner and co-star, Tom Hanks.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Elvis.")\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: He congratulated me on my performance, which I took to heart. Come on, "Toy Story" was one of my like childhood movies and being able to talk with him and meet him there, I think was very exciting.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Stevie Wonder.\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: Stevie Wonder, of course, I know Stevie Wonder.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We took Chaydon for a stroll down Hollywood Boulevard, where Elvis was among the first to get a star on the Walk of Fame back in 1960.\par \par \uc2 We wanted to bring you here.\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: Oh my gosh, I didn`t know his was here. I actually did not. I thought it was -- wow, that`s cool. I like how it`s next to The Beatles.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Would you ever want your name on a star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame?\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: Yes, of course. Honestly, my passion is just to be able to be on set and bring the world of fantasy to life.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: We invited him to see the backlot at \uc2 CBS Studios\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: It is very cool, but I think --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Where we couldn`t resist learning what it takes to dance like the King.\par \par \uc2 Being in the land of make believe, can you show me some of your Elvis moves?\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: Okay, so listen. Okay, the music, you go flat arms, you`d go wide, and you put the feet together like that, and then you put your way to one leg and then you could switch legs and put on the other leg.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Elvis Presley performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: While his sights are set on a career like fellow Aussies, Heath Ledger and \uc2 Hugh Jackman\uc2 . Having had the honor to play one of the most iconic figures in American entertainment is not lost on Chaydon.\par \par \uc2 CHAYDON JAY: To be given the opportunity to play or portray one of the most, you know, significant and impactful musicians ever was such an honor because it was really a once in a lifetime experience.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jamie Yuccas in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s quite the role to kick off your acting career.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, no kidding. I like that needle in a haystack, the way Baz Luhrmann described finding him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the movie is fantastic and it`s true, he is a needle in the haystack. He really embodies young Elvis. I don`t know if he`s got a future as a dance instructor though. He needs to find a really --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I know I was like, wait, what do you -- how do you, but can you imagine the hours of watching Elvis to try to embody that and make it just your natural way of being?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s fascinating to understand his mindset as he was walking into the tent, he was just saying, I was completely immersed in the moment with the live music, the live praising, actually taking myself back to that time, feeling like Elvis did in that moment. As a young actor, man, he`s got it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, you know what was so charming about him, right, so his first red carpet experience with Tom Hanks and Baz Luhrmann. And then, I don`t know if he`s been profiled a lot by national television. So Jamie Yuccas brings him to the star and he has such a genuine reaction saying, "Oh my gosh. I didn`t know it was here."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. He might end up with his own star.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I wouldn`t put a pass on that. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up: Are you planning to go away for summer vacation? Do you really need to? Maybe not.\par \par \uc2 We are going to share some good ideas if you want to stay put and stay home and avoid those travel nightmares. I can attest, they are real.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Renowned fashion designer, Christian Siriano wants everyone to feel their best.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Do you have to be a size zero to like high fashion?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO (Fashion designer): No. And that`s the best part. I was sick of customers saying like, "I can`t envision myself in this look." And I was like, "Oh, that`s interesting." Like, "Well, then I`m going to show you how you can envision yourself."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead we`ll visit Siriano`s New York City studio and find out how he is breaking the mold in fashion.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Time now to bring you some of the stories that are top of the table this morning and Nate is up first.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m going to start us off. Okay, so here we go.\par \par \uc2 If you`re a weekend warrior, my "Talk of the Table" is for you. A new study finds people who cram their workouts into the weekend get basically the same health benefit as people who spread them out over the week.\par \par \uc2 Researchers analyzed workout data reported by more than 350,000 Americans. They concluded that people who did two and a half hours or more of moderate exercise on the weekend lowered their risk of premature death.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just as much as people who exercise the same amount and spread out three or more times during the week.\par \par \uc2 So, if you`re working out on the weekends, you`re good.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: As long as you do the two and a half hours.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You don`t have to feel about it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 So for all you guys that are weekday warriors, stop coming up to me on a Tuesday going, "Hey, bro, did you get your lift in?" Like, "No, I didn`t get my lift in because I have multiple jobs and I`m waiting until Saturday to work out."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two and a half hours is a long time.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I know. I`m thinking about this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can spread it out Saturday and Sunday.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: An hour fifteen each?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know sometimes, you feel that stress, you`re like, "Ah, the whole week went by and I didn`t work out."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I know that`s true. That`s actually a good way to look at it. I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You don`t have to. Just do it on the weekends.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like how the study went right to premature death. There are a lot of people looking at just look better at the beach that summer, and they`re like, you`re not going to die.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: No, premature death.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is a little bit from the department of obvious, but I like it anyway because I`ve been drawn to gardening lately and this is the study linking gardening to what gardeners will tell you they already know. That it improves mental health.\par \par \uc2 Now, the research has found this by recruiting 32 women between the ages of 26 and 49 to take gardening classes. They had not gardened before, by the way -- twice a week, eight classes in total. They reported lower stress, lower anxiety, and decreased incidents of depression.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s because they`re not killing the plants like I do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. No. They`re not even talented gardeners necessarily.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: No, because I kill the plant.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that`s not good.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I once interviewed a male hand model and I asked him what he`s going to do when he retires from hand modeling and what he told me was, "I`m going to garden."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Because he couldn`t get his hands dirty then.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to put my -- yes -- my hands in this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the dirt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I relate to that --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You like a garden.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do you garden?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, I don`t, but I`m drawn to it. I find myself wanting to and I can`t even begin, but I want to.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, we`re talking about traditional plants. Now, I`ve got to confirm --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That possum is out there doing its thing.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Excellent point, Nate. Excellent point.\par \par \uc2 All right, this is really my "Talk of the Table" just to try to make you guys laugh.\par \par \uc2 This is a little boy who had a first surprise for his parents during a Fourth of July bike ride. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOY: I found this lost cat.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I don`t think it`s lost. I think it`s just --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whose cat is that?\par \par \uc2 BOY: Mom, you hold it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No. I don`t want it.\par \par \uc2 BOY: I know they don`t bite.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Well, that you know. How about we put him down?\par \par \uc2 BOY: Oh, he is my cat for now.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Well, he`s not your cat. Look, he wants to get down. Let him down.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Oh, in here?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Sure. Let him down, nice and gentle.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Let me put down in the water.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No, not in the water. Not in the water. You can put him down gentle, right there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So adorable video of this boy, he does put the cat down. Now, the cat had a collar. So we`re assuming that the owners or the home was somewhat nearby, hopefully, but the cat obviously okay there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The cat liked the boy.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I mean, right. You see a cat, you pick it up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was chilling. It was just relaxing.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I just love this little kid like "No, he`s my cat now."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love the scoop move.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: The scoop. Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that was -- I liked that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: smiles for everyone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Smiles for everyone.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to take a turn to dresses for everyone, switching gears.\par \par \uc2 Acclaimed fashion designer, Christian Siriano is famous for the diversity of his dresses and the models who wear them.\par \par \uc2 The 36-year-old known for designs that straddle both the high end and the affordable end of the fashion spectrum since winning Project Runway back in 2007. I remember him from that season I was watching.\par \par \uc2 He has become an author, an interior decorator, and a furniture creator. And during the pandemic, he led an all-hands on deck operation to produce thousands of masks for first responders.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini visited Siriano`s New York City studio to find out how he gets it all done.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: If clothes make the man or woman or individual, Christian Siriano will make you something unforgettable.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: I think that it is strange to tell someone they can`t wear something.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: His glittering, glamorous, and sometimes campy designs come in all shapes and sizes. And so do the people who wear them.\par \par \uc2 Do you have to be a size zero to like high fashion?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: No. And that`s the best part. I was sick of customers saying like, "I can`t envision myself in this look." I was like, "Oh, that`s interesting. Like well then, I`m going to show you how you can envision yourself."\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: The show starts here, at his SoHo studio, an eight- minute walk from his apartment.\par \par \uc2 Do you walk every day?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: I walk every day.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Rain or shine?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: Rain or shine. I like how you -- being able to walk to your office is amazing.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Especially when that office often extends to some of the world`s most high profile red carpets, like the Emmys, and the Grammys, and even fashion`s biggest night.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: We`re working on the Met Ball just a few weeks ago. I was here every single day all night.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Of course, of course. I hear that`s a thing.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: It`s a horrible thing.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: A horrible actually.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Siriano had three looks on the Met Gala carpet this year, but his ascension to the heights of high fashion wasn`t exactly seamless.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Project Runway.")\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: In 2007, Siriano burst onto the pop culture scene --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Project Runway.")\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: After winning the reality TV show "Project Runway."\par \par \uc2 At just 21 years old, he had to fight to be taken seriously.\par \par \uc2 When you started, the reviews were not always nice so nice.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: Yes. Totally.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I remember I read one that said your clothes were silly.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: Yes, or like or too much.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Was that a specific thing that they were needling you on?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: I think that it was like they couldn`t figure it out.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: His critics also couldn`t figure out why an aspiring serious designer would want to make cheaper collections for more affordable brands.\par \par \uc2 Did you shop at Payless as a kid?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: I did. Of course. So growing up, I didn`t want to say no to that also, because like that`s where I grew up. Like my mom is a teacher, like that we shopped there. My mom shopped at \uc2 Lane Bryant\uc2 . I did a collection for \uc2 Lane Bryant\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I also was very excited. I was never one to alienate a customer. You never know. Someone can show up at Payless one day, but you never know where they`re shopping 10 years later.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Did you ever have a moment of like panic like maybe this isn`t going to work or, or maybe --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: I have that every day. I still have it. It`s great.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Dozens of successful collaborations and more than a decade of runway shows later.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: This is our world.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I would like to live here, please.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: Yes, live here. Please.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: This is incredible.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: But if you live here, you have to start sewing.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: That`s not going to go well for you.\par \par \uc2 Siriano has mastered the tool of his trade.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: I love the color of this dress. I actually have no idea where this woman`s going and she is fabulous. I`m like, "Where are you going?"\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Oh, so you don`t know where she`s going in this dress.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: No, I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: She has just asked you to --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: She just bought this dress and I swear, I hope she wears it to the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: All of these dresses are custom, made to order.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: I`m pretty much in the whole process --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Starting with a sketch and then brought to life, stitch by meticulous stitch.\par \par \uc2 One of the first things I noticed when I walked in is also your mannequin bodies are different sizes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: Yes. Every single mannequin has a different size, you know, because everybody`s body is different.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Siriano`s consistent willingness to dress anybody and any body has helped him earn a stable of loyal celebrity clients like Lizzo, Lady Gaga, Laverne Cox, and Leslie Jones.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: And I was like, we`re going to make you look incredible.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And she looked like she felt it.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: Oh my god, we were like, "This is your "Pretty Woman" moment.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: After Jones tweeted she couldn`t get a fancy designer to work with her, Siriano replied with what else? A raised hand emoji.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: You`re coming out. You`re going to wear this red gown. You`re also going to show the world that like, yes, you`re tall and curvy, but you also have an amazing body. She really does.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: That gown and dozens of others are now part of an exhibit, "People are People" currently on display in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I think it`s important for like other people to feel represented and like celebrities on screen. Oh my God, that`s someone with my body type on screen and then they can feel more comfortable with who they are.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Kelli Dyer brought her daughter, Ruby.\par \par \uc2 KELLI DYER (SCAD Museum visitor): I try to teach my girls that, you have your body and you should be proud of it and you can wear what you want. Now I`m getting emotional.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Do you have a favorite dress?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: A favorite dress? No. Impossible. No. I have had favorite like moments, like Billy Porter`s Oscar moment, I think was probably one of my favorite moments in my career just because of what it meant.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: What did it mean?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: I just think it was the ultimate showcase of what clothes can do to transform someone`s opinion about the world.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Because in Siriano`s world, you can wear whatever you want, almost anywhere.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SIRIANO: We should be able to walk and talk and be who we want to be. We`re not curing cancer here, like it`s a dress. Calm down.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: So long as it makes you feel great.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Christina Ruffini, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That part of it, the feeling great, as somebody who grew up as a kid that was not a traditional size and didn`t have options, when you find clothes that make you feel the way you want to feel.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And make you see yourself in a different way, that`s everything. So what he does for every one of all sizes, completely hands down, love it. Love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There should be more designers that approach it from that perspective. That body positivity for everybody. It should represent every woman, every man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I mean, he says we`re not curing cancer, but you could wear the dress to a cancer benefit --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And steal the show on the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Look, and as you guys said, that yellow dress, that`s Gayle`s yellow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It could be.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We`ve got to get that dress for Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Christina Ruffini should have had a dress made for herself in the name of journalism.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the name of --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Otherwise, great story, but a missed opportunity there.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Maybe she did. We don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe she did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up next, the joys of camping and being outdoors at home. We will give you the essential tips on how to plan the perfect staycation this summer.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s the check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: From high gas prices to canceled flights, summer vacation season is turning into a nightmare for so many travelers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The pandemic prices were great, where there`s like $2.00 flight. Right now, the flights are skyrocketing and it is getting out of hand.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The border protection was just like that, it was just like so long. We were standing there for like 90 minutes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh man, this is crazy, man.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ve got to start the pump twice just to fill the tank up. It hurts double.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: But there may be a solution -- a staycation. The latest issue of "Conde Nast Traveler" includes staycation ideas for the summer.\par \par \uc2 Executive editor, Erin Florio joins us with some tips for planning your perfect local vacation.\par \par \uc2 Erin, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 We all know the reasons. I mean, we don`t want to deal with those flight cancellations and all of that, but you say if people are doing a staycation, they`re kind of doing it the wrong way, and not really focusing on that vacation part.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO (Conde Nast Traveler executive editor): Well, I think I mean, I think the unfortunate news is that, you know, this was supposed to be the summer of revenge travel, and everybody was so excited to get back out there. And now we`ve had to change our plans again.\par \par \uc2 I think COVID taught us to be flexible, which is a good thing now. The important thing to remember is a staycation does not mean you`re not having a vacation. There is a totally legitimate and great way to stay close to home or in some cases, even at home and turn it into something that feels like you`ve gone miles away, you`ve really removed yourself from your daily life.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Got to change it up at least a little bit.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: You do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you do that in part by redesigning your backyard in a way that can feel fun and luxurious?\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: Well, I mean, I think that`s part of it. That`s certainly part of it. You know, there are so many great products out there for people to buy and sort of transform their backyard into this kind of like vacation paradise, you know.\par \par \uc2 If you weren`t able to get to the National Parks or make it to the camping site that you`re planning to bring your kids to. I mean, a good idea is, you know, buy a tent, put it up in your backyard.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am going to do that. I saw the picture. I was like, for sure. Yes, that`s an easy one.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: Yes, I mean, it`s really easy. And also what I think is really great about it is that you can still do it next year. You can have a camping trip next year, but this means you`re sort of giving your kids a bit of a primer into the experience.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like a warm up.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: A bit of a warm up. And also the kitchen facilities, the bathroom facilities are a little bit nicer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: Yes. And if you really want to up the ante, you can get out your fire pit. I mean, you can really go big.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so speaking of kitchens, I`m a foodie. So when most people go on vacations, they`re looking at the weather, the nearest beaches. For me, it`s all about the menus. So you`re saying there`s a way to bring the food that you like on vacation home?\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How?\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: And I think it actually goes two ways. I think you can bring the food you travel for home to you and I think you can also stay home and go a small distance and find the food that you would get on a plane for.\par \par \uc2 So my first tip, I love this. I don`t know if any of you have ever used Goldbelly.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Gold Belly Goldbelly is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Greatest. Greatest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is it? I don`t know what Goldbelly is.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: It`s essentially like a restaurant delivery from the entire country delivering nationwide. So let`s say you had planned this great getaway in the northeast. In New England, you couldn`t make it this summer.\par \par \uc2 You can go and get a great seafood feast clam chowder and lobster rolls from Legal Seafoods in Boston, and get it delivered to you in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Lou Malnati`s Chicago Pizza. Bring it here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My mind is blown right now.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: Chicago pizza. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: I mean, one thing I will say is obviously it`s not going to reach you in an hour. You need to plan in advance, but it can make you feel like you`ve traveled somewhere while you`ve been staying home.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Also, one of the things you said that I like, hotels, like find hotel deals maybe around you and that gives you that vacation.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: Yes. I mean, I think one of the most important things to making you feel like you`re properly on vacation is actually getting out of your environment. Even if you stay in your hometown, don`t stay at home.\par \par \uc2 It is really easy actually to find hotel deals in summer, you know, be flexible. You know, don`t think of a weekend as a Saturday and Sunday. Think of it as a Tuesday and Wednesday. You`re going to find great deals.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there are some hotels in New York City that are hundreds of dollars cheaper midweek.\par \par \uc2 Another tip I always tell people is get on the phone and talk to the hotel directly. You never know you might be able to negotiate when you get a human on the phone, instead of dealing with a third-party booking agent.\par \par \uc2 And finally, if you`re not in a city but you`re close by, consider going to a city for your holiday instead of to the coast or to the park because the city is empty out and the hotel rates drop with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: These are some good tips.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are some really good ideas here. I mean, just staying in your house. You spend so much time getting your house where you want it and then you leave it for downtime, and like you should spend your downtime in it.\par \par \uc2 You and your spouse can take turns handing each other warm towels.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: And you kind of owe it yourself, too. You know, like treat yourself a little bit even if you`re staying home.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. There is no place like. That`s what we say.\par \par \uc2 Erin Florio, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FLORIO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Friends always find a way to celebrate each other`s birthdays and when you put on a morning show with your friends, you find a way to work it into the end of the show.\par \par \uc2 Shawna Thomas.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And we should.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS (CBS MORNINGS executive producer): Hi.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: A little early, but we all know I`m going on vacation.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: For your birthday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We weren`t going to let you sneak away.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: I will not be watching this show tomorrow. And so Jon Tower, thank you for telling me to put on makeup.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, it says "Happy Birthday" a cake there, but we know you do not like cake.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: Yes. I do not like cake.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We`ve solved that problem.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: I mean, I like sugar in cake, but well --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do not let them eat cake.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You like pie.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: Pie.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Pie.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Happy Early Birthday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So make a wish.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Okay, yes. Make a wish.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think I know what the wish is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know what it is.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: I am just going to tell you. It`s for my flight to take off on time today.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Otherwise, you can have a staycation birthday.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: No.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Not happening.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Would you like to announce your new talk show with that microphone. You`re looking pretty good there.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: No, but I`d like to say thank you to all of you. I have the best jobs in the world and that`s because I get to work with you all. You all in the control room and the director and Patti, the stage manager. We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well done.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220707ei77000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For July 3, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jan Crawford, Debora Patta, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,209 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And on this Fourth of July weekend on Face the Nation, we will look at the challenges facing an increasingly divided America.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s message after a week of consultation with our closest allies on the issues threatening global stability: All is well with our friends. The biggest threat to the world view of America is from within.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): You haven`t found one person - - one world leader to say America is going backwards. America is better positioned to lead the world than we ever have been.\par \par \uc2 The one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior of the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He`s not the only one angry with the Supreme Court, as they conclude their most consequential session in years, with far-reaching decisions on religion, the environment, immigration and, of course, abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 He`s hoping Democrats channel their anger and fear into support for his party in the November midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I share the public outrage that this extremist court that is committed to moving America backwards.\par \par \uc2 But, ultimately, Congress is going to have to act to codify the -- Roe into federal law.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will assess the impact of these rulings.\par \par \uc2 The court did rule in favor of President Biden`s plan to scrap a Trump era policy to make asylum seekers wait in Mexico, clearing the way for more migrants to enter the U.S. We will talk with Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas about how the administration plans to handle the influx.\par \par \uc2 With rapid-fire local court decisions causing more chaos over where abortion is now illegal, we will look at the state of maternal health in America. How can we keep our abysmal record from getting worse?\par \par \uc2 Then: What`s next for the January 6 hearings following Trump White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson`s stunning testimony?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON (Former Aide to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 ): The president said something to the effect of: "I`m the effing president. Take me up to the Capitol now."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will talk with a key member of the January 6 Select Committee, Congressman Adam Schiff, about the attempts to influence witness testimony unfavorable to the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Plus, more of our CBS News exclusive interview with the new German chancellor, \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation. Thank you for joining us this holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 We begin today with immigration and the win for the Biden administration last week in the Supreme Court, that of the ending of President Trump`s remain-in-Mexico policy.\par \par \uc2 To discuss that and more, we want to welcome Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas to this broadcast.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, good morning to you. Happy early Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS (U.S. Secretary of Homeland Security): Good morning. And the same wish to you, Margaret. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, what happens now that remain-in-Mexico is going away? Are you ending this policy immediately? And what happens to those individuals in the encampments waiting right across the border?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Margaret, we were very pleased with the Supreme Court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 So, now, in light of the favorable Supreme Court ruling, we have to wait for that ruling to reach the district court that issued an injunction preventing us from ending remain-in-Mexico. So we have several weeks to go before the district court lifts its injunction.\par \par \uc2 And, until then, we are obligated by the district court`s ruling to continue to implement the remain-in-Mexico program. And we will do so in accordance with law.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, those people will still have to wait in the camps on the Mexican side of the border, but what happens to them next?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Right now, they do have to remain in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 And then we will actually continue with their immigration enforcement proceedings. Remember, when people are encountered at the border, they are just not merely released into the United States. They are placed in immigration enforcement proceedings. And that is what will occur with these people.\par \par \uc2 Their proceedings will continue in immigration court, where they will pursue their claims for asylum. And if those claims are unsuccessful, they will be swiftly removed from the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Reuters is reporting that there are right now thousands of people who departed on Friday and are moving towards the U.S. border.\par \par \uc2 What do you need right now? Do you need more personnel for customs and border control? Do you need more equipment to tackle these smugglers that are exploiting these people?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Margaret, we are working very closely with our partners to the south, with Mexico, that breaks up very often these caravans of individuals that seek to take that dangerous journey to reach our border, only to be met with the enforcement of our laws.\par \par \uc2 We have said repeatedly. And we continue to warn people not to take the dangerous journey. We saw so tragically in San Antonio, Texas, one of the possible tragic results of that dangerous journey. And so many people don`t even make it that far in the hands of exploitative smugglers.\par \par \uc2 And we continue to enforce immigration law, as is our legal responsibility.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are saying right now -- what I hear you saying is, do not come.\par \par \uc2 But those words are not being heard. People are moving right now. So the efforts to stop the root causes are not stopping them. This horrific trafficking, the worst smuggling tragedy in U.S. history this week, with those individuals found dead in that trailer truck, that`s not stopping people.\par \par \uc2 Are you predicting that this is only going to get more significant from here, that we`re going to go beyond the record surge in migrants?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: No, I`m not predicting that at all.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, in the wake of the San Antonio tragedy -- and our Homeland Security Investigations is the lead federal agency investigating what occurred and working with the United States Attorney`s Office in the prosecution of thus far four individuals who have been charged with that heinous crime.\par \par \uc2 We`re working with our partners to the south, because this is a regional challenge that requires a regional response.\par \par \uc2 I last week spoke...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But they got past U.S. -- the U.S. border officials.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Oh, so we have a multilayered approach. Margaret.\par \par \uc2 We, of course, have our inspections at the port of entry with our sophisticated nonintrusive technology. We then have checkpoints that are staffed 24 hours a day, seven days a week. The Laredo checkpoint in question, 10,000 to 14,000 vehicles pass through that checkpoint every day.\par \par \uc2 This fiscal year alone...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, how did this smuggler get these people across? Fifty-three people died.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: These are very sophisticated transnational criminal organizations.\par \par \uc2 They have evolved over the last 30 years. In the `90s, I prosecuted them, and they were much more rudimentary. Now they are very sophisticated, using technology. And they`re extraordinarily organized transnational criminal enterprises.\par \par \uc2 And we are much more sophisticated using technology and personnel 24 hours a day. You know, we have saved more than 10,000 individuals this fiscal year alone in more than 400 vehicle inspections.\par \par \uc2 So, can a truck get through, through sophisticated means? Sometimes, yes. But I have to say, we have interdicted more drugs at the ports of entry than ever before. We`ve rescued more migrants. We`re seeing a challenge that is really regional, hemispheric in scope, and we`re addressing it accordingly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mr. Secretary, I also want to ask you here at home about what we`ve seen in the past 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 There`s been this back-and-forth between state and federal law enforcement regarding security to Supreme Court justices and protests outside their home. Does the threat go beyond picketing? Is it specific and credible?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: So we have seen a heightened threat environment over the last several months over a number of different volatile issues that galvanize people on different sides of each issue.\par \par \uc2 We in the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  become involved when there`s a connectivity between the opposition to a particular view or an ideology of hate, a false narrative and violence.\par \par \uc2 It is that connectivity to violence when we engage. And we are very mindful that the Supreme Court`s decision in reversing and overturning Roe v. Wade has really heightened the threat environment. And we have deployed resources to ensure the safety and security of the Supreme Court and the justices.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before I let you go, I do want to ask you about what we saw this weekend up in Boston.\par \par \uc2 A white supremacist group called Patriot Front marched through that city. They recently planned events, a riot in Idaho. You`re seeing this far right group the Proud Boys also disrupt events in California.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are you right now about these militias?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Margaret, I have said -- and this has been echoed by the director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- that domestic violent extremism is one of the greatest terrorism-related threats that we face in the homeland today, individuals spurred by ideologies of hate, false narratives, personal grievances to acts of violence.\par \par \uc2 And it is that violence that we respond to and we seek to, of course, prevent. We are in a heightened threat environment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mr. Secretary, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined now by California Democratic Congressman Adam Schiff. He is a member of the January 6 Select Committee and chairman of the Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and good to have you here in person.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF (D-California): Good morning. Thank you. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to pick up on what we just heard from the secretary when we were talking about this far right group the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 This was one of the militias involved in January 6. And in this incredible testimony this past week from Cassidy Hutchinson, the former Trump White House aide, chief of -- to Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , she testified she heard conversations inside the White House about this far-right group and another one called the Oath Keepers.\par \par \uc2 Is there corroborating evidence to show that there was communication between those militias in the White House?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: I don`t want to get too far ahead of what we intend to present in our next hearings, but our very next hearing will be focused on the efforts to assemble that mob on the Mall, who was participating, who was financing it, how it was organized, including the participation of these white nationalist groups like the Proud Boys, the Three Percenters and others.\par \par \uc2 And so we`ll be presenting information we have. We haven`t answered all the questions that we have. We continue our investigation into precisely the issue you`re describing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Hutchinson was specific in saying \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , the president`s personal attorney, was someone she heard talking about them.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: Well, and this is one of the reasons we had interest in his testimony and have interest in the testimony of others.\par \par \uc2 We obviously want to probe any connection between these dangerous groups and the White House. I think we`ve gotten some answers, but there`s still a great deal we don`t know that we`re endeavoring to find out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s an incredible allegation, of course. So we`ll track that.\par \par \uc2 I want to also ask you, the vice chair of the committee, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , said not prosecuting former President Trump over the attack on the Capitol would be a much graver constitutional threat to the country than the political difficulties involved with bringing charges.\par \par \uc2 She said this in an ABC interview. She also said there are possible criminal referrals, not just one, but multiple. Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: I do. I do.\par \par \uc2 You know, for four years, the Justice Department took the position that you can`t indict a sitting president. If the Department were now to take the position that you can`t investigate or indict a former president, then a president becomes above the law. That`s a very dangerous idea that the founders would have never subscribed to, even more dangerous, I think, in the case of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is someone who has shown, when he`s not held accountable, he goes on to commit worse and worse abuses of power. So I agree with Judge Carter in California. I think there was evidence that the former president engaged in multiple violations of the law, and that should be investigated.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there will be a political calculus to this as well. This is an incredibly divided country right now.\par \par \uc2 Millions of people voted for the former president and still believe, wrongly, that he won the election. Prosecuting him, isn`t there a very high risk to that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: You know, it`s certainly not a step to be taken lightly at all.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, immunizing a former president who has engaged in wrongdoing, I would agree with our vice chair, I think is more dangerous than anything else. And the decision not to move forward with an investigation or not to move forward with a prosecution because of someone`s political status or political influence or because they have a following, to me, that is a far more dangerous thing to our Constitution than following the evidence wherever it leads, including when it leads to a former president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your colleague Adam Kinzinger was on another program this morning and said new witnesses have come forward since Cassidy Hutchinson testified.\par \par \uc2 How many? How significant? Is there more new information that requires more hearings?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: You know, I think there`s certainly more information that is coming forward.\par \par \uc2 In terms of whether that will materialize into particular witnesses on this topic or that topic, we`re going to wait and see. But we are following additional leads. I think those leads will lead to new testimony. It`s part of the reason we wanted her to come before the public, is, we were hoping it would generate others stepping forward, seeing her courage would inspire them to show the same kind of courage.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Has she inspired the former White House counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  to take up the request to speak to him again?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 We`re in discussions with Mr. Cipollone`s counsel. I`m hopeful that we can work out bringing him in for testimony. He clearly has information about concerns about criminal violations, concerns about the president going to the Capitol that day, concerns about the chief of staff having blood on his hands if they didn`t do more to stop that violent attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Hard to imagine someone more at the center of things. And I hope that he`ll demonstrate the same courage we saw Cassidy Hutchinson display.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Who is attempting to intimidate the witnesses, as Congresswoman Cheney said? And how significant are the security threats against Hutchinson?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: I have to imagine the security threats are very pronounced.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, our members are feeling them and hearing them. I have to expect the same is true of her, since the former president and his enablers are going after her. We want to make sure that she is safe. We have several concerns. We have the concern over safety for witnesses. We have concern over people who are trying to influence or intimidate witnesses.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Who`s doing that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: You know, I can`t comment on specific but...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you know?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: ... we wanted to let the country know and anyone in the former president`s world know that, if they seek to intimidate witnesses, they will be referred for prosecution. And we hope that Justice Department will move against them.\par \par \uc2 But we also have a concern about the fact that some of these witnesses are sharing lawyers, that, essentially -- and this gets to some the testimony revealed during the Cassidy Hutchinson hearing -- that they`re reviewing transcripts, that they`re essentially coordinating, potentially, their stories, or that witnesses feel they`ve got big brother watching them when they sit in for their depositions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, on that, I want to ask you.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that Cassidy Hutchinson described having been told by another individual is about this tussle in the Beast, the president`s vehicle, where he allegedly lunged for the wheel demanding to be taken to the Capitol. The committee`s already interviewed Tony Ornato, the White House operations director, and Secret Service agent Robert Engel.\par \par \uc2 Was this the first time you heard about that incident? Did they back that up or contradict that testimony?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: I can`t go into the specifics of the prior testimony.\par \par \uc2 But I can say I think we`d be interested in having them come back and others that can shed light on this. But the most important thing is there doesn`t appear to be any dispute over the fact the president was furious that he could not accompany this armed mob to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 That seems to be undisputed. And the fact that the president knew that the mob was armed, wanted the magnetometers down, so they could take their arms to the Capitol, that doesn`t seem to be disputed by anyone except \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And he has, as we`ve seen in the past, no credibility at all.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Quickly, before I let you go, with your \uc2 Intel\uc2  hat on here, the bullet that was used to kill American journalist Shireen Abu Akleh has been handed over to the United States.\par \par \uc2 It`s undergoing ballistics testing right now. If Israeli soldiers did indeed kill her, what consequences should there be?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: Well, I think there needs to be an independent investigation, so that we understand exactly what happened and who was responsible and why.\par \par \uc2 Once we know that, then I think we can talk about what the consequences should be. But I do think there needs to be an objective investigation. And I`m glad that the United States can help play a part in that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman Schiff, thanks for your time.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ADAM SCHIFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Supreme Court ended its term with a number of historic and some controversial rulings.\par \par \uc2 For a breakdown and a look at what comes next, we turn to CBS News chief legal correspondent Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 You have been incredibly busy, Jan.\par \par \uc2 We had a number of these decisions this week that reflect the will of the conservative majority. President Biden called them extremist -- an extremist court. How do you characterize the decisions?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, this is a court that has shown it has clearly turned firmly to the right.\par \par \uc2 And it could have sweeping implications for American life, the democratic process. Taken together, what we have seen this term is that this is a court that is not going to get involved in these divisive policy issues, unless that is clearly within its purview.\par \par \uc2 If it is not specifically mentioned in the Constitution, then that goes to the democratic process. That`s what we saw in the abortion rights case, overturning Roe vs. Wade, was not specifically mentioned; therefore, we`re not going to resolve it. Back to the democratic process.\par \par \uc2 And if Congress can`t do his job, and Congress isn`t acting, this is a court that says administrative agencies and unelected bureaucrats, they can`t jump in and fill those vacuums if Congress isn`t acting and try to set major policy questions. That was the case involving climate change and the \uc2 EPA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, the bottom line is, this court, unlike a more liberal court, is not jumping in to fill these vacuums where Congress or the legislatures are failing to act. And that is going to mean a profound difference in the democratic process and the rule of the courts -- the role of the courts.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because it`s not clear.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, it`s not clear if lawmakers will take up these issues.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: With dysfunction in Congress, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 But they`re saying the ball is in Congress` court or the state legislatures when it`s a policy dispute, if it`s not specifically addressed by the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, to that point, because now we are seeing all these states, their courts, their legislatures have arguments over what to do next, particularly on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky, Florida, Utah, Louisiana, really significant legal battles taking place about abortion protections. What is your takeaway so far? I mean, is there a commonality to where they`re ending up?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, remember, 26 states asked the Supreme Court to overturn Roe vs. Wade and let them set their own policies on abortion rights. And that`s what we`re seeing now, the court agreed. And we`re seeing that play out across the country. Already states, almost a dozen states have laws in place ready to go to completely ban abortion in their states.\par \par \uc2 So you`re seeing abortion rights advocates go into those states and file lawsuits in state courts under state constitutions, because the Supreme Court said, it`s not in the federal Constitution, but if a state has more protective rights in their constitutions, then work it out there.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s what we`re seeing now. We`re seeing these legal battles play out as -- the same time as the state legislatures are passing their own laws, blue states passing laws to enshrine abortion access or protect it even more, for example.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 And the chaos has led to some people mistakenly thinking abortion is banned or something. It`s not. It`s...\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: That`s been the most striking thing, I think.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court did not ban abortion nationwide. They just said there`s not a right to abortion in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Therefore, it goes back to the democratic process, and states can set their own policies. If Congress wants to do something, Congress can, but it`s not in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 So now you have seen this patchwork of laws. And you`re seeing legal challenges under state constitutions. But you`re going to see different laws in different states. If you`re in New York or California or Boston or Illinois or any of those Democratic states, this ruling will not affect your life at all.\par \par \uc2 Your -- the laws in your states won`t change. It`s those red states that we see that will try to ban or greatly restrict abortion...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will follow that.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: ... and already are seeing that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about what`s coming.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Oh, God.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why do you say, "Oh, God"?\par \par \uc2 That`s not a good reaction, Jan.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It`s -- as big of a term this was -- and, of course, whenever the court overturns a nearly 50-year-old precedent, as they did with Roe vs. Wade -- next term could be as consequential, divisive as this term, or more so.\par \par \uc2 They have major cases already on the docket. They will continue to add them throughout the year. They have already got a case challenging affirmative action in college admissions. I expect the court is going to end that.\par \par \uc2 They have got a case involving kind of a conflict between free speech and gay rights and whether a state law can prohibit someone from saying on their Web site they oppose same-sex marriage and don`t want to do that business in designers and artists.\par \par \uc2 They have got voting rights. They have got an election procedure case that could have huge implications for elections. I mean, this is just the beginning...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: ... and a new justice stepping into a divided court.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: An historic new justice.\par \par \uc2 Jan, you will be back with us a lot, I understand, based on what you just sketched out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you for your analysis.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ app.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Last week we traveled to Madrid to speak with the new chancellor of Germany, \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , during a week of international meetings among top U.S. allies who are faced with a growing list of problems, including rising food and energy prices exacerbated by Russia`s invasion of Ukraine. Chancellor Scholz is one of the few European leaders who still speaks to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . So that`s where we started our conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you speak to Putin, does he acknowledge the sanctions? Does he acknowledge how much his economy has been hurt? Or does he just not care?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ (Chancellor Of Germany): I think he cares, but he will not really admit it. So you get some idea -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because it hasn`t stopped him.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: You get some idea that it really is hurting him and that he understands the deep impacts of our sanctions on his economy. And I`m always mentioning it because it`s necessary to say it.\par \par \uc2 This is now happening to a country that is not that advanced, that is really needing all the technologies from the rest of the world for having a similar standard of living and for having the chance to be part of gross (ph) -- indeed world economy. And this is now the real damage to the Russian economy that they have no chance to do this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When will Putin run out of weapons, run out of funds, or can he -- this continue for years?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: No one really knows. He is - he is the head - the leader of a very great country with a lot of people living there, with a lot of means. And he is really doing this brutal war with - and -- and he prepared for it. So, he will be able to continue with the war really a long time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you believe that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will stop at Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: I (INAUDIBLE) that all what we do will help to give him the view that this is not working and that he will not be successful.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your country has earned this reputation of overpromising and underdelivering when it comes to Ukraine. Ukraine received its very first delivery of German howitzers, this artillery, last week. Why did it take that long? We`re in the fifth month.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: So, we took a very, very hard decision to change political strategies we followed for many decades, never to deliver weapons into a country that is in a conflict.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: When we decided, for instance, to send the most modern howitzer, which you can buy on the world market, which is in use in Germany, it was very difficult to organize that this could be used in the war because you have to have some training. And we had Ukrainian soldiers in Germany. And when the training ended, in the end they came with the weapon, with the howitzers to Ukraine, and they are now in use.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the United States is doing that. They`re providing weaponry within 48 hours sometimes of the president signing, and carrying out training. Why did it take this long for Germany?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: I think -- I think you should understand that there is a difference of a country like the United States spends that much for defense, which is a very big investment, and you have a lot of weapons at your stocks. Together with the United States and the United Kingdom, we decided to deliver multi-rocket launchers to Ukraine now, which are -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Those haven`t been delivered yet.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: We are sending them and we are doing it with the means and ways we have, and with the training. And once again, there are a lot of very experienced people who yesterday looked at \uc2 Google\uc2  and today they know how to do things.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you, there are weapons, but you have to have your training and you have to have it not in Ukraine, you have to have it here in our countries. And so we will always see that Germany is one of the countries that is doing the most, because what we are sending now is the most sophisticated technology you can use. There is also anti-ballistic -- there are also weapons we give to Ukraine that they can defend the air.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The anti-aircraft missiles you`ve promised? Radar you`ve promised?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: The anti - no, they can defend a city from -- against the rockets and missiles that were sent there from Putin. And this is very expensive and very effective technology, but they will get it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The delays have led to speculation that it`s not about getting the supplies, it`s about the will of the government to actually deliver them, and whether there`s fear of provoking Putin or whether it`s years of budget cuts to your defense industry that have made it just not possible for the German military to act quickly. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: Those who are looking to the facts see that we are doing what is feasible. We are changing the way, how we spend money for defense. And this is the big increase, which will change the situation and will give us the chance to be more quick in reaction to a threat that is coming to \uc2 NATO\uc2 , the alliance, or to our country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Germany is providing about $2 billion in aid to Ukraine. That`s roughly what you spend per month on gas from Russia, on coal, on energy supplies. So, while you`re helping the Ukrainians financially, you`re also essentially giving \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  a financial lifeline.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: He cannot buy anything from the money he`s getting from us because he has all these sanctions on imports for modern technologies and things he is looking for. So, this is what he`s making very angry.\par \par \uc2 But to be very clear, when we decided on sanctions together and with all our allies, we said always we will do it in a way that we harm Putin more than us. And many countries in Europe are depending, for historical reasons, and because they are near to the place, and it is the nearest place to get the gas on the imports of gas. And will now whole Europe is deciding to go out of this dependence. This will change the scenario even on the world market.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  can use that money elsewhere, just not in the west.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: But he cannot buy --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, is it still $2 billion a month that Germany is sending to Russia?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: It is always decreasing. And we draft the sanctions in the way that they hurt Putin. And this is what we do. And, once again, we are now doing real investments into technology and pipelines and ports. And I know there are people that sometimes think that when you are having - taking a decision one afternoon, the next morning you have a port and a 40 kilometers pipeline.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, it takes time.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: But in the real life this is not happening. When Europe is deciding to go out of the import of - of gas from Russia, this will have consequences.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It will have huge - I mean this is the equivalent of them declaring war on you, by cutting gas supplies to Germany.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: Hundred - hundred -- this is -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This isn`t just your choice. They`re using that as a weapon against you.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: This is, obviously, the case. And this is why I was starting to discuss the question what to do if the gas delivery will be reduced right when I entered office. We should be very much prepared that we will have high energy prices all over the world in all countries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So Germany`s heavy industry association, BDI, warned a halt in Russian gas deliveries would make recession inevitable.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: It is not -- it will be very tough if we will have a shortage of energy supplies. Obviously, all our countries, all our life is depending on the supply of energy. And, obviously, a lot of countries -- the most countries of the world are depending on the supply from (INAUDIBLE). And so we have to prepare for a difficult situation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is weaponizing inflation. He`s weaponizing food. Is he right to bet that he can fracture the western alliance by making it harder for Europeans and Americans and everyone else to afford food in these months ahead?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: You are very right. The shortages of food, many people in the world are seeing now as a threat to them, are the direct consequence of what Russia`s aggression against Ukraine and the war he is imposing on the country. You`re right that all the rising energy prices are also a direct consequence of his doing. And he is -- he is the one that is doing the wrong things. And we are always discussing this with our partners on the globe. We are starting an initiative to support countries that have not enough food with food.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you can`t reopen the Black Sea Ports, if Putin doesn`t agree to let the food out of Ukraine, how do you lower global food prices?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: We are now collecting money for supporting the poorest countries, that they will be able to deliver food to the people. And this is our international initiative. We organized together with others for food security, and we will continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it risks global instability.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: It is a real problem and it is a real consequence of Putin`s war. And this is why it is even more necessary that we support the people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, it also puts pressure to end this conflict sooner. What is your timeline for when this can end?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: The conflict will end when Putin understands that he will not be successful, tis idea to - just to conquer part of territory of his neighbor.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Members of the \uc2 German government\uc2  have admitted it was a mistake to be so dependent on Russia for so long.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: I think it was not right that we were not prepared to have at any time the chance to change the one that is delivering gas, oil and coal to us. So We should have invested all over Europe in infrastructure that gives us the ability to change the supply from one day to the other. And I think this is the lesson that has been learned in Europe and in many other places, that you have to prepare -- be prepared for a situation like this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden also talks about this potential conflict between democracies and autocracies. Is that the biggest threat on the horizon?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: We should be clear about the threats that are coming to our future, and this is coming from autocracies, yes, because they tend to be aggressive. And this is an aspect we should be very much aware of. And I am. And this is why I organized our meeting we had in Germany with the \uc2 G-7\uc2  group of democratic -- economically successful democratic states that we invite partners from all over the globe that are also democracies for making it happen that the democracies are strong.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And by strong, it also comes with 100,000 U.S. troops in Europe and 300,000 NATO response forces in Europe. This isn`t just diplomacy, this is muscle.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: This is. And it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mr. Chancellor, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our full interview is on our website and our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Leaders at the \uc2 G-7\uc2  last week pledged an additional $4.5 billion to address what the secretary general of the United Nations is calling an unprecedented global hunger crisis. But aid organizations are warning it won`t be enough, as Russia`s war in Ukraine severs supply lines and inflation continues raging.\par \par \uc2 CBS News foreign correspondent Debora Patta is in South Sudan with this report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice over): Hunger is a never-ending season in South Sudan. For three years, this country has been battered by one climate change shock after another. Apocalyptic flooding in the north, crippling drought in the southeast.\par \par \uc2 Millions were already starving. Then came the war in Ukraine, triggering the U.N.`s biggest humanitarian crisis this century as food and fuel prices soared, tipping this country over the edge.\par \par \uc2 In Guit (ph), floodwaters have still not receded.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (on camera): I`m standing in a place where people used to live. These were their homes. This was the land that they used to cultivate and live off. And now it`s completely submerged under water.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice over): Sarah Nyawal`s (ph) entire village is gone. She has nothing to eat but the water lilies she`s collecting.\par \par \uc2 In the community of Canal Pigi (ph), every child brought to nutritionist Mona Shaik (ph) during our visit was severely malnourished.\par \par \uc2 SARAH NYAWAL: I`m afraid, you know, any child like that, we are very close to losing them.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (on camera): Really?\par \par \uc2 SARAH NYAWAL: Within days.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice over): There was Nyanjima Gatlak (ph), who walked for over a month to get food for her weak and listless eight month old baby, Kang (ph). And Nyabany Kong (ph), who already lost one child to hunger and hasn`t eaten for two weeks. Her mother-in-law, Nakoni (ph), is wasting away.\par \par \uc2 Battling almost impossible odds, the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2  is doing its best. But since Russia invaded Ukraine, their costs have risen exponentially. WFP`s Marwa Awad (ph) says they`ve been forced to suspend aid to nearly 2 million of the 6 million people they feed here.\par \par \uc2 MARWA AWAD: We`re having to do humanitarian triage. This is the worst thing that any humanitarian or aid worker has to do. We must do something to help.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Subsistence (ph) farmer Nachapa Lamunia`s (ph) own mother starved to death. This was her last bag of food rations for the year. It will be finished in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Just tell the world we need food, she implored. And when you visit again, she said, we will smile and tell stories of how we survived and find ways to help others in need.\par \par \uc2 No one should die of hunger. There`s enough food to feed everyone on earth. The stories we heard continue to haunt us. The people of South Sudan, the world, must not forget.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Debora Patta reporting in South Sudan.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: According to the \uc2 Gates Foundation\uc2 , the maternal mortality rate is higher here than in any other developed country. And the elimination of federal protection for abortion rights only underscores that reality and the risks ahead.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Henning Tiemeier is the director of the Maternal Health Task Force at \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  and he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Doctor.\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER, M.D. (Maternal Health Task Force Director at \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 ): Hello, Margaret, and good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 I think this is incredibly important because I want to put the issue of abortion itself aside for a moment and talk about pregnancy in America as these states rewrite these laws.\par \par \uc2 So, how is it possible that in the richest country in the world we have the highest maternal mortality rate? And how do we stop it from getting worse?\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: Well, I have to say two things to that.\par \par \uc2 First of all, there seems to be an issue with the data. We think it`s higher than in other developed countries, so it is higher. But some of the uptick we`ve seen recently is partly due to poor data collection. So, that has been corrected, but it is higher.\par \par \uc2 So why is it higher? We think that has to do with the general health of women in America. So, it is a background risk. And it is partly due to poverty, to poor health care during pregnancy, and, importantly, poor care after pregnancy, after delivery.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It -- the mortality rate among black mothers is three times higher than white women. Why?\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: That is correct. It is much higher. It is substantially higher. And it is -- you must understand that there`s about 700 women dying during or after labor or in the first months after delivering. Seven hundred per year. And we know that most of these deaths are preventable.\par \par \uc2 And they, indeed, occur in minorities more often and in particular in black women. And why that is, is essentially one of the biggest challenges of public health. And we see that as the top of the iceberg of poor health in women and poor health in black women.\par \par \uc2 And there are several reasons that seems to go from poverty, to discrimination, to poor care for this group of women.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So according to the CDC, nearly 40 percent of all abortions performed in this country happen among black women. So, in laying out what you did, I would base the assumption that you are projecting that the death rate for these mothers will also climb?\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: I don`t think we have good projections in numbers at the moment because that will depend on many of the issues, actually, that you touched on before, on the legal issues, on the access to abortion in other states. But we know that abortion occurs in people of poverty and in minorities much more often. We know that they have difficulties to access abortion outside the state. So we think it will impact their physical and mental health.\par \par \uc2 How many deaths, nobody knows.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: It is very hard. It will - it will - it`s -- I wouldn`t want to quantify that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No.\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: I couldn`t put a number. It depends on so many other things. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you know, we looked at Medicaid coverage in this country. It covers about 40 percent of all births in the country. And the federal government is trying to get states to take more money to extend maternal health care so it`s not just cut off at two months, but it goes for longer so women can get pelvic exams and they can get other things after they give birth.\par \par \uc2 States like Mississippi aren`t doing that. What`s the consequence if you don`t have access to health care after two months?\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: So, what you`re pointing out now is one of the big issues and one of the things that could be addressed quickly. There are numerous states, Mississippi is one of them, but don`t forget Texas is another one, and that counts in big numbers, that have not expanded, as we say, Medicaid. They have not accepted the Affordable Care Act offer to expand health care to women in the first year.\par \par \uc2 And I would actually say it should go further than that in the first year after delivery. That means that you have very little right and very little coverage. So only the very, very poor in these states are covered, but a big number of poor women, of relatively poor, low income women, women that struggle to make the time and the money to be ensured, are not covered for things like mental health, physical checkups, even -- so they will not have the pelvic examinations that are needed, you are right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So America looks a lot different now than it did in 1973. Brookings says about 40 percent of U.S. households have women as the prime bread winner. So, I want to ask you how important it is, in your view, from a medical perspective, that women be able to take recovery time after childbirth, because, of course, as you know, in this country, there is no federal guarantee of paid family leave. So if these women have to work to support their family, their job`s in question, essentially, or at least being paid for it.\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: I think this is such an important issue. It`s, in a way, underrecognized. I know that the vice president addressed some of this. But it is very important to see that we need many measures to improve maternal health. One of them would be to improve prenatal care and the other is indeed to improve postnatal care, but also to support families. And it is in particular poor disadvantaged families, buying them time. So, giving them leave -- paid leave is very important because having a child is a stress on the system.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: Imagine you have three children, you have a fourth one, then you need - you know, if you`re making a minimum low (ph), you will not manage to - to make your ends meet.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: You will not find the time to breastfeed. We see that breastfeeding is - is not going up as we wished it would because of this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: So, I argue, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Doctor -\par \par \uc2 HENNING TIEMEIER: And many of my colleagues, that we need time. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we will continue to cover your research. Thank you. We`ll cover those issues on this program as well.\par \par \uc2 I have to leave it there, though.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Here in the nation`s capital, we are surrounded with reminders of the challenges our forefathers faced in times of great conflict here at home and abroad. There are tributes to those who fought for America`s freedom from tyranny, to those who led Americans through some of is darkest times in our 246-year-old history.\par \par \uc2 There are collections of the histories of oppressed minorities who fought for equal footing among their fellow Americans. And monuments to the titans who fought for equality and justice, a fight that continues to this very day.\par \par \uc2 Sprinkled throughout there are bits of wisdom from these giants. When we look at them today, one might think that maybe they knew where we were headed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANKLIN DELANO ROOSEVELT, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: They seek to establish systems of government based on the regimentation of all human beings by a handful of individual rulers call this a new order. It is not new and it is not order.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We can gather strength from looking back as we struggle to go forward.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan. Happy Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020220703ei7300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Held A Virtual Meeting With A Group Of Democratic Governors\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 558 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We move down to the fight over abortion rights. President Biden held a virtual meeting with a group of Democratic governors today to discuss efforts to protect access to reproductive health care for all women.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, doctors and patients are struggling to navigate the evolving legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voice-over): Today, President Biden doubled down, declaring that the legal fight over abortion rights is just beginning.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : And I share the public outrage at this extremist court that is committed to moving America backwards. This is not over.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: He met with nine Democratic governors from states where abortion is still legal.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): This is a frightening time for women all across our nation, a lot of fear and anxiety out there.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Stand up! Fight back!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Confusion and uncertainty has spread now that abortion is up to the states. Abortions halted in Wisconsin after the state reverted to an 1849 ban, a law the state`s attorney general is trying to undo.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATHY KING (Medical Director, Planned Parenthood of Wisconsin): Criminalizing abortion, as we know, historically, has never stopped people from needing and wanting access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Abortions did resume in Kentucky today after a judge there granted a request by the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  for a temporary suspension. Abortion opponents want the ban upheld.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WALLS (Executive Director, The Family Foundation): Lives are being saved here in the commonwealth. And anything that undoes that would do irreparable harm.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Of the 26 states that have or are set to ban or severely restrict abortion, judges in five, including Kentucky, have temporarily allowed abortions to continue while lawsuits play out.\par \par \uc2 A Florida ban set to take effect today on abortions after 15 weeks was temporarily blocked by a judge, who said it violated state privacy protections.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Don`t like abortions...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Don`t get one!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The judge`s ruling doesn`t take effect until he signs a written order after the holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 Do providers like Planned Parenthood feel like they have whiplash with the laws changing almost day by day?\par \par \uc2 LAURA GOODHUE (Executive Director, The Florida Alliance of Planned Parenthood Affiliates): They feel whiplash, but they just are so frustrated. They feel the anxiety, the same anxiety that our patients are feeling.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: While these lawsuits play out on the state level, nationwide, women are crossing state lines to places like here in Illinois, where abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Planned Parenthood of Illinois tells us that they used to schedule about 100 out-of-state patients for abortions here per month, but, in just the last week since Roe has been overturned, they have scheduled 750 -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Adriana Diaz, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220705ei710002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson On State`s Near-Total Abortion Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed O`Keefe, Nate Burleson, Gayle King \par \uc2 1,594 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, we begin this hour with the growing political and legal fallout from the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 As a result of that ruling, abortion bans are enacted, expected, or likely to be enacted in at least 20 states.\par \par \uc2 In Arkansas, a trigger law immediately banned nearly all abortions. There are no exceptions for minors, or in cases of rape or incest. The only exception is if the mother`s life is at risk.\par \par \uc2 That law was signed in 2019 by Governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , who joins us this morning from Little Rock.\par \par \uc2 Governor, it is good to see you. Happy Friday. Happy Independence Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Thank you. We`re excited about the weekend and it is good to be with you today.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, thank you again. So your State has given about a million dollars to pregnancy centers, but that`s a fraction of what`s likely going to be needed for the thousands of unwanted pregnancies that are going to occur each year now.\par \par \uc2 What else might you and your State Legislature be planning to do to help young mothers and families?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, it`s an important question and we are addressing it.\par \par \uc2 First of all, we started this long before. You know, we took Medicaid expansion in Arkansas that provided health coverage for the working poor. We`re trying to improve our healthcare for maternal moms for prenatal care.\par \par \uc2 But what we do now and you start with the fact that last year, we had about 3,000 abortions in Arkansas. And so under the new law that the Supreme Court allowed to take effect, that means that a significant number of those will be reduced. Some of those moms will put the child up for adoption. So we`re going to have to increase our adoption services.\par \par \uc2 Others will go out of state, which I think will be a small minority, but there`ll be many that will carry that child to term and to keep that child and so we want to make sure that we have the wraparound services for that mom, both during the pregnancy, but also afterwards.\par \par \uc2 So we`re looking as to how we can beef that up. I`ve charged my Health Department and Human Services to come up with more funding for each of those needs that we see as a result of this.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So we should anticipate more funding for psychological issues potentially stemming from unwanted births or physical issues with pregnancies and births?\par \par \uc2 And also there`s this report now that at least two Arkansas State Senators say they`re looking to introduce legislation to ban women from traveling out of state to get an abortion, is that a law you would sign?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): I would not.\par \par \uc2 The challenge there is that that would be in violation of interstate commerce. Obviously, we want to discourage that because the public policy in Arkansas is to limit abortions and to carry out the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 And so, we recognize that that is something that even the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  addressed in one of the concurring opinions that this is not intended to restrict interstate commerce.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decision said each State is going to make its own decisions in this, which is traditional public health matters. Arkansas has made its decision, other States might look at it differently.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Governor, over the last few weeks, we have learned more details about January 6th and Trump`s role on the violence that the nation witnessed that day.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , called Trump "domestic threat we have never seen before." Do you agree? Do you believe that he is a danger to this nation?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, as you can see from the testimony on January 6th, and subsequent to the election where he was challenging the legality of it, the lawful transfer of party, yes, that was a threat to our democracy, that was a threat to our institutions of government, and that`s not the behavior we want to see in a responsible President.\par \par \uc2 I think the question for the January 6 Committee is one, you get facts out to the public, which is important to know. Secondly, they`re trying to make a case that there`s criminal conduct on behalf of the President, I question whether they have made that case, I don`t believe they have. I don`t know whether they can.\par \par \uc2 But the Justice Department is watching that, and we`ll see how that develops. But at this point, they have not made that criminal conduct case in my judgment.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But you agree with the Congresswoman that he is a danger to the nation. That`s what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, everybody phrases a different way. I would not be supporting him for 2024. He acted irresponsibly. During that time, he was a risk to the nation, absolutely. So that`s my statement on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you make of Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony this week?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Very compelling. I mean, anyone who appreciates the difficulty of someone being 25 and thrust into that incredible closeness to history, and what even in in her young age understood to be fundamentally wrong, and she testified to it.\par \par \uc2 So, my heart goes out to those that really stood firm during that time, and I think she clearly demonstrated her concern and love for our country. I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She did. Did you believe her, Governor? Did you believe her? Did you believe what she told us?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): I did. She came across with a great deal of credibility. But what is a challenge with the Committee is that they`re not having cross examination. They`re not having other points of view, and so that makes it more difficult.\par \par \uc2 I`m a lawyer, I`m used to the adversary system where you have different points of view presented and that was eliminated.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, they got into hearsay, and that`s where if they can`t back that up, it undermines that testimony. So, I want them to be careful, the Committee, that they present this in as fair manner as they can, and make sure they really have the facts as they present it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Many agree with you on that point. But I want to get to 2024 because you have said out loud that you`re considering a presidential bid in 2024 and President Trump will not -- former President Trump will not be a factor in your decision.\par \par \uc2 Most people don`t say that out loud. What`s your thinking here?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, I answer questions, honestly, and while we`re totally focused on 2022, obviously, there`s talk about 2024 and I had to make it clear that Trump has disqualified himself in my judgment from his actions on January 6 and leading up to that.\par \par \uc2 And so we have to go a different direction for our country for my party, and so I want to be a voice for commonsense conservatism and we`ll see how that resonates, but let`s get through 2022. We`ve got a very important election ahead.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, Governor, but it sounds like you`re considering challenging him if he decides to run in 2024. You said you answer the questions that you`re asked. Would you? Would you run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, I`m -- what I`m doing is consistent with that. I haven`t made a commitment to it. I haven`t made a decision on it. But I`m laying the foundation for that. If For that is something that`s going to be helpful to our country, so we`re looking at it, but no decision will be made until after this election in 2022.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, we look forward to your announcement right here at the table at CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got a seat right over there on the left waiting for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will keep it warm for you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We look forward to seeing you again. Thank you so much, Governor for coming on again. We always like to see you.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Always good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up. The need for -- thanks.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the need for speed on the racetrack. We`re talking about pioneering driver, her name is Hailie Deegan. Remember that -- there she is. Hey, Hailie. She is standing by with her car to talk about what oh inspires --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: There we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What inspires her to drive and preview this weekend`s race. You go, Hailee.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220705ei71000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Court Issues Decision On \uc2 EPA\uc2  And Immigration Swears In Justice Jackson\b0\par\par\uc2 ED O`KEEFE, JAN CRAWFORD \par \uc2 1,040 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The Supreme Court has wrapped up its term with two more major decisions on the environment and immigration and Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  is now on the bench as the first Black woman to serve on the nation`s Highest Court.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court this morning where the Justices are already looking forward to this fall -- Jan.\par \par \uc2 Jan, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, Ed, this was one of the most momentous terms in the Supreme Court`s history. It had those rulings on abortion, gun, climate change, school prayer, and now we have a new Justice, Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , she is going to be stepping onto a divided Court that`s going to be taking up more contentious cases next fall.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2  (\uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 ): I, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  do solemnly swear.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In a private ceremony with the Justices and her family, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  made history -- the 116th Associate Justice and first Black female on the nation`s Highest Court.\par \par \uc2 It was a moment of hope and collegiality to add in a historic and divisive term up to the very end.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Rise up Like That.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: On Thursday, the Court limited the EPA`s authority to regulate greenhouse gases from power plants, saying it exceeded its power under the 1970 Clean Air Act, which was written before climate change was an issue.\par \par \uc2 The ruling puts the ball in Congress` court to update the 52-year-old law. It is a setback for the Biden administration`s efforts to tackle climate change, but top officials vow to fight on.\par \par \uc2 GINA MCCARTHY (White House National Climate adviser): This is about protecting public health. This is about advancing our interests of our families and our workers.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to keep moving forward, even though the Supreme Court would like to look backwards and hold us back.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In a separate opinion, the High Court Thursday sided with the Biden administration, allowing it to end the Trump-era policy of requiring asylum seekers at the Southern border to remain in Mexico, while their claims were decided.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Court`s latest term saw a blockbuster ruling overturning Roe versus Wade and letting States set their own abortion policies.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A decision that sparked protests across the country as politicians and State Courts scrambled to respond.\par \par \uc2 The decision along with other rulings this term on school prayer, guns, and climate change shows a Court that is firmly turned to the right, with six conservatives and three liberals.\par \par \uc2 In replacing liberal Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , Jackson will not change the balance of the Court.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But there is a saying that a new Justice makes a new Court, so she is certain to have an impact and we`ll see that beginning in the fall when the Court takes up other cases, including affirmative action, voting rights, gay rights, and election procedures -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I watched the swearing in ceremony. It was really a wonderful thing to see. Thank you very much, Jan.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are pushing back against President Biden`s efforts to defend abortion rights after the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference overseas, Mr. Biden called the Court`s ruling outrageous and supported temporarily changing Senate filibuster rules to ensure the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is at the White House with more on the growing political battle.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Realistically, what can the President do at this point?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 At this moment on this issue, the President doesn`t have many tools at his disposal. So he`ll use the platform, the pulpit to try to rally some support and maybe rally some voters. He has an early afternoon meeting with seven Governors, mostly from states led by Democrats. In fact, all of them States led by Democrats who`ve moved to protect abortion rights/\par \par \uc2 The White House hoping to be seen -- having the President seen next to state leaders who are able to move on this issue, and the President strongly urging Congress to federally codify abortion rights and to scrap the Senate filibuster if needed to do so, allows this bill, an abortion rights bill to pass with 51 votes instead of 60.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is, he doesn`t have the votes. There are hold out Democratic senators who he has not been able to budge and have said they will be intransigent on the filibuster. So, he can ask the Senate to try to force this vote, try to change their minds and change their votes.\par \par \uc2 But even if it loses, channel the anger of voters, mobilize them ahead of November -- Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s right, Scott. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 He makes a good point that this is -- his hands are tied. We are in a system with three co-equal branches of government. He essentially needs to convince Congress to cooperate and work with them, but there are two Democrats who say I don`t want to change the filibuster for anything.\par \par \uc2 So, the President`s answer is elect more Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Ed, have you ever seen when you strongly urge Congress -- to use Scott`s phrase -- is that effective?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: No. And the former senator turned President knows that, but this is designed to get people motivated going into November and get people reasonable for Democrats and we`ll see if it works.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because right now, they don`t have the votes.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220705ei710005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Holiday Travel Headache; Holiday Weather Forecast; SCOTUS Ends Term with Big Changes; Biden Moves to Protect Abortion Rights; Russia Attack on Odessa Kills 19; Pressure on Putin; Brittney Griner Stands Trial; Calls for Arrest in Emmett Till Death; America`s Unsolved Murders; Nationwide Car Theft Concerns; The New Post-Roe Era; First Woman of SRX; Sweet Surprise; David Harbour`s Strange Journey, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Ed O`Keefe, Errol Barnett, Jan  Crawford, Scott MacFarlane, Margaret Brennan, Charlie D`Agata, Jim Axelrod,  Anthony Mason, Christina Ruffini, Carter Evans \par \uc2 13,721 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Fri-yay. Happy Friday to you. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And I`m Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re in good hands. Ed is here, Tony is off today. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Airline delays and cancellations pile up as millions take to the sky this holiday weekend. What you need to know before going to the airport.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Airlines are blaming the \uc2 FAA\uc2  for air traffic control problems. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is blaming the airlines for pilot shortages. The pilots are blaming everybody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Supreme Court faces new controversy after it limits the EPA`s power to fight climate change.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): The Supreme Court is on an absolute reactionary rampage.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Judges in Kentucky and Florida block new abortion limits since the end of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President Biden says the Senate should drop the filibuster to respond to the Supreme Court`s abortion ruling.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): We have to codify Roe v. Wade in the law.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: WNBA star, Brittney Griner is set to go on trial today in Russia on drug smuggling charges.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Fast ball. We`re out of here.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Cleveland with their second consecutive walk off win.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The historic swearing in. \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  becoming the first Black woman to serve on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show With Stephen Colbert" host): Justice Jackson credits three factors for success: Hard work, big breaks and tough skin, as opposed to the three factors of \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  success.\par \par \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  (US Supreme Court): Beer.\par \par \uc2 Beer.\par \par \uc2 Too many beers.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Come in.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show with James Corden" host): Mr. President --\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : How are you?\par \par \uc2 MAN: James Corden stopped by the White House.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Who was the guy?\par \par \uc2 MAN: He is from "The Late, Late Show," sir.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : What the hell is "The Late, Late Show?"\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: This is both a snack and a gift.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Corden offered a spruce up to the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Try those.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Mr. President. Who is Goose and who`s Maverick?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : You be my wing man.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: James Corden has fun wherever he goes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The more I see him, the more I realize we`re going to miss him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to miss him a lot.\par \par \uc2 What do you think Joe Biden thought, Ed, when he comes walking in the snack --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Who the heck is that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, okay. Who is that guy?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know we`re not thinking that about you, Ed. Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. We`re very glad Ed O`Keefe is at the table today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The best in the business.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got a big show for you today in addition to Ed O`Keefe, we`ve got more of our special CBS News series. We`re calling it "Crime Without Punishment."\par \par \uc2 The City of Baltimore believes it has answer to the rising number of murder cases where no one is arrested. \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  will show how working with Federal and State officials is boosting police department that was strongly criticized for years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, Anthony Mason talks with "Stranger Things" star, David Harbour. Millions of fans have been waiting for this season`s big finale. He tells us why viewers love his character and how a serious setback at the start of his career made him a better actor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that show, love him. Can`t wait to see that.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we want to get straight to July Fourth travel and the trouble that millions of Americans can expect this weekend. AAA predicts around 48 million people will travel for this holiday. That`s just one million shy of pre pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 Now that spike in demand seems to be overwhelming the airlines and the airports, more than 20,000 flights -- 20,000 -- have been delayed this week alone. That`s a lot of people stranded and a little irritated.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, gas prices are so close to record highs averaging just under $5.00 a gallon. Errol Barnett is standing above Interstate 395, yes, he is on overpass in downtown Washington.\par \par \uc2 Ed -- I mean, Errol. See, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Sorry about that, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: See. Errol, I know who you are. Errol, it`s really good to see you. How is it looking there?\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Hey, look, Ed O`Keefe not a bad person to be mistaken for.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you this, Gayle, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 It is busy, busy, busy out here on the roads. Thankfully, though, you see traffic behind me is moving. But AAA estimates that in many metropolitan cities around the country, folks will be trying to get out of work early this afternoon. So, you`ll see peak congestion this afternoon in LA, Chicago, Seattle, and Houston.\par \par \uc2 It`s also the busiest day for air travel this weekend, but as we`ve been reporting, these flight disruptions are really frustrating passengers and pilots alike.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The last flight, more crowded.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And people are testy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Our flight is delayed and so basically, we`re going to miss our second flight.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The planes are full, absolutely full.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: As Americans face hundreds of flight cancellations and thousands of delays, \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  staffers are voicing their own frustration.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ADLER (\uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  pilot): We want to make sure that every Delta passenger gets to every destination in the safest most on-time fashion as possible.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Off-duty pilots hit the picket line nationwide Thursday demanding wage hikes and their ballooning work hours and rampant service disruptions.\par \par \uc2 ANDY GROSS (AAA spokesperson): Typically, flying accounts for about eight to nine percent of the folks traveling. This year, it is only seven percent.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: AAA spokesman, Andy Gross tells CBS News, travel headaches and skyrocketing prices, are likely deterring Americans from flying.\par \par \uc2 The average lowest cost for airfare is up 14 percent from last year at $201.00 per ticket.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the number of Americans expected to drive for the holiday, 42 million is an all-time record.\par \par \uc2 What`s the headline for your Fourth of July forecast and what it tells us about where we are as we move through the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 ANDY GROSS: It appears that the urge to get out and go is overwhelming these high prices for everything.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Aaron Lyon (sp?), his wife Liz, and their nine-year-old daughter, Angelina are in the middle of a nearly two-week long road trip. The Montana natives are headed for Mount Rushmore and say the drive is worth the price.\par \par \uc2 LIZ LYON (Traveler): Family time is more important making memories and taking her out to see the country especially on Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Now, it is worth noting that gas prices have pulled back from their $5.00 record highs last month. That`s welcome news for drivers and yesterday, Delta`s CEO issued an apology for what he admits is unacceptable cancellations and delays.\par \par \uc2 Still, the airline has reduced its daily departures by about 100 starting today. So, a lot of excitement about the weekend and anxiety about what may go wrong -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Prepare for some delays and have some patience. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Errol, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is already warning of possible weather delays in more than a dozen airports today. Let`s bring in meteorologist, Jen Carfagno from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  with a look at the getaway forecast.\par \par \uc2 Jen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It looks like it`s going to be getting worse from here. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 JEN CARFAGNO (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Well, it will get worse and then it will get better. So, that`s some good news, right, Nate?\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re going to be watching the weather at a number of airports today. Everything you have all just talked about what the volume when you combine that with thunderstorms, it does mean delays and we`ve got airports across the southeast with likely storms and delays. In some cases, heavy rainfall which for all the folks driving could be a concern.\par \par \uc2 Houston has been looking at big rain lately. This is because of something that had potential for tropical development. It doesn`t have that potential anymore, but it does bring the rain chances to Louisiana especially as we get through the next 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 A little bit of rain left into Houston, the biggest rain, the possible flash flooding in southern Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 The nationwide across the country today, we`ve got the chance of storms in the Southeast. We do have the heat building in the Northeast. That will be fueling the threat for thunderstorms that could bring severe weather.\par \par \uc2 So this is where it gets worse before it gets better weather-wise, expecting a lot of airport disrupting weather as we get you into your Saturday. And of course we`ll keep it going through the night, better weather ahead for Sunday and then into Monday for the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Nate, you can always watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable and now live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 You have a Happy Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As long as we`re good for the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 Jen Carfagno, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The Supreme Court has wrapped up its term with two more major decisions on the environment and immigration and Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  is now on the bench as the first Black woman to serve on the nation`s Highest Court.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court this morning where the Justices are already looking forward to this fall -- Jan.\par \par \uc2 Jan, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, Ed, this was one of the most momentous terms in the Supreme Court`s history. It had those rulings on abortion, gun, climate change, school prayer, and now we have a new Justice, Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , she is going to be stepping onto a divided Court that`s going to be taking up more contentious cases next fall.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2  (\uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 ): I, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  do solemnly swear.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In a private ceremony with the Justices and her family, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  made history -- the 116th Associate Justice and first Black female on the nation`s Highest Court.\par \par \uc2 It was a moment of hope and collegiality to add in a historic and divisive term up to the very end.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Rise up Like That.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: On Thursday, the Court limited the EPA`s authority to regulate greenhouse gases from power plants, saying it exceeded its power under the 1970 Clean Air Act, which was written before climate change was an issue.\par \par \uc2 The ruling puts the ball in Congress` court to update the 52-year-old law. It is a setback for the Biden administration`s efforts to tackle climate change, but top officials vow to fight on.\par \par \uc2 GINA MCCARTHY (White House National Climate adviser): This is about protecting public health. This is about advancing our interests of our families and our workers.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to keep moving forward, even though the Supreme Court would like to look backwards and hold us back.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In a separate opinion, the High Court Thursday sided with the Biden administration, allowing it to end the Trump-era policy of requiring asylum seekers at the Southern border to remain in Mexico, while their claims were decided.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Court`s latest term saw a blockbuster ruling overturning Roe versus Wade and letting States set their own abortion policies.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A decision that sparked protests across the country as politicians and State Courts scrambled to respond.\par \par \uc2 The decision along with other rulings this term on school prayer, guns, and climate change shows a Court that is firmly turned to the right, with six conservatives and three liberals.\par \par \uc2 In replacing liberal Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , Jackson will not change the balance of the Court.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But there is a saying that a new Justice makes a new Court, so she is certain to have an impact and we`ll see that beginning in the fall when the Court takes up other cases, including affirmative action, voting rights, gay rights, and election procedures -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I watched the swearing in ceremony. It was really a wonderful thing to see. Thank you very much, Jan.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are pushing back against President Biden`s efforts to defend abortion rights after the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference overseas, Mr. Biden called the Court`s ruling outrageous and supported temporarily changing Senate filibuster rules to ensure the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is at the White House with more on the growing political battle.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Realistically, what can the President do at this point?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 At this moment on this issue, the President doesn`t have many tools at his disposal. So he`ll use the platform, the pulpit to try to rally some support and maybe rally some voters. He has an early afternoon meeting with seven Governors, mostly from states led by Democrats. In fact, all of them States led by Democrats who`ve moved to protect abortion rights/\par \par \uc2 The White House hoping to be seen -- having the President seen next to state leaders who are able to move on this issue, and the President strongly urging Congress to federally codify abortion rights and to scrap the Senate filibuster if needed to do so, allows this bill, an abortion rights bill to pass with 51 votes instead of 60.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is, he doesn`t have the votes. There are hold out Democratic senators who he has not been able to budge and have said they will be intransigent on the filibuster. So, he can ask the Senate to try to force this vote, try to change their minds and change their votes.\par \par \uc2 But even if it loses, channel the anger of voters, mobilize them ahead of November -- Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s right, Scott. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 He makes a good point that this is -- his hands are tied. We are in a system with three co-equal branches of government. He essentially needs to convince Congress to cooperate and work with them, but there are two Democrats who say I don`t want to change the filibuster for anything.\par \par \uc2 So, the President`s answer is elect more Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Ed, have you ever seen when you strongly urge Congress -- to use Scott`s phrase -- is that effective?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: No. And the former senator turned President knows that, but this is designed to get people motivated going into November and get people reasonable for Democrats and we`ll see if it works.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because right now, they don`t have the votes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Turning next to the war in Ukraine, Russian missiles struck an apartment building and resort near the City of Odessa this morning, killing at least 19 people according to local officials. Rescue crews are working to find anyone who might be trapped in the wreckage.\par \par \uc2 The attack comes a day after a NATO Summit ended in Spain with new pressure on Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries pledged to further aid to Ukraine, more sanctions against Russia, and a buildup of military forces in Europe.\par \par \uc2 "Face the Nation" moderator and chief foreign affairs correspondent, Margaret Brennan spoke exclusively with German Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  in Madrid, about how he is criticizing Putin, yet, still spending billions of dollars to Russia.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Germany is providing about two billion in aid to Ukraine. That`s roughly what you spend per month on gas from Russia, on coal, on energy supplies. So, while you`re helping the Ukrainians financially, you`re also essentially giving \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  a financial lifeline.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ (German Chancellor): He cannot buy anything from the money he is getting from us because he has all these sanctions on imports for modern technologies and things he is looking for. So this is what is making him very angry.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  can use that money elsewhere, just not in the West.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: He cannot buy --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, is it still two billion a month that Germany is sending to Russia?\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: It is always decreasing and I once again say that we decided that we do the sanctions -- that we draft the sanctions in the way that they have Putin and this is what we do.\par \par \uc2 And once again, we are now doing real investments into technology, in pipelines, in ports, and I know that the people that sometimes think that when you are having taken a decision one afternoon, the next morning you have a port and 40 kilometers of pipeline.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It takes time.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: But in the real life, this is not happening.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You can see more of Margaret`s exclusive interview with the German Chancellor on the only Sunday political talk show worth your time, "Face the Nation" with Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: WNBA star, Brittney Griner is on trial this morning in a courtroom in Russia. The two-time Olympic gold medalist faces drug charges. If convicted, she could face 10 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 US officials say she is wrongfully detained. Charlie D`Agata is monitoring this trial from London.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Court officials tell CBS News this morning, it is unclear how long the trial will take, it could be weeks or months, but a conviction against Griner is all but a slam dunk. Russian Courts have a less than one percent acquittal rate.\par \par \uc2 A very brief appearance of Brittney Griner led in handcuffs to the courtroom this morning. Today, the Judge probes the merits of the case and prosecutors lay out the charges, smuggling and transportation of cannabis.\par \par \uc2 The WNBA star was arrested in February allegedly caught with vape cartridges with traces of cannabis in her luggage. The State Department says Griner has been wrongfully detained and it is actively engaged in trying to get her home.\par \par \uc2 There is growing speculation the Kremlin plans to use Griner in a potential prisoner swap for Russian arms dealer, Viktor Bout, known as the Merchant of Death, currently serving a 25-year sentence in a Federal prison.\par \par \uc2 Now, our colleagues in Moscow confirm representatives of the US Embassy have arrived this morning and they will be attending the trial. Griner`s wife, other family members are not allowed -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a heartbreaking case and we are all watching. Thank you very much, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 A startling discovery might reopen the infamous 1955 lynching case of Emmett Till. Searchers have found the original unserved warrant for Carolyn Bryant Donham charging her in Emmett`s kidnapping as Mrs. Roy Bryant at the time.\par \par \uc2 The local Sheriff in Mississippi never arrested her. Donham accused the 14- year-old of touching and whistling at her. Her then husband and his half- brother were acquitted by an all-White jury, and then told a magazine reporter that they did it.\par \par \uc2 Donham is now in her 80s, the only suspect from the case who is still alive. The Till family wants her to be arrested. Emmett`s cousin, Wheeler Parker, Jr. was with him the night he was kidnapped. He told us that he wants justice, but he does not think that a warrant will lead to a conviction.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WHEELER PARKER, JR. (Emmett Till`s cousin): And you feel so helpless about what is going on and happening and hoping that you can get some justice, but we certainly did not get it from the Court. We did not get it from America, and we still might get it even now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Donham has made no public comment about this warrant.\par \par \uc2 You can feel the family`s frustration, but the woman in the case is now 87- 88. I don`t really see this going anywhere, but we`ll see. But I still applaud Emmett Till`s mother, who made the decision to let the public see what had happened to her son. As ugly and is horrible as it was, she wanted us all to see it and because of that, it became a game changer in the Civil Rights movement.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I know some people may say, well, this happened you know, 70 or 80 years ago, but the pain never goes away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The pain doesn`t go away.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the more details that you learn about what happened, the more frustrated you become.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The more infuriating it is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really is.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Coming up in our series "Crime Without Punishment." We take you to Baltimore to see how an innovative partnership can be a model for other cities trying to stop the surge in unsolved murders.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, actor David -- there is the music -- actor, David Harbour talks with Anthony Mason about his character`s role in the season finale of "Stranger Things."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR ("Stranger Things" actor): They are some of the best, most epic stuff we have ever shot and also, at least for Hopper, some of the most intimate stuff I`ve ever gotten to do on the show. It`s an exciting day for \uc2 Netflix\uc2  fans.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you see the color that seafoam green phone? If you love Hopper, you won`t want to miss this.\par \par \uc2 Your local news, coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. We continue our special series. We call it "Crime Without Punishment" looking at the disturbing rise in unsolved murder cases in this country.\par \par \uc2 We`ve shown why some big city police departments cannot keep pace with the rising number of killings, and how it has made the families of the murder victims very frustrated and very angry.\par \par \uc2 This morning we look at a potential solution of Federal, State and local partnership that could be -- we say could be -- a model for other cities.\par \par \uc2 Chief investigative correspondent, that`s \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2 , went to Baltimore to learn exactly how it works.\par \par \uc2 They have some history in doing this kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : They do.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: What we kept hearing from Police Chief after Police Chief, detective after detective in our collaborative investigation done by CBS News and local \uc2 CBS\uc2  stations around the country were descriptions of a crippling shortage of manpower and resources.\par \par \uc2 In Baltimore, we found an approach that seems to be gaining some traction in addressing these shortages and solving more murders -- teamwork.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: His killer was standing on that end.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: So far this week, we`ve documented a troubling trend.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: More people getting away with murder.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: The rate of making arrests in murder cases is at a 50-year low.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Baltimore Police are pleading for help as they work to solve 16 shootings and 28 armed robberies.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Baltimore is no different where police solve less than half of the homicides. Except here --\par \par \uc2 EREK BARRON (Maryland US Attorney): Plenty of room, big space.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: They just might be finding a solution to this disturbing problem.\par \par \uc2 EREK BARRON: There it is.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: It`s an approach overseen by Erek Barron, the US Attorney in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Barron hopes to boost the rate of homicide arrests with more intelligence developed from a partnership housed in a nondescript warehouse in a secured location we`ve been asked to conceal.\par \par \uc2 EREK BARRON: This is the Organized Crime Drug Enforcement Task Force.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Good morning. You had a couple of search warrants you did.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: A strike force of local and Federal authorities taking aim at Baltimore`s violent crime.\par \par \uc2 EREK BARRON: The law enforcement community is so Balkanized. You`ve got local, State, Federal. Here is where we can put all or most of them together in one place, sharing information, working together.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Sixteen people, you heard that right, shot over the weekend alone.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : We visited after a violent Father`s Day weekend added six new homicides to the city`s total.\par \par \uc2 BILLY NICKOLES (US Attorney`s Office investigative analyst): So this is a map that we generated with all the homicides and shootings in 2021 in Baltimore City.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Intelligence analyst Billy Nickoles is the dot connector in chief here. At least 338 homicides in red last year alone.\par \par \uc2 They are in every part --\par \par \uc2 BILLY NICKOLES: Every portion of the city.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Each morning, Nickoles opens an e-mail with what`s called a 24, a fresh report of each homicide, shooting, and questionable death in the city in the last 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 BILLY NICKOLES: The first thing I`m looking for is really to see whether or not our victim has anything to do with any of our cases, because, you know, if that`s the case, then it seems the logical thought would be, it has something to do with the ongoing investigation.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Nickoles worked the streets for more than 20 years as a Baltimore police officer.\par \par \uc2 So you might get something in a 24 that connects up to another homicide investigation.\par \par \uc2 BILLY NICKOLES: Or another Federal -- you know Federal investigation.\par \par \uc2 JON LENZNER (Then Baltimore Acting US Attorney): \uc2 CCC\uc2  was as organized and savvy as they are violent and relentless.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: The idea is to build big cases that take lots of murderers off the street at once. Like the 15 CCC gang members linked to 18 homicides, and 27 other attempts. They were indicted last summer on drug racketeering and conspiracy charges.\par \par \uc2 JON LENZNER: This business was built on violence, murders and shootings of rival gang members and drug dealers.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Jon Lenzner was the acting US Attorney in Baltimore at the time.\par \par \uc2 JON LENZNER: One indictment can not only lead to violent gang members serving in prison, but can also help a city clear a number of homicides that have gone unsolved for a number of years.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Erek Barron, the current US Attorney calls it the Al Capone strategy.\par \par \uc2 The violent mob boss wasn`t busted for murder in the 1930s, he was convicted on tax evasion charges.\par \par \uc2 The problem is a 50-year low in clearing murder cases. The solution, if I hear you right is cooperation.\par \par \uc2 EREK BARRON: Absolutely. It`s the biggest part of the solution. Cooperation not just among law enforcement, but the community as well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It all boils down to teamwork it seems.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Yes. You know, and after demonstrating the extent of this problem for a couple of days, it is refreshing to hear somebody talking about solutions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why do they want to keep the location a secret?\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: I think there is such pressure first of all, to find something. The Department of Justice overseeing this, always cautious by nature and wants to make sure nothing gets in the way by way of contamination or compromise.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And there has been some issues in years past with the BPD, right? It was some corruption there. That was a big story nationwide.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And that speaks to what we found in all cities, which is that compromises relationship between police and community and without that cooperation, you have an issue.\par \par \uc2 So to restore the trust that`s been eroded, every one we talked to through the entire investigation says that is the key, community-police 