om Mariah Denehie via Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: That`s Richard Goodall, the school custodian at Davis Park Elementary in Terre Haute, Indiana. The 53-year-old sang Journey`s "Don`t Stop Believin`" during a yearend program at the school and the kids went wild.\par \par \uc2 Journey`s former front-man, Steve Perry saw the clip online and commented "I love this" and how could you not? I love it when somebody gets to have their moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that he`s a custodian. I love that the kids love him. They said he got a standing ovation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: From the kids and the parents.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And he`s so good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Could he open for Beyonce?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: he can open for me anytime. I`m just going to have them follow me around and sing that song. Everybody will be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know, they probably put the flash on their phones and they were like, "Go ahead. Do your thing."\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Listen, anyone who has had a few glasses of liquid courage and tried to karaoke that song knows it`s really hard and has in my case ended tragically, so props ahead.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, they said that he tried out for "The Voice" and didn`t make it. Something tells me, he should try it again.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I agree. I agree.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He can talk famous now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead: Professional racecar driver, Hailie Deegan, the only woman to race in the SRX Series. She is waiting for us, don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, stock-car driver. Hailie Deegan talks with us about racing in primetime and taking on the boys. She is not scared on the SRX circuit.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And I`m Ed O`Keefe. Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson signed the bill allowing his state to ban abortion once Roe v. Wade was overturned. He`ll tell us why he thinks that was the right move.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson, David Harbour, star of "Stranger Things" talks with Anthony Mason about the show that made him a star. I know you`re going to binge this, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I love this show. We`re not ready for it to end, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 MAN: The planes are full. Absolutely full.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Travelers are on the move for the Fourth of July facing major flight delays and record high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 ANDY GROSS: Typically, flying accounts for about eight to nine percent of the folks traveling. This year, it is only seven percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  makes history as the first Black woman on the Supreme Court after the Court rules against regulation to fight climate change.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Rise Up Like That.")\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says he`ll respond in kind to NATO`s military buildup near Russia`s borders over the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 OLAF SCHOLZ: He has all these sanction from imports for modern technology and things he is looking for.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In our series looking at the rise in unsolved murders, we learn how Baltimore Police are now working with Federal and State officials to catch killers.\par \par \uc2 EREK BARRON: Its biggest part of the solution, cooperation, not just among law enforcement, but the community as well.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The fast move shots.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And some ace performances in Wimbledon`s second round. Rafael Nadal scores a dominant win.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes. Service game. Three aces to slam the door.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Coco Gauff has a strong finish.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Such good rhythm, consistency, placement, power.\par \par \uc2 COCO GAUFF (Professional tennis player): One of the greatest privileges that I`ve ever gotten the chance to do and I`m glad that I can keep doing it and thank you guys again for all the support.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coco Gauff, she is growing up right before eyes and looking so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is just scratching the surface.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She was a baby when she first started.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cheering her on, always.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Nice to see you like, you know, you`re watching somebody who is on their way to something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The extension, right?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, we begin this hour with the growing political and legal fallout from the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 As a result of that ruling, abortion bans are enacted, expected, or likely to be enacted in at least 20 states.\par \par \uc2 In Arkansas, a trigger law immediately banned nearly all abortions. There are no exceptions for minors, or in cases of rape or incest. The only exception is if the mother`s life is at risk.\par \par \uc2 That law was signed in 2019 by Governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , who joins us this morning from Little Rock.\par \par \uc2 Governor, it is good to see you. Happy Friday. Happy Independence Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Thank you. We`re excited about the weekend and it is good to be with you today.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, thank you again. So your State has given about a million dollars to pregnancy centers, but that`s a fraction of what`s likely going to be needed for the thousands of unwanted pregnancies that are going to occur each year now.\par \par \uc2 What else might you and your State Legislature be planning to do to help young mothers and families?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, it`s an important question and we are addressing it.\par \par \uc2 First of all, we started this long before. You know, we took Medicaid expansion in Arkansas that provided health coverage for the working poor. We`re trying to improve our healthcare for maternal moms for prenatal care.\par \par \uc2 But what we do now and you start with the fact that last year, we had about 3,000 abortions in Arkansas. And so under the new law that the Supreme Court allowed to take effect, that means that a significant number of those will be reduced. Some of those moms will put the child up for adoption. So we`re going to have to increase our adoption services.\par \par \uc2 Others will go out of state, which I think will be a small minority, but there`ll be many that will carry that child to term and to keep that child and so we want to make sure that we have the wraparound services for that mom, both during the pregnancy, but also afterwards.\par \par \uc2 So we`re looking as to how we can beef that up. I`ve charged my Health Department and Human Services to come up with more funding for each of those needs that we see as a result of this.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So we should anticipate more funding for psychological issues potentially stemming from unwanted births or physical issues with pregnancies and births?\par \par \uc2 And also there`s this report now that at least two Arkansas State Senators say they`re looking to introduce legislation to ban women from traveling out of state to get an abortion, is that a law you would sign?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): I would not.\par \par \uc2 The challenge there is that that would be in violation of interstate commerce. Obviously, we want to discourage that because the public policy in Arkansas is to limit abortions and to carry out the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 And so, we recognize that that is something that even the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  addressed in one of the concurring opinions that this is not intended to restrict interstate commerce.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decision said each State is going to make its own decisions in this, which is traditional public health matters. Arkansas has made its decision, other States might look at it differently.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Governor, over the last few weeks, we have learned more details about January 6th and Trump`s role on the violence that the nation witnessed that day.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , called Trump "domestic threat we have never seen before." Do you agree? Do you believe that he is a danger to this nation?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, as you can see from the testimony on January 6th, and subsequent to the election where he was challenging the legality of it, the lawful transfer of party, yes, that was a threat to our democracy, that was a threat to our institutions of government, and that`s not the behavior we want to see in a responsible President.\par \par \uc2 I think the question for the January 6 Committee is one, you get facts out to the public, which is important to know. Secondly, they`re trying to make a case that there`s criminal conduct on behalf of the President, I question whether they have made that case, I don`t believe they have. I don`t know whether they can.\par \par \uc2 But the Justice Department is watching that, and we`ll see how that develops. But at this point, they have not made that criminal conduct case in my judgment.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But you agree with the Congresswoman that he is a danger to the nation. That`s what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, everybody phrases a different way. I would not be supporting him for 2024. He acted irresponsibly. During that time, he was a risk to the nation, absolutely. So that`s my statement on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you make of Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony this week?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Very compelling. I mean, anyone who appreciates the difficulty of someone being 25 and thrust into that incredible closeness to history, and what even in in her young age understood to be fundamentally wrong, and she testified to it.\par \par \uc2 So, my heart goes out to those that really stood firm during that time, and I think she clearly demonstrated her concern and love for our country. I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She did. Did you believe her, Governor? Did you believe her? Did you believe what she told us?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): I did. She came across with a great deal of credibility. But what is a challenge with the Committee is that they`re not having cross examination. They`re not having other points of view, and so that makes it more difficult.\par \par \uc2 I`m a lawyer, I`m used to the adversary system where you have different points of view presented and that was eliminated.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, they got into hearsay, and that`s where if they can`t back that up, it undermines that testimony. So, I want them to be careful, the Committee, that they present this in as fair manner as they can, and make sure they really have the facts as they present it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Many agree with you on that point. But I want to get to 2024 because you have said out loud that you`re considering a presidential bid in 2024 and President Trump will not -- former President Trump will not be a factor in your decision.\par \par \uc2 Most people don`t say that out loud. What`s your thinking here?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, I answer questions, honestly, and while we`re totally focused on 2022, obviously, there`s talk about 2024 and I had to make it clear that Trump has disqualified himself in my judgment from his actions on January 6 and leading up to that.\par \par \uc2 And so we have to go a different direction for our country for my party, and so I want to be a voice for commonsense conservatism and we`ll see how that resonates, but let`s get through 2022. We`ve got a very important election ahead.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, Governor, but it sounds like you`re considering challenging him if he decides to run in 2024. You said you answer the questions that you`re asked. Would you? Would you run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, I`m -- what I`m doing is consistent with that. I haven`t made a commitment to it. I haven`t made a decision on it. But I`m laying the foundation for that. If For that is something that`s going to be helpful to our country, so we`re looking at it, but no decision will be made until after this election in 2022.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, we look forward to your announcement right here at the table at CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got a seat right over there on the left waiting for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will keep it warm for you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We look forward to seeing you again. Thank you so much, Governor for coming on again. We always like to see you.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Always good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up. The need for -- thanks.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the need for speed on the racetrack. We`re talking about pioneering driver, her name is Hailie Deegan. Remember that -- there she is. Hey, Hailie. She is standing by with her car to talk about what oh inspires --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: There we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What inspires her to drive and preview this weekend`s race. You go, Hailee.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:10. Time to check your --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up: "Stranger Things," actor, David Harbour, he tells Anthony Mason about the season finale of the wildly popular series out today.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boys and girls, start your engines because our next guest is racing into history and inspiring other women and young girls to dream big. Dream ginormous, people.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about 20-year-old professional racecar driver, that would be the lovely, Hailie Deegan. She is making her mark as the only woman -- did you hear that -- the only woman competing in the SRX racing series co- founded by former NASCAR driver, Tony Stewart, which debuted last year.\par \par \uc2 Deegan also races in the NASCAR Truck Series. Yes, she`s a badass. She joins us from Connecticut, where she is already at the speedway. She`s getting ready for the big race, that`s tomorrow. It`s airing right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and Paramount+, which as you know, are both divisions of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 , that`s us.\par \par \uc2 Hailie, good morning to you. It`s so good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HAILIE DEEGAN (Professional racecar driver): Yes, and good to see you, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Hailie, this is what I`ve been saying about you all morning. You`re the only woman out there on the track, and I said, "But she`s not scared." She`s not scared and she`s ready.\par \par \uc2 Please tell me I`m correct. You`re not scared.\par \par \uc2 HAILIE DEEGAN: Definitely not scared. I`ve done some scary stuff in my lifetime. So we`re going to have a lot of fun here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m talking about being the only woman in the field of men, how that affects you? Does it inspire you? Motivate you? Intimidate you?\par \par \uc2 HAILIE DEEGAN: Oh man. I`ve been like -- it`s been like that my whole life in racing. Growing up in racing, my dad didn`t treat me any differently. I was the only one of the girl to do it when I was eight years old, and still one of the only girl to do it and I`m 20 now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, what did you see at eight that you thought "Dad, I want to do this?"\par \par \uc2 HAILIE DEEGAN: I think seeing him doing in racing and competing at such a high level in the motorsports world is what really got me into it. When he made that transition from motocross to off-road trucks. There were other little kids racing that were my age, and I just knew I wanted to try it.\par \par \uc2 And the moment I got in a car, I knew I definitely did not want to get out of it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hailie, many people don`t realize that racing is both mental and physical. So, how do you prepare for that when you get behind that wheel?\par \par \uc2 HAILIE DEEGAN: Yes, there`s a lot of preparation that goes into the races because there are so many races throughout the year. So you kind of get in a system event, whether it`s what you eat, how you`re training, how you`re hydrating.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the times in these cars, when you start getting on some of the bigger trucks, hot days out there, it can be 130 or 140 degrees inside the car, so it definitely takes a toll on your body.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh wow.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You know, Hailie, when I saw you were joining us this morning, I thought about my father-in-law and I thought of my cousin Petey in North Carolina who were devoted NASCAR fans. And I see you know, you`re on a shorter track. There`s a little more dirt involved.\par \par \uc2 What is the biggest difference between SRX and NASCAR? And why should somebody be tuning in on Saturday night to watch?\par \par \uc2 HAILIE DEEGAN: Yes, I got my start in stock car racing on these short tracks where it is just super fun, super competitive, close tight racing. And as I started moving my way up the ranks, we got to some bigger tracks, but coming back to the SRX series and competing on these small tracks makes it entertaining. You`ve got a lot of talented drivers out there, drivers who have been racing for far more years than I have, and then mixing it up with them is always fun.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to have a lot of fun here at Stafford and I can guarantee you, it`s going to be a good show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have to tell you, Hailie, I`m just in awe of you, because at eight years old, I was playing with Barbies, and I was scared of bugs. I am still scared of bugs.\par \par \uc2 So what is your ultimate dream when it comes to racing? I`m curious about that, and have you faced barriers because you are a female in this sport?\par \par \uc2 HAILIE DEEGAN: My ultimate goal when it comes to racing is to keep moving up the ranks in the stock car world, competing at the highest level and being competitive, running up front, getting good finishes, but it`s a lot of fun. It`s a lot of work. I enjoy it a lot though.\par \par \uc2 So there are some challenges that come with it, and I feel like the hardest part is the pressure that I put on myself. I feel like I hold myself to such a high standard that I feel it, the most just from myself.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that there`s a lot of pressure definitely from the outside that affects that, but you`ve got to perform under pressure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, nothing tops the pressure you put on yourself, I so get that.\par \par \uc2 Hailie Deegan, we are cheering you on. Congratulations. Congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 HAILIE DEEGAN: Thank you. I appreciate it. You guys better head on Stafford to come out for the race this weekend.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: We would.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We better do that. Thank you, Hailie.\par \par \uc2 And you can see Hailie compete in the SRX Racing Series. That`s tomorrow night on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and on Paramount+. As you heard her say, you better get out there and see her in person. She`s in Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, it`s time for the "Uplift at Eight" where we share stories that will bring you pure joy, a light to your day.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini is here. What do you have for us this morning?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good morning, guys. So we have a story about some small acts of kindness for unsuspecting strangers. Let`s take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HARRISON PAWLUK (Social media influencer): Sorry to bother you. Is it okay if you can just hold these? Have a lovely day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Look at her face.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can tell she likes it.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Harrison Pawluk surprises Australians with everything from flowers to free food. Sometimes, just a hug.\par \par \uc2 The 22-year-old says, he originally used social media for pranks, but decided to change things up and make people happy instead. He doesn`t just deliver those smiles to the people he surprises, but also to millions on social media.\par \par \uc2 And I will say, this morning, it is us.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HARRISON PAWLUK: I want to do my part to do something that is going to help to make people be like, "Wow, there`s still faith in people, like people are still good."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I believe that, Christina. People are still good.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: But they say sometimes, a great thing is just a string of little good things all put together, like you know just anything gets us through the day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Listen, for all you aspiring influencers, that`s how you go viral.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One of the best songs ever.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" because as Gayle is saying, we`re at the table and we are --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are talking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I feel that same way about that song, "Lovely Day."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Every time you hear it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You just feel better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does. It puts me in a really good mood. Bill Withers, we thank you for that. Rest in peace.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got a summer inspired "Talk of the Table" -- millions of Americans will fire up the grill this Fourth of July weekend. Rumor has it that Nate Burleson is very good behind the grill --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little something, something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a rumor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ed O`Keefe, are you very good behind the grill?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I think so. Mrs. O`Keefe, it depends on what I`m cooking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, that`s good. But this is what caught my eye, because I just assumed that burgers and hotdogs would be the most popular item grilled.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you think it is?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: is it hot dogs? Is it chicken?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on -- yes, Nate. Chicken.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That surprise me. I disagree with it. But they said chicken is the number one thing that people want to grill, not the steaks, not the burgers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Are you surprised by that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It used to be burgers and hot dogs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Well, now it is chicken.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: We`re getting healthier.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So that`s one little --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Enough red meat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I think you`re right about that, too. That`s one little summer nugget.\par \par \uc2 The other one is about families, a lot of them are going on summer vacation and a new poll suggests that they are way overdue.\par \par \uc2 A survey of 2,000 American parents finds that more than half, 55 percent have not had a vacation in the last three years.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe has raised his hand on that.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: However, 70 percent are planning to take one within the next 18 months. You haven`t had it?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That is also true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You haven`t had it because you don`t like your family --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: No, we locked down with the kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: COVID. Yes. Oh, so COVID took away at least two years.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you had a year that you could have been? You still didn`t.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: There was a presidential election. I was supposed to have my vacation, and we didn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But with the travel issues --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I am calling the HR Department.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the cost of gas, it might not be a good time to vacation, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got to do what you`ve got to do though.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is a bold new concept in travel that has the internet split. It`s called the Sky Cruise. I want you to check this out.`\par \par \uc2 So this is a nuclear powered rendition, right? This is a scientist who drew this up, Berlin-based scientist enthusiast who said this is a 5,000 passenger plane.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Five thousand?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Five thousand, and it is just like a cruise, any other cruise. You`ll have a panoramic platform, entertainment deck, shopping malls, gyms, swimming pools, restaurants. You`ve got theaters and cinemas.\par \par \uc2 So little planes will dock on this bigger plane, and it can be in the air for months, nuclear powered, and you will be able to travel around the world. It`s a cruise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right now, it is a concept.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just in the air. It is a concept.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it`s not happening.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he is realistic about the feasibility. He is basically saying, this could be something we could pull off by 2040. But my question is to you at the table and you at home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I know some people I would like to send on that cruise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Five thousand people in the air. No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some people I would send.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what was the question, Nate?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: At least for a few months.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was the question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Would you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is giving me Titanic vibes, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Would you? Would you go?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No. I`m straight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That that`s how I`m feeling, too. Ed O`Keefe?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: All right, well, my "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, Ed O`Keefe, would you go?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I would not go, but I know people I`d send up there.\par \par \uc2 None of you. None of you, for the record.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  clapping back at G7 Summit leaders who mocked him. I was at the Summit on Sunday in Germany when the leaders joked about Putin`s tendency to show off his athleticism.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON (British Prime Minister): Jackets on? Jackets off? Shall we take our clothes off?\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU (Canadian Prime Minister):Let`s wait for the picture.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON: We have to show them we are tougher than Putin.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU: We`re going to get the bare-chested horseback riding display.\par \par \uc2 URSULA VON DER LEYEN (\uc2 European Commission\uc2  President): Horseback riding is the best.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON: We`ve got to show them -- we`ve got to show them our pecs.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They were referencing the infamous photos -- there you go -- of Putin, shirtless, riding a horse.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday he responded saying it would be a "disgusting sight" if the Summit leaders tried to copy him by undressing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He may have a point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ed, isn`t this unusual?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is going on?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To see leaders at this level. It`s kind of high school by the way.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Boys will be boys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Very high school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very high school and do they know that Putin knows where they live?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Real quick, while we have this --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Because I know he is watching. I`m going to give a shout out to my dad for his birthday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s his name?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He is up in Albany. Jim O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Oh there we go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dad.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday, Dad.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And mom there, her birthday is later this month.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you sure? Are you sure he is watching?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I told -- I texted mom, I said make sure he is watching.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, shout out to Jim and Ed said, he`s going to send you to golf anywhere you want, first class, he told me that.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Let`s see about that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, coming up, "Stranger Things," actor David Harbour talks with Anthony Mason about Season Four Finale that`s coming up that everybody has been waiting for. It`s out today by the way.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s actor, David Harbour in the latest season of "Stranger Things" on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Stranger Things" Season Four quickly became the most watched English language series ever on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , and the season`s highly anticipated final two episodes are out today.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason recently met up with Emmy and Tony-nominated actor, David Harbour. He was very candid about his own experience with bipolar disorder and scoring a role of a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 "Stranger Things" fans thought Jim Hopper tragically met his end last season, but when the show returned, we found him alive, stuck in a Russian prison plotting a heroic escape.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The showdown in the Upside Down of Hawkins, Indiana starts today.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: It`s an exciting day for \uc2 Netflix\uc2  fans.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: David Harbour who plays Police Chief Jim Hopper joins the battle against the paranormal demons of "Stranger Things."\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: They`re some of the best, most epic stuff we`ve ever shot and also, at least for Hopper, some of the most intimate.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Harbour`s Hopper isn`t just a cop.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He is surrogate father to Eleven, played by Millie Bobby Brown.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: And the character himself struck a chord with people in a very deep way. They really love Hopper.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What do you think it is they love?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: He`s the dad that you always wanted. He`s a guy who does the right thing, but doesn`t always know how to express himself.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The role has made a star of the 47-year-old actor who had supporting parts for decades playing a CIA agent in the James Bond film "Quantum of Solace."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Quantum of Solace.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent in Parkland.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Parkland.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And a police officer in the soap opera, "As the World Turns."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "As the World Turns.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Back in 1999.\par \par \uc2 That same year, he made his professional acting debut on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 Did you aim at the stage first? Or was it --\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: It was always a stage. I never aimed at film and TV. I mean, I had a fantasy life in my head where I wanted to be like a big old movie star. But when I was kid, I never thought I was pretty enough for that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Madhouse.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: But like on stage, I`m a big guy. I have a big instrument.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You`re going to have a presence.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: Yes, I do have a stage presence that I still think I have more of than I have on film.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This summer, he is starring in the play "Madhouse" with Bill Pullman in the West End of London.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Madhouse.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In a role based in part on Harbour`s own experience with mental illness.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Madhouse.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Harbour was diagnosed as bipolar at age 26.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: I had a breakdown that manifested itself in what doctors would call like, you know, a mania.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: People actually kind of described you as crazy at the time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: Yes. I mean, I describe myself as crazy at the time.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: For a time he was hospitalized and put on heavy drugs.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: Do you know anything about antipsychotics?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: They are pretty blunt force objects. They slow you down very quickly.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But it was talk therapy, Harbour says that saved him.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: And now I feel, you know, saner than I`ve ever been. Still a little crazy, but pretty sane.\par \par \uc2 I`m doing a play where I play a crazy guy, so it`s still, you know, still with me as I rehearsed.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How did you feel in the middle of all this?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: I mean, it`s awful. It`s the worst feeling in the world. Everyone is telling you, you`re actually broken. You`re sort of less than human.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So what do you do with that?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: You use it in your art, hopefully.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the best things about me was the fact that I didn`t have success. So what I`m used to doing is fighting when nobody wants me there. Like I`m used to the guy who shows up and everybody`s like, no, no, no, we`re going to give this job -- you should like pack it in -- and I`m like, "Well, I`m just going to still be here."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So how does everything that`s happened feel?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: Pretty great. I mean, if I am honest, pretty great.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ever think you`d have "Architectural Digest" come to your door?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Architectural Digest.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: No. I still don`t know that they should have.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Architectural Digest.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We visited that "Architectural Digest" apartment.\par \par \uc2 Here he is Red Garden --\par \par \uc2 Which features the original comic book cover of "Red Guardian," the \uc2 Marvel\uc2  superhero Harbour played recently in the film, "Black Widow."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Black Widow.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s fun being a superhero?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: Yes. I`ve got to say, really your inner five-year-old comes out. You have to read comic books and think about wearing capes and run down the hallway and stuff.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Black Widow.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: And it`s just that on like a Ferrari level.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It was while he was shooting "Black Widow" in London, that he connected with Lily Allen.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Lily Allen performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He knew the British singer from her videos.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: And I was like, "Oh that cute girl. Look at that cute girl on that bike."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: They met on a celebrity dating app.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: And then I saw her profile, and I thought, "Oh, I think that`s that girl."\par \par \uc2 We got married in Vegas.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In front of an Elvis impersonator.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: Yes, hell yes. Because if you`re going to go to Vegas, you might as well be made by "The King."\par \par \uc2 (Clip of "Stranger Things.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Now, Harbour is preparing for the epic conclusion of "Stranger Things" Season Four, but it`s not the end.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: There`s a fifth season still to come.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 You`ve said you knew where the show was going, and you know where the show ends.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARBOUR: I have said that. I know. The Duffer Brothers always remind me that I`ve said that, but if you ask them if I know the ending, they`ll probably tell you I don`t, just so you`re aware.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You got a sense he got a little trouble for that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But he wanted to know where his character was going. So, as obviously as he was playing the character, it conveyed the arc of where it was all headed.\par \par \uc2 The end of Season Four is going -- is epic in every way including in time, it is an act -- the eighth episode is an hour and 25 minutes. The ninth is two hours and 20 minutes. So each it is its own movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s such a great show. You know what I took from that? What`s that celebrity dating app he used, Anthony Mason? Always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re going to hook you up, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you, Anthony. You`re the best.\par \par \uc2 Up next, all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anything else you want to say about your dad`s birthday? This is funny, guys, because Ed said, "Do you mind if I say Happy Birthday to my dad a couple seconds." Then he had a whole pre-produced piece.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, the whole segment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pictures, graphics. Ed, when you were younger.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Helped produce.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shout out to Jim. We love you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday. See you Monday morning on \uc2 CBS\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "My body, my choice.)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Hell no. We don`t need Roe.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fallout from the Court`s decision ruling that abortion is no longer a constitutional right in this country.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Divisions on full display across the country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Women in at least eight states can no longer get a legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (Former US Secretary of State): We are only at the beginning of this terrible travesty that this Court has inflicted on us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Explosive testimony to the January 6 Select Committee.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON (Former White House aide): Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : President Trump knew that this was going to be a violent day.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trump slammed that testimony calling Hutchinson a phony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: CBS News has John Hinckley, Jr.`s first ever TV interview.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Would you like them to consider forgiving you?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT HINCKLEY, JR. (Attempted assassination on President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 ): Yes. They probably can`t, but I wish -- I wish they would.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN (Cost Plus Drugs co-founder): We all know people who have had to make decisions between their rent they`ve had to ration their medication and that`s just wrong. To change those power dynamics, you have to work outside the system and that`s what Cost Plus Drugs -- see how I got right back in there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Free Mom Hugs has become a nationwide movement.\par \par \uc2 SARA CUNNINGHAM (Free Mom Hugs founder): I needed someone who shared my faith to tell me it`s okay to search the matter out.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This makes me cry.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Happy Pride. I never thought this kid from Louisiana would be able to say that on national television.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That kid from Louisiana is doing A-okay.\par \par \uc2 You look very comfortable in the water there, Miss Quijano.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: I love the water.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like a fish.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: I love those manatees.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t appreciate you stealing the shine of Best Hair of the Day. You`re not supposed to have that title.\par \par \uc2 MALCOLM GLADWELL (Podcast host): It is just the three of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sorry, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wa-wa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Would you run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, I`m -- what I`m doing is consistent with that. I haven`t made a commitment to it.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: We had a bit of an internal discussion this morning, guys about whether or not it was too racy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s racy, but I think we should show it. It is a cover. It`s Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, I was looking at the horse.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: It`s Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I wasn`t --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, that makes one of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Eyes down, Mr. Burleson. Eyes down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh is that Beyonce on that horse?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I see nothing. I see nothing. I see nothing, honey.\par \par \uc2 "JUDGE" JOHN HODGMAN (Podcast host): A hot dog is not a sandwich.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But if it is torn in to two pieces of bread, it is.\par \par \uc2 "JUDGE" JOHN HODGMAN: Sir, Tony, I don`t think we have time to go into it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 "JUDGE" JOHN HODGMAN: You never order half a hotdog with a cup of soup. That`s the difference.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m going to continue to argue this one, fake internet "Judge" John Hodgman. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 "JUDGE" JOHN HODGMAN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your Honor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m curious about who wrote this line. You know which one I`m going to read?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m ready.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "I felt his naked white skin as it slid against her dark moist body, luminous in the afternoon sun. She could feel the weight of him on top of her lying heavy on her heart." Was that to Hillary Rodham Clinton?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a very long kiss, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They waited a long time to find true love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This just shows that you can find love at any age, so I say, keep hope alive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Are you talking to yourself when you say that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was going to say like, you take that Nate, you take that one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Speaking for a friend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Speaking for a friend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope it is not going to be 95, I will say that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are a catch, and all of your work family, we`ve got to vet it out first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m not playing no games.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, all right.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220701ei71000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Swearing-In Of Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Today was the final day of a politically charged Supreme Court term, and it came with major decisions for the environment and immigration. But, at noon today, one Supreme Court justice retired, and another made history.\par \par \uc2 Here \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice): I, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , do solemnly swear...\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voice-over): In a private ceremony with the justices and her family, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  made history.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The 116th justice and first black female, Jackson said she was truly grateful to be part of the promise of our great nation.\par \par \uc2 It was a moment of hope and collegiality to end a divisive term that saw a blockbuster ruling overturning Roe vs. Wade and letting states set their own abortion policies, a decision that sparked protests across the country.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, President Biden said he would support lifting the Senate filibuster to pass a federal law that could override state efforts to ban or restrict abortion and guarantee abortion access nationwide.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): And if the filibuster gets in the way -- it`s like voting rights -- it should be we provide an exception for this -- require an exception to the filibuster for this action.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And abortion rights supporters are also turning to state courts. In Florida, a judge temporarily blocked the state`s 15-week ban under the state Constitution, following similar rulings in other states.\par \par \uc2 Today, the High Court allowed the Biden administration to end the Trump era policy of requiring some noncitizens coming to the U.S. to remain in Mexico. And in a decision seen as favorable to coal companies, the court ruled against the EPA`s authority to regulate planet warming, greenhouse gas emissions at power plants.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Jan Crawford joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Justice Jackson won`t change the balance of the court, but what do we know about some of the big cases that will come next term?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Oh, I mean, major cases already on the docket about affirmative action in college admissions, voting rights, election procedures, gay rights, tremendously controversial issues.\par \par \uc2 So our newest justice is stepping into a divided court and more divisive issues on the way.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Sounds like you will be busy again.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Next year is going to be a blockbuster.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jan Crawford, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220702ei6u0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Uncertainty In States With Trigger Laws, The Clinics Seeing A Surge In Calls, Trying To Get In As Many Patients Before The Bans Take Effect\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Omar Villafranca \par \uc2 513 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we want to turn now to the fight over abortion rights, which is far from over. Ohio today became the latest state to face a legal challenge to its ban. And now in some states, like Texas, with so-called trigger laws, the question is whether women will face prosecution if they seek an abortion.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voice-over): Protesters prayed outside of a Planned Parenthood clinic today in Broward County, Florida. On Friday, the state`s 15-week ban on abortion is set to go into effect. But several groups are challenging the new law.\par \par \uc2 LAURA GOODHUE (Executive Director, The Florida Alliance of Planned Parenthood Affiliates): Abortion in Florida is legal right now. In fact, OUR -- the health centers with Planned Parenthood are really busy.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Last week`s Supreme Court ruling leaves a patchwork of state laws that has created confusion on where women can go for abortion. And many are going to Florida.\par \par \uc2 LAURA GOODHUE: If you look at the map, I mean, Florida really is the last oasis of health care, of access to reproductive health care in the South and abortion.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Several states had trigger laws in place in case Roe was overturned, and many have now effectively banned abortion or severely restricted them.\par \par \uc2 In three states, some providers have stopped offering abortion care due to uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Stand up! Fight back!\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Another battle on the abortion front? Enforcement. In Austin, Texas, Travis County district attorney Jose Garza says he thinks law enforcement can use discretion in prosecuting.\par \par \uc2 JOSE GARZA (Travis County, Texas, District Attorney): My number one job is to keep our community safe, and not prosecuting women, families who seek abortion care, not prosecuting doctors and medical staff who provide abortion care.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: But, in Wisconsin, where there is a legal fight over an 1849 law that criminalizes abortion, Sheboygan County DA Joel Urmanski says his job is clear.\par \par \uc2 JOEL URMANSKI (Sheboygan County, Wisconsin, District Attorney): I`m not going to try and go out there and be some activist prosecutor. MY job is to enforce the law as it`s written.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: This clinic here in San Antonio is able to resume abortions within the first six weeks of pregnancy. The state of Texas also started a new Web site today that gives information on abortion alternatives and adoption -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220701ei6t0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Helping Mothers In Crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Jan Crawford, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 2,504 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning we`re learning more about a shelter that offers help to pregnant women and mothers in crisis and how it could expand after the Supreme Court ruling on abortion.\par \par \uc2 St. Mary`s Shelter in Virginia opened its doors 16 years ago and says it has helped hundreds of women.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford looks at its mission to "Ensure the health of mother and baby."\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 You know, Mary`s Shelter has several homes. It is faith-based, but they`ve taken women regardless of their views.\par \par \uc2 Now many of the women were considering abortion, but Mary`s Shelter offers housing and help getting on their feet with their children for up to three years.\par \par \uc2 The Director told us, it is not a solution for everyone. It is mainly for those underserved women who feel they have nowhere else to go.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In 2014, Shawnte Mallory lost her job. She had two young kids at home and couldn`t pay her rent.\par \par \uc2 When she got pregnant again, she decided to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY (Former Mary`s Shelter client): It wasn`t what I wanted to do, it was just at the moment, it was -- that`s what everybody was telling me to do, so I felt like that was really my only option.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Then, she found out about Mary`s Shelter.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: It changed my life.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It changed your life.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: It gave me a new start. I didn`t have to give up or get rid of a child that I knew I wanted just because things got hard. I didn`t have a support system.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Twenty-five-year-old, Logan Wilkins also found Mary`s Shelter in a moment of hopelessness.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS (Former Mary`s Shelter client): I was sleeping behind a dumpster at my job.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You`re literally sleeping behind a dumpster?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: Yes. I was literally sleeping behind a dumpster eight months pregnant.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: How were you able to survive?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: It was really difficult. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON (Mary`s Shelter Executive Director and Founder): We turn no one away because we know that their situation at that moment is just that desperate.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Kathleen Wilson started Mary`s Shelter 16 years ago. More than 300 women have come through their doors, some staying up to three years in fully furnished shared homes and apartments with their children.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON: We have another kitchen upstairs.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: While they get parenting training and help with employment and housing.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON: When they leave here, that independent lifestyle becomes, you know, a real viable reality for them as opposed to just, "Well, you had the baby and you`re done," which is not the way any of the ministries I know work.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Women come from up and down the East Coast to stay here, including a woman and her children who arrived unannounced from Florida the day of our visit.\par \par \uc2 Wilson who opposes abortion say with states now free to ban or restrict abortion, more women will need help, but pregnancy shelters are not for every situation.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned that women will be forced into unwanted pregnancies?\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON: No, I don`t. I mean, I think that there will be some. I hope that they`ll reach out to other agencies like maybe an adoption agency or that families will step up. I think we`ll have to increase support groups for moms like those.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: This is not a solution for every woman who potentially may not be able to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON: Right. No. Not everybody needs a home. Not everybody needs to move out of their house. Not everybody needs to leave a violent situation, but we can help those that are having abortions because they feel hopeless.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: I had no motivation. I didn`t have any kind of guidance.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Mallory, now 31 with five daughters says Mary`s Shelter helped her find purpose.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: I am in Bible College studying for Theology. I also am a Peer Recovery Coach, so I help those that are battling with addictions, traumas from like sexual abuse, or just past traumas.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Wilkins, now a mother of four, returned to Mary`s Shelter for the second time last December while pregnant with her son, Leon. He is three months old.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: I just got a new job where -- in the career field that I want to be in, the medical field.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: So what are you doing?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: I am a scheduler, but I`m getting my medical billing and coding certification. In four months, I should be done in October.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What`s the message of your story?\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: That there is hope, that there are places around that can help you that, you know, having an abortion isn`t the final answer.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What about you know?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: Abortion isn`t the final answer. There are so many other options. And to be quite honest, it is literally all worth it when you have that baby.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, Mary`s Shelter gets its funding from private donors and fundraising events and the Director told us that it has helped open shelters in other states and hopes to keep expanding -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, Jan, it`s a beautiful option for women who want that option. The key thing is for the women who want it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for your coverage yesterday and also today, Jan. It continues to be just absolutely superb. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Thank you. Thanks, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, Charlie D`Agata switching places with James Corden as "The Late, Late Show" goes on the road to London.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Mr. James Corden, how are you?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Charlie, how are you?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Welcome to the show.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: This is wonderful. I`ve never done this. I`ve never been -- I`ve never done this before.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Neither have I.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I`ve never done -- wow.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: I`ve always wanted the experience.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: It is quite the experience.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: How does it feel?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Well, I understand now when guests say "This isn`t a very comfy couch." It isn`t. It`s true.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie D`Agata has got the hair for the job.\par \par \uc2 But, I`ll tell you what, looking pretty good at that anchor chair.\par \par \uc2 Corden talks to Charlie about overcoming long odds to find success in his effort at late night and what`s next as he eyes the end of an eight-year run as host of "The Late, Late Show."\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You meet the nicest people in the greenroom.\par \par \uc2 We`re here with billionaire, investor, entrepreneur, really all-around nice guy, Mark Cuban. Look at his shirt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at his shirt, Nate. It says "Be a Better Human."\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN (costplusdrugs.com co-founder) "Be a Better Human.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come in, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was in the back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: From one nice guy to another. Tony was in the bathroom.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: How are you? Good to see you, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he wanted to see you.\par \par \uc2 He is launching a new challenge to the drug industry. He will tell us how you can get generic medicines at a low price -- how low is low, Mark Cuban?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Like three cents sometimes. We`ll cut your cost on all your medications.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want that deal.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 And Tony, you`re kicking it off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m kicking it off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you feeling good as well, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m feeling real good. I`ve got an artifact to help aid in my "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So about 10 years ago, when I was a writer for Tina Brown`s "Newsweek," I went down in a submarine off the coast of Hawaii, and through the little portal, we found this, which is a mug that was knocked or thrown from a World War Two Navy vessel, and it was at the bottom of the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 I keep it on my desk.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you took that home?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I took it home. They grabbed it with a little claw and I got to keep it and it just stirs my imagination. I don`t know who threw it, who knocked it, how it got down there, but it just connects me to that era. Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you have to take a look at that. I`m bringing it up because explorers in the Philippines recently found --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is in good shape, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A shipwreck at more than four miles down, deepest shipwreck ever.\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of Caladan Oceanic.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a World War Two era Navy Destroyer that was sunk in battle, and it is another connection to that -- well, you see the \uc2 Corning Glass\uc2  on the bottom?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s an American made mug.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s in good shape.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So our connection to the history, even after the waves covered over is renewed again with this discovery.\par \par \uc2 This is the USS Samuel B. Roberts, popularly known as the "Sammy B," fought heroically in World War Two and that what you`re looking at is the final resting place for 89 heroes -- 89 sailors helping America win that war.\par \par \uc2 So I wanted to shout out the discovery, the service, and also bring in that little artifact because it`s -- it really just -- there`s something about touching what was touched by history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: History lost under --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The surface of the sea. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kept well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, I love it when the future of technology meets the medical space. So my "Talk of the Table" is how medical students are starting to use patient holograms to practice on.\par \par \uc2 So here is how it works. They are wearing mixed reality headsets. These students are able to see each other, interacting with multilayered and medically accurate holographic patients. They are called hollow scenarios in their lifelike holograms, and you can access this from anywhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 Students will take several modules using the technology. The first will focus on respiratory conditions, emergencies, and then they`re also working on cardiology and neurology in the development.\par \par \uc2 So I just -- I just love this because we oftentimes look at the future and it seems like the future is now. It`s not too far ahead, 10 to 20 years. Now, they`re doing so many good things that students can really access all of this technology.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They can practice now poking holes in my arm with that needle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still want Dr. Kurth in the room with me. Just saying, I`m a big baby.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is how the students practice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a follow up on his story from last week. You remember this online video that Kevin Ford revealed the goodie bag he was given us a reward for working for \uc2 Burger King\uc2  for 27 years and never missing a day of work.\par \par \uc2 You know, there was some Lifesavers, there was a movie ticket for one. There was a lanyard --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is painful.\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of FastRitz/Reddit.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, yes, we still were like, "Okay, \uc2 Burger King\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 His family thought that he deserved a little bit more than that. So, they started an online fundraiser for him.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this, last week when we told it, it raised $30,000.00. Now, it`s raised more than $170,000.00.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. And this is what Kevin Ford told TMZ about that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FORD (\uc2 Burger King\uc2  employee): If I had enough money, you know, I would buy a new Ford Hybrid, because I`m American. I`d also like to maybe take a day off and relax, and go see my friends.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, he lives in Vegas, but he has got job offers from all over the country. Somebody in Charleston, a manager said, "It would be an honor to work with you." The pitch was you could work at a beachside restaurant and have unbeatable tacos. But he said no, he turned it down. He wants to continue to work for \uc2 Burger King\uc2 , he says.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because he basically still likes it there, but he is just one of those really good guys that people heard about his story and want to do right by him, and they did.\par \par \uc2 David Spade -- we got the story because David Spade donated $5,000.00 on his own and it keeps growing and growing and it`s still up.\par \par \uc2 I think this is great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With all that newfound money, with the newfound money, I say \uc2 Burger King\uc2  go out and do the right thing and help him get his own \uc2 Burger King\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, a franchise.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right? He has enough money to put down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I like that, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, owner of a \uc2 Burger King\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`d be a great owner. He doesn`t miss a day of work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. And he likes the company and he says he wants to buy a Ford Hybrid because he`s an American.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because I`m American.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: American. We like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: American.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6s000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Fight To Provide Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Gayle King \par \uc2 958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will find out more then, but for now, we`re going to the latest on the reaction to the Supreme Court ruling allowing states to make up their own decision on whether abortion should be legal or illegal within their borders.\par \par \uc2 So far, at least seven states have gone with illegal and others are on the way along the same path. In some of those states, abortion rights supporters though, are going back to Court and they are arguing that these so-called trigger laws that outlaw abortion should not be allowed to take effect.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Jackson, Mississippi where state officials have certified that an abortion ban in that state can indeed take effect.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning. A lot of uncertainty here. What happens now?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Hi, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Well, now that the law is going to go into effect, that means all abortions will be banned here in Mississippi except in the cases of rape and the threat to the life of the mother and that means that this clinic behind me is preparing to close.\par \par \uc2 Now, with all surrounding states also getting ready to implement their own trigger laws that means women seeking the procedure will have to travel over 400 miles for a provider.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Whose choice? Our choice.")\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Outrage, celebration, and confusion across the country as the Supreme Court`s decision leaves the issue of abortion up to the states. Thirteen states have laws in place that automatically ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, State Court Judges in Utah and Louisiana temporarily blocked those so-called trigger laws. Similar lawsuits have been filed in six other states with more expected.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MA (Center for Reproductive Rights senior staff attorney): Chaos has ensued across broad swaths of the South.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Jenny Ma is senior staff attorney at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 . She filed the lawsuit challenging Louisiana`s trigger laws.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MA: The fight is far from over.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Louisiana`s three remaining abortion clinics will now stay open pending a hearing next week.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MA: We argue that those cannot legally stand because they were so confusing and so vague and so, we are challenging those laws under the Louisiana Constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Mississippi`s only abortion clinic also filed a lawsuit arguing the law goes against the State`s Constitution.\par \par \uc2 AUDRIANA LEWIS-SCHOLES (Certified midwife): Everything looks pretty good.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Audriana Lewis-Scholes is a certified midwife in Mississippi who often drives two hours to see patients.\par \par \uc2 AUDRIANA LEWIS-SCHOLES: You taking your prenatal vitamins?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AUDRIANA LEWIS-SCHOLES: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: She is worried about the lack of resources for pregnant women in the state.\par \par \uc2 AUDRIANA LEWIS-SCHOLES: Sometimes we have patients that deliver on the side of the road and that`s the things also because they couldn`t make it to the hospital in time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, the State`s Governor, \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  says that he`s looking to expand programs to help women before, during, and after pregnancy and also looking for ways to incentivize adoption and extend safe haven laws here in Mississippi -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Caitlin Huey-Burns, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton says the Supreme Court`s monumental abortion ruling could lead to other decisions turning back the clock on Civil Rights.\par \par \uc2 In her first on-camera interview about the ruling, we asked her about Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` written opinion. He said that the Court should reconsider past rulings on contraception and same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Justice Thomas has sort of floated that out there about contraceptive rights -- contraception.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And about same sex marriages. But other Justices have pushed back to say no, he is really sort of on his own with that.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t believe that?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well, he may be on his own, but he is signaling as he often did -- you know, people -- I went to Law School with him. He has been a person of grievance for as long as I`ve known him -- resentment, grievance, anger -- and he has signaled in the past, to Lower Courts to State Legislatures, find cases, pass laws, get them up.\par \par \uc2 I may not win the first, the second, or the third time, but we`re going to keep at it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you`re saying people pay attention to this.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: Yes. The people he is speaking to, which is the -- you know, right-wing, very conservative Judges and Justices and State Legislatures.\par \par \uc2 And the thing that is -- well, there are so many things about it that are deeply distressing -- but women are going to die, Gayle. Women will die.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We reached out to Justice Thomas for a comment, we have not heard back from him, and Secretary Clinton had much more to say.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in our next hour, we ask her whether she thinks senators were misled by Supreme Court Justices during their confirmation hearing. Some say that they were.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6s000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  On Abortion Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,744 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin with this, speaking of the decision. Former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton says the overturning of Roe v. Wade should be a wake-up call for Americans.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with her yesterday in the studio. This was her first on-camera interview about the Supreme Court`s monumental ruling. The decision triggered laws to prevent women in at least seven states -- seven -- from getting legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 Now Clinton was here yesterday with her co-author to discuss her novel, "State of Terror." It is co-published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which is a division of our corporate owner, that`s \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She told us that this is a risky time for American democracy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Last week, the Roe v. Wade decision was made. And even though we had gotten a heads up, many people thought it was unthinkable. A lot of people are still processing depending on your point of view.\par \par \uc2 What did you think when you heard it?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well, I was not surprised because I think that was the goal of packing the Court with Justices who are on the record for many years of being against women`s constitutional rights to make decisions about our own bodies.\par \par \uc2 I was deeply sorry that it actually happened, but now, that it has happened, I think everybody understands that this is not necessarily the only effort that we`re going to see this Court undertake to turn back the clock on Civil Rights --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Other issues.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : And Gay Rights and Women`s Rights beyond abortion. This is going to, I hope, wake up a lot of Americans. I don`t care what political party or religion you are, the question is who decides? Is the government going to be in your bedroom? Is the government going to be making these decisions?\par \par \uc2 We`re only at the beginning of this terrible travesty that this Court has inflicted on us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what do you say to supporters, though, who say listen, we are protecting the rights of unborn children. They have rights, too. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : You know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This would be the core argument.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I said that, you know, that is predominantly a belief rooted in religion, which I respect and that`s why in a pluralistic democracy like ours, the Court and Roe v. Wade said the government is not going to make this decision.\par \par \uc2 If it is your personal belief based on whatever that there is no legitimate basis for abortion, even if you`re in the hospital and you`re bleeding out, even if you`re told that you have cancer and chemotherapy is necessary, and therefore an abortion is necessary, no matter what, you get to make that decision. I might not agree with it, but you get to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 But you, and those who support your particular point of view cannot dictate that decision to every other woman. And, you know, for many years, the abortion rate was going down. We were seeing, I think greater responsibility, especially with the use of contraception.\par \par \uc2 So what is it their -- what is their ultimate goal here, if they begin to chip away at all of these rights? It truly is to erase the progress that women have made from the last 150 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, a couple of the senators have said that they were misled by the Justices. Does this say to you that we need to change the confirmation hearing process?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well, I think they were misled, in part, because they wanted to be misled. They had --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think they knew that this was going to happen?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: They either knew or they were blind to what the history of the people before them. Anyone who is surprised by this is not paying attention.\par \par \uc2 So these people were selected for this purpose, and it was a 50-year campaign. And you`ve got to give the other side, you know, lots of points for their relentlessness, their total commitment to getting what they want done, regardless of who is hurt by it, and regardless of who is stripped of rights.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it the time for the Democrats to take on the filibuster, do you think?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well, I`ve been saying for quite some time, I think the filibuster should be eliminated, at the very least for constitutional questions like voting rights, like a woman`s, you know, constitutional rights to make decisions about her own body.\par \par \uc2 So if you can`t lift it completely, lift it for those two things that have huge consequences for our country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And last question, you`re a former First Lady, former Secretary of State, former Senator, do you miss the day-to-day of politics? Is there any scenario in your brain that you would think, "I want to get back in?"\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : No, but I miss it. I miss it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is no scenario in 2024, you would even remotely consider?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : You know, I can`t imagine it. I really can`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s not a no.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : But -- well, but what I can`t imagine is staying as active and outspoken as I can because I think -- I think our country is really on the precipice, Gayle. I think that we are looking at not only the erosion of these rights, throwing the door open to unfettered, unregulated gun access, but we`re also looking at dismantling the Federal government, how it protects our air, and our water, and everything else that goes along with it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think our democracy is at stake?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re looking at January 6th hearings.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think that the democracy is at stake.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As we sit here today.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Will anything come of these hearings? People say -- some say, it`s a waste of time.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: I think these hearings have been incredibly powerful. I understand a lot of people pay no attention, but history pays attention.\par \par \uc2 I hope the Justice Department is paying attention. I hope in these midterm elections, candidates in the Democratic Party pay attention. Because you cannot let those who attacked our Capitol and those who urged them on to do so, act with impunity. That`s what happens in dictatorships and authoritarian regimes.\par \par \uc2 So I think it is important that people recognize, if there were ever a time to vote and to vote literally for your rights and our future, it is this midterm.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, you know, she had a lot to say. She was really very candid and very open, and I like what she said that history pays attention.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because, you know, people don`t say no one is paying attention to these hearings, but the first hearing, 20 million people watched. People are tuning in. People want to know exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 So we will see. Today is going to be a very big day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m so glad she ended with reference to voting in this country, because when it comes to democracies, our participation rate is abysmal relative to other countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And still people complain, though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People complain, 80 million people don`t vote. And when you see \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  on television, what I think about is all the what-ifs from 2016. What could have been different?\par \par \uc2 It was a small number of people that swung the election. She won the popular vote. If people had gone to the polls, three Supreme Court Justices that had been appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might have been appointed by her, we don`t have this decision today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, she is spot on. She said we`re turning back the clock.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And speaking of voting, today is Primary Day in New York.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know that.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we will have more of our conversation with former Secretary of State Clinton. She and her co-author, Louise Penny will discuss her book, "State of Terror" and tell us what inspired -- this is a political thriller -- and the main character in the book, she is a Secretary of State, and she`s dealing with a former President that she thinks is a bit of a knucklehead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How about that? Based on a true story. Inspired by true events, I think is the phrase.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a novel.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it`s fiction, though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It is fiction, Nate. It is fiction.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Inspired by true events, all right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`re working together. It is a great story, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I`m looking forward to that part of the conversation as well.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, in the wake of the Supreme Court`s abortion ruling, we`re going to look at a shelter that is finding new ways to help pregnant women in crisis.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:10. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, we`ll talk to "The Late, Late Show" host, James Corden in London where he is hosting his show this week. Why he is stepping away from the late night at the height of his success.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, a follow up to the story of a \uc2 Burger King\uc2  worker who got nothing but a goodie bag for his 27 years of service.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6s000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Rush On The Morning-After Pill, After The Supreme Court Abortion Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Lilia Luciano \par \uc2 461 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, the battle over abortion rights has shifted to the states where political and legal fights are currently under way, including Texas, where a judge today ruled that abortions can continue, at least for the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lilia Luciano.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): The abortion battle lines have shifted to the states.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: What we are hearing very loud and clear from our base is that this is a beginning.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In Texas today, a judge allowed clinics to resume abortions up to six weeks of pregnancy, but only until the next hearing in mid-July.\par \par \uc2 MARC HEARRON (Senior Counsel, Center For Reproductive Rights): At least for today, we can say that abortion providers cannot be prosecuted for at least the time being.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It`s one of three states where courts have put bans on hold, with Wisconsin`s attorney general suing to block a ban from taking effect there.\par \par \uc2 Nine states currently have abortion bans in effect, including Tennessee, where, as of today, abortions after the sixth week are now illegal.\par \par \uc2 The next battle over reproductive rights may have already begun, after Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  suggested the court should reconsider the case protecting access to contraception. Several major pharmacies, like \uc2 CBS\uc2  and \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2 , are imposing limits on the purchase of emergency contraception, also known as the morning-after pill, following a surge in demand.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH DOWDY (Louisiana Resident): If you don`t have that option, then you`re just stuck with options that are less effective.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Elizabeth Dowdy says she and her husband don`t want children. So she has an IUD. But she lives in Louisiana, where a law this spring would have banned abortion at fertilization, making IUDs and emergency contraception illegal. That bill failed.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH DOWDY: If an accident happens, I don`t want to have to travel across the country to a state that`s willing to have it. That shouldn`t need to happen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Here in California, voters will decide whether to amend state`s Constitution in order to protect the right to abortion during the midterms in November -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Lilia Luciano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220630ei6s000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Migrants Found Dead in Tractor Trailer; Deadly Train Crash Investigation; Surprise Capitol Attack Hearing; Former Aide to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  to Testify; The Fight to Provide Abortions; \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  on Abortion Ruling; Russian Attack on Ukrainian Mall; John Hinckley, Jr. Exclusive; Ghislaine Maxwell to be Sentenced; Helping Mothers in Crisis; "The Late, Late Show" Goes to London; Mark Cuban Live, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Omar Villafranca, Scott  MacFarlane, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Ramy Inocencio, Taylor Johnson, Major  Garrett, Vladimir Duthiers, Jericka Duncan, Jan Crawford, Charlie D`Agata \par \uc2 13,883 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday morning, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We`re all here.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 RON NIRENBERG (San Antonio, Texas Mayor): The plight of migrants seeking refuge is always a humanitarian crisis. But tonight we are dealing with a horrific human tragedy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A horrific discovery in Texas: Dozens of migrants are found dead in a truck, children are among the survivors.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: An \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  trains rails after hitting a dump truck in rural Missouri. We will have the latest on this deadly collision.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Multiple injuries and we can confirm there are three fatalities.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A Russian airstrikes struck a bustling shopping mall in central Ukraine setting the building on fire and leaving many dead.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we get a stark warning from Hillary Clinton after the Supreme Court`s controversial decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (D) (Former US Secretary of State): Women are going to die, Gayle. Women will die.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The January 6th Committee called a surprise hearing for today.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It is to present "recently obtained evidence."\par \par \uc2 MAN: First tied three-three in the ninth and the crowded Yankee Stadium is riveting, and the crowd goes wild.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The US Senior Open out in Pennsylvania and here`s the shot of the day, Tim Petrovic to save par --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Honestly what he was trying to do.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Really?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes. Wait for it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 MAN: James Corden is back at home in the UK and he took "The Late, Late Show" with him including a new carpool karaoke, this time with Lizzo.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Carpool karaoke.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She was in there sanging.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sanging -- S-A-A-A-A-N-I-N-G. Didn`t you want to be in the backseat of that car? Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Have you done carpool karaoke?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, but I would love to. He would never ask me, but I would love to.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love it when you sing, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thank you so much. Oh, Nate got jokes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Playing games.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m serious.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate got jokes. He knows I cannot sing. It`s okay, Nate. I see you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love it when you sing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. All good.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some very powerful interviews to share with you today. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , she is speaking on camera for the very first time about the Supreme Court`s historic decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I don`t care what political party or religion you are, the question is who decides? Is the government going to be in your bedroom? Is the government going to be making these decisions?\par \par \uc2 We`re only at the beginning of this terrible travesty that this Court has inflicted on us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Inflicted on us" is the words she used and she is not holding anything back. She had a lot more to say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to that conversation.\par \par \uc2 Plus, for decades, we`ve known his name, but not his voice. We`ll hear from John Hinckley, Jr., the man who tried to assassinate President Reagan.\par \par \uc2 In his first ever TV interview, Hinckley tells our Major Garrett about his state of mind in 1981 and why he says he is different now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And when you said that, Nate, I thought I`ve never heard his voice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Never heard his voice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s such a good point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`ve never heard his voice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know his face. I know his name.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Never heard him speak, until today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I look forward to that.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with tragedy in Texas. A city worker hearing a call for help found a tractor trailer with at least 46 people inside, all of them dead, some describe as hot to the touch by authorities. They`re believed to be migrants seeking a better life here in the US.\par \par \uc2 Sixteen people were taken to hospitals, all, including four children with heat related injuries. This happened in San Antonio, Texas, which is no stranger to tragedies like this one and it is where we find our Omar Villafranca.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Three people in custody this morning. We know that. What else do we know this hour?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know the names or the ages of those who are dead, but we do know that of the 16 people taken to the hospital, 12 adults, four children.\par \par \uc2 San Antonio is not new to these mass migrant deaths, but the sheer number here has officials stunned.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RON NIRENBERG: Tonight, we are dealing with a horrific human tragedy.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: San Antonio Mayor \uc2 Ron Nirenberg\uc2  urged compassion and prayers as local officials described the grisly scene that awaited first responders yesterday evening.\par \par \uc2 The first 9-1-1 calls came in just before six in the afternoon, local time after a city worker in the area heard a cry for help coming from a nearby tractor trailer.\par \par \uc2 That witness then found the doors partially open with a number of deceased individuals inside. When officials arrived on the scene, the enormity of the tragedy became clear.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HOOD (San Antonio Fire Chief): We`re not supposed to open up a truck and see stacks of bodies in there.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Temperatures in the area yesterday ranged from the high 90s to the low one hundreds.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HOOD: No signs of water in the vehicle that was a refrigerated tractor trailer, but there was no visible working AC unit on that rig.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: This is not the first migrant related tragedy to strike San Antonio in recent years. Back in 2017, ten migrants died after they were found in a sweltering semi-trailer near a local \uc2 Walmart\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the sheer number of dead in this latest incident, it is once again shedding light on a migrant crisis along the Southern border and the human smugglers who get them there and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Already this fiscal year, \uc2 US Customs and Border Protection\uc2  has recorded more than 1.5 million encounters in the southwest, nearly the same amount as all of 2021.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: We don`t know where that trailer was headed, but we`re just a few hundred yards from I-35, the interstate highway which starts at the Texas-Mexico border and goes all the way up to Minnesota. It is a known smuggler`s route.\par \par \uc2 Investigators with \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  are here on the scene and they are now leading this investigation -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning to Missouri in the latest on a deadly train crash there. Dramatic video taken from inside the train show stunned passengers after seven of the \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  trains cars flipped onto the sides.\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of Rob Nightingale.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: At least three people were killed and more than 40 hurt.\par \par \uc2 With more on what caused the crash, Taylor Johnson with our Kansas City affiliate KCTV is near the scene.\par \par \uc2 Taylor, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Officials believed that that crash happened at an uncontrolled railroad crossing, which means that the only warning that drivers had were a Stop Sign and a Railroad Crossing Sign, so that means there were no lights or crossing rails.\par \par \uc2 Now that`s typical for rural areas like this one, but the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  will look to determine just how long the engineer had to react.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of Rob Nightingale.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Damn. It happened.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Please don`t step on the glass.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: A barefoot passenger stepped on glass windows while attempting to gain their footing inside one of the cars of the \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train that had been tossed on its side.\par \par \uc2 DISPATCH OPERATOR: Train derailment. Multiple injuries. One female under the train at this time.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: The crash occurred at 12:42 in the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 ROB NIGHTINGALE: We hit a truck. Someone was crossing. Someone was crossing.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: Eight cars and two locomotives of \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 `s Southwest Chief Train Four were carrying at least 207 passengers and 12 crew members from Los Angeles to Chicago.\par \par \uc2 RON GOULET (Passenger): We were in rural Missouri.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: Ron Goulet was a passenger heading to Chicago for a connecting departure.\par \par \uc2 RON GOULET: I felt an impact. The car was turning on its side and people were flying in all directions.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: Officials say the train collided with a dump truck that was stopped at a crossing, killing one person in the truck and at least two train passengers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of Dax McDonald.)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: Cellphone video from the interior shows passengers scrambling for their belongings after the crash.\par \par \uc2 JANE CARREON (Passenger): When I returned back later, it had been completely crushed. It was kind of terrifying realizing just how close I was to not being here right now.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: At least 40 other passengers were injured, several airlifted to area hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  Chair and CBS News analyst, Robert Sumwalt.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  does now have outward facing video cameras, as well as inward facing video cameras. They will be able to see the truck and see at what point it enters the tracks.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR JOHNSON: \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  says it fully supports the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 `s investigation and they say that they`re going to send a 16-person team out here to investigate the scene of the crash. But this is the second \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  crash to happen within the last few days. One hit a car with five people inside, three were killed and two were taken to the hospital -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Taylor Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Now to Washington and some potentially explosive testimony on Capitol Hill. The Committee investigating the January 6th attack is rushing back for a surprise hearing today.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 A lot of mystery surrounding this. Everybody is saying, this better be big.\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Gayle, we`ve learned that witness is Cassidy Hutchinson, she was a top aide before President Trump`s Chief of Staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . And according to Committee Court filings reviewed by CBS News, she was in the room for key meetings in those chaotic days before January 6.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a surprised move, the January 6th House Select Committee scheduled a new public hearing Tuesday with its witness shrouded in secrecy -- until now.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned that Cassidy Hutchinson is expected to testify. Hutchinson was an executive assistant for \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , who was former President Trump`s Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson`s testimony could be key because it could reveal whether her former boss or President Trump played a role in the alleged campaign to pressure then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , the Justice Department, and local election officials to help overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 In a closed door deposition earlier this year, Hutchinson told the committee close to a half dozen Republican Members of Congress asked for a pardon after the panel claims those lawmakers spread election lies.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Are you aware of any Members of Congress seeking pardons