uey-Burns is in Jackson, Mississippi where state officials have certified that an abortion ban in that state can indeed take effect.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning. A lot of uncertainty here. What happens now?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Hi, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Well, now that the law is going to go into effect, that means all abortions will be banned here in Mississippi except in the cases of rape and the threat to the life of the mother and that means that this clinic behind me is preparing to close.\par \par \uc2 Now, with all surrounding states also getting ready to implement their own trigger laws that means women seeking the procedure will have to travel over 400 miles for a provider.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Whose choice? Our choice.")\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Outrage, celebration, and confusion across the country as the Supreme Court`s decision leaves the issue of abortion up to the states. Thirteen states have laws in place that automatically ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, State Court Judges in Utah and Louisiana temporarily blocked those so-called trigger laws. Similar lawsuits have been filed in six other states with more expected.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MA (Center for Reproductive Rights senior staff attorney): Chaos has ensued across broad swaths of the South.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Jenny Ma is senior staff attorney at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 . She filed the lawsuit challenging Louisiana`s trigger laws.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MA: The fight is far from over.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Louisiana`s three remaining abortion clinics will now stay open pending a hearing next week.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MA: We argue that those cannot legally stand because they were so confusing and so vague and so, we are challenging those laws under the Louisiana Constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Mississippi`s only abortion clinic also filed a lawsuit arguing the law goes against the State`s Constitution.\par \par \uc2 AUDRIANA LEWIS-SCHOLES (Certified midwife): Everything looks pretty good.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Audriana Lewis-Scholes is a certified midwife in Mississippi who often drives two hours to see patients.\par \par \uc2 AUDRIANA LEWIS-SCHOLES: You taking your prenatal vitamins?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AUDRIANA LEWIS-SCHOLES: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: She is worried about the lack of resources for pregnant women in the state.\par \par \uc2 AUDRIANA LEWIS-SCHOLES: Sometimes we have patients that deliver on the side of the road and that`s the things also because they couldn`t make it to the hospital in time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, the State`s Governor, Tate Reeves says that he`s looking to expand programs to help women before, during, and after pregnancy and also looking for ways to incentivize adoption and extend safe haven laws here in Mississippi -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Caitlin Huey-Burns, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  says the Supreme Court`s monumental abortion ruling could lead to other decisions turning back the clock on Civil Rights.\par \par \uc2 In her first on-camera interview about the ruling, we asked her about Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` written opinion. He said that the Court should reconsider past rulings on contraception and same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Justice Thomas has sort of floated that out there about contraceptive rights -- contraception.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And about same sex marriages. But other Justices have pushed back to say no, he is really sort of on his own with that.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t believe that?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well, he may be on his own, but he is signaling as he often did -- you know, people -- I went to Law School with him. He has been a person of grievance for as long as I`ve known him -- resentment, grievance, anger -- and he has signaled in the past, to Lower Courts to State Legislatures, find cases, pass laws, get them up.\par \par \uc2 I may not win the first, the second, or the third time, but we`re going to keep at it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you`re saying people pay attention to this.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: Yes. The people he is speaking to, which is the -- you know, right-wing, very conservative Judges and Justices and State Legislatures.\par \par \uc2 And the thing that is -- well, there are so many things about it that are deeply distressing -- but women are going to die, Gayle. Women will die.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We reached out to Justice Thomas for a comment, we have not heard back from him, and Secretary Clinton had much more to say.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in our next hour, we ask her whether she thinks senators were misled by Supreme Court Justices during their confirmation hearing. Some say that they were.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 In the war on Ukraine, the country`s President says Russia committed a terrorist attack by destroying a mall full of shoppers. A missile hit the mall in Kremenchuk yesterday, killing at least 18 people and wounding many more.\par \par \uc2 Crews are pulling the wreckage apart this morning to help rescue workers search for more victims.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is there at the scene.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 This charred building is what is left of the Amstor Shopping Mall. Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says it was absolutely a targeted attack by Russia against innocent civilians.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Thick smoke swirled from the shopping complex Monday afternoon, a sign far and wide that Russian forces had struck again.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "This is a dangerous situation. Get to the shelter," yelled this policewoman while firefighters worked to put out the inferno and bystanders put up the injured into ambulances.\par \par \uc2 Others look for the names of loved ones on the list.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: At the hospital, this injured man said he saw lots of wounded people, burned people, some covered in blood.\par \par \uc2 His wife said a blast threw her into the air.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "It`s really scary. I tell women who left the country with their kids, don`t come back."\par \par \uc2 G7 leaders meeting in the German Alps said the attack was abominable and promised nearly $30 billion in new aid for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: President Zelenskyy in a televised address group said the country needs more modern and effective weapons.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And that`s to help shoot down incoming missiles. The frontline is just about 125 miles east from here and it is more a question of when, not if Russian rockets will strike again -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ramy Inocencio in Ukraine for us. Ramy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: The sentencing of Ghislaine Maxwell today could lead to high drama in the Courtroom. How we could learn more about her connection to sexual abuser, Jeffrey Epstein.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up: CBS News says John Hinckley, Jr.`s first ever TV interview. He shot President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  41 years ago. He is free now and he says he has changed completely.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett talks with Hinckley about the assassination attempt that almost -- almost -- changed history.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, James Corden is heading into his final year hosting "The Late, Late Show" -- single tear on that, James Corden -- we caught up with him in London for a lookback including, which carpool karaoke segment he`ll never forget.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I vote for Adele.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. That`s an invitation.\par \par \uc2 What do you get? Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Your local news --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time for the washing machine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Spin cycle.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 In his first ever TV interview, we are hearing now from the gunman who tried to assassinate President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  41 years ago.\par \par \uc2 John Hinckley is his name -- John Hinckley, Jr. -- and he changed the course of US history in 1981 when he shot and wounded the President and three other people outside a Washington, DC hotel.\par \par \uc2 He was a mentally ill, 25-year-old and after 30 years at a psychiatric facility, Hinckley`s restrictions were gradually lifted, and earlier this month, a Judge granted him full freedom.`\par \par \uc2 Hinckley sat down with our Major Garrett for an interview you will only see on CBS News.\par \par \uc2 Major, good morning. We know his name. We know his face. We have never heard his voice.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 One of the bullets that Hinckley fired ricocheted off the Presidential limousine, entered Ronald Reagan`s body and lodged within an inch of the President`s heart, nearly killing him.\par \par \uc2 Hinckley told us he thought the President was a "nice man" and a "good President." Those are direct quotes, but that his mental illness left him deeply divorced from reality.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR. (Attempted assassination on President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 ): I went to the Washington Hilton Hotel and he came out from giving a speech and I was right there and I fired shots at him, which so unfortunately hit other people, too.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you have any recollection of that feeling at that moment?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: No.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: None?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I don`t. It`s such -- it is such another lifetime ago, and I can`t tell you now the emotion I had, you know, right as he came walking. I just can`t tell you that.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mostly, that is something you can`t remember.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Because you don`t want to remember it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Maybe, maybe, but you`re right, it is something that I don`t want to remember. It was all just so traumatic.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The assassination attempts sent President Reagan to the hospital for nearly two weeks, paralyzed White House Press Secretary James Brady and wounded police officer Thomas Delahunty, and Secret Service agent Tim McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I shot four people, and I`m sorry to the Reagan family, the Brady family, the other families of the victims. I`m sorry to Jodie Foster, for bringing her into this.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Hinckley depressed, isolated, and delusional was obsessed with actress Jodie Foster. By attempting to kill President Reagan, he thought he could impress her.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I feel badly about that.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: What about the Reagan`s?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I feel badly for all of them. I mean -- I mean, I have true remorse for what I did. I know that they probably can`t forgive me now, but I just want them to know that I am sorry for what I did.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Have you ever thought about what would have happened? Had you succeeded?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Well, I mean, history would have been changed with, you know, a new President.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That`s it?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I mean, yes. It would have been a big historical event. I`m glad I did not succeed.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You are?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Oh, yes. Very much so.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You traumatized the nation, too.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I did.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Are you sorry for that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Yes. I`m sure the country was traumatized. I am very sorry for that.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Is there any part of you that wonders how the John Hinckley of that era could have gotten to that point?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I did not have a good heart. I was doing things that, you know, a good person doesn`t do so, it is hard for me at all -- to relate it on to that person back then.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Hinckley was judged mentally unfit to stand trial and spent more than 30 years at a mental institution in DC. He was released to his mother`s care in 2016 and granted full freedom earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Is your story and your freedom, justice?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Why is it just?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Because I was -- I was not just a cruel, calculating criminal in 1981. I truly believe I had a serious mental illness that was preventing me from knowing right from wrong back then.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Hinckley believes decades of treatment and medication make him no threat today.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I`ve been in hundreds, maybe thousands of therapies, individual and group. I`ve been the most scrutinized person in the entire mental health system for 41 years.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Is one of the reasons you`re sitting down with me to try to soften the public perception of you?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Yes. I`m just trying to show people, I`m kind of an ordinary, ordinary guy who is just trying to get along with everybody else.\par \par \uc2 If you just think I`m just some crazy person, I`m not that anymore at all.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The Reagan family, Brady family, and representatives for Jodie Foster did not reply to our request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Do you think you`ll ever have a chance to get closure with them?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I don`t know. I really don`t think the Brady family or the Reagan family or Jodie Foster, I don`t think they want to hear from me.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Would you like them to consider forgiving you?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: Yes. They probably can`t, but I wish -- I wish they would.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you want to give them a reason to?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I feel terrible -- terrible for what I did, and I`ve had remorse for many years for what I did. And if I could take it all back, I would. I swear, I would take it all back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Hinckley lives alone with his cat, Theo, whom he calls his best friend. He hopes to create a career as a singer/songwriter.\par \par \uc2 Three venues had small concerts booked for Hinckley, but they were all canceled due to concerns for security -- Hinckley`s and the audience`s and for the foreseeable future, Hinckley tells us, there`ll be no public performances -- guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Major. It is interesting to hear from him after all these years, to hear what he was thinking or better, what he was not thinking.\par \par \uc2 I can see why the families don`t want to talk to him, but I think the public wanted to know exactly what was going on and with -- I don`t mean this in a disrespectful way, but he seems still on medication. Is he? Is he okay mentally? He seemed heavily medicated to me.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So I asked, Gayle, if he was taking any medication specifically for his psychiatric illnesses. He said he is taking two pills a day, and he is sworn to his therapists and his doctors that he will stay on those meds.\par \par \uc2 He believes he is better and more stable. I think everyone can see he has kind of a flat effect and that might be the result of not only the medication, but long-term therapy.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know. He didn`t seem threatening to me, but we wanted to get his appraisal of this situation and get his apology on the record if we could, and that`s what we did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s what you did. Well, he clearly knew when you were asking him, clearly wanted to say something.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Major.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Appreciate it, Major.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Ghislaine Maxwell faces sentencing today for her role in Jeffrey Epstein`s sex abuse ring. We`ll look at what is expected to be a dramatic and very emotional day in Court.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The former socialite convicted of leading teenage girls to be sexually abused by Jeffrey Epstein will be sentenced today.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors want Ghislaine Maxwell to serve 30 years or more in prison. Now, her lawyers say she deserves far less.\par \par \uc2 Maxwell denies abusing anyone.\par \par \uc2 Our national correspondent, Jericka Duncan is outside the New York City Courthouse where Maxwell will learn her fate.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning to you. What time is the hearing? And what are you expecting?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Gayle, we expect to hear from women who said they survived the abuse of Jeffrey Epstein and Ghislaine Maxwell, all of this is really a culmination of accusations, which led to a conviction and now, it is up to a Judge as to how much time Ghislaine Maxwell will face behind bars.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Six months after Ghislaine Maxwell`s guilty verdict, she will learn if she will likely spend the rest of her life behind bars for recruiting and luring teen girls as young as 14 to be sexually abused by her longtime confidant, Jeffrey Epstein.\par \par \uc2 Four victims testified against her at trial.\par \par \uc2 Among them, Annie Farmer who told the Court, Maxwell at one point fondled her breasts at Epstein`s New Mexico ranch. In her victim impact statement, she is expected to tell the Court today, "The number of people harmed is impossible to measure," and ask the Judge to consider the, " ... ongoing suffering of the many women abused and exploited by Maxwell," including Annie`s Maria Farmer.\par \par \uc2 Maria wasn`t part of this trial, but in 2019, she told \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Anthony Mason that Epstein and Maxwell sexually assaulted her.\par \par \uc2 MARIA FARMER (Jeffrey Epstein and Ghislaine Maxwell accuser): I hate her more.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You hate --\par \par \uc2 MARIA FARMER: Ghislaine more, way more.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because?\par \par \uc2 MARIA FARMER: Because she`s a woman, and she fed women to him.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Maria worked the front desk in Epstein`s New York City townhouse in the 1990s when she was 26 years old.\par \par \uc2 MARIA FARMER: I saw Ghislaine going to get the women. She went to places like Central Park. I was with her a couple times in the car and she would dash out and get a child.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What did she say she was doing when she did this?\par \par \uc2 MARIA FARMER: Getting Victoria`s Secret models.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But Maxwell`s lawyers have argued she only became a target when Epstein died by suicide in 2019 while awaiting trial.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: This Judge knows this case inside and out.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: CBS News legal expert Rikki Klieman says it`s highly unlikely that Maxwell will get the roughly five years her team is seeking.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: This judge is going to be looking at a high end.\par \par \uc2 Ghislaine Maxwell is likely not going to get out of prison at any kind of viable age for her to live another new life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Maxwell`s attorneys say that she was put on unjustified suicide watch over the weekend, which they say hindered her ability to prepare for today`s sentencing, which is of course still going forward -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Jericka, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And a quick programming note for our viewers, we will be talking to one of those victims tomorrow. This is an interview you`ll hear on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 But up next now, Vlad Duthiers has got the stories you`ll be talking about today, but first, it is 7:44. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, you`re here. We`re tight. Take it away.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, born to run here.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to start with a new setback in the battle to free Brittney Griner.\par \par \uc2 Russian officials say the WNBA star will go on trial this week. The Olympic gold medalist appeared at a Courthouse outside Moscow for her preliminary hearing yesterday. The trial is set to begin on Friday and the Court extended Griner`s detention until the week before Christmas.\par \par \uc2 The Phoenix Mercury star was detained back in February when Russian authorities claim they found vape cartridges containing cannabis oil in her luggage at the Moscow Airport. The State Department says Griner is wrongfully detained and many say, a trial is a way for Russia to give the appearance of justice.\par \par \uc2 Griner faces up to 10 years in prison if she is convicted.\par \par \uc2 I just want to point out in addition to the horror of Brittney Griner being in prison, it highlights the inequity that the WNBA players face in the United States.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, very good point.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Rookies make $60,000.00 a year; in Russia, they can make a million dollars for the season.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why she was there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s why she is there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Most have them play overseas, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s heartbreaking to see what is happening to her though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It really is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is in the middle of a political game. It is very, very scary.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What else have you got, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so public figures standing up for a cause sometimes fade away after the news cycle moves on, not Matthew McConaughey of Uvalde, Texas. He has been calling for new gun laws since a gunman killed 21 people at an elementary school in his hometown last month.\par \par \uc2 McConaughey responded to the Gun Control Bill signed by President Biden on Saturday with this op-ed in "The Austin American-Statesman" headlined "America, the Land of And," that`s the title.\par \par \uc2 The Oscar winner wrote that in America, "We have freedom and responsibility. Yes, we have a mental health issue in our country, and we need to create guardrails to keep guns out of irresponsible hands." He prays Congress for agreeing to enhance background checks, and increasing funding for mental health support in schools.\par \par \uc2 Some requests he made during an emotional visit to the White House earlier this month did not end up in the new law, including raising the minimum age to buy an AR-15 rifle from 18 to 21.\par \par \uc2 Very vocal on this and is continuing to sort of shine a light on what he sees as a real issue in this country. But he talks about this notion of bringing us together. It`s not about red or blue, it is not about Republican or Democrat, it`s about finding commonsense solutions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He says this is a human issue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I will forever --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take politics out of it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I will forever support Matthew McConaughey and for the issues that he shows up for.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He doesn`t want to play the "what about ism game" like, what about mental health?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also it`s an "And."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. He`s not talking about, you know, well, if you say this, then I can point to this. It`s really an issue that faces all of us. We have to come together to fix it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he is serious about it, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Kudos to Matthew McConaughey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, kudos to another guy who feels the need --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For speed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was waiting. I was hoping somebody was going to get that.\par \par \uc2 All right, so of course we`re talking about "Top Gun: Maverick" which has soared to a milestone at the global box office. The long-awaited sequel to the 1986 blockbuster flew past the billion dollar mark in ticket sales over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 It is only the second movie to hit that number during the pandemic. "Spider-Man: No Way Home" was the first; "Top Gun: Maverick" is also the first billion dollar hit for its star, Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 I was actually surprised about that because I feel like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is surprising.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is always making like bank --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Blockbuster hits.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. Exactly. He is Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 "Top Gun: Maverick" is distributed by \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , of course, which is owned by our very own parent company, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the movie --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should all be sending him some flowers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is still soaring, too as well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Paramount is going to send flowers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congratulations, Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Send him some flowers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, our sit down with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , you won`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, more of our fascinating conversation with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , why she hopes the end of Roe v. Wade will be a wake-up call for America.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil. Americans pay more for prescription drugs than people in most other countries. Well, billionaire businessman, Mark Cuban says he`s got a solution, and he is here to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. We`ve got a bit of "The Late, Late Show" early, early today. James Corden talks with Charlie D`Agata in London about why he is stepping down after a remarkable eight-year run.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. He`s going to have a good time this week.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RON NIRENBERG: We are dealing with a horrific human tragedy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Forty six people are dead inside a tractor trailer in San Antonio in what appears to be a case of human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HOOD: There was no visible working AC unit on that rig.\par \par \uc2 DISPATCH OPERATOR: One female under the train.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Three people are dead after a train crash in rural Missouri.\par \par \uc2 RON GOULET: People were flying in all directions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In his first ever TV interview, John Hinckley, Jr. tell CBS News about the day in 1981 he tried to assassinate President Reagan.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HINCKLEY, JR.: I`ve had remorse for many years for what I did, and if I could take it all back, I would.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  speaks on-camera for the first time following the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: We are only at the beginning of this terrible travesty that this Court has inflicted on us.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show With Stephen Colbert" host): On Thursday, we were in the Ed Sullivan Theater, and tonight, we`re in Medieval Times.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That Roe v. Wade decision is also getting a lot of attention across Late Night.\par \par \uc2 WANDA SYKES (Actress): I`m a Black, gay woman, and I have a daughter, so I`m not doing so well, right now.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show with James Corden" host): We are instantly back to a dark age where a Court has imposed the minority political view on a country for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody is still talking about that decision.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they will be probably for years and decades.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. We will all remember that day.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with this, speaking of the decision. Former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton says the overturning of Roe v. Wade should be a wake-up call for Americans.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with her yesterday in the studio. This was her first on-camera interview about the Supreme Court`s monumental ruling. The decision triggered laws to prevent women in at least seven states -- seven -- from getting legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 Now Clinton was here yesterday with her co-author to discuss her novel, "State of Terror." It is co-published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which is a division of our corporate owner, that`s \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She told us that this is a risky time for American democracy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Last week, the Roe v. Wade decision was made. And even though we had gotten a heads up, many people thought it was unthinkable. A lot of people are still processing depending on your point of view.\par \par \uc2 What did you think when you heard it?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well, I was not surprised because I think that was the goal of packing the Court with Justices who are on the record for many years of being against women`s constitutional rights to make decisions about our own bodies.\par \par \uc2 I was deeply sorry that it actually happened, but now, that it has happened, I think everybody understands that this is not necessarily the only effort that we`re going to see this Court undertake to turn back the clock on Civil Rights --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Other issues.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : And Gay Rights and Women`s Rights beyond abortion. This is going to, I hope, wake up a lot of Americans. I don`t care what political party or religion you are, the question is who decides? Is the government going to be in your bedroom? Is the government going to be making these decisions?\par \par \uc2 We`re only at the beginning of this terrible travesty that this Court has inflicted on us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what do you say to supporters, though, who say listen, we are protecting the rights of unborn children. They have rights, too. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : You know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This would be the core argument.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I said that, you know, that is predominantly a belief rooted in religion, which I respect and that`s why in a pluralistic democracy like ours, the Court and Roe v. Wade said the government is not going to make this decision.\par \par \uc2 If it is your personal belief based on whatever that there is no legitimate basis for abortion, even if you`re in the hospital and you`re bleeding out, even if you`re told that you have cancer and chemotherapy is necessary, and therefore an abortion is necessary, no matter what, you get to make that decision. I might not agree with it, but you get to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 But you, and those who support your particular point of view cannot dictate that decision to every other woman. And, you know, for many years, the abortion rate was going down. We were seeing, I think greater responsibility, especially with the use of contraception.\par \par \uc2 So what is it their -- what is their ultimate goal here, if they begin to chip away at all of these rights? It truly is to erase the progress that women have made from the last 150 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, a couple of the senators have said that they were misled by the Justices. Does this say to you that we need to change the confirmation hearing process?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well, I think they were misled, in part, because they wanted to be misled. They had --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think they knew that this was going to happen?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: They either knew or they were blind to what the history of the people before them. Anyone who is surprised by this is not paying attention.\par \par \uc2 So these people were selected for this purpose, and it was a 50-year campaign. And you`ve got to give the other side, you know, lots of points for their relentlessness, their total commitment to getting what they want done, regardless of who is hurt by it, and regardless of who is stripped of rights.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it the time for the Democrats to take on the filibuster, do you think?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : Well, I`ve been saying for quite some time, I think the filibuster should be eliminated, at the very least for constitutional questions like voting rights, like a woman`s, you know, constitutional rights to make decisions about her own body.\par \par \uc2 So if you can`t lift it completely, lift it for those two things that have huge consequences for our country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And last question, you`re a former First Lady, former Secretary of State, former Senator, do you miss the day-to-day of politics? Is there any scenario in your brain that you would think, "I want to get back in?"\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : No, but I miss it. I miss it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is no scenario in 2024, you would even remotely consider?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : You know, I can`t imagine it. I really can`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s not a no.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : But -- well, but what I can`t imagine is staying as active and outspoken as I can because I think -- I think our country is really on the precipice, Gayle. I think that we are looking at not only the erosion of these rights, throwing the door open to unfettered, unregulated gun access, but we`re also looking at dismantling the Federal government, how it protects our air, and our water, and everything else that goes along with it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think our democracy is at stake?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re looking at January 6th hearings.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think that the democracy is at stake.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As we sit here today.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Will anything come of these hearings? People say -- some say, it`s a waste of time.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: I think these hearings have been incredibly powerful. I understand a lot of people pay no attention, but history pays attention.\par \par \uc2 I hope the Justice Department is paying attention. I hope in these midterm elections, candidates in the Democratic Party pay attention. Because you cannot let those who attacked our Capitol and those who urged them on to do so, act with impunity. That`s what happens in dictatorships and authoritarian regimes.\par \par \uc2 So I think it is important that people recognize, if there were ever a time to vote and to vote literally for your rights and our future, it is this midterm.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, you know, she had a lot to say. She was really very candid and very open, and I like what she said that history pays attention.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because, you know, people don`t say no one is paying attention to these hearings, but the first hearing, 20 million people watched. People are tuning in. People want to know exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 So we will see. Today is going to be a very big day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m so glad she ended with reference to voting in this country, because when it comes to democracies, our participation rate is abysmal relative to other countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And still people complain, though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People complain, 80 million people don`t vote. And when you see \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  on television, what I think about is all the what-ifs from 2016. What could have been different?\par \par \uc2 It was a small number of people that swung the election. She won the popular vote. If people had gone to the polls, three Supreme Court Justices that had been appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might have been appointed by her, we don`t have this decision today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, she is spot on. She said we`re turning back the clock.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And speaking of voting, today is Primary Day in New York.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know that.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we will have more of our conversation with former Secretary of State Clinton. She and her co-author, Louise Penny will discuss her book, "State of Terror" and tell us what inspired -- this is a political thriller -- and the main character in the book, she is a Secretary of State, and she`s dealing with a former President that she thinks is a bit of a knucklehead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How about that? Based on a true story. Inspired by true events, I think is the phrase.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a novel.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it`s fiction, though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It is fiction, Nate. It is fiction.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Inspired by true events, all right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`re working together. It is a great story, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I`m looking forward to that part of the conversation as well.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, in the wake of the Supreme Court`s abortion ruling, we`re going to look at a shelter that is finding new ways to help pregnant women in crisis.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:10. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, we`ll talk to "The Late, Late Show" host, James Corden in London where he is hosting his show this week. Why he is stepping away from the late night at the height of his success.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, a follow up to the story of a \uc2 Burger King\uc2  worker who got nothing but a goodie bag for his 27 years of service.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning we`re learning more about a shelter that offers help to pregnant women and mothers in crisis and how it could expand after the Supreme Court ruling on abortion.\par \par \uc2 St. Mary`s Shelter in Virginia opened its doors 16 years ago and says it has helped hundreds of women.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford looks at its mission to "Ensure the health of mother and baby."\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 You know, Mary`s Shelter has several homes. It is faith-based, but they`ve taken women regardless of their views.\par \par \uc2 Now many of the women were considering abortion, but Mary`s Shelter offers housing and help getting on their feet with their children for up to three years.\par \par \uc2 The Director told us, it is not a solution for everyone. It is mainly for those underserved women who feel they have nowhere else to go.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In 2014, Shawnte Mallory lost her job. She had two young kids at home and couldn`t pay her rent.\par \par \uc2 When she got pregnant again, she decided to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY (Former Mary`s Shelter client): It wasn`t what I wanted to do, it was just at the moment, it was -- that`s what everybody was telling me to do, so I felt like that was really my only option.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Then, she found out about Mary`s Shelter.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: It changed my life.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It changed your life.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: It gave me a new start. I didn`t have to give up or get rid of a child that I knew I wanted just because things got hard. I didn`t have a support system.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Twenty-five-year-old, Logan Wilkins also found Mary`s Shelter in a moment of hopelessness.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS (Former Mary`s Shelter client): I was sleeping behind a dumpster at my job.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You`re literally sleeping behind a dumpster?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: Yes. I was literally sleeping behind a dumpster eight months pregnant.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: How were you able to survive?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: It was really difficult. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON (Mary`s Shelter Executive Director and Founder): We turn no one away because we know that their situation at that moment is just that desperate.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Kathleen Wilson started Mary`s Shelter 16 years ago. More than 300 women have come through their doors, some staying up to three years in fully furnished shared homes and apartments with their children.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON: We have another kitchen upstairs.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: While they get parenting training and help with employment and housing.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON: When they leave here, that independent lifestyle becomes, you know, a real viable reality for them as opposed to just, "Well, you had the baby and you`re done," which is not the way any of the ministries I know work.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Women come from up and down the East Coast to stay here, including a woman and her children who arrived unannounced from Florida the day of our visit.\par \par \uc2 Wilson who opposes abortion say with states now free to ban or restrict abortion, more women will need help, but pregnancy shelters are not for every situation.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned that women will be forced into unwanted pregnancies?\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON: No, I don`t. I mean, I think that there will be some. I hope that they`ll reach out to other agencies like maybe an adoption agency or that families will step up. I think we`ll have to increase support groups for moms like those.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: This is not a solution for every woman who potentially may not be able to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN WILSON: Right. No. Not everybody needs a home. Not everybody needs to move out of their house. Not everybody needs to leave a violent situation, but we can help those that are having abortions because they feel hopeless.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: I had no motivation. I didn`t have any kind of guidance.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Mallory, now 31 with five daughters says Mary`s Shelter helped her find purpose.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: I am in Bible College studying for Theology. I also am a Peer Recovery Coach, so I help those that are battling with addictions, traumas from like sexual abuse, or just past traumas.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Wilkins, now a mother of four, returned to Mary`s Shelter for the second time last December while pregnant with her son, Leon. He is three months old.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: I just got a new job where -- in the career field that I want to be in, the medical field.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: So what are you doing?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: I am a scheduler, but I`m getting my medical billing and coding certification. In four months, I should be done in October.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What`s the message of your story?\par \par \uc2 SHAWNTE MALLORY: That there is hope, that there are places around that can help you that, you know, having an abortion isn`t the final answer.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What about you know?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN WILKINS: Abortion isn`t the final answer. There are so many other options. And to be quite honest, it is literally all worth it when you have that baby.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, Mary`s Shelter gets its funding from private donors and fundraising events and the Director told us that it has helped open shelters in other states and hopes to keep expanding -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, Jan, it`s a beautiful option for women who want that option. The key thing is for the women who want it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for your coverage yesterday and also today, Jan. It continues to be just absolutely superb. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Thank you. Thanks, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, Charlie D`Agata switching places with James Corden as "The Late, Late Show" goes on the road to London.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Mr. James Corden, how are you?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Charlie, how are you?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Welcome to the show.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: This is wonderful. I`ve never done this. I`ve never been -- I`ve never done this before.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Neither have I.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I`ve never done -- wow.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: I`ve always wanted the experience.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: It is quite the experience.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: How does it feel?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Well, I understand now when guests say "This isn`t a very comfy couch." It isn`t. It`s true.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie D`Agata has got the hair for the job.\par \par \uc2 But, I`ll tell you what, looking pretty good at that anchor chair.\par \par \uc2 Corden talks to Charlie about overcoming long odds to find success in his effort at late night and what`s next as he eyes the end of an eight-year run as host of "The Late, Late Show."\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You meet the nicest people in the greenroom.\par \par \uc2 We`re here with billionaire, investor, entrepreneur, really all-around nice guy, Mark Cuban. Look at his shirt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at his shirt, Nate. It says "Be a Better Human."\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN (costplusdrugs.com co-founder) "Be a Better Human.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come in, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was in the back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: From one nice guy to another. Tony was in the bathroom.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: How are you? Good to see you, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he wanted to see you.\par \par \uc2 He is launching a new challenge to the drug industry. He will tell us how you can get generic medicines at a low price -- how low is low, Mark Cuban?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Like three cents sometimes. We`ll cut your cost on all your medications.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want that deal.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 And Tony, you`re kicking it off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m kicking it off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you feeling good as well, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m feeling real good. I`ve got an artifact to help aid in my "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So about 10 years ago, when I was a writer for Tina Brown`s "Newsweek," I went down in a submarine off the coast of Hawaii, and through the little portal, we found this, which is a mug that was knocked or thrown from a World War Two Navy vessel, and it was at the bottom of the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 I keep it on my desk.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you took that home?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I took it home. They grabbed it with a little claw and I got to keep it and it just stirs my imagination. I don`t know who threw it, who knocked it, how it got down there, but it just connects me to that era. Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you have to take a look at that. I`m bringing it up because explorers in the Philippines recently found --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is in good shape, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A shipwreck at more than four miles down, deepest shipwreck ever.\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of Caladan Oceanic.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a World War Two era Navy Destroyer that was sunk in battle, and it is another connection to that -- well, you see the \uc2 Corning Glass\uc2  on the bottom?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s an American made mug.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s in good shape.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So our connection to the history, even after the waves covered over is renewed again with this discovery.\par \par \uc2 This is the USS Samuel B. Roberts, popularly known as the "Sammy B," fought heroically in World War Two and that what you`re looking at is the final resting place for 89 heroes -- 89 sailors helping America win that war.\par \par \uc2 So I wanted to shout out the discovery, the service, and also bring in that little artifact because it`s -- it really just -- there`s something about touching what was touched by history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: History lost under --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The surface of the sea. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kept well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, I love it when the future of technology meets the medical space. So my "Talk of the Table" is how medical students are starting to use patient holograms to practice on.\par \par \uc2 So here is how it works. They are wearing mixed reality headsets. These students are able to see each other, interacting with multilayered and medically accurate holographic patients. They are called hollow scenarios in their lifelike holograms, and you can access this from anywhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 Students will take several modules using the technology. The first will focus on respiratory conditions, emergencies, and then they`re also working on cardiology and neurology in the development.\par \par \uc2 So I just -- I just love this because we oftentimes look at the future and it seems like the future is now. It`s not too far ahead, 10 to 20 years. Now, they`re doing so many good things that students can really access all of this technology.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They can practice now poking holes in my arm with that needle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still want Dr. Kurth in the room with me. Just saying, I`m a big baby.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is how the students practice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a follow up on his story from last week. You remember this online video that Kevin Ford revealed the goodie bag he was given us a reward for working for \uc2 Burger King\uc2  for 27 years and never missing a day of work.\par \par \uc2 You know, there was some Lifesavers, there was a movie ticket for one. There was a lanyard --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is painful.\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of FastRitz/Reddit.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, yes, we still were like, "Okay, \uc2 Burger King\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 His family thought that he deserved a little bit more than that. So, they started an online fundraiser for him.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this, last week when we told it, it raised $30,000.00. Now, it`s raised more than $170,000.00.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. And this is what Kevin Ford told TMZ about that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FORD (\uc2 Burger King\uc2  employee): If I had enough money, you know, I would buy a new Ford Hybrid, because I`m American. I`d also like to maybe take a day off and relax, and go see my friends.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, he lives in Vegas, but he has got job offers from all over the country. Somebody in Charleston, a manager said, "It would be an honor to work with you." The pitch was you could work at a beachside restaurant and have unbeatable tacos. But he said no, he turned it down. He wants to continue to work for \uc2 Burger King\uc2 , he says.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because he basically still likes it there, but he is just one of those really good guys that people heard about his story and want to do right by him, and they did.\par \par \uc2 David Spade -- we got the story because David Spade donated $5,000.00 on his own and it keeps growing and growing and it`s still up.\par \par \uc2 I think this is great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With all that newfound money, with the newfound money, I say \uc2 Burger King\uc2  go out and do the right thing and help him get his own \uc2 Burger King\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, a franchise.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right? He has enough money to put down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I like that, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, owner of a \uc2 Burger King\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`d be a great owner. He doesn`t miss a day of work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. And he likes the company and he says he wants to buy a Ford Hybrid because he`s an American.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because I`m American.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: American. We like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: American.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Switching gears, James Corden, he is not American, but he is fantastic. He is bringing "The Late, Late Show" to London this week for the last time. I say big sigh on that.\par \par \uc2 The host has confirmed he`ll be leaving his show here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  next summer. He has an all-star lineup for the show this week, but first, our Charlie D`Agata turned the tables on Corden in the country where he was born.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: James Corden has taken the show on the road and across the pond.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: It is so great to be back, to be in my hometown --\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Returning to London for the first time in three years and the last time ever, and we got a chance to switch sides with the star.\par \par \uc2 Mr. James Corden.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Charlie, how are you?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Welcome to the show.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: This is wonderful. I`ve never done this. I`ve never been -- I`ve never done this before.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Neither have I.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I`ve never done -- wow.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: I`ve always wanted to.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: It is quite the experience.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: How does it feel?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Well, I understand now when guests say this isn`t a very comfy couch. It isn`t. It`s true.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Among the London leg features, carpool karaoke with Lizzo, and a segment from the Oval Office as the President`s PA.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): Come in.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Mr. President --\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Well, how are you?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I`m very well, sir. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But Corden will soon be bowing out.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: This will be -- this will be my last year hosting "The Late, Late Show."\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Calling it a day on late night after an eight-year run.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I don`t know if I`ll ever truly realize what we did with the show, how far the odds were stacked against us, and how we just managed to somehow pull it off.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It seems like you`re getting emotional.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: How can you not? It would be -- it would be weird not to be, where I was so absolutely definite in my head that we would be on the air for three months and then be fired. It was -- it wasn`t -- it wasn`t an if, it was a when.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Instead, "The Late, Late Show" and the host himself a relative unknown in the US went stratospheric in part by blowing up the internet.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The carpool karaoke with Adele alone racked up more than 250 million views.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And there aren`t many people on the planet who get invited to join right in a jet with Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But there is one particular highlight that struck a chord with Corden.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: The carpool karaoke with \uc2 Paul McCartney\uc2  in Liverpool. I sort of knew when we were doing it that we might be capturing something special.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I remember turning to my best friend, Ben, who is the executive producer of the show and saying, "That`s the best thing we will ever shoot."\par \par \uc2 If my granddad was here right now, he`d get an absolute kick out of this.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MCCARTNEY (Beatles): He is.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Their emotional journey, included a surprise performance at a pub.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: All have these things you`re like, if these were auction prizes, I think they`d go for millions and millions of dollars. If an auction prize was Tom Cruise will fly you around in a fighter jet.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: And he`ll be Maverick and you can be Goose.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But James, my friend --\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: That brings us to the big question. Why? Why are you leaving us?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I don`t know what else we can do. We can`t jump out of another plane with Tom Cruise or drive down Penny Lane singing "Penny Lane" with \uc2 Paul McCartney\uc2  or shoot carpool karaoke is with Adele and the First Lady.\par \par \uc2 You know, you have to make a decision and go, I never ever wanted this to be something I was ever bored with or just a thing that I do.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Whether the next thing he does takes place in America or takes him back here to Britain, he won`t say.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show with James Corden.")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But late, late night won`t be the same without someone like James.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Charlie D`Agata, London.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is going to be so missed. I`m so glad we still have another year. Guess who he really want for carpool karaoke?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Who?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The one person he hasn`t gotten.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Who is that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Guess? Queen Bee.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh Beyonce. Oh that would be good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I so hope she`ll do it. I so hope she`ll do it in this final year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That would be amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s gotten everybody else that he wanted, but he`s left us with such a legacy. What I think is so great is we didn`t know who he was.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I never heard of him, and now, everybody adores this man, myself included.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. He is so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know who else we adore? Our next guest --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, billionaire investor --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Super cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mark Cuban, he will be here to explain why he thinks he can sell prescription drugs for less than Americans are paying now.\par \par \uc2 Sit right there, don`t you go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Americans spend around $365 billion a year for prescription drugs and the prices here are higher than in many other countries, but Mark Cuban, billionaire investor says he has got a way to help with a new online pharmacy called costplusdrugs.com, and the company says it can offer lower prices for more than 800 generic drugs.\par \par \uc2 Mark, who is also owner of the NBA`s \uc2 Dallas Mavericks\uc2  is here to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know other billionaires do other things with their time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And their money.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And money. Why did you want to do this? And then how are you able to do this?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. How?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: I mean, the why is very simple. I mean, we all know people who have had to make decisions between their rent and food and medication or they`ve had to ration their medication, and that`s just wrong in the United States of America in 2022.\par \par \uc2 And so the why was really easy, and the how, I literally had a doctor, Dr. Alex Oshmyansky, who cold e-mailed me and he was building a pharmacy that was able to make rare drugs more cost effectively and I was like, "Why limit it`s a rare drugs? Let`s build a company, and rather than working within the traditional system, let`s work outside of it and just have complete transparency and sell our drugs at a set markup of cost plus 15 percent." So that`s what we do.\par \par \uc2 If you go to costplusdrugs.com, put in the medication that you take, and you`ll see one. What we pay for it for manufacturers, you`ll see our markup of 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: We charge a $3.00 handling fee, and then we charge $5.00 for shipping. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 And when you add that all up --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It sounds so simple. It almost sounds too good to be true. Remember what your mom said if it`s too good to be true --\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: It`s too good to be true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: But what`s happened is the legacy drug industry, it`s worked for them. Right? So why change it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: And typically, when someone starts a company to try to disrupt drug pricing, they try to work within the existing system because that`s where the money is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: And so you know, I`m blessed financially. So I was able to work with Alex and say, "Look, let`s go after generics first. Let`s be completely transparent, so people can trust us and let`s sell drugs." And it`s just blown up. It`s insane the way we`ve changed people`s mind.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it is shaking up the industry.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: For sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All the while, you said, you don`t spend money on marketing. This is word of mouth. People that are actually saying, "This is helping my life."\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Without question.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And unfortunately, there are more and more people who are looking to save money anywhere in their life, because so much else in their life is going up in price.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Without question.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So without question, I mean, do you think at this point, we`re headed for a recession and more trouble?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: You know what, I can only control what I can control, right? Our mission at Cost Plus Drugs is to be the low cost provider for every medication on the planet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Right? And so right now, we`re at 800 plus generics, but we`ll be adding branded medications as well. So we`re talking to all the biggest companies, and our biggest challenge is just getting them to sell to us. But once we get there, we really think we can disrupt and turn the whole pharmaceutical industry upside down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So this guy just sends you a cold e-mail.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t know him?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Never.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you believe it when you read it? Did you think, "I want to get into this" or did you --\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Because why not follow up, right? So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was it the "Shark Tank" instinct kicking in? You know --\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: A hundred percent. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: A hundred percent. But you know, Dr. Oshmyansky is a radiologist. He has a PhD in Mathematics. He does scientific research. And just for fun, he went to Law School for a year, you know. He is kind of a smart guy, and so that came through very quickly.\par \par \uc2 And he did the work, right? And that`s the key. So, you know, he does -- he is the CEO and runs the minute-by-minute, day-by-day and I deal with strategic and marketing and finances. And, you know, we`re growing so quickly. We`re already past our two-year expectations and we`ve only been shipping for five months.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so fascinated by you, Mark Cuban.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We all are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Honestly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, a lot of people admire you because you have found success, you are wealthy. The question I have, when did you feel like you needed to make more of an impact on the world that you live in?\par \par \uc2 Because like Tony opened up, with multimillionaires, billionaires, they spend their time, spend their money doing things that are very self- serving.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why do you feel like you want to serve the world around you?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Look, I`m the luckiest guy in the world, you know, and to be able to find something that can change people`s lives for the better, why won`t I? My next dollar is not going to change my life one way or the other. You know, it`s not going to make a difference for my children or their children or their children.\par \par \uc2 But if, you know, when you meet people or having experienced it myself when I didn`t have any money. Like you look at my fingers, and you can see I couldn`t afford a doctor, right? I can tell you other stories.\par \par \uc2 But to be able to create medication -- be able to make medicine affordable, that`s a game changer. It can change people`s lives.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you ever think about politics, Mark? Only because you`ve talked about that Biden administration that you said has absolutely zero charisma, you`ve said that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re somebody that nobody can question the charisma you have. And I know it takes more than charisma. But do you ever think about politics?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: I mean, like everybody in this country right now, you think about it, and you wonder, you know, "Could I do a better job" or any number of a thousand people do a better job? But the underlying problem isn`t whether it`s me, it`s really the duopoly that we have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Right? The fact -- do we really think that the Republican and Democratic Parties present the best candidates? And the answer is no. Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: And are there better ones?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On both sides.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: On both sides, right, and so I`m a big fan of rank choice voting because I think it opens up the candidate pool significantly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love rank choice voting.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: I mean, you know, we can go talk for days about that kind of stuff, but I think you know, all the things that we talk about, whether it`s abortion, whether it`s gun control, that`s just about power dynamics.\par \par \uc2 And I think to change those power dynamics, you have to work outside the system, and that`s why Cost Plus Drugs -- see how I got it right back there --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: That`s how costplusdrugs.com was born --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will say it for you, costplusdrugs.com.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Real quick before we go, in one word, cryptocurrency.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: I`m still bullish.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bullish. Mark Cuban, we`ll be right back. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am, too. I am, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s not the word I expected.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have 20 seconds to get off the air, but I`m looking at your shirt, Mark, I think it says it all "Be A Better Human." Do you have any closing words before we go?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: Yes, if you`re looking to save money on your mediation, go to costplusdrugs.com.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve been taking notes.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: What`s more important --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s that called again?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Did I get it right?\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: costplusdrug.com.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it. You got that people?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: See you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No advertising budget.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN: None.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve got no advertising.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Word of mouth, baby.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220628ei6s000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Landmark Decision\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was 1973, nearly 50 years ago when the Supreme Court issued Roe versus Wade. That landmark decision determined the Constitution protects a woman`s right to an abortion, at least in the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 Now, the case struck down many State and Federal abortion laws that were considered to cause an undue burden, but that ruling was not the final word.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re going to go to the CBS News archives to review the history of Roe v. Wade and the conflict that has lasted for decades.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hundreds of thousands of women married and unmarried are desperately seeking to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: Before the 1973 ruling of Roe v. Wade, 30 states banned abortion outright.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I figured that I have one child, I should do the best I can for that child, rather than having two and not being able to.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I don`t feel very good about it, but it is something I had to do.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A child in the womb has rights and needs the protection of law.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In a landmark ruling the Supreme Court today legalized abortions.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Other opponents are now talking of a constitutional amendment to reverse today`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We gather here today on the first anniversary of one of the blackest days in the history of American jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think the pro-life movement is kind of flexing its muscles.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Murder is murder.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: In the decades that followed the decision, changes to many Federal and State laws sought to curb or expand abortion access.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When a teenager here in Indiana decides she wants an abortion, but can`t tell her parents, she generally makes the 110-mile trip to Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 MAN: She died as a result of a botched illegal abortion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: People in general are going to start voting on abortion.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re simply telling the awful truth that little baby boys and girls are being dismembered.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Keep your hands off our right to choose.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: While many have condemned this campaign to frighten doctors and clinic directors, the truth is, said one doctor today, it`s working.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Abortion has long been a sensitive issue in this country. It has divided Americans for decades and has recently sparked intense debate over organizations like Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The suspect in the deadly siege of a Planned Parenthood clinic is due in a few hours to make his first Court appearance.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (US Attorney General): Threats of violence and actual violence against the Justices, of course, strike at the heart of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: The 2016 presidential election brought renewed focus on the fight over Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE (Then FOX News Channel anchor): Do you want to see the Court overturn Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: Well, if we put another two or perhaps three Justices on, that`s really what`s going to be -- that will happen.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2  (\uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice): Roe v. Wade is an important precedent of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oklahoma is the latest state to move closer to restricting abortion access.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I was raped, and I was 19.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Abortion is murder, and I feel that way. Period.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Other states like Indiana, Nebraska are considering similar laws.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Fifty years of progress being undone will not be accepted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Spreading fallout from that stunning -- and stunning is the word -- Supreme Court leak of a draft opinion that suggests that Roe v. Wade is set to be overturned.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there you have it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And here we are in 2022. The history is long when you look at how long we`ve been discussing and the people thought that this day would never happen. It seemed unthinkable. And yet, here we are in 2022.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nearly a 50-year fight to get to this moment, and now people are saying, it`s going to be another 50-year fight for there to be a change.\par \par \uc2 People also talking about minority rule about unelected judges. We`ve got 80 million people who don`t vote at all. This as a result.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I say what`s not going to be a fight, breaking news, I heard Tony Dokoupil just re-signed his contract.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did. I didn`t know we were going to work that in --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just got the thing and it says you`re going to buy a new pair of shoes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I got them on actually, Gayle. Thanks for inspiring --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6r000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The History Behind the Abortion Battle\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 1,460 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to continue our coverage of the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Now, this follows nearly half a century of conflict and a concerted campaign by anti-abortion groups.\par \par \uc2 Long before this weekend`s protests, we`ve seen violence against abortion clinics and the providers, too, and frequently vicious -- and vicious is the word -- harassment of women seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 There has also been a relentless effort to change the landscape politically in Congress, legislatures, and the Courts.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford takes a look at how we got here.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve got a landmark ruling, the Supreme Court today legalized abortions.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In 1973, Roe versus Wade was a bombshell.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The anti-abortion laws of 46 states were rendered unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Four states that only recently passed laws legalizing some form of abortion, while 13 others have begun allowing it with restrictions.\par \par \uc2 By stepping in to make abortion legal nationwide, some Justices at the time hope the decision would end the debate over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 But the anti-abortion rights movement mobilized almost immediately, beginning first at the grassroots level.\par \par \uc2 This is the first March for Life and that was a year exactly after Roe came down.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI (March for Life President): Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Jeanne Mancini is President of March for Life.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI: The March has grown younger and bigger almost every single year.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: How important has it been to have that yearly reminder that we`re still here?\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI: I can`t think of a more symbolic picture of Roe not being settled law than seeing the growing number of marchers.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: That social movement became more strategic in 1992.\par \par \uc2 RANDALL TERRY (American activist): Today, three Reagan-Bush appointees stabbed the pro-life movement in the back.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Supreme Court was poised to overturn Roe by a five-four vote until the last minute switch by Justice \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2 , who joined fellow Republican nominees, \uc2 Sandra Day O`Connor\uc2  and David Souter to save it.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER (Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America President): That moment and 1992 was deeply disappointing. It also helped me understand how much we could depend upon Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Which is how much?\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: Which is not much. That was the point where we realized, unless we strengthen this political arm at the center of the movement. We will continue to lose.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Within months, Marjorie Dannenfelser who has been called the woman who has been called "The woman who killed Roe" founded that political organization, \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: Every time there was a devastating blow, the pro- life movement grew. That is a mark of a successful movement.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The next devastating blow came in the 2000s.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY (R-UT): I would welcome a circumstance where there was such a consensus of this country that we said we don`t want to have abortion in this country at all, but that`s not where we are.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What Dannenfelser said Republicans paid lip service to the movement and lost the presidency twice.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: We said no way is this ever going to happen again. We`re going to reward our friends, punish our political enemies.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In 2014, the group helped defeat several pro-abortion rights senators from battleground states, and in 2016, got assurances from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : The Justices that I`m going to appoint will be pro-life. They will have a conservative bent.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: And that is why we are here today talking about the overturn of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Activists on the left joined in the fight, arguing abortion bans put lives in danger and pouring millions of dollars into recruiting and supporting candidates.\par \par \uc2 But this court decision has some admitting they`ve fallen short.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): This is a concerted, coordinated effort, and yes, they are winning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A conservative legal movement spent the decades since Roe promoting politicians and legal scholars who believed it was not the role of Judges to create new rights in the Constitution, including abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 President Trump succeeded in nominating three to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: I`m very proud that Roe versus Wade is now gone.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI: I didn`t think it would happen in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You didn`t think it would?\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI: It`s a total historic moment in our country`s history. And yet, it`s not as I said, it`s not the end.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, as for that next step, there is a plan to take the fight to the State Legislatures, to continue to push for more restrictions, even at the Federal level of prohibition on those mail order abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you know, Jan, we all had a heads up with that leaked draft, but I thought it was interesting that you said last half hour, that even though we had the heads up, people on both sides were surprised.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No matter where you stood, you were -- it was sort of jaw dropping, no matter what you felt.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think we were so surprised?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I just think that Roe has been with us for a generation or more of women and the Court came so close to overturning it in 1992, it fell one vote short. Many people thought the issue was over.\par \par \uc2 And so I think there was some complacency, and the question now is, because people were surprised, what does that mean for these women? You know, the states that are most likely to ban abortion or restrict abortion are states where women are in most need.\par \par \uc2 So what are the programs there for these women? Obviously, the anti- abortion groups say they are working, their programs are already in place in a number of states. Governors, we saw a Governor Noem on "Face The Nation yesterday saying she has a website, trying to get resources for pregnant women who no longer can access abortion.\par \par \uc2 Are those programs going to be enough? Are they going to work? Are we, the media and others going to continue to focus on these women who now right now don`t have the healthcare they need before the decision on Friday?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Jan, picking up on the subject of surprise. You`ve covered personally, the Supreme Court for almost 30 years. You`ve got a best-selling book on the topic.\par \par \uc2 There are a few people more authoritative. You`ve interviewed four or five Justices sitting on the Court now. Did anything in this process surprise you?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: No. No. I mean, as we saw in the piece, there has been an orchestrated campaign to try to get more conservative Judges on the Courts that have conservative legal philosophies and that`s what they`ve done.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these Judges believe that you read the Constitution very narrowly. Liberal Judges believe you should read it more expansively. And so, I think the writing was on the wall and that`s why Republicans have spent so much effort trying to get the Senate and here is what the result was today or Friday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jan, thank you very much. More to come from her.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the Mayor of Chicago, that would be \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , where the city just had another weekend of violence there. Will a newly signed gun control bill makes Chicago safer? That`s a question we`re going to ask her.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:09, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, teenagers everywhere pick up her books and they don`t put them down. Best-selling author, Jenny Han will be here to talk about her popular trilogy now a TV series, "The Summer I Turned Pretty."\par \par \uc2 Plus, the verdict on some of the most trivial, but mind-boggling issues out there, the host of "The Judge John Hodgman Podcast" sets the record straight.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6r0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 States That Allow Abortion Could See A Flood Of Patients From States That Don`t\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Adriana Diaz, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,503 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Supreme Court`s decision striking down Roe v. Wade could create a new wave of pregnant people traveling to states where abortion is more widely available. Take a look at this map for instance, Illinois, which has strong abortion protections in place is surrounded by states that have or could have restrictions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 So here`s a question, will Illinois be ready to handle thousands of new abortion patients?\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz is at a Planned Parenthood clinic in Waukegan, Illinois, just eight miles from the Wisconsin border. She is looking at grassroots efforts to help women travel to get abortions.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Opponents to abortion tell us they are very concerned that tens of thousands of women will be traveling here to Illinois to get the procedure, but Planned Parenthood actually opened this facility so close to the Wisconsin border to provide that access and other groups have volunteers transporting women both within Illinois and from out of state and they`re about to get busier.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Lori (sp?) who lives north of Chicago says getting behind the wheel is an act of activism.\par \par \uc2 LORI (Midwest Access Coalition volunteer): I`ve had people sleep the entire way home.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So people falling asleep in this backseat right here?\par \par \uc2 LORI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With this blanket?\par \par \uc2 LORI: With that very same blanket.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She drives women who need a ride to get an abortion both locally and from out of state. Lori is a volunteer with the Midwest Access Coalition known as MAC. The nonprofit helps fund everything from lodging and transportation to childcare for women who need those resources to get the procedure.\par \par \uc2 She says she`s driven about 20 women in the last three years.\par \par \uc2 What do you remember about the last woman you drove for?\par \par \uc2 LORI: Yes. She`s a single mom. She wasn`t getting a lot of support from her former husband. And she said, "I just can`t manage it as much as I would like to." And it was just -- it was heartbreaking and it was encouraging at the same time that she had an avenue to terminate her pregnancy because she wanted to and felt she needed to.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With Roe overturned, Planned Parenthood estimates that up to 30,000 additional patients could travel to Illinois for abortions over the next year.\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA (Executive Director of MAC): People in Illinois are going to be feeling a ripple effect.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the Supreme Court`s decision. We spoke to Diana Parker Kafka, Executive Director of MAC.\par \par \uc2 She said demand for the organization`s services had already tripled this year.\par \par \uc2 Your phones are ringing off the hook.\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: Yes, like my phone is vibrating every five seconds now.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Really?\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She worries without Roe, Illinois healthcare facilities will be stretched to capacity.\par \par \uc2 You expect people will have to be turned away?\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: Oh, yes. Yes. There will be people that we won`t be able to see through their abortion care that need it and we`re also thinking about plans on how to support those people.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There are anti-abortion rights activists who believe that it`s wrong to help women cross state lines to get an abortion if she is coming from a state where it`s banned. What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: I don`t think bodily autonomy should be limited by borders. These are people making a decision that is really essential for their lives for their families.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Is your organization helping people, women skirt state laws?\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: I wouldn`t say they`re skirting state laws. If someone has an appointment at a clinic in Illinois and that care is legal, then we absolutely will help them get there.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And for some volunteers like Lori, their passion is personal. She had an abortion in her 30s before she was ready for a family.\par \par \uc2 LORI: I knew immediately, I thought about this, "No, I`m going to have a child right now." I was lucky it was after Roe. I`m old enough to clearly remember before Roe was passed, and I know what those experiences were like.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She says ensuring access to safe legal abortions is what drives her.\par \par \uc2 Is it hard dropping folks off?\par \par \uc2 LORI: Yes. Yes, it is. It is.\par \par \uc2 I think about them walking through the doors. They stay with me. And it reinforces my commitment to keep on doing this as long as necessary, which is looking like it`s going to be a while.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It is not a crime for women to travel to another state for an abortion. In fact, Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  noted in his concurring opinion that interstate travel is protected under the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Also some local prosecutors in places like St. Louis, Missouri and Dallas, Texas have said they will not prosecute anyone who helps a woman get an abortion despite the bans in those states -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Adriana, some powerful information there. Thirty thousand new patients potentially.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 It is now 7:48. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, Jan Crawford shows how conservatives got to this point in the abortion battle.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: How do we get from Roe v. Wade in 1973 to here in 2022? We`ll look at the history to help us understand what lies ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Chicago is at the center of many of America`s most contentious issues right now, including access to abortion, also a surge in gun violence. The city`s Mayor, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  will join us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Author, Jenny Han`s stories about women coming of age are powerful and they are hilarious. She`ll tell us about the new streaming series, "The Summer I Turned Pretty."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The summer I turned pretty.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that title. But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hell no, we don`t need Roe.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m 21 and I`m terrified.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "My body, my choice.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Millions of Americans are waking up this morning without the constitutional right to abortion after the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: A victory for the rule of law and above all, a victory for life.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): The Court literally taking America back 150 years.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: We now have a patchwork of laws. So, the right to an abortion depends on the state that you live in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The effects of the Supreme Court`s decision ripple across the country.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We want to respect the rights of the baby.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This has been the law of the country since my grandmother. My daughter is sitting here with less rights than I have.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the largest gun control bills in decades becomes Federal law.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We can reach compromise on guns.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shocking video shows of stands collapsing at a bullfight in Colombia, at least four people have been killed.\par \par \uc2 MAN: To McKinnon bouncing, but -- and a goal. The auditorium looking in, makes it two to one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the \uc2 Colorado Avalanche\uc2  spoiled Tampa`s quest for a third straight Stanley Cup with a Game Six victory.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Five seconds to go. Up it comes to the line. Colorado has won the Stanley Cup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, Brian Mullen, our star hockey player here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Where is Brian? There he is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He predicted this from the beginning --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did he really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. All right. You go, Brian.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know nothing about hockey, but I picked the other team and Brian said I was wrong. He was right, again.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations to Colorado.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know less than nothing. Hockey, we were watching.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that was hockey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nice one.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6r0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trigger Laws Are Banning Abortions And In States Welcoming A Wave Of Patients\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 1,260 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, now to a closer look at the growing divide over abortion rights where states have become the new front lines. The last abortion center in Mississippi has 10 days before it has to close its doors, but in states like Illinois, clinics are expecting an influx of patients from neighboring states banning the procedure.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adriana Diaz will report from one of those surge states in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, CBS Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Jackson, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Caitlin.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 This clinic behind me is going to shut down in just 10 days. And volunteers over the weekend saw an influx of patients, more than double the amount they usually do. Once it closes, the next closest clinic will be over 400 miles away.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): The countdown son at Mississippi`s only abortion provider. The clinic, known as the Pink House, is adding more patients before shuttering next week.\par \par \uc2 Derenda Hancock has volunteered for over a decade.\par \par \uc2 DERENDA HANCOCK (Patient Escort): We`re never going to hide in the shadows again.\par \par \uc2 Sorry.\par \par \uc2 And it`s there to show they`re going to fight until the last day.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The clinic is at the center of the Supreme Court case that overturned Roe v. Wade, a decision championed by Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TATE REEVES (R-Mississippi): And we fight this battle, a battle whose -- we have been fighting for almost 50 years.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But some health care professionals across the Magnolia State are concerned the already strained health care system isn`t prepared for what`s next.\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL (CEO, Sisters in Birth): The governor claims that we`re moving towards pro-life phase two. What does pro-life phase two look like?\par \par \uc2 How long was she is labor?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Getty Israel is CEO of Sisters in Birth. The nonprofit community health organization serves pregnant women in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL: Mississippi is one of the top four states with the highest rate of young women who are uninsured, the same young women who don`t have access to reproductive care, namely, contraception.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In the Mississippi Delta, one of the poorest regions in the nation, entire counties are without an obstetrician.\par \par \uc2 Audreanna Lewis-Sholes, a certified nurse midwife, travels two hours to see patients. She says expectant mothers often rely on hospitals over an hour away.\par \par \uc2 AUDREANNA LEWIS-SHOLES (Certified Nurse-Midwife): Sometimes, you have patients that deliver on the side of the road. And that`s a thing also, because they couldn`t make it to the hospital in time.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And, soon, more women will be bringing pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 Are you prepared for an influx of women coming to you now that...\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL: No. No. I`m understaffed. I`m under-resourced. I`m not prepared. But they`re already coming. They are already coming.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voiceover): I`m Adriana Diaz in Illinois, which is preparing for an influx of out-of-state patients seeking abortions here.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER WELCH: Illinois is an oasis in a vast abortion desert.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Planned Parenthood of Illinois CEO Jennifer Welch says they`re expecting as many as 30,000 additional patients a year.\par \par \uc2 Since Friday`s decision, demand for abortion services has increased in Illinois, which is surrounded by states that restrict abortion access. That`s why Planned Parenthood built new health centers near Wisconsin and Indiana and expanded telehealth visits for medical abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER WELCH: Planned Parenthood of Illinois is here to serve patients from any state, because abortion is legal here in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 AMY GEHRKE (Executive Director, Illinois Right to Life): I would say that Illinois is a haven of death surrounded by states that recognize that preborn babies are fully human from the moment of conception.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Amy Gehrke, executive director of Illinois Right to Life, says she`s fighting against Illinois becoming a so-called abortion destination.\par \par \uc2 AMY GEHRKE: Governor Pritzker has basically rolled out the welcome mat for women from other states to come here for abortion.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But volunteers like Laurie with the Midwest Access Coalition are working to ensure the road is clear for women seeking abortions here.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE (Midwest Access Coalition): And this is different than anything I have done before because it is so personal. It`s one-on-one. It`s taking my time to actually be with one person and help them get where they need to go and back home again.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They give rides to women to abortion clinics, at times from out of state, and they`re preparing to get busier.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE: People that I drive have the need to have someone help them. They are -- they`re alone.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The Midwest Access Coalition says their daily call volume has more than doubled since Friday`s decision.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s legal to cross state lines for health services. But, in Missouri, which borders Illinois, a lawmaker there has introduced a proposal allowing private citizens to sue those who help women get abortions out of state -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Adriana Diaz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There was another major decision from the Supreme Court involving prayer in schools. In a 6-3 vote, the court ruled in favor of a former high school football coach who lost his job after praying on the field with students after games. The coach sued his Washington state school district, claiming it violated his constitutional rights. The latest ruling from the conservative majority court further narrows the separation between church and state.\par \par \uc2 Mystery surrounds a surprise January 6 hearing announced just today. The committee says the hearing, scheduled for Tuesday afternoon, will present recently obtained evidence, raising the expectations of new bombshells.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa is here with the latest.\par \par \uc2 All right, Robert, everybody wants to know, who`s this surprise witness?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Norah, this was a surprise announcement.\par \par \uc2 The committee has been looking for more information, gathering new evidence, pushing witnesses to share more. And they have something so urgent, they believe they need to have a hearing tomorrow. We know they have been engaged in discussions with Ginni Thomas, the spouse of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , about her efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 We also know they have been poring over previously unseen footage from documentary filmmaker Alexander Holder, who was here just at this table last week. But we don`t know who the star witness will be. We do know, according to a source close to the committee, that the person who testifies tomorrow will shine a very bright light on what happened in and around the Capitol attack.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And Robert Costa will be with us for our special coverage of the hearing. That`s tomorrow at 1:00 p.m. Eastern. We will see you then.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220630ei6r0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Multiple Fatalities After A Train Going From Los Angeles To Chicago Derails With 243 Passengers On Board\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Errol Barnett \par \uc2 834 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we have several big headlines as we come on the air.\par \par \uc2 America`s battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 And the breaking news: multiple fatalities after a train going from Los Angeles to Chicago derails with 243 passengers on board. Train cars topple over in Missouri after colliding with a truck. We`re tracking the fast- moving developments about those who are hurt and trapped inside.\par \par \uc2 The abortion rights fight in America, a country divided. We`re in states where trigger laws are banning abortions and in states welcoming a wave of patients.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER WELCH (President and CEO, Planned Parenthood of Illinois): Illinois is an oasis in a vast abortion desert.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news: New evidence leads to a surprise January 6 hearing tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The war in Ukraine. Russian missiles hit a crowded shopping mall, possibly over 1,000 people inside, the fear tonight of a rising civilian death toll.\par \par \uc2 The hunger crisis in South Sudan. We met this mother who walked for more than a month to get food for her 8-month-old, so weak he can barely eat. Debora Patta reports tonight in depth on the risk of starvation for millions.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s other top headlines: new video tonight of WNBA Brittney Griner after 130 days in Russian detention.\par \par \uc2 And a terrifying moment in Colombia, the deadly collapse killing at least four, including a 1-year old.\par \par \uc2 And we will end tonight with the heartwarming story of a 7-year-old cancer patient`s dream come true.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS With Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this busy Monday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with breaking news and horrific scenes from America`s heartland, an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train knocked on its side in a Missouri cornfield. At least three people were killed, and there are reports of numerous injuries. Officials say the train, traveling from Los Angeles to Chicago with more than 250 passengers and crew on board, partially derailed after hitting a truck at a crossing.\par \par \uc2 There are reports two Boy Scout troops were among the passengers. It happened near Mendon, Missouri. That`s a rural town of less than 200 people. Weather at the time of the crash does not appear to have been a factor.\par \par \uc2 The wreck comes weeks after the Biden administration announced more than $350 million in grants to improve the infrastructure of the world`s largest and most extensive rail system.\par \par \uc2 CBS News transportation correspondent Errol Barnett has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: There are several injuries. There is a male subject trapped in the bathroom. We know of one female under the train.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT (voiceover): Urgent calls of the derailment near Mendon, Missouri, indicated several passengers were hurt.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: Multiple injuries, multiple fatalities. Requesting all available units.\par \par \uc2 ROB NIGHTINGALE (Passenger): Damn it.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Rob Nightingale was inside one of the turned-over train cars recording the disorienting aftermath. After being checked on by a crew member, he captured this view of the wreckage.\par \par \uc2 ROB NIGHTINGALE: We hit a truck. Someone was crossing tracks.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  says approximately 243 passengers and 12 crew were on board when the train barreled into the truck.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR MICHAEL PARSON (R-Missouri): It is very unfortunate. Any time you have a derailment of a train and multiple cars, it`s not a good day.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN DUNN (Missouri State Highway Patrol): The train had approximately eight cars, including a baggage car. Seven cars have derailed. There are multiple injuries and we can confirm there are three fatalities, two on the train and one in the dump truck.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: This comes after an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  passenger train struck a car on a rail crossing in Northern California Sunday. And, in September, three passengers were killed when eight cars went off the tracks in Montana.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Now, late tonight, the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  says it is sending a 14-member go team to Missouri to determine what went wrong.\par \par \uc2 Investigators will look into the train`s braking system, its forward-facing cameras, and its event data recorder. It picks up all sorts of components from the moving train, Norah, like how quickly the emergency horn was activated. That, plus interviews with passengers like Rob, will eventually help them determine just what led to this.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A horrific scene.\par \par \uc2 Errol Barnett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220630ei6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Abortion Rights Fight In America, A Country Divided\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 565 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, tonight, the fallout from the Supreme Court ruling about abortion rights is growing, this after three straight days of protests across America. The ruling has left some legal confusion. There is also a surge in demand for over-the-counter emergency contraceptive pills, forcing retailers like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and \uc2 CBS\uc2  now to limit purchases.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): From New York to Los Angeles, protesters took to the streets.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Claim our rights!\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: With angry crowds squaring off with jubilant supporters over the historic ruling that ends a federal constitutional right to abortion and allows states to decide whether or not to ban it.\par \par \uc2 Largely peaceful, there were scattered episodes of vandalism and another attack on an anti-abortion pregnancy center like this one in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 With the ruling, at least eight states have made abortion illegal and more than a dozen more expected to ban or severely restrict it. But abortion rights supporters in conservative states are not giving up the legal fight.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, abortions resumed after a state court judge temporarily blocked Louisiana`s trigger law. In others, abortion rights groups are arguing their state`s constitutions give more protection than the federal constitution and contain a right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Karrie Galloway away of Planned Parenthood Utah.\par \par \uc2 KARRIE GALLOWAY (Planned Parenthood Utah): A woman no longer has bodily autonomy to make decisions about her reproductive health care. The state politicians have that authority over her body. And that doesn`t JIBE with the Utah Constitution.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Abortion rights opponents, having triumphed in a 49-year bat toll overturn Roe, also are looking to what is next.\par \par \uc2 Republican governors like South Dakota`s \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  say they are committed to help women with more support and new programs, while also working to implement other restrictions, like banning women from getting abortion pills through the mail.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (R-South Dakota): In South Dakota, we`ve already had a bill passed that said on telemedicine abortions that we don`t believe it should be available, because it is a dangerous situation for those individuals without being medically supervised by a physician.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Vice President Harris said the White House will fight those efforts.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): We will do everything within our power as an administration through the executive branch to ensure that women have access to the medication they need.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And, tonight, a state court judge has temporarily blocked Utah`s trigger ban under its state Constitution. And we can expect to see more of these battles, as state courts and legislatures take up this issue and decide what the right to abortion is going to look like in their states -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220630ei6r0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Abortion Ruling Aftermath; Mississippi`s New Abortion Reality; Biden Responds to SCOTUS Ruling; Future of Abortion Right; New Missile Systems to Ukraine; New Missile Strikes on Kyiv; Landmark Gun Legislation; Reacting to the Roe Decision; On the Frontlines; The History Behind the Abortion Battle; Challenges in Chicago; 2022 BET Awards; The Summer I Turned Pretty; Settling Life`s Biggest Disputes; A Landmark Decision, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Jan Crawford,  Caitlin Huey-Burns, Ed O`Keefe, Ramy Inocencio, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 13,349 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 There is a new reality in America following the Supreme Court`s decision on Friday to overturn Roe v. Wade. That ruling has sent shockwaves through all aspects of American life, as all of us come to terms with exactly what this means for women, for medical care, and for families.\par \par \uc2 Here`s just some of the reaction in today`s "Eye Opener."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Supreme Court has ruled on the future of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering.)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD singing "Jesus Loves the Little Children.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are here to bless you and your baby.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What about after you had a baby, are you going to help me take care of the baby? Are you going to get me Social Services?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This ruling was illegitimate. It must not be accepted.\par \par \uc2 MAN: For people in the states, the fight is now in your door.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My body.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: My choice.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They are chanting, "My body, my choice." It`s not. The science says that`s not -- that`s a baby.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We won`t give up this fight.\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN chanting.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Of course, women should have rights, obviously. But also the unborn.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whose choice?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Our choice.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Leaving it in the states is better all the way around for everyone.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Most of the rallies were peaceful though police Tased one protester and arrested a handful of others in Greenville, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And we saw demonstrators outside the Capitol, some of them even trying to get into the building.\par \par \uc2 Teargas was dispersed and police showed up at the Capitol in riot gear.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I have lived with the fundamental rights in abortion through my whole life and this is just wrong.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Worried about what could come next?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes. I`m kind of concerned about how like guns have more rights than we do.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: More Black women dying, more poverty for African-Americans. I`m fearful for what`s next.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "We will vote.")\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are out here, and we`re not going to stop and we`re not going away now and we`re not going away for as long as it takes to restore our freedoms and completely free --\par \par \uc2 MAN: There are people in our church who probably didn`t agree with the Court`s decision was, but you know, we`re going to love them and pray for them, too.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Whose streets?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Our streets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 You can see and hear the passion on both sides. For many people, it was a very rough weekend and for others. They were literally cheering in the streets.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Shock. Celebration.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody feels very strongly about this issue.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Something fundamental has shifted in this country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re absolutely right, and here is where America stands this morning on abortion rights following that monumental Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 Women in at least eight states can no longer get a legal abortion. Many more states are expected to outlaw or severely restrict the procedure in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 Now, that would establish an abortion free zone stretching for thousands of miles in the middle of the country.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Meanwhile, the latest CBS News poll shows that a solid majority of Americans, 59 percent do not like this High Court`s decision. They disapprove of it. That includes nearly seven in 10 women.\par \par \uc2 Also a majority, 52 percent in our poll say that reversing Roe v. Wade is a step back for America at large.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is outside the Supreme Court for us.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning to you. We mentioned the shock, the outrage, confusion. What happens now?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning, Tony. You know in overturning Roe versus Wade and letting states decide how they`re going to handle it. We now have a patchwork of laws. So the right to an abortion depends on the state that you live in.\par \par \uc2 Abortion has been one of the most divisive issues of our time, and we saw those divisions on full display across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "My body, my choice.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Protests erupted across the country after the Supreme Court`s historic ruling to end the constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Hell no, we don`t need Roe.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: With the ruling, at least eight states have placed near total bans on abortion and within months, it could be illegal or severely restricted in almost half the states in the country.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): A victory for the rule of law and above all, a victory for life.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In Illinois Saturday, former President Trump celebrated the news and the three conservative Justices he nominated to the court who voted to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Those prayers have been answered to the generations of Americans in the pro-life movement, as well as countless constitutional conservative.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Democrats warned of the impact in states with already high maternal mortality rates and children in poverty.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): This is not just a crisis of Roe, this is a crisis of our democracy. Forcing women to carry pregnancies against their will, will kill them.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Republican governors like South Dakota`s \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  say they are prepared to help women with more support and new programs, while also working to stop women from getting abortion pills through the mail.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R-SD): In South Dakota, we`ve already had a bill passed that`s set on telemedicine abortions, that we don`t believe it should be available because it is a dangerous situation for those individuals without being medically supervised by a physician.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Democratic Governors where abortion will remain legal said their states will welcome all women for abortions, while others said they would work to make sure it is legal.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): So this is a fight like hell moment. We are taking -- using every tool we have to fight for reproductive rights for Michigan women.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Meanwhile, confusion and concern about how the ruling might impact other rights in light of Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` separate opinion, which no other Justice joined, calling on the Court to reconsider cases on contraception and gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Americans say they think it`s likely that the Supreme Court will limit same sex marriage and access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Gay marriage, contraceptives -- all these things until they strip us of all our rights.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Even though the Court`s majority said it is hard to see how we could be clearer that those cases are not threatened because abortion is different, because they wrote, it involves another life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now over the weekend, the protests remained largely peaceful and this isn`t the end. The Court in coming days is going to rule on other divisive issues, including religion in schools and climate change -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is not over yet.\par \par \uc2 Jan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi led the challenge that finally overturn Roe v. Wade. That state`s law limiting abortion was declared unconstitutional by Lower Courts until the Supreme Court ruled on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Jackson, Mississippi outside the state`s only abortion clinic where there is concern that women will lose access to a wide range of essential services.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Well, this clinic behind me is known here as the Pink House, but it is also known as being the center of that landmark ruling, and over the weekend, they actually expanded hours to try and see as many patients as they could before the Attorney General here makes them close their doors.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Dorinda Hancock and her volunteer group, the Pink Defenders spent the weekend as they have for years, escorting women past protesters and into Mississippi`s only abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 DORINDA HANCOCK (The Pink Defenders volunteer): They can block this road, because they know those are patients who need access to get right in that driveway.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: She says, the defenders will be there until the clinic closes its doors for good.\par \par \uc2 DORINDA HANCOCK: Always been through here. It will always be a part of our lives. The patients that we`ve seen, and that we`ve been able to give a little comfort to --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But elsewhere, the decision is seen as an answered prayer. It was at the center of Sunday service at Broadmoor Baptist Church where the Pastor encouraged adoption.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Roe versus Wade was overturned. And I pray, yes, we thank God for that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Is the state prepared for the consequence?\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL (Sisters in Birth founder): No, no. It`s not prepared.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Getty Israel founded Sisters in Birth, a nonprofit community health organization serving pregnant women in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL: Mississippi is one of the top four states with the highest - - highest rate of young women who are uninsured, the same young woman who don`t have access to reproductive care, namely contraception, and now she is pregnant and she`s going to present with risk factors like obesity, pre- hypertension, pre-diabetes. She is more likely to raise that baby in poverty.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In the Mississippi Delta, one of the poorest places in the nation, some counties are without a single obstetrician or pediatrician. Already, expectant mothers either don`t have access to medical professionals or must drive hours.\par \par \uc2 WAIKINYA CLANTON (Jackson, Mississippi Southern Poverty Law Center Director): The thing that`s going to keep me up at night is the women who will lose their lives because they don`t have access to care.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Waikinya Clanton, the Director of the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  in Jackson sees this as a call to action.\par \par \uc2 WAIKINYA CLANTON: We will not back down. The people here, particularly the women here in Mississippi are primed for this moment.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But in a state with the highest rate of infant mortality, of low birth weight, and of young child poverty, Israel says the Federal and local governments need to step up.\par \par \uc2 And what does it mean for these children?\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL: Well, I see it as a ditch. Put the mother in the ditch. Put the child in the ditch with her. Now, add another one and cover it with dirt. That`s what we`re dealing with.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, Tony, most of the states around Mississippi are actually also banning abortions, so women seeking the procedure will have to either fly long distances or drive long distances. And also at question here is whether there will be additional restrictions on ordering abortion pills by mail -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Caitlin Huey-Burns outside the clinic where it all started.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is under pressure from his own party this morning to respond and respond strongly to the Supreme Court`s action, but he is overseas at the moment for the latest G7 Summit in Germany and that is where we find Ed O`Keefe traveling with the President. He joins us now from Austria.\par \par \uc2 Ed, it is early, but everyone is wondering what does this mean politically?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, Tony, good morning from the G7 Summit where the President is focused on his international agenda even though he and his party are facing fallout from the Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 There is at least some encouraging short term political gain potentially for Democrats in all of this. Look at our new CBS News poll that finds 50 percent of registered Democrats say the Court`s decision makes them more likely to vote this fall, the kind of boost of enthusiasm the party is going to need up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 The poll also finds majority support for Congress passing a Federal law to legalize abortion rights nationwide. That`s now quickly become a key Democratic Party campaign pledge.\par \par \uc2 The President is facing pressure from fellow Democrats to more forcefully respond to the ruling. Progressives like Senator Elizabeth Warren once again over the weekend calling to add more Justices to the Supreme Court and to get rid of the Senate filibuster to make it easier to pass Democratic priorities.\par \par \uc2 But the White House says the President doesn`t support expanding the size of the High Court.\par \par \uc2 Here in Europe. President Biden says none of the world leaders he has been meeting with have mentioned the abortion ruling to him, even though many of them criticized it in the hours after the Court weighed in last week.\par \par \uc2 Here at the Summit today, the focus is on unity around Ukraine. The leaders heard virtually earlier today from Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much, Ed O`Keefe, reporting from Austria.\par \par \uc2 The High Court`s ruling on Roe upends nearly 50 years of legal precedent. Now, that raises a lot of legal questions. Constitutional scholar, Jeffrey Rosen joins us now to help us sort this out. He is President and CEO of the nonpartisan, \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Jeffrey. It`s really good to have you here on this morning in particular.\par \par \uc2 You know, the polling shows that the majority of Americans did not want Roe v. Wade overturned. So, why does it -- why is the Supreme Court seemingly so out of step with the American public?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN (\uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2  President and CEO): Well, the majority opinion said that it`s their job to ignore public opinion and to follow the Constitution wherever it leads. The fact that they didn`t change their opinion after it leaked, should suggest that they`re determined to hold the line there, and they may be out of step with America in a way that would be historically unusual in American politics.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How far now can states go to enforce these abortion bans?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN: Well, this is the central question and the legal issues are going to be so complicated. First, can a woman travel out of state to get an abortion? Justice Kavanaugh said "yes." He said that the right to travel protects your right to go to a state that is pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 But a bunch of the anti-choice states are going to be trying to prosecute doctors out of state who offer abortions or those who aid and abet them. There are only two precedents on those. Again, they`ll be tested in Court.\par \par \uc2 The pro-choice states will refuse to extradite women who cross state lines. So the bottom line is, it is going to be tied up in the Courts for a very long time and very, very complicated state by state.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but you see, a lot of companies are already stepping up saying we will reimburse women who have to travel out of state. What impact do you think this will have?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN: Well, it`ll have huge effects on where people decide to work and a lot is going to turn on the availability of the abortion pill, which you were talking about earlier.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department last week said that states may not ban the abortion pill based on disagreement with its health or safety and generally, Federal regulations preempt state regulations, but a bunch of states are already moving to ban the abortion pill. Those will be tested in Court. And ultimately, it`s not clear what the Supreme Court will say about this preemption issue, so there once again, we`re not going to know the final answer for a long time to come.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jeffrey, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote in his opinion that the Court should reconsider same sex marriage and access to birth control. Now, the majority opinion also took pains to say we`re not going to do that, this is different, but give us a reality check here.\par \par \uc2 Does what \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote portend other rights being threatened in this ruling?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN: Well, as Jan Crawford said earlier, the majority said abortion is different than gay marriage and contraception because it involves a fetus, and there is another difference between contraception and abortion, namely that there was a tradition of protecting contraception in the 60s and the Courts said there is no tradition of protecting abortion.\par \par \uc2 But the liberal Justices were not convinced. There is an amazing line in the dissent where the liberals say the majority says it`s not going to question same sex marriage and contraception, scouts honor. They basically said, you can`t bank on that promise.\par \par \uc2 So the liberal Justices aren`t convinced. Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  obviously said that he is willing to reconsider those laws. So once again, tremendous legal uncertainty on the central issues of our time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It affects so many things politically, socially, medically. It`s still -- so much more to discuss here.\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Rosen, thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In other news this morning, the United States is ready to send more military aid to Ukraine. Senior administration officials tell CBS News the White House plans to announce this week it is providing an advanced surface-to-air missile defense system.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday for the first time in three weeks, Ukraine`s capital was struck by Russian missiles.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: A rare barrage of Russian missiles flew over Kyiv on Sunday, and upon impact, plumes of smoke rose from a kindergarten and residential building. Six people were injured including this seven-year-old girl and her Russian mother. Her father did not survive.\par \par \uc2 That missile hit the roof of their apartment complex, but it`s actually not the first time that it`s been hit. The last time was about two months ago.\par \par \uc2 Now one possibility is that Russian forces are targeting a Soviet era arms factory that is literally this one across the street.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  said some missiles had been shot down, but called for more modern defenses from the international community that has started to arrive. These destroyed military trucks are believed to be the first Russian targets hit by US supplied guided rocket systems known as HIMARs that arrived in Ukraine just days ago.\par \par \uc2 Too late to save the eastern city of Severodonetsk, that city fell on Saturday as Ukrainian troops were ordered to pull out.\par \par \uc2 Russia is cementing its control over the whole Donbas region, a territorial and morale hit to Ukraine in this war now starting its fifth month.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings, Ramy Inocencio, Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back here in the US in what might have been our top story on another morning, the most extensive gun control legislation in nearly three decades is now Federal law.\par \par \uc2 President Biden signed the bipartisan Safer Communities Act on Saturday calling it consequential -- a consequential bill that in his words would save lives.\par \par \uc2 The $13 billion legislation enhances background checks for gun buyers under 21, makes it harder for domestic abusers to purchase a firearm and provides more money for mental health services including $750 million for states to establish Crisis Intervention Programs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re so right, Tony, on another day, that would have been a top story because that`s a very big deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Biggest bill on guns in three decades.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, we`ve got reaction to the abortion ruling from women on both sides of this debate. Their responses are important for all of us to hear today. But first it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, Adriana Diaz rides along with volunteers in a state literally caught in the middle of the new reality after Roe -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 Oops, I think we may have a frozen shot there.\par \par \uc2 She is at Planned Parenthood in Illinois where abortion providers are getting ready for an influx of patients. Tens of thousands of women will likely travel to Illinois for services they cannot get in their home states. Ahead, she will ride along with volunteers who drive women who cannot afford transportation to abortion clinics.\par \par \uc2 Her story coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, some amazing guests you do not want to miss, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " number one bestselling author Jenny Han talks with us about a new Prime video series. It`s called "The Summer I Turned Pretty" and it`s based on her wildly popular books.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the esteemed and Honorable Judge John Hodgman, not really a Judge, will be here to weigh in on some of the least important, but most debatable hot topics on our minds. For example, is a hot dog a sandwich?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is chili a soup or a stew?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Stew.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stew.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will find out. And the debate begins.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The gallery has spoken.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, here is a reminder, you can get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on the go podcast, hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS at 7:30. Pretty picture there of the Supreme Court because we`re focusing on the fallout from the Court`s decision ruling that abortion is no longer a constitutional right in this country.\par \par \uc2 More than half of women questioned in the latest CBS News poll said that overturning Roe v. Wade, this decision we`re talking about will make women`s lives worse.\par \par \uc2 But not all women feel that way. This morning, we`re hearing from a diverse group of five: Kate Finberg, Savannah Ackerman, K. Bryant, Abigail Galvan, and Amanda Holliday-Bembridge. They all have deep feelings on abortion and different feelings, some support abortion rights, others oppose them and the reactions are very fresh.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford interviewed them shortly after the Supreme Court announced its decision.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT (Pro-choice): I`m pro-choice,\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN (Pro-life): I am pro-life.\par \par \uc2 KATE FINBERG (Pro-choice): Pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE (Pro-choice). I`m pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN (Pro-life): I am against all forms of violence and I consider abortion a violence.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Court just overturn Roe versus Wade a couple of hours ago. How are you feeling right now in one word?\par \par \uc2 KATE FINBERG: Concerned.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Concerned.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN: Just surreal.\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: For me, it is just more of the same.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I guess you would say, not surprised.\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: Yes. Not surprised.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: I`m feeling hopeful.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: I would say frustrated, empathetic, saddened. Any of those.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What does that mean personally? I mean, the fact that the Supreme Court has overturned Roe versus Wade?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: I`m frustrated for the women that won`t be allowed to have the choice that they are being told what to do by someone that doesn`t know them or their circumstances.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: You can go to different parts of the country and different people want different things. So, I actually think that the best way to be able to minister to women and give them what they need is by having it be at the state level. They have more control and are able to exercise more of their choice.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What about you?\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: I don`t agree that the states are going to do a better job because obviously, many of the states have not. They are completely and outright banning abortion.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN: For me personally, there has been this huge focus on legality, right? It`s never how are we helping the women on the ground. What is the reason that people are actually getting abortions?\par \par \uc2 KATE FINBERG: I`m more upset with the Supreme Court because I feel like their decision that they`ve made is actually not reflective of what Americans largely polled, have indicated that they believe.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Do any of you, you know, have girlfriends or friends or anyone that you said, like, here`s where we can find common ground, is it just either or?\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: It`s actually -- I changed my stance on the position. When I was growing up, so I came from a very low income background, I was only told this narrative that abortion is the way to go and anyone who is against abortion doesn`t like women, they don`t support women. And that, quite frankly, isn`t true.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You reject the pro-life label.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN: Because it has come to me in so many things. And I think, again, like the super mainstream movement is super hypocritical. I think there is no other way to say it.\par \par \uc2 My viewpoints don`t come from religion, don`t come from anything else, it comes from this fact that each human being is distinct and important and should be respected and valued.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m against abortion in the same way I`m against police brutality, and torturous acts within prisons and the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It seems like one thing you agree with, whether you`re on the left or the right, or somewhere in between, is that a lot more should be done for women and children? You`re saying that the anti-abortion movement could be doing more?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN: Yes, and they should be and they should have been the whole time and I am so annoyed every time I hear the "Now the real work begins" because it`s no, you should have been doing it.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What about the abortion rights movement?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: The bigger problem is that we can have all these conversations, but when you have White Christian male viewpoints, making the decisions at the end of the day are the ones that are the loudest on TV and social media and that is the rhetoric that gets passed and we miss the point of listening to all the other issues or figuring out all the things that surround this decision to support maternal health or prenatal/postnatal care.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You`re anti-abortion. Do you think that the movement should focus its efforts now on changing the laws in states like Maryland or the District of Columbia or California to make it more restrictive?\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: Let`s look at all these underlying issues that are the root causes of why people are having to make these decisions. The danger in pushing for abortion is it`s just like you, like you don`t think you have another option like, you`re just told this is what you do.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: Isn`t that the opposite then? Like if when you saying if you feel like the only option is abortion, but if you take that option away, the only option is life, you`re not giving a choice.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: So it will be decided on the state level in that sense, but I would say that --\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: That on an individual level.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: It takes that away from the mom, or the pregnant person?\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: Well, I would say actually that if we`re not focusing on the legal issue of abortion, then perhaps we would be focusing our attention on all of these other supports.\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: I don`t think any one of us in here is satisfied with how politicians don`t do what we ask them to do. They are not listening to the problems.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: So does the idea that it is now going to be politicians that get to decide this issue of abortion scare you?\par \par \uc2 K. BRYANT: Honestly, it doesn`t scare me. I try to get involved in things that reflect the way that I would like the world to be and I don`t think that we consider enough how what we do on our daily lives can affect even the five of us here talking about abortion today.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Is that a positive thing? It is a positive talking about this?\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: Because I was a little concerned about coming here and what I found is that there is a lot of commonality in what we have to say and I hope that the viewers actually listen to it and think about that and stop thinking about, "Okay, do you have access? Yes or no." But what about the other things? Those other things are so much more important.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: I`m very impressed by each of these women that I`m joined here today with. I don`t like that, like we`ve been pitted against each other when I feel like we would be more powerful working together.\par \par \uc2 KATE FINBERG: I understand that it`s nuanced. I understand it is complex. I feel like the pro-life movement is not doing enough and I feel like the pro-choice movement is not doing enough to support women and I feel like that`s what`s really important and that`s not getting heard.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think any of us think -- feel that abortions are everyone`s number one choice, right? I think we would like to create a world but that`s not what women want and women need.\par \par \uc2 And so looking at it that way, I think there`s common ground that we can come together on to try and create more of that kind of a world.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, the women that we talked to are from Maryland and the Washington, DC area, and we had this interview planned for Friday morning, before we knew that the Court was going to drop its decision overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 So when some of the women arrived at the hotel where we spoke with them, they had no idea that Roe was history.\par \par \uc2 Back to you guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a very intelligent and coherent conversation. So we do know it can be done. But, Jan, I like the point you raised, is it either/or. It`s so deeply political, so deeply polarizing. Where do we go from here?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Right, and is consensus even possible?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is the question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is the question.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is it possible?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what are your thoughts, Jan? You`ve been covering this from the very beginning and you`ve seen it up close and personally. You`ve talked to many people on both sides of the issue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Almost 30 years of work, Jan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it is an amazing body of work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we`ve come to this moment.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, I think that in many ways, we saw this on Friday. We had an idea that this was going to happen, right? And yet everyone was shocked on both sides like this really did happen.\par \par \uc2 But this is part of an orchestrated campaign that we`re going to be talking about in the eight o`clock hour to overturn Roe versus Wade, going back to 1974, the very next year when Roe was creating -- establishing a constitutional right to an abortion. So the people in the anti-abortion movement never gave up when they suffered losses, they regrouped and moved on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Jan Crawford, thank you very much and appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, states that allow abortion could see a flood of patients from states that don`t.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz rides with volunteers, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Supreme Court`s decision striking down Roe v. Wade could create a new wave of pregnant people traveling to states where abortion is more widely available. Take a look at this map for instance, Illinois, which has strong abortion protections in place is surrounded by states that have or could have restrictions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 So here`s a question, will Illinois be ready to handle thousands of new abortion patients?\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz is at a Planned Parenthood clinic in Waukegan, Illinois, just eight miles from the Wisconsin border. She is looking at grassroots efforts to help women travel to get abortions.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Opponents to abortion tell us they are very concerned that tens of thousands of women will be traveling here to Illinois to get the procedure, but Planned Parenthood actually opened this facility so close to the Wisconsin border to provide that access and other groups have volunteers transporting women both within Illinois and from out of state and they`re about to get busier.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Lori (sp?) who lives north of Chicago says getting behind the wheel is an act of activism.\par \par \uc2 LORI (Midwest Access Coalition volunteer): I`ve had people sleep the entire way home.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So people falling asleep in this backseat right here?\par \par \uc2 LORI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With this blanket?\par \par \uc2 LORI: With that very same blanket.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She drives women who need a ride to get an abortion both locally and from out of state. Lori is a volunteer with the Midwest Access Coalition known as MAC. The nonprofit helps fund everything from lodging and transportation to childcare for women who need those resources to get the procedure.\par \par \uc2 She says she`s driven about 20 women in the last three years.\par \par \uc2 What do you remember about the last woman you drove for?\par \par \uc2 LORI: Yes. She`s a single mom. She wasn`t getting a lot of support from her former husband. And she said, "I just can`t manage it as much as I would like to." And it was just -- it was heartbreaking and it was encouraging at the same time that she had an avenue to terminate her pregnancy because she wanted to and felt she needed to.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With Roe overturned, Planned Parenthood estimates that up to 30,000 additional patients could travel to Illinois for abortions over the next year.\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA (Executive Director of MAC): People in Illinois are going to be feeling a ripple effect.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the Supreme Court`s decision. We spoke to Diana Parker Kafka, Executive Director of MAC.\par \par \uc2 She said demand for the organization`s services had already tripled this year.\par \par \uc2 Your phones are ringing off the hook.\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: Yes, like my phone is vibrating every five seconds now.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Really?\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She worries without Roe, Illinois healthcare facilities will be stretched to capacity.\par \par \uc2 You expect people will have to be turned away?\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: Oh, yes. Yes. There will be people that we won`t be able to see through their abortion care that need it and we`re also thinking about plans on how to support those people.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There are anti-abortion rights activists who believe that it`s wrong to help women cross state lines to get an abortion if she is coming from a state where it`s banned. What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: I don`t think bodily autonomy should be limited by borders. These are people making a decision that is really essential for their lives for their families.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Is your organization helping people, women skirt state laws?\par \par \uc2 DIANA PARKER KAFKA: I wouldn`t say they`re skirting state laws. If someone has an appointment at a clinic in Illinois and that care is legal, then we absolutely will help them get there.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And for some volunteers like Lori, their passion is personal. She had an abortion in her 30s before she was ready for a family.\par \par \uc2 LORI: I knew immediately, I thought about this, "No, I`m going to have a child right now." I was lucky it was after Roe. I`m old enough to clearly remember before Roe was passed, and I know what those experiences were like.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She says ensuring access to safe legal abortions is what drives her.\par \par \uc2 Is it hard dropping folks off?\par \par \uc2 LORI: Yes. Yes, it is. It is.\par \par \uc2 I think about them walking through the doors. They stay with me. And it reinforces my commitment to keep on doing this as long as necessary, which is looking like it`s going to be a while.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It is not a crime for women to travel to another state for an abortion. In fact, Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  noted in his concurring opinion that interstate travel is protected under the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Also some local prosecutors in places like St. Louis, Missouri and Dallas, Texas have said they will not prosecute anyone who helps a woman get an abortion despite the bans in those states -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Adriana, some powerful information there. Thirty thousand new patients potentially.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 It is now 7:48. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, Jan Crawford shows how conservatives got to this point in the abortion battle.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: How do we get from Roe v. Wade in 1973 to here in 2022? We`ll look at the history to help us understand what lies ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Chicago is at the center of many of America`s most contentious issues right now, including access to abortion, also a surge in gun violence. The city`s Mayor, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  will join us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Author, Jenny Han`s stories about women coming of age are powerful and they are hilarious. She`ll tell us about the new streaming series, "The Summer I Turned Pretty."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The summer I turned pretty.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that title. But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hell no, we don`t need Roe.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m 21 and I`m terrified.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "My body, my choice.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Millions of Americans are waking up this morning without the constitutional right to abortion after the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : A victory for the rule of law and above all, a victory for life.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): The Court literally taking America back 150 years.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: We now have a patchwork of laws. So, the right to an abortion depends on the state that you live in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The effects of the Supreme Court`s decision ripple across the country.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We want to respect the rights of the baby.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This has been the law of the country since my grandmother. My daughter is sitting here with less rights than I have.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the largest gun control bills in decades becomes Federal law.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We can reach compromise on guns.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shocking video shows of stands collapsing at a bullfight in Colombia, at least four people have been killed.\par \par \uc2 MAN: To McKinnon bouncing, but -- and a goal. The auditorium looking in, makes it two to one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the \uc2 Colorado Avalanche\uc2  spoiled Tampa`s quest for a third straight Stanley Cup with a Game Six victory.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Five seconds to go. Up it comes to the line. Colorado has won the Stanley Cup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, Brian Mullen, our star hockey player here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Where is Brian? There he is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He predicted this from the beginning --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did he really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. All right. You go, Brian.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know nothing about hockey, but I picked the other team and Brian said I was wrong. He was right, again.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations to Colorado.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know less than nothing. Hockey, we were watching.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that was hockey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nice one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to continue our coverage of the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Now, this follows nearly half a century of conflict and a concerted campaign by anti-abortion groups.\par \par \uc2 Long before this weekend`s protests, we`ve seen violence against abortion clinics and the providers, too, and frequently vicious -- and vicious is the word -- harassment of women seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 There has also been a relentless effort to change the landscape politically in Congress, legislatures, and the Courts.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford takes a look at how we got here.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve got a landmark ruling, the Supreme Court today legalized abortions.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In 1973, Roe versus Wade was a bombshell.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The anti-abortion laws of 46 states were rendered unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Four states that only recently passed laws legalizing some form of abortion, while 13 others have begun allowing it with restrictions.\par \par \uc2 By stepping in to make abortion legal nationwide, some Justices at the time hope the decision would end the debate over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 But the anti-abortion rights movement mobilized almost immediately, beginning first at the grassroots level.\par \par \uc2 This is the first March for Life and that was a year exactly after Roe came down.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI (March for Life President): Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Jeanne Mancini is President of March for Life.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI: The March has grown younger and bigger almost every single year.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: How important has it been to have that yearly reminder that we`re still here?\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI: I can`t think of a more symbolic picture of Roe not being settled law than seeing the growing number of marchers.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: That social movement became more strategic in 1992.\par \par \uc2 RANDALL TERRY (American activist): Today, three Reagan-Bush appointees stabbed the pro-life movement in the back.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Supreme Court was poised to overturn Roe by a five-four vote until the last minute switch by Justice \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2 , who joined fellow Republican nominees, \uc2 Sandra Day O`Connor\uc2  and David Souter to save it.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER (Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America President): That moment and 1992 was deeply disappointing. It also helped me understand how much we could depend upon Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Which is how much?\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: Which is not much. That was the point where we realized, unless we strengthen this political arm at the center of the movement. We will continue to lose.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Within months, Marjorie Dannenfelser who has been called the woman who has been called "The woman who killed Roe" founded that political organization, \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: Every time there was a devastating blow, the pro- life movement grew. That is a mark of a successful movement.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The next devastating blow came in the 2000s.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY (R-UT): I would welcome a circumstance where there was such a consensus of this country that we said we don`t want to have abortion in this country at all, but that`s not where we are.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What Dannenfelser said Republicans paid lip service to the movement and lost the presidency twice.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: We said no way is this ever going to happen again. We`re going to reward our friends, punish our political enemies.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In 2014, the group helped defeat several pro-abortion rights senators from battleground states, and in 2016, got assurances from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : The Justices that I`m going to appoint will be pro-life. They will have a conservative bent.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: And that is why we are here today talking about the overturn of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Activists on the left joined in the fight, arguing abortion bans put lives in danger and pouring millions of dollars into recruiting and supporting candidates.\par \par \uc2 But this court decision has some admitting they`ve fallen short.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): This is a concerted, coordinated effort, and yes, they are winning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A conservative legal movement spent the decades since Roe promoting politicians and legal scholars who believed it was not the role of Judges to create new rights in the Constitution, including abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 President Trump succeeded in nominating three to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER: I`m very proud that Roe versus Wade is now gone.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI: I didn`t think it would happen in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You didn`t think it would?\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI: It`s a total historic moment in our country`s history. And yet, it`s not as I said, it`s not the end.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, as for that next step, there is a plan to take the fight to the State Legislatures, to continue to push for more restrictions, even at the Federal level of prohibition on those mail order abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you know, Jan, we all had a heads up with that leaked draft, but I thought it was interesting that you said last half hour, that even though we had the heads up, people on both sides were surprised.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No matter where you stood, you were -- it was sort of jaw dropping, no matter what you felt.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think we were so surprised?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I just think that Roe has been with us for a generation or more of women and the Court came so close to overturning it in 1992, it fell one vote short. Many people thought the issue was over.\par \par \uc2 And so I think there was some complacency, and the question now is, because people were surprised, what does that mean for these women? You know, the states that are most likely to ban abortion or restrict abortion are states where women are in most need.\par \par \uc2 So what are the programs there for these women? Obviously, the anti- abortion groups say they are working, their programs are already in place in a number of states. Governors, we saw a Governor Noem on "Face The Nation yesterday saying she has a website, trying to get resources for pregnant women who no longer can access abortion.\par \par \uc2 Are those programs going to be enough? Are they going to work? Are we, the media and others going to continue to focus on these women who now right now don`t have the healthcare they need before the decision on Friday?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Jan, picking up on the subject of surprise. You`ve covered personally, the Supreme Court for almost 30 years. You`ve got a best-selling book on the topic.\par \par \uc2 There are a few people more authoritative. You`ve interviewed four or five Justices sitting on the Court now. Did anything in this process surprise you?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: No. No. I mean, as we saw in the piece, there has been an orchestrated campaign to try to get more conservative Judges on the Courts that have conservative legal philosophies and that`s what they`ve done.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these Judges believe that you read the Constitution very narrowly. Liberal Judges believe you should read it more expansively. And so, I think the writing was on the wall and that`s why Republicans have spent so much effort trying to get the Senate and here is what the result was today or Friday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jan, thank you very much. More to come from her.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the Mayor of Chicago, that would be \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , where the city just had another weekend of violence there. Will a newly signed gun control bill makes Chicago safer? That`s a question we`re going to ask her.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:09, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, teenagers everywhere pick up her books and they don`t put them down. Best-selling author, Jenny Han will be here to talk about her popular trilogy now a TV series, "The Summer I Turned Pretty."\par \par \uc2 Plus, the verdict on some of the most trivial, but mind-boggling issues out there, the host of "The Judge John Hodgman Podcast" sets the record straight.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Violent weekend in Chicago, another one, five people killed, 20 others wounded, shootings all across the city and the youngest victim, a five-month-old girl, but help me be on the way.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, President Biden signed the most significant Federal gun control bill in nearly three decades. It`s a measure that will toughen background checks for gun buyers under 21 years old and provide billions of dollars for mental health services.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the Mayor of Chicago, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 . Mayor, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 We just mentioned this new piece of legislation. How do you think it might help your city?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT (D) (Chicago, Illinois): Well, first of all, thank you for having me this morning. Yes, another tough weekend, but what I don`t want to get lost in the shuffle is that we are down 15 percent in shootings and 10 percent in homicides. But I think what this weekend underscores is that we are wash in illegal guns.\par \par \uc2 And so what the legislation I think will help us is, there is a provision against trafficking and straw purchasing, and we need our Federal partners to be very vigorous in enforcing this new law, something we fought very hard for. And I`m glad finally, we`ve seen some positive steps in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 But we need more Federal assistance in enforcing this new law.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you just said you`re awash in illegal guns there in Chicago. When you look at crime in the city, is that the core issue? Is that what`s behind all these shootings? Is that the number one cause?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT: There is no question. Ninety-plus percent of our homicides are as a result of illegal gunfire, whether it`s gang related, but increasingly, what we`re seeing is petty disputes ending in gunfire.\par \par \uc2 So the fact of the matter that our police department last year took 12,000 illegal guns off the street, which is more than New York or LA combined tells you the challenge that we face.\par \par \uc2 Our officers are heroic, every single day, making the necessary stops, making the arrests, and finding these illegal firearms. But we are fighting against a sea of them coming over the border, from other states.\par \par \uc2 So gun trafficking is a significant problem for cities like Chicago, New York, LA. Every major city faces this challenge. So, I`m gratified that the Federal government finally has answered the pleading of victims and survivors, and mayors and other municipal officials that we need additional tools to be able to fight against this tide of illegal guns.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, Mayor Lightfoot, it`s good to see you. I was in Wilmington, North Carolina and I went to a place called True Colors Brewery where they are working with active gang members, rival gang members who are working together. I`ve never seen anything like it before.\par \par \uc2 And what I learned there over the weekend is that the majority of the people in the gangs, it`s a very small number of people who are actually violent, and maybe we should target that.\par \par \uc2 Is that the experience in your city, too? You know, you hear gangs and it is very scary. It`s very threatening and very, very sinister. But what I learned is that if you just target the very small number of gang members who are violent, that that could make the difference.\par \par \uc2 Is that the case in Chicago? We start every weekend with another bad weekend in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT: There is no question that we`ve got to be very specific with the people who are driving violence, and Gayle, it`s not like in the 80s and 90s in Chicago, where there was an established gang hierarchy.\par \par \uc2 What we see are much smaller groupings, we call them cliques, but a lot of this violence is also driven by petty disputes among people that aren`t necessarily affiliated with a gang.\par \par \uc2 But what we need to do is be very diligent in pushing our Courts to hold violent and dangerous people in our city accountable. They can`t keep getting released back into the streets.\par \par \uc2 And again, I`m mindful of the Constitution. Everybody`s presumption of innocence, but there are some people who are truly a demonstrated danger to our community. They need to be locked up pretrial and we would see a much more significant decrease in what we`re already seeing in our city.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, abortion is protected under Illinois State Law. We had a story earlier in this broadcast that chances are that your city or your state, in particular, your state, and your city, as well, will be overwhelmed with people coming in to get abortions in your city.\par \par \uc2 Are you prepared for that? Are you anticipating that? Are you worried about it?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT: Well, I`m certainly worried about it, and I`m worried about it for the women who are now going to be forced to travel from their home state to cities like Chicago, to get the kind of reproductive care that they should have, as a right in their home state.\par \par \uc2 We`ve already seen a surge in the number of people coming particularly after the Court upheld the terrible Texas law, and I think that that surge is only going to increase in the coming months and years.\par \par \uc2 So we are getting ourselves prepared. I authorized an additional $500,000.00 to support our reproductive healthcare providers, but there is more that needs to be done. We need to make sure that there are resources for travel, for lodging, and for aftercare.\par \par \uc2 We shouldn`t be in this circumstance, but this is a reality given what the Supreme Court did to American women on Friday, but we will be ready. We will remain a beacon of justice for all and we will make sure that women who need reproductive care are able to get it in the City of Chicago.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mayor Lightfoot, we thank you for your time. We know you have a very full plate in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Mayor.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from BET Awards.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is about that time. That`s Lizzo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s about damn time. My name is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her opening number at last night`s BET Awards. It was a celebration of Black excellence in entertainment with Taraji P. Henson hosting for the second year in a row.\par \par \uc2 Jazmine Sullivan won Best Female R&B Pop Artist. The Weeknd was named Best Male R&B Pop Artist. Sean Diddy Combs accepted this year`s Lifetime Achievement Award, and a surprise appearance, Kanye "Ye" West joined Baby Face to honor Diddy calling him his favorite artist.\par \par \uc2 West explained what Combs meant to him while he was coming up in hip hop.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KANYE "YE" WEST (Rapper): Back then it was so many rules to hip hop and he broke all of them and broke down all the doors.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Shades of "Pulp Fiction" there.\par \par \uc2 And the end of his own speech, Combs pledged $1 million to a pair of HBCUs, \uc2 Howard University\uc2  and Jackson State.\par \par \uc2 What`s Ye wearing? I`m trying to --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ll have to take our word for it that that`s who it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can tell it was his voice. That was him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And a lot of good moments on the show last night.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Taraji.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the creator of "The Summer I Turned Pretty," don`t you like that title? Yes. Best-selling author, Jenny Han is in our progressive greenroom. She`ll talk about turning her popular books into a very popular TV series.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Jenny Han.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re very excited for our next guest. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " number one best-selling author. Did you catch that? "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " number -- can I say it again, Jenny Han?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One more time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " best-selling author, that would be Jenny Han. She`s written beloved young adult novels like "To All the Boys I`ve Loved Before," and "PS, I Still Love You." Her books are published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which by the way is a division of our parent company, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Summer I Turned Pretty.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Han`s famous trilogy, "The Summer I Turned Pretty" is now a new series on Prime Video. It follows Belly, a teenage girl, whose family stays in a beach house every summer with her mom`s best friend and her sons, Conrad and Jeremiah.\par \par \uc2 Now, this summer just ahead of her 16th birthday, the boys start to realize Belly -- Isabella -- she isn`t a kid anymore. In this scene, Belly confronts Conrad for trying to derail her first date.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Summer I Turned Pretty.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jenny Han looking lovely in Oscar de la Renta, thank you very much. Drop the microphone, Miss Han. I don`t think that clip really does it justice, the dynamics between your characters.\par \par \uc2 By the way, can I just say, I`m Team Conrad. I`ll just say that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You are yes?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I am. And I didn`t even waver.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t waver. I got very concerned as I was watching how it`s going to turn out, I`m Team Conrad. But I love the title. I feel let`s see, "The Summer I Turned Pretty." I love that title, Jenny, what does it mean?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN ("The Summer I Turned Pretty" author and showrunner): To me, it means stepping into your own power. I think it`s a moment of like coming of age, of growing up, and you know, feeling yourself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Feeling good about yourself.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Belly certainly does that.\par \par \uc2 And the whole time I`m reading it, I`m thinking is it loosely based on you or how you felt or what you did?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: I think the moments of agony and embarrassment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: I can draw from my own life, but I would say that I was thinking about you know, I think girlhood in general.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. The soundtrack is amazing. That`s the other thing you don`t get from that clip is that it is shot on this beautiful beach in North Carolina and the soundtrack is incredible.\par \par \uc2 It`s got Taylor Swift. It`s got Olivia Rodrigo. It`s got Cardi B.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got to imagine the budget for all of that was a big-time budget. Like why was it so important to get the right songs at the right time?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: To me, I think that there`s always like a song of summer. You think back to a moment and you remember like the song and the feeling, and I wanted people to watch the show and feel nostalgic and have a memory of something and kind of hit into, I think that pulse point of being young and in love.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, Gayle talked about your inspiration. Maybe it`s taken from your life, but you also say that Taylor Swift -- T. Swift was a large -- she loomed large in your writing process. Tell me about that.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: I mean, I`ve been a fan for years. As I was writing the books, I was listening to her music, I was listening to "Fearless." I almost dedicated --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Fearless."\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Yes, the second book to her because she just has a way I think with her storytelling ability. I just tap so quickly into emotions that I need to get to when I`m writing. That was a big, like, my biggest wish was to get one of her songs on the show, and because I knew it meant a lot to the fans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: They had been begging me for years. And so that was like the best gift I could give them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It is definitely on point.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, it is true that the music that Taylor Swift writes actually dovetails with your work. In other words, the stories that she tells about young love, about rejection, about fear are echoed in your writing.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: I think -- I think that she understands young womanhood in a way and also love stories that means a lot to me as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m fascinated by you, Jenny, because you were told years ago by publishers that, you know, people won`t read stories about Asian- Americans and about Asian-American families.\par \par \uc2 At first, Belly wasn`t an Asian girl. True?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: That`s true. And then --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you were told that, did it hurt your feelings? Did you believe it?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: I think it didn`t even really hurt my feelings because it was just kind of so matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that why Belly is White in the original novel?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: In the original novel, you know, I had tried to sell a book with an Asian main character before this one and people weren`t really interested in it. I think the main -- the thing I would hear is like, we already have like a book about them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have one.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: We would one. And so you know, with "The Summer I Turned Pretty," it felt like a story that I hoped would have a certain kind of like effervescence to it that people could lock on to and then after that, I did "To All the Boys" and I was able to kind of write my own ticket once I had garnered like trust from an audience that might not have picked up a book with someone that didn`t look like them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, Jenny said, "There are going to be some changes around here." I like that. And even on the cover that you have, Belly, because you said you don`t really see Asian-American characters on covers.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Yes. And you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is this a first?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: It was -- well, "To All the Boys" was the first book to hit the bestseller list with an Asian girl on the cover and even with my first book, it is important to me to put a picture on the back of it because at first they were like, we don`t really need, but it wasn`t really done at that time. And I thought, well, young -- I want other young Asian women to see that and think it`s possible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What is staggering success to start writing, have the books go to number one, and now to have a big time series and Season Two has already been approved, right?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You started shooting.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Production.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Yes, I`m about to leave today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you delayed it for us.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: I delayed. I was supposed to go yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh really.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re telling your fans, go on TikTok and do what? What do you want them to do?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Go on TikTok, and I don`t know, they`re making such amazing content about the show that to me, it means a lot just as a creator to see all the stuff that you make. It is so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so, are you Team Conrad or Team Jeremiah?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Oh my gosh, Gayle. Don`t get me in trouble.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, who are you? Tell me?\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: I`m Team Belly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re Team Belly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Whoever she wants.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Team Belly, too, I`ve got to say.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Are you?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m Team Belly, too, but I`m also Team Conrad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, but the Big Team -- the Big Team Belly, you`ve got to be on one of these -- all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I love the line between the moms, too. I love I love that storyline, want everything would be all right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And for the oldies out there, it`s the same beach where they shot "Dawson`s Creek."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know -- Jon says we`ve got to go. But, you know, Jenny, I was just going to watch one episode, just to get a feel of it, and then, I ended up watching the whole damn thing.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: I`m so honored.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh no, it`s good. It`s really.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Tony said he`s going to watch it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I`ve actually resisted. It`s a long story, but I`m going to watch with my older kids because I think it`ll bring up all kinds of important conversations.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Okay. Well, I hope they like it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, they are young --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They will.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Jenny Han. You look great\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So wonderful. Thank you. Love your work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cheering you on. Cheering you on.\par \par \uc2 JENNY HAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Keep it coming.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: The verdicts are in. Author, actor and humorist, his name is John Hodgman -- I should say "Judge" in finger quotes.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Judge John. He is here. He brought his opinions with him. He`s going to weigh in on some issues that may not be a big deal, but they are to a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 But first it is 8:37, times check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Parks and Recreation.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You might know John Hodgman for his appearances on shows like "Parks and Recreation" and "The Daily Show" or maybe as the PC in some memorable \uc2 Apple\uc2  commercials.\par \par \uc2 This morning, all rise, not literally because he`s a fake judge. He joins us in his fake official capacity as the host of "Judge John Hodgman." It`s a podcast where he settles real-life disputes on topics like is chili a soup?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: His ruling agrees with Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, good. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. Is a hot dog a sandwich? Verdict on that one?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. Although if the bun is torn and it becomes two pieces of bread, yes.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE JOHN HODGMAN ("Judge John Hodgman" Podcast host): I`m just looking at Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is Wednesday, there shall be more like this. Hodgman will host a free live justice event at New York City`s Lincoln Center.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And he joins us now to settle some of our own hot disputes.\par \par \uc2 Judge, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE JOHN HODGMAN: Good morning. And yes, please stay seated. I`m just so glad we`re all awake.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just a regular everyday American.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was the process like becoming a fake certified official judge on the internet?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : Very easy. I got a podcast. So anyone can do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you`re self-proclaimed. You`re self-proclaimed.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : Like most experts operating in America today, I am self- proclaimed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right. So the PhD stands for Piled Higher and Deeper.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : I have a Bachelor`s Degree in Literary Theory, no experience whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What should people anticipate on Wednesday?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : Well, I, and my co-host, my bailiff, Jesse Thorn, will get up on stage, Damrosch Park, outside under the stars at Lincoln Center and we`ll have litigants, real-life people who have real small disputes, get up on stage and give their sides of the story.\par \par \uc2 We have a wife and a wife, one of whom does not recognize Daylight Savings Time, refuses to change the clocks, even in her own car. The other wife doesn`t like it. I appreciate the stance on principle, but it could be confusing.\par \par \uc2 We have another couple a guy and his girlfriend. He loves to take long hot baths and then lounge around in his robe on the couch for an hour afterwards. She doesn`t like it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s wrong with that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is against leisure?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, what`s wrong with that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : I am inclined to agree with this guy. He needs to steam off after a long hot bath. She says he gets the couch all wet. I will ask questions. My suspicion is he looks like a dork on the couch in his robe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, you know --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there are things to do and you`re on the coach.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re supposed to be impartial, Judge John, but people say, people listen to you on a regular basis say whenever there`s a dispute between a husband and a wife, you will rule against the husband. True?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : That has nothing to do, first of all, like all Judges, I am not impartial.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : I do have leanings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : And if anything, I leaned in favor of husbands when the show started, but I`ve been doing this now for many years and I have found through data in heterosexual couples, dudes are wrong, almost always wrong.\par \par \uc2 I`ve seen the scatter graph. They`re always wrong because they`ve always got schemes. Always have got schemes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Absolutely true. I like that you say you take cases that are so trivial and absurd that no one will really take this on and I have a trivial and absurd.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : Please.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It drives me nuts that when people open up the kitchen cabinets and leave them open. Now, people could say, "Gayle, just close it." But it just -- it makes me -- it just is irritating to me if I walk in the kitchen and all the cabinets are open or the microwave is sitting open.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is your house haunted, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : Gayle --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going back to when I was --\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : You might be having a poltergeist problem.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This could be beyond Earth.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, I`m going back to when I was married and sometimes when my favorite daughter comes, she does the same thing.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : Yes, I lived with someone who did the same thing as well, and Gayle, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Should we get over it?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : You and I are friends. No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : You and I are friends, a hotdog is not a sandwich. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But if it is torn into two pieces of bread, it is.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : Sir, Tony, I don`t think we have time to get into it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, all right. We do have --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it does irritate you though, the cabinets thing --\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : Of course, their natural state is be closed, and I don`t want to be bonking in my head on those.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. Good.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : But you have to be mindful. One of the tenets, a flipside a law of our show is be mindful of the work you leave for others.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : You leave a cabinet open, someone else has to close it. Either through their labor or their accident bunk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. What was yours, Tony?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN : You tear it in half. If it gets torn in half, it doesn`t -- you`re still not going to ever cut it in half. You`d never over half a hotdog with a cup of soup. That`s the difference.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t want to continue to argue this one, fake internet, Judge John Hodgman, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE JOHN HODGMAN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your Honor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good luck on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It sounds fun.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN: Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m going to be there to heckle you on the hotdog question.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HODGMAN: I hope you will.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was 1973, nearly 50 years ago when the Supreme Court issued Roe versus Wade. That landmark decision determined the Constitution protects a woman`s right to an abortion, at least in the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 Now, the case struck down many State and Federal abortion laws that were considered to cause an undue burden, but that ruling was not the final word.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re going to go to the CBS News archives to review the history of Roe v. Wade and the conflict that has lasted for decades.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hundreds of thousands of women married and unmarried are desperately seeking to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: Before the 1973 ruling of Roe v. Wade, 30 states banned abortion outright.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I figured that I have one child, I should do the best I can for that child, rather than having two and not being able to.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I don`t feel very good about it, but it is something I had to do.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A child in the womb has rights and needs the protection of law.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In a landmark ruling the Supreme Court today legalized abortions.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Other opponents are now talking of a constitutional amendment to reverse today`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We gather here today on the first anniversary of one of the blackest days in the history of American jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think the pro-life movement is kind of flexing its muscles.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Murder is murder.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: In the decades that followed the decision, changes to many Federal and State laws sought to curb or expand abortion access.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When a teenager here in Indiana decides she wants an abortion, but can`t tell her parents, she generally makes the 110-mile trip to Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 MAN: She died as a result of a botched illegal abortion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: People in general are going to start voting on abortion.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re simply telling the awful truth that little baby boys and girls are being dismembered.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Keep your hands off our right to choose.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: While many have condemned this campaign to frighten doctors and clinic directors, the truth is, said one doctor today, it`s working.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Abortion has long been a sensitive issue in this country. It has divided Americans for decades and has recently sparked intense debate over organizations like Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The suspect in the deadly siege of a Planned Parenthood clinic is due in a few hours to make his first Court appearance.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (US Attorney General): Threats of violence and actual violence against the Justices, of course, strike at the heart of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: The 2016 presidential election brought renewed focus on the fight over Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE (Then FOX News Channel anchor): Do you want to see the Court overturn Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Well, if we put another two or perhaps three Justices on, that`s really what`s going to be -- that will happen.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2  (\uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice): Roe v. Wade is an important precedent of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oklahoma is the latest state to move closer to restricting abortion access.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I was raped, and I was 19.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Abortion is murder, and I feel that way. Period.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Other states like Indiana, Nebraska are considering similar laws.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Fifty years of progress being undone will not be accepted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Spreading fallout from that stunning -- and stunning is the word -- Supreme Court leak of a draft opinion that suggests that Roe v. Wade is set to be overturned.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there you have it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And here we are in 2022. The history is long when you look at how long we`ve been discussing and the people thought that this day would never happen. It seemed unthinkable. And yet, here we are in 2022.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nearly a 50-year fight to get to this moment, and now people are saying, it`s going to be another 50-year fight for there to be a change.\par \par \uc2 People also talking about minority rule about unelected judges. We`ve got 80 million people who don`t vote at all. This as a result.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I say what`s not going to be a fight, breaking news, I heard Tony Dokoupil just re-signed his contract.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did. I didn`t know we were going to work that in --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just got the thing and it says you`re going to buy a new pair of shoes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I got them on actually, Gayle. Thanks for inspiring --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220627ei6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Residents Are Concerned The Health System Isn`t Prepared For What`s Next In Jackson, Mississippi\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 576 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It was a Mississippi law banning abortions after 15 weeks that triggered the decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns is there in Jackson tonight with more on what`s next.\par \par \uc2 Good evening.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Well, the State`s Attorney General will be the one to decide when Mississippi`s abortion ban goes into effect and that could come any day now, her decision, and that comes as residents here and across the country are grappling with what comes next.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Emotions boiled over in South Carolina, with protesters from both sides of the abortion debate fighting in the streets of Greenville.\par \par \uc2 (Clip courtesy of Bill Bartholomew: The BartholomewTown Podcast)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In Rhode Island, a Republican state Senate candidate is accused of punching his Democratic rival at a demonstration at the Statehouse. He has now dropped out of the race charged with assault.\par \par \uc2 Within months, abortion could be illegal in roughly half the country. That includes North Dakota, where the state`s only abortion clinic plans to move across the border to Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 Today in Mississippi, where the last clinic is just days away from closing, the decision was front and center at Broadmoor Baptist Church, where Mark Stewart (sp?) is a parishioner.\par \par \uc2 MARK STEWART (Broadmoor Baptist Church Parishioner): My family, you know, when we talk about it, I mean, that`s -- you know, that`s what we`ve all been praying for.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But other residents are afraid of what`s to come.\par \par \uc2 BONNIE GLOVER (Resident of Madison, Mississippi): The people who are protesting about abortions, are they feeding those kids who is here now, or concerned about the ones who haven`t gotten here?\par \par \uc2 WAIKINYA CLANTON (\uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2 , Jackson Director): The thing that`s going to keep me up at night is the women who will lose their lives because they don`t have access to care.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Waikinya Clanton is Director of the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 WAIKINYA CLANTON: We weren`t prepared even before the decision. I mean, we have probably one of the most destitute healthcare systems here in the country that is going to impact hundreds and thousands of people right here in the Magnolia State.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Once surgical abortion is banned here and elsewhere, the issue becomes the future of restrictions over the abortion pill and whether women will be prosecuted for ordering them by mail.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Caitlin Huey-Burns for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to Texas hearings into the police response to the Uvalde School shooting resume tomorrow. This weekend, the last funeral was held for the 21 victims who were shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 Uziyah Garcia was laid to rest in San Angelo, Texas. The 10-year-old is remembered for his contagious laugh and his love for trampolines.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020220630ei6q0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protests Roll On After Roe Falls As American Women Begin The Week Without The Constitutional Right To An Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Christina Ruffini \par \uc2 867 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight: Supreme aftershocks.\par \par \uc2 Protesters: My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Protests roll on after Roe falls as American women begin the week without the constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER (D-Michigan): We are using every tool we have to fight for reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (R-South Dakota): The Supreme Court did its job, it fixed a wrong decision it made many years ago.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: While activists gear up for a new battle.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: I`m Caitlin Huey-Burns in Jackson, Mississippi where residents are concerned the health system isn`t prepared for what`s next.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, Russia intensifies attacks in Ukraine, missiles striking targets across the country.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders meeting in Germany take aim at \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I`m Ed O`Keefe at the G7 Summit. Leaders of the world`s largest developed economies mock Putin and plan more sanctions against him.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Summer travel takes off with challenges on the road and in the air.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: I`m Danya Bacchus at Burbank Airport where passengers are facing full flights and soaring prices.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Pride parades march across the country with new urgency.\par \par \uc2 And later, it`s a book that cast a spell on the world. Tonight, we look back at 25 years of "Harry Potter" mania.\par \par \uc2 CHILDREN: Harry Potter!\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening and thanks for joining us on this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Advocates on both sides of the abortion battle say their fight is not done. Anti-abortion rights forces are vowing to use Friday`s Supreme Court ruling reversing 50 years of Federal protection of abortion to push for near total bans in every state, while abortion rights groups promise to harness anger in the streets, turning it into action at the ballot box this November.\par \par \uc2 A new CBS News poll taken right after the Court`s decision finds most people disapprove of the decision to overturn Roe. A majority call it "a step backward for America."\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Christina Ruffini is on Capitol Hill and leads us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 Christina, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 What you`re seeing now is the legal fallout from this decision as states all over the country scramble to figure out what exactly their laws on abortion are. Some are trying to enshrine protections into their State Constitutions, while others are trying to ensure the Federal government doesn`t create a workaround for some of the strictest abortion bans in the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: Abortions in the State of South Dakota immediately became illegal, unless it was to save the life of a mother.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: With her state`s trigger law now locked into place, Republican Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  was asked if South Dakota would include exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: I just never believed that having a tragedy or a tragic situation happen to someone is a reason to have another tragedy occur.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I`m going to fight like hell.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voiceover): In Michigan, Democratic Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is asking its Supreme Court to clarify what applies. The current abortion law or a ban that`s been dormant, but on the books since the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: What I`m trying to fight for is the status quo in Michigan and there are reasonable restrictions on that. There is no common ground, which is the sad thing.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: According to a new CBS News poll out today, 56 percent of Americans think the overturn of Roe v. Wade will make women`s lives worse, and more than half say they think the Supreme Court is likely to put limits on gay marriage or access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): A victory for life.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: At a rally in Illinois, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told the crowd that his work nominating conservative Justices made the ruling possible.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We got almost 300 Federal Judges and three great Supreme Court Justices confirmed to do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Christina, what recourse do lawmakers have who are clearly not happy with this reversal?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Well, Democrats, including Senator Elizabeth Warren are now advocating to add Justices to the Supreme Court. The Constitution doesn`t specify a number and historically, it`s ranged anywhere from five to 10. Although President Biden has previously said he would not support Court packing -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It started off originally with six.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020220630ei6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For June 26, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Strassmann, Jan Crawford, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation. I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 Last week, we saw several seismic developments here in Washington that will impact Americans and our democracy for years to come. We are living in the thick of unprecedented upheaval around the country and the world, and we are all feeling its impact.\par \par \uc2 Just this morning, our CBS News poll puts the number of Americans feeling that our democracy is under threat at 72 percent.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in nearly 50 years, tens of millions of American women begin the week without the protection of a constitutional right to an abortion. It`s a significant victory for the anti-abortion rights movement.\par \par \uc2 But Friday`s Supreme Court decision to direct states to regulate abortion on their own terms has resulted in chaos and confusion as to the ruling`s practical impact and, not surprisingly, enormous anger from abortion rights supporters and fear sparked by rhetoric from those who think laws should be more restrictive.\par \par \uc2 Just a day earlier, we saw dramatically different actions from the legislative and judicial branches on guns, as the Supreme Court cleared the way for nearly all American adults to carry concealed weapons in public.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, just a few hundred yards away, Congress, who had been crippled by inaction on significant gun control legislation for decades, responded to the recent massacres in Uvalde and Buffalo by passing a bipartisan law expanding background checks for gun buyers under 21, incentivizing state red flag laws, and providing states with funding for mental health and school safety.\par \par \uc2 In the January 6 Committee hearings, we heard testimony that President Trump repeatedly pressed some state election officials and Justice Department officials to interfere in the election with the knowledge that his actions were illegal.\par \par \uc2 These fissures in our three branches of government and the test of our democracy come as President Biden joins world leaders in Germany to discuss ways to keep the global economy afloat and to counter Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 The challenges facing the president and our country are nothing short of enormous. These challenges are taking their toll on Americans` view of how Mr. Biden is handling his job. Our new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll shows only 41 percent of Americans approve of his job. That`s the lowest we have recorded for his presidency.\par \par \uc2 We begin today with CBS News senior national correspondent Mark Strassmann in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): Chaos in the Carolinas, protesters squaring off in Greenville, South Carolina, one of many weekend flash points.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, this pickup truck`s driver bowled right through abortion rights protesters.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Supreme Court (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: From noisy crowds in New York to Los Angeles, we`re a country convulsed by the Supreme Court`s Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 This constitutional milestone...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Goodbye, Roe! Goodbye, Roe!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... caps a half-century crusade by conservative activists.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: A motto that my mom taught me in her ministry was, our job is not to make abortion illegal. It`s to make it unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Enraged abortion rights supporters say Dobbs robs women of a fundamental freedom...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My body, my choice!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... and gives state lawmakers, mostly men, control over their bodies and futures.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #1: We assumed that was going to be forever. It was accepted by the Supreme Court, by the Constitution of the United States. To have that threatened now and other things being threatened now because of this caving in, it`s very, very scary. It is scary to democracy. This is not democracy.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Within months, abortion could be illegal in roughly half the country.\par \par \uc2 By the end of July, these 13 states will activate so-called trigger bans. In at least seven states, it`s already happened, all or most abortions now banned.\par \par \uc2 In this group of states, the Dobbs decision could see anti-abortion legislation unblocked by lower courts or resurrect pre-Roe abortion bans. Abortion`s future is uncertain in parts of America, and, in 20 states, abortion rights remain solidly in place, for now.\par \par \uc2 Georgia is one of a dozen states to pass a so-called fetal heartbeat bill. The Dobbs decision could clear the way for them to become law and to end legal abortions at six weeks.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We will abolish abortion!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: America`s high fever over abortion makes a dialectic impossible. We`re not going to talk this one out. And, in many ways, Dobbs settles little.\par \par \uc2 Abortion rights opponents, empowered, want more state restrictions.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: This decision must not stand!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Abortion rights supporters, still standing, vow never to go back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mark Strassmann reporting there from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 One of the states finding a restrictive abortion ban that predates Roe vs. Wade is Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Its Democratic governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , joins us this morning from Lansing.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER (D-Michigan): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, in your state, abortion is still temporarily available due to a court injunction on a 1931 law that was already on your books that would outlaw it, with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 I know there`s confusion in your state. Like, what exactly is the status of your appeal to the state Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Well, this was precisely why I filed my lawsuit a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people questioned whether or not it was timely or even necessary. And it`s become very clear that both it is timely, not only that it is urgent, and absolutely necessary. There is a lot of confusion about what this means for IVF, for practitioners.\par \par \uc2 And some hospitals are interpreting it, trying to understand with prosecutors all over the board. And that`s why our Supreme Court needs to act. I have teed up this lawsuit asking them to recognize a fundamental due process and equal protection right to privacy and right to bodily autonomy and reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 And it is time for them to weigh in and recognize this right under our state Constitution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So what happens if you lose?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Well, we`ve got -- we`re going at it. We`re pulling out all the stops. This is a fight-like-hell moment.\par \par \uc2 So our partners filed this other lawsuit. They got the injunction. That is on appeal, so it`s precarious. And there`s an effort to collect signatures and amend our Constitution. so we are taking -- using every tool we have to fight for reproductive rights for Michigan women, and Ohio women and Indiana women who come to Michigan for their health care.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, in every state, now state capitals are going to make these decisions.\par \par \uc2 So I want to get to the specifics. Roe vs. Wade, previously guaranteed abortion up to viability, roughly 24 weeks of pregnancy. If the courts strike down this ban you are fighting, and you have to craft a new law here, is there compromise that is possible here? Can you settle on 20 weeks, 15 weeks, anything less than Roe?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: What I`m trying to fight for is the status quo in Michigan. And there are reasonable restrictions on that.\par \par \uc2 With the current legislature that I have, there is no common ground, which is the sad thing. They`ve already introduced legislation to criminalize and throw nurses and doctors in jail. They`ve all endorsed the 1931 law, as has all of the Republican people running for governor.\par \par \uc2 They want abortion to be a felony, no exceptions for rape or incest. That`s the kind of legislature that I`m working with. That`s the kind of matchup I`m going to have this fall. And that`s why this is such a scary moment for Michigan women and our families.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, according to the \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling, most recent we did, most Americans feel abortion should be legal within the first trimester, so 12 weeks of pregnancy, roughly. A third say it should be legal after that as well.\par \par \uc2 If you`re saying you can`t talk this out with your state legislature, can you at least put it on the ballot? Did -- does the public get to decide here? How do you move forward?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Yeah, so 70 percent of the people in our state do support a woman being able to make that choice herself, whether that`s a choice that they individually would support or not, would make or not.\par \par \uc2 I am horrified, as are so many women who are 50 years old or in my generation, that the thought that my daughters will have fewer rights than I have had virtually my whole life.\par \par \uc2 But I take heart in the fact that the vast majority of people in this state support a woman having that right to choose. I was raised by a pro-choice Republican father. And there are pro-choice Republicans and independents out there. We need them to join this fight.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. But, then, if you put it out there to the public, they`ve got to pick and make a specific statement here. Is anything less than Roe possible? Is compromise possible?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I think compromise is possible.\par \par \uc2 We`ve already seen Michigan enact some some -- some restrictions on abortion. We have a waiting period. There`s -- beyond viability, there -- it`s not accessible unless it`s for the life of the mother, but -- the woman.\par \par \uc2 But this is a moment where we are seeing how extreme the Michigan GOP has gotten, this -- this radical agenda to deprive women of the ability to make their own most important economic decision they`ll make in their lifetime, when and whether or not to have a child.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 But, I mean, how much do you think that the Democratic Party or Democratic leadership failed here? This was telegraphed for so long. Should the federal government now do more?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Well, I have been urging for months Democratic leaders to use every tool at their disposal to fight to protect women`s reproductive autonomy and right to make our health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 I have used every tool in my toolbox. We all have different sets, this has now been pushed down to the states. And that`s why I`m fighting so hard to protect this right in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you aren`t looking for more from the federal government at this time?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: As I said, I am urging every, every pro-choice leader to use every tool in their toolbox. So I`m hopeful and believe that the Biden administration is going to do that. I can just tell you what I am doing here on the ground in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about this Homeland Security warning that domestic violent extremists may intensify violence. In the bulletin that \uc2 CBS\uc2  obtained, it specifically mentioned an incident in Michigan related to a pro-abortion rights group.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are you about violence? What are you seeing on the ground?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I am concerned about a lot of things happening in the United States right now.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, the last couple of decisions that came out of this \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  are -- make America a lot more dangerous, more guns, fewer rights, less health care. It is scary.\par \par \uc2 And, as a lawyer, it crushes me to say that even I am losing faith that these important institutions that are supposed to be above the politics of the day are now being corrupted. And that`s what we`re seeing out of our \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . And I am very concerned about our long-term prosperity, our homeland security and our safety.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But this warning about threats to federal, state government officials, including judges, are you concerned about active threats in Michigan?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Of course I am.\par \par \uc2 I -- Margaret, I have been the recipient of so much ugliness and hate often stoked by the former president. This is a really scary moment. And with the proliferation of the ugly rhetoric, the scary proliferation of guns in America, and the fewer and fewer restrictions, I think that any parent who sends their child to school, any politician or policymaker who makes a hard decision, we now have to be much more fearful on a whole new level.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I`m assuming you can`t get your state to establish a red flag law at this point, despite the federal incentive?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I`m still pushing.\par \par \uc2 But considering the makeup of the legislature that I have to work with, it`s been very hard to even get them to protect themselves by forbidding guns in the state capitol. So, it is -- that`s a part of this gerrymandered legislature and this kind of radicalized Republican Party in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor Whitmer, we will be watching what happens next in your state. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: South Dakota is one of 13 states with so-called trigger laws that locked new abortion restrictions into place after Roe vs. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Republican \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  is the governor of that state and has a new memoir out titled, "Not My First Rodeo: Lessons from the Heartland."\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (R-South Dakota): Good morning. Thank you so much for having me on. Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m hoping you can clarify some things, because I know, under South Dakota law, abortions are now criminal acts, only exceptions if it`s necessary to preserve the life of a pregnant female.\par \par \uc2 You`re calling a special session. What exactly do you want to change or implement here?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: Well, what happened with the Supreme Court decision this week is that abortions in the state of South Dakota immediately became illegal unless it was to save the life of a mother.\par \par \uc2 And so that was in place as soon as that decision was made. And what I believe will happen is that, if we do go into a special session, that there`ll be debate around how we can support these mothers. I have already launched a Web site that`s -- it`s called Life.SD.gov.\par \par \uc2 And it`s to get resources to individuals who have an unplanned pregnancy or,if they`re in a crisis situation, that we will coordinate financial assistance, people that will come alongside them during this time, also make sure that they get health care and access that they may need.\par \par \uc2 It lists all the nonprofits, different organizations that work with them, and then also can extend to families that may want to adopt their baby should they choose to give their baby up for adoption. So it`s an incredible resource that does more to support these women that are in a situation that was unplanned and really does leave them in a situation where they`re not prepared for the news that they have that they`re expecting a baby.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 On that, though, America has the worst maternal mortality rate of any developed country. What specifically are you doing for these women who, not just when they have the baby, but during their pregnancy? Are you giving them paid leave? Are you giving them more health care rights in your state? Are you giving them more state funding?\par \par \uc2 What exactly are you doing to keep them alive during their pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: You know, I think that will be a lot of the debate that will go on now in every different states, now that the Supreme Court has made this decision.\par \par \uc2 The power to make these decisions really goes to each individual state. We`ve already talked about that in South Dakota. What`s the state`s role in this and what can we do to help these individuals that are in these situations, get them the health care that they need to help their baby be born healthy and help them be parents or help them choose a loving family to raise that child?\par \par \uc2 So, these will...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you`re still figuring out specific -- OK.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: Well, and, you know, this trigger law was put into place years ago. And it was to go into -- into law as soon as the Supreme Court made a decision such as it did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: So that`s the debate and discussion that we`re having.\par \par \uc2 But I think what`s incredible and what`s going on is that the people will decide.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: Elected officials at the state level is who they`ll be talking to, to decide what their state`s laws look like.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota`s, obviously, under our past several years and -- have stood for life and defending life.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: And I think we`ll continue to have those debates on how we can support these mothers and what it means to really make sure that we`re not prosecuting mothers ever in a situation like this when it comes to abortion, that it will always be focused towards those doctors who knowingly break the law to perform abortions in our state.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So there`s a lot of confusion here.\par \par \uc2 So, South Dakota is one of 30 states that will limit access to telemedicine abortions, which allow patients to receive these pills in the mail that would allow them to end their pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The president has said he`s going to use the Justice Department to intervene here if there`s an attempt to stop women from receiving these pills.\par \par \uc2 Are you prepared for that kind of legal battle? Are you actually going to seize mail? How would you stop women from actually receiving this federally approved medication?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: Well, we`ve already addressed this in many ways in the state of South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 I brought a bill that would ban telemedicine abortions, which means a doctor of the Internet or over the phone could prescribe an abortion for an individual, because these are very dangerous medical procedures. A woman is five times more likely to end up in an emergency room if they`re utilizing this kind of...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is an \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved drug.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: ... method for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s something that should be under the supervision of a medical doctor. And it is something in South Dakota that we`ve made sure happens that way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. But that`s what I`m asking you for specifics of, because...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: I think that...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: ... are at the state level.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because, at the state level...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: And that`s the debate that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, but what is happening here is incredibly important and precedent-setting.\par \par \uc2 This is a federally approved drug. Are you saying the state of South Dakota is now going to overrule the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and decide which drugs are going to be available to its residents?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: And many of those decisions are made at the state level. They absolutely are. That`s what states do. There`s certain rights that are protected...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Will you stop -- if it`s sent in the mail, will you intercede and stop it from being received?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: There are certain protections that are guarded under the Constitution of the United States. The rest of these items are left to the states. The 10th Amendment guarantees us that.\par \par \uc2 What the Supreme Court said was that the Constitution does not give a woman the right to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: That means that, in each state, they will make the decision how they handle these situations.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly. And that`s...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: In South Dakota, we`ve already had a bill passed that set on telemedicine abortions, that we don`t believe it should be available, because it is a dangerous situation for those individuals without being medically supervised by a physician.\par \par \uc2 So...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, so it sounds like you`re ready to fight the Justice Department on that one.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about something else the president said. He said that his administration will oppose any state governments that try to block the mail, search a person`s medicine cabinet or control a woman`s actions by tracking data on apps that she uses.\par \par \uc2 Is South Dakota going to do that kind of surveillance or adopt laws like Oklahoma and Texas have, which incentivize civilians to report on their neighbors?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: Margaret, that`s never been the conversation in South Dakota. And I don`t anticipate that we will ever do that.\par \par \uc2 We take privacy rights very important. We -- our -- protect our freedoms and our liberties here. We will make sure that mothers have the resources, protection and medical care that they need. And we`re being aggressive on that. And we`ll also make sure that the federal government only does its job.\par \par \uc2 We saw in unprecedented ways that this administration has been overstepping its authority and they`ve been punishing the American people. And we`ve seen increased energy costs, supply chain challenges, freedoms taken away.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: It`s been incredible the amount of problems this country is having because of the discussion and the debate and the policies out of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 My job as governor is to do my job, and that`s protect my people from these bad policies.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So how are you going to handle corporations operating in your state that say they will provide health care and financial support to employees who travel out of state for abortions?\par \par \uc2 Are you going to go after those companies or go after those individuals?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: Well, Margaret, I don`t know if you`re paying attention to really the debate that`s going on in South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Those aren`t the conversations we`re having. People in South Dakota really recognize that this decision...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you won`t sue \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , for example, or Amazon, who operate in your state, and said that they will do that for their employees? \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and Amazon will not face litigation from the state of South Dakota?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: We`re going to continue to support these mothers, make sure that they are protected from any kind of prosecution that would happen, make sure that these babies are recognized and that every single life is precious, and that we enforce our laws.\par \par \uc2 And I will continue to make sure that the people of our state know they can speak to their elected representatives...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: ... that will make those decisions closer to home than what we`re seeing at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, so I think you were saying you`re not going to go after corporations there.\par \par \uc2 Let me just ask you here what a fellow...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: We have no intention to do that, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 A fellow Republican, Congresswoman Nancy Mace of South Carolina, was on this program a few weeks ago. And I want to play something she said, because she says she is anti-abortion rights, but she has an exception for rape and incest. Listen to her.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): I`m a rape victim myself. And when you realize what`s happened in your life, the trauma, the emotional, the mental, the physical trauma in a woman`s life, that decision -- she should make that decision with her doctor and between her and her God.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you respond to her? Are you open to exceptions for rape and incest?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: I think it`s tragic what Nancy went through, and my heart goes out to every single woman who`s had to go through that situation. I don`t know what that`s like.\par \par \uc2 What I would say is that I believe every life is precious. Our trigger law does reflect that if, it`s to save the life of a mother, that an abortion is still legal. And we know so much more using technology and science than we did even 10, 15 years ago about what these babies go through, the pain that they feel in the womb, and we will continue to make sure that those lives are protected.\par \par \uc2 And I just have never believed that -- that having a tragedy or tragic situation happened to someone is a reason to have another tragedy occur.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So no exception for rape or incest?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: So, I would prefer that we continue to make sure we go forward and that we`re putting resources in front of these women and walking alongside them, getting them the health care, the care, the mental health counseling and services that they should need to make sure that we can continue to support them and build stronger families far into the future as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, no exceptions?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: You know, this is a debate that`s going to continue to happen from state to state, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: And I think that`s what`s unique about the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 I love that about this country, is that we have a very limited federal government. The Supreme Court did its job.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: It fixed a wrong decision it made many years ago and returned this power back to the states, which is how the Constitution and our founders intended it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: So the fear tactics I hear coming out of so many people, from so many pundits and those in the media, scaring women, saying that they`re going to have a big -- bigger risk of death...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Well, Governor...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: ... because of this decision, what this is going to do is give them...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: ... the ability to weigh in with their local elected officials...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM: ... to make sure that their state statutes reflect what they need to have their health care and their options and their babies protected and supported into the future.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly, Governor, which is why we asked you here today, to try to clarify that. And we`ll track it as you figure them out.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR, or we`re available on demand.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ app. And we`re replayed on our CBS News Streaming Network at noon Eastern.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with our chief legal correspondent, Jan Crawford, and a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We will also get an update on the January 6 hearings.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 For more on the impact of the court`s decision, we turn to our chief legal correspondent Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 Jan, this was a huge decision. You accurately predicted it for months, back in December.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Back in December on your show.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what we know is the Supreme Court ruled a state can now ban abortion, not only before viability, which is 24 weeks, but at any time in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 So, what does that mean? What does post-Roe America look like?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, I think, was you saw from your excellent conversations with Governor Whitmer and Governor Noem, there`s going to be a patchwork of laws based on what the states there think about abortion and the right to abortion. So, it will depend on where you live. And if you`re a woman in a state that bans abortion, unless you can get your legislatures and your governor to change that law, you`re going to have to travel to another state to get an abortion, or try to get a medical abortion, those pills by mail, but, as you saw from Governor Noem, some states are going to try to ban that as well.\par \par \uc2 But the point is, women do have more options now, I guess. If we look back to what it was like before Roe, there are more options. Abortion is generally more discussed. There`s more support for women seeking abortion than there was then. But in some ways also the conversation has gotten more punitive now. You see efforts to punish women and efforts to turn neighbors into bounty hunters of sort.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s going to be interesting to see how that works out as we progress.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, it`s a great point and I think we`re just beginning this conversation, really, about abortion.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the decision itself made at the court, the security that day was incredible. And we know the Supreme Court justices themselves are under heavy guard.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, there`s been a significant amount of threats. I mean, and even before the ruling, we saw an attempted assassination attempt, a man charged with attempting to assassinate Justice Kavanaugh.\par \par \uc2 The protests we`ve seen so far, though, they`ve largely been pretty peaceful. And I think groups on both sides are really trying to condemn any violence and keep the debate really focused, not on violence, but on the real issues that go to the core of women`s rights and the rights, as the other side says, to the -- of the unborn.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Justice Thomas wrote that this court should revisit decisions related to gay marriage and contraception. But the conservative majority, Justices Alito, Justices Kavanaugh, they both said in their opinion that it doesn`t call those things into question. So, which is it, and does it set our country on a course towards, you know, political and legal conflict?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, yes, more political and legal conflict than we have now, right?\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I mean, Justice Thomas wrote that separate opinion that the language is obviously very jarring for some people to read. But he has one vote and it takes five. And the court majority said it`s hard to see how we could be any more clear that those cases, the right to contraception, the right to same-sex marriage are not in doubt. That abortion is different, because, as the court said, it involves a life. You had Justice Kavanaugh writing a separate opinion making that point, emphasizing that point. Those cases are not at risk.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, there are not five votes on the Supreme Court to reexamine those cases. There is one.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it opens that conversation about, do you need to put those things into law, codify them? There`s so much more here, Jan. You`re going to be busy. I`m sure you`ll be back with us. Thank you for your analysis here.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 In last week`s January 6th committee hearings we learned of President Trump`s pressure campaign on state election officials in Georgia and Arizona, along with efforts to recruit his own Justice Department, then led by Acting Attorney General Jeffrey Rosen, to overturn the election and claim fraud without evidence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): Mr. Rosen said to Mr. Trump, quote, DOJ can`t and won`t snap its fingers and change the outcome of the election.\par \par \uc2 How did the president respond to that, sir?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD DONOGHUE: He responded very quickly and said, just say it was corrupt and leave the rest to me and the Republican congressmen.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A top Trump aide also testified that at least five members of Congress, who helped spread Trump`s lies about fraud in the 2020 election, later sought pardons for their roles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (Republican Congressman from Illinois): The only reason I know to ask for a pardon is because you think you`ve committed a crime.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: California Congressman Pete Aguilar is a number of the January 6th Select Committee and he joins us from New York.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We also heard sworn testimony that the former president spoke to the Justice Department and Homeland Security about seizing voting machines. There was testimony this week that a Pentagon official and the defense secretary - the acting defense secretary were chasing a conspiracy theory about Italian satellites changing votes. These are outrageous ideas, but how far did this actually get?\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: Well, I think what the testimony and what we have laid out clearly indicates that the president, you know, knew he lost the election, and then he continued to gravitate to these conspiracy theories along the way, in November and December, calling the election corrupt, as we heard the Department of Justice officials. And then, when ever legal door had closed and he lost over 60 lawsuits, then the pressure campaign to the Department of Justice, to his own vice president. That`s what we saw. But there was no shortage of conspiracy theorists in his ear each and every time. The text messages to Mark Meadows laid that out theory after theory, individuals bringing things up that had no basis in fact and that his own Department of Justice refuted.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Does Vice President Pence, who you just mentioned, does he need to come and testify before your committee?\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: Well, that was the hearing that I led, was all about the president`s pressure on the vice president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: We heard directly about that pressure campaign from his top legal counsel at the time. And we think it was an important hearing. And clearly there is a lot more there. And we would, obviously, love to gather more information.\par \par \uc2 But I think we clearly laid out the case that the president had no regard for the vice president`s safety. Never reached out to him that day at all and was willing to sacrifice his own vice president while stopping a peaceful transfer of power if it meant holding on to power himself.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you don`t have a firm no from the former vice president?\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: You know, I`m not going to get into, you know, conversations about future interviews or witnesses. But what I can tell you is that the committee has said from the very beginning, more information is good. And we`re always going to be willing to take in more information about what happened on January 6th, and what were the causes that led up to January 6th.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: But clearly, as we have laid out, in five strong hearings, that this is just about telling the facts, and that`s all we`re concerned about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, we look at our own polling and we see that confidence in the U.S. electoral system is not faring well. Most Americans think at least somewhat likely in the future, according to our polling, election officials will refuse to certify result for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 Do you think, based on the work you`ve been doing, that the public should feel more confident?\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: Well, I think the public should be aware of this. And I think that`s exactly what the hearing has sought to do, which is, how do we protect democracy? This is bigger than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This is bigger than one individual. How do we protect democracy and make sure that we stand up for the rule of law? And clearly there were individuals who did their job that day and leading up to January 6th. Brad Raffensperger and other elected officials who truly did their job. But, in the future, there will be an option - or there is a possibility that people may not do their job, and I think that`s the problem that we - that we face.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s a little bit of competing background noise there, but I do want to ask you, we heard from those Justice Department officials who testified this week about Mr. Trump`s plans to install this lawyer, Jeffrey Clark, as the acting attorney general to help his scheme here to overturn the election. And \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the vice chair, made a passionate plea for the White House Counsel, Pat Cipollone, to come and talk to you.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to subpoena him? Why is he not on the docket?\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: Well, there will be future hearings. There will be more witnesses. That`s what I can say. I`m not going to get into specifics for witnesses. But I believe that the vice chair was very clear that we would love to hear from Pat Cipollone. There are other witnesses who we feel will add to what we are doing and the work product that we`re putting together on future hearings that we have when Congress reconvenes.\par \par \uc2 We look forward to these future hearings. This is about piecing together this puzzle for the American public.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: And so we know clearly what`s at stake in protecting democracy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to read the names here of the individuals we heard this week actually asked for pardons due to their role on January 6th. Congressman Jim Jordan, Congresswoman Marjorie Taylor Greene inquired about presidential pardons. A White House aide testified that Matt Gaetz, Mo Brooks, Andy Biggs, Louis Gohmert, Scott Perry, all sitting congressmen, explicitly asked for pardons.\par \par \uc2 What action should be taken against your fellow lawmakers?\par \par \uc2 PETE AGUILAR: Well, many of these lawmakers we have asked to come before the committee. We have sent them letters. We have sent some subpoenas to them.\par \par \uc2 What`s important is that we tell the truth. But as my colleague Adam Kinzinger mentioned, there really isn`t -- I think the American public understands this, folks asking for pardons generally feel that they did something illegal. And so I think it`s important that the public understands that. I think what people understand about the January 6th committee is that we only present things based in evidence and fact. That`s exactly what we laid out this week. We look forward to laying out, you know, more facts about what happened, as well as this topic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we will look for those further hearings.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Congressman, for your time today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined now by Marc Short. He was former Vice President Pence`s chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Marc. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT (Former Vice President Mike Pence`s Chief of Staff): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ve been hearing a lot about you. I`m glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 You were with the former vice president on January 6th when he sheltered and when he ultimately went ahead and certified the election results that day.\par \par \uc2 Is he watching these hearings, and why is he dragging out that decision on whether to cooperate?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: Oh, I don`t know that I`d say he`s dragging out a decision on that, Margaret. I think as -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He made one?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: As far as the question of, is he watching, I think we all lived it and - and I don`t think that probably - I don`t think he`s waiting with bated breath and watching these hearings in the same extent that perhaps some inside the beltway are.\par \par \uc2 Last week, for instance, as the hearings were going on, he was out campaigning in Illinois for Darin LaHood and Esther Joy King. He was campaigning for Steve Chabot. He was up in New York campaigning for \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . He`s trying to make sure Republicans have a great midterm cycle. And that`s where his focus is, looking to the future and not relitigating the past.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Congressman Aguilar should not expect the former vice president to come speak to the committee?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: I`m going to leave that conversation to the vice president and his council to answer that, Margaret. But I think it would be incredibly unprecedented and I think it would - it`s also, I think, conversations between a president and a vice president that there is a separation of power that should be respected. And let`s keep in mind that there is currently a former vice president who occupied the Oval Office. Do you want Congress being able to drag up former vice presidents for certain subpoenas or for certain testimony? I think we`d create a terrible precedent.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This has never happened before in history.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean this is incredible. You lived it, as you say.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: We did live it. And, Margaret, I think that both myself, and, as you know, the chief counsel, has been under subpoena, have testified for many hours. And so we`ve told, I think, the story of what the vice president`s team witnessed and saw.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let`s ask -- I want to ask you about the team.\par \par \uc2 Former Trump chief of staff Mark Meadows, your counterpart, his name comes up again and again. You just heard the congressman mention it too.\par \par \uc2 The acting attorney general testified just a few days ago that it was Meadows who was distributing this bizarre conspiracy theory about Italian satellites. I mean he was sending gifts, or wanted to, to Georgia state election officials.\par \par \uc2 How complicit was he in this lie?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: You know, Margaret, I think that Mark would often say to me that he was working to try and get the president to concede and accept the results of the election, and, at the same time, it was clear he was bringing in lots of other people into the White House that were feeding the president different conspiracy theories. I think that Mark was telling different audiences all sorts of different stories. And so I think, as I`ve said on many occasions, I believe the president was very poorly served by the team that he had around him, and I think that they fed him many conspiracy theories about the events that conspired on Election Day and then the following.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there were individuals who stood up to the president and said that`s not true, that`s false, that`s unconstitutional, that`s illegal. The vice president was one of them. Mark Meadows did not.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: I think the vice president`s been a consistent, constitutional conservative his whole life and I think that, you know, I think we`re all proud of the way that he handled that day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about abortion in a moment, but just here on this topic. The January 6th committee revealed this week that the chief of staff for Senator Ron Johnson of Wisconsin was trying to pass along fake electors to the vice president to -- right before he was set to certify the election. Were you aware of this scheme? Was the senator directly involved?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: I have no reason to believe that the senator was directly involved. I know that Chris Hodge (ph) and our director of elector (ph) affairs hared with me that in texts that came in, and I had said to Chris respond not to deliver that to the vice president because what we learned when we sat down with the parliamentarian, Margaret, is that, honestly, this happens every cycle, that members send in separate fake sets of electors every time, every four years, but they come in to the archives or the parliamentarian and they dismiss them. If they`re not certified, it`s kind of meaningless.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But are you saying what Ron Johnson`s staff was doing was totally kosher?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: No, I`m not - I`m not saying - no, I didn`t say that, Margaret. I said that what happens is that individuals across the country can send in their own set. They do it all the time. It means nothing, though, unless a state has certified it. And so -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is a senator`s staff.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: And so we intentionally were like, no, there`s no interest in seeing a separate set that has not been certified by the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But wasn`t that troubling to you? I mean this isn`t just someone randomly sending it in the mail, it`s a senator.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: I think it was clear at that point of where we were in this that there were other electors that were being submitted. So, I can`t say that it was necessarily shocking at that stage of events.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Was he the only one who tried to do this?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: I don`t know of any other member of Congress that -- or staff that tried to no. But I have no reason to know that Ron Johnson was behind that or not. It was a staff to staff conversation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It pretty incredible, frankly.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: There were a lot of incredible things that happened around that day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. We could talk for the next hour, Marc, about that day.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: We could, Margaret, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you as well though about the former vice president who, as you mentioned, has been out there on the campaign trail. One of the things that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is known for is being a staunch opponent of abortion and very much pro-life.\par \par \uc2 You played such a key role in getting these three Supreme Court justices on the court who did vote to overturn Roe v. Wade. The conservative majority directly linked to the administration you served in.\par \par \uc2 You are now talking about, the vice president is -- former vice president, a ban on abortion. So going further than where we are right now. What does a ban on abortion look like?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: Well, I think that the vice president has been champing life since the very beginning of his public career, and I think he was one of the ones in Congress, as you know, who advocated for pro-life position and advocated for ending of taxpayer funding of abortions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exceptions for rape or incest?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: I think that where we believe that life begins at conception, but I think the vice president has always accepted exceptions for rape and incest because he believes that we should continue to move forward toward a more perfect union that includes the protection of life. I mean, Margaret - -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What about like what Governor Youngkin in doing in Virginia, 15 weeks? Is there room for compromise like that?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: Margaret, I think that we are going to continue to champion life wherever we can. And if that means that you`re able to extend more protections for unborn children, we`re going to advocate for that.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s important to keep in mind that there never was -- there never was anything that justices could revert to, to say, in Roe v. Wade there was a protection or a certain right for women to - for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: Even \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  herself said it was highly unusual for them to have such judicial activism in the case of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. She did.\par \par \uc2 Do you oppose the kind of surveillance and punitive action against women?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: I think that we as - we as conservatives are always better when we`re speaking from a position of compassion than condemnation. And so we should be acknowledging that there are multiple victims in abortion, not just the child, but also the mother. And we should be looking to provide those women with the care and services that they need.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Marc Short, good to have you here. Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: Margaret, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We now want to welcome \uc2 David Malpass\uc2 , president of the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 , (\uc2 World Bank Group\uc2  President): Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are a lot of stressors on the global economic system right now. How do you describe where we are?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 : It`s a sharp slowdown, including even China. So, we`ve seen the world growth fall by half since January in terms of GDP growth. But there`s also shortages. There`s inflation. And the food shortages for the poorer countries are becoming a significant concern. They already are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean global inflation, it`s not just the U.S., it`s other wealthy countries around the world. But you`re also describing a recipe for global instability?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 : It feeds in. When there`s not enough food that -- for weaker countries, poorer countries, that causes instability. And it`s a big factor in the turnover of governments that`s been, you know, occurring in quite a few of the countries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Covid caused the deepest global recession since World War II. Now the world economy is in danger because of this Russian invasion in Ukraine. How do you avoid a global recession with all of these factors?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 : Some countries, it`s going to be very hard to do that. I think that leadership from the stronger countries is very important. There are a lot of possibilities, tools. For example, the central banks have many more tools than in 2008. There are regulatory tools that they have. They now hold huge bond portfolios. Those could be - those are all funded by money from banks.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is, there needs to be lots more prod production, and that`s most available to the strongest countries. The U.S. is the world`s biggest economy and can increase production more than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, if you were talking to Jerome Powell, the chair of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 , you would say focus less on interest rates, focus more on what?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 : He`s got multiple tools. One is regulatory policy. The Fed is an important regulator of banks. So let the banks lend more. But then, also, on the bond side, reducing the bond portfolio would return more money to banks. All of the money being used to hold the bond portfolio comes from banks. And if they had more, they could lend, and also the non-bank sector of the U.S. economy, that`s one of the most innovative, and it could put more money into the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, a State Department official said a few days ago, food crisis will be at least a three-year problem. What times horizon do you put on the food crisis and the energy crisis?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 : I`ll say one thing, it`s possible to produce enough to soften that crisis. But at the rate that we`re going right now, the fertilizer isn`t being made. You know, fertilizer comes -- a giant source of fertilizer is from natural gas through the ammonium channel, into the most useful fertilizer, and it also is used to make the electricity that converts the minerals into fertilizer. And that`s just not happening. So, a lot of the world is shutting down for lack of fertilizer. And then those shortages of crops will last for multiple years. We need to break that cycle and do it pretty forcefully now through announcements.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  chair said this week in this country that recession is certainly a possibility, in part because of higher interest rates. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  puts the odds of a recession at 50 percent. What`s your projection here for the world`s most important economy?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 : We put out a report three weeks ago that didn`t have the U.S. in recession, but we said in downside scenarios there could be. And so I don`t disagree with those estimates that you`re saying there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, do you agree with Fed officials when they say it`s going to take at last two years, a couple of years, before we get inflation back down to 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 : It`s going to take time to come down. But, again, that depends on what are - what`s your forecast for oil prices, for natural gas prices, for fertilizer. It`s going to take months and months, and maybe two years, to bring inflation back down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you very much for your time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MALPASS\uc2 : Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And a reminder, if you can`t watch the full FACE THE NATION, you can set your DVR or we`re available on demand.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020220626ei6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Wyoming Where A New Abortion Clinic Is Challenged By A Court Ruling And An Arsonist\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Major Garrett \par \uc2 792 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The Supreme Court`s decision means abortions will not be available in many conservative states which already passed laws banning the procedure. Wyoming is among them as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Major Garrett reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RIATA WALKER (Mother): It`s really easy to say I`ll never have an abortion until you find yourself in the impossible situation where you need one.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT (voiceover): In January of 2020, Riata Walker discovered that her 20-week-old fetus had severe heart defects and chromosomal complications, had she brought the fetus to term, doctors said it would not have survived.\par \par \uc2 RIATA WALKER: We had to terminate a pregnancy for medical reasons for a daughter we desperately wanted.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So Walker and her husband drove more than four hours from their home in Casper, Wyoming, to a hospital in Denver.\par \par \uc2 RIATA WALKER: You don`t know what choices you would make until you`re in that situation and it`s really easy on a hypothetical to say, well, I`d never choose that.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Wyoming`s so-called trigger law will soon outlaw most abortions in the state, a place where access to care was already sparse. This woman`s health clinic in Casper would have been the only one in Wyoming to offer surgical abortions.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I call on everyone, no matter how deeply they care about this decision, to keep all protests peaceful, no intimidation.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: But intimidation may have already come.\par \par \uc2 JULIE BURKHART (Wellspring Health Access clinic President): It`s all damaged.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The clinic, which was preparing to open this month was attacked by what police believe was an arsonist. Fire damage was extensive.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Casper Police Department.)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Security camera footage shows a middle of the night intruder carrying a gas can.\par \par \uc2 Julie Burkhart`s nonprofit is now rebuilding the clinic.\par \par \uc2 JULIE BURKHART: Do I want to be here talking about someone who committed an act of domestic terrorism in our building? Absolutely not. I wanted to be moving furniture in.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Hail Mary, full of grace the Lord is with thee.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Opposition to abortion rights in Wyoming is fervent. Demonstrators gathered to protest outside the clinic weekly.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PYATT (Anti-abortion Rights Activist): We`ve prayed that some way the Lord would prevent this place from opening. So, if it happens to be vandalism and a fire, I`m good with that.\par \par \uc2 RIATA WALKER: The hatred from especially at that side that I have witnessed is extreme and it is cruel, and we`re all human beings and we`re all trying to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: For Walker, that meant a deeply painful personal decision.\par \par \uc2 RIATA WALKER: You come out of this whole experience with a stigma attached to you that you had an abortion and you`re also a grieving mom.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Walker and her husband gave birth to a son, Philip, in January 2021 and hold tight to the memory of the daughter they lost.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett, CBS News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, a call to action. Volunteers who drive women to get abortions are expecting to get a lot busier.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: An estimated 40 million women of childbearing age live in states that have or could limit abortion access. Some will depend on volunteers who drive women at times across state lines to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What do you remember about the last woman you transported?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Yeah, she was a single mom. She wasn`t getting a lot of support from her former husband, and she said, "I just can`t manage it as much as I would like to." And it was just -- it was heartbreaking and it was encouraging at the same time that she had an avenue to terminate her pregnancy because she wanted to and felt she needed to.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The story behind this controversial effort and how they`re preparing for a spike in demand post Roe, Monday on "CBS MORNINGS."\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, honoring a champion: How his legacy was rescued after being nearly erased from history.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220628ei6p000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Wave Of Reaction From Around The World About The Abortion Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Ian Lee \par \uc2 385 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The Court`s ruling is reverberating far beyond the country`s borders. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ian Lee is in our London Bureau with the international reaction -- Ian.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Adriana, the Court`s ruling made the front pages despite the UK`s own current political turmoil underscoring the significance of the decision.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip of protesters)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE (voiceover): When the Supreme Court struck down Roe versus Wade, the whole world was watching and reacted.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of protesters in Paris)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: In Paris, several dozen protesters took to the streets in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  tweeted: "Abortion is a fundamental right for all women, it must be protected." Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  also took to social media saying, "The news coming out of the United States is horrific. No government, politician, or man should tell a woman what she can and cannot do with her body."\par \par \uc2 British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  also condemned the decision.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 : Clearly, it has massive impacts on people`s thinking around the world. It`s a very important decision. I`ve got to tell you, I think it`s a big step backwards.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of crowd celebrating)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: The overturning of Roe comes as many countries have eased restrictions on abortions in recent years, including overwhelmingly Catholic Ireland.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of crowd celebrating)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Mexico and Colombia.\par \par \uc2 But the Vatican praised yesterday`s decision saying: "By choosing life, our responsibility for the future of humanity is at stake."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: According to the Center for Reproductive Rights, the United States now joins just two other countries who have restricted abortion in the 21st Century, compare that to roughly 50 countries over the past decades who`ve made getting an abortion easier for women -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Ian Lee, thanks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220628ei6p0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Questions About A Future Fallout\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 450 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The abortion ruling immediately poses new questions about how far the Supreme Court could go on personal matters.\par \par \uc2 CBS News chief legal correspondent, Jan Crawford, joins us now from our Washington Bureau.\par \par \uc2 Jan, what`s the reality check on this decision affecting matters beyond abortion, especially with Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  writing that contraception, even same sex marriage, could now be called into question?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, I think that was pretty jarring, right, for a lot of people to read. But the point is, that`s long been his position and he just hasn`t gotten any takers for it. He is one vote, and you`ve got to have five Justices come on board in agreement to do anything or make any changes in the law, and the Court repeatedly emphasized in that decision that those cases, the right to contraceptives, the right to same sex marriage were different, that they were not cast into doubt by this decision because abortion, the Court said is different because it involves another life. Those cases are individual rights that would be at stake.\par \par \uc2 So they emphasize that`s not on the table, saying it`s hard to see how we could be much clearer about that. But you know, like I said, Thomas is one vote and it needs five, which he does not have.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And Jan, abortion rights` opponents are, of course, celebrating but for abortion rights advocates, what legal avenue do they have, if any, to restore abortion access nationwide?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, legally, it`s hard to see what they would have. Politically they have several options, right, they can try to get Congress to pass a law guaranteeing abortion access across the country or if they live in a state where abortion is now considered illegal, they are going to have to mobilize, vote, run for office, and try to get the State Legislatures to change those laws because the Court, the Supreme Court is out of the abortion rights question.\par \par \uc2 They`ve left it up to the State Legislatures. Congress might have some say, but it`s not going to be for now the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Jan Crawford, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220628ei6p0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Mississippi Where The State`s Last Abortion Clinic Is Seeing Its Final Patients\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 859 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Today, demonstrators converged in cities across the country demanding to be heard. Thirteen states have laws on the books that ban abortion almost immediately, the others are poised to ban or greatly restrict it.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Jackson, Mississippi tonight, outside the very clinic at the center of the Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good evening, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 Well, this clinic actually expanded hours today to try to accommodate as many women as possible before the Attorney General says they have to close their doors and here has also been center stage for demonstrations just like the ones we`ve seen across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip of crowds protesting)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Large crowds returned to the streets of Los Angeles today. Across the country, the Court`s ruling fueled tensions.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from M SULLIVAN)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In Cedar Rapids, Iowa, a pickup truck drove through abortion rights of borders.\par \par \uc2 And in Washington, DC, protests and celebration.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of crowd celebrating)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Here in Mississippi today, protesters gathered outside the states only abortion clinic. It`s at the very center of the Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We`ve always been a pro-life state, but now we get to actually live it out.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The clinic known as the Pink House could shut down in just weeks.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We`re not going down. We`re not giving up.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Would you love your little baby?\par \par \uc2 DERENDA HANCOCK (Pink House Defenders): That`s what they`re doing right now is showing us they can block this road, because they know those are patients who need access to get right in that driveway.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Derenda Hancock has led the Pink House Defenders for a decade. The volunteer group escorts patients safely into the clinic.\par \par \uc2 DERENDA HANCOCK: We have a 19-year-old who drove here by themselves from the Delta, it is the poorest part of Mississippi, two to two and a half hours, maybe in a car that she hopes makes it here, and she`s already jumped through a hundred hurdles to get here.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Mississippi is one of 26 states where abortion will soon be illegal.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TATE REEVES (R- Mississippi): Mississippi is a God-fearing place and we fight this battle.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Thirteen of those states currently have laws that would ban abortion immediately.\par \par \uc2 DERENDA HANCOCK: I guess, it`s just the end. All we`ve been through here will always be a part of our lives.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So Caitlin, where will women in Mississippi who want to get an abortion go once that clinic closes?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Well, Adrianna, most of the surrounding states also have laws on the books that would ban abortion, so women seeking care will actually have to drive many hours or even fly to faraway places like where you are in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, Caitlin Huey-Burns in Jackson, Mississippi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, at the White House, the President signed into law the first major gun control legislation passed by Congress in nearly 30 years.\par \par \uc2 The bipartisan compromise seemed unimaginable until a recent series of mass shootings, including in Uvalde, Texas and Buffalo, New York.\par \par \uc2 The President also criticized the Supreme Court`s recent rulings on guns and abortion, calling them "terrible decisions."\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Christina Ruffini is on Capitol Hill tonight with more -- Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good evening, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 As you can probably hear behind me, there have been a couple of hundred demonstrators here at the Court for most of the day. Some came to protest the decision, but others came to celebrate the landmark overturn of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Now, the focus will shift to Congress where Senate Democrats are trying to work with a handful of pro-abortion rights Republicans to codify the rights from Roe into Federal Law. The issue is they won`t have the 10 votes they need to overcome a filibuster, and the same holds true for other things Congress could try to do, like protect a woman`s right to travel out of state to get the procedure or to get abortion pills through the mail.\par \par \uc2 And here in the district where abortion is available and legal, there is a special concern that if Republicans take back both chambers after November midterms, they could outlaw abortion here in the district, something they have the power to do without the permission or consent of the 700,000 residents of this city -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Christina Ruffini, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220628ei6p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Why The Head Of Planned Parenthood Calls Overturns Roe V. Wade A Health Care Crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 978 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Earlier, we spoke with \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 . She`s the president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , the leading provider of abortions in America. The organization also provides preventive care, like birth control, testing, and treatment for STDs and cancer screening.\par \par \uc2 We began by asking her which women will be most impacted by today`s decision.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2  (President and CEO, \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 ): It affects people with low incomes, black, brown, indigenous, people of color, folks who already are living at the margins. Those are going to be the people who are most harmed.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How many clinics across America from \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  will be closed?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 : What I can tell you right now is happening is that affiliates are -- our health centers are fighting to get every single patient in that they can see right now before the state issues an injunction.\par \par \uc2 What I can tell you is just, the devastation that every provider, every front-line staff member, the call center staff, the calls that they are getting in from the patients, the tears that they are -- that they are hearing is absolutely overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How many women do you think will be denied abortion services and will have to perhaps try and travel to another state?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 : Hundreds of thousands of women will be living in states that will be seeking access to abortion. They won`t be able to get the care in the state that they need.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Justice Alito and the other conservatives wrote today in their majority opinion that this will not affect contraceptive rights.\par \par \uc2 In the opinion, he said, abortion rights are -- quote -- "inherently different" from rights regarding -- quote -- "contraception and same-sex relationships."\par \par \uc2 Is that how you see it?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 : No, not at all.\par \par \uc2 We see state after state introducing incredibly extreme and harmful legislation related to criminalizing IVF, criminalizing IUDs, criminalizing emergency contraception.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wait. So, you`re saying you already see some state legislatures...\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 : They`re introducing these kinds of laws, yes.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: To make it criminal to travel to another state to get an abortion?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 : Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What would be the effect of that?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 : We`re seeing people spying on each other, neighbors spying on each other, all for -- to deny people the right to make decisions about their own bodies, and so the increased criminalization that can happen.\par \par \uc2 And, again, I`m not sure how they will enforce these kinds of laws. But these are the kinds of things that are being contemplated. And we have to be very alarmed about those.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There has also been international reaction, with countries around the world condemning today`s decision.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  called it horrific, while French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  said abortion is a fundamental right for all women. British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  called it a big step backwards. The \uc2 United Nations\uc2  said it was a huge blow to women`s human rights and gender equality.\par \par \uc2 The Vatican, however, praised the decision, saying it is a powerful invitation to reflect and it challenges the whole world.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on tonight`s CBS Evening News, we will tell you about an historic vote right here on Capitol Hill on the first major gun control legislation in decades.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: History was also made today when Congress sent to President Biden`s desk the first major new gun control legislation in nearly 30 years.\par \par \uc2 Democrats banded together with 14 Republicans in the House and 15 in the Senate to pass a bill that, among other things, would enhance gun background checks for people under 21 and help states to implement red flag laws to keep guns out of the hands of people deemed dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next: one year after that deadly condominium collapse, a major settlement for the victims.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There was a somber gathering in Surfside, Florida, today, marking one year since that 12-story condominium collapsed, killing 98 people.\par \par \uc2 The victims ranged in age from 1 to 92 years old. First lady Jill Biden attended the memorial, thanking first responders who spent weeks searching for survivors and remains. Yesterday, a judge approved a settlement of just over $1 billion for the victims.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We end tonight here in the nation`s capital, with the Supreme Court behind me, after covering the January 6 hearings, the new gun reform bill, and now this landmark decision, marking what has been a consequential week in American history.\par \par \uc2 If you ever needed a reminder that your vote matters, today is the day. For decades, conservatives worked to overturn Roe vs. Wade. Today, they were victorious, in part by electing President Trump, who voted to appoint justices who would end abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden said Congress must act, that voters can have the final word. Those voices will be heard in the coming months before the next elections, a reminder, no matter what side you are on, your vote matters.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s CBS Evening News. I`m Norah O`Donnell at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220628ei6o0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Pending Border Battle Between States On Opposite Sides Of Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jamie Yuccas \par \uc2 591 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, we want to go now to the front lines of a pending border battle between states on opposite sides of abortion. Washington state has recently expanded abortion rights, while neighboring Idaho is ready to impose one of the nation`s strictest bans in the country.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jamie Yuccas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Abortion is health care!\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS (voice-over): This is the scene across the West in states that share a common border but have opposite views on abortion rights. Today`s decision is expected to embolden those who already protest the clinic in Spokane, Washington.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: We stand for the unborn, for the preborn!\par \par \uc2 KARL EASTLUND (Planned Parenthood of Greater Washington and North Idaho): They`re here nearly every day. They`re constantly trying to intimidate and badger our employees. We have had protesters follow staff home.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Washington state now has 40 abortion clinics, Idaho just three. In a statement, Idaho`s governor praised the Supreme Court for defending "preborn babies, who deserve protection."\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JAY INSLEE (D-Washington): We are going to fight like hell to keep Washington a pro-choice state.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Washington state is expecting a nearly 400 percent increase in abortion patients. Already, nearly 60 percent of those coming for care on the border are from Idaho. Others have driven more than 20 hours from Texas.\par \par \uc2 Mother of two Elisabeth Keifer Kraus is thankful she did not have to travel to another state. Life-threatening conditions caused her to terminate two early pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 ELISABETH KEIFER KRAUS (Abortion Rights Supporter): I did not have the time. I would have died bleeding out in a car on the side of the road trying to get somewhere that would help me. And that is the future we are about to face.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But, nationwide, assaults against abortion providers are up 128 percent.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE WEISS (Planned Parenthood Provider): Well, my husband put it this way: How does it feel to work somewhere where 70 percent of the population thinks that you`re the devil?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: This clinic was vandalized last July. Now employees are preparing for the worst.\par \par \uc2 Do you put them through training?\par \par \uc2 KARL EASTLUND: Absolutely. We have our own local security director. We work with a national security team. We have contract security on site. And we absolutely absolute do drills.\par \par \uc2 SARAH DIXIT (Planned Parenthood of Greater Washington and North Idaho): It very much feels like there`s a doomsday clock ticking somewhere, and we just don`t know when it`s going to go off.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: This is a young crowd here to support not only women`s rights, but also protections, they tell me, for same-sex couples.\par \par \uc2 Those I talk to in the crowd say they are concerned about their friends and family across the country, and that this is motivating them to vote in the midterm election -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Yuccas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220628ei6o0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Rights Become A Central Issue In The Upcoming November Midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Omar Villafranca \par \uc2 575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, the national abortion landscape is rapidly changing, with at least 13 states set to ban the procedure through so- called trigger laws within 30 days or sooner. And, today, we learned some clinics are already canceling patient appointments.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voice-over): For the second time in almost 50 years, Roe v. Wade, the landmark abortion case originally filed in Texas, has sent shockwaves across the country.\par \par \uc2 Today in Dallas, windows were boarded up around the federal building in anticipation of unrest, while Texas abortion providers prepared for a new reality.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMNA DERMISH (Planned Parenthood Greater Texas): I`m heartbroken as I sit here today thinking about all the people who need access to essential health care, to abortion, who will be denied that care because of this opinion.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Today, Arkansas` attorney general was elated.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE RUTLEDGE (Arkansas Attorney General): It`s almost as if we had a crystal ball, and I`m so glad that we had that wisdom and guidance to have this ready.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Effective immediately, abortion is now illegal in at least seven states, including Oklahoma, where medical staff performing abortions could face prison time.\par \par \uc2 And at least eight of the trigger law states do not have exceptions for rape or incest. In Louisiana, the last abortion clinic shut their doors today. Meanwhile, in Wisconsin, an age-old law banning abortions never went away.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: I`m Lana Zak outside of the capitol in Madison, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood here had proactively decided that they were going to stop scheduling appointments as of tomorrow. But with today`s Supreme Court decision, it means that all the women with scheduled procedures have now been turned away, based off of an 1849 law that the doctors I spoke with called overly broad and antiquated.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATHY KING (Planned Parenthood of Wisconsin): I am scared, because of the scared because of the way our criminal ban in Wisconsin is written, that there is no exception to physical health.\par \par \uc2 I am scared that some people will be allowed to get to the point where it does risk their life, and that maybe people will lose their lives because of this ban.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Back here in Texas, the trigger law will now ban nearly all abortions from the moment of fertilization, except when it`s needed to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: To give you an idea of what this ruling means, if a woman here in Dallas, Texas, wanted to get an abortion, to get to the nearest place, she would have to travel more than 350 miles, two states away, to Wichita, Kansas -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That puts it in perspective.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 At Least 13 States Set To Ban The Procedure Through So-Called Trigger Laws Within 30 Days Or Sooner.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220628ei6o0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Nation`s Highest Court Overturns Roe V. Wade, Taking Away The Constitutional Right To An Abortion After Nearly 50 Years\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 1,149 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we also heard from presidents, past and present. President \uc2 Obama\uc2  called the ruling devastating. Michelle Obama called it horrifying. And President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said this gives the rights back to the states, as it should have been long ago.\par \par \uc2 We have a team of correspondents across the country tonight covering every angle of this story.\par \par \uc2 We will start tonight with our chief legal correspondent, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 Jan, this has been quite a remarkable day.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, Norah, I mean, Roe v. Wade is one of the few decisions most Americans know by name. And, today, the Supreme Court said it`s history.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voice-over): When the crowd heard the court had overturned Roe v. Wade, there were cheers.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #1: It took every single one of us, people across the political and ideological spectrums, to make this a reality!\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A reality that, for abortion rights supporters, was hard to believe.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #2: This is a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #3: I`m 21, and I`m terrified!\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In the 5-4 vote, the justices overturned the landmark 1973 ruling that guaranteed a woman`s right to abortion, sending the issue back to the states to set their own policies.\par \par \uc2 "Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. Its reasoning was exceptionally weak, and the decision has had damaging consequences," wrote \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  in a decision joined by four other conservatives. "It is time to heed the Constitution and return the issue of abortion to the people`s elected representatives."\par \par \uc2 Twenty-six states had asked the court to overturn Roe; 13 already have laws on the books that would ban abortion almost immediately. The others are poised to ban or greatly restrict it.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Today, the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  expressly took away a constitutional right from the American people.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: President Biden blasted the decision.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : It`s a sad day for the court and for the country.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We won`t go back!\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: On Capitol Hill, Democratic lawmakers marched to the court, while Republicans said the ruling was overdue.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAYRA FLORES (R-Texas): Hallelujah. I woke up this morning praying for this. And I never thought that it would happen.\par \par \uc2 WALTER CRONKITE (Former CBS News Anchor): Good evening. In a landmark ruling, the Supreme Court today legalized abortions.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It took almost 50 years of annual Marches For Life, political muscle, and justices with a strong conservative legal ideology.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  voted to uphold the 15-week Mississippi abortion ban but refused to join the majority decision to overturn Roe or a subsequent 1992 opinion. With the court`s newest justices, all nominated by former President Trump, there were five votes without him.\par \par \uc2 Republican \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Democrat Joe Manchin, who voted for \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , said they were duped, with Collins releasing a statement that: "They were both insistent on the importance of supporting longstanding precedents."\par \par \uc2 But also, a factor, the 2020 death of Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , a champion of reproductive rights, gave Trump his third nominee, who provided the key fifth vote.\par \par \uc2 The three remaining liberals joined in a fiery dissent: "Whatever the exact scope of the coming laws, one result of today`s decision is certain, the curtailment of women`s rights and of their status as free and equal citizens."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Jan is with me here with more.\par \par \uc2 So, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote a separate opinion as well. It has some wondering, is same-sex marriage, is contraception next?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, that`s right, because those are grounded in some of the same rights. But the court in the majority opinion said, no. How could we be any more clear? This case is different. Abortion is different because it involves a human life.\par \par \uc2 Those cases, the right to contraceptive, the right to same-sex marriage, could -- are not being cast in doubt or threatened by this ruling, the court said.\par \par \uc2 But Justice Thomas wrote that separate opinion saying he thought they could be on the table. But that`s one justice. It takes five.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But what if the states move on same-sex marriage or to block contraception?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, then that would be challenged. It would come right back to the Supreme Court, and you would probably have eight justices then -- Thomas might be here -- saying, get out of here.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Jan Crawford, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The real-life and political implications of today`s ruling cannot be overstated. President Biden called it a sad day for the court and for the country. He promised to keep fighting for reproductive rights, but made it clear it is up to Congress and the voters.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 And, Nancy, interesting to hear from the president, but what`s he planning to do?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Well, he`s taken a couple of steps already, Norah, and we`re told there could be more to come.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what he has done already. First, he has directed the Department of Justice to protect women from being punished by their home states if they seek an abortion out of state. Second, he has directed the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to expand access to \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved medications that are used to terminate an early pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some states are already trying to ban those pills, and there`s sure to be legal fights over that, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But, Nancy, the president seemed to make clear too, even to his supporters, that he can`t do much on this on his own, right?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he argued today that the only way at this point to restore a woman`s right to choose is for Congress to pass a federal law codifying that right. And he said they`re only going to have the numbers to do that if voters elect more pro-abortion rights candidates this fall, setting up a key election message that we are already hearing, Norah, from Democrats up and down the ballot tonight.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220628ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Missile Systems To Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, Ramy Inocencio, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In other news this morning, the United States is ready to send more military aid to Ukraine. Senior administration officials tell CBS News the White House plans to announce this week it is providing an advanced surface-to-air missile defense system.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday for the first time in three weeks, Ukraine`s capital was struck by Russian missiles.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: A rare barrage of Russian missiles flew over Kyiv on Sunday, and upon impact, plumes of smoke rose from a kindergarten and residential building. Six people were injured including this seven-year-old girl and her Russian mother. Her father did not survive.\par \par \uc2 That missile hit the roof of their apartment complex, but it`s actually not the first time that it`s been hit. The last time was about two months ago.\par \par \uc2 Now one possibility is that Russian forces are targeting a Soviet era arms factory that is literally this one across the street.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  said some missiles had been shot down, but called for more modern defenses from the international community that has started to arrive. These destroyed military trucks are believed to be the first Russian targets hit by US supplied guided rocket systems known as HIMARs that arrived in Ukraine just days ago.\par \par \uc2 Too late to save the eastern city of Severodonetsk, that city fell on Saturday as Ukrainian troops were ordered to pull out.\par \par \uc2 Russia is cementing its control over the whole Donbas region, a territorial and morale hit to Ukraine in this war now starting its fifth month.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings, Ramy Inocencio, Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back here in the US in what might have been our top story on another morning, the most extensive gun control legislation in nearly three decades is now Federal law.\par \par \uc2 President Biden signed the bipartisan Safer Communities Act on Saturday calling it consequential -- a consequential bill that in his words would save lives.\par \par \uc2 The $13 billion legislation enhances background checks for gun buyers under 21, makes it harder for domestic abusers to purchase a firearm and provides more money for mental health services including $750 million for states to establish Crisis Intervention Programs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re so right, Tony, on another day, that would have been a top story because that`s a very big deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Biggest bill on guns in three decades.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, we`ve got reaction to the abortion ruling from women on both sides of this debate. Their responses are important for all of us to hear today. But first it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, Adriana Diaz rides along with volunteers in a state literally caught in the middle of the new reality after Roe -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 Oops, I think we may have a frozen shot there.\par \par \uc2 She is at Planned Parenthood in Illinois where abortion providers are getting ready for an influx of patients. Tens of thousands of women will likely travel to Illinois for services they cannot get in their home states. Ahead, she will ride along with volunteers who drive women who cannot afford transportation to abortion clinics.\par \par \uc2 Her story coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, some amazing guests you do not want to miss, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " number one bestselling author Jenny Han talks with us about a new Prime video series. It`s called "The Summer I Turned Pretty" and it`s based on her wildly popular books.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the esteemed and Honorable Judge John Hodgman, not really a Judge, will be here to weigh in on some of the least important, but most debatable hot topics on our minds. For example, is a hot dog a sandwich?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is chili a soup or a stew?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Stew.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stew.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will find out. And the debate begins.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The gallery has spoken.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, here is a reminder, you can get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on the go podcast, hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6g0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Reacting To The Roe Decision\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Jan Crawford, Gayle King \par \uc2 1,858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS at 7:30. Pretty picture there of the Supreme Court because we`re focusing on the fallout from the Court`s decision ruling that abortion is no longer a constitutional right in this country.\par \par \uc2 More than half of women questioned in the latest CBS News poll said that overturning Roe v. Wade, this decision we`re talking about will make women`s lives worse.\par \par \uc2 But not all women feel that way. This morning, we`re hearing from a diverse group of five: Kate Finberg, Savannah Ackerman, K. Bryant, Abigail Galvan, and Amanda Holliday-Bembridge. They all have deep feelings on abortion and different feelings, some support abortion rights, others oppose them and the reactions are very fresh.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford interviewed them shortly after the Supreme Court announced its decision.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT (Pro-choice): I`m pro-choice,\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN (Pro-life): I am pro-life.\par \par \uc2 KATE FINBERG (Pro-choice): Pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE (Pro-choice). I`m pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN (Pro-life): I am against all forms of violence and I consider abortion a violence.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Court just overturn Roe versus Wade a couple of hours ago. How are you feeling right now in one word?\par \par \uc2 KATE FINBERG: Concerned.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Concerned.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN: Just surreal.\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: For me, it is just more of the same.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I guess you would say, not surprised.\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: Yes. Not surprised.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: I`m feeling hopeful.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: I would say frustrated, empathetic, saddened. Any of those.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What does that mean personally? I mean, the fact that the Supreme Court has overturned Roe versus Wade?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: I`m frustrated for the women that won`t be allowed to have the choice that they are being told what to do by someone that doesn`t know them or their circumstances.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: You can go to different parts of the country and different people want different things. So, I actually think that the best way to be able to minister to women and give them what they need is by having it be at the state level. They have more control and are able to exercise more of their choice.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What about you?\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: I don`t agree that the states are going to do a better job because obviously, many of the states have not. They are completely and outright banning abortion.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN: For me personally, there has been this huge focus on legality, right? It`s never how are we helping the women on the ground. What is the reason that people are actually getting abortions?\par \par \uc2 KATE FINBERG: I`m more upset with the Supreme Court because I feel like their decision that they`ve made is actually not reflective of what Americans largely polled, have indicated that they believe.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Do any of you, you know, have girlfriends or friends or anyone that you said, like, here`s where we can find common ground, is it just either or?\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: It`s actually -- I changed my stance on the position. When I was growing up, so I came from a very low income background, I was only told this narrative that abortion is the way to go and anyone who is against abortion doesn`t like women, they don`t support women. And that, quite frankly, isn`t true.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You reject the pro-life label.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN: Because it has come to me in so many things. And I think, again, like the super mainstream movement is super hypocritical. I think there is no other way to say it.\par \par \uc2 My viewpoints don`t come from religion, don`t come from anything else, it comes from this fact that each human being is distinct and important and should be respected and valued.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m against abortion in the same way I`m against police brutality, and torturous acts within prisons and the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It seems like one thing you agree with, whether you`re on the left or the right, or somewhere in between, is that a lot more should be done for women and children? You`re saying that the anti-abortion movement could be doing more?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH ACKERMAN: Yes, and they should be and they should have been the whole time and I am so annoyed every time I hear the "Now the real work begins" because it`s no, you should have been doing it.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What about the abortion rights movement?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: The bigger problem is that we can have all these conversations, but when you have White Christian male viewpoints, making the decisions at the end of the day are the ones that are the loudest on TV and social media and that is the rhetoric that gets passed and we miss the point of listening to all the other issues or figuring out all the things that surround this decision to support maternal health or prenatal/postnatal care.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You`re anti-abortion. Do you think that the movement should focus its efforts now on changing the laws in states like Maryland or the District of Columbia or California to make it more restrictive?\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: Let`s look at all these underlying issues that are the root causes of why people are having to make these decisions. The danger in pushing for abortion is it`s just like you, like you don`t think you have another option like, you`re just told this is what you do.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: Isn`t that the opposite then? Like if when you saying if you feel like the only option is abortion, but if you take that option away, the only option is life, you`re not giving a choice.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: So it will be decided on the state level in that sense, but I would say that --\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: That on an individual level.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA HOLLIDAY-BEMBRIDGE: It takes that away from the mom, or the pregnant person?\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: Well, I would say actually that if we`re not focusing on the legal issue of abortion, then perhaps we would be focusing our attention on all of these other supports.\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: I don`t think any one of us in here is satisfied with how politicians don`t do what we ask them to do. They are not listening to the problems.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: So does the idea that it is now going to be politicians that get to decide this issue of abortion scare you?\par \par \uc2 K. BRYANT: Honestly, it doesn`t scare me. I try to get involved in things that reflect the way that I would like the world to be and I don`t think that we consider enough how what we do on our daily lives can affect even the five of us here talking about abortion today.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Is that a positive thing? It is a positive talking about this?\par \par \uc2 K BRYANT: Because I was a little concerned about coming here and what I found is that there is a lot of commonality in what we have to say and I hope that the viewers actually listen to it and think about that and stop thinking about, "Okay, do you have access? Yes or no." But what about the other things? Those other things are so much more important.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GALVAN: I`m very impressed by each of these women that I`m joined here today with. I don`t like that, like we`ve been pitted against each other when I feel like we would be more powerful working together.\par \par \uc2 KATE FINBERG: I understand that it`s nuanced. I understand it is complex. I feel like the pro-life movement is not doing enough and I feel like the pro-choice movement is not doing enough to support women and I feel like that`s what`s really important and that`s not getting heard.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think any of us think -- feel that abortions are everyone`s number one choice, right? I think we would like to create a world but that`s not what women want and women need.\par \par \uc2 And so looking at it that way, I think there`s common ground that we can come together on to try and create more of that kind of a world.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, the women that we talked to are from Maryland and the Washington, DC area, and we had this interview planned for Friday morning, before we knew that the Court was going to drop its decision overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 So when some of the women arrived at the hotel where we spoke with them, they had no idea that Roe was history.\par \par \uc2 Back to you guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a very intelligent and coherent conversation. So we do know it can be done. But, Jan, I like the point you raised, is it either/or. It`s so deeply political, so deeply polarizing. Where do we go from here?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Right, and is consensus even possible?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is the question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is the question.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is it possible?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what are your thoughts, Jan? You`ve been covering this from the very beginning and you`ve seen it up close and personally. You`ve talked to many people on both sides of the issue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Almost 30 years of work, Jan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it is an amazing body of work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we`ve come to this moment.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, I think that in many ways, we saw this on Friday. We had an idea that this was going to happen, right? And yet everyone was shocked on both sides like this really did happen.\par \par \uc2 But this is part of an orchestrated campaign that we`re going to be talking about in the eight o`clock hour to overturn Roe versus Wade, going back to 1974, the very next year when Roe was creating -- establishing a constitutional right to an abortion. So the people in the anti-abortion movement never gave up when they suffered losses, they regrouped and moved on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Jan Crawford, thank you very much and appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, states that allow abortion could see a flood of patients from states that don`t.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz rides with volunteers, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6g0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Ruling Aftermath\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Jan Crawford, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 1,273 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 There is a new reality in America following the Supreme Court`s decision on Friday to overturn Roe v. Wade. That ruling has sent shockwaves through all aspects of American life, as all of us come to terms with exactly what this means for women, for medical care, and for families.\par \par \uc2 Here`s just some of the reaction in today`s "Eye Opener."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Supreme Court has ruled on the future of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering.)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD singing "Jesus Loves the Little Children.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are here to bless you and your baby.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering.)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What about after you had a baby, are you going to help me take care of the baby? Are you going to get me Social Services?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This ruling was illegitimate. It must not be accepted.\par \par \uc2 MAN: For people in the states, the fight is now in your door.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My body.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: My choice.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They are chanting, "My body, my choice." It`s not. The science says that`s not -- that`s a baby.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We won`t give up this fight.\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN chanting.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Of course, women should have rights, obviously. But also the unborn.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whose choice?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Our choice.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Leaving it in the states is better all the way around for everyone.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Most of the rallies were peaceful though police Tased one protester and arrested a handful of others in Greenville, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And we saw demonstrators outside the Capitol, some of them even trying to get into the building.\par \par \uc2 Teargas was dispersed and police showed up at the Capitol in riot gear.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I have lived with the fundamental rights in abortion through my whole life and this is just wrong.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Worried about what could come next?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes. I`m kind of concerned about how like guns have more rights than we do.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: More Black women dying, more poverty for African-Americans. I`m fearful for what`s next.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "We will vote.")\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are out here, and we`re not going to stop and we`re not going away now and we`re not going away for as long as it takes to restore our freedoms and completely free --\par \par \uc2 MAN: There are people in our church who probably didn`t agree with the Court`s decision was, but you know, we`re going to love them and pray for them, too.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Whose streets?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Our streets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 You can see and hear the passion on both sides. For many people, it was a very rough weekend and for others. They were literally cheering in the streets.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Shock. Celebration.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody feels very strongly about this issue.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Something fundamental has shifted in this country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re absolutely right, and here is where America stands this morning on abortion rights following that monumental Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 Women in at least eight states can no longer get a legal abortion. Many more states are expected to outlaw or severely restrict the procedure in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 Now, that would establish an abortion free zone stretching for thousands of miles in the middle of the country.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Meanwhile, the latest CBS News poll shows that a solid majority of Americans, 59 percent do not like this High Court`s decision. They disapprove of it. That includes nearly seven in 10 women.\par \par \uc2 Also a majority, 52 percent in our poll say that reversing Roe v. Wade is a step back for America at large.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is outside the Supreme Court for us.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning to you. We mentioned the shock, the outrage, confusion. What happens now?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning, Tony. You know in overturning Roe versus Wade and letting states decide how they`re going to handle it. We now have a patchwork of laws. So the right to an abortion depends on the state that you live in.\par \par \uc2 Abortion has been one of the most divisive issues of our time, and we saw those divisions on full display across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "My body, my choice.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Protests erupted across the country after the Supreme Court`s historic ruling to end the constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Hell no, we don`t need Roe.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: With the ruling, at least eight states have placed near total bans on abortion and within months, it could be illegal or severely restricted in almost half the states in the country.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): A victory for the rule of law and above all, a victory for life.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In Illinois Saturday, former President Trump celebrated the news and the three conservative Justices he nominated to the court who voted to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Those prayers have been answered to the generations of Americans in the pro-life movement, as well as countless constitutional conservative.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Democrats warned of the impact in states with already high maternal mortality rates and children in poverty.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): This is not just a crisis of Roe, this is a crisis of our democracy. Forcing women to carry pregnancies against their will, will kill them.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Republican governors like South Dakota`s \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  say they are prepared to help women with more support and new programs, while also working to stop women from getting abortion pills through the mail.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R-SD): In South Dakota, we`ve already had a bill passed that`s set on telemedicine abortions, that we don`t believe it should be available because it is a dangerous situation for those individuals without being medically supervised by a physician.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Democratic Governors where abortion will remain legal said their states will welcome all women for abortions, while others said they would work to make sure it is legal.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): So this is a fight like hell moment. We are taking -- using every tool we have to fight for reproductive rights for Michigan women.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Meanwhile, confusion and concern about how the ruling might impact other rights in light of Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` separate opinion, which no other Justice joined, calling on the Court to reconsider cases on contraception and gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Americans say they think it`s likely that the Supreme Court will limit same sex marriage and access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Gay marriage, contraceptives -- all these things until they strip us of all our rights.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Even though the Court`s majority said it is hard to see how we could be clearer that those cases are not threatened because abortion is different, because they wrote, it involves another life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now over the weekend, the protests remained largely peaceful and this isn`t the end. The Court in coming days is going to rule on other divisive issues, including religion in schools and climate change -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is not over yet.\par \par \uc2 Jan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6g00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Mississippi`s New Abortion Reality\b0\par\par\uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,768 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mississippi led the challenge that finally overturn Roe v. Wade. That state`s law limiting abortion was declared unconstitutional by Lower Courts until the Supreme Court ruled on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Jackson, Mississippi outside the state`s only abortion clinic where there is concern that women will lose access to a wide range of essential services.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Well, this clinic behind me is known here as the Pink House, but it is also known as being the center of that landmark ruling, and over the weekend, they actually expanded hours to try and see as many patients as they could before the Attorney General here makes them close their doors.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Dorinda Hancock and her volunteer group, the Pink Defenders spent the weekend as they have for years, escorting women past protesters and into Mississippi`s only abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 DORINDA HANCOCK (The Pink Defenders volunteer): They can block this road, because they know those are patients who need access to get right in that driveway.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: She says, the defenders will be there until the clinic closes its doors for good.\par \par \uc2 DORINDA HANCOCK: Always been through here. It will always be a part of our lives. The patients that we`ve seen, and that we`ve been able to give a little comfort to --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But elsewhere, the decision is seen as an answered prayer. It was at the center of Sunday service at Broadmoor Baptist Church where the Pastor encouraged adoption.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Roe versus Wade was overturned. And I pray, yes, we thank God for that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Is the state prepared for the consequence?\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL (Sisters in Birth founder): No, no. It`s not prepared.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Getty Israel founded Sisters in Birth, a nonprofit community health organization serving pregnant women in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL: Mississippi is one of the top four states with the highest - - highest rate of young women who are uninsured, the same young woman who don`t have access to reproductive care, namely contraception, and now she is pregnant and she`s going to present with risk factors like obesity, pre- hypertension, pre-diabetes. She is more likely to raise that baby in poverty.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In the Mississippi Delta, one of the poorest places in the nation, some counties are without a single obstetrician or pediatrician. Already, expectant mothers either don`t have access to medical professionals or must drive hours.\par \par \uc2 WAIKINYA CLANTON (Jackson, Mississippi Southern Poverty Law Center Director): The thing that`s going to keep me up at night is the women who will lose their lives because they don`t have access to care.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Waikinya Clanton, the Director of the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  in Jackson sees this as a call to action.\par \par \uc2 WAIKINYA CLANTON: We will not back down. The people here, particularly the women here in Mississippi are primed for this moment.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But in a state with the highest rate of infant mortality, of low birth weight, and of young child poverty, Israel says the Federal and local governments need to step up.\par \par \uc2 And what does it mean for these children?\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL: Well, I see it as a ditch. Put the mother in the ditch. Put the child in the ditch with her. Now, add another one and cover it with dirt. That`s what we`re dealing with.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, Tony, most of the states around Mississippi are actually also banning abortions, so women seeking the procedure will have to either fly long distances or drive long distances. And also at question here is whether there will be additional restrictions on ordering abortion pills by mail -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Caitlin Huey-Burns outside the clinic where it all started.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is under pressure from his own party this morning to respond and respond strongly to the Supreme Court`s action, but he is overseas at the moment for the latest G7 Summit in Germany and that is where we find Ed O`Keefe traveling with the President. He joins us now from Austria.\par \par \uc2 Ed, it is early, but everyone is wondering what does this mean politically?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, Tony, good morning from the G7 Summit where the President is focused on his international agenda even though he and his party are facing fallout from the Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 There is at least some encouraging short term political gain potentially for Democrats in all of this. Look at our new CBS News poll that finds 50 percent of registered Democrats say the Court`s decision makes them more likely to vote this fall, the kind of boost of enthusiasm the party is going to need up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 The poll also finds majority support for Congress passing a Federal law to legalize abortion rights nationwide. That`s now quickly become a key Democratic Party campaign pledge.\par \par \uc2 The President is facing pressure from fellow Democrats to more forcefully respond to the ruling. Progressives like Senator Elizabeth Warren once again over the weekend calling to add more Justices to the Supreme Court and to get rid of the Senate filibuster to make it easier to pass Democratic priorities.\par \par \uc2 But the White House says the President doesn`t support expanding the size of the High Court.\par \par \uc2 Here in Europe. President Biden says none of the world leaders he has been meeting with have mentioned the abortion ruling to him, even though many of them criticized it in the hours after the Court weighed in last week.\par \par \uc2 Here at the Summit today, the focus is on unity around Ukraine. The leaders heard virtually earlier today from Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much, Ed O`Keefe, reporting from Austria.\par \par \uc2 The High Court`s ruling on Roe upends nearly 50 years of legal precedent. Now, that raises a lot of legal questions. Constitutional scholar, Jeffrey Rosen joins us now to help us sort this out. He is President and CEO of the nonpartisan, \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Jeffrey. It`s really good to have you here on this morning in particular.\par \par \uc2 You know, the polling shows that the majority of Americans did not want Roe v. Wade overturned. So, why does it -- why is the Supreme Court seemingly so out of step with the American public?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN (\uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2  President and CEO): Well, the majority opinion said that it`s their job to ignore public opinion and to follow the Constitution wherever it leads. The fact that they didn`t change their opinion after it leaked, should suggest that they`re determined to hold the line there, and they may be out of step with America in a way that would be historically unusual in American politics.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How far now can states go to enforce these abortion bans?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN: Well, this is the central question and the legal issues are going to be so complicated. First, can a woman travel out of state to get an abortion? Justice Kavanaugh said "yes." He said that the right to travel protects your right to go to a state that is pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 But a bunch of the anti-choice states are going to be trying to prosecute doctors out of state who offer abortions or those who aid and abet them. There are only two precedents on those. Again, they`ll be tested in Court.\par \par \uc2 The pro-choice states will refuse to extradite women who cross state lines. So the bottom line is, it is going to be tied up in the Courts for a very long time and very, very complicated state by state.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but you see, a lot of companies are already stepping up saying we will reimburse women who have to travel out of state. What impact do you think this will have?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN: Well, it`ll have huge effects on where people decide to work and a lot is going to turn on the availability of the abortion pill, which you were talking about earlier.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department last week said that states may not ban the abortion pill based on disagreement with its health or safety and generally, Federal regulations preempt state regulations, but a bunch of states are already moving to ban the abortion pill. Those will be tested in Court. And ultimately, it`s not clear what the Supreme Court will say about this preemption issue, so there once again, we`re not going to know the final answer for a long time to come.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jeffrey, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote in his opinion that the Court should reconsider same sex marriage and access to birth control. Now, the majority opinion also took pains to say we`re not going to do that, this is different, but give us a reality check here.\par \par \uc2 Does what \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote portend other rights being threatened in this ruling?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN: Well, as Jan Crawford said earlier, the majority said abortion is different than gay marriage and contraception because it involves a fetus, and there is another difference between contraception and abortion, namely that there was a tradition of protecting contraception in the 60s and the Courts said there is no tradition of protecting abortion.\par \par \uc2 But the liberal Justices were not convinced. There is an amazing line in the dissent where the liberals say the majority says it`s not going to question same sex marriage and contraception, scouts honor. They basically said, you can`t bank on that promise.\par \par \uc2 So the liberal Justices aren`t convinced. Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  obviously said that he is willing to reconsider those laws. So once again, tremendous legal uncertainty on the central issues of our time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It affects so many things politically, socially, medically. It`s still -- so much more to discuss here.\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Rosen, thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220630ei6g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Rights Group Looks To Open Clinic In State Lacking Care\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Major Garrett \par \uc2 847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In other news, the Supreme Court`s decisions upcoming decision on abortion may have far reaching consequences, especially in rural parts of America where abortion access is already very limited. Thirteen states have so-called trigger laws making most abortions illegal if the Supreme Court overturns the Roe v. Wade ruling, which says women have a constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of those states is Wyoming where there is strong opposition to abortion rights and would be providers have been met with violence.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett has that story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Much of Wyoming looks like this: Windswept and wide open. Abortion in this conservative rugged state is currently legal up to viability usually 24 weeks of pregnancy, but no providers here offer surgical abortions.\par \par \uc2 JULIE BURKHART (Wellspring Health Access Clinic Founder): Services have been lacking here.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Julie Burkhart is trying to change that, just as the Supreme Court considers Roe`s fate.\par \par \uc2 JULIE BURKHART: This is the waiting room.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Her group, Wellspring Health Access is behind the effort to open this women`s health clinic in Casper this month, making it the only abortion provider for hundreds of miles.\par \par \uc2 But in late May, it became a crime scene.\par \par \uc2 JULIE BURKHART: It gets kind of messy over here.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A fire ripped through the building scalding its walls, singeing floorboards and pushing back the opening date as much as six months long after the Supreme Court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 JULIE BURKHART: Do I want to be here talking about someone who committed an act of domestic terrorism in our building? Absolutely not. I wanted to be moving furniture in.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The fire is being investigated as arson. Surveillance video shows the suspect carrying a gas can into the waiting area in the middle of the night.\par \par \uc2 The clinic will offer a range of women`s health services including abortions if they`re still legal in Wyoming, whenever it opens.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PYATT (Anti-abortion Rights Activist): Casper is a strong pro-life community. So don`t expect open arms.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Opposition to the clinic is vocal.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Blessed art thou among women --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Each Thursday, demonstrators like Mike Pyatt gather outside to protest.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PYATT: We`ve prayed that some way the Lord would prevent this place from opening. So, if it happens to be vandalism and a fire, I`m good with that.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I don`t want this to be in my city. This is a horrible evil.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Wyoming`s Republican Legislature and Governor enacted a law in March that will ban nearly all abortions if the Supreme Court overturns Roe versus Wade. The law makes exceptions for cases of sexual assault, incest, and for the physical health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 It won`t shut down the clinic in Casper, but it would still mean people like 24-year-old Hannah, whose identity we`ve concealed will have to seek care out of state.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH: The pregnancy that I had. There was -- it was pretty much a death sentence.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Hannah says her pregnancy earlier this year worsened already complicated health issues and she feared it could even kill her.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH: I was withering away. I was a wreck of a human being. If it would have been an option to carry it to term I would have.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So in February, she drove from her home in Gillette, Wyoming seven hours to a Planned Parenthood in Montana nearly 500 miles away.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH: Any woman, any uterus holder who`s had to have an abortion does not think it`s the easy choice. It`s never the easy choice.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A choice made that much harder by the distance and expense.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The new clinic is about two hours from Hannah`s home. Right now, there are six states that have just one abortion provider. Gayle, Oklahoma, currently has none.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, Major, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Broadway`s biggest night and big is the word. We`ve got all the big winners at the Tony Awards. And there is a new EGOT winner. The initials are JH. That`s a good clue.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from the 75th Annual Tony Awards)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And a reminder, also known as an invitation you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. What do you get? Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That`s the deal.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220615ei6d000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Latest On The Flooding In Miami\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Manuel Bojorquez \par \uc2 707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s been a massive cleanup effort in South Florida today after a powerful storm flooded areas, forcing rescues and turning high- priced cars into waterlogged trash. That system went on to become the first named storm of the season, Tropical Storm Alex. It is now out in the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez reports from South Florida.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Some parts of South Florida got more than a foot of rain this weekend, leaving cars and drivers stranded in knee-deep water in Miami and making others look more like amphibious vehicles.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Whew!\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: It was the result of a tropical system not organized enough to be a named storm at the time, but still packing a punch, leading to water rescues and some flooded homes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is just the beginning season of the rain, and we`re already flooded out.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Forecasters have predicted an above-average hurricane season this year. And, just days into it, they urge residents in hurricane- and flood-prone parts of the country to be prepared and not rule out tropical systems just because they aren`t named storms.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT MOLLEDA (Meteorologist, National Weather Service): The water can be dangerous with any type of tropical system. And when they move over an area like South Florida or any other part of the U.S. coastline, this is the end result that can happen. So we just have to be ready.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The rainfall also overwhelmed parts of Miami-Dade County`s sewer system.\par \par \uc2 And due to the run-off, officials have to shut down one beach, and they advise against swimming along certain parts of the coastline here tonight - - Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 More storms are in the forecast this week, with severe thunderstorms from the Plains to the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, excessive heat warnings are in effect across Texas, as dangerously high temperatures are in the forecast. Temperatures are expected to top 100 degrees, and more than 60 record high temperatures could be broken across the South and the West this week.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead on tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS: a great white shark sighting just off the Jersey Shore; and what you need to know about a promising drug to fight breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: File this under the news, you`re going to need a bigger boat.\par \par \uc2 Along the Jersey Shore, boaters and beachgoers are on the lookout.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Fell right in front of him.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Great white shark...\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Fell right in front of him.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: ... right off the coast of Sea Isle.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s right. That`s a 12-foot, 1,000-pound great white shark that was spotted over the weekend about a mile offshore from the beach and resort towns of Sea Isle City and Avalon.\par \par \uc2 Great whites migrate this time of year, and it`s not unusual for them to be seen off the Northeast Coast.\par \par \uc2 All right, tonight, there is a promising drug in the battle against breast cancer. \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  says a drug of -- a study of its drug Enhertu stopped tumor progression for nearly 10 months, compared to five months with chemotherapy. The drug is already on the market for patients with advanced breast cancer, and the company hopes it could be used to treat early stage disease.\par \par \uc2 We have all sent a message we wished we could take back. Well, today, \uc2 Apple\uc2  announced a new feature allowing you to do just that. The upgrade means you can edit or undo a message, an iMessage.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next: a flight to remember for some very special athletes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220610ei660008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Texas in Mourning; Heartbreak in Texas; Grappling with Grief; Heated Debate over Gun Reform; School Safety Debate; Buffalo Victims Remembered; Ukraine on the Offensive; Processing Grief; Tackling Gun Violence Overseas; High Flying Role; Jesse Williams Live; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Michelle Miller, Dana Jacobson, Tony Dokoupil, Lilia  Luciano, Ed O`Keefe, Janet Shamlian, Scott MacFarlane, Jericka Duncan,  Imtiaz Tyab, Holly Williams \par \uc2 13,008 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday, May 26th. I`m Gayle King,\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I`m Michelle Miller.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And I`m Dana Jacobson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, Tony. as you know. is in Texas. We`ll get to him in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 We`re learning new details about the mass shooting in Texas and speaking to the families of the victims, including a man who lost his little girl.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA (Amerie Jo Garza`s father): I don`t want people keep telling me to -- I don`t want to do anything. I just want my baby back. I just want my baby back.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: The horrifying attack is reigniting the gun reform debate between those who see a problem with guns and those who don`t.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE (D) (Texas gubernatorial candidate): This is totally predictable when you --\par \par \uc2 MAYOR DON MCLAUGHLIN (Uvalde, Texas): Sir, you`re out of line. Sir, you`re out of line.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And we`re in Buffalo where mourners gathered for the funerals of two people killed in the racist massacre.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He gave all that he had for all that he believed in.\par \par \uc2 MAN: President Biden signing an executive order on police reform two years after the death of George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): It`s a measure of what we can do together to heal the very soul of this nation.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oklahoma Governor Kevin Stitt has signed this country`s strictest abortion law. It bans the procedure from the stage of fertilization.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tom Brady was on the golf course with a film crew and they happen to be using a drone.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: At face value, Brady thinks a hole in one.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is it real? I don`t know. But I do know it`s really cool.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In the NBA Playoffs, the Celtics crushed the heat in Miami.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And before they tipped off Game Five, the Heats PA announcer had a message for the fans.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: The Heat urges you to contact your state senators to leave a message demanding their support for commonsense gun laws.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL\uc2  ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): Some people say this is a mental health problem, other say it is a gun problem. It is both, and it can be both. So let`s work on both of those.\par \par \uc2 There have been 27 school shootings so far this year in this country, and it is May.\par \par \uc2 How does this make sense to anyone? These are our children.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jimmy, it doesn`t make sense to anyone and there are no answers, and you can`t even explain it.\par \par \uc2 You know, all the shootings are bad and heartbreaking, but there is something about children, defenseless children, that just hits very differently.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: There is no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For everybody, for everybody.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS again. Today, we`re getting right to Uvalde, Texas, that`s where we`re learning more about Tuesday`s shooting, which killed 21 people, 19 of them children. As always a case, we`re starting to hear more about these little joys of light in our family. Tony is there.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Tony. All we can say is that they are deeply loved, and now they are deeply, deeply missed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Extraordinarily loved, extraordinarily missed. And you`re so right, Gayle, there is just something about a school and the school behind me is really the epicenter of circles of grief in this community. They begin there, but ultimately, they are as big as the entire city, this entire region, which is in fact why the front page of a small paper here that comes out on Thursdays has this on it today.\par \par \uc2 The entire front section completely blacked out. It puts us in mind of the victims of course, we`re thinking of them, the two dedicated teachers, those 19 children we`ve been talking about. Their whole lives ahead of them, graduations and birthdays, families of their own one day, ultimately, all of that cut short, cut off.\par \par \uc2 And you also think of the other kids, the teachers who were witnesses to this carnage and also the first responders, thinking of them as well, some with family in this very school. It`s really an entire community that`s grieving.\par \par \uc2 And on that subject, what you see behind me, the Memorial, it`s a crime scene around it, but you`ve got crosses. You`ve got teddy bears, you`ve got flowers, and how did they get there? Well, we`ve been watching, it`s been an amazing thing as different officers and investigators take flowers from members of the community and patiently somberly walk them inside police lines and arrange that Memorial, allow those crosses, each one with the name of a victim there, all of it once again representing the grief of a nation.\par \par \uc2 President Biden on that subject plans to visit Uvalde very soon. It`s his second trip in less than two weeks to console those suffering from the result of a mass shooting. Think about that for a second.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, we spent some time trying to retrace this attack ourselves and in the process, we had an emotional conversation with a man grieving the loss of his own daughter. Her school is basically in her backyard. We`re going to get to that in just a minute, but we are also learning details about the investigation itself and Janet Shamlian is here with that. So, Janet, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 There are new questions about the response to this after the timeline indicates that the shooter may have been in that classroom as long as an hour before authorities broke into the room where he had barricaded himself and killed him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Flags at Robb Elementary School are flying at half-staff as a community reckons with a senseless tragedy. Some parents wondering "What if?"\par \par \uc2 MAN: If it could have been my child, would I do what I`ve done?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Now, law enforcement is sharing more about the gunman, a Uvalde local with no criminal history.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say he bought the semi-automatic weapon used in the school shooting from this sporting goods store May 17th, not long after he turned 18. He returned one day after that to buy 375 rounds of ammunition, and two days after that, to purchase another semi-automatic gun.\par \par \uc2 By 11:00 AM, Tuesday, officials say, he had sent a private \uc2 Facebook\uc2  message about plans to shoot his 66-year-old grandmother with whom he lived. Sources tell CBS News, they had fought over a cell phone bill.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): The second post was, "I shot my grandmother." The third post was, "I`m going to shoot an elementary school."\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Then, driving from his grandmother`s home and crashing her car near the school, entering through a back door.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Police say the gunman got past school security without shooting his weapon, and by the time he opened fire, arriving police were at a disadvantage focusing on evacuating students instead of engaging him.\par \par \uc2 Some witnesses say they begged officers to enter the building and intervene.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: He is now firing -- go in there. Go in there.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are taking care of --\par \par \uc2 (INCOMPREHENSIBLE conversation.)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And this new video shows the chaotic moments outside the school as police hold back parents.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And those officers that arrived on the scene and put their lives in danger, they saved other kids.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: By one estimate, it was more than 40 minutes before law enforcement was able to trap the gunman behind a barricade, inside the classroom where he killed 19 students and two teachers and where he was eventually shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 As his grandmother recovered in the hospital, Wednesday, his grandfather said he didn`t know there were guns in the house and expressed remorse.\par \par \uc2 (ROLANDO REYES speaking foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: "I feel pain for everyone," he said. "A lot of the kids are grandchildren of friends of mine. I`m sorry to all the families for everything."\par \par \uc2 And last night, people from across Uvalde and beyond gathered to mourn the lives lost.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Governor on Wednesday said that the massacre shows that there is a growing mental health crisis not a need for gun reform here in Texas, and the School Superintendent, Tony, is vowing that this community will move forward, but for the families of those children and those teachers, that is going to be very hard to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very hard indeed. What is not in dispute, Janet, thank you very much, of course is the magnitude of the loss here and we`re learning more about the victims. Among them, 10-year-old Xavier Lopez. His cousin described him as bubbly. He loved to dance and he was excited to go swimming this summer -- excuse me.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano has been speaking to heartbroken families as well and talking to some of the youngest survivors.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 You know I met an eight-year-old boy yesterday who told me that he was already used to the active shooter drills at the school. He knew what to do. But this time, he knew it wasn`t a drill because he heard gunshots.\par \par \uc2 He said that`s when he, his teachers and his classmates went into hiding quietly.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY SILVA (Second grade): We hid behind desks and drawers.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Second grader, Timothy Silva (sp?) was in the classroom right next to the one where the shooting rampage unfolded.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY SILVA: We were scared and the teacher kind of telling us we can pray.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She said you can pray. You were all praying together?\par \par \uc2 (TIMOTHY SILVA nodding yes.)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: For 40 minutes, his mother had no idea if he was alive.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I started panicking crying. I was thinking that the shooter was shooting everywhere that it was going to go through one of the walls and shoot him.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Timothy finally made it out safe with his teacher and classmates.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY SILVA: I was happy I didn`t get shot.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But 19 of his schoolmates and two teachers just a wall away were shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 ELLIE GARCIA (Ten years old): Hey, guys --\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Ten-year-old Ellie Garcia was the second oldest of five daughters.\par \par \uc2 SIRIA RISMENDI (Ellie Garcia`s aunt): She was very sweet.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Her aunt, Siria Rismendi (sp?) said she loved to dance and also play sports.\par \par \uc2 SIRIA RISMENDI: She was very happy and very outgoing.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Alexandria Aniyah Rubio`s parents were at school earlier that same day to celebrate her. She was awarded that morning for being a good citizen.\par \par \uc2 Her mom said on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , "We had no idea this was goodbye."\par \par \uc2 And these two fourth grade teachers are being praised as heroes.\par \par \uc2 Relatives briefed by police say they died trying to protect their students.\par \par \uc2 Eva Mireles had been a teacher for 17 years. In a statement, her daughter described her as the half that makes me whole.\par \par \uc2 Irma Garcia was just days away from finishing up her 23rd year of teaching. She was a proud mom of four.\par \par \uc2 LALO DIAZ (Coroner): It was something that I would have never thought I`d had to see in Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Coroner, Lalo Diaz had the grim job of walking through the crime scene, which in a small town like this one meant, he knew some of the victims.\par \par \uc2 LALO DIAZ: Children that were kids to people that I know. We know each other very well. It`s just hard.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: As we`ve been reporting, parents were asked to give their DNA to identify the children. I asked the coroner about that. He said mainly, of course, it`s for accuracy. There was a lot to go through. And also the other reason he said is because the parents could not go into this kind of crime scene to identify the children.\par \par \uc2 The priority was to give answers to those parents who were waiting for them. He also told me he hopes that today is the day when families will receive the bodies of their children if the Medical Examiner in San Antonio can finish the autopsies -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lilia, just that phrase, "the bodies of their children." It`s the details, they really get you, and those two teachers in the prime of their life. Lilia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We wanted to see the path that this gunman took yesterday, and so we went on a little walk around this community, trying to trace his steps on his way to this school and in the process, we ran into a parent of one of the victims who told us to our surprise, his daughter was among those who were lost.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We headed in search of where it all started and quickly ran into Juan Carranza who says he heard the gunman crash his vehicle into a nearby ditch and come out shooting.\par \par \uc2 JUAN CARRANZA (Uvalde, Texas resident): The two people over here, the criminal, they were trying to help him. But the gunman fired at them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 JUAN CARRANZA: When the gunman crossed the fence, he is walking slowly to the back of the Robb School, and then he fired -- he was firing -- he was firing with a rifle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Into the air or at the school?\par \par \uc2 JUAN CARRANZA: Into the air.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And right next door, we found something we maybe should have expected since elementary schools are almost always designed to serve the communities right around them, a parent whose own daughter had died in the attack.\par \par \uc2 I know, come here.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA (Amerie Jo Garza`s father): I just want her back. I just want her back. I want to get out of this. I don`t want to go to the funeral home. I don`t want -- I don`t want people keep telling me to -- I don`t want to do anything. I just want my baby back. I just want my baby back. Please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amerie Garza`s life was taken at a school we could see from her own backyard.\par \par \uc2 I have a fourth grader also.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: I don`t know what to do, man. I just -- she didn`t deserve that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: She didn`t --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, she didn`t.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: I just want to protect my little girl. Like the last time I saw her -- I just dropped her up at school and that was going to be the last time I would see her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That trip to school had been a little tradition for the pair and the first indication that that morning`s journey might be the last came in the form of a text message from Amerie`s mother, around noon.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: She texted me, and she was like, "I heard gunshots." Because apparently there was just like two, and then I guess, he drove over here. He wrecked his truck. By the time I got over here, he was already in the school.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amateur video from outside the school appears to show Garza along with other family members pleading for answers from the police. About 10 hours later, authorities did confirm Amerie was among the dead.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: She just turned 10 two weeks ago. She was 10.\par \par \uc2 I just want to know what happened in her last moments. I want to know what was she was doing, why he did that to her. Why did she get shot? Like, I just want to know what she did?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On Tuesday, Garza had walked his little girl to school on a small path next to his home, and given her a dollar for the cafeteria where there were biscuit sandwiches on the menu for breakfast, and slice peaches for lunch.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything that the country needs to know, that your Congressman needs to know, that the Governor needs to know?\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: I think everybody knows that nothing -- nothing changes. I mean, nothing`s going to change. I mean, it always happens in the small town, nobody expects anything bad to happen, and then it happens and everybody wants to make changes to prevent it from happening. And then it dies down a little bit and then it happens again. And then we`re just in a cycle.\par \par \uc2 I just want my baby home, I don`t care. I don`t care about anything. I don`t care about nothing at all.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, Gayle, you can see the anguish there. And, you know, it`s the details. Amerie was an honor roll student. It`s just really hard here, and we`re all going to pack up and eventually we`ll go home, but this community is going to be living with this forever. And it`s hard to imagine that there is nothing that anyone can do to stop this from happening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Tony, that`s why this is so aggravating and so painful, because there is something that can be done. People just don`t want to do what needs to be done. There is -- I don`t even know how we can continue to watch this happen time after time after time, and say that it`s okay for these assault rifles to be on the streets, and now blame it on mental health.\par \par \uc2 I don`t care who you are. Nobody needs an assault rifle in this country, unless you are a trained professional. It just doesn`t make any sense. So when we say there`s nothing we can do, that`s just not true.\par \par \uc2 And the pain that that man felt, I saw him say, you know, she was such a good girl. You know, she brushed her teeth every day. I mean, that was such a relatable, and such a thing that as parents, we all know, when we all rejoice.\par \par \uc2 This pain is too much. This pain is too much. And we keep hearing this, too. Maybe this will be the time, maybe this will be the time. I have to keep holding on to that. But it was a beautiful exchange between the two of you. You could see, he needed you at that moment. You know what, Tony, you needed him to? You needed him, too. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did, and Gayle, I would just underscore -- I just want to underscore that that`s one parent. There are 18 other families who also lost a child. So, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. Thinking about them all the time. Thank you, too.\par \par \uc2 Many Americans, as you see are frustrated with a capital F at the lack of government action following these attacks. And one familiar political figure in Texas brought his frustration to Uvalde`s yesterday. That`s Democratic candidate for Governor, \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 , who confronted Republican Governor Greg Abbott, accusing him of doing nothing.\par \par \uc2 The former Senate and presidential candidate was shouted down by other officials. Ed O`Keefe is at the White House with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE: The time to stop the next shooting is right now and you are doing nothing.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In the midst of a news conference led by Texas Republican Governor Greg Abbott on the Uvalde shooting, suddenly, his Democratic opponent, \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  rose to confront him.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR DON MCLAUGHLIN: This isn`t the place to talk to, so --\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE: You said this was not predictable. This was totally predictable.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR DON MCLAUGHLIN: Sir, you are out of line. Sir, you are out of line. Sir, you are out of line. Please leave this auditorium.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Local officials, including the mayor of Uvalde called to get him kicked out.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR DON MCLAUGHLIN: I can`t believe you`re a sick son of a [bleep] that would come to a deal like this to make a political issue.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE: This is on you until you choose to do something.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: O`Rourke, who trails Abbott in polls had called for a ban on military style assault weapons during his failed 2020 presidential bid, later explained why he showed up.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE: We`ve got to stand up to this or we just accept this theater and business as usual and we accept the next shooting.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And in Washington, Senate Democrats say they`ll try again to pass gun safety legislation already passed in the House.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Please, damn it, please, think if it was your child or grandchild.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But the legislation lacks the necessary support as many Republicans say gun restrictions won`t stop mass violence.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Getting weapons, dangerous, any firearm is potentially dangerous in the hands of a deranged lunatic. At the end of the day, the issue here is not the firearm.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The Texas shooting coincided with a previously scheduled confirmation hearing for Steven Dettelbach to lead the \uc2 Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives\uc2 , an agency that investigates gun crimes and hasn`t had a permanent boss in seven years.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DETTELBACH (Nominee for \uc2 Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives\uc2  director): I vow to never let politics in any way influence my action as ATF Director.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: As tragedy once again exposes all too familiar political divides, as for Governor Abbott, he is scheduled to attend the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2 `s annual meeting in Houston this week where former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is also set to appear, but Abbott wouldn`t say yesterday whether he still plans to be there -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It is 7:30, Thursday, May 26. And here are some of the stories that we are following today, starting with the second emergency shipment of baby formula. A cargo plane flew from Germany to Washington yesterday, carrying formula to be sent to hospitals and retailers as soon as this weekend.\par \par \uc2 On Capitol Hill, lawmakers grilled the head of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  who admitted the agency was too slow to react to shortages. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  spent months investigating and finally closing a contaminated production plant owned by \uc2 Abbott Labs\uc2 . Abbott plans to restart production next week with products getting to customers around June 20th.\par \par \uc2 A top executive apologize to parents yesterday saying, "We let you down."\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma`s Governor has signed the nation`s strictest abortion ban. The new law prohibits all abortions starting from fertilization. It includes exceptions for saving the life of the mother and if the pregnancy is the result of rape or incest that has been reported to law enforcement. The new law allows private citizens to sue anyone who helps a woman terminate her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma and other Republican led states are restricting abortion access ahead of a Supreme Court ruling that could overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania`s Republican Senate primary will be decided by a recount with TV celebrity, Dr. Mehmet Oz leading former hedge fund CEO David McCormick by less than one half percent of the vote. That close margin triggers the state`s Automatic Recount Law. Oz or McCormick will take on Democrat, John Fetterman in November.\par \par \uc2 The recount will last until June 7th, that is nearly two weeks from now.\par \par \uc2 And Pope Francis says the United States should tighten its gun laws because of the deadly mass shooting in Uvalde, Texas. At his regular Wednesday audience, the Pope said he was heartbroken by the massacre at the elementary school. He called for a commitment to stricter gun restrictions on gun sales, saying quote, "So that tragedies like this cannot happen again." End quote.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2015, the Pope challenged gun manufacturers who touted their religious beliefs -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Pope is speaking out.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: He is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope people listen to him. Thank you, Dana.\par \par \uc2 Many school districts around the country, they are improving or they are reviewing building security after Tuesday`s massacre. There is also more talk about the so-called hardening of schools and in many places, the Federal government is paying the bill for that.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with the numbers that add up to tens of millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Gayle, good morning to you. Congress is paralyzed by gridlock and changing gun laws, but some schools aren`t just standing by idly, they want money to tighten up their security.\par \par \uc2 And a review of public records by CBS News shows Washington has been doling out quite a bit of it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Many students were greeted with extra security on Wednesday in the wake of the shootings in Uvalde. School districts from Texas to Missouri to Arizona to Virginia tell CBS News they are reviewing policies or staffing or ordering new training to keep students safe.\par \par \uc2 Amid the congressional stalemate over new gun laws, the Superintendent in Millville, New Jersey isn`t waiting. He has been upgrading security for months.\par \par \uc2 TONY TRONGONE (Millville, New Jersey Superintendent of Schools): The security vestibules, we have cameras. We have a -- we have a response, panic alarms to first responders.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Today, a series of Senators is recommending hardening of schools. But a review of Justice Department records obtained by CBS News show Washington has already spent more than $150 million since 2018 doing so, through School Violence Prevention Grants and for a wide range of items.\par \par \uc2 The record shows schools in Roselle, New Jersey sought grants for new digital security radios. In Logan County, West Virginia, the Justice Department provided $194,000.00 to help pay for cameras, for door locks, and training for in-school police.\par \par \uc2 Because you`re concerned about mitigating the risk of a school shooting, is that part of the motivation?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DIAL (Chapmanville Regional High School principal): The primary number one responsibility of all school personnel is to make certain that every student that attends our schools are returned safely to their parents that evening.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: We found the money distributed by the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  in grants has more than doubled since 2018. Even New Jersey Democrat \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  who has championed school safety grants in the past says hardening of schools is not a solution to gun violence.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): I mean what kind of testimony is it that we declare that we can`t protect our schools from people with guns, so we`re going to build bunkers and castles for our children? And does that solve this problem? No.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The U.S. Attorney General has just asked Congress for $53 million next year for these school security grants and we went through them and found a number of schools including that Logan County, West Virginia District, used some of the money for mental health training for staff -- Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Another community still reeling from a mass shooting, up next, we`ll show you how Buffalo remembered and honored the hero of the recent supermarket shooting.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Roughly 1,600 miles northeast of Uvalde, another community in Buffalo, New York is grieving from a recent mass shooting. Mourners gathered yesterday to honor a retired police lieutenant killed trying to stop the gunman.\par \par \uc2 It was one of two funerals yesterday for the victims of the racist attack that killed 10 Black people.\par \par \uc2 Our national correspondent, Jericka Duncan is in Buffalo near the site of the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning to you, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 You know there are growing memorials at each corner here surrounding this Tops. It is clear that this community will not forget about these 10 lives lost and yesterday we did have the pleasure and the honor of attending the funeral service for Aaron Salter, Jr. He is credited with warning shoppers to run. He also fired at the gunman and simply put the Buffalo Police Commissioner said, he saved lives.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Inside this church near Buffalo, grief was everywhere. This time for 55-year-old Aaron Salter, Jr. Former Buffalo Deputy Commissioner Kimberly Beaty.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY BEATY (Former Buffalo Deputy Commissioner): This is tough. Aaron with a good man, a good person, and we all deserve better.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: His casket was draped in an American flag, a fitting tribute to the husband, father of three and retired Buffalo Police Officer. Salter was on duty as a security guard at Tops when he became one of the 10 Black people gunned down by an 18-year-old who law enforcement officials say was motivated by hate.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH GRAMAGLIA (Buffalo Police Commissioner): Aaron bravely fought evil that day.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: His life inspired many.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He gave all that he had for all that he believed in protecting and saving lives.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: After the service, it was back to serving the community for local volunteers.\par \par \uc2 TRAE THA TRUTH (Rapper and philanthropist): Right now, I can see a lot of hurt.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wednesday, rapper and philanthropist, Trae tha Truth alongside social justice organization, Until Freedom joined in. He came to Buffalo with an 18-wheeler full of food and toiletries, filling a void left behind from the now closed neighborhood Tops grocery store.\par \par \uc2 While planning this trip, he learned of Tuesday`s massacre at the elementary school in Texas, Trae`s home state.\par \par \uc2 TRAE THA TRUTH: These are babies, you know, like, I have kids that age and younger. I couldn`t even imagine what type of pain that is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And it`s that pain that drives him to tragedy after tragedy. This morning, he is off to Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 TRAE THA TRUTH: I`ve got to be there for them just as well. I`m going to go and be with the families, reach out to the mayor, see what assistance they need and let them know that Houston is definitely behind them and other places are behind them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A lot of people here in Buffalo expressing their concerns for what happened in Texas, and as it pertains to this mass shooting, the funerals will be held Friday and Saturday -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad that you`re there, Jericka. You know, that story seems so long ago, but actually it really isn`t. The story didn`t happen weeks ago. So, I think it`s an important reminder.\par \par \uc2 You know how people say what we need in this country is a good guy with a gun. That man was a good guy with a gun and lost his life trying to help and save others.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Saved a lot of lives. But like you said, he is not with us anymore.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Very tragic.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It`s tough. I know, Jericka was on a flight to cover that, that funeral when all of this happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All of this other story broke.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It`s -- it`s just -- there are no words. There are no words and I feel for Tony. He has children, the age of those children. You know, when you cover these things, and you have people in your lives like that, it really impacts you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It affects everyone. I mean, the entire country is mourning with them. You think of people that have gone through this before. It`s -- I hate saying a club, but it`s a group of people no one wants to be a part of. These two communities tied together with others forever.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Forever.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In a way they don`t want.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What we all know is whether you have children or you don`t have children. Everybody knows when your child goes to school, they`re not supposed to come home in a body bag.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: No, they are not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And part of the problem, too is not only did your child die, but your head goes nuts when you think about how they died. And that`s why this is so very painful for all of us with a beating heart.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, we want to take you close to the frontlines in Ukraine. Yes, there is still a war going on, on top of everything. CBS News Imtiaz Tyab, his team run from shelling, dodge Russian drones and duck for cover. They`re going to give us an inside look at the war.\par \par \uc2 But right now, it is 7:44. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: As Russia`s war on Ukraine enters its fourth month, President Zelenskyy has dismissed the idea that Ukraine should give up some land in order to make peace. This morning, we have a firsthand look at what Ukrainian troops are facing near the frontlines.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` Imtiaz Tyab and his crew came dangerously close to Russia`s relentless attacks on Ukrainian villages. Imtiaz shows us what happened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Deep in the Ukrainian countryside, there are only a handful of farm houses and fields here.\par \par \uc2 But for weeks, Russia has been targeting this village relentlessly. It is here we meet Major Sergey (sp?), he and his soldiers have been defending this area for weeks, but have recently changed tactics.\par \par \uc2 I just want to be clear, this is now an offensive. You`re targeting Russians.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR SERGEY (Ukrainian soldier): Yes, we are targeting every day, because we exactly -- we need to do this.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: But Russian forces whose positions are just two miles from here aren`t backing down.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: We just had some incoming Russian shelling. We`re just on our way to find a shelter in this disused farmhouse.\par \par \uc2 The closest place to take cover is under a tree as the strikes intensify.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And the shelling gets closer and closer.\par \par \uc2 That was some pretty serious incoming from the Russian side. This was an area they occupied up until six weeks ago, and they really want it back.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Getting closer.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: A Russian drone is spotted circling above, it is now or never.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Go, go, go.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: So we start sprinting and are led down some stairs into a nearby wood cellar, where Ukrainian forces have been taking refuge for weeks.\par \par \uc2 That`s more incoming from the Russian side. They`ve really just been shelling this area pretty frequently.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The strikes are now just 50 yards away. With us is Oksana Kozivenko (sp?).\par \par \uc2 Is this normal for this area?\par \par \uc2 (OKSANA KOZIVENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "By any measure, this is not normal," she says. "The Russians are giving us a hard time, but we can tell they`re getting weaker." After several more strikes, we`re given the order to make a run for it through fields of wheat, which are now a frontline and back to our vehicles that drive as quickly as we can towards safety.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Imtiaz Tyab on the Kherson frontlines.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Harrowing. Happy to say that Imtiaz and his crew are okay, but it just shows you how dangerous it can be bringing the story to you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Meanwhile, it has moved from the top story. The war has not ended there. They continue to fight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up, Tony will have more from Uvalde, Texas and we`re going to talk to a member of a team that will help children and the community there deal with the trauma of what happened.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, the hard, hard days ahead in Uvalde, Texas. Tony is still there speaking to families who are struggling to move forward. And now, they`re demanding answers.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: We will also talk to a psychologist with the difficult job of trying to help children in Uvalde. She tells us what those kids need most right now.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And why are there so many mass shootings in America? We will head overseas to look at how other countries have responded to gun violence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A very good question. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight o`clock."\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: I just want her back. I just want her back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re in Texas retracing the moments leading up to that horrific shooting at an elementary school and we`re remembering the victims.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have a fourth grader also.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: I don`t know what to do, man.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Please, damn it. Please. Think if it was your child or grandchild.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: President Biden is once again calling for gun reform, but many lawmakers are listing familiar reasons to oppose it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Any firearm is potentially dangerous in the hands of a deranged lunatic.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In Buffalo, mourners gather to honor two victims of the racist supermarket massacre, including a retired police officer who exchanged fire with that gunman.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH GRAMAGLIA: Aaron bravely fought evil that day.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: We just had some incoming Russian shelling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our team in Ukraine is shelled by Russian artillery and sees firsthand what many villages on the frontlines are experiencing.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE: The time to stop the next shooting is now and you are doing nothing.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And Texas governor candidate \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  is removed from a news conference after confronting his opponent, Governor Greg Abbott, on the issue of gun.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): That`s not true Beto. They`re not doing nothing. They`re yelling at you for pointing out that they`re doing nothing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour heading back to Tony Dokoupil. He is in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Tony, we know it`s going to be another very hard day for the families there because they are now members of a club that no parent wants to be in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s right, Gayle. The sun is coming up here on Day Two of the day everything changed here in Uvalde, Texas, and we`re going to learn more, of course, about what happened, but also what it cost this community.\par \par \uc2 Some of the things families are saying, "My one and only baby," "My sweet baby sister," "The keeper of my soul," just three of the ways that family members are describing the 21 people murdered here. You see them on your screen now.\par \par \uc2 And as we think about all of them, we also want to show you a little bit more of our conversation with Angel Garza, whose daughter Amerie was killed on Tuesday because I think it shows the tradeoffs here. The human costs of a disturbed young man having the freedom to buy a weapon in his own community, and then turning it on a local school, a school in that very community.\par \par \uc2 Garza told us that he went to Robb Elementary School behind me when he heard about the shooting and he just wanted to help.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can hear someone crying inside.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: That`s her mother. So, I was a -- I am med-aide, so I went over there and there was a little girl just covered in blood head to toe and soaked in blood and shit. You know, plasma in her hair. It looked like you know, she got hit. I thought she got hit and she was hysterical.\par \par \uc2 And she said that she was fine. She said that they shot her best friend. She said my daughter`s name.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s your daughter`s name?\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: Amerie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s her last name?\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: Amerie Garza.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What kind of little girl was she?\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: She was the -- she was the sweetest thing. You know, she`s -- she`s so creative. She just got an award for being the most creative. She doesn`t hurt anyone. She doesn`t bully. She does the right thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have a fourth grader also.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: I don`t know what to do, man. I just -- she didn`t deserve that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARZA: She didn`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, she didn`t.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Angel doesn`t know what to do there. His family has got a long road ahead. They told us that the memories are just too thick on the ground here around this school. They came back to get close yesterday and then they`re going to go out of the area because it`s just too difficult.\par \par \uc2 What to do though is a question that the next generation is confronting. There is a nonprofit group I want to tell you about, it`s called Students Demand Action. And they`re urging walkouts today in schools all across the country, guys. They`re calling for change.\par \par \uc2 And as I send it back to you, I`ll tell you, their mission is a mission that I think a lot of people share in this country, a hope, a belief that we can -- we can -- we can make it possible that other families will not be forced to do what the families here are doing, which is in the days ahead, having to bury their children because of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We can make it possible, Tony, because this is so preventable. I think that`s the other thing that is so frustrating. This does not have to happen in the United States of America, and it continues to happen.\par \par \uc2 Tony, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right now we`re going to turn to another element of this story being joined by the Chief of Psychology at \uc2 Texas Children`s Hospital\uc2 , Karin Price. She`s helping lead a trauma response team that is going to offer support to the Uvalde community. We`ve heard how tight of a community it is there.\par \par \uc2 Karin, good morning to you. I think so many people are wondering, what do you say to those members of the community, and this isn`t your first time dealing with a school shooting like this?\par \par \uc2 KARIN PRICE (\uc2 Texas Children`s Hospital\uc2  Chief of Psychology): That`s correct. The things that we say to the community are that we`re here to support you, you`re our neighbors, and we want to help in any way that we can.\par \par \uc2 We want to mobilize resources to address any of the needs that the community has beginning now, but also in the long run, as they recover and move on to the next phase of this tragedy.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Just hearing in Tony`s interview, they`re hearing that young girl who saw her best friend get shot, how do you help a child start to process that?\par \par \uc2 KARIN PRICE: Right now, in this immediate timeframe, we don`t worry about processing the trauma, we worry about keeping kids safe, we worry about the adults who are around them, the people that care for them and take care of them, getting the help that they need to be the support that their parents need, to let them know that all feelings that they have are okay to keep structure and routine in their children`s lives and to give them the space to talk about the pieces that they want to talk about and not push them to talk about the things they`re not ready to talk about yet.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You`re heading into Uvalde, you`re going to the frontlines. What`s your first order of business when you actually meet with the parents and the survivors?\par \par \uc2 KARIN PRICE: Our first order of business is to make sure that they are armed with the information they need to support their children the best. Those are things like making sure that parents are getting the support they need to manage their emotions.\par \par \uc2 All emotions are okay in this situation. These families have been through truly an unspeakable tragedy, but they need to get their help and support away from their children. So that when they are sitting with their children, when they`re laying in bed with them at night, they`re there to give the support and comfort that their child needs to deal with their own feelings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This story is so big, Karin, I think it`s different when it`s children -- innocent, defenseless children at a school of all places. So even people who are watching this are filled with outrage, filled with grief, filled with a lot of anger, too.\par \par \uc2 What`s the best way to deal with that, as you`re watching these images and listening to these heartbreaking stories?\par \par \uc2 KARIN PRICE: One of the things that we suggest is making sure that you have an outlet, whether that is your partner, your spouse, a family member, a best friend, somebody that you`re connected with in your community to process those feelings with.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is to make sure that everybody is also taking care of themselves. We can`t help other people if we are not taking care of ourselves. For people that are really traumatized by the pictures that they see and the stories that they hear, they may need to take a break and be really careful about what they are exposing themselves to, and we also need to be really careful about what our children are being exposed to.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what can you say about mental health at this time? You know, it seems that conversation is now moving to this as a mental health issue. We need to do something about mental health, which is also true, but there are a lot of mentally ill people that don`t go and do this. Isn`t this so much bigger than a mental health issue?\par \par \uc2 KARIN PRICE: Mental health is certainly part of the picture. I don`t think it is the full story. We know that we have a mental health crisis in this country and really in many, many countries across the world in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, we need to do our best to create systems of care to take care of children, adults, families, at any stage where they might be experiencing mental health concerns.\par \par \uc2 I do think that we can talk about as information comes out, how we could make things different in the future, what signs we need to look for that somebody might be experiencing the kind of mental health concerns that could lead them to be dangerous while also recognizing that you`re right, the vast majority of people with mental illness are not dangerous. They`re not a danger to society.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Well, Karin, we`re glad your team will be there for this community. Sorry that you`ve had this experience already in order to go serve them. Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 Other countries have seen deadly mass shootings, so why is America so different when it comes to passing stricter gun laws?\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is in London and has some answers.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:10, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Coming up, we`ll speak with actor, Jesse Williams about his new role on the Broadway stage, after 12 seasons as a doctor on the hit show "Grey`s Anatomy."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Plus, "Top Gun: Maverick" actor, Glen Powell will join us to talk about training to be a pilot for his role alongside \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2 . How he says the original Top Gun inspired him to be an actor.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: In the wake of the Texas School shooting, it is unclear if lawmakers will do anything to tighten gun laws. That has not been the case in other countries which have experienced mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is in London.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning. How are people there reacting to what happened here?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Well, good morning, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 Many people here in the U.K. feel a special bond with the U.S. and it is painful to see American children killed like this. But a lot of people I`ve spoken to here also say they`re mystified, they`re confused, because here and in other countries where there`s been a massacre, the government brings in tougher gun control laws and they cannot comprehend why that doesn`t happen in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Sixteen young children and their teacher have been shot dead by a gunman at a primary school near Sterling.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: In 1996, the United Kingdom was horrified by the murder of five and six-year-old children by a lone shooter.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I just can`t believe something like this.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: It was the deadliest mass shooting in British history. And after a grassroots campaign, the government here banned the private ownership of all handguns.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Good evening. All Australians tonight share the horror of the Port Arthur massacre.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: In Australia, also in 1996, a lone gunman murdered 35 people. In response, the then conservative Australian government banned nearly all semi-automatic rifles and shotguns and launched a program to buy back more than 600,000 weapons from those who already owned them.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HOWARD (Former Australian Prime Minister): The greatest civil right you have is to stay alive. Staying alive and being free from random attack is a far more precious civil rights than owning a gun.\par \par \uc2 MAN: These are our most popular firearms.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: In Australia in 2015, we found the nation`s tough gun control laws widely popular, even with this gun shop owner.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you were in America, you`d probably sell a lot more guns and make a lot more money.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, I suppose sometimes it`s not all about the money.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: From Columbine, to Sandy Hook, and Parkland, school shootings have broken the hearts of Americans, but there has been no major change to gun ownership laws in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 DR. JONATHAN METZL (Vanderbilt University Center for Medicine, Health and Society director): We`ve got a gun lobby and a corporate gun lobby that has its tentacles so deeply into our political process.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Dr. Jonathan Metzl is a sociologist who told us he has witnessed the gun lobby pressure U.S. politicians into withholding support for tougher laws.\par \par \uc2 DR. JONATHAN METZL: The lesson of those other countries is really that people came together across the political divide.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Were countries are able to do that because they don`t have powerful gun lobbies?\par \par \uc2 DR. JONATHAN METZL: A hundred percent yes. Definitely, yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: One of the Parkland survivors called out Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  for taking campaign money from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAMERON KASKY (Parkland shooting survivor): Senator Rubio, can you tell me right now that you will not accept a single donation from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And even that didn`t change anything.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): That people buy into my agenda, and I do support the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But in New Zealand, another mass shooting in 2019, fifty one people killed in two mosques once again prompted legal change outside the U.S., a ban on military style semi-automatic weapons and assault rifles.\par \par \uc2 This week, Prime Minister \uc2 Jacinda Ardern\uc2  explained how they did it.\par \par \uc2 JACINDA ARDERN (New Zealand Prime Minister): Now, we have legitimate needs for guns in our country for things like pest control and to protect our biodiversity, but you don`t need a military style semi-automatic weapon to do that, and so we got rid of them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Clearly, assault gun deaths plummet after its new laws came into effect. And here in the U.K., there are so few guns on the streets that even the police don`t generally carry firearms.\par \par \uc2 And ladies, while in the U.S. this year, there`s been more than one mass shooting a day on average, in other wealthy countries, they are extremely rare.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, I know.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Staggering.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Thank you, Holly. People are looking at us going "What is wrong with the United States of America?" You know, we are four percent of the population, but 42 percent of mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And so much is being said out there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: In social media, this one really caught my eye: "Let`s take a moment to honor the sacrifice of our brave school children who lay down their lives to protect our right to bear arms." People are fighting mad about what has taken place.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the question is, will lawmakers hear their cries?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I say again, when we talk about the right to bear arms that was written at a time when people had muskets, they did not have -- we have a very pesky fly in the studio -- they did not have the assault rifles that were killing innocent people, innocent children.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Good reporting by Holly Williams and reminding all of us, obviously.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Of what has happened elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look who`s here in our greenroom. It`s Glen Powell, one of the stars of the blockbuster new movie "Top Gun: Maverick." Good to see.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL ("Top Gun: Maverick" actor): Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you ready for your victory lap?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Get ready, Glen.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I`m really excited to show everybody, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Get ready. We called \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2  this morning, he said you were really good. He loved working with you.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Well, welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 "Top Gun: Maverick" is one of the most highly anticipated movies of the year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And with good reason.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That`s true, Gayle. That is true.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: The high-flying sequel follows Navy pilot, Pete Mitchell nicknamed "Maverick" as you might know, as he trains a new generation of pilots for a very special mission.\par \par \uc2 One of them is called "Hangman" played by actor, Glen Powell. He previously starred in the Oscar-nominated film, "Hidden Figures" and Netflix`s "Set It Up."\par \par \uc2 In this clip, a very confident Hangman talks to the other elite pilots about who is going to be the team leader of this special mission.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That`s darn confident, oh my goodness. It`s "Top Gun: Maverick" oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: We`ve all dated the Hangman.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, we have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But go ahead, finish the lead. Finish the lead.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, yes, yes. \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2  is obviously producing this big one, which is like \uc2 CBS\uc2 , part of the \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  family and Glen Powell, boy, he is joining us this morning. It`s hot here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I love how call it the confidence. Is that what we are calling it these days?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, what do you want to call it, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Beep. And you`re right, we all know one of those.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Dated one of those.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes, yes. You want flying, you don`t want your daughter dating him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, you don`t. I mean, you turned this role down when \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2  called you initially.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I did.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Why? And what changed her mind?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: You know, I originally auditioned for Miles Teller`s role, which was the son of "Goose." And then you know, the role wasn`t that great on the page, and then I got to sit down with Tom, who kind of told me his approach to making movies and I said -- he goes, "All right, look dude, I know you don`t want to do the movie. What kind of career do you want to have?" I was like, "Dude, you, man."\par \par \uc2 He`s like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to be you.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: And he goes, "Well, do you know how I do that?" I was like, "Yes, you choose great roles." He goes, "No, I choose great movies and I make the roles great." And so we got to basically make this role together, which was kind of a dream come true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tom -- did Tom have an influence on you? And I only ask that, because now we hear that you`ve actually gone and gotten your pilot`s license. Was that something you had thought about doing ever before?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I feel like I`ve always wanted to get my pilot`s license. It`s always like on your bucket list. But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it`s not. Glen is absolutely not. Go ahead, you`ve always what --\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I mean, it is like something that just like for me, like it`s always like that spontaneous sense of adventure that you can just like pick up and fly anywhere, anytime, and because Tom put together this flight program where he literally, we`re flying in the Extra 300, the Cessna, the L-39, and the 18s. We flew in all of these different aircraft, and I pretty much got all the hours I would need and I knew so much about aviation that I could just finish it up. So now --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So these scenes are actually you.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Oh, yes, we`re in the back of real F-18s in this entire movie.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you afraid, Glen?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: There were a couple moments. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because the movie, I have to say is amazing. I can`t wait for America to see it. I can`t wait for them to see you in it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But were you afraid? I mean, it`s more to me than just a couple of moments when we look. Half the time, I didn`t know what I was looking at. I just knew it was very scary.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: You know, we`re trying to put audiences on like a serious thrill ride. That`s why Tom was like, "Hey, we`ve got to shoot this thing for real." There were days in which we were flying like 50 feet off the ground, 500 knots through canyons where I didn`t tell my mom what I was doing.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Very smart.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: But overall, I mean, we`re flying with the best pilots in the Navy. So, we sure got it done.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: How many G`s were you -- because I had gone up in one.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, me too.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I did not do well, put it that way.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Oh, you went up in the back of an 18?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, I did not do well at all.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: It`s tough. Right?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So how many did --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you pass out or throw up?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I didn`t pass out, but I was ready to throw up, yes?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I was G-7. It`s insane.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, how many --\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Wait, you were in the 18, too?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Seven G`s?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, 7G`s.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: How many did you do?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I beat Norah O`Donnell. It is insane.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: You`re like in a very small group of people who have actually gotten to do that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: It`s like a really -- it`s like, not many people get that right. It`s hard to describe.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I think the load limit of an 18 is seven and a half G`s.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: We sometimes push that a little bit, but yes, it`s -- yes, it`s tough.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You`re impressed, aren`t you?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I`m very impressed. I really am. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, and I`ve been on \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  and I love that. That`s my speed. But when you got your pilot`s license, we hear that Tom surprised you. What did he do?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes. So, you know, Tom was just so excited because he just loves aviation. He literally shows up to set in his plane, takes off in his plane. He just makes it really cool.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: He`s \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: He is \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2  and he is Maverick. So, you know, that just sort of love of aviation just kind of became infectious. So as I was getting my pilot`s license, he would follow up with me and be like, "Hey, like, you know, I hear your cross country is coming up. You`re about to do your solo."\par \par \uc2 So whenever I finished my check ride, I got on the ground. Somebody walked up and handed me an envelope. And inside that envelope, it said, "Welcome to the skies, Tom." And it was a certificate for us, driving lessons.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: So yes, now, I`ve started racing cars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So back to Tom`s question. I love him saying, "Dude, what kind of career do you want? What kind of career do you want, dude?" You`ve been through this experience you`re on --\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen, I saw you in "Great Debaters" years ago. So as you sit here today, what are you thinking, Glen Powell about your career?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Honestly, I`m just like really grateful that I`ve had mentors like Denzel and Tom and these people who have like kind of pushed me to do this thing. I mean, like Hollywood is a place that -- it`s a gauntlet and like, I didn`t know anybody when I first did this and Denzel was the one who gave me the confidence to actually move out to Hollywood in the first place.\par \par \uc2 And Tom has been an amazing friend and mentor to like, on whatever this journey is after this, I feel more confident because he`s been in my life.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And when you saw \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2  in "Top Gun" with your dad as a kid, this influenced the entire career, right?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes, it was like -- but you know, watching "Top Gun" with your dad is -- I mean, I would recommend everybody go watch this movie with. It`s such a great, you know, it`s a parental movie. It`s a movie that you know if you`re a mother or father, it gives you all the feels as you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All the feels.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: And I just feel like watching this movie with my dad especially like being at the U.K. premiere and watching my dad literally sit with Tom and like joke and talk movies. I was literally like getting choked up, I was like, "Oh my God."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s so nice.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Tom just the best with my family and you know it feels very full circle for a movie that kind of kick started it all to kind of --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Full circle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, Glen we`re going to watch it with the crew later.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yay.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I am so excited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are very excited about that.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes, I`d love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And shout out to your family because your sister, Lauren (sp?) is here. She says you`re the greatest brother -- that you are the greatest brother because you include the family in everything and clearly, we can see that and hear that. And you play a jerk so well it is clearly acting. Really acting.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Glen Powell, and congratulations. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Thank you, guys for having me on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Top Gun: Maverick" premieres tomorrow only in theaters and you`ve got to see it on the big screen. It`s worth it.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Cannot wait.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is worth the price.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up, cannot wait for this either, we`re going to be talking with another great actor, Jesse Williams in our greenroom. He`s going to tell us about his Broadway debut in "Take Me Out." Hi Jesse -- and getting nominated for a Tony Award.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:37. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Grey`s Anatomy.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is actor, Jesse Williams, playing Dr. Jackson Avery on "Grey`s Anatomy." You`ve seen him there. Williams played Dr. Avery for 12 seasons on the hit drama. Now, he has taken his talent to the Broadway stage.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Mr. Williams. He`s earned a Tony nomination for his Broadway debut. That`s hard to do. Williams plays Darren Lemming, a Major League Baseball player in the show. It`s called "Take Me Out."\par \par \uc2 In this scene, he talks with his teammate, Kippy about coming out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Take Me Out.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So confident. Jesse Williams joins us now. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS (Actor): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was there Tuesday night, Seat G-113. Did you see?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: I`m so glad I didn`t know. I`ve decided I don`t want to know if people are there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh you don`t want to know?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Yes, it just adds another level of awareness.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get that. I get that.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Thanks for coming though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You didn`t hear me go, "Jesse." I was very classy.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: That was you the guys escorted out?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That was me. But I`ve read that you said that you wanted something that was insurmountable, that was terrifying, that was extremely difficult.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did you want that? And why was this it for you?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: This was definitely it for me. I think, I wanted to get out of my comfort zone. You know, I want -- I had been somewhere where I got very comfortable. I was able, you know, for a variety of reasons.\par \par \uc2 You know, I was on that show for a long time, able to raise my children, be close. It`s all very kind of -- I know where I am. It is very predictable. Nothing`s going to be earth shattering, and I wanted to do something next that was absolutely scary and got me a little uncomfortable. Challenge me. Put me in a place to reshape.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What scares you about it, Jesse?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Well, a couple of things. I mean, it`s live.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Take Me Out.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: A, I get to get back to New York.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An audience is right there.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: You`re live, you`re on the tight rope. You`re on the high wire. And so, you have to be -- you have to be present. You have to be present.\par \par \uc2 It can change. It can change every show every minute, every audience is different. And you also -- there`s a freedom through repetition. You know on TV and film you do -- you say a line once and you`ll never say it again in your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: This is the opposite. You get to really explore every nook and cranny of -- and every nuance as we were talking about earlier -- of that person`s life and experience. So, there`s a lot there. It`s liberating. It`s liberating thing. You feel very much alive.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And newsflash, in case there are those that don`t know, there`s a little bit of nudity.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: A little bit.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Just a little bit.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: That`s the word we like to use.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s not a little bit. Newsflash. And I`m not complaining. I am not complaining.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: No.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: No, no, no, but I would imagine --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For a reason.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It`s real.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s not gratuitous, we can say that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: No.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: But that was a big -- certainly, that was scary. Certainly, that was something, you know if that`s what you`re asking, like, when I first heard about the play, you know, people use shorthand, right? So there`s a -- there`s nudity. It`s baseball, gay.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Okay, I`m certainly not going to be naked on stage. That`s, no, thank you. And then I read it and then it was too late. Once I read it, I was hooked.\par \par \uc2 Richard Greenberg is an absolutely spectacular writer, and I didn`t have a choice. It really wasn`t a decision to make. It`s smart. It`s on the bleeding edge. It`s relevant.\par \par \uc2 It`s got incredible -- incredibly dense comedy and drama and pain and anguish.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: There is a real arc -- as an actor, you look for an arc, you look for a challenge. There are very interesting characters that you can play. There are also situations you can play that you`re experiencing something very interesting. And this character goes through an absolute whirlwind. And that is a very difficult thing to do eight times a week.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And how do you --\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Emotionally, it`s really actually quite devastating, which I didn`t anticipate to this degree, right? You can read it, you can rehearse it, but then living it. Your body doesn`t know the difference, you know, without giving too much away, this character goes through a great deal of pain. So that`s a little difficult to sustain over time.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I was going to say, almost, how do you handle that? Because especially thinking of a day with a matinee.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You`ve got to get back up and go back for a new audience.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Yes. I`m still -- I`m always still figuring it out. But you certainly have a meditation practice. I figured out how to ground myself afterwards, kind of put some of that away. And we have such a great group of folks that we are working --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You really do.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: My teammates are incredible. So, we rely on each other. And look, this is my first time doing a three-act play. First time is certainly on stage on Broadway. So I`m always asking questions. I`m always looking for best practices, best ideas from anybody.\par \par \uc2 I`m a student, really, I`m just kind of happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I`m fascinated by it, Jesse, because it is -- I`ve never seen anything like it on stage and there is a massive shower scene. So all of the actors on stage are nude, and when it is first revealed, I always look to see what`s the audience -- no one reacts. And I`m surprised that there`s no tittering.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is no snickering, there`s no, "Woo." There`s none of that.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Yes, I find that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does that surprise you? Because listen, I have to be very honest. I went with my nephew, you know, and he`s sitting there. He is drooling behind his mask. My glasses start fogging, because I was breathing so hard. I was looking because it really is beautifully, beautifully done.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the audience just sort of takes it all in.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And first time you did it. What -- did you have expectations?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: I think I did have an expectation that there would be some vocalization.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Vocalization. People, in their discomfort, we make sounds.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Right, right, right.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Just to try to -- and also the audience is part of the play.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: This thing, you all react to each other. You wonder why she is laughing. What the hell is so funny about that? Did I miss something? There`s a lot of interactivity there.\par \par \uc2 But that silence?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: It`s really interesting. It is -- it does reflect some maturity, it does reflect also, the reason it`s in the play is because it lets the audience, it forces the audience to confront the situation that the characters are confronting, which is as men, what is -- what is it to be a man? Right? How is it -- can I be a man without being homophobic?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: Or is that how I define what masculinity is by demonstrating what I`m not? And where do I look? How do I behave differently now that there is somebody who has come out that`s present in my life in these characters lives, now we can`t communicate our masculinity through certain gestures, the homophobia? I have to look up I have to make sure that nothing can be perceived differently. That tension that you feel is what the characters are going --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they take our phones, but somebody, there`s always a jerk in every audience, took the picture and it was released online. And I thought it was sad because it`s taken out of context.\par \par \uc2 Did that make you angry? Did that hurt your feelings? Or did you just think, okay?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: You know, I just kind of -- things happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rolled with it?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: You`ve got to -- I came here on a mission. I am, you know, working my plan and I`ve got to do a show. And I`ve got to do two shows, and you`ve just got to keep one foot in front of the other. Yes. Yes, but you know, there`s -- by and large, we`re having -- we have a very kind of sacred and wonderful interaction and terrific audiences. They are listening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. The play is really good.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: And paying attention, and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s good and you are fantastic.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And everything.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WILLIAMS: I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much for coming in.\par \par \uc2 You can see Jesse in "Take Me Out" on Broadway at Second Stage`s Hayes Theater until June 11th. You`ve got to hurry.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: After the last couple of days we`ve had, you need a little something to go soothe.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Top Gun" is one, and this is number two. You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220526ei5q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Overseas Abortion Laws\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Holly Williams \par \uc2 953 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Supreme Court plans to issue more rulings this morning as we wait for a final decision on the Justices` possibly overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In the latest CBS News poll, 50 percent of Americans say they would, in their words, care a lot if the ruling that makes abortion legal nationwide is thrown out. If that happens, it would run against decisions by other countries to make abortion more available.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams recently visited one of them, Ireland, and met a woman who talked about her own difficult experience.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMY CALLAHAN (Lives in Ireland): The doctor, during the scan got very quiet, and she turned the screen away and she said, "Is your husband here?"\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Amy Callahan (sp?) is originally from North Carolina, but has lived in Dublin with her Irish husband for 17 years. When she got pregnant with their second child in 2017, she was ecstatic until her 12- week scan.\par \par \uc2 AMY CALLAHAN: The top of the baby`s skull hadn`t formed properly so the whole brain was outside of the skull, and she said, it`s not a pregnancy. The baby is not going to live, and she said --\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: At most, she was told the baby would survive a few days after birth. She felt an abortion was the kindest choice.\par \par \uc2 AMY CALLAHAN: I just couldn`t imagine how it would -- how it would work that you would give birth only for -- only to watch the baby die and probably struggle to breathe and probably be in pain.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But in Ireland, abortion was illegal in almost all cases, forcing Amy to travel to England for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 She told us she was lucky she could afford to do so. Instead of carrying a baby for months that she`d never take home.\par \par \uc2 AMY CALLAHAN: I think it`s inhumane to me like, as a mother, it would have been but also to the baby. It just seems like an incredibly horrific way to pass away.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ireland is nearly 80 percent Catholic and used to have some of the most restrictive abortion laws in the world. So strict that in 2012, when Savita Halappanavar began to miscarry her baby at 17 weeks pregnant, she was denied an abortion. As a result she developed sepsis, which killed her.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Never again.")\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The death fueled angry protests, eventually leading to a referendum on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Keep your rosaries, over ovaries.")\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: With more than two thirds of voters in support, abortion was finally legalized in Ireland in 2018.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Yes, yes, yes.")\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: In fact, abortion is now widely available in nearly all of Europe, though with some conditions. If Roe v. Wade is overturned and some U.S. states ban abortion even in cases of rape, they will be joining a club that includes Iraq Egypt and Nicaragua.\par \par \uc2 There are still many in Ireland who oppose abortion rights, while for others, the new laws don`t go far enough.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is now legal in all circumstances in Ireland until the 12th week of pregnancy, and in very limited circumstances, until the 24th week.\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER BOYLAN (Obstetrician): In 40 years --\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Dr. Peter Boylan says women should be free to make the best decision for themselves without the law interfering at all. He`s an OB-GYN who has delivered more than 4,000 babies.\par \par \uc2 What is your advice to Americans about what the realities of living in a place where a woman can`t get an abortion?\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER BOYLAN: Living in a country the size of the United States and the size of the individual states where a woman cannot access abortion care will end up with maternal death. There is no question about that. Women will die as a consequence of this.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: He says if Roe v. Wade is overturned, it`s the poorest women in America who can`t afford to travel outside their state who will suffer the most, while women who get abortions could face prosecution.\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER BOYLAN: It`s medieval. You know, what is supposed to be the greatest democracy in the world. It`s just -- it`s a tragedy for the United States.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Amy Callahan says nobody should judge women who choose to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 AMY CALLAHAN: I think it`s a very sad move. There are so many different reasons why somebody might want to end a pregnancy. I think it`s going to have a detrimental effect on women and children.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: For CBS MORNINGS, Holly Williams, Ireland.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is such a personal decision.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, we`ll take a look at what`s not on the menu for school lunches, as districts struggle with higher costs for food and other supplies.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, I like to think of it as an invitation, too, because you can always get the morning`s news. How? By subscribing to our CBS News MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. What do you get? Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes, and everybody likes a deal? Yes, you do?\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220525ei5n0008f\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Arrives In Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nancy Cordes \par \uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re going to begin with breaking news from South Korea on President Biden just moments ago, he made remarks as he kicks off a strategically crucial trip to visit allies in Asia.\par \par \uc2 South Korea has a brand-new leader already being tested by renewed threats from the North, and of course China will be high on the agenda as well.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is traveling with the President in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The President has made this trip halfway around the world to bolster U.S. alliances at a pivotal time. And while he`s here in Asia, he`s going to sit down with the leaders of South Korea, Japan, Australia, and India as they contend with new provocations from China and North Korea.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Biden landed in Seoul and headed straight to a \uc2 Samsung\uc2  semiconductor plant with South Korea`s new President. \uc2 Samsung\uc2  is building a similar factory in Texas.\par \par \uc2 MAN: President Biden, President Yoon --\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This visit comes as President Biden seeks to establish a new economic framework in Asia, one that can challenge China`s dominance and doesn`t rely on supply from autocratic countries like Russia.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2  speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: As President Biden stops in South Korea, the White House is girding for a possible provocation from the North, with U.S. Intelligence indicating the North Koreans might try to test a long-range missile or even a nuclear missile while Mr. Biden is here.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN (U.S. National Security Adviser): We are coordinating closely with our allies in both Korea and Japan on this.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Ryu Hyun-woo is a former North Korean diplomat who defected in 2019. He says the timing of North Korea`s saber rattling is no accident.\par \par \uc2 (RYU HYUN-WOO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: "This is a chance," he says, "To send a strong message to South Korea and the U.S. to change their policies. For example, lifting sanctions on North Korea are a ban on joint military exercises between South Korea and the U.S."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: U.S. officials say neither of those things will happen as long as North Korea continues to pursue its nuclear program. Amid this heightened security situation, the President is no longer considering making a stop at the DMZ, the demilitarized zone between South and North Korea -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Nancy Cordes reporting from South Korea. We thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the fight over abortion and a new law passed in Oklahoma that is now the most restrictive abortion law in this country. Oklahoma is one of 26 states that are certain or likely to ban abortion if the Supreme Court allows it. That`s according to a leading abortion rights group.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford covers the Supreme Court for us, and Jan, what does this new Oklahoma law say? Because either depending on your point of view, you`re either very concerned or you`re cheering.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, that`s the whole issue of abortion, right? It`s so divisive. And this law, of course, would prohibit abortion at any point in pregnancy except to save a woman`s life or in the case of rape or incest and they are only if it is reported to law enforcement. It will take effect immediately after Governor Kevin Stitt signs it, which he has said he will do.\par \par \uc2 It will go further than this similar Texas law, which bans abortion as early as six weeks and gives private citizens the right to enforce it by suing abortion providers.\par \par \uc2 Now, that Texas law is in effect while it is being challenged in court and that has caused some Texas women to seek abortions in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma closed that door though earlier this month when it enacted its own six weeks ban, so this latest legislation now, it goes even further. It says abortion is banned any time after fertilization.\par \par \uc2 Emergency contraception though, the so-called "morning after pill" that would not be affected.\par \par \uc2 Now other states like Indiana and Nebraska, they are considering similar laws. All of this of course, anticipating that the Supreme Court will overturn Roe versus Wade this summer and allow each state to set its own abortion policy -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220521ei5k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 New Steps to Tackle Formula Crisis; Stock Market Selloff; Buffalo Battles Back; Abortion Rights Fight; Monkeypox Case in the U.S.; Push to Join \uc2 NATO\uc2 ; War Crimes in Ukraine; COVID Cases on the Rise; Starliner Ready for Launch; Living with Loss; Moving Mosaics; Reflecting on their Big Day; Maye Musk Live; Wellness Book Picks; \uc2 CBS\uc2 ; War Crimes in Ukraine; COVID Cases on the Rise; Starliner Ready for Launch; Living with Loss; Moving Mosaics; Reflecting on their Big Day; Maye Musk Live; Wellness Book Picks;\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nikole Killion, Jill  Schlesinger, Jericka Duncan, Debora Patta; North Korea Nuclear Concerns;  Elizabeth Palmer, Ed O`Keefe, David Agus, Kris Van Cleave, Vladimir  Duthiers, David Begnaud \par \uc2 14,142 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday, the 19th of May. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We are all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Congress takes action to increase baby formula supplies, and President Biden invokes a defense production act to increase domestic output.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): I`ll do everything possible to ensure there`s enough safe baby formula and that is quickly reaching families who need it the most.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The markets suffered their biggest drop in nearly two years amid concerns about retailers, how the White House is responding.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): It`s not something that we keep an eye on every day.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The first Russian soldier to stand trial for war crimes in Ukraine pleaded guilty to killing an unarmed civilian.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And New York investigates social media sites where the alleged Buffalo gunman spread his hate.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KATHY HOCHUL (D-NY): It`s time we fronted it, head on.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A monumental agreement in the soccer world will allow equal pay for the United States men`s and women`s national teams.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s a pack -- we`re in trouble. Oh, he hit it again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Golden State beat Dallas, Game One of the Western Conference Finals, 112-87.\par \par \uc2 MAN: One-two pitch. Swinging a fly ball. Hiura has won it with a two-run homerun.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Keston Hiura, the unlikely hero for the Brewers hits the second walk- off homerun of his career to clinch the series.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A Gatorade bath, and that`s got to feel good.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Fellow late night host \uc2 Jimmy Kimmel\uc2  has tested positive for COVID once again. Getting COVID twice is kind of my thing.\par \par \uc2 If you`re going to steal my bit, Jimmy, I have no choice, but to steal one of yours, too. It`s time for mean tweets. "Get better Kimmel `cause you can`t get worse."\par \par \uc2 "Sad to hear that COVID sidelined my third favorite Jimmy after Fallon and Dean Sausage."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going to pull through this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can tell their good friends.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are good friends. They are going to pull through it, not a lot of Jimmy`s can outrank the sausage.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s at the top of the list.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like it. That was really sweet.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a big show for it. We will be talking to a lot of remarkable and strong women today. David Begnaud visiting Amanda Kloots nearly two years after she lost her husband and Broadway star, Nick Cordero to the coronavirus. Amanda has been an advocate for others who are grieving while also grieving herself.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS (TV personality): It`ll hit me at a ton of bricks out of nowhere, and I was pulling into the driveway and a song from Journey came on that mixing and Rock of Ages, and I just -- I looked over at, you know, the passenger seat and it was empty, and hits you like a ton of bricks.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy. A lot of people know just how she feels, unfortunately. Too many people. It`s a conversation about staying hopeful. And David also just learned, discovers a little bit of breaking news about Amanda`s personal life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that 74 years old -- correction 74 years young Maye Musk is now the oldest woman ever to appear on the cover of the "Sports Illustrated" Swimsuit Edition. We`ll ask her about that and also about her son Elon`s bid to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s always good to get a mother`s point of view.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to be Mrs. Musk when I grow up. You can`t wait for her to get here.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with the new efforts to help families that are hit by America`s shortage of baby formula. It is a very big club as, you know, while the country`s largest supplier, Abbott imports millions of cans to make up for the shutdown of its Michigan plant, President Biden has taken the rare step of invoking the Defense Production Act to boost domestic supplies.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is on Capitol Hill where the \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner is testifying later today on overcoming these shortages. A lot of very angry and concerned parents, Nikole, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 And in his prepared remarks, \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner, Dr. Robert Califf says that he is acutely concerned about the availability and safety of baby formula, but that may not be enough to satisfy lawmakers who want to know how we got to this point.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I know parents all across the country are worried about finding an infant formula to feed their babies.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: President Biden says he is directing his administration to do everything possible to address the national baby formula shortage. He will invoke the Defense Production Act to ensure manufacturers have the necessary ingredients to make safe infant formula.\par \par \uc2 The emergency action allows the President to direct companies to prioritize orders for the Federal government, which has only been used about a dozen times in the last two decades, mostly during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The President is also launching Operation Fly Formula to import more product from overseas to speed up delivery.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROSA DELAURO (D-CT): We know where the product is, and we know we do not have enough.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): The bill is passed.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: House lawmakers approved two bills Wednesday night, one would provide $28 million to the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  to prevent future formula shortages.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY HARRIS (R-MD): This bill just continues the Democrat strategy of throwing money at the same bureaucrats who caused the crisis.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The other loosened restrictions for families in the Women, Infants and Children Nutrition Program.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAHANA HAYES (D-CT): I want to say to the moms struggling that we hear you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Connecticut`s Jahana Hayes received WIC benefits as a teenage mom and sponsored the measure.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAHANA HAYES (D-CT): I know what it means to run out of milk in the middle of the month, but many of these families, they had their WIC benefits, but couldn`t even use them at the store because the options that were available to them were off the shelves.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: About half of the formula purchase nationwide is through the WIC Program, which is why having more options could be critical for these families. Both of these bills now head to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 As for the \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner, he will face Congress again next week, along with executives from some of the major formula manufacturers -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So the parents of infants just hold on, formulas coming soon.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, we turn to Wall Street where stocks had their worst day in nearly two years yesterday. There was a huge selloff after major retailers announced a drop in their profits.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here to help us out like she always does.\par \par \uc2 Jill, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It seemed like stocks were starting to stabilize, why such a big selloff?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : We had two big companies, Target and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  reporting their quarterly earnings and both companies said something very similar. They are paying more for a lot of their costs. They`re not passing that all on to consumers. And at the same time, their favorite customers are coming into the stores, but they`re not buying as much.\par \par \uc2 When you think about Target and \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , they are kind of bellwethers for us, for consumers. And so now the fear is among investors. Uh-oh, high prices, people not spending as much, corporate profits going down, maybe there`s a recession. And oh, boy, did we see that selling escalate yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, we`ll talk about investors in a second, but speaking of recession, is this a sign that a recession is coming?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Sometimes we look at the stock market, it is called a forward looking indicator. But you know, stocks can sell off and actually not mean that there is a recession coming. I think we`re on the cusp right now, remember, recessions are a normal part of the business cycle. So we will have a recession.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a scary word for people here.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It is, and you know what, it is scary because when you have a recession, you can have job losses and wages might stop going up and there could be a period here where things slow down and wages are not rising enough to actually afford all the stuff that is getting more and more expensive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I see you`re wearing red, which is the color of love, danger, anger, and adventure. So for investors, which one of those feelings should they latch on to?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You know, that little voice in your head that says, "Don`t just sit there, do something."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I want you to reverse it. I want you to say, "Don`t do something, just sit there."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t panic.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Stick to your game plan. If you need money within 12 months, you`ve got to get out, but if you don`t need any money within 12 months, stay the course. You`re investing for decades in the future.\par \par \uc2 I know it`s hard to do. Deep cleansing breath.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just relax. Don`t panic.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Try.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jill, you`re the best. Dollar, dollar Jill, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, new this morning, the Biden administration is turning up the heat in the battle to protect abortion rights ahead of an upcoming Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 So let`s get straight to Ed O`Keefe at the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration, as you said is vowing to continue to fight for abortion rights as the Supreme Court`s decision on the issue nears, and today Vice President Harris is set to meet with doctors and nurses who treat those seeking the procedures.\par \par \uc2 She said to me virtually this afternoon, with abortion providers largely working in states with some of the most restrictive abortion laws, we`ll have those so-called trigger laws that would immediately ban or greatly restrict abortion access if Roe versus Wade is overturned by the Court, as is now expected.\par \par \uc2 The White House is trying to keep this issue in focus as election season continues and as we await a final ruling, the \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  are now helping provide protection to Supreme Court Justices and their families amid concerns about threats to them -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I see they are mowing the grass at the White House this morning. We could hear you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Once again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Once again, thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You`re paying for that. Taxpayer funded.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Money well spent, money well spent.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Take care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to go back to Buffalo again this morning where the suspect in the killing of 10 people at a grocery store is back in Court this morning. Members of the NFL`s \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  visited the scene of the shooting yesterday. The players and the coaches, they prayed, they served lunches, and they handed out groceries to people in the neighborhood who really need this.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan is in Buffalo with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, I bet many people are glad to see them yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 They absolutely were. It was exciting to see those professional athletes in East Buffalo. These are people who admire them of course, but more importantly, it was about acknowledging the depravity of what happened and paying their respects.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is joy, this is love, and this is unity.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  touched down in East Buffalo Wednesday to the surprise of the community. They`re pain put on pause, even if only for a moment.\par \par \uc2 JOSH ALLEN (\uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  quarterback): To be out here and to see people smiling and laughing again, that warms my heart.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In a show of solidarity, the team handed out groceries and served meals to residents now left without a grocery store after Saturday`s deadly shooting at Tops Supermarket.\par \par \uc2 Tops is giving free bus rides to its store a little over three miles away, a temporary fix for a long rooted economic divide.\par \par \uc2 LODINA CLYBURN (Buffalo, NY resident): I grew up here. There used to be a house here, a furniture store. There was a barber shop, a fish market.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But that divide wasn`t always the case says 67-year- old Lodina Clyburn.\par \par \uc2 What changed? What changed?\par \par \uc2 LODINA CLYBURN: It`s hard for me to answer that question. Some of the business didn`t come back after there were some riots and protests. Some of them didn`t come back. The tearing up of the 33.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Route 33 split the city into east and west and resulted in even more businesses losing money. Not to mention, the Bills moved away from this part of town to the suburbs.\par \par \uc2 The problems on the East Side had been mounting for decades. The majority of people who live here are Black. It`s why the alleged gunman targeted this community, but it is also why Jeremy Hoyle brought his wife and three- year-old daughter to the Memorial.\par \par \uc2 Why did you come down here?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY HOYLE (Buffalo, NY resident): My family and I, we want to be allies. I think it`s important to show our daughter that and that`s why we`re here. I am upset for our community and I am upset for our city.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And officials are also offering free mental health counseling as well as compensation for funeral costs, and just to set the scene that you`re seeing behind me in front of Court, we`ve seen a number of officials, Sheriff`s Deputies, bomb squad and tactical units getting ready for the Court appearance of that accused gunman -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Salute to the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2 . It doesn`t heal the pain, but it is a much needed distraction in the interim.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Jericka. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 A potentially dangerous virus almost never seen in the U.S. has turned up in Massachusetts. Monkeypox is normally found only in Africa, but there have been cases recently in Britain, Portugal, and also Spain. Health officials say the patient in Massachusetts traveled to Canada last month. He is hospitalized and in good condition.\par \par \uc2 Monkeypox is treatable, and most patients recover in a few weeks, but it can be deadly. The CDC says, it is preparing for possible other cases to show up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Overseas now, the Senate confirmed an Ambassador to Ukraine for the first time in three years last night, and while that might be an important symbol of support for that embattled country, President Biden`s meeting with the leaders of Finland and Sweden today represents international resistance against Russia.\par \par \uc2 Both countries have applied to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  in part to protect against Russian aggression. And the timing is also interesting, it comes as a Russian soldier pleaded guilty to war crimes. This is a first in the Ukrainian capital city of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 He had been accused of killing a 62-year-old man and as Debora Patta reports, it is all part of Ukraine`s effort to prove that Vladimir Putin`s Army has been targeting civilians.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN crying.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: A mother`s naked grief, and a wife`s anguished tears.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: In Mala Rohan, this family has gathered to bury the man they love for a second time.\par \par \uc2 (TAMARA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "God, why did you take my son," wails 83-year-old Tamara. Just two days earlier, war crimes prosecutors came to Ludmila Yorchenko`s (sp?) home to exhume her husband`s body. They are building a case to bring the perpetrators to justice.\par \par \uc2 Mikhael Yorchenko (sp?) was killed when Russian troops deliberately aimed their attack at his village. Their humble backyard now a crime scene, Ludmila shows investigators were Mikhail lay dying after both his legs were blown off.\par \par \uc2 Ludmila`s husband came out to feed the animals when he was hit by shrapnel. You see some of it there still lying on the ground, and later they buried him here in this shallow grave.\par \par \uc2 Leading the field team, Oleksander Illenkov (sp?) who swapped his business suit for body armor.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDER ILLENKOV (Prosecutor): We are civil prosecutors, but we are on war and we are doing our job.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And he is busy. Today`s exhumation, the 14th one this month. It`s grim work, but beneath that blanket is the love of Ludmila`s life.\par \par \uc2 (LUDMILA YORCHENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "He was such a big wonderful man. We lived together for nearly 30 years," she sobs. "I just can`t accept that he`s dead." Mikhail`s body was taken full forensic examination. Now, it`s come home. A final caress from Ludmila and the tormented cries of his mother, who simply cannot let go.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Debora Patta, Mala Rohan, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s very tough to hear.\par \par \uc2 Now to troubling new developments out of North Korea today. It appears the threat of more nuclear test is increasing again, even as the country deals with a major COVID outbreak. Now, this comes as President Biden heads to Asia later today.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has more on this story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Almost exactly four years ago, North Korea blew up its nuclear test tunnels at Punggye-ri.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Rigging it with explosives --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ben Tracy was one of the reporters invited to watch. It was supposed to be a step forward towards de-nuclearization, but now, North Korea is taking a step back.\par \par \uc2 JOE BERMUDEZ (CSIS senior fellow): The reality is that they didn`t destroy the tunnels. They just destroyed the portal.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Analyst, Joe Bermudez says new satellite pictures show North Korea has been digging a brand new entrance into an intact old test tunnel.\par \par \uc2 JOE BERMUDEZ: Apparently, it goes pretty far and they could have a device in place as soon as the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: A device is a nuclear bomb, which unlike the 2018 explosions will be detonated deep underground.\par \par \uc2 Under orders from leader Kim Jong-un, North Korea has been test firing missile after missile this year, designed to be nuclear tipped and some powerful enough to hit the United States.\par \par \uc2 The calculation is if they`ve got nuclear weapons, nobody is going to attack them, least of all the U.S.\par \par \uc2 JOE BERMUDEZ: Correct.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: How times have changed. Believe it or not, this is the New York Philharmonic playing in North Korea`s capital Pyongyang in 2008, back when North Korea seemed open to scaling back its nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 There was another burst of optimism in 2018 when President Trump met Kim Jong-un for talks, but they collapsed into a dangerous stalemate.\par \par \uc2 And the mood music from Pyongyang is loud and belligerent.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: A few days ago, Kim Jong-un announced North Korea has been hit with its first major COVID outbreak.\par \par \uc2 But even a lethal COVID outbreak isn`t going to deter Kim Jong-un from his nuclear ambitions, and as President Biden heads to Asia for his first official visit, the U.S. has to consider how it`s going to respond.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Elizabeth Palmer, Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, Dr. David Agus is here. He will help us understand the latest COVID developments in this country, and the numbers are concerning, it is like a third of Americans are now in areas that are considered high risk.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, the legendary head coach from Alabama, Nick Saban sounds off on the new athlete pay rules in college football. Why he is accusing other coaches of buying players.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s latest effort to get its long awaited space vehicle off the ground. It could give \uc2 NASA\uc2  a big boost.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, we`ve got your 7:30 headlines for you with Tony. Plus, David Begnaud checks in on Amanda Kloots. You remember Amanda`s story, she lost her husband, Nick Cordero to COVID nearly two years ago. "The Talk" cohost tells David Begnaud know about being hit by moments of grief and keeping Nick`s memory alive.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: 7:30 now, May 19th. Here`s a quick check of your morning headlines: Recession fears drive down stocks. That`s the top story in "The Wall Street Journal" today, after a pair of major stock indexes had their worst day since that pandemic selloff back in 2020. Those were dark days indeed.\par \par \uc2 Investors dumped shares of big retailers like Target and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  amid fears that they will not be able to keep passing on cost to consumers, not forever anyway.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the numbers, the Dow is now down 14 percent on the year. The S&P 500 down 18 percent. That is just two points shy of an official bear market.\par \par \uc2 Now, to a big update on a story we`ve been reporting on for years, a breakthrough agreement in the Senate that would help veterans expose the toxic burn pits while in uniform. Right now internal reporting from the VA shows nearly 80 percent of burn pit related disability claims are denied. This deal would shift those numbers expanding eligibility and benefits to more than 3.5 million veterans.\par \par \uc2 It would also make good on a push from President Biden himself who believes burn pits in Iraq might have caused the cancer that killed his son, Beau. A vote is expected later this summer.\par \par \uc2 Finally Pennsylvania Senate GOP primary appears headed for an automatic recount. Celebrity TV, Dr. Mehmet Oz leads his opponent, former hedge fund exec, David McCormack by just 0.1 percent, that`s over a thousand votes, just barely over a thousand votes.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump who has endorsed Oz took to his own social media company overnight to urge the doctor to quote, "Declare victory." He wants him to do it right now. This would be the seventh recount triggered by a 2004 Pennsylvania law -- and a little tidbit, Gayle, as I send it back to you, none of those recounts have ever changed the initial outcome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know I`ve heard that. And another example of every vote counts, every vote counts.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s very important to do that.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to the disturbing new -- disturbing new COVID trend across the country. About a third of Americans are in high risk areas. That`s according to the CDC. The agency`s Director is strongly recommending mask in public indoor spaces. We know how to do this, people.\par \par \uc2 This comes as daily COVID cases are up about 26 percent from the previous week, and hospitalizations are up 22 percent.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor, Dr. David Agus joins us now, to help us make sense of all of this. I don`t know if we can ever make sense of it, David, but we`re glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 You know, the numbers are really very troubling. So what`s causing it, number one? And how bad do you think it`s going to get?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: So the new omicron variants are tremendously infectious, which is why it`s just so easy to spread, so the numbers are going up. They`re going to continue to go up over the next several weeks. We`re seeing people who are either not vaccinated or vaccinated a long time ago get really sick, and they`re being hospitalized and that`s certainly a worrying trend.\par \par \uc2 That being said, we`re going to live with it. We`re not going to shut down, we`re not going to have mass mandates. But the recommendation, when you`re in a crowded place or you`re close to someone, wear a mask. Please do that. Have consideration for others. It makes sense.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it`s interesting now, when you`re on planes, they always -- they make an announcement now as part of the warm up, to please respect the decision of the person sitting behind you, whether -- beside you -- whether you wear the mask or not. Let`s all be respectful of the decisions people make.\par \par \uc2 But we`re also now starting to hear stories of people getting COVID again. You know, there was a false belief that if I got it once, and I can`t get it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I am good. But that`s not happening now.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: No, unfortunately not. I mean, this virus is changing literally dramatically. And so when it does that, it doesn`t give you immunity to the prior strain you had. So we`re seeing people who had omicron in December and January who are getting omicron again, and so the virus is changing.\par \par \uc2 It`s not your immune system is bad, or the vaccines are bad, per se. It really is the virus is changing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you`re getting a different strain of omicron?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: A different kind of omicron. So there is omicron BA.1, BA.2, BA.4, BA.5 -- all of those are different ones and they are radically different viruses.\par \par \uc2 That`s why we have to keep immunity at a very high level with that booster and we, as a country, are failing getting the booster. And I get the fatigue over it, but please get that booster if you`re high risk, especially.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let me ask you, does the booster protects you from getting COVID? Because I had a conversation with a family member. And he said, "I`m fully vaccinated. I`ve seen people that are boosted get COVID, so why do I need to get boosted if these variants are coming in, they are so rapidly changing?"\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Great question. So the vaccines prevent serious illness. They don`t prevent upper respiratory infection which is spreading the virus.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got-cha.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: So you can get mildly ill if you had that booster, but what we worry about are the hospitalizations. As hospitals are filling up now as Gayle said, we`re not doing cancer surgeries and heart surgeries and the entire country suffers as the hospitals get full and we can`t have that.\par \par \uc2 So if you get that booster, you`re going to have a mild cold, which is not bad. You don`t have that booster, you were vaccinated a year plus ago, it could be really worse or you could be hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re hearing anecdotes about people who take this five-day course of Paxlovid after they contract COVID and then they get a rebound case. Only anecdotal, but what do you know about this?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: It`s real. I mean, and so you know, we all know people who that`s happened to. I have dozens of patients that`s happened to. Paxlovid is a remarkable drug, it blocks hospitalization, 95-plus percent.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, it was very quickly developed, and probably the dose is either too low, or you need a longer course because these people get better. They test negative and then three or four days later, they start to test positive again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s why it`s frustrating, David, because you think, I got Paxlovid, I`m good. And now you`re saying, that doesn`t always work, too.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Right. But listen, it works. You didn`t get seriously ill. You weren`t hospitalized, but you did test positive again, unfortunately. And what we`re probably going to see is some newer data to make it either a 10-day course, or a slightly higher dose in some people.\par \par \uc2 It`s still working, but we`re perfecting how it is working. And it`s really important to know that three to four days later, you test positive, you are infectious. So watch out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sick of asking the question, but do you see light at the tunnel? When is it over?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: I think we are seeing light at the tunnel, right? We`re going back to a new normal here. And the new normal is, you know, we`re going to have these spikes up and down. We`re going to put masks on, take them off, but we`re going to live with the virus.\par \par \uc2 We`re not hiding from it anymore and that`s going to continue to happen and we have to stop the panic. Realize if we`re higher risk, we need that booster. Maybe we need to -- you know, if we have any symptoms, take a test and then take the drugs if we need to.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, we`re not going to panic, we`re not going to shut down again. We really have learned and science is going to enable us to go forward in a positive way, but it ain`t over.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Agus, thank you so much. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s new mission to space: Why \uc2 NASA\uc2  is counting on the Starliner finally reaching the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 And here is a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What a deal that is, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back. So don`t you go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re looking at Cape Canaveral in Florida, where \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s Starliner spacecraft is on the launch pad set for a crucial test this evening.\par \par \uc2 The aerospace giant plans to send its multibillion dollar vehicle to the International Space Station after a series of delays. Kris Van Cleave is at the Kennedy Space Center.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning. What more can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Starliner has already experienced more than two years of delays costing \uc2 Boeing\uc2  around $600 million. The aerospace giant is hoping the third time will be the charm here.\par \par \uc2 A successful mission could clear the way for Starliner to carry astronauts for the first time and get \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s Space Program back on track.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: For the third time, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s Starliner has been rolled out to the launch pad and hoisted a top of 172-foot tall Atlas V Rocket. Its mission: Autonomously dock with the International Space Station giving \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s reputation and place in the space race a much needed boost.\par \par \uc2 MIKE FINCKE (Astronaut): We need to go up fly this spacecraft without humans on it, demonstrate the automated systems and come back safely.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In 2014, \uc2 NASA\uc2  gave \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  multibillion dollar contracts to develop new spacecraft that could fly American astronauts to the ISS, ending the dependence on Russia.\par \par \uc2 Newcomer, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  could do it for $2.6 billion. \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , the tried and true aerospace juggernaut was awarded $4.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Ignition. Lift off.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: While \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  soar launching five \uc2 NASA\uc2  crews on its Dragon spacecraft to the ISS since 2020, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s Starliner stumbled.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And lift off. The rise of Starliner.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: This 2019 launch got Starliner into space, but a software glitch prevented its onboard engines from firing on time.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And we do have an off nominal insertion reported.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The spacecraft failed to reach the ISS. A second attempt last August was scrubbed after tests at the launch pad discovered 13 stuck valves in Starliner`s propulsion system.\par \par \uc2 MIKE FINCKE: We think we solved the problems, so now the crew were no longer concerned.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Astronaut Mike Fincke is training to be on Starliner`s next mission.\par \par \uc2 What are you personally watching for?\par \par \uc2 MIKE FINCKE: We`re looking for the technical side to work as well as it can. If everything looks good, we have a nice smooth the entry and landing, then it just builds up confidence and reduces risk for the next crew.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And where is your level of confidence right now?\par \par \uc2 MIKE FINCKE: MY confidence is really high. There`s the unknown unknowns, of course, but everything that we can do ahead of time, I think we`ve done.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Launch is currently set for about seven o`clock local time this evening. If all goes well, astronauts can hitch a ride on Starliner before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 If you`re wondering why does \uc2 NASA\uc2  want a capsule from \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in space? Redundancy is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 Tony, think about it this way. If you need to take the kids to the doctor and your car, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , has a flat tire, wouldn`t it be great to be able to go into the garage and hop into \uc2 Boeing\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, yes. That`s landed for me. I appreciate it. Way to personalize it, Kris Van Cleave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, Vlad Duthiers has got the stories you will be talking about today. But first, it is 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Vlad, got a lot of love at the -- CBS Paramount.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was shocked. It was a moment. It was very surreal. I was very shocked.\par \par \uc2 Because you all know, I told you my story about last time I was on the red carpet and none of the photographers lifted up their camera and one guy was like, "All right, here you go."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t believe that for a second.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Beat it kid. You got one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Movie stars, all right, speaking of movie stars and singers, let`s begin with Selena Gomez, who is getting personal about mental health.\par \par \uc2 The 29-year-old singer and actress joined First Lady Jill Biden at the White House for a Forum on Youth Mental Health hosted by MTV Entertainment yesterday. Gomez who revealed two years ago that she has bipolar disorder got emotional. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SELENA GOMEZ (Singer/actress): Bringing attention to mental health through media or just by talking about your own journeys can help. That sets the example that it is a topic that can and should be discussed freely and without shame.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the event was held ahead of Mental Health Action Day, which is today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s great she is speaking up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is an example.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Bravo, Selena Gomez.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about this now. Famed college football coach, Nick Saban is complaining about Name, Image, Likeness deals in college sports again. The man who has led Alabama to six national championships said last night that rival Texas A&M quote "bought every player" in its new recruiting class.\par \par \uc2 Saban acknowledged NIL deals are here to stay, but said it`s going to cost Alabama recruits when donors and collectors at other schools can offer them more money. He said, this will eventually lead to schools paying players outright.\par \par \uc2 Saban called out another team by name. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICK SABAN (Football coach): We have a rule right now that says you cannot use Name, Image, and Likeness to entice a player to come to your school. Hell, read about it in the paper. I mean, Jackson State paid a guy a million dollars last year that was a really good Division One player to come to their school. It was in the paper and they bragged about it. Nobody did anything about it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So in a tweet yesterday, head coach Deion Sanders said, "You best believe I will address that lie." That`s what he wrote. "Coach Saban told tomorrow."\par \par \uc2 Lots to unpack in what Nick Saban said. First of all, the audacity to say that a Division One player shouldn`t go to an HBCU, there is that. But also, my man has made millions of dollars off of the backs of young college players over the course of his career.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure. But is there some truth to a player getting money to go to Jackson State?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We don`t know. Deion Sanders said he is going to address that, but you know I hear you, Vlad, you know him saying Travis Hunter was a really good D1 player. He still is a D1 player.\par \par \uc2 He just chose an HBCU in Jackson State.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m going to say this is the world evening itself out, balancing itself out because Alabama, for so long has been a recruiting monster --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All the teams are buying players anyway when you`re building nap pods and getting them deals for shoes and clothes and everything. Recruiting is already a money involved enterprise.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes. We weren`t going to talk about the "Upfront," but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what I like about the "Upfront" yesterday, we walked, and the three of us, we`re feeling pretty good. We walked on --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: On the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Photographers are taking our picture. They go, "Guys, where`s Vlad? Where`s Vlad?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where is Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I didn`t believe you all. I came out and I did not believe you all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then they told us, "Get out of the picture. We want Vlad."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good looking crew. We had Nate. Nate actually grabbed the camera.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, they`re counting us off. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: None of the shots worked, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It worked.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s true. I talked with the photographer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, Maye Musk at 74, she is on the cover of the "Sports Illustrated" Swimsuit Issue. You go, Mrs. Musk. We will ask her about that and what she thinks about her son, that would be Elon`s bid for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil. Art can be even more powerful when you find it in places you don`t expect. Artist, Nick Cave, tells Vlad what inspired his stunning work in the Times Square subway station.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Amanda Kloots told her story to the world when she lost her husband, Nick, to COVID. Now nearly two years later, David Begnaud has a conversation with her. You won`t want to miss.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all pulling for her. But first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 REP. ROSA DELAURO (D-CT): We know where the product and we know we do not have enough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Biden invokes the Defense Production Act to address a nationwide shortage of baby formula.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : To ensure that manufacturers have the necessary ingredients to make safe, healthy infant formula here at home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Growing recession fears hammer the markets. They suffered their worst drop in nearly two years.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : There could be a period here where things slow down, and wages are not rising enough to actually afford all the stuff that`s getting more and more expensive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The alleged gunman behind the racist supermarket attack is in court today while the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  tried to help the community recover.\par \par \uc2 JOSH ALLEN: To be out here and to see people smiling and laughing again, and -- that warms my heart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh no. COVID cases are increasing again in this country. Hospitalizations are up, too. Health officials say we need stronger measures and more money to fight this virus.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT (Singer/songwriter): My mistakes led to the best things in my life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And call her Dr. Taylor Swift, the singer/songwriter has been awarded an honorary degree by \uc2 New York University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT: Getting back up, dusting yourself off, and seeing who still wants to hang out with you afterward and laugh about it, that`s a gift.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She did a great job in her speech. If you haven`t seen it, I think it`s worth going online to check it out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I will check it out. I just want to say she deserves that doctorate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I agree.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And if you`re hating on her, you better shake it off, Taylor. Shake it off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You better stop it. You better stop hating on Taylor Swift. She did great. She did a great job yesterday.\par \par \uc2 As we continue to mark the loss of one million lives to COVID in this country, we`re taking a look back at one Broadway star`s incredible, but ultimately unsuccessful fight to beat this virus.\par \par \uc2 Nick Cordero is his name. He got sick with the COVID in March 2020. That was at the height of the pandemic`s first wave. Remember those days.\par \par \uc2 His wife, Amanda Kloots shared his fight publicly for months with us. She even appeared here on CBS MORNINGS sharing the updates.\par \par \uc2 Sadly, Nick died after a courageous four-month battle.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two years after Nick`s death, our lead national correspondent, David Begnaud visited his friend, Amanda at her home in California to find out how she and her young son, there`s Elvis, how they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Elvis and mom. Hi, boo.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for having us to your house again.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Oh my God, of course.\par \par \uc2 ELVIS CORDERO (Amanda Kloots` son): He`s got a camera.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: A lot of cameras.\par \par \uc2 For the last 22 months, Nick Cordero`s song "Live Your Life" has become a sort of anthem for his widow, Amanda Kloots.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Nick Cordero`s performance.)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I`ve got the song stuck in my head every time I listen to it.\par \par \uc2 During Nick`s fight, people around the world joined in to sing that song.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of people singing "Live Your Life.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It became the title of Amanda`s book, which is now being made into a movie. And over the last nearly two years, she has embraced the idea of living her life, even as she grieves.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I love creating. I love staying busy. I don`t idle well, David.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Talk.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Last year, Amanda joined \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` "The Talk" as a cohost.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Talk.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s where she and I first connected.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I love you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I love you, too. Break a leg.\par \par \uc2 And I got a chance to learn her story.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Talk.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: To some it may seem like Amada has got it all together.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: What`s your name?\par \par \uc2 ELVIS CORDERO: Elvis Cordero.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And maybe has even moved on from the tragedy that thrust her in to the public eye.\par \par \uc2 We sometimes look at happy people and we think, "Are they happy all the time?" But when you slow down and when it gets quiet, what are the things that get to you?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: For me, it is not even when I like slow down. Right now for me, grief, it`ll hit me at a ton of bricks out of nowhere. For instance, I went to a show last night, I got home and I was pulling into the driveway and a song from Journey came on that mixing and "Rock of Ages." And I just -- I looked over at, you know, the passenger`s seat and it was empty, and it hits you like a ton of bricks.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, you like, go into the house and it`s all fine. But like, it`s those moments where you`re just like, "What happened?" And then it hits you, you`re alone. I`m alone. I`m all alone. I lost him.\par \par \uc2 ELVIS CORDERO: I lost you.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Nick Cordero home video.)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Sweet little Elvis was just a year old when his dad died due to COVID complications.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 68th Annual Tony Awards.)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Nick and Amanda met in New York City. They were both performing in the show "Bullets over Broadway." He was the lead. She was in the ensemble.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I remember thinking, "Oh, no, I like this guy." And then, I was done. I was done for.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Amanda Kloots and David Cordero home video.)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Knocking this down.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Just as the pandemic was taking hold across the country, they moved from New York to Los Angeles. It was around then, mid-March when Nick started feeling sick.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I watched him walk into Cedar Sinai Hospital and he never - - he never came out.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And thereby your journey of really sharing with the world what was happening started.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I just finished my daily visit.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Amanda began sharing updates about Nick`s health on social media and here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: He`s fighting. I see it every day.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Give us some of the TikTok`s of what you told people.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Oh my gosh. Well, he went on a ventilator. You know, went into a medically-induced coma. He got sepsis. His heart stopped. He had his right leg amputated. That was like the first couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Nick died in July 2020, and it was an alarm bell for many Americans. A healthy young man with no preexisting conditions, dying as a result of the virus.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I miss having somebody to come home to and I miss laughing with somebody.\par \par \uc2 Every time I retell this story, it is amazing how helpful it is in this grieving journey.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Really?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Grief does not stop. Loss, death, it does not end. You know, there`s no, "Oh, it`s been a year, you must be better. Right?"\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Right.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: You must have -- you cried it out, you know. That is so not the case.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She`ll tell you, being so open about her life hasn`t always been easy.\par \par \uc2 You had some people who sort of came after you and were critical and felt like you know, in the book, you divulge things like being allowed to visit him, and they thought that you know, you had sort of overlooked this privilege that you had. How did you -- how did you take that?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: You know, I -- listen, in life, people are going to come at you at all angles for anything you do. You know, I mean, they came at me when I said, I started dating again. Everybody, you know, had their opinions. I just kind of let those things roll off my back because what I would like to say to those people, is if you could have lived with me for those 95 days, you would never ever say what you just said to me. So that`s how I think about it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I must have missed the breaking news. You`ve been dating.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Oh, it`s not breaking news.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I missed that post. So have you been on a date?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I have dated. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You have?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: How did that go?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I mean, yes, that`s -- you know, listen, I --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Past tense or current?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: I`m not currently dating anyone, no. I want to love again. I want to have another person in my life. I want Elvis to have a father figure in his life.\par \par \uc2 You know, you`re not taking the place of Nick. It`s not ever that. It`s just that Nick was a part of my life.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to make him still, dada.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: As Elvis gets older and life keeps moving on, Amanda told us before we left, somewhat emphatically, Nick will always be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 It just occurred to me there must be a moment where you have said out loud, "Thank you, Nick for this" or "Thank you, Nick for that."\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Oh, every day.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There`s something about Nick which has brought about a lot of blessing in your life.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA KLOOTS: Oh, so yes. Oh, a hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, David. Amanda was always such a sparkle during that time. I think she helped a lot of people when she shared her story, because you`re right. It was such a wake-up call when Nick got sick, because he was healthy. We started looking at this and went, oh, this is really serious. And when we lost him after four months, we also couldn`t believe that because I thought she couldn`t go through all this and not come out on the other side.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s so good to see her looking so happy and that Elvis is thriving and to know grief does not stop. Grief is a living, breathing thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t end.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It`s a headline I`ll remember from the story. And to that point, I want to remind the audience, there was no vaccine when Nick got sick. And following his death, Amanda is a massive vaccine advocate. She`s like, "David, I don`t push it on people, but I let everybody know what I believe."\par \par \uc2 And she said to me about love. She said, if we could have had the conversation in the hospital, she said, "I just know Nick would have said to me. Baby, love -- love again. Go find love."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a beautiful celebration of his life, and we got some video of David Begnaud with the hula hoop. We`ve got to find the full tape.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hips still alive.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: How do you like that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. We do hope she finds love again. She`s quite a catch. She is so lovely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, we all know that. Thank you, David. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She will.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see her with that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You bet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, a new project that honors a great artist and entertains commuters at the same time. Look at that. It is 8:10, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, Maye Musk, the mother of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is breaking a new barrier by appearing on the cover of "Sports Illustrated" Swimsuit Issue. She will tell us why she doesn`t worry about age.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Deep under our studio here in Times Square is a subway station that gets more than 100,000 commuters a day. It`s typically a pretty dim and unlovely place, but it just got a little bit more comfortable, I`m happy to say. Take a look at these mosaics unveiled as part of renovations to that subway station and it`s a design from the acclaimed artist, Nick Cave, who was tasked with creating a piece to represent -- this is a big thing -- the soul of New York City.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, you spoke to Nick and you got to see these pieces up close. What did you see?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It really is the soul of New York City in this artwork. The Times Square Station practically beneath our feet here is the busiest in the entire subway system.\par \par \uc2 Now, all those commuters, 640,000 a day have something new to set their eyes on.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with Cave about what he hopes people will take away from his works of art.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Beneath the hustle and bustle of America`s busiest crossroads lies a subway platform that is as vibrant as the billboards above it.\par \par \uc2 NICK CAVE (Artist): This is how my brain -- my brain sort of feels.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thanks to division of artist, Nick Cave, whose dynamic mosaics will be seen by millions at the Times Square subway station.\par \par \uc2 NICK CAVE: We`re on the go, we`re moving quickly. But there`s also moments where everything just stop and we take in that moment. And so it`s really about that and being able to appreciate the sort of art that you`re surrounded by.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The mosaics are based on Cave`s signature Soundsuits, sculptures that were first born out of the 1992 police beating of Rodney King.\par \par \uc2 NICK CAVE: As a Black man, I was in shock. I really didn`t know what to do in that sort of moment. I am grateful, thankful that art has always been my savior. You know, it`s the place where I knew that I could go to flush out these emotions and these sort of frustrations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: His first Soundsuit was made of twigs he found in a Chicago Park. The fluid motion of the sculpture made him realize it`s something he could wear.\par \par \uc2 NICK CAVE: The moment that I put it on me, he had my gender race class. And so I was forced to look at something without judgment. A lot of the Soundsuits are built on discarded materials, something less than sort of bringing value to the unvalued and building something from nothing.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA BLOODWORTH (\uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , Director, MTA Arts and Design): There`s so much about this work, to experience and to take in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sandra Bloodworth heads the program that chooses art for the public transit system. She said Cave`s mosaics embody the experience commuters should have.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA BLOODWORTH: First thing they`re going to do reach out and touch it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA BLOODWORTH: It brings beauty underground and beauty that brings out our better nature, that brings out the best of us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that`s what you want when you`re riding the subway. Right?\par \par \uc2 SANDRA BLOODWORTH: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The mosaics are based on photographs of Cave`s Soundsuits during performances, now set in the bedrock of New York City.\par \par \uc2 You just glanced over when you were talking about it. What were you thinking of?\par \par \uc2 SANDRA BLOODWORTH: I`m just sort of thinking that I can`t believe it. I can`t believe that probably 30 years ago, I was writing in a journal, and I wrote this statement working toward what I`m leaving behind.\par \par \uc2 And to think that this is one of those moments, this is here forever.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I know, we`re writing that down, "Working toward what I am leaving behind."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s deep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is very deep.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because he really, in his artwork expresses the soul of people that he comes into contact with. And now, the commuters downstairs in the subway station will have their soul touched by this artwork.\par \par \uc2 It`s really, really remarkable. You`ll see it up close and personal. It`s unlike anything I`ve ever seen before on a mosaic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love his colors.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So much color.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love color.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And so much movement.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the actual Soundsuits that he called them Soundsuits, they`re made with just everyday objects that he finds, sometimes in the street -- twigs and pieces of cloth that he builds into these suits which are now represented in these mosaics.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, "The Uplift at Eight" with Vlad. Can`t get enough of Vlad here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, you heard the paparazzi, Gayle. The wanted more Vlad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Give the people what they want.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Giving the people what they want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should give people what they want. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To answer that burning question, Tony Dokoupil, yes, I am bringing myself in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bringing yourself into this world.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Into this new segment that we call "The Uplift at Eight." It is where we share stories that will bring some joy to your day. We can all use a little bit of that.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re talking about a simple act, making an impact on Graduation Day. This is Harrison Kramer holding up mirrors for graduates at Regent University in Virginia to help them adjust their caps and gowns before they cross the stage to get their diplomas.\par \par \uc2 He`s not a graduate`s dad. He is just a volunteer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? He is just a volunteer who has done this for nearly 20 years. Harrison says it started when he was a student. He told us he wants to make sure every single graduate feels special.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HARRISON KRAMER (Graduate of Regent): I said, "Wow, you did it," and holding up these mirrors in a lot of ways for me is like saying, "Hey, you know what? I honor you and I congratulate you." And I say, "You did it. You did it."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love this. So Harrison told us he didn`t know about that TikTok video or that it got millions of views until a coworker told him he was famous. Isn`t that great?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s "The Uplift."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Vlad. I am uplifted. Maye Musk, Elon`s mom is here in our progressive greenroom.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that are at the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, you want to kick it off for us?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. I`m in pole position. My "Talk of the Table" is the first "Upfront" under our new \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  umbrella. It`s a big old umbrella. It`s a huge event, where we unveil our new talent programming and not to brag, but we think we did okay yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The blue carpet last night featured Paramount`s biggest stars from "Star Trek`s" Picard, Sir Patrick Stewart, to "NCIS Hawaii" Vanessa Lachey, Drew Barrymore, and others. But actually my ""Talk of the Table" is about Drew.\par \par \uc2 It`s a moment we realized that we had -- how shall you say -- a twinning look?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE (Actress): What would be doing right here. Oh, that style, gunslingers. Here we go. Okay. Whose compression sock is sexier? Do you have one?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Drew. No way.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Gayle, I always knew we were soulmates.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Once again, another way to bond with you.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I love you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know it was so weird. We were walking also, and I saw Drew stop and get out her phone. I go, I don`t know what she`s doing, but I`m going to take out my phone, too. And then it turns out she hurt -- she fell, she said just walking on the sidewalk and slipped in a crack. I just jumped out of bed. I don`t know what the hell is going on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know which one is worse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say something is happening --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we`re both okay. We`re both okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, with those compression socks. My "Talk of the Table" is really kind of just an advisory from my good friend Nate here. I know you`re pushing Latoya to let you have a dog.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I told you to keep up with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, talk to me, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But there is a study out from \uc2 Sealy\uc2 , the mattress company that surveyed people and found that two-thirds of people allow pets to sleep in the bed and this is the part that`s relevant to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A majority of people in relationships prefer sleeping with the pet over the partner.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So if you get that dog, you may end up in the dog house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, you`re right. And then --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Literally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you seen Mrs. Burleson? You are not going to prefer sleeping with your pet over your wife.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no. The wife preferring the pet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, the wife.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, Nate. Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You may end up in the dog house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t believe that either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, kicked to the curb.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know about that. If I do get a puppy, the puppy is going to sleep on the floor, not in the bed. That`s not how we roll in the Burleson house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, you`re very confident in your abilities, right? You keep your wife happy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s all --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is all in good fun because I talked to Latoya the other night and you`re not getting that dog.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk about it. That dog is going to be downstairs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s not happening. You know what I am saying?\par \par \uc2 All right, so I have a really good "Talk of the Table." My "Talk of the Table" is the four groundbreaking women on the cover of "Sports Illustrated" Swimsuit issue. That`s right. It`s on the newsstands today. They are music superstar, Ciera, Kim Kardashian, model Yumi Nu, and of course Maye Musk, the mother of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 74 years young, Musk is making history as the magazine`s oldest swimsuit cover star. Her modeling career spans half a century and she has broken barriers in the industry before.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look at the moment Musk learn she made the cover.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I needed to be here to tell you.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK (Elon Musk`s mother): Okay.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That you`re on the cover of the "Sports Illustrated."\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Swimsuit -- oh, yay, yay.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so cool. Hello, cover girl.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Joins us in the studio, Maye Musk, go, you. So listen, when they first even invited you to participate in the shoot. You didn`t even know you`re going to be the cover. But when they first asked you to be on the shoot, did you think, "Great idea. I can`t wait to do this."\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Not at all. I said why? And then it was my CAA agents, they said -- they can`t believe it, but they are so excited that they want me. And then they said they`ll fly me to Belize. And now, my daughter Tosca was with me and she had shot a movie in Belize. She said, "You`ve got to go, if only for Belize." So then I said, "Okay." Then I went.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Maye, what is your beauty routine? And please don`t tell me you drink a lot of water. What is it?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: I`m a dietitian. So, I eat very well and I drink when I`m thirsty. And I certainly don`t carry water around with me all the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you feel self-conscious about your body? You know, this is the swimsuit issue. The pictures are great. Let me just show --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there`s a two piece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you were -- I am going to wrong -- I had marked it. Are you self-conscious about your body?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t look for it. There it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, it is on screen. I don`t have to show it up. Were you self-conscious about your body?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Well, I`ve never been considered swimsuit model. Nobody booked me for swimsuits. I`ve been modeling since I was 15. So that`s a long time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at those pictures, Maye. Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: And so then, I thought well, I went there, but then once I did the first swimsuit and they all said, "Oh great, great, great." I`m saying, okay, so I said to them, "Please PhotoShop me like crazy."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but they didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you don`t need PhotoShop the way you are right now.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: But then you have good lighting here to.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How did your kids respond when they saw you on the cover?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: They said "Congrats." They thought it was amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, speaking of your kids, you mentioned --\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Yes, yes. Tosca says, "Argh."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, Tosca describes you as unstoppable.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just when you think she`s getting older, and you say, "No, not yet." Yes. She says you`re unstoppable. Do you feel unstoppable?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: I`m unstoppable. I mean, I am just getting started.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you said at 74, this is the happiest you`ve ever been. What is the key to happiness?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: And that you don`t mix with nasty people anymore. You don`t tolerate that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: You can say no to things. You know, before that, you`re just very polite and you do things you don`t want to do and I just live alone with my dog. The dog makes me very happy. And then I fly around so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re enjoying life.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: I am in demand.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re in demand.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So speaking of nasty people and things in demand, there`s a social media platform that exists. It`s called \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes. I heard of it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People think there`s some nastiness that goes on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Somebody recently put a bid to buy it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is also in demand and there`s a guy named \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , your son who is trying to buy the thing. I`m curious, do you ever give him business advice? Do these conversations come up?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Yes, I did tell him don`t do a car company, electric car company and a rocket company, and then --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You did?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Yes, and a solar company. I said, just choose one. You`d be working too hard at \uc2 PayPal\uc2 , so now you need to just choose one. And so obviously, he doesn`t listen to me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, I work in TV. I played in the \uc2 NFL\uc2  for 11 years. Oftentimes, my mom will call me and she`ll say, "Son, what did you just say? You shouldn`t have said that."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And why did you do that? Have you ever called Elon and said, "I saw what you wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or heard what you said, dial it back?"\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You allow him to choose his words? Say what he wants.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: You know, he -- you have to be responsible for whatever you say. And yesterday, I was tweeting out all the bots that have seven followers, but a thousand likes and they are all malicious comments.\par \par \uc2 But these people don`t have any followers, how do they get a thousand people liking their malicious statements, and then retweeting it 400 times, and retweeting it with saying more malicious things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Seems like an attack.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: There`s something organized that`s going on there. You can`t just make that up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re active on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are there other changes you hope your son brings to the platform?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Actually, I haven`t given much thought other than yesterday, I was angry. #AngryMom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing wrong with that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Angry. No, nothing wrong with that at all. You are fiercely protective of him. He recently tweeted, "If something happens to me and I die of unforeseen circumstances," which I thought was very troubling when I read it. What do you say -- are you worried about his safety? What do you think when he tweets things like that?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Well, when I see all the malicious comments on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , then I really do worry about him. Yes. I mean, a mother always worries about her children. I`ve got three, but he is the one I`m worried about. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elon is a very polarizing figure, and people are interested in him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I`m curious about what he was like when he was younger.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When you saw him --\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: It is in my book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know, but we have viewers right now.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: I left some copies here for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`d love -- thank you for that. But what was it like when you looked in his eyes when he was a child or a teenager? Did you see this man who was eventually going to have such an impact on the world?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Well, at three, I said, "You`ve got to get in." I took him to nursery school, because I already had two other little babies. And I said - - and they said, but he is a bit young to send him to nursery school. And I said, but he`s a genius. And they rolled their eyes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Every parent says that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You thought he was a genius at three. Why? Why did you think that?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: He would converse with me and make sense, and he was so observant of things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: And very curious. And yes, so I was right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have a nickname for him. Do you have a pet name for him? I`m curious.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: We used to call him genius boy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Genius boy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When it comes to geniuses, I hear that they have tremendous strengths. But they`re also --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s like after he spills the milk on the counter. Okay, genius. Get a paper towel.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Are there any weaknesses, and I don`t want to say weaknesses. It`s a weird word to use. But when you look at geniuses, oftentimes there`s things that they have hang ups about, is there anything that --\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: You know that when you have a genius, they could be in the basement doing genius things and never apply it. And fortunately, Elon also has a business brain and he realizes that what he wants to do, he needs to apply it and he has to go through a lot of hardships to fight the fossil fuel industry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually think he`s brilliant and just always thinking outside the box.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he`s a doer. He doesn`t just have an idea, but he wants to execute his ideas.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I want to end with you because I`m so -- I`m so in awe of this cover. I`m so proud of you. I want to be you when I grow up, Maye Musk.\par \par \uc2 But this cover is a symbol of beauty for so many people when we look at it. When do you feel you`re most beautiful?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Well, first of all, with this cover, I want women to be able to walk on the beach in a swimsuit and not be embarrassed about their bodies, because we are like that, and men will walk out and they will be obese, and then they have kind of swim suit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they are wearing Speedo.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: A Speedo, and then they are arrogant and confident, I`m saying yes, and we don`t insult them. But man, when we walk down the beach, we get comments.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: So we`ve just got to get --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those are weak men, by the way.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Thank you. I agree with you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I agree with you a hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Bravo. And thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can see more at swimsuit.com. The issue is on newsstands today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today, you can get your copy.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it`s 8:40. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sex and the City.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s Charlotte from "Sex and the City" embarrassed to shop for self-help book, but attitudes have changed self-health and wellness books have skyrocketed in popularity.\par \par \uc2 For Mental Health Awareness Month, Amazon Books editorial director, Sarah Gelman joins us right now with some books to help improve your life.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN (Amazon Books editorial director): Great. How are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So things have evolved. People aren`t afraid to walk in that aisle, look at the Self-Help Section and say, "You know what this book is for me, and I`m going to proudly grab it and then go buy it" in front of the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Nate, I do love that clip. Because you look at that girl, you go "I don`t want to be like that."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, and later, Charlotte orders that book on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So why do you think, things have changed?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: You know, self-help has really been rebranded as wellness. And it`s now this idea that there`s nothing wrong with you, you don`t need help. It`s really wellness is you`re great who you are and we`re just going to enhance that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your first recommendation is a book about communication. Tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. This is called "Speaking in Thumbs: A Psychiatrist Decodes Your Relationship Texts So You Don`t Have To," by Mimi Winsberg. This book totally surprised me. It`s really about language and communication.\par \par \uc2 And Dr. Winsberg realized that the way that people start dating these days is through messaging, whether in a dating app or texts, and she is helping us get better at this.\par \par \uc2 So she teaches us things like when you put too much punctuation in a text, you`re over eager. But too little, and you`re dismissive.\par \par \uc2 And even though this is about relationships, to me, it`s really for anyone. We all text, think about texting your family or your boss. We all have a lot to learn, and this book is so fun and helpful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s interesting, if you put too much punctuation, you`re over eager.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, think about like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I always do a lot of exclamation --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, exclamation points -- four is like -- reel it back. Reel it back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So five is too many.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Five is too many. I think so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another book you recommend is about how people think about sorrow and longing.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s that one?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: "Bittersweet" by Susan Cain. She wrote a book about introverts called "Quiet." And this book really talks about the subtitle is "How Sorrow and Longing Make Us Whole." So her argument is really that Americans are so focused on positivity. It is turn your frown upside down. It`s Happy Birthday. And other cultures are okay with sadness.\par \par \uc2 Think about how we celebrate the day of the dead, and think about how when you`re sad, a sad song feels so good, or you`re heartbroken you want to curl up and watch "The Notebook" on the couch. There`s a reason why those things feel good in the moment and her argument is really that without the darkness, we can`t appreciate the light.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we are not comfortable with sadness though. We want to rush through it.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: But we should be. We should be.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We want to make it go away.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love -- I love the kind of mash up books, so for people who like Brene Brown and Peloton workout, you recommend?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t see the connection there.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: "Speak" by Tunde Oyeneyin. So Tunde is a Nigerian-American Peloton instructor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is fantastic, too.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I mean, yes, look at her face. She looks like she doesn`t have a care in the world. But she`s actually had a really challenging life. And this is what this book is about.\par \par \uc2 So she was an obese teenager -- child and teenager. She had to work really hard to feel comfortable in her body. She also lost her father and a brother at a fairly young age and the first time she auditioned for Peloton, she didn`t make it.\par \par \uc2 I love this book for people that look at \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and think like these people have no troubles in the world. This book definitely proves that wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Brene Brown?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, I think fans of Brene Brown and also Glennon Doyle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, when it comes to offering a book like this to someone, do you recommend it before you buy it? Or do you just say, "You know what, I`m going to send you something. I hope you like it."\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Oh, I`ve never done that. I don`t know. I recommend books all the time. So, I feel like people aren`t offended when I say, maybe you should read this book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When you just recommend it.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Maybe you should read it. I have been recommending "Speaking in Thumbs" to basically everyone I`ve talked to.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when it comes to complex trauma, you`ve chosen a memoir.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought that was an interesting choice.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. "What My Bones Know" by Stephanie Foo. This is great for people that love "The Body Keeps the Score." And to me, it`s a more modern take on that, and it is very personal.\par \par \uc2 So she is looking at -- she writes about the trauma she experienced as a child. Her diagnosis of complex PTSD, which just basically affects people that have undergone trauma for long periods of time and she attacks it like a problem and works to heal herself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How are you able to talk about so many different books with no notes, no nothing so coherently? How do you do that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Smarty pants.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, smarty pants.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Doing it at the book reports section.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, I marvel. You must have been a very good book report - -\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All A`s.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Thank you for saying that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sarah Gelman, you are the best.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way, I would argue that all books are wellness books in a sense because if you put your phone down and just do something else, it`s good for the mind, it is good for the soil.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Totally agree with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Food for the soul. You`re right about that. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Before we go, our friend and colleague, Adriana`s baby, Isabela reacted recently to the trumpets at the top of our show. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Baby Isabela shrieking at CBS MORNINGS intro.)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She loves the trumpets.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the trumpet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We love the trumpets, too, Isabela.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is eight months old. I am glad she approves.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scream of love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bye, Isabela. Bye, Isabela.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220519ei5j0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Oklahoma Passes The Nation`s Strictest Bill In The Country, Prohibiting Nearly All Abortions Starting At Fertilization\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 602 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, now to the fight over abortion rights ahead of a landmark Supreme Court decision that could overturn Roe vs. Wade. Oklahoma legislators passed an anti-abortion bill outlawing abortion from the moment of fertilization. It would be the most restrictive law in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford with the details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voice-over): The legislation would prohibit abortion at any point in pregnancy, except to save a woman`s life or in the case of rape or incest. It would take effect immediately after Governor \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2  signs it, which he has said he will do.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KEVIN STITT (R-Oklahoma): We believe life begins at conception, and we are going to protect life in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The law goes further than a similar Texas law, which bans abortion there after six weeks and gives private citizens the right to enforce it by suing abortion providers, with $10,000 awards. It`s in effect while being challenged in court, causing some Texas women to seek abortions in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 But Oklahoma closed that door last month, when it enacted its version of the six-week ban. Today`s would ban abortion before six weeks. And with the Supreme Court likely to overturn Roe vs. Wade, other conservative states are considering similar laws. More than a dozen already have so-called trigger laws that would immediately ban or greatly restrict abortion if the court overturns Roe.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  met virtually today with abortion providers in some of those states just after Oklahoma passed its near total ban.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): It`s outrageous, and it`s just the latest in a series of extreme laws around the country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Jan joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of anticipation about that Supreme Court ruling. When could that come down?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, it`s been more than two weeks since a draft of that opinion leaked that showed, at least at some point, a majority of the justices were ready to overturn Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 When a final decision comes, it could be any time. We don`t know. We also don`t know what the final vote is going to look like or whether one of the justices may have changed their minds.\par \par \uc2 But, in the meantime, Norah, there has been a significant increase, according to law enforcement, in the number of abortion-related violent threats, which means that the court and the justices are now under additional security.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But a decision could come as soon as Monday?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Potentially.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Potentially.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jan Crawford, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, here in the nation`s capital today, President Biden met with the leaders of Sweden and Finland to discuss their bids to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 . The unanimous approval of member nations is required to join the alliance. And while the president offered his strong support for the proposal, \uc2 NATO\uc2  member Turkey remains adamantly opposed.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220520ei5j000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Stock Market Selloff\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Jill Schlesinger \par \uc2 813 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, we turn to Wall Street where stocks had their worst day in nearly two years yesterday. There was a huge selloff after major retailers announced a drop in their profits.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here to help us out like she always does.\par \par \uc2 Jill, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It seemed like stocks were starting to stabilize, why such a big selloff?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : We had two big companies, Target and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  reporting their quarterly earnings and both companies said something very similar. They are paying more for a lot of their costs. They`re not passing that all on to consumers. And at the same time, their favorite customers are coming into the stores, but they`re not buying as much.\par \par \uc2 When you think about Target and \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , they are kind of bellwethers for us, for consumers. And so now the fear is among investors. Uh-oh, high prices, people not spending as much, corporate profits going down, maybe there`s a recession. And oh, boy, did we see that selling escalate yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, we`ll talk about investors in a second, but speaking of recession, is this a sign that a recession is coming?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Sometimes we look at the stock market, it is called a forward looking indicator. But you know, stocks can sell off and actually not mean that there is a recession coming. I think we`re on the cusp right now, remember, recessions are a normal part of the business cycle. So we will have a recession.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a scary word for people here.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It is, and you know what, it is scary because when you have a recession, you can have job losses and wages might stop going up and there could be a period here where things slow down and wages are not rising enough to actually afford all the stuff that is getting more and more expensive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I see you`re wearing red, which is the color of love, danger, anger, and adventure. So for investors, which one of those feelings should they latch on to?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You know, that little voice in your head that says, "Don`t just sit there, do something."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I want you to reverse it. I want you to say, "Don`t do something, just sit there."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t panic.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Stick to your game plan. If you need money within 12 months, you`ve got to get out, but if you don`t need any money within 12 months, stay the course. You`re investing for decades in the future.\par \par \uc2 I know it`s hard to do. Deep cleansing breath.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just relax. Don`t panic.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Try.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jill, you`re the best. Dollar, dollar Jill, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, new this morning, the Biden administration is turning up the heat in the battle to protect abortion rights ahead of an upcoming Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 So let`s get straight to Ed O`Keefe at the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration, as you said is vowing to continue to fight for abortion rights as the Supreme Court`s decision on the issue nears, and today Vice President Harris is set to meet with doctors and nurses who treat those seeking the procedures.\par \par \uc2 She said to me virtually this afternoon, with abortion providers largely working in states with some of the most restrictive abortion laws, we`ll have those so-called trigger laws that would immediately ban or greatly restrict abortion access if Roe versus Wade is overturned by the Court, as is now expected.\par \par \uc2 The White House is trying to keep this issue in focus as election season continues and as we await a final ruling, the \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  are now helping provide protection to Supreme Court Justices and their families amid concerns about threats to them -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I see they are mowing the grass at the White House this morning. We could hear you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Once again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Once again, thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You`re paying for that. Taxpayer funded.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Money well spent, money well spent.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Take care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220520ei5j0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For May 15, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Chen, Charlie D`Agata, Robert Costa, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,188 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation. I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot to get to today, but we begin with breaking news from Buffalo, New York, where an 18-year-old male is in police custody following a mass shooting that killed 10 and injured three others.\par \par \uc2 That massacre is being investigated by the Justice Department as a hate crime and an act of -- quote -- "racially motivated violent extremism."\par \par \uc2 President Biden referred to it as an act perpetrated in the name of white nationalist ideology, and he has called for an end to hate-fueled domestic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 CBS News correspondent Nancy Chen reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: A shooter, a mass shooter.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voice-over): A frantic scene after a gunman opened fire at a Tops supermarket Saturday afternoon starting in the parking lot. Police say the heavily armed suspect shot four people outside, killing three, before moving inside the store.\par \par \uc2 GRADY LEWIS (Witness): When I first saw him shooting, he shot a woman, he shot a deacon, he shot another woman, and then he went in the store and started shooting again.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: That`s when police say he encountered a retired police officer working as a security guard, who fired multiple shots that hit the gunman, but didn`t impact him because of his tactical gear. The suspect then killed the guard.\par \par \uc2 Officers say the suspect held a gun to his own neck after encountering police, but eventually surrendered.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GARCIA (Erie County, New York, Sheriff): This was pure evil. It was straight-up racially motivated hate crime.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Of the 13 people shot, 11 were black.\par \par \uc2 The suspect, 18-year-old Payton Gendron, is believed to have posted a hate- filled manifesto shortly before the shooting, which he also livestreamed on the social media platform Twitch, authorities say.\par \par \uc2 A law enforcement source told CBS News the suspect allegedly yelled racial slurs during the attack.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-New York): To see that sense of security shattered by an individual, a white supremacist, who has engaged in an act of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Gendron is from Conklin, New York, about three-and-a-half- hours from Buffalo. He was arraigned hours after the attack on a first- degree murder charge, pleading not guilty.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: A first-degree murder charge carries a sentence of life in prison without parole if convicted.\par \par \uc2 The suspect is being held without bond and is set to appear in court again on Thursday -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nancy Chen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We want to go now to the governor of New York, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: Good morning. I`m happy to be here, to be on this show, but it`s very a tragic day for all of us here in Western New York. It`s my hometown of Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, our condolences to you and to that community.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned about further violence in your state?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: Well, we are taking proactive measure to make sure that we`re monitoring all social media platforms, because this -- this information was out there.\par \par \uc2 This was on a manifesto that was written a while back. And so we`re very concerned about what other information is perpetrated out there on social media platforms and are out there being disseminated globally.\par \par \uc2 So, this information from yesterday`s attack is already out there. It was livestreamed. The intent of this individual was telegraphed in advance. So I`m calling on social media platforms to be making sure that they`re doing a better job monitoring the hate speech that`s out there, especially when it`s directed against populations and comes under the guise of white supremacy terrorism, which is exactly what happened here in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to come back to that in a moment, but I want to ask about the weapon that this shooter used.\par \par \uc2 You`ve said it was legally obtained. You`ve also said that the shooter had been at one point under the surveillance of medical authorities because of past comments he had made about carrying out a shooting. How was he allowed to buy and to hold on to that weapon?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: That is exactly what`s being investigated now.\par \par \uc2 I understand that he wrote something when he was in high school and that that was being investigated. So we`re going to get to the bottom of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, it`s possible that he should not have been sold that weapon? Is that an oversight in the state?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: Well, no, we don`t know that. We don`t know that right now. But I`m going to get to the bottom of and find out right now.\par \par \uc2 This would have happened a little while back. He`s 19 years old. Apparently, he was investigating when he was a high school student, brought to the attention of the authorities. He had a medical evaluation based on something he had written in school. And so we`re going to find out what happened in the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I understand.\par \par \uc2 I know you just mentioned going online and taking what`s out there in the social media space seriously. You`ve called it a feeding frenzy for white supremacy. How do you actually regulate this without impeding on free speech?\par \par \uc2 You have a number of media and social media companies with big offices in your state. Specifically, what are you asking them to do?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: No, we want them to stay in our state.\par \par \uc2 We also want them to be more vigilant and use the resources they have to hire more people, change their algorithms, be able to identify the second that this hate speech appears, and let there be a determination by law enforcement quickly.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement also monitors this as well. I mean, we have the \uc2 FBI\uc2  monitoring. We also have state police. So we need a multifaceted approach. We need vigilance, not just law enforcement, but also from the platforms that are allowing this to spread.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: They have a responsibility as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Justice Department has called this an act of racially motivated violent extremism. You used a sharper word. You said white supremacist terrorism.\par \par \uc2 I know your state classifies assault based on race or religion as a terror attack. There`s no federal statute that does that. Should there be?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Federal terrorism -- there are domestic terrorism laws on the books. This can be prosecuted under state or federal laws right now. It started with our district attorney at the state level. So this individual is not going to see the light of day again, whether it`s under federal prosecution or state under our domestic terrorism laws or just murder one.\par \par \uc2 This person murdered ten innocent victims in our community just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor, good luck to you. Thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the mayor of Buffalo, Byron Brown.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Mr. Mayor. And our thoughts, our condolences are with you and your community.\par \par \uc2 How are you all handling this?\par \par \uc2 BYRON BROWN (Mayor of Buffalo New York): Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 It is obviously very painful, very raw, very fresh. We`re wrapping our arms around the families of those whose lives were lost. We`re standing strong as a community and working to not let this horrible act of hate detract from us being a loving, warm, welcoming community.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The shooter was allegedly motivated by white supremacist ideology.\par \par \uc2 I know that you are the first African-American mayor of Buffalo, which, as a city, has been called very segregated, if not one of the most in the country.\par \par \uc2 How do you all unite in the wake of something like this?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BYRON BROWN: We`re a midsize American city of over 278,000 people, and this part of the city, 80 percent African-American, but diverse, with people of many different backgrounds living in this community.\par \par \uc2 We are certainly saddened that someone drove from hundreds of miles away, someone not from this community, that did not know this community, that came here to take as many black lives as possible, who did this in a willful, premeditated fashion, planning this.\par \par \uc2 But we are a strong community. And we will keep moving forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is there an ongoing threat? Are your residents safe today?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BYRON BROWN: I think the question that we need to ask ourselves, are any residents safe in this country anywhere?\par \par \uc2 We have to focus on sensible gun control. That said, after all of these mass shootings that have taken place in this country for different reasons, year in and year out, month in and month out, week in and week out, let Buffalo, New York, be the last place that this kind of mass shooting happens.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mr. Mayor, good luck to you and thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BYRON BROWN: Thank you very much, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the economy.\par \par \uc2 And we learned this week that the cost of things like food and energy rose compared to last month.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of Transportation Pete Buttigieg joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Mr. Secretary.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG (U.S. Transportation Secretary): Good morning. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: As a representative of the administration, I do want to ask you a little bit about this reaction to the events in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 You were once a mayor. Do you think that there should be a federal law criminalizing domestic terrorism? The president used that phrase, but that`s not really on the books.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, I will let the president speak to the legal outlook with regard to the definition of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 But whether it`s called that legally or not, this was terrorism, this was hate. And this would be a good day for every politician in this country left, right and center, every media figure in this country, left, right and center, to come out and unequivocally condemn white nationalism, so-called Replacement Theory, and any other hateful ideology that could have contributed to something like this, before it happens again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But should there be a federal statute that elevates things when those terms you just threw out there -- should there be an association directly with terrorism?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Again, we don`t know, obviously, all of the details that fit the legal definitions.\par \par \uc2 What we know is that somebody traveled a long distance with an AR-15 to hunt human beings, to hunt black people. And we need to make sure that we root out that kind of hate and, by the way, that we have a conversation about the availability of the kind of tactical weaponry that he seems to have had.\par \par \uc2 And yet we seem to be having that conversation over and over and over again as a country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you a little bit on a personal note.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been talking about this baby formula shortage nationwide that`s been ongoing now for months. You have infants at home. Do you have problems getting a hold of formula?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Yeah, this is very personal for us.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got two nine month old children. Baby formula is a very big part of our lives. And like millions of Americans, we`ve been rooting around stores, checking online, getting in touch with relatives in other places where they don`t have the same shortages to see what they can send over. And we figured it out. We`re all set, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 But I think about what that would be like if you`re a shift worker with two jobs, maybe you don`t have a car. You literally don`t have the time or the money to be going from store to store. That`s why this is such a serious issue. And that`s why it`s getting attention at the highest levels, including, of course, direct involvement by the president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, and this is going to be an issue Congress takes up this week.\par \par \uc2 I know the president said more action is coming, but this has been ongoing for months. There are supply chain issues already. Then you have the issue with this one plant, Abbott, whistle-blower in September, February, the recall. It`s May. Why has it taken so long? And why did the president on Friday seem to say that it was new information to him?\par \par \uc2 He said, if we`d been better mind readers, I guess we could have done something earlier.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, look, the administration acted from day one after the recall, taking steps like creating more flexibility for the WIC program to help rebalance the availability of formula in the States. There are more actions that are underway, including looking at imports.\par \par \uc2 But, fundamentally, we are here because a company was not able to guarantee that its plant was safe. And that plant has shut down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that is the federal government`s job as regulators to help ensure safety of the plant.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: As regulators, yes, but let`s be very clear.\par \par \uc2 This is a capitalist country. The government does not make baby formula, nor should it. Companies make formula. And one of those companies, a company which, by the way, seems to have 40 percent market share, messed up, and is unable to confirm that a plant, a major plant, is safe and free of contamination.\par \par \uc2 So the most important thing to do right now, of course, is to get that plant in Michigan up and running safely. And that`s the work that`s going on between the company and the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . It`s got to be safe and it`s got to be up and running as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 But this is the difference between a supply chain problem, in other words, a problem about moving goods around, and a supply problem, which has to do with whether they`re being produced in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Now, the administration`s also been working with other companies to try to surge their production. That`s led to an increase in production, which is helping to compensate. But, at the end of the day, this plant needs to come back online safely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`ll have more on that later in the show, but -- because I know you`re not the \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner.\par \par \uc2 Let me talk to you about the things you are involved more in, which is supply chain and procurement.\par \par \uc2 How is the administration making sure that those essential ingredients that are actually required for something like formula are actually available?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: So, a shortage of ingredients is not what led to the shutdown of the facility, right?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, but it is a factor that has led to price inflation. It`s one of the factors among many that has been blamed for months of problems with baby formula even before the recall in February.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Right, but America has the productive capacity to create the baby formula that we need. I think what...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you`re trying -- bringing it in from Europe right now.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Right.\par \par \uc2 But that`s because, again, we`ve got four companies making about 90 percent of the formula in this country, which we should probably take a look at. And one...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that an oligopoly?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Yeah, I mean, it`s basically a series of monopolies that have added up into enormous market concentration.\par \par \uc2 By the way, this is an issue the president has been talking about in area after area after area, whether we`re talking about fertilizer, whether we`re talking about other things in our agriculture sector, or more generally.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there are contracts there too...\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... because this is also part of -- I`m using the term food stamp program.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it`s a part of a government assistance program.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Which is exactly why, again, from day one after the recall...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This isn`t just a private sector problem, is my point. The federal government is directly involved in some of these arrangements.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: A plant shutting down because a company can`t assure that it is physically safe from contamination is the responsibility of the company.\par \par \uc2 The responsibility of the regulator is to ensure, as they take steps to get it ready, that it will in fact be safe when it comes back online.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have so much more to get to as well.\par \par \uc2 I want to get you on inflation. Gas prices, highest ever price in the country, $4.45 a gallon national average. Are you asking Americans to drive less?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: No, what we`re asking Americans to do is to obviously recognize that we`re working this issue because we`re feeling it, too. I mean, all of us see that pain at the pump.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Should Americans drive less?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Look, what we want to do is create options for Americans to be able to get where they`re going more affordably.\par \par \uc2 It`s why we up the fuel economy standards, so that, by the 2026 model year, the cars will be so much more efficient, if you have a gas car, that you -- if you used to have to fill up four times a month, it might be three now.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we`re also working to make electric vehicles more affordable, because that has a huge benefit, especially in terms of protecting families from these kinds of price volatility.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Those battery components are also a supply chain issue.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, are we going to take that as an excuse to do nothing and do the same thing forever, or are we going to take that as an issue to work? We`re taking it as an issue to work on. But, right now, with existing technology, we know that we can get more Americans into these vehicles.\par \par \uc2 And we also know, right, that with gas prices on the rise -- and the president`s acted with the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. He`s acted with ethanol flexibility to try to stabilize those prices. But we also know, right now, that we could be lowering other costs for Americans.\par \par \uc2 And this is the most important thing I think we need to take a hard look at right now, when we`re fighting inflation with everything that we`ve got, that we made the case to lower the cost of insulin to $35 and faced basically unified Republican opposition, tried to lower the cost of prescription drugs, and were blocked from doing so by congressional Republicans, who then come around and want to talk about inflation, without offering a plan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the public isn`t interested in things that didn`t work, right, or didn`t pass or aren`t law. They want to know what`s going on right now,at their kitchen table and in their pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well I think they want to know what Congress is going to do to lower their costs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let`s talk about that because I want to...\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: And we`re making the case for that to happen...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about what the administration thinks in regard to what some congressional Democrats, like Senator Warren -- Speaker Pelosi also says she`s putting forward a bill about price gouging by companies and banning unconsciously excessive pricing.\par \par \uc2 This has been called dangerous and misguided nonsense by the Obama administration economic adviser Jason Furman. Do you agree that it is nonsense?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: I`m not familiar with all of the details of that legislation.\par \par \uc2 But what I can tell you from an administration perspective is that there is guidance going out to crack down on price gouging where we see it. If price gouging arises in the formula market or the fuel market...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that what -- how much of a factor is that, though?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Look, one thing that we know is that we`re in this moment, right, where we`re feeling -- Americans are feeling the pinch on product after product.\par \par \uc2 And some companies have become ridiculously profitable, notably including oil companies, which have specifically said they`re not going to use the permits and the production capacity that they have. Why would they? They`re incredibly profitable right now. They`re not complaining. They`re not unhappy about the situation.\par \par \uc2 The public is unhappy. The president is unhappy. And we`re taking action.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re going to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Buttigieg, good to have you here in person.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And good luck with the baby formula.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we turn now to Dr. \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 , former Trump \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  board member. Good morning to you, Doctor.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2  (Former \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to tap into your perspective, as someone who ran the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You just heard the administration`s view that this baby formula shortage is really the failure of one company here and that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  isn`t necessarily fully responsible for ensuring things at that plant. I wonder how you respond to that.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, these look, were persistent problems that appear to have been handled poorly, certainly by the company.\par \par \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t exert all the oversight that they could have of that facility. There were known problems with that facility going back many years. There were findings on previous inspections.\par \par \uc2 The agency had a 34-page whistle-blower report in hand making pretty serious allegations that there was data falsifications information -- data falsification information withheld from inspectors. So these should have prompted more aggressive action earlier.\par \par \uc2 I think, now that that facility has been shut by Abbott and production isn`t going on, it`s going to be hard to clear the facility. The overhang of allegations of data falsification are going to be the kinds of allegations that are hard for the agency to clear, even if they`re not able to prove a causal relationship between the infections that we saw in children and the facility itself, which -- which, so far, the agency hasn`t been able to prove, and they may never be able to prove that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 You just said that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t do all it could. We know they didn`t inspect the Abbott facility back in 2020. They stopped inspecting some places during the pandemic that weren`t mission-critical.\par \par \uc2 Isn`t baby formula mission-critical? How does the facility not get inspected by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, look, it is mission-critical.\par \par \uc2 And during the government shutdown, we actually preserved inspections of infant formula plants because of the risks associated with those facilities.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you were in office.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : And that facility probably should have been inspected, especially -- when I was in office -- especially given the fact that there had been prior findings there.\par \par \uc2 The fact that \uc2 FDA\uc2  went into that facility this year and found five different strains of Cronobacter, that is a serious concern. And it doesn`t appear to have been a state-of-the-art facility, based on the findings in that 483.\par \par \uc2 So, they should have been under close supervision. Look, we have -- we have sort of the worst of both possible worlds right now. We have a regulatory scheme that is stringent enough that it does create obstacles to getting into the market for new entrants. There`s only been one new entrant in the last 15 years that`s a domestically based manufacturer, a company, ByHeart.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, it doesn`t provide stringent enough oversight of the resulting oligopoly -- three companies control 80 percent of the market -- to ensure that there`s no snafus that can cause shutdown of those facilities.\par \par \uc2 And so, when you do have a shutdown, when the market`s that concentrated, it creates these distributed shortages that we`re seeing right now that are very hard to resolve. They`re going to eventually need to get that facility reopened.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : The timeline for that is very unclear right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that`s what I wanted to ask you. I`m sure the \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner will be questioned about that on Capitol Hill this week.\par \par \uc2 But you said it`s a broken market. And you`re pointing to regulation failures. That whistle-blower report said they failed to maintain proper records and released untested baby formula. There`s all sorts of allegations in here.\par \par \uc2 Does this sound like criminal behavior to you?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Potentially.\par \par \uc2 And that whistle-blower report was head -- sent to the head of the Office of Criminal Investigations at \uc2 FDA\uc2 . So it does appear to be a sophisticated whistle-blower. Remember, this division at \uc2 FDA\uc2  is nine people. And it was even fewer people when I was there. It`s grown in recent years.\par \par \uc2 And we made some budget requests to increase the size of that group. So the entire industry in this country is overseen by nine people. This has been an under-resourced part of the agency for a very long time, and that`s contributing, I think, to these challenges that the agency is facing, trying to exert more vigorous and more efficient oversight.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Those allegations are going to be very hard to clear.\par \par \uc2 And another issue may be that the people who the whistle-blower has implicated -- and \uc2 FDA\uc2  has interviewed that whistle-blower -- may be the same people now making representations to the agency about the safety of that facility.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : And, if that`s the case, that`s going to complicate issues.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We have more to talk about with you, as always. So stay with us. And, on the other side of the break, we`ll continue the conversation.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIALS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR, or we`re available on demand. Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ apps.\par \par \uc2 And we`re just -- replayed on our CBS News Streaming Network at noon Eastern.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We want to continue now with former \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  board member Dr. \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gottlieb, we`ve been talking about the baby formula shortage.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about COVID, but just to pick up on something you said, I think you told me there are only nine people who oversee the entire baby formula industry in this country. Nine?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 And I think -- I think there were -- I believe there were three when I started at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . We got some more resources for that group. There`s been more resources added since then. And there`s a budget request from the current administration to add four more people.\par \par \uc2 But, yeah, only nine people right now oversee -- oversee the entire industry in the United States. And it was less than that just several years ago.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s astounding.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about COVID. We hit this horrendous milestone this week of one million deaths over the entire course of this pandemic. Right now, we`re averaging about 326 deaths a week. So we`ve come a far way. But we heard from both Dr. Fauci and Dr. Walensky this week that they have started putting on masks when they go indoors once again. There`s concern about an uptick.\par \par \uc2 What do you see in terms of trend lines? Where are we?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, look, we`re definitely seeing a surge of infection, particularly in the Northeast and parts of the Mid-Atlantic right now.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the modeling going on in those states, states like Connecticut and New York, it does appear that the infections are peaking right now. And it`s mostly a wave of infection driven by B.2 and this new Omicron variant, B.2.12.1 that appears to be more contagious and have more immune escape than prior variants of Omicron.\par \par \uc2 It looks like most of the people who are getting infected aren`t people who were previously infected with B.1, but some portion of the 40 percent of people who escaped the prior wave of Omicron and are now getting caught by this current wave.\par \par \uc2 I do believe that cases will continue to come down -- wastewater data collected by cities does show overall cases coming down -- and that we shouldn`t have a big wave of infection this summer, although there are models floating around the administration that does show a big wave of infection this summer.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is, we didn`t see that in 2020. We didn`t see it in 2021, when B.117 emerged in the spring. So this summer should be a backstop against continued spread of this variant, but it does pose a risk for the fall. And it`s going to be important to learn whether or not the new -- newly formulated vaccines that are now in development will cover this B.2 variant well.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully, they will. I believe they will, but that remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But -- so you reject the idea of a summer surge, even though scientists like Dr. Birx, who was on this program just a few weeks ago, is predicting and seeing a trend line that makes her very concerned that could happen, because it`s happened before?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Yeah, and there is a model that the White House was briefed on last week that shows a big surge of infection in the summer driven by B.2 as it moves into the Midwest and the West.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it is certainly possible, but other people disagree with that model. There is the potential that you see a slow burn through the summer. I think it`s more likely that you`re going to see infection levels come down. Remember, we thought that there was going to be a big surge last year and this summer with B.117 when it emerged in the spring.\par \par \uc2 And as we got into the later spring, infection levels came down. We had a relatively quiet June and July, and then Delta came along in late August and started to create a new wave of infection.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s probably the pattern we`re going to see again, where June and July are relatively low, people do feel safe again, and then, as we head into the late summer...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : ... probably B.2.12.1 is going to emerge or B.2 mostly in the South.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Dr. Gottlieb, we`ll be watching that. Thank you for your insight, as always.\par \par \uc2 Well, voters in five states head to the polls for midterm primary elections this Tuesday, and those contests will determine which party candidates will be on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 But it is the contest among Republicans in Pennsylvania that is attracting a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 Our Robert Costa tells us why.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voice-over): Top Republicans are flocking to Pennsylvania in the final days of the state`s red-hot Senate primary race, knowing the state will be a crucial battleground in this November`s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TED CRUZ (R-Texas): Every Republican running for office says: "I love \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TED CRUZ: "No, no, no, I love \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  even more. No, no, no, I have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tattooed on my rear end."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And the top three contenders are all pitching themselves as champions of Trump`s political legacy.\par \par \uc2 But voters face a conundrum: Who exactly fits the bill? Television personality Dr. Mehmet Oz won Trump`s endorsement last month. But Oz has since struggled to fend off two challengers, retired hedge fund manager David McCormick, whose wife, Dina Powell, served in the Trump administration as a deputy national security adviser, and hard right candidate Kathy Barnette, whose fiery and deeply personal message on abortion has caught fire with grassroots conservatives.\par \par \uc2 KATHY BARNETTE (R-Pennsylvania Senatorial Candidate): But it definitely made me become very adamant about the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But once polls in recent days showed Barnette jumping into the top tier, she also faced new and intense scrutiny of her past, including homophobic and anti-Muslim statements. Barnette has mostly denied and deflected, hoping to keep up her momentum.\par \par \uc2 And on Saturday at what was billed as her final campaign rally, Barnette spoke to supporters in Bucks County alongside \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who was endorsed hours earlier by Trump in the race for governor.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Stand behind the cone, please.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But CBS News and others were refused entry, no access, no questions allowed.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: They told us, no presser.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: OK.\par \par \uc2 Trump has been watching her rise carefully, and warned his supporters to stick with Oz, arguing Barnette, if nominated, will -- quote -- "never be able to win in November."\par \par \uc2 And her rivals are sounding the alarm.\par \par \uc2 You have called Kathy Barnette a mystery person. What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-Pennsylvania Senatorial Candidate): I called Kathy Barnette a mystery, because, every time she answers a question, she raises a bunch more questions. She`s not transparent about so many aspects of her basic biography, that we don`t know who she is.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Would Kathy Barnette be a risky bet for Republicans in November?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK (R-Pennsylvania Senatorial Candidate): Well, listen, I have gotten to know Kathy on the campaign trail. I respect her story.\par \par \uc2 But Kathy has been tested. She was tested in the last 24 months in a congressional seat, which she lost by 20 points.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But the race remains a tossup, with voters divided.\par \par \uc2 For Face the Nation, this is Robert Costa, reporting from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Senior foreign correspondent Charlie D`Agata has the latest on the diplomatic front and the battlefield -- Charlie.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian government is claiming a key victory in the battle for Kharkiv. And with Finland now on the verge of joining \uc2 NATO\uc2 , President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  faces the prospect of sharing an 800-mile border with a \uc2 NATO\uc2  partner.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): Finland today formalized its intention to apply for \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership, with Sweden likely to follow suit in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 SAULI NIINISTO (President of Finland): We have today a historic day. Finland will maximize its security.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: President Putin has already warned the Finnish president he`s made a mistake in joining the alliance.\par \par \uc2 It comes as Russia has faced significant losses, pulling back from Ukraine`s second largest city of Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 (VERA KUSOLOPENKO SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But the shelling continues; 67-year-old Vera Kusolopenko lost everything to a Russian missile.\par \par \uc2 (VERA KUSOLOPENKO SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "This was my home," she said, "and, yesterday, it was burned down."\par \par \uc2 Russia has now turned its firepower on Eastern Ukraine`s industrial Donbass region, targeting infrastructure, bridges, oil refineries warehouses.\par \par \uc2 But standing here, looking at the size of this crater in the middle of a dirt road in a quiet residential neighborhood, it`s hard to know exactly what the Russians were aiming for.\par \par \uc2 The sheer devastation caused by an airstrike in Bakhmut that tore homes apart, leaving residents homeless and furious.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "We need help," a woman shouts in despair. "Everything is destroyed, broken," salvaging what`s left of their homes and their lives.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: British military intelligence reports that, despite that kind of bombardment, Russia has failed to achieve substantial territorial gains in the past month, and it`s likely Russia has lost around a third of the ground forces it committed to the invasion of Ukraine -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to return now to the economy and the financial challenges facing this country.\par \par \uc2 We turn to the former CEO and current senior chairman of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Lloyd Blankfein\uc2 , who joins us from Water Mill, New York.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2  (Senior Chairman, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, Americans haven`t experienced inflation like this in 40 years now.\par \par \uc2 And the prices year over year are pretty incredible, when you look at them, gas up 44 percent, eggs 23 percent increase year over year. There`s spillover into the services too now. I mean, you look at hotel prices, 23 percent increase.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... airlines over 30.\par \par \uc2 What does all of this indicate to you?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Well, wage as well.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you how we got here. We had this massive exogenous event, COVID, lockdowns all around the world. And, at the time that this was -- that this was beginning, it was a huge crisis.\par \par \uc2 And I would say -- you say worse in 40 years, kind of unprecedented that everywhere in the world all lock down at the same time. In response, there was a massive public policy response, and -- to overwhelm it. And it was a little bit of fighting the last war, in some ways, because, in the financial crisis, you recall, the feeling in the aftermath was it took a long time to recover from that.\par \par \uc2 So, this time, we were going to go big. And we went big. And that created a lot of liquidity. And all those dollars are change -- are chasing -- are chasing assets.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 So...\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : So, we have too much growth, too much stimulus.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Too much growth. Too much stimulus.\par \par \uc2 So you agree with the \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... when they point to things like all the fiscal spending adding to inflation?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, at the time, it was very uncertain. And the most important thing was to not have it -- was to not lose all those jobs and have a massive crisis.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : And so they reacted. And I think they reacted sensibly with what they knew at the time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : And you can argue about that, but that`s all with the benefit of hindsight.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, let`s talk about what`s happening now to try to control it. So it is the Federal Reserve`s job.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know this.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, for our audience, it`s the central banker`s job control inflation here.\par \par \uc2 Chairman Powell said getting it down to 2 percent is going to involve some pain. What does that indicate to you? And do you think the Fed is doing what is needed right now?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Well, the object is, there`s an imbalance, too much demand.\par \par \uc2 And what you have to do is, you have to slow down that demand. You have to slow down the economy. And so they`re going to have to raise rates. They`re going to have to curtail, hopefully reduce the number of positions that are unopened, because they -- and increase the size of the labor force.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to involve some pain. And the real pain is not so -- is partly what the Fed is going to do, but it`s just that this inflation, some of it is sticky. It`s going to be -- you know, we have something like 8 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 Some of that is transitory. Some of that is transitory, will go away. Eventually, the war in the Ukraine will be over. Some of the supply chain shocks will go away. But some of it will be a little bit stickier and it will be with us for a while. And while we`re talking about this in the macro sense, overall, for individuals and certainly the individuals at the bottom quartile of the pie sharing, it`s going to be quite difficult and oppressive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Difficult and oppressive.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you lived through the last financial crisis...\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  obviously a key part. You know it very well.\par \par \uc2 When you say it took a long time, it took about 10 years to recover from the last financial crisis..\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Yeah. That`s quite a long time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 So, given what you`re saying is unprecedented, what does recovery look like? Are you saying strap in for more than a decade of struggle here?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Well, it`s -- well, no, no. It`s a little bit different. There was a lot -- a lot of different things going on.\par \par \uc2 And there were -- it`s always at least a little bit different. This is -- this is kind of much different. And, there, you had the banks in trouble, a lot of distress, a lot of liquidity issues, big credit issues. Nobody was sure who was able to pay their debts as they arose.\par \par \uc2 And that took -- and then, again, the financial system is the intermediary by which Fed accomplishes its activity. That`s not impaired today. Actually, the consumer is starting out at a strong level. There is a lot of -- it is going to be hard for people to have savings, but they already have savings. They`re not necessarily going to increase it quickly because of inflation.\par \par \uc2 But they`re starting in a much better place than we were then.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : And the Fed has very, again, powerful tools.\par \par \uc2 Some of this will transition away. Some of the supply chain issues, again, will go away. China won`t be locked down forever. The war will not go -- in the Ukraine will not go on forever. Some of it -- and some of these things are a little bit stickier, like energy prices.\par \par \uc2 And there are some elements of the supply chain that are going to be a lot stickier.\par \par \uc2 I will give you an example. We were the beneficiary for...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : ... a very long time of the globalization of the economy, which made goods and services cheaper because we took advantage of cheap labor and countries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Well, how good do we feel, with what we`ve learned to be relying -- and this as part of your last talk with -- with Secretary Buttigieg.\par \par \uc2 How comfortable are we now to rely on those supply chains that are not within the borders of the United States that we can`t control?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : Do we feel good about getting all our semiconductors from Taiwan, which is, again, an object of China?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you think we`re headed towards recession?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : We`re certainly heading -- it`s certainly a very, very high risk factor.\par \par \uc2 And there`s -- but there`s a path. It`s a narrow path. But I think the Fed has very powerful tools. It`s hard to finely tune them, and it`s hard to see the effects of them quickly enough to alter it. But I think they are -- I think they`re responding well.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s -- it`s...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 : It`s definitely a risk. If I were running a big company, I would be very prepared for it. If I was a consumer, I would be prepared for it. But it`s not baked in the cake.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, \uc2 Lloyd Blankfein\uc2 , thank you for your insights.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Former Secretary of Defense \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  is out with a new book called "A Sacred Oath," which chronicles his time in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2  (Former U.S. Secretary of Defense): Margaret, great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to talk to you about a number of things,. but you did say recently that, after the events of January 6, which took place when you were out of office, that you now consider President Trump a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 The committee that`s investigating January 6 is about to begin public hearings. And they`ve said they have in their possession a draft executive order that would have had the then-defense secretary seize voting machines, and that the Department of Justice and the Pentagon would then be involved somehow in stopping the transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it`s important for that committee to lay out these facts to the public?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: Well, first, let me extend my condolences, by the way, to the families and friends of those tragically murdered yesterday in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 But, to your question, yes, I think the January 6 Committee needs to get to the bottom of the truth of what happened on January 6, the events leading up to it, and understand it, so, we -- there is a degree of kind of accountability, and, second, we have lessons learned to make sure it doesn`t happen again. It`s absolutely important.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it`s -- I mean, just even laying that out to you, it is kind of astounding to hear.\par \par \uc2 And in your book, you write General Milley actually had an agreement with the other members of the Joint Chiefs, the lead military commanders in the country, to all resign if President Trump tried to use the military to stop the transfer of power. You write about personally being concerned that that`s what he was trying to do.\par \par \uc2 So, you saw evidence or you had good reason to believe there was an attempt here to basically stage a coup?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: I had a lot of concern about what might or might not happen in the months leading up to the election, right?\par \par \uc2 There was talk about conducting strikes against -- military strikes against other countries. The president through the summer was talking about sending troops into Seattle and Portland.\par \par \uc2 And I write about in the final days, I had this private meeting with the head of the National Guard and General Milley, and I talk about what might or might not happen the day after the election, concern that there may be the use of the military somehow to influence the outcome.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I -- there`s been a lot of criticism about why I didn`t speak up. It`s because I wanted to be there on the spot if any of these things happened to be the circuit-breaker, because the only two people in the United States that can deploy troops, U.S. military troops, are the president and the secretary of defense.\par \par \uc2 And I was in that pivotal position to act if I thought something was outlandish, irresponsible or would affect the institution of DOD or our country. That`s what it came down to for me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, that`s why you didn`t resign.\par \par \uc2 But why didn`t you speak out as soon as you left office? I know you started writing the book within months.\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But why didn`t you speak publicly about all of this?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: Well, the election was over.\par \par \uc2 I think, like many of us, I figured the president would -- would challenge the election, like others have done in the past, and, after a few weeks, it would be over, and we`d have a peaceful transition of power.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But he did. There was an impeachment hearing on -- about what happened with January 6...\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... and about whether there were attempts to stop the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 You`re saying you actually were worried about that yourself.\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: Well, I was concerned.\par \par \uc2 You always have to think through alternative scenarios, what might or might not happen. And I would have spoken out if called for to do it. I said on another network I would have certainly spoken out if he had won the election.\par \par \uc2 But he didn`t. And at that point in time, I was patiently waiting to see what would happen, make sure that the peaceful transfer of power happened. As you know, I joined my other -- the living secretaries of defense, wrote an op-ed on January 3, three days prior to the transition, expressing our concern about the peaceful handover of power and warning the Pentagon, if you will, about the importance of them doing their duty.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You talk about and have spoken quite a lot this past week about the events in Lafayette Square.\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it`s an important bit of the public record.\par \par \uc2 You were in the Oval Office with the president and he spoke about a very specific number, 10,000, of active-duty troops potentially being sent into the streets of Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 I want to play a clip for you here because I asked the then-Attorney General Bill Barr about exactly that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A senior administration official told our \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin that, in a meeting at the White House on Monday morning, the president demanded that 10,000 active-duty troops be ordered into American streets.\par \par \uc2 Is that accurate?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR (Former U.S. Attorney General): No, that`s completely false. That`s completely false. Sunday night...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The president did not demand that?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL BARR: No, he did not demand that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why do you seem to have different recollections?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 You know, I wrote about this in my book that Bill Barr and I have different recollections. Of course, if you go through my story, you`ll understand that the president calls over to the Pentagon earlier that morning and talks about 10,000 troops.\par \par \uc2 That`s when I`m first made aware of this request. And, look, I don`t know why we have different recollections. I think, in all these cases, people hear or see different things. But I`m 110 percent confident of what the president was seeking that morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The former attorney general said it was completely false.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that was an effort to deliberately mislead the public?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 Again, people have different recollections. People have asked me about things that I simply can`t recall. All I know is, the way we defused this is Bill Barr, to his credit, because he was a good partner on this stuff, put forward 5,000 law enforcement officers, and I put forward up to 5,000 National Guard to take care of this.\par \par \uc2 I mean, do the math. 5,000 and 5,000. In my mind...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re trying to retrofit this 10,000 arbitrary number.\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: Well, I`m -- yes, I`m trying to kind of give him his 10,000 without giving him 10,000 active-duty troops.\par \par \uc2 And we pulled it off, and thank goodness. It was -- it was the way to kind of get that down, get out of the room and get on with what we needed to do.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you concerned that, if the former president stands for election, that he will surround himself with people who you are deeply critical of who didn`t try to short-circuit?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you were very critical of Robert O`Brien, the national security adviser. You write about the chief of staff, Mark Meadows. You talk about Stephen Miller, all people who were egging on some of these instincts.\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: Yeah, absolutely, he will.\par \par \uc2 He figured this out in 2020, after he beat impeachment. He talks about it. I describe this moment in the book, where he thinks about the people he should put into office. And so, yes, that is a concern of mine, if he runs and is reelected, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But should any of those people have any proximity to public office right now?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE MARK ESPER: I don`t think so, but that`s my opinion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, they`re names that we`re watching and we will continue to cover.\par \par \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 , a lot more in this book. It is worth reading. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Watch the CBS Evening News With Norah O`Donnell tomorrow night for an exclusive interview with the president of the company that makes the Enfamil formula for babies.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for Face the Nation, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020220515ei5f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Is Also Deeply Personal\b0\par\par\uc2 Lilia Luciano, Kate Raddatz \par \uc2 507 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The battle over abortion has transformed American politics over the past half century and the divide is deepening.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is also deeply personal. In tonight`s "Weekend Journal," Kate Raddatz of CBS station WCCO shares one woman`s story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATE RADDATZ (voiceover): Emily Richter and her husband, Bryce, couldn`t wait to see their baby during her anatomy scan.\par \par \uc2 EMILY RICHTER: We were excited. We were -- we were first-time parents. We were excited to find out that she was a girl. I knew, like I had this feeling that she was a girl.\par \par \uc2 KATE RADDATZ: And she was a girl. But during that routine appointment on March 13th, of 2020, their lives changed forever.\par \par \uc2 EMILY RICHTER: You could see the tech, she was -- she was quiet. She was suddenly quiet and her affect had changed and then they got to her head and she turned the whole machine off and she said, "I need to go get your OB."\par \par \uc2 KATE RADDATZ: Richter`s daughter was diagnosed with a serious birth defect called anencephaly, which causes a baby to grow without parts of the brain and skull. Babies with the condition are stillborn or die shortly after birth.\par \par \uc2 It affects around one in 4,600 babies born every year in the U.S. according to the C.D.C. There was also a hole in this baby`s heart. Richter was 19 weeks pregnant.\par \par \uc2 EMILY RICHTER: I mean, it was the worst news we could have gotten. It`s still the worst news we`ve received in our lives.\par \par \uc2 KATE RADDATZ: Doctors said Richter`s life was also at risk.\par \par \uc2 EMILY RICHTER: Because of the severity of our case, my amniotic fluid was on the verge of sepsis.\par \par \uc2 KATE RADDATZ: Richter made the decision to terminate the pregnancy she had so badly wanted.\par \par \uc2 EMILY RICHTER: She was still my baby and I carried her for almost 20 weeks by the time my procedure could be scheduled.\par \par \uc2 KATE RADDATZ: Richter went on to get pregnant again and the couple now has a one-year-old boy. She shares her story with the hope others understand the different reasons someone may have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 EMILY RICHTER: Removing access to this type of care is devastating for a lot of women, for any birthing body, honestly, this could be anyone.\par \par \uc2 KATE RADDATZ: Kate Raddatz, CBS News, Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Stories that can be illuminating.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the CBS WEEKEND NEWS, we shift gears.\par \par \uc2 Ford rolls out a new pickup truck and it is selling lightning fast.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220517ei5e000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Rights Protesters Rally In Washington, D.C. And Across The Country\b0\par\par\uc2 Lilia Luciano, Christina Ruffini \par \uc2 525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And today, protesters gathered across the country to rally for abortion rights. Tensions around the issue have ratcheted up since a bombshell leak from the Supreme Court and a wave of abortion bans and restrictions taking hold in several states.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Christina Ruffini is in Washington, D.C., the site of one of the largest protests -- Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good evening, Lilia.\par \par \uc2 Yes, these are the biggest demonstrations we`ve seen in D.C. since that leaked Supreme Court draft opinion, but no matter what is decided, demonstrators here today told us they`re not backing down.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voiceover): From the National Mall to the steps of the Supreme Court, women, men, and children stepped out in support of reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 In cities across the country, abortion rights supporters voiced their opposition to a leaked draft majority opinion that could overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Some speakers emphasized the lack of support for women, especially poor and minority women who do decide to have children.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It doesn`t seem like they`re very pro-life once that baby is actually born, so I think it`s really hypocritical.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Kava Brandres (sp?) marched with her daughter.\par \par \uc2 KAVA BRANDRES (Mother who joined daughter in abortion rights protest): It`s a Human Rights issue. It`s a Civil Rights issue. It`s a justice issue.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And for congressional Democrats, abortion rights are now a key re-election issue.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: If you make abortion illegal, if you take away our rights to make our personal decisions about our bodies, we will see you at the ballot box in November.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: With chants echoing off the hallowed marble columns, conservative Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said Friday, the leak undermines trust in the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 ): You lose that trust, especially in the institution that I`m in. It changes the institution fundamentally. You begin to look over your shoulder.\par \par \uc2 It`s like kind of an infidelity.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: But many on the street today said for them that trust is already broken.\par \par \uc2 FONDA FRALIN (Protester in Washington, D.C.): I feel like so many people worked so hard to get where we are now and now, just like generations of work are going to -- is going to be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: One of the organizers called today Day One of a Summer of Rage, and if you look behind me, that non-scalable fence is staying up.\par \par \uc2 We`re expecting a decision sometime in June -- Lilia.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Christina Ruffini, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220517ei5e0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2  About The Possibility That The Supreme Court Will Overturn Roe V. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Dana Jacobson \par \uc2 1,741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour with the Senate Republican Minority Whip, \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , who joins us at the table to talk about the battle over abortion rights after that stunning -- and stunning is the word -- the leak from the Supreme Court on Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 He says a Democratic push to enshrine a right to an abortion into Federal law goes too far and is out of step with most Americans. But just last week, a CBS News poll found that 64 percent of Americans do not want Roe v. Wade overturned.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , we`re happy to say joins us at the table. We love it when you come into the studio. It`s nice to have you in person.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk to you about your vote last week. You voted against to codify Roe v. Wade. Why did you vote that way? What was your thinking?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I`ve been -- I have a history in my time in public life of being pro-life, but that vote was more than just codifying Roe, which is why they lost even people who typically support Roe versus Wade on our side, people like Senators Collin and Murkowski -- Collins and Murkowski.\par \par \uc2 But basically, it was more expansive. It went beyond Roe, and it was a piece of legislation, I think they knew was going to fail. We had that vote actually a few weeks ago, and I think Senator Schumer did it to satisfy political constituency that wanted that vote, but knew it was going to fail.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He thinks it is going to galvanize Democrats in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is there any concern on the Republican side that this issue could in fact, galvanize Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I think that they`re looking for something to distract from the economy, inflation, and the border, and some of the issues that they would rather not talk about. I think this is an issue, this is a pivot -- a clear pivot on their part to try and get on something that they think they can play offense with.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of it is going to come down to, and the politics around this issue is very contentious, always has been.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fraught is a good word to describe that on both sides.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Fraught is a good word. It is. And so how that plays politically remains to be seen because there are many places around the country, obviously, where you are, if you`re a candidate running and you`re a pro-life candidate that`s going to work to your political advantage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But most Americans it seems, support Roe v. Wade, would you support it with some restrictions?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I think to your point, Gayle, if you look at the way this polls, you know Roe versus Wade, basically the decision, the Supreme Court decision said that the state`s interest becomes compelling at the point of viability, which keeps moving back.\par \par \uc2 And you know, right now 65 percent of Americans don`t think you ought to have abortions past the first trimester. So into the second or third trimester, 80 percent believe you shouldn`t have them after the second trimester, and we`re really out of step with the world.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`re one of only seven countries in the world that allows abortions after 20 weeks, and those include, of course, North Korea and China. Most European nations, France is at 14 weeks, Germany is at 12 weeks. So most countries have found a political consensus around restrictions that are, I guess, we start much sooner in a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that where you are?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I was going to say, so why not support something that had those restrictions in those timelines that you speak of?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): I think the question for a lot of people is going to be, is it a human life and when does that -- when does life begin? I think what you`re going to see is State Legislatures because this doesn`t do away with it, it just returns it to the state.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To the state level, yes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Are going to have to find political consensus, and some states are probably going to define it differently. My position has always been exceptions, rape, incest, life of the mother, but a ban.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m wondering if you`re still enjoying your job, and I`m asking you that because there`s a bestselling book out right now, I know you`ve heard of it, "This Will Not Pass" it says.\par \par \uc2 You consider not running for re-election this year, but Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said, "Hold on just a second senator." So first, are you enjoying your job? And why did you -- is it true that you considered not running for re-election?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I did --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that a good smile?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): I`ve got -- well, I`ve got, you know, like you, I`ve got grandkids now running around. It was -- it was a hard -- hard decision. But in the end you do things because you think it`s some place you can make a difference, and I`d like to believe public life is still a place where you can make a difference.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And going back to the beginning discussion, there is one thought -- we hear so much about as you talk about, when does life begin? There`s a lot of talk of what the rights are of being a fetus, be it an unborn child, however you refer to that? What about the women and the families that we`re talking about and their rights, too? How do you balance that in the consideration?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I think you have to -- you`ve got to be compassionate. And you know, the solution if you`re a pro-life person, and I am, and if you believe that`s a human life, then you believe it needs to be protected. But there is also -- there is -- there`s a mom and a lot of vulnerable moms, and I think that the responsibility for people who hold a pro-life position isn`t just to state that and check the box, you`ve got to actually demonstrate that you`re willing to help care for moms who are going to carry babies to the point of birth.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Or give moms a chance to have that say, a potential mom to control some of what is their body also. Trust in them, and in doctors, also.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of the questions is -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, no, I mean, I think that you`re right. And that`s why, you know, as you look at this issue, as I`ve said, you`ve got to do it in a way, I think there`s a compassionate way and understand that your impact.\par \par \uc2 Now I`m not -- I`m a middle-aged White guy, I`m probably not the person you should be talking about a subject like this, but I also have a wife and two very accomplished daughters who are pro-life.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re a middle aged White guy in a position of power to make a difference on something to millions.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): And I think you have to listen -- you have to listen very carefully what people are saying. But in the end, if I`m going to err, I`m going to err on the side of life.\par \par \uc2 But I think and how you determine the course of action is going to be largely based upon where the political consensus is, and I think, you know, right now, this is a decision that has been made by nine unelected Judges, and it is now going to go back to the people and their elected representatives, which is, I think, where a lot of folks think issues like this should be decided.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a very big deal for a government to tell a woman she has to carry a pregnancy through to term.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think we all agree on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And would you tell some of your other middle aged White men that we like, middle aged White men here at the table. Spread the word. We`re very friendly here.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We like old people, right? We like everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like all people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That comes very close to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like all points of view.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That comes very close to calling me middle aged, and I still claim to be youthful.\par \par \uc2 Senator Thune, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right now, it is 8:08. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Florence and The Machine front woman, Florence Welch, set out to create a classic album, then the pandemic struck.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: The record was supposed to be like a confident and cohesive, like well-rounded body of art like -- and I think it`s a descent into madness.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s what we all did.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Florence and The Machine performance.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I said it earlier, sometimes that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Welch talks with Anthony Mason about writing and dancing her way through the chaos with her new album, "Dance Fever."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220517ei5d000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Baby Formula Crisis; Formula Shortage Response; Devastating Fire Aftermath; The Pandemic`s Ongoing Impact; Five Republicans Subpoenaed; Brutality on the Battlefield; "Faces of COVID"; Abortion Rights Fight; The Modern Loss Handbook; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Dana Jacobson, Adriana Diaz, Nancy  Cordes, Scott MacFarlane, Anthony Mason, Tom Wait, Debora Patta, David  Begnaud, Vladimir Duthiers, Rebecca Soffer \par \uc2 13,652 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday. You made it. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And I`m Dana Jacobson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay, Dana is here. Nate is off.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The White House vows to fight the baby formula shortage that could drag on for months. We`ll talk to desperate parents.\par \par \uc2 JAYLENE ORELLANA (Mother): Sometimes I do cry at night. It`s hard physically, mentally, financially.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy is among five Republican lawmakers subpoenaed by the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 REP. SCOTT PERRY (R-PA): This is proof positive that this is about headlines.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: At the White House, President Biden ordered the flag lowered to half-staff marking one million COVID deaths since the pandemic started.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): One million empty chairs around the family dinner table.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Firefighters scrambled to contain a wildfire that burned through an upscale neighborhood in California.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I heard it was bad. But this is so devastating.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Severe storms are rocking the Upper Midwest amid destructive winds, some gusting over 100 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Kentucky Derby winner, Rich Strike will not run in the Preakness. This will mark the fourth year straight without a Triple Crown winner.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Philadelphia 76ers\uc2  couldn`t handle the heat of Miami.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He`s got Butler, Gibson up for the slam.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And the Heat advanced to the Eastern Conference Finals.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is Big Face Coffee back?\par \par \uc2 JIMMY BUTLER (\uc2 Miami Heat\uc2  basketball player): He is back. He has been back. No free cup. I`m charging everybody.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show with James Corden" host): According to a new poll, half of Americans think life in France is better than life in the United States, to which French people were like, "What a coincidence. We think we`re better than you guys, too." I am sure this goes both ways. I`m sure someone out there in France right now is like, "Bonjour, I wish I had -- how you say Doritos."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not just saying this, but Paris is my favorite city. I think it`s gorgeous there and I love the accent.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a beautiful city. Lovely country. We`ve got the French fries though. Do we need anything else?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We have the Doritos.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We are sharing an inspiring story with you today. A work of empathy from someone who has decided that the best way to remember the million Americans who have died from COVID is to honor as many of them as possible, one name at a time.\par \par \uc2 David Begnaud meets the man behind the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, "Faces of COVID" and learns how much it means to families trying to heal.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Plus Florence Welch is excited to be back with The Machine performing on stage and sharing a new album. She tells Anthony Mason making music right now means embracing the chaos.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH (Florence and The Machine): The record is supposed to be like confident and cohesive, like well-rounded body of art.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Florence and The Machine performance.)\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: And I think it`s a descent into madness.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s what we all did.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Nothing bad about that sometimes. After time off in the pandemic, she has a lot to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she`s talking to Anthony. Can`t wait for that conversation.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with a challenge that is very quickly becoming a crisis for many parents of young children. You all keep hearing these stories -- we all do -- about moms and dads finding empty shelves when they go looking for new supplies of formula for their little babies.\par \par \uc2 This is a national shortage and it -- we`re sorry to say -- is getting worse.\par \par \uc2 Formulas now 43 percent out of stock nationwide. That`s up from 31 percent last month. Nancy Cordes will have more on the Biden administration`s response in just a moment, but first, we`re going to go to Adriana Diaz for more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, the nation`s leading supplier of baby formula Abbott, which is headquartered outside Chicago, they say they could start their Michigan plant back up in within two weeks, but then it could take two months for that new product to land on store shelves.\par \par \uc2 As you might remember Abbott had to shut down that plant after a recall and parents have become consumed by the hunt for formula.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: New mom, Jaylene Orellana is so desperate for baby formula, she is willing to drive 14 hours to pick up formulas she had her aunt buy in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 JAYLENE ORELLANA: I`d rather be safe like have cans stocked up than sorry to my daughter that not knowing when she is going to eat.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This week, the 19-year-old from Houston drove three hours to get formula from a dad who didn`t need it in Austin. They connected on a social media site like this one where parents post if they need formula or have extra.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that you`re not going to be able to find formula for your baby one of these days?\par \par \uc2 JAYLENE ORELLANA: I am. I`m willing to drive, whatever I have to drive.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: As the shortage grows, families are scrambling, but pediatricians like Dr. Joshua Wechsler are warning parents not to water down formula or try homemade recipes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOSHUA WECHSLER (Pediatric gastroenterologist): You can`t ensure that micronutrient calorie density is all correct, that you`re using the appropriate water source potentially. There might be a lot of issues.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Those who find formula face a tough choice, like Michigan mom, Destiny Featherstone, whose youngest is four months old.\par \par \uc2 DESTINY FEATHERSTONE (Michigan mother): Yesterday, there was like, just enough for her month`s supply, and I wanted to get it all, but I was like, I don`t want to take from the other babies.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Virginia mom, Erica Lopez says she never knows if she`ll find formula on the shelves.\par \par \uc2 ERICA LOPEZ (Virginia mother): When my daughter doesn`t drink the whole bottle, I`m like, oh my gosh, she just wasted like two to three ounces. I`m like, that was like a scoop that we could have saved.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Back in Texas, Orellana says she buys extra formula when she sees it, but not for the reason you might think. She says she is sending the extra cans to other families to help them.\par \par \uc2 JAYLENE ORELLANA: I bought it to help. I mean, I know I how it feels. It`s hard.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Adriana, this is heartbreaking for a lot of parents. Listen, you`ve got a new baby in the house, a favorite daughter. You`re covering the story, but are you part of the story, too?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Well, Gayle, you know, it has been stressful to find formula for my eight-month-old, Isabella, but you know, one time actually at a store I was able to find the last two boxes of formula and I grabbed them.\par \par \uc2 But then honestly, I heard Tony Dokoupil`s voice in my head telling me not to hoard because you guys have been talking about it on the show. So I ended up leaving a box. Luckily we recently signed up for a formula subscription service just days before they stopped taking new members. , we`re really fortunate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I`m sorry my voice is in your head, but I do think another parent is very happy you did that -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is what happens when Tony Dokoupil gets in your head it. Adriana, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 But it is something to think about. I hadn`t really thought about it either, honestly until you said it.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to Nancy Cordes. She is at the White House with more on the story. Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 What is the Biden administration doing about this?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Well, they`re now fielding questions from consumer advocates, Gayle, about the F.D.A.`s inspection process, people wondering whether this harmful bacteria that was found in the plant in Michigan that Adriana mentioned could have been detected sooner.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, officials here at the White House say they are going to cut some packaging regulations to help get new products on the shelves faster. They`re also calling for crackdowns on price gouging, which as you can imagine could become a big problem and they`re working on importing more formula from overseas.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, President Biden spoke with some of the CEOs of major retailers and manufacturers about how to increase production and inventory. And the White House isn`t ruling out the possibility that the President might invoke the Defense Production Act to quickly ramp up supply but here`s the bottom line, Tony, they say as of right now they really can`t predict when parents will see more supply on store shelves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that is the truly scary part. Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Moving on to weather, a destructive storm system called a derecho turned deadly in the Northern Plains. Take a look at this massive cloud of dust in the southern part of South Dakota yesterday caused by dangerous straight line winds gusting over a hundred miles an hour, tearing down trees and power lines in the eastern part of that state.\par \par \uc2 A tornado damaged the community of Castlewood and at least one person was killed in Sioux Falls.\par \par \uc2 Across state lines, in Minnesota, high winds knocked trained cars off the tracks and at least one person was killed in that state.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Out west in California, crews are still struggling to contain a dangerous wildfire. It has burned at least 200 acres and destroyed at least 20 homes in an affluent area south of Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 The risk of further destruction forced at least 900 people to evacuate. No one fortunately has been killed, but two firefighters have been hurt.\par \par \uc2 Tom Wait from our Los Angeles station, KCBS, has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SASSAN DARIAN (California Resident): I`ve never experienced anything like this in my life.\par \par \uc2 TOM WAIT: Sassan Darian was live streaming on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  as the flames race through Laguna Niguel toward his father`s five-bedroom home.\par \par \uc2 SASSAN DARIAN: Papa, let`s go.\par \par \uc2 TOM WAIT: While firefighters doused the flames, Darien admitted he and his family waited too long after being told to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 SASSAN DARIAN: We had embers on us. My daughter said we`re on fire, when we were in the car. I`m like, "Oh my God, there are embers on us."\par \par \uc2 So things got really out of control really quickly.\par \par \uc2 TOM WAIT: Like Darian, other residents of the affluent neighborhood are returning to absolute destruction.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I heard it was bad but this is so devastating.\par \par \uc2 TOM WAIT: Firefighters already battling hotspots with hotter and drier conditions will likely have to contend with high winds going into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. SHANE SHERWOOD (Orange County Fire Authority): That will still continue to challenge all of our firefighters and our efforts toward containing this fire.\par \par \uc2 TOM WAIT: While investigators are looking into electrical circuit activity in the area as a possible cause, firefighters warn that these kinds of fires will be more common.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s really indicative of the prolonged drought, the climate change, everything we`re talking about. This is a different environment and there is no sign that that`s going to change.\par \par \uc2 TOM WAIT: Leaving those like Darian to pick up the pieces.\par \par \uc2 SASSAN DARIAN: It`s just surreal because I lived -- I was here yesterday. While it was happening, I had to rush out and now the house is totally gone.\par \par \uc2 TOM WAIT: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Tom Wait in Laguna Niguel, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turning now to the pandemic where President Biden ordered American flags to be flown at half-staff yesterday to mark one million coronavirus deaths in this country. Members of Congress also held a moment of silence outside the U.S. Capitol as new COVID cases continue to rise all across the country.\par \par \uc2 The latest daily average hit nearly 85,000.\par \par \uc2 White House COVID Response Coordinator, Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2  joins us now to talk about all of it.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jha, good morning. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about COVID funding. We`ve got $10 billion in funding held up in the Senate right now. If the country doesn`t get that money, how vulnerable would you say are we going to be?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2  (White House Coronavirus Response Coordinator): Yes, good morning, Tony. Thanks so much for having me here.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we put in an emergency request for $22.5 billion actually to what we thought was sort of really the bare minimum we needed to get us through this fall and winter. Without that money, we`re not going to have enough of a new generation of vaccines that Americans are going to need this fall for everybody. We`re going to run out of treatments. We`re going to run out of testing.\par \par \uc2 So it`s pretty urgent that Congress act and Congress act now, so we can be prepared for what`s coming.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking ahead, you said recently that we could see a hundred million new COVID cases in the fall and winter. That`s an eye- popping number. A lot of people are raising their eyebrows. And some have said that maybe that`s a politically motivated number in order to free up this funding.\par \par \uc2 Where`s your data on that? And what do you say to folks who say you may be playing with the numbers?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 : Well, first of all, what I would say is you know, we`re looking at a range of scenarios for planning. What we`re doing in the White House is we`re planning both for the summer, as well as for the fall and winter. And we`re looking at a range of models, both internal as well as external.\par \par \uc2 And some of them suggests that if we don`t -- if we go into the fall and winter with no treatments, with no new generation of vaccines, no treatments, we could be looking at a pretty substantial wave of infections.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had one each of the last two winters, and we obviously need to be planning for such scenarios. Obviously, we hope that doesn`t happen. Obviously, we hope Congress steps up and does its job, but we`ve got a plan for those scenarios.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, let`s talk vaccines. Kids under five could have a vaccine approved for them next month, but parents according to polls are still quite reluctant to vaccinate kids that young. What do you say to those parents? And how important is vaccinating the very young to getting us into the so-called endemic stage of this pandemic?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 : Yes, so in terms of kids under five, we`re going to obviously have to see the data and what it shows before making a determination and the F.D.A. will make that. I will say so far the data on kids and vaccines has been very, very clear.\par \par \uc2 I have three children, all of whom are vaccinated. Why? Because the benefits of vaccines clearly outweigh the risks. These are very, very safe vaccines, and they provide a very high degree of protection.\par \par \uc2 I expect we`ll see the same thing for kids under five. We`ll see where the data ends, but I really do think that it is very important for kids to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2  speaking as a doctor and as a parent, we appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 : Thank you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On Capitol Hill, the House Select Committee investigating the deadly attack on the U.S. Capitol has now subpoenaed House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy and four other Republican lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 The panel wants to speak to them about the events surrounding the January 6th insurrection as the Committee gets ready to present its findings to the American public perhaps as early as next month.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more on this story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A crime and attack unlike any in American history has led to a congressional investigation unlike any other. Now, after nearly 1,000 interviews, the January 6th Select Committee has its sights on five Republican Congressmen, each of whom has refused to appear voluntarily, including the G.O.P. leader in the House, Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 The Committee knows McCarthy spoke with then President Trump before, during, and after the attack, something he talked about during an interview with CBS News on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I`ve spoken to the President. I asked him to talk to the nation to tell them to stop this.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: McCarthy says the current investigation is not legitimate and the Committee acknowledges these subpoenas mark an unprecedented moment.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): An attempted coup and a violent insurrection at the Capitol interrupting the peaceful transfer of power are unprecedented events.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: As for the other members called, Alabama`s Mo Brooks did a fiery speech before Trump at the White House Ellipse, January 6th.\par \par \uc2 REP. MO BROOKS (R-AL): Today, is the day American Patriots start taking down names and kicking (bleep).\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Committee says Ohio`s Jim Jordan talked to Trump on the 6th and texted White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  earlier about blocking the electoral count, and that Congressman Andy Biggs and Scott Perry were both involved in discussions related to efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 REP. SCOTT PERRY (R-PA): This is all about distracting America from their abysmal record of running America into the ground.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: All five congressmen have criticized the subpoenas and called the Committee polarized with seven Democrats and two Republicans. The Committee hasn`t said what happens if these Congressmen defy the subpoenas, whether they`ll be referred for contempt of Congress, but we do know the Committee`s first hearing is June 9th, with a final report likely weeks later -- Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to Ukraine and new allegations of possible war crimes. Officials there say an attack by Russian forces on two schools killed three people and wounded 12 in the northern part of the country.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  responded by saying all Russian Commanders who gives such orders are simply sick and incurable.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta is in Ukraine, which is now in its 11th week of war.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Fury on the battlefield, what Russia has lacked in military strategy so far, it makes up for with brute force and a seemingly endless supply of deadly artillery.\par \par \uc2 Before Russian shells rained down on these buildings, they were full of chattering students, their future now up in smoke.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "What`s the point of destroying schools?" Asked Ukraine`s \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . "Any Russian Commander who gives such an order is sick."\par \par \uc2 In Mariupol Azovstal steel plant, those Ukrainian soldiers barricaded inside astoundingly continue to hold off Russian troops with every bit of reserve they have left.\par \par \uc2 And in Kharkiv, Ukraine continues to push Russia back. But for its civilians, it`s been over 11 weeks of endless misery.\par \par \uc2 In the village of Stepanki, 52-year-old Olga Karpenko`s (sp?) daughter could not hide from a Russian tank that fired directly at her house, and it is still not completely safe to exhume her daughter`s body as she relives the trauma.\par \par \uc2 (OLGA KARPENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "She didn`t die straight away," she said. "She suffered for almost a whole day." All she has left now is heartbreak.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Deborah Patta, Kyiv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says his deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is now on hold pending a key detail. We will tell you what he wants to know before moving ahead, but first, it is 7:17. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, David Begnaud speaks to the man behind "Faces of COVID", that`s a social media memorial to victims of the pandemic and find out what he hopes that we will all learn from their stories.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS, we appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are getting --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is 7:30, Friday, May 13th.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a check of your morning headlines starting with some hope for parents dealing with this national shortage of baby formula. The White House met with retailers and manufacturers and is now saying the plan is to import more formula from overseas to ramp up production here at home and cut back on regulations, but no big promises, no big solutions here either, besides maybe a crackdown on price gouging.\par \par \uc2 Truth is, this shortage will still last for months. That`s the expectation.\par \par \uc2 All right turning overseas, to the West Bank City of Ramallah where thousands poured into the streets to honor Palestinian-American journalist Shireen Abu Akleh. Guards carried her flower and flag-draped coffin to a memorial ceremony yesterday after she was killed while covering an Israeli military raid in the nearby city of Jenin.\par \par \uc2 While Palestinian leaders say she was killed by Israeli soldiers, Israel says it is not yet clear who fired the fatal shot and the dispute now is over who gets to investigate.\par \par \uc2 She was, we should point out wearing body armor and a blue vest that clearly marked her as a member of the media.\par \par \uc2 Finally the end on a lighter note, and a really truly happy bit of breaking news this morning, the 96-year-old Queen of England, the monarch spotted at a Royal Windsor Horse Show today first time seen in public in a month. She`s had some mobility issues.\par \par \uc2 She`s in the front row or front seat there have a Range Rover, I guess that solved the mobility issues. She didn`t get out, but she chatted with some people with a smile, looks very healthy. It`s very good news overall, I think.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I should point out for our American viewers, when I first saw that picture of her in the passenger seat, I thought it was a driver`s seat and I had to remind myself how it is reversed over there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is not actually driving the Range Rover.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right. But you`re so right though, Tony about the smile. She looks so good and it is so good to see her. Look at that smile.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Genuine. What a genuine smile.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And genuine. And the guy next to her, too, also seems very happy that he is standing next to the Queen, so all good. All good.\par \par \uc2 In March 2020, one began memorializing people who died from COVID on the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, "Faces of COVID" is what he called it. His name is Alex Goldstein, and he has paid tribute to thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 Every day, he highlights five or six stories in a conversation with our lead national correspondent David Begnaud. He explained why these tributes are so very important to family members who have lost loved ones and are so meaningful to him as well.\par \par \uc2 David, good morning, looking forward to the story.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Good morning, my friend.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the remarkable things about Alex Goldstein is that he knows a little bit about thousands of people`s lives. He has been memorializing COVID victims online nearly every day for more than two years. In fact, he said, "David, the only time I took a break was when my dad died of prostate cancer."\par \par \uc2 Other than that, he`s done it every single day.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Alex Goldstein has waited nearly a year and a half --\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN (Creator "Faces of COVID"): Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: To meet Karen Nascembeni.\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI (COVID widow): In the flesh. How are you?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: I`m okay. How are you?\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: I`m good. Better now for seeing you in the flesh.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: You know --\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: It`s been too long in the making.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: I`ve never met anybody from "Faces of COVID".\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: Really?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: It`s always just been from behind the screen.\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: And so you`re the first person I`ve actually met whose loved one was on the platform.\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: You are kidding. My God.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: I know.\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: How lucky. I feel honored.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Karen`s late husband, Steven Richard, "a kind, funny, gentle soul, whose hearts and hands were always open to helping others" was memorialized on the "Faces of COVID" \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, which was created by Goldstein as a way to personalize the pandemic`s toll.\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: No one else has put a face to these victims the way you have. Otherwise, they`re just a number. You`ve brought humanity to this god awful pandemic.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: Can you tell me a little more about Steven?\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: He was one in a million. We had a mad love affair. You know, for the 30 years that we were together. He was salt of the earth. He was a kayaker. He was a photographer, and he was the type that could be really invisible. So he always got the most poignant shots.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: How did you meet?\par \par \uc2 KAREN NASCEMBENI: We met through a mutual friend and I looked at him and I thought, "Oh my God, he`s the boy next door. This is the kind of normal guy I should like."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Goldstein has featured more than 7,000 stories on "Faces of COVID".\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: I try to wake up each morning, look at the submissions that have come in from family members, do a bit of my own research. I`ve done it almost every single day since March 28th, 2020. It has become more of a required ritual in my life than I think I could have ever anticipated.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: In many posts, it is the picture which gives him pause.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: All that "Faces of COVID" tries to do is get folks to stop just for a minute, and bear witness and look at their faces and say their names and create just a little space for them.\par \par \uc2 I love this one, this quote that the family gave, "He was never one for religiosity, but he wanted you to know that if you find him in heaven, he will gladly accept a good beer and a juicy steak, Brazilian, please, American food is terrible."\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is really vulnerable stuff. You are opening up on the most famously toxic platform in social media. You are putting your loved one out there with a couple of lines about who they were. And then you`re holding your breath to see what the world does with it.\par \par \uc2 And the things that people share, it just always blows me away.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This is like a digital cemetery. You just put some of the quotes that I wish headstones had on them.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: Right. It`s a good way to put it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Roughly 5,000 People saw the face of Linda Benson of Oklahoma City.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: This was one where the photo hit me, just this kind of relaxed smile. You talk about what would you want on your tombstone? As a parent, "My brother and I were raised and taught by a wise lady and we are forever grateful for her love." She was a sweet, sensitive and encouraging person.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, what else could you want as your legacy? This one was particularly powerful because --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Who is this?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: So this is Dr. Chandra, 57, of Ohio. He was a cardiologist. And I had found his story through a news article. And his son reached out and saw it on "Faces of COVID" and said, "Can I add some words?"\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What were the words his son wanted to add?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: He said that "Dr. Chandra was a fierce advocate for his patients and for the health of his community. He spent his life educating others on preventative medicine, caretaking to him was not a profession, but a calling. He approached everyone as equals with kindness and love. He is remembered fondly by his community, one that stretches three continents."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The 38-year-old Goldstein, who spent his late 20s, as a press secretary for a former Massachusetts governor sees the "Faces of COVID" \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account as a first draft of history.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: This history is unfolding right now as we speak.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Do you feel protective about it?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: Oh, yes. I feel a responsibility to make sure that these people are treated with dignity and respect.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I heard someone say over the course of the pandemic, in speaking about someone who died of COVID, may their memory be a blessing. You are preserving memories.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: I`m trying. It is too easy in this pandemic, if it hasn`t happened to you to look the other way. And we need to be uncomfortable and vulnerable enough to sit with it, and to look at it and to say the names and look at the pictures. I know how hard it is, I feel it every morning. But the alternative, it`s not acceptable.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What has been the impact of this \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account?\par \par \uc2 ALEX GOLDSTEIN: You know, what I hope the impact of "Faces of COVID" has been is to restore some semblance of empathy into how we see each other. Anyone can come here and say goodbye and watch a bunch of strangers participate in that with them, and I think there`s meaning in that. I hope there`s meaning in that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: All those faces, and so many more.\par \par \uc2 You know, one story we didn`t highlight was this. There was a woman who reached out to Alex saying that she was thinking about her dad hysterically, as she termed it one night, and so she just googled his name just to see what she might find.\par \par \uc2 And sure enough, up popped her dead. The daughter told Alex, seeing that tweet with his name and his face made me feel like there was someone out there that would help me feel like my dad is not just a number. You honored our pain, she said.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That`s the power of this, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: David, yes, you did that and so did he. I like, "May their memory be a blessing." You know, it`s interesting to me when you lose someone you love, most people think, well, I won`t bring it up because it`s uncomfortable. But people actually want to talk about their loved ones. They want to share that with you and why they meant much.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I love what he`s doing and I love that you brought this story to us, David Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank you. And remember, Gayle, there were times in the pandemic, where a tweet was sometimes the only way that someone could actually have the public celebrate their loved one because you couldn`t hold a funeral. Remember that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right. I do remember, and you can`t turn away. Thank you, David Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow, I do feel good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That could be your theme song even with the wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It so describes you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The spin and the split, and do my little --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All you need is your --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know that`s your alarm getting out of bed in the morning.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I want to see the split.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Throwing the sheet off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s coming. It`s coming. It`s coming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know you can do that James Brown little swag.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, doesn`t everybody know how to do the little --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at that. Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? Everybody knows how to do the James Brown.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clark on the camera is embarrassed for you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everybody here is embarrassed for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, I`ve got some breaking news here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is some breaking news now, you may have seen it pop up in your notification on your phone, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  -- it deals with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and his mega deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This morning, the billionaire tweeted the $44 billion deal is temporarily on hold. He said it`s been delayed pending more details on fake and spam accounts on the social media platform. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment about Musk`s announcement.\par \par \uc2 So here`s what`s going on. There was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does this mean, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There was a Reuters -- when Mr. Musk tweeted, he linked to a Reuters article that suggested that there were less than five percent of users on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that were spam or bot accounts. For some reason, I don`t know if it`s because he thinks it`s too low or he thinks that there will be, you know, that there are more than \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is announcing. But that`s apparently why he said he has put this deal on hold.\par \par \uc2 If he doesn`t go through with the deal, he`ll have to pay a billion dollar penalty. Remember that --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, he would have to pay a billion dollar penalty. It`s not sure how that would work. But he has put up \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock and he`s also sold \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock. Meanwhile, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has taken a massive hit over the last month, it`s down 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is his thing. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is the thing that he would like to own, but \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is his real thin and that stock has been crushed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s been crushed. I read somewhere he`s lost about $100 billion on paper money as stocks have come down in value.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Bad joke then would be what`s a billion dollars then once you`ve lost a hundred billion?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, exactly. But a billion dollars for him I mean it`s a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You still don`t want to lose a billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t want to lose a billion dollars. And you don`t want to give the impression that this was sort of a massive troll that you -- you know, this is something that you`re pinning your desires on and it`s not really that big of a deal whether or not --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no. He is always very strategic. I don`t know. I don`t know what this means. This will be interesting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes. It would be very interesting to see what happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What else have you got, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, I`ve got the need --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For speed.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: For speed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We can`t wait for "Top Gun: Maverick."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony you knew the answer to that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You know the high five thing, the back and high five, right? The high five.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know the high -- it goes like this. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t know that that was from that movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: High five, this way and then you`ve got to go the other way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got a team here.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: When you miss each other.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve got to go like this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we`re going to do it. Okay, here we go.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Miss each other --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This way and that way. Yes. The need for speed, "Top Gun: Maverick."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I get the volleyball part.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is back in the theaters of course, as Maverick, \uc2 Pete Mitchell\uc2 . Can`t wait for the film to come out. Obviously, so tomorrow, \uc2 CBS\uc2  is airing \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2  "Top Gun" as part of "\uc2 CBS\uc2  Saturday Night Movies."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Gun: Maverick.")\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Volleyball scene.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To celebrate -- to celebrate the original film ahead of the sequel later this month.\par \par \uc2 And throughout the broadcast, there will be exclusive behind the scenes look at "Top Gun: Maverick" which hits theaters on May 27th.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, that`s good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  is the parent company of \uc2 CBS\uc2 . Very excited for this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Excited about this movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Everybody is.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right, it`s got --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even if it wasn`t Paramount+, I would be --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m still very excited.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How`s it going?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes. There has been a lot of buzz, really good buzz about it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very good reviews.\par \par \uc2 Best thing about "Top Gun" is you learned that actually real fighter pilots don`t have those cool call signs. They don`t -- are not called Maverick. They`re not called Goose. They`ve got other names.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they`ve got Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Other names?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Like the guys, laughing people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, thank you. Coming up, Anthony Mason with Florence Welch of Florence and The Machine. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 Local weather right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Seven forty seven. Time for your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You never know who you`ll see in the greenroom. Ahead, we`ll talk with one of the top Republicans in the Senate that would be you, top Republican, Minority Whip, Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2  about the possibility that the Supreme Court will overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, the intense debate over Roe v. Wade. We will ask Republican Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , what happens if it gets overturned?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil. What do we mean when we talk about resilience? Well, it`s not all about putting on a brave face as we`ll learn from the author of "The Modern Loss Handbook."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And I`m Dana Jacobson, Florence Welch and her band Florence and The Machine are blasting back after pandemic anxiety. Anthony Mason asked her about their brand new album.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear it`s a great conversation, but of course, it`s Anthony.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight o`clock."\par \par \uc2 The national shortage of baby formula is quickly becoming a crisis.\par \par \uc2 DESTINY FEATHERSTONE: There was like, just enough for her month`s supply and I wanted to get it all, but I was like I don`t want to take from the other babies.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fire crews are battling a wildfire in Southern California that`s already destroyed at least 20 homes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s really indicative of the prolonged drought, the climate change, everything we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy is among five Republicans subpoenaed by the Committee investigating the January 6th riot.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: All five Congressmen have criticized the subpoenas and called the Committee polarized.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Doncic deep in his bag like the fries were at the bottom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the \uc2 Dallas Mavericks\uc2  force a game seven in their playoff series against the Phoenix Sun.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Dinwiddie launching from three, a little bit of icing, some sugar on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Put some sugar on that.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with the Senate Republican Minority Whip, \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , who joins us at the table to talk about the battle over abortion rights after that stunning -- and stunning is the word -- the leak from the Supreme Court on Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 He says a Democratic push to enshrine a right to an abortion into Federal law goes too far and is out of step with most Americans. But just last week, a CBS News poll found that 64 percent of Americans do not want Roe v. Wade overturned.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , we`re happy to say joins us at the table. We love it when you come into the studio. It`s nice to have you in person.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk to you about your vote last week. You voted against to codify Roe v. Wade. Why did you vote that way? What was your thinking?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I`ve been -- I have a history in my time in public life of being pro-life, but that vote was more than just codifying Roe, which is why they lost even people who typically support Roe versus Wade on our side, people like Senators Collin and Murkowski -- Collins and Murkowski.\par \par \uc2 But basically, it was more expansive. It went beyond Roe, and it was a piece of legislation, I think they knew was going to fail. We had that vote actually a few weeks ago, and I think Senator Schumer did it to satisfy political constituency that wanted that vote, but knew it was going to fail.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He thinks it is going to galvanize Democrats in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is there any concern on the Republican side that this issue could in fact, galvanize Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I think that they`re looking for something to distract from the economy, inflation, and the border, and some of the issues that they would rather not talk about. I think this is an issue, this is a pivot -- a clear pivot on their part to try and get on something that they think they can play offense with.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of it is going to come down to, and the politics around this issue is very contentious, always has been.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fraught is a good word to describe that on both sides.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Fraught is a good word. It is. And so how that plays politically remains to be seen because there are many places around the country, obviously, where you are, if you`re a candidate running and you`re a pro-life candidate that`s going to work to your political advantage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But most Americans it seems, support Roe v. Wade, would you support it with some restrictions?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I think to your point, Gayle, if you look at the way this polls, you know Roe versus Wade, basically the decision, the Supreme Court decision said that the state`s interest becomes compelling at the point of viability, which keeps moving back.\par \par \uc2 And you know, right now 65 percent of Americans don`t think you ought to have abortions past the first trimester. So into the second or third trimester, 80 percent believe you shouldn`t have them after the second trimester, and we`re really out of step with the world.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`re one of only seven countries in the world that allows abortions after 20 weeks, and those include, of course, North Korea and China. Most European nations, France is at 14 weeks, Germany is at 12 weeks. So most countries have found a political consensus around restrictions that are, I guess, we start much sooner in a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that where you are?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I was going to say, so why not support something that had those restrictions in those timelines that you speak of?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): I think the question for a lot of people is going to be, is it a human life and when does that -- when does life begin? I think what you`re going to see is State Legislatures because this doesn`t do away with it, it just returns it to the state.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To the state level, yes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Are going to have to find political consensus, and some states are probably going to define it differently. My position has always been exceptions, rape, incest, life of the mother, but a ban.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m wondering if you`re still enjoying your job, and I`m asking you that because there`s a bestselling book out right now, I know you`ve heard of it, "This Will Not Pass" it says.\par \par \uc2 You consider not running for re-election this year, but Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said, "Hold on just a second senator." So first, are you enjoying your job? And why did you -- is it true that you considered not running for re-election?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I did --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that a good smile?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): I`ve got -- well, I`ve got, you know, like you, I`ve got grandkids now running around. It was -- it was a hard -- hard decision. But in the end you do things because you think it`s some place you can make a difference, and I`d like to believe public life is still a place where you can make a difference.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And going back to the beginning discussion, there is one thought -- we hear so much about as you talk about, when does life begin? There`s a lot of talk of what the rights are of being a fetus, be it an unborn child, however you refer to that? What about the women and the families that we`re talking about and their rights, too? How do you balance that in the consideration?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, I think you have to -- you`ve got to be compassionate. And you know, the solution if you`re a pro-life person, and I am, and if you believe that`s a human life, then you believe it needs to be protected. But there is also -- there is -- there`s a mom and a lot of vulnerable moms, and I think that the responsibility for people who hold a pro-life position isn`t just to state that and check the box, you`ve got to actually demonstrate that you`re willing to help care for moms who are going to carry babies to the point of birth.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Or give moms a chance to have that say, a potential mom to control some of what is their body also. Trust in them, and in doctors, also.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of the questions is -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, no, I mean, I think that you`re right. And that`s why, you know, as you look at this issue, as I`ve said, you`ve got to do it in a way, I think there`s a compassionate way and understand that your impact.\par \par \uc2 Now I`m not -- I`m a middle-aged White guy, I`m probably not the person you should be talking about a subject like this, but I also have a wife and two very accomplished daughters who are pro-life.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re a middle aged White guy in a position of power to make a difference on something to millions.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): And I think you have to listen -- you have to listen very carefully what people are saying. But in the end, if I`m going to err, I`m going to err on the side of life.\par \par \uc2 But I think and how you determine the course of action is going to be largely based upon where the political consensus is, and I think, you know, right now, this is a decision that has been made by nine unelected Judges, and it is now going to go back to the people and their elected representatives, which is, I think, where a lot of folks think issues like this should be decided.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a very big deal for a government to tell a woman she has to carry a pregnancy through to term.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think we all agree on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And would you tell some of your other middle aged White men that we like, middle aged White men here at the table. Spread the word. We`re very friendly here.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We like old people, right? We like everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like all people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That comes very close to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like all points of view.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That comes very close to calling me middle aged, and I still claim to be youthful.\par \par \uc2 Senator Thune, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right now, it is 8:08. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Florence and The Machine front woman, Florence Welch, set out to create a classic album, then the pandemic struck.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: The record was supposed to be like a confident and cohesive, like well-rounded body of art like -- and I think it`s a descent into madness.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s what we all did.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Florence and The Machine performance.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I said it earlier, sometimes that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Welch talks with Anthony Mason about writing and dancing her way through the chaos with her new album, "Dance Fever."\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dog Days are Over.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s "Dog Days are Over" from the multiplatinum selling band, Florence and The Machine. Their new album "Dance Fever" out today.\par \par \uc2 And Anthony Mason sat down with front woman Florence Welch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Florence+ the Machine performance.)\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: The record was supposed to be like a confident and cohesive, like well-rounded body of art. I think it`s a descent into madness, but that`s what it was.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s what we all did.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Florence+ the Machine performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Florence and The Machine`s fifth album, "Dance Fever" emerged from the pandemic. The lockdown was especially distressing for the artist known to swirl across the stage and sing from this scaffolding.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: I don`t think I`ll ever take a live performance for granted again, like I was so grief stricken when I thought that that would never come back.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Equally upsetting early on was the loss of physical connection with her family.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: My whole family had COVID, so I was terrified.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She was especially missing her niece, Bonnie, born on Florence`s 30th birthday.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: And my niece tried to run to me because she`s three and my sister had to stop her.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You saw the consequences of what this thing --\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: Yes, that uncomplicated love and joy that our relationship had, I think the kind of the -- I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No, it`s okay.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: And they just go up so fast, so the loss of two years of getting to hug her and she was fine with it though. She didn`t care. She was just a kid, you know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But you missed it.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: I missed it a lot.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "King.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: For Florence, now 35, family and performing are opposing urges.\par \par \uc2 Her new song "King" is about the artist`s ego, battling an internal desire for domesticity.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "King.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s a very striking line.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: So I did write that one in my kitchen after an argument, and so the song is about the splitting of two desires and the sort of agony of --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "King.")\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: I think the witchery of it is that it feels like a possession, in a sense.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Which part of it feels like a possession?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: Well, the sudden desire for maybe like family and domesticity and it feels like something that`s coming up upon me despite, "Oh, it`s here, it`s time. Got to go. Got to get started."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "King.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Do you distrust that instinct?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: Which one?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ah, question. I don`t think you distrust music or do you?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: I distrust my sense of knowing what makes me happy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Florence and The Machine performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In her songs, Florence has often fought epic battles with her fears, at first they appeared mythologically as mighty oceans.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Florence and The Machine performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But since she got sober in 2014, she has named her demons more directly.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Free.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In the video for her new song "Free" actor, Bill Nighy plays the part of her anxiety always by her side.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Free.")\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: I am eight years sober, and I think even before the pandemic, my struggles with mental health were really like really intense. Because actually what sobriety does is it -- you come to see yourself clearer than you ever have done before and a lot of the stuff that you drank to get away from is just there all the time.\par \par \uc2 That`s always a nice feeling when you`ve written a song and you put it life --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We met in Sir John Soane`s Museum in London in the 19th Century home of a renowned architect that Florence had been eager to visit.\par \par \uc2 This is such a cool room.\par \par \uc2 In its moody rooms, she talked about singing in a lower register on the new record.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: And that was the monster voice. So yes, a lot of this record is monster voice\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Florence and The Machine performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you enjoy doing a monster voice?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: I love it, because I think there`s also such an expectation, especially if in the public eye if you`re seem to be particularly like a good person, or even this sense of like you`re an angel. I am not an angel, I am like a recovering alcoholic.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "King.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Florence shot the stunning videos for "Dance Fever" with Director Autumn de Wilde this past November in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: It was really magical, and you know, we just have been so heartbroken and mainly just trying to check the people are okay.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is everyone okay?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: So far, like, yes, the crews and the dancers got out. They`ve just been through hell.\par \par \uc2 And Autumn de Wilde was like, but what about this work? Should we even put it out? And her assistant was like, no, we want our work to be shown. And like, we`re really proud of this. You know, this is what`s been taken away.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Free.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The video for "Free" ends with this dedication: "To the spirit, creativity and perseverance of our brave Ukrainian friends."\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE WELCH: We had a really special time there, and so far, thank God, people seem to have made it to safety.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And those videos are absolutely stunning, just beautiful. And it really does highlight what`s at stake in Ukraine, which was not of course, the intent when they made them, but the effect is really powerful.\par \par \uc2 I will also say, Florence took up dancing after she got sober, and I asked her why. She actually said she envisioned songs with dance now, and she said because I needed someplace to put my energy and it is all coming out in the music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was so candid with you. Had you talked to her before?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`ve talked to her four or five times over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You guys do seem to have a rapport. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I don`t think it`s possible for Florence to have a guarded moment. She puts everything on the table, which is one of the reasons it`s such a pleasure to talk to her.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: What she expressed with her niece also, so many people could relate to and it`s true. The niece didn`t care, but she cared in that moment what she missed out on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So true.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We appreciate it. You can hear more of Anthony`s conversation with Florence Welch on today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast. Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, as we mark the very unfortunate loss of around a million Americans to COVID in this country, many people may be struggling to cope. We`ll talk to the author of a new book that -- a new book, rather that may help you come out stronger. It`s good.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you -- what do we call this segment, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Table because we`re at the table and we`re talking and Tony Dokoupil, you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m going first. I`ve got a more serious topic than usual for talk of a table and it has to do with babies, also parents. One of the reason parents to newborns are so tired is not only because they don`t sleep well, it`s because you are worried all the time that at some point in the night something`s going to go wrong.\par \par \uc2 There is a phenomenon known as Sudden Infant Death Syndrome, and about 3,400 babies in the U.S. every year a die of SIDS. That`s according to the C.D.C., and it has been a mystery for all of time.\par \par \uc2 Well, researchers in Australia have now come up with an explanation. They studied blood samples from hundreds of infants, and they found that babies who died in this previously mysterious way, from SIDS, had much lower levels of a particular enzyme in their blood. This is compared to babies who died in other ways and living infants.\par \par \uc2 And it`s an enzyme tied to the trigger in your body to wake you up if you`re not sleeping.\par \par \uc2 So, the reason I think this is so amazing is twofold. First, parents are wracked with such unbelievable guilt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Guilt. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And doubt when this goes wrong and they blame themselves and they wonder if they could have done something differently. This suggests, no, it`s something in the body that you cannot control.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing that`s amazing is the doctor who led the research, Carmel Harrington, 29 years ago lost her own baby to SIDS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad you`re sharing this story, you know, SIDS is always so frightening, and for years, we were told don`t put them on their tummies because you know, when my kids were growing up, you put them on their tummies. You had blankets and pillows and all sorts of things.\par \par \uc2 And now, there is nothing in the crib, you wrap them up like little burritos and they`re laying on their backs. This is important.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Takeaway is, you`ve got to do all that stuff anyway.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You do all that stuff, there`s still a thing that can happen.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: There is still something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that`s not your fault, and so the next step now that you have an answer here is let`s find a treatment.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That is exactly what I was thinking. Now maybe, move on from that. Inspiring, if you can look at it that way.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is inspiring. This is amazing.\par \par \uc2 A speech by a valedictorian, a student diagnosed with nonverbal autism. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BONKER (Rollins College valedictorian): I have struggled my whole life with not being heard or accepted, here I stand, each day, I choose to celebrate small victories, and today, I am celebrating a big victory with all of you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So that`s Elizabeth Bonker who graduated from Rollins College in Florida, Sunday. She used a text to speech computer program so she can verbalize her speech. She uses one finger to type. She is just 24 years old.\par \par \uc2 This commencement speech was amazing. Here are some of the standouts from it. "God gave you a voice, use it."\par \par \uc2 "The irony of a nonspeaking autistic encouraging you to use your voice is not lost on me because if you can see the worth in me, then you can see the worth in everyone you meet."\par \par \uc2 And she went on to tell them, she is one of the lucky few non nonspeaking autistics who has been taught to type, "That intervention unlocked my mind from its silent cage, enabling me to communicate and to be educated."\par \par \uc2 And the power of that, she started a nonprofit to try to make this kind of communication accessible to all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like what you said inspiring. Inspiring with a capital I.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, yes. Find that speech. Watch it all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. Congratulations, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is about the winner of an auction to be a space tourist on a Blue Origin rocket. This is inspiring, too in a different way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To win an auction, it means you just pay a lot of money, right?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Then you win.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but listen what he`s doing though. \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2  is his name. He is the CEO of the hedge fund, \uc2 Citadel\uc2 . He placed a winning bid of $8 million dollars on Monday. It was a buy one give one auction and a benefit for Robinhood. Well, that`s a nonprofit in New York City. I think this is very cool. You go \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He won two seats. The second will be given to a New York City teacher, and it turns out, Griffin is not taking the trip to space. So he`s giving the seat away to another educator.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Very nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No word on when they will launch. It`s not often that people spent $8 million and it doesn`t have anything to do with them.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is giving in -- this is what \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2  said: "I hope this moment will ignite the imagination of our students and inspire the next generation to push the boundaries of humanity." So I think it is awesome. Blue Origin and Griffin will participate in the selection process for picking the two teachers. But this is a great thing for two lucky teachers out there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very cool. I want to talk to those teachers when they emerge and see who they are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And talk to \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2  too about why he is doing it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Indeed. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 Up next, to a tough topic, the one a lot of people are dealing with today. It`s a conversation about grief and the loss of a loved one.\par \par \uc2 Author Rebecca Soffer is in our progressive greenroom. There she is. We`ll find out about her experience with profound loss and how she is using that experience to help others.\par \par \uc2 But first time, for a check if your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: As the nation marks one million COVID deaths, we want to draw your attention to a stunning statistic. It is estimated that each death has a significant impact on as many as nine people.\par \par \uc2 A new book called "The Modern Loss Handbook" is an interactive guide for moving through grief and building resilience. Author Rebecca Soffer lost her mother in a car crash 16 years ago. Four years later, her father died suddenly.\par \par \uc2 After struggling to manage her own grief, Soffer cofounded her own online community to help others through the process. Rebecca Soffer is joining us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER ("The Modern Loss Handbook" author): Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You know, it`s interesting, we talk about grief, I think even with kids at a younger age, so people know they know the steps, but why the need then to have a handbook still anyway?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Because we still kind of suck at this conversation. I mean, can we use that word on morning news shows? I think so. We do a really bad job at normalizing something that is just one of the most normal human experiences that a person can have.\par \par \uc2 I mean if we`re going to love anybody that`s alive, we`re going to grieve them when they`re gone and we kind of get hit -- we get bogged down and like, all the right things that we should be saying and the platitudes that we`re fed and like that doesn`t end up helping anyone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t help. I like how you said, this is not an old school grief book, no angels, pastels, no kittens -- and you like kittens - -\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No toxic positivity or suggestions to find gratitude and everything. What do you mean toxic positivity?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: I mean, like, you know, the insistence with like that gridded smile of like, no bad days, but like the prayer hands, and if you want to use prayer hand emojis, that`s fine. Like, I`m not judging. I`m talking about the intent behind it, which is like, if you`re close to hearing somebody`s actual story of what`s actually going on, you`re probably forcing some toxic positivity down their throat.\par \par \uc2 There are bad days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: There are bad days even 10 years down the line. That`s okay. And it`s not just okay, it`s like a normal thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you said sometimes, Rebecca, it is okay to be on the floor and it`s not a heap and that`s all right. Listen, I like the less pithy ways, but accurate stages of grief, total poop show. Oh, wow. I think I`m totally fine. Sheer panic. I hate people. I need people. No, I actually do hate people.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: This is an accurate flowchart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, in one day.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Of what is inside of my brain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Yes, and you know, you experience all those things, sometimes in one day, sometimes in one hour, and sometimes over the course of just like, the entire arc of the experience.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I`m just trying to show again, like, at all points toward this is all normal, like as long as you`re not hurting yourself or anybody else in the process, just you do you. You grieve here on life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As I think people can tell, this is an irreverent guide. There are stickers in the back, there`s something called sad-libs, but I`m curious for people who are confronting other people, meeting other people who are going through this process, how do you signal that thing that you think is so important that you`re open to hearing whatever the reality is?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Yes, I mean, we were talking about this earlier and I think that we get so overwhelmed by like, look, this is the United States. We like fixing things. We like having the fix. We like making things better.\par \par \uc2 You can`t make it better for another person. If they`ve lost somebody they love, all you can do is show up for them and make it clear that whatever they`re going through, you`re aware that it`s not contagious. Grief is not contagious. That`s a message I`d like to get through.\par \par \uc2 By sharing your story with someone, you`re not going to make them sad, or you`re not going to like make it so that that`s going to happen to them, and if you just make it clear that you know, you`re willing to sit with them in a place that`s really uncomfortable, sometimes sad, sometimes rage- y, sometimes darkly hilarious, and sit with them through it. That is what they need.\par \par \uc2 They need acknowledgement. They need to feel like they`re being seen in something really hard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said that grief is exhausting and draining, and you could be grieving for -- everyone can be grieving the same person differently.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But, I saw so many things that I`ve done wrong reading your book, by the way. I think, you know, it is irreverent, but it also is very serious. You give us some practical things.\par \par \uc2 When somebody is at work and they return back to work, the worst thing you can say is "How are you?"\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How many times have we all done that, Rebecca?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can`t say that?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: No. Okay, so I`d like to say that I screw up on a daily basis, not only in every --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How are you doing? Yes, you say --\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: But how are you doing? I think it`s more about like anchoring something in time. You know, like, I`ve gotten a lot of hands on my shoulders with like, "How are you?" Like in the stairwells, and I feel very cornered, and I feel like it`s really incumbent upon me to share that I`m completely fine, because the person doesn`t actually want to know or they feel uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 And I think that when you ask more specific questions, like "How are you doing today?" "How is today for you?" You know, "Hey, I see that there`s an anniversary coming up, like Mother`s Day," like anchoring something within time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So those are triggering things.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Yes, and just be aware of that and just say, "I know this is coming," Like, "I`m here to talk. I`m here to text." You know, it`s not that hard to show meaningful support.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And actually be there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, grief milestones are cool time machines. I thought your book was so good.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So good, and you said when all else fails, you can just say to the person, "It`s nice to see you."\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought, it`s nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I could say Gayle definitely means it because she`s actually taking my book. I`m going to send it to somebody else.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: I`ll send you another one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mine is all written up, yes. It`s true.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It kind of helps, as you said, get everybody out of their own heads when it comes to this.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SOFFER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We obviously all enjoyed the book, "The Modern Loss Handbook." If Gayle is not bringing you a copy, it does go on sale Tuesday. You can get your hands on it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up on "CBS Saturday Morning," the beauty industry is responsible for sending nearly 900 pounds of waste to landfills every minute. Think about that -- every minute.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We`re going to introduce you to an entrepreneur who is helping salons keep the planet and their clients beautiful. It`s really an incredible work that they are doing across Canada and the U.S. We`ll dive into that.\par \par \uc2 And on "Sunday Morning," Tony speaks with comedian and "SNL" star Michael Che. He talks about his journey from public housing. Why standup is his first love and the new season of his HBO --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a great --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You got to hang out with him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, I know. It was really fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I like him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Like two seconds after that clip we just showed, he gave me some tips on like if I were doing my first open mic, like how to be -- you know, I was not brave enough to try a few jokes. I saved the ears of our crew.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I can`t wait to see that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was really cool. And by the way, Che, I asked him, "Are you named after Che Guevara? The Cuban Revolutionary?" He is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. He is?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dad was a history buff. How about that? Much more to come Sunday.\par \par \uc2 All right, and Monday in our "Kindness 101" series, Steve Hartman and his kids bring us a story of determination about an auto mechanic who switched gears to become a doctor in his 40s. He told Steve that he didn`t let the naysayers sway him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARL ALLAMBY (Auto mechanic turned doctor): I`ll tell you what, nine years is going to pass anyway, so I`d rather be some place I want to be than some place that I could have been.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dr. Allamby shares how he stayed the course. That`s Monday on "Kindness 101." Love that series.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that, too. You know, another series, I love, David Begnaud has a piece we ran earlier about the "Faces of COVID." This just in from Alex Goldstein, the man who is doing what David called a digital cemetery. He said he is now getting flooded with submissions now from people who want their loved ones featured on "Faces of COVID."\par \par \uc2 He said: "That was my definition of success. I can`t thank you enough for getting the word out. If you need any reminder of why you`re in this business. Here`s why."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People see it, and now they want to participate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I do. I think it`s so important. We keep throwing around this number, this million people, but they are dearly loved and dearly missed, and we don`t forget that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We`ll see you on Monday on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, a look back at all that mattered.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: A U.S. Defense official says 10,000 Russian soldiers are on the move north of Mariupol and they are massing on the outskirts of this village.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Forces allegedly bombed a school where the civilians were sheltering.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA KVIEN (United States Charge d`affaires ad interim to Ukraine): I haven`t seen any clear victories for President Putin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The death toll from the COVID-19 pandemic has now reached one million Americans --\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : One million empty chairs around the family dinner table.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Those whose names and stories may never be known publicly. May their memories be a blessing.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY (Veteran): I wanted to read one book. I don`t care if it`s about Mickey Mouse. I want to read one book before I die.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He said get in there and learn, baby, because you ain`t going to learn it in a pine box. That is as real as it gets.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They`re off. Coming up on the outside. Oh my goodness, Rich Strike has done it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said you were putting all of your money on the horse, Summer is Tomorrow at the derby. I`m just curious, how did that go?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Not the best. Maybe I should stick to the weather.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the record, the horse finish dead last.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dead last. Dead last.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The very back of the pack.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Oh no. Oh no. Oh no.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Forget me not.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Forget me not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, very good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There you go. Yes. You know the "Men in Black" version.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Galaxy defenders.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE MONSTER: Mecha Cookie Monster here to save the day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I got this. We`re going to go backstage.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE MONSTER: Cookie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to have some. We`re going to have some.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE MONSTER: Cookie. We`ve got cookie.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mecha Builders.")\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE MONSTER: You need me to smash anything this morning. Oh, yes, that guy over there. Me see.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That guy right there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll send it out there to you.\par \par \uc2 What`s up, Phil?\par \par \uc2 PHIL: Prompter one. I will --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You good, Phil?\par \par \uc2 What`s going on, Coach? How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 BILL COWHER: How you doing, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m good.\par \par \uc2 BILL COWHER: I`ve got to tell you, the Nickelodeon game that you guys have put on, my grandkids watch it and looking forward to seeing it on Christmas Day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, I was going to ask you about that. Now, you need to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Doja Cat performance.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dolly Parton with who -- Doja Cat. Mexican pizza, fans including Doja Cat lobbied very successfully for its return. I haven`t tried it, but now, I kind of want to do it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what makes it Mexican?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t have the answer for that, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m like uh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You showed pictures of Kirby --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When you were talking about her curls.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it was just pictures of the baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, there`s Kirby`s curls.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The baby is in every picture -- the baby has no hair.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, is the baby in the picture? I didn`t notice that, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In Thailand, the Health Minister has announced a plan in that country to send up to a million cannabis plants free of charge to households all across the country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Probably some people watching this right now that are like, "You know, where`s my passport?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would be nervous getting the cannabis plant shipped to me. I can`t keep basil or cilantro alive on the counter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The lettuce is going bad in a couple of days.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everything is going to wilt in my house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How are you going to keep a cannabis plant alive?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220513ei5d000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Rights Vote Fails\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Scott MacFarlane, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 532 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Female House Democrats staged a protest to show their disapproval of the Senate vote yesterday as a nation waits for a landmark ruling on the issue from the Supreme Court. Scott MacFarlane is outside the High Court for us once again this morning.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Congress took a largely symbolic vote on this measure to codify Roe versus Wade and as expected it failed, raising the stakes higher for this ruling from the High Court.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: No twist, no turns, it went nearly straight down party lines.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice President of the United States): The motion is not agreed to.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Senate blocked debate on a measure writing some federal abortion rights into law with all Republicans --\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Their extreme proposal goes way, way beyond codifying the status quo.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And West Virginia Democrat, Joe Manchin opposing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): We should not be dividing this country further than we`re already divided.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  presided over the vote.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And this vote clearly suggests that the Senate is not where the majority of Americans are on this issue.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Inside, House Democrats crossed the Capitol chanting --\par \par \uc2 (U.S. REPRESENTATIVES chanting "My decision, my body.")\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And outside.\par \par \uc2 So what do you do next?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Well, next we just start campaigning like crazy.\par \par \uc2 GOV. J.B. PRITZKER (D-IL): I think it needs to be a drumbeat.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  says Democrats in his own party are not sounding the alarm loud enough leading into the midterm elections. His State of Illinois is now surrounded by five states that are set to restrict or completely end access to abortion if Roe versus Wade is struck down by the High Court as anticipated.\par \par \uc2 GOV. J.B. PRITZKER (D-IL): We need to be talking about this making sure that voters know about this, from now through November, and frankly as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: As crowds continue to gather near some of the Justices` home, state and local law enforcement agencies are issuing awareness reports that demonstrations may become flashpoints for violence with little to no warning.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Outside the homes of these Supreme Court Justices, there has been a request for added protection from the Governors of Virginia and Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Saturday, abortion rights groups rally at the Washington Monument across from the White House as they try to continue to have their voices heard --Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We know this will continue to be the topic of conversation. Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220513ei5c0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Senate Votes On Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Scott MacFarlane, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 780 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today, the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is set to debate abortion rights ahead of that highly anticipated Supreme Court ruling. The Majority Leader says today`s vote will shine like a floodlight to expose where every senator stands on this very important issue.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane reports from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Protesters chanting.)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A week of protests, vigils, and student walkouts nationwide give way to a seminal debate today in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Why is something that is repeatedly referred to as settled law on the threshold of being swept away like so much dust?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Senate Democrats are fighting for a bill already passed in the House that would protect abortion access nationwide. They need 60 votes to pass it, but there is no indication they`ll get there.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): This radical pro-abortion legislation removes a range of common sense protections that exist in states across the country.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And with the critical November midterms hanging in the balance the decision may not be a strong driving force in elections. CBS News polling found if Roe versus Wade is overturned, it`s likely to have no effect on nearly half of Democrats and three-quarters of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary): There is spillover into labor force participation --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But according to the nation`s first female Treasury Secretary, it could have a major effect on women in the workforce and their ability to boost earning potential through higher education.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 : Eliminating the right of women to make decisions about when and whether to have children would have very damaging effects on the economy and would set women back decades.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Senate is poised to block this measure by the end of the day and the Supreme Court is poised to rule by the end of next month -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Got it, Scott. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news overnight: A reporter for the Al Jazeera Network was shot and killed while covering an Israeli raid in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is tracking the latest developments for us from London.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz, it`s always sad when a journalist dies in the field. Reportedly, this journalist was wearing one of those familiar blue flak jackets that said "press" clearly.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, what else do we know?\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 What we do know is that Shireen Abu Akleh set out early this morning to cover an Israeli raid in the town of Jenin. She was as you say wearing a helmet and body armor that was clearly marked as "press" when she was shot. Now, the journalist seen here next to her said they were targeted by Israeli Forces.\par \par \uc2 Her employer, Al Jazeera said the veteran reporter was and I`m quoting here, "assassinated in cold blood by Israeli forces."\par \par \uc2 Now, Israel`s Prime Minister called her death unfortunate and blamed it on armed Palestinians claiming they were quote "firing indiscriminately." A second journalist was shot in the back, but survived. Ali Al-Samudi says Israeli soldiers shot both him and Abu Akleh. Now, the U.S. Ambassador to Israel is calling for a thorough investigation.\par \par \uc2 Israeli forces have carried out near daily raids in the West Bank amid a series of deadly attacks inside Israel with Jenin a main flashpoint. Now on a personal note, I knew Shireen who was both Palestinian and American. We used to work together in Jerusalem and she was a kind and generous colleague, always looking to give voice to those who were so rarely heard from.\par \par \uc2 But more than anything, she was a pioneer, a household name across the Arab world, Shireen started her career in the 90s when there were few Arab women reporting on the issues that affected their communities. She truly inspired a generation of other Arab women to do the same and will be greatly missed -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I`m so sorry that you knew her, and so sorry for your loss, Imtiaz. It is just another painful reminder about how dangerous this job can be. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220512ei5b0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Key Vote, As Democrats Try To Make Access To Abortion The Law Of The Land\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 615 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, here in Washington, Senate Democrats failed in a bid to make the landmark Roe vs. Wade decision federal law, but succeeded in putting members on the record when it comes to abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 The move comes as the Supreme Court could be on the verge of overturning the constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The motion is not agreed the.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): Even with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  presiding, Senate Democrats fell short of the 60 votes needed to advance the Women`s Health Protection Act to enshrine abortion rights into federal law.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The Senate failed to stand in defense of a woman`s right to make decisions about her own body.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Democrats argue the measure would codify Roe vs. Wade by preserving access to abortion services, while Republicans declared it too extreme.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-Washington): When I said this was a five-alarm fire, I meant it.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JAMES LANKFORD\uc2  (R-Oklahoma): I`m being called a radical extremist because I believe she`s valuable.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin was the only Democrat to side with Republicans against the legislation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-West Virginia): We should not be dividing this country further.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: My body!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My decision!\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: House Democrats walked over to the Senate chamber for the vote.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Abortion is a human right!\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Protests have intensified around the country following the Supreme Court`s leaked draft opinion, including outside of the High Court and some justices` homes.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We are not your incubator!\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Personal threats have also increased among some lawmakers, like Maine`s \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , who called police after protesters left messages outside of her home.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR SUSAN COLLINS (R-Maine): I have received threatening phone calls, letters, threats of sexual assault, threats against my family members.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Organizers are gearing up for another round of demonstrations this weekend in major cities, including along the National Mall in Washington.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA MARQUEZ (Associate Director For Organizing, Planned Parenthood): Our hope is that, through these mobilizations and through our organizing efforts, our elected officials will know that no politicians can hold this over us.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Several states are also readying so-called trigger laws that would restrict access to abortion if Roe is overturned, the governor of Illinois telling CBS News his state is bracing for an influx of women seeking abortions should they take effect in neighboring states.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR J.B. PRITZKER (D-Illinois): We`re trying to provide logistics help, a place to stay, anything we can do to help them exercise their reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Tonight, the attorney general has directed the \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  to ensure the justices` safety, and he`s being briefed on security around the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has also learned that state and local law enforcement agencies remain on high alert. The justices are scheduled to meet tomorrow -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s going to be busy.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion outside the court, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220512ei5b0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 The Fight against Inflation; Senate Votes on Abortion Rights; Journalist Killed in West Bank; New Details in Fugitive Capture; Winds Fuel Massive Wildfire; Unrelenting Battles in Ukraine; Passenger Forced to Land Plane; Growing Baby Formula Shortage; COVID Cases on the Rise; Controversial Bus Search; Slow Burning Success; Navigating Travel and COVID; Cheslie Kryst`s Legacy; Mental Health Support; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nancy Cordes, Imtiaz  Tyab, Charlie D`Agata, Justin Michaels, Janet Shamlian, Anthony Mason,  Scott MacFarlane, Omar Villafranca, Vladimir Duthiers, Peter Greenberg, Sue  Varma \par \uc2 14,142 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 New inflation numbers are out today, President Biden says it`s his top domestic priority.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): I know you`ve got to be frustrated. I know. I can taste it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senators vote today in a bill to protect Americans abortion rights, why Democrats are pushing it even though it`s bound to fail.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Every American will see how they voted.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Terrifying emergency landing in Florida, the passenger with no flying experience had to swoop in to land the plane.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the national baby formula shortage forces some parents to take desperate measures to feed their kids.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR MILLER (Mother): Literally waste a whole tank of gas trying to find formula and maybe only find one can.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Former Celebrity Chef Mario Batali had been found not guilty of criminal indecent assault and battery.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tom Brady has signed a massive 10-year contract with FOX Sports to become an analyst once he retires.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL\uc2  ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): I have very bad news. Tom Brady will be playing three years after he is dead. Tom Brady is not going to stop playing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says if his deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  goes through, he will let former President Trump back on the social media site.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show with James Corden" host): Pluses and minuses go.\par \par \uc2 IAN KARMEL ("The Late, Late Show" head writer): Is it okay yet to say how funny he was on there or no?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I think some parts of Americans will say that, yes. I don`t get the feeling this is one of them.\par \par \uc2 IAN KARMEL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Two to two. Ground ball. Velasquez throws across, he`s done it. Reid Detmers knows a no hitter.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Something he will remember the rest of his life. Solo, no, no for the rookie, baseball is a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But, Jon -- Jon Towers said please don`t explain that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a big night in baseball. I got no hits. That`s why they call it a no hitter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but I was saying you can`t have a no hitter without the help your teammates.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right. Right. It`s a team effort, a lot of good feeling behind that pitch there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The pitcher always gets the credit, Tony, but really.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely. That`s definitely true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a team effort. Jon Tower, just said, "Please don`t explain that again."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re right. You`re right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And there is your Baseball 101 with Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we`ve got a lot of big stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Today, we want to share all of them including something causing serious stress for parents right now, including in my own household, baby formula running low all across the country. We`re talking about empty shelves and for some families, a real threat of running out of food for their babies. Janet Shamlian dove into what`s behind the problem and what parents can do about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, Glass Animals are by some measures the biggest British band since the Spice Girls. Their song "Heat Waves" is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason met with the band in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heat Waves".)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anthony Mason met with the band in Las Vegas and learned just how close they came to tragedy before their breakout. It`s a story that you`ll definitely want to see.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have heard that song.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have heard that song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course. Everyone has heard of that song.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, that`s a great start to the show. Tony knows a song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle knows baseball. I`ve heard a popular song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s wrong with this picture here?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is going to be a great show.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with big news expected today on the cost of living in this country. Gas prices hit another record high overnight and that`s part of why lots of other essentials like groceries are also more expensive.\par \par \uc2 Is there any relief in sight? That is the question.\par \par \uc2 New numbers out today are expected to show we may be turning the corner, but not fast enough for most Americans. Nancy Cordes is at the White House where President Biden says tackling inflation is his top domestic priority.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden is going to be heading to Illinois later this morning to talk with farmers about the very real impact that inflation and supply chain shortages are having on their ability to grow food, and the farmers we spoke to warn that we could see more empty store shelves in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I know that families all across America are hurting because of inflation.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Biden, Tuesday, called inflation the country`s top economic challenge and pointed the finger at two causes.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The first cause of inflation is the once in a century pandemic. A second cause, Mr. Putin`s war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And U.S. farmers bearing the brunt, facing higher prices for fertilizers, seeds, and diesel fuel.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BOYD (Farmer): So you`re looking over $50.00 plus or $60.00 just to fill up one time in my tractor.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: John Boyd is a fourth generation soybean and cattle farmer in Virginia. He says his upfront costs have roughly doubled in one year.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BOYD: We weren`t able to pay for the seeds. So the struggle is real, and the struggle is going to be real for the American people in the coming months as it relates to food.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Krista Swanson, who grows corn and soybeans in Illinois, worries what will happen to those who can`t cover the new costs.\par \par \uc2 KRISTA SWANSON (Farmer): Farming is, you know, a passion and it`s a way of life, but it also is a livelihood. And so, you know, you have to be able to make some money to stay in it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: On Capitol Hill, Republicans argued the White House hasn`t done enough to help struggling Americans.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): President Biden`s mismanagement of the economy and inflation has really trapped Americans in an inflation nightmare.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mr. Biden argued the G.O.P. is serving up blame, but no solutions.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : And what`s the congressional Republican plan? They don`t want to solve inflation by lowering your costs. They want to solve it by raising your taxes and lowering your income.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Biden said yesterday he is considering releasing President Trump`s tariffs on Chinese goods in order to try to bring down the cost of goods here in the U.S., and this morning, all eyes are going to be on the monthly Consumer Price Index report. Economists looking for any sign, Gayle, that inflation has peaked.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody hopes it has. Thank you very much, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 Today, the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is set to debate abortion rights ahead of that highly anticipated Supreme Court ruling. The Majority Leader says today`s vote will shine like a floodlight to expose where every senator stands on this very important issue.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane reports from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Protesters chanting.)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A week of protests, vigils, and student walkouts nationwide give way to a seminal debate today in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Why is something that is repeatedly referred to as settled law on the threshold of being swept away like so much dust?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Senate Democrats are fighting for a bill already passed in the House that would protect abortion access nationwide. They need 60 votes to pass it, but there is no indication they`ll get there.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): This radical pro-abortion legislation removes a range of common sense protections that exist in states across the country.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And with the critical November midterms hanging in the balance the decision may not be a strong driving force in elections. CBS News polling found if Roe versus Wade is overturned, it`s likely to have no effect on nearly half of Democrats and three-quarters of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary): There is spillover into labor force participation --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But according to the nation`s first female Treasury Secretary, it could have a major effect on women in the workforce and their ability to boost earning potential through higher education.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 : Eliminating the right of women to make decisions about when and whether to have children would have very damaging effects on the economy and would set women back decades.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Senate is poised to block this measure by the end of the day and the Supreme Court is poised to rule by the end of next month -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Got it, Scott. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news overnight: A reporter for the Al Jazeera Network was shot and killed while covering an Israeli raid in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is tracking the latest developments for us from London.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz, it`s always sad when a journalist dies in the field. Reportedly, this journalist was wearing one of those familiar blue flak jackets that said "press" clearly.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, what else do we know?\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 What we do know is that Shireen Abu Akleh set out early this morning to cover an Israeli raid in the town of Jenin. She was as you say wearing a helmet and body armor that was clearly marked as "press" when she was shot. Now, the journalist seen here next to her said they were targeted by Israeli Forces.\par \par \uc2 Her employer, Al Jazeera said the veteran reporter was and I`m quoting here, "assassinated in cold blood by Israeli forces."\par \par \uc2 Now, Israel`s Prime Minister called her death unfortunate and blamed it on armed Palestinians claiming they were quote "firing indiscriminately." A second journalist was shot in the back, but survived. Ali Al-Samudi says Israeli soldiers shot both him and Abu Akleh. Now, the U.S. Ambassador to Israel is calling for a thorough investigation.\par \par \uc2 Israeli forces have carried out near daily raids in the West Bank amid a series of deadly attacks inside Israel with Jenin a main flashpoint. Now on a personal note, I knew Shireen who was both Palestinian and American. We used to work together in Jerusalem and she was a kind and generous colleague, always looking to give voice to those who were so rarely heard from.\par \par \uc2 But more than anything, she was a pioneer, a household name across the Arab world, Shireen started her career in the 90s when there were few Arab women reporting on the issues that affected their communities. She truly inspired a generation of other Arab women to do the same and will be greatly missed -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I`m so sorry that you knew her, and so sorry for your loss, Imtiaz. It is just another painful reminder about how dangerous this job can be. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in the United States, the murder suspect whose escape lead to a nationwide manhunt is back in the Alabama jail that he escaped from.\par \par \uc2 Casey White was extradited from Indiana last night following his capture there on Monday. An Indiana coroner says his alleged accomplice, Corrections official, Vicky White, no relation died by suicide following a self-inflicted gunshot wound to the head.\par \par \uc2 Police have released footage of the fugitives` this final moments on the run.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca has more on the story and a warning here, some of the images you`re about to see are graphic.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from dashcam video of car chase via Evansville Police Department.)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: This dashcam video from police in Evansville, Indiana shows escaped inmate Casey White as he is dragged from the wreckage of his getaway vehicle.\par \par \uc2 White who faced his capital murder charges is eventually walked over to the hood of the police car in handcuffs.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: I`m going to go for the gun first. Okay, I`m going to go for the gun.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Moments later, officers pulled former Alabama Corrections officer Vicky White from the overturned car, as well as the firearm they believe she used to shoot herself.\par \par \uc2 DAVE WEDDING (Vanderburgh County Sheriff): Members of the U.S. Task Force basically rammed the vehicle and pushed it into a ditch and we later found out, had they not done that, the fugitive was going to engage in a shootout with law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Authorities later recovered four loaded handguns, a loaded AR-15 style rifle and ammunition from his car. In addition to camping gear and roughly $29,000.00 in cash.\par \par \uc2 The fugitives` arrest came a week and a half after they were first reported missing and five days after Evansville Police first inspected their previous getaway car, which had been abandoned at a local car wash.\par \par \uc2 It was that tip, one of close to 600 the U.S. Marshal Service said it received that helped steer authorities to the Whites` hideout at this motel and ultimately the chase that ended in their capture.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Omar Villafranca.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now turning to New Mexico where the nation`s largest wildfire continues to burn with bone dry and windy conditions. Fire crews there are having trouble containing the fire.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has already issued a disaster declaration and F.E.M.A. teams are on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Justin Michaels from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is in New Mexico with more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Plumes of smoke dot the skyline of northern New Mexico. The flames of two fires, the Hermit`s Peak and Calf Canyon have now merged making it the largest wildfire burning in the country.\par \par \uc2 RYAN BERLIN (Fire Information Officer): This has been a nonstop fight, day and night.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: Fire Information Officer, Ryan Berlin says the fires path and inconsistent weather have been a constant obstacle to contain the flames.\par \par \uc2 RYAN BERLIN: This fire is in some very rugged terrain that is inaccessible and Mother Nature with the winds is throwing us punches.\par \par \uc2 We`re building containment lines on top of containment lines.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: The region has been in an extreme drought since the summer of 2020. That coupled with high temperatures, bone dry humidity, and strong wind gusts means crews need all the help they can get.\par \par \uc2 It`s an assault from the air and the ground. You can see the Blackhawk helicopter taking off with a bucket full of water going to an active fire line trying to work hard to put that out.\par \par \uc2 Some of the 1,700 personnel on the ground and in the air are working hard in this section of New Mexico to work against a fire that right now has the upper hand.\par \par \uc2 Cindy Collins who lives in a town just 12 miles away from the fire says the fires are especially tough to take after a cold and gray winter.\par \par \uc2 CINDY COLLINS (New Mexico resident): Now a fire, and it`s springtime and the spring is just the celebration. And here we are in spring, everyone`s garden is dying and there`s a huge roaring fire.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 `s Justin Michaels in Las Vegas, New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Overseas to Ukraine now where the government says Russian forces have been suffering setbacks around the key city of Kharkiv. The footage we`re looking at now shows -- apparently shows burnt out rushing vehicles seeming to back up the Ukrainian President`s claims his forces are pushing Russian troops further away from the country`s second largest city, but Russia has made gains elsewhere in the east, and Charlie D`Agata reports now from inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: When Nadia (sp?) and her husband found out where the airstrike came down in the eastern town of Bakhmut, they rushed to this neighborhood as fast as they could and soon discovered the bodies of their relatives.\par \par \uc2 (NADIA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "They were kind, very good people," she said.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian military has little defense against Russia`s overwhelming firepower, where the fight has become a bloody battle of trench warfare. Drone footage alleges to show a Russian tank destroyed near Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian soldiers who drove out Russians northeast of the city found rows of shot up cars, scattered clothes, a baby seat, a stroller, apparent evidence of civilians killed, even as they tried to flee.\par \par \uc2 And yet the Russian offensive in Donbas has been relentless, flattening small towns in the march toward the strategic cities of Kramatorsk and Sloviansk, where we found heavily fortified cities protected by well-armed troops, endless bunkers, concrete blocks and anti-tank defenses ringing the outskirts.\par \par \uc2 And it doesn`t stop there. This fighting position is right in the center of the city. If Russia and ground forces were to advance, they would face a whole network of these dug in trenches.\par \par \uc2 And they are everywhere. Soldiers say, the Russian invaders would face guerrilla warfare at every turn.\par \par \uc2 Volodymyr Kutnenko (sp?) is a medic who`s been battling Russian separatists since 2014.\par \par \uc2 Have you treated any Russian soldiers?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR KUTNENKO (Medic): Yes, of course. Of course.\par \par \uc2 (VOLODYMYR KUTNENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "We provide them with the full range of medical support," he said. "We exchange them for our prisoners of war, but our prisoners of war are returned in a terrible medical conditions."\par \par \uc2 And as the brutal battle nears, soldiers here say they know the most difficult days lie ahead.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Charlie D`Agata in Sloviansk, Easter Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, the worsening shortage of baby formula nationwide. One couple shows us the extreme measures that they`re taking to find supplies for their baby. It is getting scary out there.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, like something out of a movie, a passenger with no experience as a pilot is forced to take over and land a plane. We will play you the audio from inside the cockpit.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  weighs in on whether he`d let former President Trump back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  if his bid for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  goes through.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a story now about a mid-air scare that none of us would ever want to face. A passenger without any flight experience landed a plane yesterday after the pilot suffered a medical emergency. Just take a listen to the dramatic audio between that passenger and air traffic control.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PASSENGER: I`ve got a serious situation her. My pilot has gone incoherent. I have no idea how to fly the airplane.\par \par \uc2 CONTROL TOWER: Roger. What`s your position?\par \par \uc2 PASSENGER: I have no idea. I can see the coast of Florida in front of me, and I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 CONTROL TOWER: Maintain wings level and just try to follow the coast, either north or southbound. We`re trying to locate you.\par \par \uc2 Just continue to slow descent until you get to 5,000 feet and just continue south or continue northbound over the beach and we`ll give some further instructions, get you toward an airport.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Controllers then guided the passenger through landing procedures for that Cessna aircraft and the good news is, he nailed the landing. No word on his name or the condition of the pilot who suffered that emergency. But as for the fill-in pilot, he is one chill dude.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll say. I`ll say. I love the air traffic control saying, "Just continue your descent." How do you even do that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what I am saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then you see the air traffic controller, Nate, and he`s giving instructions from a picture of the dashboard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I would be listening. That`s not enough information. You`ve got to tell me more, please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have no idea how to fly this airplane.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good teacher from air traffic control.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He did it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And good calm under pressure performance from that fill-in pilot. I`d love to know his name.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But don`t you think you could do that if you`re terrified? And you know that your life depends on it. Wouldn`t you just follow every single instruction?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`d follow directions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No matter how scared you are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Down to the detail.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would like to think I could.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we will never find out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, the outrage over a bodycam footage of a police raid in Georgia. They stopped a bus filled with college lacrosse team members, what they say they were looking for inside that bus and what the team says, they were wrongfully targeted for.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll have your 7:30 headlines. Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS here at 7:30.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the headlines we`re following today.\par \par \uc2 Keep an eye on the Senate where Democrats are pushing a vote, a longshot measure to protect abortion rights on the Federal level, all of it in reaction to that draft majority opinion that 