y formula nationwide jumped to 43 percent just last week. Now in November, that number was only 11 percent. Big difference there.\par \par \uc2 We are now seeing empty store shelves and months` long wait for the products that your babies need. Janet Shamlian spoke to a family struggling to deal with the shortages.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For Taylor and Kenny Miller, every drop of formula counts.\par \par \uc2 The Katy, Texas couple is rationing it for their four-month old daughter, Magnolia.\par \par \uc2 How many stores have you had to go to, to find the formula you need?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR MILLER: I lost count.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: You`ve lost count?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: That many?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR MILLER: Yes, that many. We`ll literally waste a whole tank of gas in a day trying to find formula and maybe only find one can.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: the couple has just over four cans left, a two-week supply.\par \par \uc2 How much of a stressor is this for you?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR MILLER: It`s very stressful. I mean, the other day, I actually found formula and I bought all the cans and I feel like a bad person, but my kid have got to survive.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: It`s a nationwide problem. In at least five states, more than 50 percent of sellers are out of stock. Twenty-five states in D.C. are between 40 and 50 percent empty, up from seven states just a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR MILLER: There`s family in Louisiana. I had some friends in Georgia and Tennessee look. We have family in Delaware literally coast to coast and nobody has found anything.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: With empty shelves becoming the norm, sellers are limiting purchases to help stabilize inventory.\par \par \uc2 A February recall by manufacturer \uc2 Abbott Laboratories\uc2  contributed to the national shortage, but experts say supply chain issues also played a role. Now, the average cost of the most popular baby formula products is up as much as 18 percent over the last 12 months.\par \par \uc2 DR. DYAN HES (Gramercy Pediatrics Medical Director): Our biggest fear is that parents dilute the formula, and this is a big no-no.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dr. Dyan Hes is a pediatrician at New York City`s Gramercy Hospital. She and the F.D.A. are warning parents against making formula themselves.\par \par \uc2 DR. DYAN HES: That`s how babies end up having seizures or dying or becoming malnourished because most of those recipes are outdated, and they can be dangerous and they`re not scientifically based.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A crisis parents hope ends quickly.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Janet Shamlian, Houston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you can tell firsthand.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is terrifying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is happening in New York.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we are not wasting a drop now. I used to -- it always take four ounces at night, I used to put five in the bottle in case she was hungry, no more. Like we are in conservation mode at this point, because we know there`s no more if we run out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It has to be scary for new parents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. And people say oh, mother`s milk is free. But you know, some women can`t breastfeed, other women are going back to work and they have jobs because they`ve got to make money to pay for everything else.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It doesn`t suit them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the milk doesn`t always flow either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As a nursing home. It doesn`t -- it is not as easy as you may think.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a big, big problem in this country, one of many.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, new concerns about the spread of COVID subvariants. What`s the key to navigating this latest twist in the pandemic? Dr. Celine Gounder will give us her advice in just a moment and a reminder, also an invitation, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Get the day`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here we go again. You`ve heard this story before.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing new indications that COVID is not over in this country. Twenty-four states are reporting an increase in hospitalizations, and cases have jumped more than 50 percent nationwide over the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes as New York City raises coronavirus alert level last week to medium risk triggering a recommendation, but not a requirement that people wear masks in public indoor settings.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder is an epidemiologist who is editor-at-large for Public Health in Kaiser Health News and joins us now to help sort it all out for us.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you again, Dr. Gounder. But this is -- I don`t know anybody in this room that hasn`t heard of someone who has it now, who may be in the hospital; first, second time are getting it again, what is happening? We heard that there is going to be a summer surge, but it`s not summer yet. Why is this happening now?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER (Infectious Disease Specialist and Epidemiologist): Well, a couple of factors. So one, you have this -- or several of these ultra-super infectious omicron subvariants that are spreading across the country that got started here in New York City, but are now spreading elsewhere. Secondly, people are just tired. They`re not masking, they`re not social distancing. They`re not doing the things they did earlier in the pandemic. And so of course, the virus doesn`t really care what we think, it`s going to spread.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right. Are the people who are getting it, are they vaccinated? Are they boosted? What is their status in most cases?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: There are some of both. The vaccines are excellent at keeping people out of the hospital --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the hospitalizations are up.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: But they don`t -- hospitalizations are up, and most of that is driven by people who are not vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, speaking of surges, do you expect a fall and winter surge?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: We do. And modeling out of the Biden administration would indicate, we might have something like a hundred million infections this fall, which is pretty similar, actually, to what we saw during the omicron wave over the Holidays.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. So if people are eligible for booster, should they wait until then to be better protected in the fall and winter?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or wait until the fall to get it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I wouldn`t try to game this out here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The timing.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I think it`s been so hard to predict how this virus is going to spread, how it`s going to mutate. I would say if you`re over 50, you are eligible. If you`re immunocompromised, you`re eligible, go ahead and get that fourth dose.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what if you`re under 50? You don`t think you should get it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, if you have an immunocompromising condition, if you have some other underlying medical illness where your physician thinks you`re high risk, follow your physician`s advice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It feels like regardless of the hospitalization rates or the case counts that the country is moving toward a situation where fewer people are really afraid of the risk, right? So there`s a lot more casual - - people get it, they`re like, yes, I got it. I`m just watching it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel great. I`m 