ions, options that you can exercise at home that are easy, cheap to do, in addition to going into a facility and getting tested with a PCR.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Dr. Celine Gounder. Good information, as always, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories we`ll be talking about today. But first, it is 7:43 time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch" with Mr. Duthiers, and this is a very special "What to Watch," a one story "What to Watch.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, because it`s an interesting story. It`s an important story that a lot of people are buzzing about it, so we`re going to share it right now on "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 The president of Delaware State University, which is a Historically Black Colleges expressing outrage after a bus of his lacrosse players was stopped and searched in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 It happened at the end of last month when Sheriff`s Deputies pulled over the bus as it traveled back home from a game in Florida. Their bus was initially stopped for an alleged traffic violation, but it then turned into a search for drugs. Members of the lacrosse team say they were being racially profiled.\par \par \uc2 Police bodycam video of the incident was released.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: The reason why I`m stopping you is for a left lane violation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is the moment Liberty County Sheriff`s Deputies pulled over a bus of women`s lacrosse players last month. After talking to the driver, the Sheriff`s Deputy pokes his head into the bus.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: Ladies, How are you all doing?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When he returns to the car, he makes this remark to a fellow Deputy.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: There is a bunch of dang school girls on the bus. There`s probably some weed. Maybe.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Deputies then informed the driver they`re going to search the luggage under the bus. When Deputy brings out a drug sniffing dog, they return to the bus to explain what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: How do we g o from being in the wrong lane to going through our bags and checking for marijuana?\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: There are times where we end up finding some children that are missing from the families. We find large amounts of money that nobody knows anything about. Okay? But large amounts of cocaine, marijuana, heroin, methamphetamines, anything like that, okay?\par \par \uc2 If there is anything in you all`s luggage, we`re probably going to find it, okay?\par \par \uc2 I`m not looking for a little bit of marijuana, but I`m pretty sure you guys` chaperones probably going to be disappointed in you if we find any.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Deputies proceed to search the bags going through the women`s personal belongings. At one point they, pull out a wrapped gift.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: What is that?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: I have no clue. My aunt gave it to me.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: Your aunt gave it to you? You don`t know what it is?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: No. I didn`t open it yet. I was going to wait till I got back to school. I can open it now.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: No, what did she tell you it was?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: She didn`t tell me anything.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: Where did you -- where did you get it from?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: My aunt. In Georgia.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: I mean, in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: When I was in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: We`re going to open it.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: We`re going to find out exactly what it is.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: But however, this is the type of stuff that we look for.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: After around 20 minutes of searching, the Deputies find no contraband.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: All right ladies, 