 if you pull over a bus fine, you might have reason to, but once you get inside, I spent four years of my life on college busses like that going back and forth, as soon as you poke your head and you know exactly what it is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it`s no longer a probable cause situation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And here is the trauma. Many of these young women had never had an interaction with police in their lives. And this is the first interaction they are going to have with a police officer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In the long list of people who should be upset about this, the people of Georgia should be upset because your officers have better things to do. What are you doing on that bus?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And to say: "Thank you. Have a nice day." How about you end it with, "I apologize for wasting your time."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. Thank you, Vlad, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this hour. We are very honored to have April Simpkins here. She`s a mother of entertainment correspondent, Cheslie Kryst, who died by suicide. April, joining us today is really an act of love and she has got a very powerful message.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil. COVID is still hanging around, but some COVID travel policies are not, we will help you navigate the new rules of the road and the skies this vacation season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. The band, Glass Animals are global superstars, but it is a near tragedy for them to learn what`s truly important. It`s quite a story and Anthony`s got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of course, he does, because he`s Anthony.\par \par \uc2 But first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 President Biden says curbing inflation is his top domestic priority as new data tells us if it`s getting any better.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BOYD: We weren`t able to pay for seeds, and the struggle is going to be real for the American people in the coming months as it relates to food.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New Mexico issues a disaster declaration with the nation`s largest wildfire accelerating.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: It`s an assault from the air and the ground against a fire that right now has the upper hand.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Zelenskyy says Russian troops are being pushed out of the Kharkiv area as battles continue elsewhere in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: In the center of this city, if Russian ground forces were to advance, it would face a whole network of these dug-in trenches.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A nationwide baby formula shortage is putting parents and their children at risk.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR MILLER: The other day I actually found formula and I bought all the cans and I feel like a bad person, but my kid has got to survive.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There`s ball four. That`s going to bring up Judge.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the \uc2 Yankees\uc2 ` dream season continued last night. They were down in the ninth until Aaron Judge went out and did something about it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Judge into the left field. There it goes. See you. Walk off homerun. And the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  have come back to win it. Oh, man, oh man. What a blast by Judge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you said, Aaron Judge did something about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He did something about it. As a Mets fan, I hate the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  win, but that`s exciting. That`s good for baseball.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Isn`t he negotiating a contract?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think he is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I think he`s going to get about $400 -- maybe $500 million.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s only his first walk off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go. Congratulations to the \uc2 Yankees\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour with the biggest British band in the world right now. You`ve heard of them.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of "Heat Waves.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that middle of June -- that of course is Glass Animals with their inescapable record breaking super hit, it is called "Heat Waves." Now, the song was released in 2020. It went viral in this pandemic, before finally hitting number one this past March.\par \par \uc2 Glass Animals are in nominations for every single major music award show this past year including the Grammys and this weekend`s Billboard Music Awards. Anthony Mason met up with Glass Animals at the Neon Museum in Las Vegas before last month`s Grammys.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, what do they have to say? Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Glass Animals biggest album yet, "Dreamland" came out of one of their most difficult times as a band. We spoke about "Heat Waves," slow burn success, and the band`s long unlikely journey to the top.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Glass Animals performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No one is more surprised at the success of "Heat Waves" than the band itself.\par \par \uc2 You now have the longest number one by a British band since the Spice Girls.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY (Glass Animals lead vocals): Bonkers.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We met Glass Animals in Las Vegas where they were up for a Best New Artist Grammy --\par \par \uc2 But you`re not exactly new.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: We feel fresh. We feel fresh.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh, it is recording.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Glass Animals performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: On their recent American tour --\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: That was just like crazy excitement. The energy -- the energy was -- it was fizzing. It felt like people were going to like explode.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Fans came in costume and carrying signs.\par \par \uc2 What did the signs say?\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: Can`t remember.\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD (Glass Animals drum kit): All sorts of stuff you do not want to know.\par \par \uc2 EDMUND IRWIN-SINGER (Glass Animals bass guitar): I`d sacrifice a goat for you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s quite touching actually.\par \par \uc2 EDMUND IRWIN-SINGER: I am not -- yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The four members: Dave Bayley, Joe Seaward, Drew MacFarlane, and Ed Irwin-Singer met in Oxford, England.\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD (Glass Animals drum kit): I was born in Oxford with Ed and Drew and Dave were both born here in America and came and joined us at slightly different times at school.\par \par \uc2 So we became friends there. Dave is very much Texan when he arrived.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Was he?\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: I came. I was on a horseback.\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: Texas Dave, I remember him with a Stanton and a banjo strapped to his back.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Bonding over music, they formed a band. But Bayley, who was studying Medicine at King`s College in Oxford, hit a fork in the road.\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: They`ve actually had quite a serious decision to make, which was, do I stick with this Medical Degree, which I`ve started, which is a career or do I throw it all in the bin and chase something else.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: Chase something else.\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: This like wild horn.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: Everyone had always told you is not a good career choice.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There are a lot of your friends who have become doctors.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: They are now, yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, and when you`re talking to them, do you ever think, "Gee, I made the wrong choice."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Gooey.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Glass Animals started catching on. Their song "Gooey" went platinum in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 But in 2018, in the middle of touring their second album, tragedy struck suddenly.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: And I remember exactly where I was. I was standing on my bed, trying to fix a light bulb.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Seaward had been out riding a bike when he was hit by a truck. He suffered a broken leg, a fractured skull, and brain damage.\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: I had to relearn how talk, how to walk, how to think, read, like everything was sort of taken away. It was like -- it was brutal in that way.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: You had one word, you could say the word "I."\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: I could say "I." I got very frustrated because --\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: You got so frustrated.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: For Bayley, who`d studied neuroscience at Oxford --\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: It was very frightening. I don`t want to get too explicit, but there was a lot of bone pushing on certain areas of your brain.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Everything would turn on a critical operation.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: I was like, yes, holding your mum`s hand, just being like, this is the -- this is make or break point. Oh my gosh. I mean, I`m crazy thinking about it now having thought about it properly. Such a long time.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The 14-hour surgery was successful.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: Then the first words you said to me after you came out is that a seagull took your brioche. I go, "Oh my God." Everyone was like, `Whoa, okay, I think he might have lost it now."\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: A seagull stole my brioche. And they were like, cool, Joe. And then, I was just like, no, actually, I just saw that happen.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It did happen.\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: That window was open and the seagull came in.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: That was it. That was it.\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: And then everyone, like I just remember everyone laughing.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: Yes, it`s like these small moments of relief in this like really deeply dark time.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Your Love (Deja Vu)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Remarkably, about six months later, the band was back together. Bayley says Seaward`s accident caused him to reflect on pivotal moments of his own life.\par \par \uc2 In the new album`s title track "Dreamland."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dreamland.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And in "Heat Waves," which is about the loss of a close friend.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heat Waves.")\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: "Heat Wave" is almost admitting to myself that it`s quite healthy to let yourself miss someone and quite healthy to let yourself feel those things.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The video had to be shot during lockdown in London.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: That was the most heartwarming video I`ve ever been part of.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Bayley asked his neighbors to help.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: I slipped these notes through everyone`s letter boxes on a street next to mine and then just said "Film me at seven o`clock if you can and upload the video to this \uc2 Dropbox\uc2  link" and got to seven o`clock, I was like, is anyone actually going to film me? Everyone is not leaning out.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How many videos did you end up having submitted to you?\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: I think it was about 50 to 60 videos. Some people who had proper cinema cameras like set it up on the roof with a tripod and it was - - it was just -- it was so cool.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Glass Animals performance.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Then the song quietly went out to conquer the world.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Glass Animals performance.)\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: It was quite a slow band, wasn`t it?\par \par \uc2 JOE SEAWARD: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: Two years.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: From the moment it broke into the Billboard chart at Number 100, till the day it hit number one took 59 weeks.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: I don`t know. I feel like a mum with a baby. It`s still your baby.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE BAYLEY: Then your baby`s gone out and made loads of friends.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Glass Animals performance.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Literally billions of friends. Unfortunately, the Glass Handles did not get to go to the Grammys because Dave Bayley got COVID just before.\par \par \uc2 And so we want to thank, by the way, the follow-up interview we had to do was a Zoom. We want to thank Norah Restaurant in West Hollywood and Legacy Records in New York for hosting our remote chat with Dave.\par \par \uc2 What a year and a half they`ve had.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a story.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, an amazing story. They`re up for -- by the way, they`re up for the Billboard Awards this weekend for them, so and they will be at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is what your pieces always do for me, Anthony. We all know that everybody has a story, but you always find the story behind the story, just that thing you just showed about the video, how he got everybody to shoot for him, and that turned into a thing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, everybody had to get sort of ingenious during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Didn`t they? And that`s -- I mean, and remarkably, when he was making that song, they thought nothing is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nothing. Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, some people will say it`s a coincidence that your friend studied neuroscience, and you end up with a traumatic brain injury. But I`ve always thought that coincidence is God`s way of remaining anonymous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So it was written that he knew more about what he was going through because he actually studied it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, I asked Joe Seaward, I said, what -- in all of what`s happened to you, what`s been the most rewarding? He said, you know, he said, I hate to almost say this, but you know, everybody believes their friends are the greatest friends in the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He said, I learned that when I went through this accident, and he said in some ways, I don`t regret it because it gave me that and to go through all of this with my great friends, is really a gift.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s powerful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who would have thought that you and June would rhyme? But you in June, I love the lyrics. I like it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You can`t get that song out of your head.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Number one song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Number one song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s going to be with you all day. Thank you, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And our travel guru, Peter Greenberg, he has traveled to our progressive greenroom, there he is.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, he`ll share the best ways to protect your summer vacation plans from COVID interruptions.\par \par \uc2 But first it is 8:11 AM, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, April Simpkins says she not only lost her daughter, but her best friend when former Miss USA Cheslie Kryst died by suicide earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Simpkins will join us to talk about Cheslie`s private struggle with depression and what the grieving process has been like.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your travel plans could get more complicated because of the rising number of COVID cases right now and this summer, and here is a big difference from previous years: You may have more trouble getting all of your money back if you test positive.\par \par \uc2 Just take a look at \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 . It recently announced it is dropping cancellation exceptions for COVID that`s starting May 31st, which means a host`s original rules will apply.\par \par \uc2 CBS News senior travel adviser, Peter Greenberg is here with how to navigate COVID concerns while you travel.\par \par \uc2 Peter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of COVID concerns and a lot of it has to do with these cancellations, which people sometimes buy insurance for thinking they`re doing good by themselves. What`s the story with travel insurance?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, I love the concept of travel insurance. It`s the execution where you have a problem, and during the last two and a half years, it was the largest number of complaints from travelers because if you make your reservations online, and so many people do, you cannot finish the transaction unless you either opt in or opt out of the insurance.\par \par \uc2 And most people with the best of intentions starting in 2018, picked the option to go in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always opt out.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, you did the right thing, but -- but they it, but had no idea what they were covered for and worse, they had no idea what they were not covered for. And then of course the pandemic hits, their trip gets canceled. They go to use their insurance and they find out that, let`s say somewhere on the website of the insurance company, which was never displayed on the website, when they made the reservation was a paragraph that said "Oh by the way, we don`t cover from pandemic. Bye-bye."\par \par \uc2 So millions of dollars were lost, and basically people were out of their money.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: So they bought worthless insurance and they didn`t get the cancellation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So is there a good time to buy? But I`m a person, I don`t buy the warranty if I`m renting a car, I don`t get the extra whatever that comes with that. But are there times that you actually should get the insurance?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Absolutely. But you need to know how to get it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How to get it.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: And where to get it from. Don`t buy it online. Don`t buy it from the travel provider themselves because many of them self-insure. So, if they go out of business, so does your insurance. Go through a third- party. Go to a travel agent, a travel adviser. They can walk you through the hieroglyphics of that policy language so you know what the exclusions are because every policy has an exclusion.\par \par \uc2 There are agents` exclusions, there are pre-existing medical exclusions. There are even destination exclusions. If the \uc2 United States government\uc2  says it`s a level four, don`t go, your vacation to Syria will not be covered.\par \par \uc2 So you get the concept.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: The point is, go through that route, and you`ll be protected.\par \par \uc2 Now the cost of that is about six to 10 percent of the cost of your trip, and then it`s worth it. As long as you know what you`re covered for, as well as what you`re not covered for.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony mentioned COVID and how it complicates travel, maybe not as much as it used to. Most countries don`t require a test anymore. The U.S. does, but it`s a bit complicated. What do you need to tell travelers about COVID test and traveling?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, the good news is most countries have gotten rid of the requirement for testing if you can show proof of vaccination, so you get to go in without that. There are no quarantine rules anymore as long as you prove vaccination. The problem is getting back home.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the U.S. still has a rule in place that you still have to show a negative COVID test, within 24 hours of your return flight to be able to come back to the United States. Other countries have dropped that.\par \par \uc2 There is a huge move on to get the United States to drop that, too, for reciprocity reasons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t matter where you go, outside -- you`ve got to get it.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: You`ve got to get it all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got to get back in. The people that review it, though, are the airline employees. It`s not a public health authority. And one thing I will just point out from that graphic, it says 24 hours. It is one calendar day.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, if you`re flying late in the day and you think, oh --\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Yes. People get confused about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You get confused. It`s important. You get more flexibility than you think. It`s one calendar day from your departure date.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Right. Now there`s another kind of insurance everybody really needs to have.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: And that`s medical evacuation and repatriation insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s that?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Because your medical coverage in the United States doesn`t cover you when you go overseas. Your health insurance does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is medical evacuation?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: It means that if you get sick or injured overseas, in the best policies, they`ll pay to get you stabilized over the air. They`ll consult with your own doctor who knows your medical history better than your physician, and then if needed, they will fly you back on a medically equipped jet to the doctor and medical facility of your choice.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to read the language.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you`re saying, get that medical evacuation.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: You`ve got to read the language. You`ve got to get that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because recently we`ve seen some actual deaths at resorts when people were traveling on vacation.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Right. Well, not just the deaths. I saw a guy in Cairo one day who decided to stupidly try to climb the pyramids. He fell down, broke his hip, he had to be taken home. He didn`t have the insurance. They had to buy four first class tickets to put the stretcher in, a fifth first class ticket for the nurse they required to have with him. His airfare costs alone were $87,000.00.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Boom.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That view.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: You need to get that insurance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That view is priceless though. I would have been that guy, I`ve got to say. I relate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Cost you an arm and leg, literally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re trying to get back in, you test positive. How long do you have to stay in that country?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, the good news is there are insurance policies now, the market is adjusting where you will get covered if you have to get tested positive and they`ll pay for your expenses while you`re quarantined.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Peter Greenberg, thank you always. Good advice and always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: You`ve got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, we will talk with April Simpkins. She is the mother of the late former Miss U.S.A., Cheslie Kryst. Simpkins will join us here in the studio to reflect on her daughter`s legacy.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 As many of you know, May is Mental Health Awareness Month and this morning, we are hearing from April Simpkins. She is the mother of former Miss U.S.A. and "Extra" correspondent Cheslie Kryst. Cheslie died by suicide in January. She was 30 years old.\par \par \uc2 Her family says that she had suffered from severe depression for years. And please note, we have to tell you ahead of time, the subject matter of this conversation may be difficult for some viewers, but we`re very glad that April Simpkins is here this morning.\par \par \uc2 Please know how glad we are to see you.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS (Cheslie Kryst`s mother): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And to have this this conversation.\par \par \uc2 You know, to those of us who knew and loved Cheslie, you know, I see her as so vibrant. That lion mane of hair. She was so confident and so beautiful. And I think for so many of us, it was such a shock and a surprise.\par \par \uc2 But I like the point that we need to make today that people don`t understand that depression comes in many forms. There isn`t a one size fits all, and I think many people think, April, you`re in the fetal position. You`re walking around in your sweatpants. You look a mess. You sound a mess. And that is not always the case.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: It`s very true. Even in the case with Cheslie, she was bubbly and lively. And when she came in through the front door, it felt like an announcement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: All of my other children would come through the front door very quietly and hit the kitchen and say hello, but she would throw that door open and say, "Hi, mom."\par \par \uc2 Depression doesn`t always look like someone is sad or burdened. It is something that, for Cheslie, she learned to live with.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re so glad you`re here because it`s an important conversation and because we know you`re still going through a grieving process here.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Could you talk a little bit about what that process has been, like?\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Difficult. I`ve never lost someone so close to me. So, I don`t know what grief should look like.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, Cheslie told me you were her best friend. It`s not just a daughter.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were her best friend.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: That`s correct. And there are moments when grief hits me very hard. And many times it`s in the morning, because that was how we started our day, talking together, FaceTiming -- and one of the things Cheslie and I talked about after her first attempt was starting every day off with gratitude.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When was her first attempt? She was 30 when she was --\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: It was many years ago.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Many years.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: She was in her 20s, and so when we would start our calls in the morning, it was gratitude and what we were looking forward to that day and that for her was very helpful.\par \par \uc2 And so now in the mornings, to not have that makes the mornings very hard even for me. The shower became my best friend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: It`s where I can grieve the best. The warm water feels comforting. I`ve stood in my shower and weep and cried and screamed and dropped to the floor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But April, had you been worried about her? You said that she - - there had been an attempt in her 20s and now, she was 30.\par \par \uc2 Yes, most -- of course we didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You of course did. Had you been worried about her?\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: You know, I lived in two worlds. I loved her dearly and deeply. But the other world was knowing that she was struggling with depression, and she and I had very intimate conversations about what she needs from me for support. And she did have a support group.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: And so I gave her what she needed from me and Cheslie took from everyone what she needed from them -- a hug, a smile, a kind word. She got what she needed. And so while it didn`t plague me, it was always there in my mind.\par \par \uc2 I knew to do the things that she required of me as someone who was part of her support, but also her mom, and her best friend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. She also gave to people what they needed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: On a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been going through grief counseling. For those who might be dealing with something like this, would you encourage them to go and then also what have you taken away from those sessions?\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Thank you. I would absolutely encourage anyone to specifically see a grief counselor. I have a counselor that I`ve worked with for many years, but David and I have also been talking with a grief counselor.\par \par \uc2 To lose someone so suddenly feels like they were snatched, like she was snatched from my life. I don`t know how to grieve that way. I`ve never had to before, so I don`t know what it looks like and talking with someone to find out that what I`m feeling, what I`m doing is normal, and it is healthy.\par \par \uc2 Feeling what I feel is normal and healthy, and some days I feel great. I feel like I`m April again; and other days, I don`t feel like myself. I`ll think I`m okay, and then I`ll realize that you know my hair has been matted for two days and I need to just take care of myself and I realize in those moments I`m not okay and that`s okay to not be okay in those moments.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It seems like people that knew Cheslie immediately thought about the great memories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I have a ton of them. You know, I`ve worked with her closely. But for you, what are some memories that come to mind that help you deal?\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Oh, Cheslie and I spent a lot of time together. The trip that we took to Turks and Caicos last year was just everything because we both just had a chance to have fun.\par \par \uc2 Many times when I would travel with Cheslie, it was for work. Either her work as Miss U.S.A. or something that was related to what I was doing. But at Turks and Caicos, we just hung out. We built sandcastles, we walked on the beach, we had breakfast, we shopped. That is one of my favorite memories of her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. I miss her laugh.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Her laugh would light up the room.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Miss her laugh. Miss her voice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Make you smile.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her take on life, the way that she thought about things. And I learned later that she was a high functioning depression. She was high functioning and a very depressed person that we didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 Is there a way that -- you know, because we don`t dwell on what we miss, what we could have done, but is it something that should be shared so that people could reach out and help? What is the right thing to do in this situation?\par \par \uc2 Because I really struggled after. But I found some comfort now, in knowing that this was something that she had talked about, that she had talked with you about. I was still struggling with it, April. What is the answer? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Yes. I will say this about Cheslie. She was a very strong woman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: And what she needed, she sought and she received. Everyone isn`t, you know, we`re not saviors. You may not be the person she needed to counsel about her depression from, but what you did provide her, she needed, someone to talk to, mentor her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: A co-worker who smiled at her and gave her a kind word, laughed with her. She took what she needed. And I hope that people who knew her and loved her will find comfort in knowing that you did your part.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you know, you gave her what she needed.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Absolutely. Absolutely. And so, sometimes in that, I find comfort in knowing how strong of a woman she was.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She was.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: And that she sought what she needed when she needed it, in every area of her life. So there wasn`t really something to see. If she didn`t want you to see it, you weren`t going to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think she put on the face?\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She did.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Yes. And there was, you know, there was laughter she needed. And sometimes when someone finds out that you have something like depression or severe depression, they`ll treat you differently. They treat you with kid gloves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: And that wasn`t what she needed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you don`t want that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is most helpful to you during this time? What is the most -- and I know it isn`t one size fits all, too. I think I should make that clear. It is different things for different people.\par \par \uc2 You know, Wynonna Judd said something interesting the other day after her own mother`s death, she said it`s so hard to be blessed and broken in the same place. And I wonder what would be most helpful? What is most helpful?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And how can we honor Cheslie?\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Oh. Cheslie, you know, she left a legacy of kindness, of advocacy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Of helping and supporting others. And it`s in those acts that I see her. And so I`ve just been asking so many people, advocate, be kind. Those are ways that I know Cheslie has impacted someone enough. So, they are doing something to honor her. That means everything.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Well, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, it means everything to us for you to come today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really does.\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s such an important conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thinking of you, thinking of her, thinking of Dave and your family and other children, your other children, too?\par \par \uc2 APRIL SIMPKINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re still a mom, you`re still a mom to many people.\par \par \uc2 If you or someone you know is seeking mental health resources, you can call the \uc2 National Alliance on Mental Illness\uc2 . There`s a helpline and the number is 1-800-950-6264. Say it again, 1-800-950-6264. In a crisis, Please text N-A-M-I to 741741.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ll talk to a clinical psychiatrist, Dr. Sue Varma.\par \par \uc2 But first, a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re continuing our conversation about former Miss U.S.A. and "Extra" correspondent, Cheslie Kryst`s struggle with severe depression. She died by suicide in January.\par \par \uc2 You just heard from her mother, April Simpkins. We are now joined by board certified psychiatrist, Dr. Sue Varma, and we should note that she never treated Kryst.\par \par \uc2 You know, I want to tap back into something that April said. She mentioned living with the sadness. What did you think when you heard those words?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: I think it takes great courage to live in sadness, and I`m a believer that grief and gratitude can coexist. I think a lot of times people expect you either to move on or to be living on the floor in your sweatpants and grieving. And I think it looks very different for different people and to be able to sit with pain to me, that is one of the greatest hallmarks of emotional maturity.\par \par \uc2 To say this is where I am right now, I`m taken over by a wave of sadness, and I`m going to live with it. And I know that this sadness will lift and I will have brighter moments as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: April also said something very interesting about people thinking they`re saviors, right? So many people cared about Cheslie and would have wanted to do something, but Cheslie didn`t reach out, and she`s sort of freeing people, April is, by saying, look, if she didn`t reach out to you, she was getting that support somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s really important for people to understand, it`s about where you draw the line between one`s responsibility and what to do, what they could have done.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And information that should be shared. You know, is it something that should be shared?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: You know, ideally, you know, it`s a back and forth conversation to say, I`m really concerned about you, I`m worried about you. You look amazing, but I imagine that you have told me sometimes that this is hard for you, that you`re struggling. What`s going on for you inside?\par \par \uc2 I think it`s so hard because we want to be perceived as women who can have it all, so to speak, but what`s the price? You know, this whole idea of high functioning depression, depression typically isn`t high functioning. Depression has people on the floor, in the sweatpants not getting up, not taking care of themselves, not grooming. That`s the hallmark of it.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re really thrown off when we have somebody who is perfectly put together and saying all the right things and smiling at us, and you`re like, there is no way I would have ever seen this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So how do you check in with somebody? Because what`s scary is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like the point you made, though, Tony about savior. Can you follow up? I want to know what that means.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When April said you, you can`t be a savior.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes. And look, I have the utmost respect for everybody here and I know that, you know, when you`re a high achieving person who is used to being a mentor and being a support system in the community and being a role model, you can look back and say, "What did I do wrong?"\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s really important what April said is you didn`t do anything wrong. You were available, this person knew that you existed, they knew that they could call you at any time. But it`s us that puts the blame that I could have done something, it`s that Monday morning quarterback.\par \par \uc2 It`s part of grieving is to say, could I have, should I have, what could have been different? One split second, one phone call, could something have been different? That`s what that savior complex -- and sometimes it`s about guilt.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s about guilt -- survivor guilt also, "Why am I here? Why are they not here? What`s wrong with this picture?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How would someone know that they either need to reach out to an individual that might be struggling with depression? Or how would that individual who is waking up and they`re not -- they`re not understanding the space that they`re in and they`re not understanding that they could be depressed? How do they know that they need to reach out to somebody?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes. So if you find that you`re having a persistently low mood, so in high functioning depression, what`s different is that it doesn`t have the clear, nice discreet episodes that major depressive disorder has, where it`s like, you know, two weeks or four weeks.\par \par \uc2 In high functioning depression, it`s also called persistent depressive disorder, where it`s like a chronic low level of what we call dysthymia. It`s a low mood, and you`re still functioning.\par \par \uc2 But if you`re having this low mood that doesn`t go away, and you say that "I don`t have pleasure and the things that I once used to enjoy," or "If me getting put together and showing up in the world is exhausting." Like if I had to go to lunch with Gayle and then I come home and I sleep for five days, because I was like, "Oh my God, it was great, but I`m depressed. Like that took everything out of me."\par \par \uc2 And then checking in with people to say, I know you`re doing so much, like you`re on this show, you`re on that show, like, are you taking time for yourself? No, really? Like, what does that look like? What does your self- care look like?\par \par \uc2 Therapy, mental health treatment for all of us should be a part of our self-care, even from a preventative point of view. Like that`s so foreign to us that we would go for an annual screening for a mental health checkup, but it really should be a part of it. And it is, asking people two simple questions. Have you felt depressed lately? Are you feeling down? And are you getting pleasure out of the things he once used to enjoy?\par \par \uc2 So that should be something doctors should be asking and we should be asking each other. Are you still -- is this fun for you? Or is this a lot for you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My primary care doctors said that at the last checkup, and I was impressed. I was like, "Oh, this is new" and I like it.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes, yes. Two questions can save a person`s life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Being that I worked with Cheslie Kryst for so long, I`ve struggled with the reality coming to grips with what happened, and what I should have or should not have done.\par \par \uc2 This show has been healing to me, so I want to thank CBS MORNINGS, and also April Simpkins for coming in, sitting down because that was not easy, and thank you, Dr. Sue Varma. We appreciate you so much.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Again, if you or someone you know is seeking mental health resources, you can call the \uc2 National Alliance on Mental Illness\uc2  Helpline at 1-800-950-6264. In a crisis text in N-A-M-I to 741741. That is N-A-M-I, 741741.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tomorrow we`re going to take you to a remote part of the Southwest drained by drought where once there was water. You`re going to see how landmarks that had been submerged for half a century are coming back into view.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: This bridge was covered by water, so what we`re boating under, you used to be able to vote over.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We used to be able to boat over the top of this bridge.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And do you think we`ll be hiking under here?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I think we`ll be hiking under here probably in the next few years.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: This really is amazing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing and spooky. Ben Tracy goes on a journey to show us the remarkable changes brought on by climate change. That is tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And on today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast, the cofounder of menswear brand, Bonobos, Andy Dunn discusses coming to terms with his bipolar disorder diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 He also talks about working to remove the shame surrounding mental illness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Work we`re all trying to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all are trying to do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why we really appreciate April coming in today. That`s part of it. Talking about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, tough conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trying to remove the stigma.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We thank you for watching us today. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220511ei5b000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Growing Fears Of Violence, As Anger Grows In The Wake Of The Leaked Draft Opinion From The Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 469 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, there are growing fears tonight of violence, as anger grows in the wake of the leaked draft opinion from the Supreme Court. Protests are taking place in cities all across the country, including in front of the homes of some of the conservative justices.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CORY BOOKER (D-New Jersey): We will defend Roe.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): From a rally in New Jersey to a school walkout in Northern Virginia, abortion rights supporters continued mass demonstrations in cities around the country...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Abortion is a human right!\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: ... as the Senate prepares to vote this week to codify abortion protections into federal law.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): The vote will shine light on every single one of us.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A CBS News poll shows nearly two-thirds of Americans surveyed wants Roe vs. Wade kept as is, while those supporting its reversal say it would be a protection for the unborn.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My right, my choice!\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The fervent divide sparked heated protest over the weekend outside of the homes of Supreme Court Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): Violence, threats and intimidation have no place in political discourse.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The White House also condemned an attack on an anti- abortion organization in Madison, Wisconsin, Sunday, which is being investigated as an arson. And CBS News has learned law enforcement officials are monitoring threats from activists on both sides of the debate.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-Kentucky): Trying to scare federal judges into ruling a certain way is far outside the bounds of First Amendment speech.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said in a new interview a national abortion ban is possible if Roe is overturned and Republicans take back the majority.\par \par \uc2 New York Senator Kirsten Gillibrand:\par \par \uc2 You take the minority leader at his word?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KIRSTEN GILLIBRAND (D-New York): As you should. He said he`s going to do terrible things, and he does do the terrible things. And he really has had no holds barred in this attack on women, an attack on their autonomy and their independence, their freedom to make these really important decisions.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Democrats still don`t have this 60 votes needed to protect abortion rights. So, if this week`s vote fails, they say it will be on the ballot this November.\par \par \uc2 As for those protests, another one is scheduled tonight outside the home of Justice Alito, who authored that leaked opinion -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion on Capitol Hill for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220511ei590002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Voting on Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Scott MacFarlane, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 544 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Turning now to the intense debate over the possibility that Roe v. Wade might be overturned. Over the weekend, protesters gathered and tensions continued to rise across the country.\par \par \uc2 A new CBS News poll shows nearly two thirds of Americans want Roe v. Wade to be kept in place and abortion rights preserved. Thirty-six percent disagree. Some Democrats are now trying to shift this argument from the Courts to Congress.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Scott. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a Congress that`s often gridlocked on many things, they`re about to begin debating abortion rights law. There is no real prospect of the legislation here becoming law this week, but it`s a chance for both parties to lay down political markers days after the leak of that draft Supreme Court opinion and months before the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN protesting.)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: After a weekend of protests outside the Supreme Court, outside churches, and even at the homes of some of the High Courts` Justices, the abortion debate shifts to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  later today as Democratic Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  begins the proceedings ahead of a Wednesday vote to codify abortion rights into law.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Every American will see how every senator stands. They can`t duck it anymore.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A bill has already passed at the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2 , but has no clear path to the 60 votes needed in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): He calls it a bill to codify Roe versus Wade, but that`s not in fact what the bill does. It is a radical abortion bill, which sadly reflects where today`s Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a weekend interview with "U.S.A. Today," Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said it is possible legislators could see a nationwide ban on abortion if the Court overturns Roe versus Wade and if Republicans win control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 But in new CBS News polling, two-thirds of women under 50 said overturning Roe could lead to more restrictions on contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP chanting "Rise up for abortion rights.")\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A concern Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  expressed on "Face the Nation."\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): This is about respect for privacy. What`s next? What`s next?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Despite the protests that have developed ahead of the Supreme Court ruling, there are political analysts and candidates who say this may not be the swing issue for the midterm elections, an election cycle so far, partly defined by inflation, especially food and gas prices.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see if this debate and this week change that -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Indeed, we will. Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220511ei590005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Putin Leads Victory Day Parade; Russian Airstrike Kills Dozens in School; First Lady Visits Ukraine; Targeting Russia`s Economy; Voting on Abortion Rights; American Tourist Deaths; Extreme Fire Weather; Our Unfinished March; Comeback Colt; Inside Trump`s White House; Finding Mental Healthcare; M is for Mecha Cookie; It`s Never Too Late to Learn; Rethinking History Lessons, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Jericka Duncan, Nate Burleson, Debora Patta,  Charlie D`Agata, Nancy Cordes, Scott MacFarlane, Manuel Bojorquez, Vladimir  Duthiers, Steve Hartman, Meg Oliver, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 13,564 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday, May 9th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I`m Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off, but we`re in good hands. Here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 President Putin watches a military parade in Russia`s Red Square after his forces bomb a school in Ukraine. We are on the frontlines.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The force of the blast was so powerful it tore through this entire residential neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new CBS News poll shows nearly two-thirds of Americans are against overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): With Court has slapped women in the face in terms of disrespect.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  describes disturbing ideas from former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2  (Former U.S. Secretary of Defense): Maybe we have the U.S. military shoot missiles into Mexico.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Mystery surrounding the deaths of three Americans at a resort in the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Reports said the victims experienced nausea and dizziness.\par \par \uc2 FRANK WEDDING (Tourist): Like most tourists, we`d like to know what happened why it happened.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The manhunt for an escaped Alabama inmate and the Corrections officer accused of helping him continues.\par \par \uc2 This is new surveillance footage.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bottom of the ninth, \uc2 Padres\uc2  trailing to nothing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Gone. One o`clock homerun. Happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They are off in the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The most improbable Kentucky Derby win in more than a hundred years.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Rich Strike went off at 80 to one --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Rich Strike has done it in a stunning unbelievable event.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from U2 performance in Ukrainian bomb shelter.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: A surprise concert inside a subway station turned bomb shelter in Kyiv performed by \uc2 YouTube\uc2 `s Bono and The Edge.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s the 35th Anniversary of "With or Without You."\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Really? Thirty five.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Feeling old? Feeling old?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Never.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Classic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am. I am.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are the same age, so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know we`re on the same track.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a big show for you today, including disturbing allegations by former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  in a new book about his time in the Trump administration. It includes the former President`s response to a Black Lives Matter protest in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : "Can`t you just shoot them? Just shoot them in the legs or something?" And he is suggesting that that`s what we should do, that we should bring in the troops and shoot the protesters.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The Commander-in-Chief was suggesting that the U.S. military shoot protesters.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Yes, in the streets of our nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: American protesters.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : That`s right. Shocking.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Trump disputes that account. \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  will be here in studio. We`ll ask him why he now believes Trump is a threat to American democracy\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We also have a different kind of story from our Steve Hartman, the latest in his "Kindness 101" series about self-improvement and how a World War Two veteran shows us it`s never, I mean, never too late to close the book on the challenge of a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. I wonder what the challenge is. Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Same here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, we`ve got to begin with the news: The war in Ukraine and Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has a new message of defiance.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Putin used an Annual Moscow Parade held to mark victory in World War Two as an occasion to justify the invasion with false claims and propaganda. He blamed Ukraine and the West and took no responsibility for the atrocities allegedly committed on his orders.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta is in Ukraine for us with what Putin had to say.\par \par \uc2 Debora, quite a speech. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Putin used the defeat of Nazi Germany to defend his war in Ukraine despite speculation there was no victory declaration and no hint of a stronger push on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Marching in Moscow`s Red Square, a show of Putin`s power.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2  speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "Today," he said, "You are defending what your grandfathers fought for." He used the day to blame \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion for his war and bolstered the lie he is denazifying Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2  speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "The danger grew every day," he said. "Russia made a preemptive move against aggression."\par \par \uc2 In Russia, losses are touted as victories, allegations of atrocities dismissed as a hoax, Kremlin controlled media targeting even the very young.\par \par \uc2 But in Ukraine, Putin`s war is seen as a series of ruthless attacks and strategic blunders, Russian troops stand accused of nearly 10,000 war crimes.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  put a new spin on that old idiom, "Raining on your parade."\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "Very soon, there will be two victory days in Ukraine. He said, and someone won`t have any, we won then, we will win now."\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s resistance is the inspiring story of this awful war.\par \par \uc2 Returning to Kyiv, acting U.S. Ambassador Kristina Kvien summed it up succinctly.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA KVIEN (Acting U.S. Ambassador to Ukraine): ... Ukrainians, who across the entire country, including those in the east of Ukraine, refused to bend to his will and told the Russians "Go F yourself."\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Russia may have put on an impeccable parade, but that military precision has not been mirrored on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: There is a very muted commemoration of Victory Day here in Ukraine. Understandably, people are consumed by the war they are fighting right here right now -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Putin`s parade in Moscow came just hours after Russian forces allegedly bombed a school where dozens of civilians were sheltering in the east of the country. Local officials estimate at least 60 people were killed.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in Ukraine for us as well.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, what more can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Well, Jericka, many people are already calling the attack on the school a war crime, and this is where the consequences of President Putin`s decisions are played out, in the cities and towns all along the frontline.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Reduced to burning and smoldering rubble in what Ukrainian authorities say was a direct hit. The school were terrified families sought shelter from Russia`s bombardment. We have found evidence of indiscriminate shelling everywhere we`ve gone. The battle scarred village of Velyka Novosilka sits right on the warpath of the Russian advance from the south.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. Defense official says 10,000 Russian soldiers are now on the move north of Mariupol and they are massing on the outskirts of this village, which has clearly come under repeated attack.\par \par \uc2 We came upon Iryna Ilyenko (sp?) and her neighbor Valentina Hajnova (sp?) surveying the ruins of their homes.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Amid the constant crack of mortar fire and artillery.\par \par \uc2 Does that happen a lot?\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Are you going to try to leave now?\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: She says she can`t leave her bedridden husband behind, "Whatever God has prepared for me," she said, "Let it be."\par \par \uc2 To the northeast, the city of Kramatorsk is also in the firing line, residential neighborhoods devastated by Russian firepower. In the ruins, children`s toys and other personal belongings, more lives torn apart in Putin`s relentless drive to seize this region of Eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Now Ukrainian forces have been able to slow down the Russian advance, but they`ve got little defense against this artillery and airstrikes and that has only intensified by the day -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Charlie D`Agata in Ukraine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here in this country, President Biden met virtually with \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders as part of renewed efforts to isolate Russia by cutting off Moscow`s cash flow from oil exports.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 We also saw First Lady Jill Biden visiting Ukraine over the weekend for Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 That`s right. The White House tells reporters that the First Lady has wanted to go to Ukraine since March and of course, we know that her President has expressed his desire to visit as well, but in the end, she was the one who got there first in a trip that came together just a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN (First Lady of the United States): Happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA (First Lady of Ukraine): Oh, thank you very much. Happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: First Lady Jill Biden arrived in a Ukrainian border town Sunday bearing flowers for the country`s First Lady \uc2 Olena Zelenska\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Zelenska has not been seen in public since the start of the Russian invasion. Her location and her children`s kept secret during the war.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: I wanted to come on Mother`s Day. I thought it was important to show the Ukrainian people that this war has to stop.\par \par \uc2 (OLENA ZELENSKA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: We all feel your support, and we all feel the leadership of the U.S. President.\par \par \uc2 (President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Her husband Ukrainian President Zelenskyy, spoke with some \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders on Sunday including President Biden who joined the call from Wilmington, Delaware. The group announced that all seven countries will either phase out or totally ban imports of Russian oil in an effort to target one of Russia`s biggest sources of income.\par \par \uc2 Many European countries are dependent on Russian oil and until yesterday, only the U.S. had banned it. Separately, the U.S. also unleashed a new round of sanctions on Russia`s biggest state television networks, barred Americans from consulting on business with anyone in Russia and set a host of new visa restrictions for Russian elites.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s Ambassador to the U.S. welcomed the moves.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA (Ukrainian Ambassador to the U.S.): We count on all of our friends and allies to help us with everything so we can stop Russia while it is still in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: As Russia was celebrating its Victory Day, the U.S. was sending diplomats back to the U.S. Embassy in Kyiv for the first time. Now, this was just a temporary visit, but the State Department is hoping to reopen the embassy fully sometime in the coming weeks -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Nancy Cordes for us at the White House. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the intense debate over the possibility that Roe v. Wade might be overturned. Over the weekend, protesters gathered and tensions continued to rise across the country.\par \par \uc2 A new CBS News poll shows nearly two thirds of Americans want Roe v. Wade to be kept in place and abortion rights preserved. Thirty-six percent disagree. Some Democrats are now trying to shift this argument from the Courts to Congress.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Scott. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Jericka, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 In a Congress that`s often gridlocked on many things, they`re about to begin debating abortion rights law. There is no real prospect of the legislation here becoming law this week, but it`s a chance for both parties to lay down political markers days after the leak of that draft Supreme Court opinion and months before the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN protesting.)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: After a weekend of protests outside the Supreme Court, outside churches, and even at the homes of some of the High Courts` Justices, the abortion debate shifts to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  later today as Democratic Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  begins the proceedings ahead of a Wednesday vote to codify abortion rights into law.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Every American will see how every senator stands. They can`t duck it anymore.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A bill has already passed at the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2 , but has no clear path to the 60 votes needed in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): He calls it a bill to codify Roe versus Wade, but that`s not in fact what the bill does. It is a radical abortion bill, which sadly reflects where today`s Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a weekend interview with "U.S.A. Today," Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said it is possible legislators could see a nationwide ban on abortion if the Court overturns Roe versus Wade and if Republicans win control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 But in new CBS News polling, two-thirds of women under 50 said overturning Roe could lead to more restrictions on contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP chanting "Rise up for abortion rights.")\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A concern Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  expressed on "Face the Nation."\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): This is about respect for privacy. What`s next? What`s next?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Despite the protests that have developed ahead of the Supreme Court ruling, there are political analysts and candidates who say this may not be the swing issue for the midterm elections, an election cycle so far, partly defined by inflation, especially food and gas prices.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see if this debate and this week change that -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Indeed, we will. Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to a scary story out of the Bahamas where investigators are searching for answers after three American tourists died in their hotel rooms and another had to be airlifted to a hospital. All four victims were staying at the Sandals Emerald Bay Resort in Great Exuma.\par \par \uc2 Health officials there have not determined what caused the deaths, but say foul play is not suspected. The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  says, it is closely monitoring the investigation and our Manuel Bojorquez has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL DARVILLE (Bahamas Minister of Health and Wellness): There were some signs of individual presenting to the clinic with nausea, vomiting, and some symptoms.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The Bahamas Health Minister says it all began Thursday night, two American tourists at the Sandals Emerald Bay Resort complained they were feeling ill and were treated at a local medical facility.\par \par \uc2 The next morning, the couple was found dead in their resort villa. The man police say was slumped against a wall. The woman was on their bed, both showed signs of convulsion.\par \par \uc2 Minutes earlier, police had found another man dead on the floor of his villa.\par \par \uc2 ABC News reports, it was Birmingham, Alabama resident Vincent Chiarella and that his wife, Dawn has survived after experiencing severe swelling and paralysis.\par \par \uc2 She was airlifted to \uc2 Princess Margaret Hospital\uc2  in the capital, Nassau, about a 30-minute flight away. Now, health officials are waiting on autopsy reports to determine what caused the tourists` mysterious deaths.\par \par \uc2 JASON TOTH (Tourist): Hopefully nothing`s going to happen to us, but --\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Jason Toth and Sydney Rosenquist arrived at the Sandals Resort for their honeymoon, one day after the victims` bodies were found.\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY ROSENQUIST (Tourist): It definitely means a cause for concern because you want to know what happened.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: It`s certainly very irregular.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: As a general rule, CBS News medical contributor Dr. David Agus says it is important to have access to your health records when you travel and to listen to your body.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: If you start to feel ill and it`s not getting better that you go to an emergency room wherever you are, and if the treatment doesn`t work, go back to that emergency room.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Manuel Bojorquez.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We should point out, we did reach out to Sandals for comment and they told us they followed protocol, alerting medical personnel and local law enforcement as soon as they were aware of the health emergencies. They also say they`re now actively working in support of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 It`s a very tragic story. This is a highlight of people`s lives and ends up being the end of it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, tragic and disturbing. You know, it`s one of those places that my wife and I love to visit. And like you said, people want to go to relax and enjoy themselves before they return back home and their life ends there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, now to dangerous weather conditions in New Mexico where wind gusts topping 60 miles an hour have fueled wildfires. The Calf Canyon Fire has now burned an area nearly the size of New York City.\par \par \uc2 For the forecast, we turn to meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, how long will this hot and dry weather last?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Sadly, it is going to be a problem for most of the week, and we`ll show you the critical fire danger that we have today and it goes all the way up to extreme, which is the worst possible level that you could have.\par \par \uc2 So, why is it so bad? Well, we have two big factors here: Very strong winds that will be blowing through and also very dry conditions. Our relative humidity is five to 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 Again, you could think of it like if you take a test and you get a five or 15 percent, that is failing. It`s really bad.\par \par \uc2 And also, we have to deal with the heat. Look at Abilene, 104. Our record today is 101 that was set back in 1886. So we could be breaking longstanding records. The heat will expand through Friday from Texas to the Midwest, all the way into the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, you can catch all-day coverage featuring our virtual view technology on The Weather Channel Television Network.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Stephanie, before you go, on Friday, you said you were putting all of your money on the horse, "Summer is Tomorrow at the derby. I`m just curious. How did that go?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Yes, it started out well, and then we didn`t finish so great. But Nate, you know, I was thinking about it. Maybe you should invite me to your dinner party because when I pick wine, I also pick it by the cute name or the cute look of it, and so yes, not the best of it. Maybe I should stick to the weather.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, wine and weather. Stephanie, it`s all good. Because when you said bet on that horse, I kept my wallet in my pocket.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the record, the horse finish dead last.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dead last. Dead last.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was at the very back of the pack.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Oh no. Oh no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s all right, it`s all right. We still love you.\par \par \uc2 All right, and speaking of the derby coming up, we talked to the trainer of this year`s historic Kentucky Derby winner about the long road that led to that finish line and that incredible victory.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:18 to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, we`ll be talking about a range of big issues from abortion rights if Roe v. Wade is overturned to voting rights in the wake of Court decisions nationwide, all with former U.S. Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 . He is right there in our greenroom to talk about his new book and why he thinks American democracy is on the brink of collapse.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Mr. Attorney General. We`ll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Coming up, basketball star, Brittney Griner was noticeably absent when the WNBA season tipped off. We will show you how players showed solidarity with Griner who has been detained in Russia since February.\par \par \uc2 And later, an exclusive guest in-studio will reveal the newest member of the "Sesame Street" family, and here`s a hint, it involves cookies.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pretty morning shot of Washington, D.C. there.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS here at 7:30.\par \par \uc2 Former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  believes our voting rights are in serious danger right now. In his new book, "Our Unfinished March" is a history of voting in this country and a picture of the situation we are in today, a dark picture at that.\par \par \uc2 He describes, quote, "Our warped Senate, our gerrymandered House, our malapportioned Electoral College and our politicized Supreme Court, which are the reasons America is a democracy in need of serious renovation." Close quote.\par \par \uc2 Holder is Chairman of the National Democratic Redistricting Committee, which challenges electoral maps and he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Attorney General, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER (Former U.S. Attorney General): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You describe a picture of American democracy, flawed in all three branches of government and I want to get to that and the solutions, but I want to begin with the judicial branch. If Roe is overturned, and abortion rights are no longer guaranteed in this country, you would support the end of the filibuster to pass a law providing access.\par \par \uc2 Tell me how that might work?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Yes, I think that what`s likely to happen is that the decision may actually occur that Roe is overturned, I suspect the language that you saw in the leaked legal opinion will not appear as caustic. I think that it is overheated.\par \par \uc2 But if Roe were to be overturned, and if we could get past the filibuster, you could pass a Federal statute that would declare as the law of the land and women would have the right to reproductive choices that the American people support.\par \par \uc2 I think that is something we have to understand here. The Supreme Court is about to do something that is inconsistent with the will of the American people and upon which the American people have for the past 50 years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everything you write about in this book is about making sure majority rules in this country and we have a system at the moment where minority rules in some cases. Removing the filibuster would be a way to make sure majority positions take hold.\par \par \uc2 One criticism would be that it creates a ping pong effect. Republicans come into power, they ban abortion access. Democrats come back and in power, they flip the light switch the other direction. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Well, we have to believe in the American people and believe in our system. The ping pong effect would be modified by the fact that through the electoral process, if a party comes in, takes power, and puts in place positions that are inconsistent, again, with the desires of the American people, they get voted out.\par \par \uc2 So yes, there is the potential for the ping pong. But you know, we have to believe in the American people as opposed to debasing the systems that we have put in place that for too long, keep the will the American people from directing the way in which the nation should proceed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Before we move on, I have to ask, as a former Attorney General, would \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  have any options that you see if Roe is overturned?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: You know, there`s the possibility I suppose of executive actions. But I think the real rejoinder to any decision that the Court might make would be Federal legislation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, so let`s talk voting rights and voting access. What do you think the biggest threat is today to voting rights?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: I think it`s a whole range of things. We see voter suppression, we see gerrymandering. We see a whole range of ways in which you`re trying to keep the will of the American people from being expressed in the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 This is something that is not inconsistent with what we have seen before and this is what I talk about in the book "Our Unfinished March" where we have seen in the 19th Century, the 18th Century, the 20th century attempts made to empower a few and disenfranchise the many.\par \par \uc2 But I think the thing we have to get, and what I try to talk about in the book, is that ordinary people were able to fight those people who tried to hold on to power in an unjustified way, and actually empower the American people.\par \par \uc2 The arc that we have seen in this country has always been to empower more people, get more people have the right to vote, and we can do that, again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I mean, voting is the right that guarantees all the others. That`s very true, but on the subject of gerrymandering, and you`re committing your career to ending gerrymandering and improving voting access, both parties do it. Right?\par \par \uc2 So how do you get them to disarm and come to the table and agree on your solution, which would be the sort of independent commissions?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Well, see, we can`t fall into some notion of equivalency here because in a lot of ways, that`s false. What \uc2 Princeton University\uc2  said is what -- they looked at the gerrymandering the Republicans did in the last cycle in 2012, and said it was the worst gerrymandering of the last half century.\par \par \uc2 Now, there have been some maps that Democrats would draw on this cycle that I don`t agree with. New York and Maryland among them, but those pale in comparison to what Republicans have done in Texas, Georgia, potentially, in Florida. You`re comparing apples and oranges in a lot of ways and I am committed -- I`m committed to fighting for fairness because I`m also confident that if the process is fair, the Democrats, progressives will do just fine. We don`t have to cheat. Republicans have to cheat in order to win.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think they would disagree, but strong statement there. You have a lot of strong ideas in the book. Some of the proposals to increase majority rule in this country would be to term limit Supreme Court justices to meter appointments from Presidents, you want to get rid of the Electoral College. You want to add two new states, Puerto Rico and D.C.\par \par \uc2 You yourself in the book, say some of these ideas might seem radical, is there one that you think we should focus on right now and could get done?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: I mean, I think the possibility exists for all of them to get done. Because the reality is that, you know, if you look back at our history, everything seemed like it was impossible. We had to end an American system of racial apartheid. I`m sure Dr. King had moments we thought, "We can`t pull this off," and yet they did.\par \par \uc2 Women were not likely to get the right to vote. Alice Paul, somebody who we don`t talk about an awful lot, led the movement, and now women have the right to vote. So yes, we could do away with the Electoral College as a way in which you can do it without amending the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 We can end partisan gerrymandering, there is Federal legislation that was considered and that should have been passed. All of these things are possible if we limit ourselves and say they`re impossible, they will never happen. If we say they`re possible and work towards them, we can make it happen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Mr. Attorney General, I now understand why you end the book an idealist.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Yes, I`m an idealist because I believe in the American experiment, I believe in this country.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much. Appreciate you being here.\par \par \uc2 "Our Unfinished March" goes on sale tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And speaking of impossibilities, history was made with a stunning win against 80 to one odds at this year`s Kentucky Derby. Coming up, the trainer tells us the story behind that victory and how it almost didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh my goodness. The longest shot has won the Kentucky Derby. Rich Strike has done it in a stunning unbelievable event.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re looking at an astonishing upset at the Kentucky Derby where underdog, Rich Strike carved his name into history books with a remarkable come from behind win. The colt entered Saturday`s race at 80 to one odds, 80 to one odds, and became the derbies biggest upset since 1913.\par \par \uc2 But as Meg Oliver shows us, the horse wasn`t the only one who stole the show. His trainer, Eric Reed celebrated his own comeback victory after nearly quitting the sport five years ago.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Rich Strike is coming up on the inside, oh my goodness. The longest shot has won the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Rich Strike sent the crowds roaring as he stormed to victory, winning the 148th Kentucky Derby on Saturday. The moment marked the second biggest upset in the race`s history.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Friday morning at 8:00 AM. This horse was not in the race.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They`re off.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The colt was a last minute entry after another horse was scratched Friday morning and had only one previous win in his career.\par \par \uc2 ERIC REED (Rich Strike trainer): I rang my father and I said, "We`re going to win the Kentucky Derby."\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The victory was a first for his trainer, Eric Reed.\par \par \uc2 In that final stretch, as he crossed the finish line, walk us through it?\par \par \uc2 ERIC REED: I almost blacked out. I just couldn`t imagine when he put his head in front that we had won the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Reed knows firsthand how important it is to persevere in the sport. He almost quit training five years ago after he and his wife lost nearly two dozen courses in a barn fire at his Training Center in Lexington, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 How did you emotionally come back from that?\par \par \uc2 ERIC REED: It was about as low a point in my life that you could get at that time. I had to make a decision, was I going to let that end my career? Or was I going to fight back one more time? And I`m glad I chose to fight back one more time.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: His father, Herbert Reed, who is a former trainer was overcome with pride standing next to his son after his surprising win.\par \par \uc2 HERBERT REED (Eric Reed`s father): Unbelievable. I am just elated. It`s unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: I`ve watched your dad break down after the derby. How did that feel?\par \par \uc2 ERIC REED: Probably the proudest day of my life.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: For CBS MORNINGS, Meg Oliver.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Unbelievable upset.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Eight to one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s wild.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It`s like, we need those stories though. You know, we always love a good underdog.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So another horse couldn`t go, and then Friday, they got the word --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, at the last minute. Can you imagine?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And ended up winning the derby?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s fate, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good day for that horse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories you`ll be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What`s the other --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay. I`m being told, that`s not Will Smith.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Will Smith was the remake.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, he was the remake.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Men in Black" the movie.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But who was the original?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Patrice --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Come on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOU