 an interview you won`t want to miss.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 , we will talk to him about why he believes former President Trump`s behavior makes him a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I`m Jericka Duncan, a student is changing the game by writing a more inclusive history for future generations, as she is honoring women who`ve been forgotten by historians and educators.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Today`s "Kindness 101" story from Steve Hartman is all about self-improvement and achieving your dreams no matter how long it takes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, it is like a dream come true.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Dozens of Ukrainians are feared dead after Russia bombs a school being used as a shelter.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The Ukrainian forces have been able to slow down the Russia in advance, but they`ve got little defense against this, artillery and airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): The leaked decision that became law is an abomination.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Protesters rally outside the homes of some Supreme Court Justices as the Senate prepares to vote on protecting abortion rights by law.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): It is a radical abortion bill, which sadly reflects where today`s Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: An investigation is underway after three American tourists are found dead at a resort in the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL DARVILLE: There were some signs of individuals presenting to the clinic with nausea, vomiting, and some symptoms.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And graduates of Wiley College in East Texas got to supersize the price when their student debts were paid in full by an anonymous donor.\par \par \uc2 HERMAN J. FELTON JR. (Wiley University president and CEO): The first thing I want to do is tell all of you who have balances that you no longer have balances.\par \par \uc2 You are debt free. You do not owe the college a penny. If you have a balance, you had a balance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I love when that happens. I wish it didn`t have to happen because college was affordable in the first place, but I digress. We`ve got a big interview to kick off the hour. So, let`s get to it.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with a revealing firsthand account of what happened inside the White House while former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in office. All of it according to former Secretary of Defense \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 . He has got a new book. It`s called "A Sacred Oath: Memoirs of a Secretary of Defense during Extraordinary Times," and it`s a detailed account of the nearly 18 months he served under President Trump from June 2019 to November of 2020.\par \par \uc2 The former Secretary writes about the challenges of working for Trump, including allegations that the President suggested shooting at protesters in the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Esper joins us for his first live television interview about this book.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2  (Former U.S. Secretary of Defense): Thanks. Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m glad you wrote the book. I`m glad you served in your role, but I have to start with kind of a process question, which is, why did you accept this job in the first place, having just seen how President Trump treated your predecessor, General Mattis?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : You know, I served my country since the age of 18. When I went to West Point, 10 years in the Army, active duty combat, Gulf War, another 11 years in the Guard and Reserve, it was another chance for me to once again serve my country and in a way that I thought could be meaningful, and help deliver on our nation`s security.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Clearly, it was meaningful.\par \par \uc2 As you write in the book, you had to stop a lot of bad things from happening. I`d love to know if there was a particular moment, a high point or really a low point where you felt like the world order as we know it, or the country, as we know, it was in danger of spinning out of control.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Well, as I talk about my book, it`s June 1st, right? We go from June 1st to June 2nd, protests around the country, hundreds of cities, unrest, the President is pushing the call on active duty troops. It is not relenting.\par \par \uc2 And as we roll into June 3rd, I decide the night of June 2nd that I`ve got to get out there and say something. The Republic feels wobbly. Somebody needs to put their hand on the wheel, foot on the brake and kind of calm things down, and I thought that me coming out publicly and saying, look, I do not support the invocation of the Insurrection Act. It doesn`t make sense at this point in time, that that might help. And I -- and I believe it did, frankly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do you think that President Trump fully understood the consequences of his actions when he was in office?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : With regard to this? No. He was just so upset and angry about the protests in the street that he wanted to suppress it. He wanted to get it under control.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You also wrote that President Trump asked the military if he could shoot missiles into Mexico, and you basically explained to him that that`s illegal. It`s an act of war.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he said, "Well, we`ll shoot those missiles at cartels and drug houses." When you told him that, what was his response?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Well, look, yes, he wanted to shoot drug labs. And as I say, I get it, right? We all are troubled by drugs in America. He was trying to find a way to go after this, but there are right ways to do it and wrong ways to do it and when I said no, you can`t. It would be an act of war, illegal. He said, well, look nobody will know. They just won`t find out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In your book that you wrote here, you said that our nation`s institutions in our democracy are only as strong as the people who are serving in those roles. It`s likely that former President Trump will run again in 2024. What are the consequences of him running and possibly being nominated as the next President of the United States?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : I`m glad you read that and highlighted that, and you`re right. I think our institutions are only as strong as the people and I was blessed to serve with wonderful people at the Pentagon who really put country first. And so that`s one of the things you have to look at when you elect a President.\par \par \uc2 Who will they bring in around them? Who will be in their Cabinet? And I think, this is, in some ways a cautionary tale about what the future may hold if President Trump runs in 2024.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What is at stake? I mean, here we are talking about Ukraine. We`re still always, you know, keeping a close eye on China, Iran. But what is at stake if President Trump were to run again?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Well, look, my view is whoever runs has to put country first over self. You have to have integrity and principles. You have to be willing to reach across the aisle and advance the country`s greater good and President Trump doesn`t meet those marks for me. So we have to be very cautious about who we elect to be President.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Is he competent enough to hold office?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : He has held office, but I would say for all the Republicans like myself, who were attracted to the policies he advanced: Less regulation, lower taxes, a stronger military and so on, there is a whole other crew of Republicans out there who advance those same principles without the turmoil, without the bitterness and divisiveness and the courseness that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brings.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Like who?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : I`m not going to start naming names today. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 The 2024 will start after the midterms. Let`s see who emerges, let`s see who will find some distance between them and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What does it say about the state of politics in this country that after your experience with President Trump in the 2020 election, you could not bring yourself to vote for President Biden?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Well, it`s -- look, I worked with President Biden, if you will, when I served on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee. I know who he is, what he was. I think we should have better choices out there than what we have today.\par \par \uc2 Out of a nation of 330 million people, we have got to have a better set of candidates to really help lead our country and they have to put country first. They have to aspire to greater things.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Do you have any interest?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : I couldn`t win an election. I`m too much in the middle, I guess. You know, I consider myself a Reagan Republican.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In the book, you talk about how President Trump is unprincipled, hearing you say that you think he`s also unfit, I think is a new line that you`ve crossed here. And I`m curious, you know, you`re going to get involved now with the publication of this book with a kind of tit- for-tat back and forth with the former President.\par \par \uc2 So for example, he no commented on the shooting missiles into Mexico. But he says it`s a lie that he suggested shooting protesters in Washington in that fateful summer. What`s your response to that?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : People will choose to believe who they want to believe. I will tell you that I sent this my draft out to over two dozen four-star officers, senior civilians, even a few Cabinet members to review nearly all or parts of the book and to get it -- to help me get it as accurate, complete, and fair as possible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So one of the four no`s that you would Mark Milley had when you were trying to hold order there in the administration was you didn`t want to politicize the Department of Defense. I remember speaking to General Mattis about his book when it came out. He was also not wanting to do that.\par \par \uc2 Your book seems to go further. And now in this interview, you`re going further. Are you worried with the book and with your statements that you risk politicizing the very department you wanted to protect?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Well, look, clearly, I`m out of office now. I was a political appointee, so there is some expectation that you have one foot in the political camp and one foot outside the political camp. But I`ll tell you what? The Department of Defense has a strong ethos. It`s a resilient organization.\par \par \uc2 We are blessed to have the service members, officer -- NCO-enlisted members serving that we do. I think the institution will endure.\par \par \uc2 For 240 some years, it`s been defending America, it will endure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Quickly, what are your thoughts on the Biden administration`s approach on the war in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Well, as I said, I think it was mixed at first. I thought they were moving too slowly and not enough. I said, from day one, we should implement full sanctions and give the Ukrainians everything they need to do the job. But I think it`s picked up.\par \par \uc2 I think the President has done a good job in terms of bringing along the allies. I think very good job in terms of the strategic release of information to reveal what Putin was trying to do surreptitiously.\par \par \uc2 So I think we`re in a much better spot today than where we were initially.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Secretary Esper, as you put in the very front of the book, "The Oath of the Secretary of Defense is to the Constitution."\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Most important thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : Thank you all very much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The book "A Sacred Oath" goes on sale tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And ahead, our special series "Kindness 101" Steve Hartman and his kids give us an important lesson in self-improvement. See the inspiring story of a D-Day veteran who was determined to overcome huge odds decades later.\par \par \uc2 But first, 8:09. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Coming up. May is Mental Health Awareness month, but not everyone knows where to go for help. Well, we`ll talk about how to connect with all the right resources with Jamie Howard from the Child Mind Institute.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: May is Mental Health Awareness Month, and we`re having important conversations about mental health every Monday this month. One in five Americans will experience mental illness in any given year and one in five children struggle with mental health, but finding and paying for mental healthcare can be challenging and overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 Research shows 46 percent of adults with mental illness haven`t received healthcare for it.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Howard is senior clinical psychologist from the Child Mind Institute. Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE HOWARD (Child Mind Institute Senior clinical psychologist): Good morning. Good. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So there are many different types of mental healthcare, where -- what are the different types? And then where do you start?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE HOWARD: Right. Okay.\par \par \uc2 So the term "therapist" can be confusing because it refers to a wide array of professionals. So one is a licensed clinical social worker that you might hear of. This is someone who has a Master`s Degree with specialization in providing therapy, then there is a clinical psychologist like myself, and the letters you might see are PhD or Psy-D and we specialize in providing therapy as well.\par \par \uc2 We also do research and provide testing. And then there`s psychiatrists, and they have MDs, they go to medical school, and they usually tend to focus on prescribing medicine. So there`s all of these different professionals who can provide mental healthcare.\par \par \uc2 One of the most important things you want to look for is what we call evidence based mental healthcare. So that means that there`s research to support the various elements of the therapy that your provider is administering.\par \par \uc2 And one of the most important things is to remember, regardless of what type of mental healthcare provider you have, you want them to be licensed and credentialed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so how do you know as a parent when it`s time to seek out professional help?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE HOWARD: Right. So there are a few things we want to keep in mind when you`re monitoring your child`s mood or behavior, you want to think about the duration. How long has this been going on for?\par \par \uc2 If it has been up to a month or so that`s when we start to think that treatment might be indicated, and you should at least get an evaluation. You also want to consider the intensity? So is this symptoms, so for example, you have a very irritable teenager, is this like any other teenager? Are they just kind of difficult right now? Or is this more significant?\par \par \uc2 You can sort of get a sense by talking to other parents or teachers, pediatrician to get a sense of that. Then you also want to look at the frequency. If it is happening most days, the mood or behavior problem that you`re noticing, then it`s also a good indication to get treatment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some people may make a call and say, "All right, it`s time" whether it`s for myself as an adult or my family members, my kids, but then they can be booked up for months at a time.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE HOWARD: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what do you do in that situation?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE HOWARD: So one thing we like to recommend is that you start with either your general practitioner or your pediatrician, because sometimes they can actually treat the medical issues underlying a psychological condition. And then if not, they also usually have really good community resources to refer you to.\par \par \uc2 They might sort of have their finger on the pulse of who is available and where is a good place to go. The other thing to keep in mind is telehealth. So, it`s sort of the silver lining of the pandemic in so many ways because it has reduced a lot of barriers to care for families, for adults and children alike.\par \par \uc2 So I still do a lot of telehealth in my practice, even though we`re able to meet in person, people just tend to prefer it because you don`t have to deal with parking and transportation. It just cuts down on a lot of hassle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jamie, you`re the best. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE HOWARD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, this morning, we`re revealing the newest "Sesame Street" puppet. Yes, we`ll show you how this snack loving cookie loving robot uses science and math to solve big problems.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You heard it here first on CBS MORNINGS. We`re revealing the newest "Sesame Street" puppet, Mecha Cookie stars in the new show "Sesame Street" "Mecha Builders."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mecha Builders.")\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The show reimagines Elmo, Cookie Monster, and Abby Cadabby as robot heroes in training. They`re called Mecha Elmo, Mecha Cookie, and Mecha Abby and they use STEM superpowers to solve problems.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk with Mecha Cookie in just a moment, but before we do, we`ve got a first look at a new episode where the "Mecha Builders" work to stop a runaway boulder.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mecha Builders.")\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: She really did, and we are super excited to introduce you to Mecha Cookie who is in our greenroom right now with the one and only Vlad. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good morning and Mecha Cookie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE ("Sesame Street."): Yes. Good morning. Mecha Cookie Monster here to save the day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You really are.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Do they need to be saved?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m always in need of saving, Mecha Cookie.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: What can we do? Everything okay in show this morning? Can we help out in any way?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now that you`re here, you can help -- you`ve got a lot of very cool gadgets including, I love this cookie counter.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: That`s me cookie clock.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Your cookie clock.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How does that work?\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Well, me can grow to a mega giant and me can shrink to mega tiny and when cookie clock go to zero, me go back to normal size.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you`ve got a lot of these gadgets.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Me also have a handy hammer hand. Me hand can turn into a hammer so we can smash things. You need me to smash anything this morning around here?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, I`ve got a lot of things around here beginning --\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The crew members.\par \par \uc2 But hold on a second, let me ask you about --\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Oh yes, that guy over there me think.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The guy right there.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Okay, then he first on list.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: His name is Klaus.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Klaus.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To hammer.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Me see you in a second, Klaus.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me ask you about solving the world`s problems. You do have a process that you and your friends Mecha Abby and Mecha Elmo use to help solve problems.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: We do. Yes. We like to make a plan.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: And then we test the plan and then we solve problem. Yes, sometimes we not get it right away.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: You know, you have to test it and try it and test it and fail and fail and then finally, you hopefully, you solve problem and save the day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that. We love that, Mecha Cookie. I got this, we`re going to go back stage.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Oh, Cookie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to have some. We are going to have some.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Cookie. Me got cookie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You can watch --\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Can Klaus come and have Cookie with us?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You can watch Sesame Street "Mecha Builders" today on Cartoonito.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Cartoonito.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Cartoon Network and tomorrow --\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Cookie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On HBO Max.\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Cookie. Cookie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up next --\par \par \uc2 MECHA COOKIE: Cookie. Klaus, get over here.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that we are talking about at the table -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are at the table.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got a story that caught my eye. It reminded me of my Florida boyhood. Take a look at this. This is called Jay`s Sandbar Food Boat. It`s a beautiful boat there. Hand crafted by Mr. Jeremy Licht, serves gator bites and clam nachos to boaters on the Intracoastal Waterway. They`re off of Fort Lauderdale.\par \par \uc2 These are really common things down in South Florida. People love them. You get delivery by dinghy, you can swim up with your credit card in your teeth and like kind of yell the order through that.\par \par \uc2 This community establishment unfortunately on Sunday got hit by a little bit of a wave. One of the pontoons snapped and take a look at it now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It went under in four feet of water on a sandbar there in Fort Lauderdale. So I mean, no one was hurt, fortunately. Everybody got off and they`re hoping to rebuild, but I just wanted a shout out to an establishment like that, which takes me back to my childhood.\par \par \uc2 They`re trying to rebuild. Jay is trying to raise $85,000.00 which is all it takes to put a pontoon boat out on the water and believe it or not, that is a registered official mobile food vehicle in Florida.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It looks totally --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So it is like a food truck on the water.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a food truck on the water. They have been inspected. They passed. They were good to go. They just got a little unlucky. So they`re hoping to bounce back and --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I have a feeling ...\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love seeing vehicles like that.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: ... you highlighting it will definitely help.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, they`ve got a -- I think they have a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Google\uc2  it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Which is good, but I was going to crack a joke, but that`s somebody`s business and I hope he gets back on his feet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was just say back on the water.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was reached for comment for the local paper, and he said, "stuff happens," in a more colorful word.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So Florida.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Florida. I love that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, I hope they get back on the water and back to business.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so I have something pretty cool that I`m excited about. My "Talk of the Table" is Hot Wheels. They are launching the first remote controlled wheelchair toy.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that`s right. So check this out.\par \par \uc2 First of all, how cool is this? Hot Wheels is expanding representation for kids who use wheelchairs. The toy is creating collaboration with five-time Wheelchair Motocross World Champion and Paralympic athlete, Aaron "Wheelz" -- "Wheelz" is his nickname -- Fotheringham.\par \par \uc2 He has spina bifida, was born with that and was a full-time wheelchair user by the age of eight and Hot Wheels said you know what, we need more representation in this space.\par \par \uc2 And Jerry Cody, Director of Product Design for Hot Wheels, RC and Innovation said he hopes the toy will inspire children to push past perceived limitation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh I am sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I saw that story, and the only reason I didn`t pick it is because when I read the headline, I thought that Hot Wheels was designing a full scale wheelchair. I didn`t realize it was a toy. I thought they were like doing a brand extension.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You thought like a souped up wheelchair?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Going 60 down the sidewalk?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was like, "I don`t know."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`d like to see that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it would be kind of cool.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, good for them. And like you said, representation matters. So good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, so my talk of the table is a video that Tyler Perry shared of an American Sign Language interpreter really getting into her work during a Janet Jackson concert over the weekend. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Janet Jackson concert.)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Perry posted the video to \uc2 Instagram\uc2  writing: "Can everyone in the world hire this beautiful woman to sign everything? She is giving me so much joy. God bless her. We should all approach our job this way." He says, "I`m going to work like that this Monday and says Happy Mother`s Day."\par \par \uc2 You know, this is the first time Janet Jackson has performed in three years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But going back to that ASL interpreter, we don`t know who she is, but she is obviously making an impact. She has over 124,000 likes already. It`s been up for 23 hours.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And when Tyler Perry signs off on it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Get ready. More comments.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A TV show coming from Tyler Perry about her life.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I`ll bet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She knows who she is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: She`ll be the next movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nice job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we`re going to switch gears now and go to our continuing special series "Kindness 101" with Steve Hartman. That`s where we share stories that are built around themes of kindness and character and the people who have mastered those qualities.\par \par \uc2 This morning`s lesson is self-improvement. Steve and his kids introduce us to a man who shows, really reminds us that you`re never too old to start something new.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning. I`m here with my daughter Merrill to celebrate the most important teacher you have -- you.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: That`s right. We`re talking about self-improvement.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Emmett, you want to take a stab at defining this one? I know it`s a toughy.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Self-improvement is improving yourself.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Genius. He gets that from me, you know?\par \par \uc2 I`m curious. Have you guys ever tried to improve something about yourself and struggled?\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: I`m trying to think. Do you have any ideas?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Oh, I have plenty of ideas.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Spit them out.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But this is self-improvement, not dad improvement. How about you, Merrill?\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: It was hard getting to have a lot of friends.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I`m thinking more about just ways you want to make yourself better.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: Oh, I was not done.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: And it`s kind of just like sometimes I`ve raised my voice that my friends and --\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Maybe you could start by not interrupting her.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: I agree.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Okay, self-improvement can be harder than it sounds, which is why it`s helpful to have motivation.\par \par \uc2 And today, I have that for you. A story pulled from our archive of the most inspiring Americans. This one, the perfect role model for self-improvement.\par \par \uc2 Inside a single-wide in Cookson, Oklahoma, a tortured soul lives alone.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY (World War Two veteran): It`s a hard life. Let me tell you that you ain`t never lived hard if you go through what I`ve been through.\par \par \uc2 This is me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Ed Bray served in World War Two. He was at Normandy on D- Day. He has two Purple Hearts and more than a dozen other medals. But to this day, he still can`t even read what they`re for, not because it`s too painful --\par \par \uc2 Victory.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: Victory.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But because he simply can`t read.\par \par \uc2 You know what that word is? E-U-R-O-P-E?\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: No.\par \par \uc2 The toughest thing that has ever happened to me in my life if not being able to read.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He said you were at Normandy though.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Illiteracy can be that damning.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: I`ve covered this up for 80 years. Nobody in this town knows I can`t read.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Until he retired. Ed worked a civilian job at an Air Force Base refueling planes. A co-worker helped him with the forums and whatnot.\par \par \uc2 At home, his wife covered for him for 62 years, until she died in `09.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: Put you guys some coffee on.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Today, Ed manages okay, but the soldier in him still refuses to surrender.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: I wanted to read one book. I don`t care if it`s about Mickey Mouse. I want to read one book before I die.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Over the years, Ed says many people have tried to school him, but invariably, either the teacher or the student would get frustrated and give up.\par \par \uc2 Then a few months ago, a friend suggested he see a Professor of Reading Education here at Oklahoma`s Northeastern State University.\par \par \uc2 TOBI THOMPSON (Northeastern State University professor): He told me I was wasting my time. And I said, well, we`ll just sit and chat a couple times a week. Is that okay?\par \par \uc2 Very good.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Tobi Thompson says eventually, their weekly talks gave way to flashcards.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: And everything started clicking -- four --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He got pretty good at the sight word.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: One.\par \par \uc2 TOBI THOMPSON: Catch up.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But the real breakthrough came when at the age of 89, Ed read this book about George Washington.\par \par \uc2 TOBI THOMPSON: It gave me goosebumps and it still does.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: To power a car.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He read three more shortly after, each one --\par \par \uc2 TOBI THOMPSON: Very good. Good job.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The same dramatic ending.\par \par \uc2 TOBI THOMPSON: Did you ever think you`d read that?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Each one, the same lesson for anyone considering self- improvement.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: Get in here and learn, baby, now. Because you ain`t going to learn in that pine box.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Just learned to read and already a poet.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: And that`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Ten years later, the one thing no one has been able to read is Ed`s obituary. He`s still with us. And he`s with us.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: I`m ready, baby.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Okay. You went around and talked to a lot of schools after our story. What was your message to them?\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: To tell them, children to read books and put them phones away.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That`s good advice.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: This telephone they`ve got today is the worst invention we`ve ever made. They are a pain. I can`t hardly run my own.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Do you think you inspired other people to read?\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: Oh yes. I`ve got boxes and boxes of letters from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: How old are you now?\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: Ninety-nine.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Can you believe you made it that long?\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: No. I don`t know what God`s got me to living for.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I do.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: What`s your reason?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: To inspire other people, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: Thank you, guys a million.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ED BRAY: You made my day today.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So that`s class for today. Hopefully meeting Ed will inspire a better you. We`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 MERRILL HARTMAN: Until then, stay kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ed`s full of wisdom.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, he is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen, he said: "Get in there and learn, baby, because you ain`t going to learn it in a pine box." That is as real as it gets. If you want to learn something, do it now because time is ticking.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It is. It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time is ticking. It`s "Kindness 101." He is 99.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Do you believe he is almost a hundred?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He might. It would be "Kindness 101." He`ll be 101. We`ll revisit him in two more years.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I said that during the break.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know, I stole it from you. You thought it was from me. I thought I`ll just take that. I self-improve through acquisition.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plagiarism.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh, thank you for that kindness.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was not kind, Tony.\par \par \uc2 All right, here we go, coming up, how one high schooler led the charge to get more women in History curriculums and made some history herself. Her amazing story is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 But first, a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Our series, "Changing the Game" profiles extraordinary women who are making a difference now and for future generations.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re introducing you to a Virginia teenager bringing new attention to women who are often left out of the History books. The high schooler developed her own curriculum for elementary and middle school students to recognize the accomplishments of women with an emphasis on women of color.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion shows us how she did it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Prasidha Padmanabhan is leading a modern day crusade to rewrite the History books ...\par \par \uc2 PRASIDHA PADMANABHAN (WEAR Organization President): Some of these are really easy, some of them are not.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: ... in one of the country`s largest school districts. The 17-year-old is creating an entire course of study for elementary and middle school students in Fairfax County, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 What motivated you to develop this curriculum?\par \par \uc2 PRASIDHA PADMANABHAN: Honestly, I think it was just my own experience with the law lack of education around women`s history I felt like especially women of color, we are often just -- their stories would go untold.\par \par \uc2 I am a first generation Indian and my mom is an Indian immigrant, and so I think growing up hearing her stories has always been something that I have taken and wanted to learn more about and always been inspired by.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: She founded a group called WEAR, Women for Education, Advocacy and Rights. The organization launched a petition last year to integrate more women`s history in their local schools. It comes amid a heated debate over public education curriculums in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin signed an Executive Order banning public educational programs that he claims are inherently divisive, like critical race theory.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to any parents out there or students who feel, "Why am I learning this? Why should I have to learn this?"\par \par \uc2 PRASIDHA PADMANABHAN: I think that, you know, if we disregard what really happened in our history, then we`re not getting the full picture and we`re not going to be informed citizens going forward.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: When you first read her proposal, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 DR. DEBORAH MARCH (Fairfax County Schools Cultural Pedagogy Specialist): I thought I need to meet this person.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Prasidha`s efforts caught the attention of Dr. Deborah March, an education specialist who connected her with Fairfax County teachers.\par \par \uc2 DR. DEBORAH MARCH: If you just look at curriculum standards, and notice who gets to be an individual with a name and a biography and who is identified as a group? Women during the Civil War, right? And so, Prasidha really helped us lift up the individual stories and contributions of women.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: From the Revolutionary War --\par \par \uc2 PRASIDHA PADMANABHAN: We all know Paul Revere was the man who said that the British are coming. But Sybil Ludington was a 16-year-old girl who rode horseback overnight to deliver the same exact message.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: To the Civil War --\par \par \uc2 PRASIDHA PADMANABHAN: Susie King Taylor, who was a nurse during the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And civil rights.\par \par \uc2 PRASIDHA PADMANABHAN: Claudette Colvin started the Montgomery Bus Boycott, she actually started it nine months before Rosa Parks.\par \par \uc2 JULIE CARRILL (Teacher): We`re going to use some resources that WEAR has created --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Each lesson is crafted as a presentation to spark discussions about game-changing female figures.\par \par \uc2 JULIE CARRILL: She was born to a free Black family in the north.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Julie Carrill (sp?) teaches sixth grade American history.\par \par \uc2 Even you are saying, this is a lesson for you as well.\par \par \uc2 JULIE CARRILL: We featured five different women in the lesson today and I hadn`t never heard of any of them.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A learning experience for teacher and students.\par \par \uc2 GIRL #1: It means that maybe I can do something important.\par \par \uc2 GIRL #2: I think that they should be taught all year round.\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: If we learned more about this, who knows how many more women we would acknowledge and know.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: What is your ultimate goal?\par \par \uc2 PRASIDHA PADMANABHAN: My ultimate goal is that every student across Virginia and across the country just learns equally about women. I don`t ever want a student to ask the question, "Why does Women`s History matter?" I want people to just know that I want Women`s History to be history and I want that to be a concept that every student learns and understands.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: For CBS MORNINGS, Nikole Killion, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think this is so powerful. And you know, for the simple fact that our education growing up depends on the schools we`re in, and then once we get to college, it is tailored towards the degrees that we`re pursuing.\par \par \uc2 So there`s gaps that we all have, and I think this young lady said, you know what, we can change that we can open up our curriculum and really highlight some of these women that we are not learning about.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, and I think the children of today, they have \uc2 Google\uc2  right there. They have \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . They can look up documentaries or it seems like they are seeking out that information to see a young woman like Prasidha do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It`s encouraging.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I love it. She studies about women that have made an impact and then she became one.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, look at that. Full circle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we are reflecting on Mother`s Day on this Mother`s Day Monday. Anyone want to share their experiences?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It was relaxing. I saw my mother, I got to go to church because I was off on Sunday and I hadn`t been. Shout out to First Corinthian Baptist Church in Harlem, just saying.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You went to church. Is that the only place you went?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I went to a casino, too with my mom. I know. I know. What a juxtaposition.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Did you make any Mother`s Day money?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: She made some.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, that`s great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, what about you? What did you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did the classic. Had the kids draw a card. Gave her some time on her own. Cooked dinner. The three straws.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I did the same thing. I did the brunch in the city. The spa day. Surprise my wife and I cooked at home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way, congratulations to both of you raising the human child to age 18.\p